window.AWKIT_DATA = {"builtAt":"2026-11-04T05:47:41.150Z","styles":[{"id":"S1-A1","name":"字雨","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-A1.jpg","markdown":"# A1 字雨\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · A 字\n- 长什么样：话像雨一样落下来，越积越多。\n- 适合：被说教、重复的话、压力\n- 关键词：字雨、落下、堆积\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"字雨\"的动画：话像雨一样落下来，越积越多。\n适合讲：被说教、重复的话、压力\n关键词：字雨、落下、堆积\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.A1(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.A1 = (c, w, h, t, s) => {\n  if (!s.init) { s.init = 1; s.r = rng(3); s.drops = []; s.pile = []; for (let i = 0; i < 26; i++) s.drops.push(spawn(true)); }\n  function spawn(first) { const r = s.r, base = Math.min(w, h) / 17, k = [0.55, 0.7, 0.9, 1.2, 1.7][Math.floor(r() * 5)]; return { txt: PARENT[Math.floor(r() * PARENT.length)], size: base * k, x: r() * w * 0.92 - w * 0.04, y: first ? -r() * h * 1.2 : -base * 2, v: (h / 300) * (36 + base * k * 3.2) }; }\n  const dt = clamp(t - (s.lt ?? t), 0, .05); s.lt = t;\n  c.fillStyle = vgrad(c, h, '#0b0e15', '#050608'); c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n  const ground = h - 10 - Math.min(s.pile.length, 60) * h * 0.004;\n  c.textBaseline = 'alphabetic';\n  for (const p of s.pile) { c.font = `${p.size}px ${SERIF}`; c.fillStyle = `rgba(150,164,190,${p.a})`; c.fillText(p.txt, p.x, p.y); }\n  for (const d of s.drops) {\n    d.y += d.v * dt;\n    if (d.y >= ground) { s.pile.push({ txt: d.txt, size: d.size * 0.9, x: d.x, y: ground + s.r() * 8, a: .32 }); Object.assign(d, spawn(false)); }\n    c.font = `${d.size}px ${SERIF}`; c.fillStyle = `rgba(214,224,242,${0.25 + d.size / (Math.min(w, h) / 17) * 0.35})`; c.fillText(d.txt, d.x, d.y);\n  }\n  if (s.pile.length > 70) s.pile.splice(0, 8);\n  glowDot(c, w * 0.5, h - 14, 14, '#f1b25c', .55 + .35 * (Math.sin(t * 5) > 0 ? 1 : 0.2));\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-A2","name":"光标打字","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-A2.jpg","markdown":"# A2 光标打字\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · A 字\n- 长什么样：黑底一个光标，一字一字打，打了又删。\n- 适合：说不出口的话、心里的独白\n- 关键词：打字、光标、删掉\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"光标打字\"的动画：黑底一个光标，一字一字打，打了又删。\n适合讲：说不出口的话、心里的独白\n关键词：打字、光标、删掉\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.A2(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.A2 = (c, w, h, t, s) => {\n  if (!s.init) {\n    s.init = 1; let tt = 0, str = ''; const st = [[0, '']];\n    const ops = [['type', '我其实一直想跟你说'], ['wait', 1.1], ['del', 6], ['wait', .5], ['type', '没事。'], ['wait', 1.6], ['del', 3], ['wait', .4], ['type', '……'], ['wait', 1.6], ['clear']];\n    for (const [k, v] of ops) {\n      if (k === 'type') for (const ch of v) { tt += .17; str += ch; st.push([tt, str]); }\n      else if (k === 'wait') tt += v;\n      else if (k === 'del') for (let i = 0; i < v; i++) { tt += .07; str = str.slice(0, -1); st.push([tt, str]); }\n      else { tt += .4; str = ''; st.push([tt, str]); }\n    }\n    s.st = st; s.len = tt + .8;\n  }\n  const tm = t % s.len; let i = 0; while (i + 1 < s.st.length && s.st[i + 1][0] <= tm) i++;\n  const str = s.st[i][1], since = tm - s.st[i][0];\n  c.fillStyle = '#0b0b0c'; c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n  const fs = Math.min(w / 10.5, h / 7); c.font = `500 ${fs}px ${SERIF}`; c.textBaseline = 'middle';\n  const x0 = w * 0.1, y0 = h * 0.5; c.fillStyle = '#ece8df'; c.fillText(str, x0, y0);\n  const cx = x0 + c.measureText(str).width + fs * 0.12;\n  const on = since < .45 || Math.floor(tm * 1.8) % 2 === 0;\n  if (on) { c.fillStyle = '#f1b25c'; c.fillRect(cx, y0 - fs * 0.55, Math.max(2, fs * 0.07), fs * 1.1); glowDot(c, cx, y0, fs * 0.9, '#f1b25c', .18); }\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-A3","name":"大字冲击","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-A3.jpg","markdown":"# A3 大字冲击\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · A 字\n- 长什么样：一个词一个词砸进画面，跟着节拍。\n- 适合：情绪爆发、金句、MV 副歌\n- 关键词：大字、砸下来、跟节拍\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"大字冲击\"的动画：一个词一个词砸进画面，跟着节拍。\n适合讲：情绪爆发、金句、MV 副歌\n关键词：大字、砸下来、跟节拍\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.A3(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.A3 = (c, w, h, t, s) => {\n  const L = [[\"你\", .17, .33, .30, 0], [\"懂\", .40, .30, .22, 0], [\"什么\", .70, .33, .17, 0], [\"我\", .19, .64, .20, 0], [\"都是\", .43, .62, .14, 0], [\"为你好\", .63, .84, .16, 1], [\"听话\", .80, .60, .13, 0], [\"！\", .90, .84, .22, 1]];\n  const beat = .4, len = L.length * beat + 1.4, tm = t % len, k = Math.floor(tm / beat), age = tm - k * beat;\n  const m = Math.min(w, h), hit = k < L.length ? Math.exp(-age * 9) : 0;\n  c.fillStyle = mixHex('#08080a', '#2a2a2e', hit * .5); c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n  const sx = (Math.sin(t * 91) * 6) * hit, sy = (Math.cos(t * 77) * 6) * hit;\n  c.save(); c.translate(sx, sy); c.textAlign = 'center'; c.textBaseline = 'middle';\n  for (let i = 0; i <= Math.min(k, L.length - 1); i++) {\n    const [txt, fx0, fy, fz, red] = L[i], newest = i === k, port = h > w, fx = port ? .5 + (fx0 - .5) * .85 : fx0;\n    const sc = newest ? 1 + .9 * Math.exp(-age * 16) : 1;\n    c.font = `900 ${fz * m * (port ? .95 : 1.45) * sc}px ${SANS}`;\n    c.fillStyle = red ? (newest ? '#ff4a32' : '#a3392a') : (newest ? '#ffffff' : '#55565c');\n    c.fillText(txt, fx * w, fy * h);\n  }\n  c.restore();\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-A4","name":"字的隧道","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-A4.jpg","markdown":"# A4 字的隧道\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · A 字\n- 长什么样：字排成一圈一圈，镜头往里钻。\n- 适合：想不通、越想越深\n- 关键词：隧道、旋转、往里钻\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"字的隧道\"的动画：字排成一圈一圈，镜头往里钻。\n适合讲：想不通、越想越深\n关键词：隧道、旋转、往里钻\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.A4(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.A4 = (c, w, h, t, s) => {\n  c.fillStyle = '#04050a'; c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n  const N = 14, R = Math.hypot(w, h) * 0.5, txt = '为什么是我为什么不能说为什么';\n  c.textAlign = 'center'; c.textBaseline = 'middle';\n  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {\n    const p = ((i / N) + t * 0.08) % 1, r = R * (0.03 + Math.pow(p, 2.6) * 1.6), a = Math.sin(Math.PI * p) * .9;\n    if (r < 3) continue;\n    const n = 20, rot = i * .37 + t * .18 * (i % 2 ? 1 : -1), fs = r * 0.15;\n    c.font = `500 ${fs}px ${SERIF}`; c.fillStyle = `rgba(${lerp(120, 230, p)},${lerp(140, 225, p)},${lerp(200, 240, p)},${a})`;\n    for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) { const an = rot + j / n * TAU; c.save(); c.translate(w / 2 + Math.cos(an) * r, h / 2 + Math.sin(an) * r); c.rotate(an + Math.PI / 2); c.fillText(txt[(i * 3 + j) % txt.length], 0, 0); c.restore(); }\n  }\n  glowDot(c, w / 2, h / 2, Math.min(w, h) * .12, '#7f8fd6', .25);\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-A5","name":"字散了再聚","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-A5.jpg","markdown":"# A5 字散了再聚\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · A 字\n- 长什么样：一句话散成粒子，聚成另一句话。\n- 适合：转念、换一种理解\n- 关键词：散开、聚成、变成\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"字散了再聚\"的动画：一句话散成粒子，聚成另一句话。\n适合讲：转念、换一种理解\n关键词：散开、聚成、变成\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.A5(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.A5 = (c, w, h, t, s, dpr) => {\n  if (!s.init) {\n    s.init = 1; const P = ['都是我的错', '那不是我的错'], pts = [];\n    for (const p of P) {\n      const [oc, ox] = offscreen(w, h, 1), lines = h > w ? [p.slice(0, Math.ceil(p.length / 2)), p.slice(Math.ceil(p.length / 2))] : [p];\n      const fs = Math.min(w * 0.82 / Math.max(...lines.map(l => l.length)), h * 0.34 / lines.length);\n      ox.fillStyle = '#fff'; ox.font = `900 ${fs}px ${SERIF}`; ox.textAlign = 'center'; ox.textBaseline = 'middle';\n      lines.forEach((l, i) => ox.fillText(l, w / 2, h / 2 + (i - (lines.length - 1) / 2) * fs * 1.15));\n      const d = ox.getImageData(0, 0, oc.width, oc.height).data, st = Math.max(2, Math.round(fs / 26)), arr = [];\n      for (let y = 0; y < oc.height; y += st) for (let x = 0; x < oc.width; x += st) if (d[(y * oc.width + x) * 4 + 3] > 128) arr.push([x, y]);\n      pts.push(arr);\n    }\n    const r = rng(9), N = Math.min(1800, Math.max(pts[0].length, pts[1].length));\n    s.p = []; for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) { const a = pts[0][Math.floor(r() * pts[0].length)], b = pts[1][Math.floor(r() * pts[1].length)], an = r() * TAU, rr = Math.sqrt(r()) * Math.min(w, h) * .55; s.p.push([a[0], a[1], b[0], b[1], w / 2 + Math.cos(an) * rr * 1.4, h / 2 + Math.sin(an) * rr, r()]); }\n  }\n  const q = t % 9; c.fillStyle = '#07070a'; c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n  let col;\n  for (const p of s.p) {\n    let x, y, k;\n    if (q < 2) { x = p[0]; y = p[1]; k = 0; }\n    else if (q < 3.3) { const e = eOut((q - 2) / 1.3); x = lerp(p[0], p[4], e); y = lerp(p[1], p[5], e); k = e * .5; }\n    else if (q < 4.9) { const e = ss(0, 1, (q - 3.3) / 1.6 - p[6] * .15); x = lerp(p[4], p[2], e); y = lerp(p[5], p[3], e); k = .5 + e * .5; }\n    else if (q < 7.4) { x = p[2]; y = p[3]; k = 1; }\n    else { const e = ss(7.4, 9, q); x = lerp(p[2], p[0], e); y = lerp(p[3], p[1], e); k = 1 - e; }\n    const j = 1.2; x += Math.sin(t * 2 + p[6] * 40) * j; y += Math.cos(t * 1.7 + p[6] * 30) * j;\n    col = mixHex('#9aa9c4', '#f6be72', k); c.fillStyle = col; c.fillRect(x, y, 1.6, 1.6);\n  }\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-B1","name":"流动的丝线","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-B1.jpg","markdown":"# B1 流动的丝线\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · B 粒子\n- 长什么样：无数细线跟着看不见的风流动。\n- 适合：思绪、情绪在流、平静下来\n- 关键词：流动、丝线、风\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"流动的丝线\"的动画：无数细线跟着看不见的风流动。\n适合讲：思绪、情绪在流、平静下来\n关键词：流动、丝线、风\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.B1(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.B1 = (c, w, h, t, s, dpr) => {\n  if (!s.init) { s.init = 1; [s.bc, s.bx] = offscreen(w, h, dpr); s.bx.fillStyle = '#05080d'; s.bx.fillRect(0, 0, w, h); const r = rng(5); s.r = r; s.p = []; for (let i = 0; i < 800; i++) s.p.push([r() * w, r() * h, r() * 200, r()]); }\n  const b = s.bx, sc = 3.2 / Math.min(w, h); b.fillStyle = 'rgba(5,8,13,.07)'; b.fillRect(0, 0, w, h); b.lineWidth = .8;\n  for (const p of s.p) {\n    const a = nz(p[0] * sc, p[1] * sc, t * .12) * Math.PI, sp = Math.min(w, h) / 260;\n    const nx = p[0] + Math.cos(a) * sp, ny = p[1] + Math.sin(a) * sp;\n    b.strokeStyle = `hsla(${195 + p[3] * 40},60%,${62 + p[3] * 25}%,.4)`; b.beginPath(); b.moveTo(p[0], p[1]); b.lineTo(nx, ny); b.stroke();\n    p[0] = nx; p[1] = ny; p[2]--;\n    if (p[2] < 0 || nx < 0 || nx > w || ny < 0 || ny > h) { p[0] = s.r() * w; p[1] = s.r() * h; p[2] = 80 + s.r() * 200; }\n  }\n  c.drawImage(s.bc, 0, 0, w, h);\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-B2","name":"萤火光尘","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-B2.jpg","markdown":"# B2 萤火光尘\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · B 粒子\n- 长什么样：暖色光点慢慢往上飘，有远有近。\n- 适合：温暖、回忆、找回光\n- 关键词：萤火、光尘、暖\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"萤火光尘\"的动画：暖色光点慢慢往上飘，有远有近。\n适合讲：温暖、回忆、找回光\n关键词：萤火、光尘、暖\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.B2(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.B2 = (c, w, h, t, s) => {\n  if (!s.init) { s.init = 1; const r = rng(7); s.p = []; for (let i = 0; i < 70; i++) s.p.push({ x: r() * w, y: r() * h, z: r(), ph: r() * TAU, f: .5 + r() * 1.5 }); }\n  const g = c.createRadialGradient(w * .5, h * .7, 0, w * .5, h * .7, Math.hypot(w, h) * .7); g.addColorStop(0, '#231409'); g.addColorStop(1, '#060403'); c.fillStyle = g; c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n  c.globalCompositeOperation = 'lighter'; const m = Math.min(w, h) / 300;\n  for (const p of s.p) {\n    const z = p.z, size = (1.2 + z * z * 24) * m, vy = (5 + z * 16) * m;\n    const y = ((p.y - t * vy) % (h + 60) + h + 60) % (h + 60) - 30, x = p.x + Math.sin(t * .4 * p.f + p.ph) * 18 * m;\n    const fl = .55 + .45 * Math.sin(t * p.f * 2.2 + p.ph), a = (z > .8 ? .13 : .55) * fl;\n    glowDot(c, x, y, size * (z > .8 ? 1.6 : 1), z > .5 ? '#ffb664' : '#ffd79a', a);\n    if (z < .8) { c.fillStyle = `rgba(255,236,200,${.8 * fl})`; c.beginPath(); c.arc(x, y, Math.max(.6, size * .12), 0, TAU); c.fill(); }\n  }\n  c.globalCompositeOperation = 'source-over';\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-B3","name":"粒子聚形","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-B3.jpg","markdown":"# B3 粒子聚形\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · B 粒子\n- 长什么样：粒子聚成心、房子、人，再散开。\n- 适合：讲具体的东西：家、关系、一个人\n- 关键词：聚成、形状、散开\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n> 这一条的提示词是重新写过的详细版：画面规则、颜色、时间轴都写死了，DeepSeek 和 Opus 5.5 都能直接用。{{ }} 里是要你自己填的内容。\n\n你是一位前端动效工程师。请写**一个完整的 index.html 文件**：只用原生 HTML + JavaScript + Canvas 2D，不要任何外部库、字体文件或图片。做一段\"粒子聚形\"动画：几千颗发光的小粒子聚成一个形状，停住，散开，沿弧线流向下一个形状再聚拢，循环。\n\n## 内容\n\n- 四个形状依次是：心 → 房子 → 人 → 一个汉字（{{默认：家}}）。\n- 每个形状停住时，画面下方出现一行字幕，四句依次是：{{四句，用｜分开。默认：先有一颗心｜再有一个房子｜里面住进一个人｜合起来叫家}}。\n\n## 输出要求（必须照做）\n\n1. 只输出一个 html 代码块，不要解释。代码必须完整可运行：不要用省略号，不要写\"其余同上\"\"此处略\"。\n2. canvas 固定 1920×1080；用 CSS 让它在窗口里等比缩放并居中，多出来的部分是黑边。\n3. 画面是时间 t（秒）的纯函数 `draw(t)`。不要用 `Math.random`；所有随机都用带种子的 mulberry32(2026)，并且只在启动时生成一次。不要在 draw 里累积状态（不要用\"上一帧的残影\"）。这样同一个 t 每次画出来都一模一样。\n4. 默认循环播放（requestAnimationFrame，t = 已播放的秒数）；按空格暂停/继续；网址带 `?t=3.3` 时只显示第 3.3 秒那一帧（静止）。\n\n## 常量\n\nN = 5000 颗粒子；每个形状一个周期 PER = 4.8 秒，共 4 个形状，总循环 19.2 秒；形状的缩放 SC = 1080 × 0.36；形状中心在 (960, 520)。\n\n## 形状（坐标在 [-1,1] 的方块里，y 向下）\n\n```js\nconst heart  = (x,y)=>{ y=-y*1.15+.1; x*=1.15; return Math.pow(x*x+y*y-1,3)-x*x*y*y*y<=0; };\nconst house  = (x,y)=>(Math.abs(x)<=.6&&y>=-.1&&y<=.75&&!(Math.abs(x)<.14&&y>.38))||(y<-.1&&y>-.8&&Math.abs(x)<=.8*(y+.8)/.7);\nconst person = (x,y)=>(x*x+(y+.55)*(y+.55)<=.05)||(y>=-.28&&y<=.85&&Math.abs(x)<=.25+(y+.28)*.2);\n```\n\n- 心、房子、人：在 [-1,1]² 里用带种子的随机点做\"拒绝采样\"，落在形状里的留下，凑够 N 个。\n- 汉字：用一个 400×400 的离屏 canvas 画白色粗体字（`bold 340px \"Noto Serif SC\",\"Songti SC\",\"SimSun\",serif`，水平居中，`textBaseline='middle'`，y=210）；`getImageData` 之后每隔 2 个像素取一个 alpha>128 的点，换算到 [-1,1]；用带种子的洗牌后取 N 个（不够就重复取，并加 ±0.004 的抖动）。\n- 四组目标点各自洗牌一次。第 i 颗粒子在四个形状里分别去各自的第 i 个目标点——所以看起来是\"同一批粒子在换形状\"。\n\n## 每颗粒子的固定参数（启动时生成一次）\n\n角度 `a`（0–2π）、幅度 `m`（0–1）、相位 `ph`（0–2π）、闪烁频率 `f`（0.6–1.6）、大小 `s`（0–1）、是否带光晕 `glow`（20% 为 true）。\n\n## 位置（核心，照这个算）\n\n设 k = floor(t / PER) % 4（当前形状），n = (k+1) % 4（下一个形状），q = t % PER（形状内的时间）；A = 第 k 个形状里第 i 个目标点，B = 第 n 个形状里的第 i 个目标点。\n\n- 进度 e = smoothstep(2.4, 4.2, q)。q 在 0–2.4 秒时 e=0（停住）；2.4–4.2 秒在变形；4.2–4.8 秒 e=1（落定）。\n- 控制点 C = (A+B)/2 + (cos a, sin a) × (0.35 + 0.9·m)。\n- 位置 P = (1−e)²·A + 2(1−e)e·C + e²·B（二次贝塞尔，所以粒子走弧线，先炸开再汇聚）。\n- 微动：P 再加 (cos(ph + 1.3t), sin(1.7·ph + 1.1t)) × 0.012 × (1 − sin(π·e))。\n- 屏幕坐标 = (960 + P.x × SC, 520 + P.y × SC)；整体再以画面中心 (960, 540) 为基准乘 zoom = 1 + 0.015·sin(0.33t)。\n\n## 怎么画\n\n1. **背景**：#07080C 铺满；中心一个径向渐变（半径 800）从 `rgba(40,46,66,0.30)` 到 `rgba(7,8,12,0)`；再画 220 颗固定位置的暗星点（启动时随机一次，透明度 0.15–0.4，随 t 轻微闪烁）。\n2. **粒子**：用 `globalCompositeOperation = 'lighter'` 叠加发光。颜色：心 #F08A8A，房子 #F1C070，人 #A9C6FF，汉字 #F6EBD0；变形时按 e 在\"当前形状的颜色\"和\"下一个形状的颜色\"之间线性混合。\n3. 每颗粒子的亮度 L = 0.75 + 0.25·sin(f·t + ph)；半径 r = 1.1 + 1.6·s（像素）；基础透明度 0.55·L。\n4. **拖尾**：每颗粒子画 3 次，分别在时间 t、t−0.045、t−0.09（用同一个位置函数重新算，不要保存历史）；透明度依次 ×1、×0.5、×0.25，半径依次 ×1、×0.8、×0.6。\n5. **光晕**：glow=true 的粒子再多画一个半径 ×3、透明度 0.05 的大圆。\n6. **字幕**：每个形状停住时（q 从 0.3 到 1.0 淡入，q 从 2.1 到 2.5 淡出），在 (960, 960) 居中显示该形状对应的那一句，衬线体 46px，颜色 #EDE6D6，最大透明度 0.85。\n\n## 不要\n\n爆炸的闪光、镜头光晕、彩虹色、第二种背景、实时计时器驱动的随机、在 `draw` 里保存上一帧的状态。\n\n## 写完后自己过一遍（不要输出）\n\n- 同一个 t 刷新两次，画面是否一模一样？\n- t=1.8 时看到的是不是一颗由粒子拼成的心？t=3.3 时是不是正在炸开、向房子流去？\n- 一帧画 5000×3 次粒子，会不会卡？卡的话可以把光晕只给 glow 粒子画。\n"},{"id":"S1-B4","name":"星空连线","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-B4.jpg","markdown":"# B4 星空连线\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · B 粒子\n- 长什么样：点和点连成网，中间那颗暖的是你。\n- 适合：人和人的关系、联结、孤独\n- 关键词：星座、连线、网\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"星空连线\"的动画：点和点连成网，中间那颗暖的是你。\n适合讲：人和人的关系、联结、孤独\n关键词：星座、连线、网\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.B4(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.B4 = (c, w, h, t, s) => {\n  if (!s.init) { s.init = 1; const r = rng(13); s.n = []; for (let i = 0; i < 60; i++) s.n.push([r() * w, r() * h, (r() - .5) * 12, (r() - .5) * 12]); }\n  const dt = clamp(t - (s.lt ?? t), 0, .05); s.lt = t;\n  c.fillStyle = vgrad(c, h, '#070b18', '#03040a'); c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n  const D = Math.min(w, h) * .24, me = [w * .5 + Math.sin(t * .3) * w * .08, h * .5 + Math.cos(t * .23) * h * .08];\n  for (const p of s.n) { p[0] += p[2] * dt; p[1] += p[3] * dt; if (p[0] < 0 || p[0] > w) p[2] *= -1; if (p[1] < 0 || p[1] > h) p[3] *= -1; }\n  c.lineWidth = .7;\n  for (let i = 0; i < s.n.length; i++) for (let j = i + 1; j < s.n.length; j++) {\n    const a = s.n[i], b = s.n[j], d = Math.hypot(a[0] - b[0], a[1] - b[1]);\n    if (d < D) { c.strokeStyle = `rgba(150,175,230,${(1 - d / D) * .45})`; c.beginPath(); c.moveTo(a[0], a[1]); c.lineTo(b[0], b[1]); c.stroke(); }\n  }\n  for (const p of s.n) { const d = Math.hypot(p[0] - me[0], p[1] - me[1]); if (d < D * 1.3) { c.strokeStyle = `rgba(241,178,92,${(1 - d / (D * 1.3)) * (.35 + .3 * Math.sin(t * 3 + p[0]))})`; c.beginPath(); c.moveTo(p[0], p[1]); c.lineTo(me[0], me[1]); c.stroke(); } }\n  c.fillStyle = '#dfe6ff'; for (const p of s.n) { c.beginPath(); c.arc(p[0], p[1], 1.4, 0, TAU); c.fill(); }\n  glowDot(c, me[0], me[1], 16, '#f1b25c', .7); c.fillStyle = '#fff1d8'; c.beginPath(); c.arc(me[0], me[1], 2.6, 0, TAU); c.fill();\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-B5","name":"呼吸的圆","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-B5.jpg","markdown":"# B5 呼吸的圆\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · B 粒子\n- 长什么样：一团粒子跟着呼吸胀大、缩小。\n- 适合：安抚、慢下来、冥想\n- 关键词：呼吸、胀缩、慢\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"呼吸的圆\"的动画：一团粒子跟着呼吸胀大、缩小。\n适合讲：安抚、慢下来、冥想\n关键词：呼吸、胀缩、慢\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.B5(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.B5 = (c, w, h, t, s) => {\n  if (!s.init) { s.init = 1; const r = rng(17); s.p = Array.from({ length: 750 }, () => [r() * TAU, Math.sqrt(r()), r()]); }\n  const b = .5 - .5 * Math.cos(TAU * t / 8), R = Math.min(w, h) * .33 * (.55 + .45 * b);\n  c.fillStyle = '#061213'; c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n  glowDot(c, w / 2, h / 2, R * 1.3, '#4fb7a8', .12 + .1 * b);\n  c.fillStyle = mixHex('#5fbfb0', '#dff3ec', b);\n  for (const p of s.p) { const a = p[0] + t * .05 * (p[2] - .5), rr = p[1] * R + Math.sin(t * 1.3 + p[2] * 50) * 2; c.fillRect(w / 2 + Math.cos(a) * rr, h / 2 + Math.sin(a) * rr, 1.4, 1.4); }\n  const rising = Math.sin(TAU * t / 8) > 0; c.font = `500 ${Math.min(w, h) / 20}px ${SERIF}`; c.textAlign = 'center'; c.textBaseline = 'middle'; c.fillStyle = 'rgba(220,240,235,.55)'; c.fillText(rising ? '吸气' : '呼气', w / 2, h / 2 + Math.min(w, h) * .42);\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-C1","name":"水墨晕开","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-C1.jpg","markdown":"# C1 水墨晕开\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · C 线与墨\n- 长什么样：墨在宣纸上一团一团晕开。\n- 适合：中国感、回忆、哀伤\n- 关键词：水墨、晕开、宣纸\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"水墨晕开\"的动画：墨在宣纸上一团一团晕开。\n适合讲：中国感、回忆、哀伤\n关键词：水墨、晕开、宣纸\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.C1(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.C1 = (c, w, h, t, s, dpr) => {\n  if (!s.init) {\n    s.init = 1; s.paper = paperTex(w, h, dpr, '#e9e2d2', 21); const r = rng(23);\n    s.blobs = [[.3, .45, .26, .2], [.62, .38, .2, 1.5], [.48, .66, .16, 3.0], [.78, .62, .12, 4.2]].map(([x, y, R, t0]) => ({ x, y, R, t0, k: Array.from({ length: 16 }, () => [(r() - .5) * .5, (r() - .5) * .5, .4 + r() * .6]) }));\n  }\n  const tm = t % 10; c.drawImage(s.paper, 0, 0, w, h); const m = Math.min(w, h);\n  c.globalCompositeOperation = 'multiply';\n  for (const b of s.blobs) {\n    const age = tm - b.t0; if (age <= 0) continue; const g = 1 - Math.exp(-age * .9), R = b.R * m * g;\n    for (const [dx, dy, kk] of b.k) { const gr = c.createRadialGradient(b.x * w + dx * R, b.y * h + dy * R, 0, b.x * w + dx * R, b.y * h + dy * R, R * kk); gr.addColorStop(0, 'rgba(28,28,32,.22)'); gr.addColorStop(.7, 'rgba(28,28,32,.08)'); gr.addColorStop(1, 'rgba(28,28,32,0)'); c.fillStyle = gr; c.beginPath(); c.arc(b.x * w + dx * R, b.y * h + dy * R, R * kk, 0, TAU); c.fill(); }\n  }\n  c.globalCompositeOperation = 'source-over';\n  const sz = m * .085, sx = w * .06, sy = h - sz - h * .07; c.fillStyle = '#b3352a'; c.fillRect(sx, sy, sz, sz);\n  c.fillStyle = '#f3e6d6'; c.font = `700 ${sz * .38}px ${SERIF}`; c.textAlign = 'center'; c.textBaseline = 'middle'; c.fillText('俯仰', sx + sz / 2, sy + sz / 2);\n  const f = ss(8.6, 10, tm); if (f > 0) { c.globalAlpha = f; c.drawImage(s.paper, 0, 0, w, h); c.globalAlpha = 1; }\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-C2","name":"一笔画","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-C2.jpg","markdown":"# C2 一笔画\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · C 线与墨\n- 长什么样：一根线慢慢画出山、太阳和一个小人。\n- 适合：讲一个人、一件事的轮廓\n- 关键词：一笔画、线描、生长\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"一笔画\"的动画：一根线慢慢画出山、太阳和一个小人。\n适合讲：讲一个人、一件事的轮廓\n关键词：一笔画、线描、生长\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.C2(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.C2 = (c, w, h, t, s) => {\n  if (!s.init) {\n    s.init = 1; const m = Math.min(w, h), P = (x, y) => [x * w, y * h], circ = (cx, cy, r, n) => Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, (_, i) => [cx * w + Math.cos(-Math.PI / 2 + i / n * TAU) * r * m, cy * h + Math.sin(-Math.PI / 2 + i / n * TAU) * r * m]);\n    s.strokes = [\n      [[.04, .72], [.96, .72]].map(p => P(...p)),\n      [[.06, .72], [.18, .5], [.27, .6], [.42, .36], [.55, .56], [.66, .47], [.79, .61], [.94, .52]].map(p => P(...p)),\n      circ(.76, .27, .07, 48),\n      circ(.3, .61, .02, 20),\n      [[.3, .633], [.3, .68], [.29, .72]].map(p => P(...p)), [[.3, .68], [.31, .72]].map(p => P(...p)), [[.288, .665], [.3, .645], [.312, .665]].map(p => P(...p)),\n    ];\n    s.total = 0; for (const st of s.strokes) { st.len = 0; for (let i = 1; i < st.length; i++) st.len += Math.hypot(st[i][0] - st[i - 1][0], st[i][1] - st[i - 1][1]); s.total += st.len; }\n  }\n  const tm = t % 10, p = eOut(clamp(tm / 6.5, 0, 1)) * s.total, fade = 1 - ss(8.8, 10, tm);\n  c.fillStyle = '#121722'; c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n  c.strokeStyle = `rgba(242,239,232,${fade})`; c.lineWidth = Math.max(1.4, Math.min(w, h) / 220); c.lineCap = 'round'; c.lineJoin = 'round';\n  let left = p, tip = null;\n  for (const st of s.strokes) {\n    if (left <= 0) break; c.beginPath(); c.moveTo(st[0][0], st[0][1]);\n    for (let i = 1; i < st.length; i++) { const sl = Math.hypot(st[i][0] - st[i - 1][0], st[i][1] - st[i - 1][1]); if (left >= sl) { c.lineTo(st[i][0], st[i][1]); left -= sl; tip = st[i]; } else { const k = left / sl; tip = [lerp(st[i - 1][0], st[i][0], k), lerp(st[i - 1][1], st[i][1], k)]; c.lineTo(tip[0], tip[1]); left = 0; break; } }\n    c.stroke();\n  }\n  if (tip && tm < 6.6) glowDot(c, tip[0], tip[1], 14, '#f1b25c', .8);\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-C3","name":"心里的等高线","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-C3.jpg","markdown":"# C3 心里的等高线\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · C 线与墨\n- 长什么样：像地图上的等高线，一直在起伏。\n- 适合：情绪起伏、心里的地形\n- 关键词：等高线、地形、起伏\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"心里的等高线\"的动画：像地图上的等高线，一直在起伏。\n适合讲：情绪起伏、心里的地形\n关键词：等高线、地形、起伏\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.C3(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.C3 = (c, w, h, t, s) => {\n  const cols = 60, rows = Math.max(8, Math.round(cols * h / w)), cw = w / cols, ch = h / rows;\n  const cen = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4].map(i => [.5 + .32 * Math.sin(t * .13 * (1 + i * .3) + i * 2), .5 + .3 * Math.cos(t * .11 * (1 + i * .2) + i * 1.3), .16 + .05 * i]);\n  const f = (x, y) => { let v = 0; for (const [cx, cy, r] of cen) { const dx = (x - cx) * (w / Math.min(w, h)), dy = (y - cy) * (h / Math.min(w, h)); v += Math.exp(-(dx * dx + dy * dy) / (r * r)); } return v + .08 * Math.sin(x * 9 + t * .3) * Math.cos(y * 7); };\n  const G = []; for (let j = 0; j <= rows; j++) { const row = []; for (let i = 0; i <= cols; i++) row.push(f(i / cols, j / rows)); G.push(row); }\n  c.fillStyle = '#0c120f'; c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n  for (let L = 1; L <= 11; L++) {\n    const lv = L * .13; c.strokeStyle = L === 6 ? 'rgba(244,196,120,.95)' : `rgba(214,182,128,${.18 + L * .04})`; c.lineWidth = L === 6 ? 1.6 : .8; c.beginPath();\n    for (let j = 0; j < rows; j++) for (let i = 0; i < cols; i++) {\n      const a = G[j][i], b = G[j][i + 1], d = G[j + 1][i], e = G[j + 1][i + 1];\n      const idx = (a > lv ? 8 : 0) | (b > lv ? 4 : 0) | (e > lv ? 2 : 0) | (d > lv ? 1 : 0); if (idx === 0 || idx === 15) continue;\n      const x = i * cw, y = j * ch, T = [x + cw * (lv - a) / (b - a), y], R = [x + cw, y + ch * (lv - b) / (e - b)], B = [x + cw * (lv - d) / (e - d), y + ch], Lf = [x, y + ch * (lv - a) / (d - a)];\n      const seg = { 1: [Lf, B], 2: [B, R], 3: [Lf, R], 4: [T, R], 5: [Lf, T, B, R], 6: [T, B], 7: [Lf, T], 8: [Lf, T], 9: [T, B], 10: [T, R, Lf, B], 11: [T, R], 12: [Lf, R], 13: [B, R], 14: [Lf, B] }[idx];\n      for (let k = 0; k < seg.length; k += 2) { c.moveTo(seg[k][0], seg[k][1]); c.lineTo(seg[k + 1][0], seg[k + 1][1]); }\n    }\n    c.stroke();\n  }\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-C4","name":"涟漪","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-C4.jpg","markdown":"# C4 涟漪\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · C 线与墨\n- 长什么样：一滴落下，一圈圈荡开。\n- 适合：一句话的影响、余波\n- 关键词：涟漪、水滴、扩散\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"涟漪\"的动画：一滴落下，一圈圈荡开。\n适合讲：一句话的影响、余波\n关键词：涟漪、水滴、扩散\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.C4(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.C4 = (c, w, h, t, s) => {\n  c.fillStyle = vgrad(c, h, '#082027', '#020708'); c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n  const r = rng(29), per = 1.15, k = Math.floor(t / per), m = Math.min(w, h); c.lineWidth = 1;\n  for (let i = k - 4; i <= k; i++) {\n    if (i < 0) continue; const rr = rng(i * 7 + 1), x = (.12 + rr() * .76) * w, y = (.25 + rr() * .6) * h, age = t - i * per;\n    for (let j = 0; j < 4; j++) { const a = age - j * .2; if (a <= 0) continue; const R = a * m * .17, al = Math.max(0, 1 - age / 4.4) * (1 - j * .2) * .75; c.strokeStyle = `rgba(170,225,230,${al})`; c.beginPath(); c.ellipse(x, y, R, R * .42, 0, 0, TAU); c.stroke(); }\n    if (age < .25) glowDot(c, x, y, 10, '#cdf3f5', (1 - age / .25) * .8);\n  }\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-D1","name":"雨窗","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-D1.jpg","markdown":"# D1 雨窗\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · D 光与天气\n- 长什么样：玻璃上的雨滴，后面是虚化的夜灯。\n- 适合：等待、夜里、想念\n- 关键词：雨窗、夜、虚化灯光\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"雨窗\"的动画：玻璃上的雨滴，后面是虚化的夜灯。\n适合讲：等待、夜里、想念\n关键词：雨窗、夜、虚化灯光\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.D1(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.D1 = (c, w, h, t, s, dpr) => {\n  if (!s.init) {\n    s.init = 1; const r = rng(31); [s.bg, s.bx] = offscreen(w, h, dpr); const b = s.bx; b.fillStyle = vgrad(b, h, '#0b1020', '#05060b'); b.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n    const cols = ['#ffb35c', '#ff6a4d', '#6fd1e0', '#fff1cf', '#ffd27a'];\n    for (let i = 0; i < 46; i++) glowDot(b, r() * w, h * (.25 + r() * .7), (8 + r() * 36) * Math.min(w, h) / 300, cols[Math.floor(r() * cols.length)], .28 + r() * .25);\n    s.dots = Array.from({ length: Math.round(w * h / 500) }, () => [r() * w, r() * h, .6 + r() * 2.2]);\n    s.sl = Array.from({ length: 9 }, () => ({ x: r() * w, y: r() * h, v: 0, r: 2 + r() * 2.2, ph: r() * 10, tr: [] }));\n  }\n  const dt = clamp(t - (s.lt ?? t), 0, .05); s.lt = t;\n  c.drawImage(s.bg, 0, 0, w, h);\n  for (const [x, y, r] of s.dots) { c.fillStyle = 'rgba(190,210,235,.12)'; c.beginPath(); c.arc(x, y, r, 0, TAU); c.fill(); c.fillStyle = 'rgba(255,255,255,.35)'; c.fillRect(x - r * .3, y - r * .4, Math.max(.6, r * .35), Math.max(.6, r * .35)); }\n  for (const d of s.sl) {\n    const go = Math.sin(t * 1.3 + d.ph) > .2; d.v = lerp(d.v, go ? 60 * h / 300 : 2, .1); d.y += d.v * dt; d.x += Math.sin(t * 3 + d.ph) * .15;\n    d.tr.push([d.x, d.y]); if (d.tr.length > 60) d.tr.shift();\n    if (d.y > h + 10) { d.y = -10; d.x = Math.random() * w; d.tr = []; }\n    c.strokeStyle = 'rgba(200,220,240,.12)'; c.lineWidth = d.r * .6; c.beginPath(); d.tr.forEach((p, i) => i ? c.lineTo(p[0], p[1]) : c.moveTo(p[0], p[1])); c.stroke();\n    const g = c.createLinearGradient(d.x, d.y - d.r, d.x, d.y + d.r); g.addColorStop(0, 'rgba(255,255,255,.5)'); g.addColorStop(1, 'rgba(120,140,170,.2)'); c.fillStyle = g; c.beginPath(); c.ellipse(d.x, d.y, d.r, d.r * 1.25, 0, 0, TAU); c.fill();\n  }\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-D2","name":"窗光与尘","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-D2.jpg","markdown":"# D2 窗光与尘\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · D 光与天气\n- 长什么样：一束光从窗照进来，灰尘在光里飘。\n- 适合：安静、时间、老房子\n- 关键词：窗光、灰尘、光束\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"窗光与尘\"的动画：一束光从窗照进来，灰尘在光里飘。\n适合讲：安静、时间、老房子\n关键词：窗光、灰尘、光束\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.D2(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.D2 = (c, w, h, t, s) => {\n  if (!s.init) { s.init = 1; const r = rng(37); s.p = Array.from({ length: 170 }, () => [r() * w, r() * h, r() * TAU, .4 + r() * 1.4]); }\n  c.fillStyle = '#0b0806'; c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n  const wx = w * .08, wy = h * .08, ww = w * .2, wh = h * .36;\n  const beam = new Path2D(); beam.moveTo(wx + ww, wy); beam.lineTo(wx, wy + wh); beam.lineTo(w * .52, h * 1.02); beam.lineTo(w * 1.02, h * .66); beam.closePath();\n  const g = c.createLinearGradient(wx, wy, w * .8, h); g.addColorStop(0, 'rgba(255,222,165,.34)'); g.addColorStop(1, 'rgba(255,200,140,.03)');\n  c.globalCompositeOperation = 'lighter'; c.fillStyle = g; c.fill(beam);\n  c.fillStyle = 'rgba(255,214,160,.12)'; c.beginPath(); c.moveTo(w * .52, h); c.lineTo(w * .7, h * .86); c.lineTo(w, h * .7); c.lineTo(w, h); c.fill();\n  c.globalCompositeOperation = 'source-over';\n  c.fillStyle = '#f4e2c2'; c.fillRect(wx, wy, ww, wh); c.fillStyle = '#0b0806'; c.fillRect(wx + ww / 2 - 2, wy, 4, wh); c.fillRect(wx, wy + wh / 2 - 2, ww, 4);\n  for (const p of s.p) {\n    const x = (p[0] + Math.sin(t * .2 + p[2]) * 20 + t * 4) % w, y = (p[1] + Math.cos(t * .17 + p[2]) * 16 - t * 2 + h * 10) % h;\n    const lit = c.isPointInPath(beam, x * (c.getTransform().a), y * (c.getTransform().d));\n    c.fillStyle = lit ? `rgba(255,236,200,${.5 + .4 * Math.sin(t * 2 + p[2])})` : 'rgba(255,220,170,.05)'; c.beginPath(); c.arc(x, y, p[3] * (lit ? 1 : .7), 0, TAU); c.fill();\n  }\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-D3","name":"雪与火星","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-D3.jpg","markdown":"# D3 雪与火星\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · D 光与天气\n- 长什么样：雪往下落，几点火星往上飘。\n- 适合：告别、结束、冷里的一点热\n- 关键词：雪、火星、往上飘\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"雪与火星\"的动画：雪往下落，几点火星往上飘。\n适合讲：告别、结束、冷里的一点热\n关键词：雪、火星、往上飘\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.D3(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.D3 = (c, w, h, t, s) => {\n  if (!s.init) { s.init = 1; const r = rng(41); s.f = Array.from({ length: 170 }, () => [r() * w, r() * h, r(), r() * TAU]); s.e = Array.from({ length: 14 }, () => [r() * w, r() * h, r(), r() * TAU]); }\n  c.fillStyle = vgrad(c, h, '#0e1627', '#04060b'); c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h); const m = Math.min(w, h) / 300;\n  for (const [x0, y0, z, ph] of s.f) { const y = (y0 + t * (8 + z * 26) * m) % (h + 10), x = x0 + Math.sin(t * .6 + ph) * 10 * m; c.fillStyle = `rgba(235,242,255,${.35 + z * .55})`; c.beginPath(); c.arc(x, y, (.6 + z * 2.4) * m, 0, TAU); c.fill(); }\n  c.globalCompositeOperation = 'lighter';\n  for (const [x0, y0, z, ph] of s.e) { const y = h - ((h - y0 + t * (20 + z * 30) * m) % (h + 20)), x = x0 + Math.sin(t * 1.4 + ph) * 14 * m, fl = .5 + .5 * Math.sin(t * 9 + ph * 5); glowDot(c, x, y, 7 * m, '#ff9a3d', .45 * fl * (y / h)); c.fillStyle = `rgba(255,214,150,${fl * (y / h)})`; c.fillRect(x - .8, y - .8, 1.6, 1.6); }\n  c.globalCompositeOperation = 'source-over';\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-D4","name":"天亮","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-D4.jpg","markdown":"# D4 天亮\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · D 光与天气\n- 长什么样：天从深蓝慢慢变暖，太阳升起来。\n- 适合：结尾、希望、重新开始\n- 关键词：日出、渐变、天亮\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"天亮\"的动画：天从深蓝慢慢变暖，太阳升起来。\n适合讲：结尾、希望、重新开始\n关键词：日出、渐变、天亮\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.D4(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.D4 = (c, w, h, t, s) => {\n  if (!s.init) { s.init = 1; const r = rng(43); s.st = Array.from({ length: 90 }, () => [r() * w, r() * h * .6, r()]); }\n  const K = [['#04060f', '#0d1430'], ['#0c1233', '#3b2e58'], ['#26305e', '#d0766a'], ['#4f79b5', '#f6c27f']];\n  const p = (t % 12) / 12, seg = p < .75 ? p / .75 * 3 : 3, i = Math.min(2, Math.floor(seg)), f = ss(0, 1, seg - i), back = ss(.9, 1, p);\n  const top = mixHex(mixHex(K[i][0], K[i + 1][0], f), K[0][0], back), bot = mixHex(mixHex(K[i][1], K[i + 1][1], f), K[0][1], back);\n  c.fillStyle = vgrad(c, h, top, bot); c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n  const dawn = clamp(seg / 3, 0, 1) * (1 - back);\n  for (const [x, y, z] of s.st) { c.fillStyle = `rgba(255,255,255,${(1 - dawn * 1.4) * (.3 + z * .6)})`; c.fillRect(x, y, 1.2, 1.2); }\n  const m = Math.min(w, h), sy = lerp(h * .86, h * .5, eOut(dawn)); glowDot(c, w * .62, sy, m * .5, '#ffb46a', .35 * dawn); c.fillStyle = mixHex('#ff8a4a', '#fff1c8', dawn); c.beginPath(); c.arc(w * .62, sy, m * .07, 0, TAU); c.fill();\n  const hill = (base, amp, col, ph) => { c.fillStyle = col; c.beginPath(); c.moveTo(0, h); for (let x = 0; x <= w; x += 6) c.lineTo(x, base + Math.sin(x / w * 5 + ph) * amp + Math.sin(x / w * 13 + ph) * amp * .3); c.lineTo(w, h); c.fill(); };\n  hill(h * .74, h * .04, mixHex('#0a0f20', '#3a3550', dawn * .6), 1); hill(h * .82, h * .03, mixHex('#05070e', '#1d1a28', dawn * .5), 3);\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-E1","name":"极简几何","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-E1.jpg","markdown":"# E1 极简几何\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · E 图形与纸\n- 长什么样：圆、方、半圆在纯色上滚、落、长出来。\n- 适合：把复杂的事讲清楚\n- 关键词：几何、扁平、色块\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"极简几何\"的动画：圆、方、半圆在纯色上滚、落、长出来。\n适合讲：把复杂的事讲清楚\n关键词：几何、扁平、色块\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.E1(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.E1 = (c, w, h, t, s) => {\n  const tm = t % 6.5, m = Math.min(w, h), ly = h * .68, out = eOut(ss(4.6, 5.6, tm)) * w * 1.2;\n  c.fillStyle = '#ebe5d6'; c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n  c.save(); c.translate(-out * 0, 0);\n  c.fillStyle = '#161616'; c.fillRect(w * .06, ly, w * .88 * eOut(tm / .8), Math.max(2, m * .012));\n  const cr = m * .14, cx = lerp(-cr, w * .46, eOut((tm - .4) / 1.3)) + out; c.save(); c.translate(cx, ly - cr); c.rotate((cx / cr)); c.fillStyle = '#d9432f'; c.beginPath(); c.arc(0, 0, cr, 0, TAU); c.fill(); c.fillStyle = '#ebe5d6'; c.fillRect(-cr * .08, -cr, cr * .16, cr * .5); c.restore();\n  const sq = m * .2, sqx = w * .72 + out, sqy = lerp(-sq, ly - sq / 2, eBack((tm - 1.1) / .9)), rot = eBack((tm - 3) / .7) * Math.PI / 2;\n  c.save(); c.translate(sqx, sqy); c.rotate(rot); c.fillStyle = '#2047c9'; c.fillRect(-sq / 2, -sq / 2, sq, sq); c.restore();\n  const yr = m * .18 * eBack((tm - 2) / .8); if (yr > 0) { c.fillStyle = '#f2b630'; c.beginPath(); c.arc(w * .24 + out, ly, yr, Math.PI, TAU); c.fill(); }\n  c.restore();\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-E2","name":"剪纸层叠","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-E2.jpg","markdown":"# E2 剪纸层叠\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · E 图形与纸\n- 长什么样：一层层纸片有阴影，前快后慢地移动。\n- 适合：童年、故事、温柔的段落\n- 关键词：剪纸、纸片、层叠\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"剪纸层叠\"的动画：一层层纸片有阴影，前快后慢地移动。\n适合讲：童年、故事、温柔的段落\n关键词：剪纸、纸片、层叠\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.E2(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.E2 = (c, w, h, t, s, dpr) => {\n  if (!s.init) { s.init = 1; s.paper = paperTex(w, h, dpr, '#f1e9dc', 47); }\n  c.fillStyle = vgrad(c, h, '#f4ecdf', '#dcebe5'); c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n  const m = Math.min(w, h); c.save(); c.shadowColor = 'rgba(80,60,30,.25)'; c.shadowBlur = 14; c.fillStyle = '#fff8e7'; c.beginPath(); c.arc(w * .72, h * .26, m * .1, 0, TAU); c.fill(); c.restore();\n  const cols = ['#cfe3df', '#9fc7c1', '#6fa59f', '#43807b', '#235a57'];\n  cols.forEach((col, i) => {\n    const base = h * (.42 + i * .11), amp = h * (.05 + i * .012), off = t * (6 + i * 10) * m / 300;\n    c.save(); c.shadowColor = 'rgba(20,40,40,.35)'; c.shadowBlur = 10; c.shadowOffsetY = -3; c.fillStyle = col; c.beginPath(); c.moveTo(0, h);\n    for (let x = 0; x <= w + 6; x += 6) { const u = (x + off) / w; c.lineTo(x, base + Math.sin(u * (3 + i) + i * 1.7) * amp + Math.sin(u * 11 + i) * amp * .25); }\n    c.lineTo(w, h); c.closePath(); c.fill(); c.restore();\n    if (i === 2) { const hx = ((w * .3 - off) % (w * 1.3) + w * 1.3) % (w * 1.3) - w * .15, u = (hx + off) / w, hy = base + Math.sin(u * 5 + i * 1.7) * amp + Math.sin(u * 11 + i) * amp * .25, hs = m * .07; c.fillStyle = '#2f6f69'; c.fillRect(hx - hs / 2, hy - hs * .9, hs, hs); c.beginPath(); c.moveTo(hx - hs * .65, hy - hs * .9); c.lineTo(hx, hy - hs * 1.5); c.lineTo(hx + hs * .65, hy - hs * .9); c.fill(); c.fillStyle = '#ffd98a'; c.fillRect(hx - hs * .12, hy - hs * .55, hs * .24, hs * .3); }\n  });\n  c.globalAlpha = .5; c.globalCompositeOperation = 'multiply'; c.drawImage(s.paper, 0, 0, w, h); c.globalCompositeOperation = 'source-over'; c.globalAlpha = 1;\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-E3","name":"字符画","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-E3.jpg","markdown":"# E3 字符画\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · E 图形与纸\n- 长什么样：画面全是字符拼成的，像老电脑屏幕。\n- 适合：科技、冷、记忆碎片\n- 关键词：字符画、终端、像素\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"字符画\"的动画：画面全是字符拼成的，像老电脑屏幕。\n适合讲：科技、冷、记忆碎片\n关键词：字符画、终端、像素\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.E3(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.E3 = (c, w, h, t, s) => {\n  const cs = Math.max(7, Math.round(Math.min(w, h) / 26)), cols = Math.floor(w / (cs * .62)), rows = Math.floor(h / cs), ch = ' .,:;-=+*#%@';\n  c.fillStyle = '#0a0703'; c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h); c.font = `500 ${cs}px ${MONO}`; c.textBaseline = 'top';\n  const lx = .5 + .3 * Math.cos(t * .7), ly = .5 + .3 * Math.sin(t * .9);\n  for (let j = 0; j < rows; j++) for (let i = 0; i < cols; i++) {\n    const x = i / cols, y = j / rows, ax = (x - .5) * w / h, d = Math.hypot(ax, y - .5);\n    let v = .5 + .5 * Math.sin(d * 22 - t * 2.4); v *= Math.exp(-d * 1.6);\n    v += .9 * Math.exp(-(((x - lx) * w / h) ** 2 + (y - ly) ** 2) / .02);\n    const k = Math.min(ch.length - 1, Math.floor(clamp(v, 0, .999) * ch.length)); if (!k) continue;\n    c.fillStyle = `rgba(255,179,71,${.35 + v * .65})`; c.fillText(ch[k], i * cs * .62, j * cs);\n  }\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-F1","name":"图表生长","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-F1.jpg","markdown":"# F1 图表生长\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · F 讲解\n- 长什么样：柱子一根根长出来，数字跳动，再连成线。\n- 适合：讲数据、研究结论\n- 关键词：图表、数字、生长\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"图表生长\"的动画：柱子一根根长出来，数字跳动，再连成线。\n适合讲：讲数据、研究结论\n关键词：图表、数字、生长\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.F1(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.F1 = (c, w, h, t, s) => {\n  const tm = t % 8.5, V = [12, 19, 27, 33, 41, 58], N = ['小学', '初中', '高中', '大学', '工作', '现在'], fade = 1 - ss(7.6, 8.5, tm);\n  c.fillStyle = '#0f1418'; c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h); c.globalAlpha = fade;\n  const l = w * .1, r = w * .94, top = h * .2, bot = h * .8, fs = Math.max(9, Math.min(w, h) / 24);\n  c.fillStyle = '#8f9aa5'; c.font = `500 ${fs}px ${SANS}`; c.textBaseline = 'alphabetic'; c.textAlign = 'left'; c.fillText('示例数据：心里那句话想说出口的次数', l, h * .11);\n  c.strokeStyle = '#2c3640'; c.lineWidth = 1; for (let k = 0; k <= 3; k++) { const y = lerp(bot, top, k / 3); c.beginPath(); c.moveTo(l, y); c.lineTo(r, y); c.stroke(); }\n  const bw = (r - l) / V.length, pts = [];\n  V.forEach((v, i) => {\n    const e = eOut((tm - .2 - i * .25) / .8), bh = (bot - top) * v / 60 * e, x = l + bw * i + bw * .22, ww = bw * .56;\n    c.fillStyle = i === V.length - 1 ? '#f1b25c' : '#5d7fa3'; c.fillRect(x, bot - bh, ww, bh);\n    c.fillStyle = '#e8ecef'; c.textAlign = 'center'; c.font = `500 ${fs}px ${MONO}`; if (e > 0) c.fillText(Math.round(v * e), x + ww / 2, bot - bh - fs * .5);\n    c.fillStyle = '#8f9aa5'; c.font = `400 ${fs * .9}px ${SANS}`; c.fillText(N[i], x + ww / 2, bot + fs * 1.4);\n    pts.push([x + ww / 2, bot - (bot - top) * v / 60]);\n  });\n  const lp = clamp((tm - 2.6) / 1.4, 0, 1); if (lp > 0) { c.strokeStyle = '#f1b25c'; c.lineWidth = 2; c.beginPath(); const n = lp * (pts.length - 1); c.moveTo(pts[0][0], pts[0][1]); for (let i = 1; i <= Math.floor(n); i++) c.lineTo(pts[i][0], pts[i][1]); const fi = Math.floor(n); if (fi < pts.length - 1) c.lineTo(lerp(pts[fi][0], pts[fi + 1][0], n - fi), lerp(pts[fi][1], pts[fi + 1][1], n - fi)); c.stroke(); }\n  c.globalAlpha = 1;\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-F2","name":"关系图","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-F2.jpg","markdown":"# F2 关系图\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · F 讲解\n- 长什么样：人和人之间的箭头一条条画出来，箭头上写着那句话。\n- 适合：家里的关系、因果\n- 关键词：节点、箭头、关系图\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"关系图\"的动画：人和人之间的箭头一条条画出来，箭头上写着那句话。\n适合讲：家里的关系、因果\n关键词：节点、箭头、关系图\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.F2(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.F2 = (c, w, h, t, s) => {\n  const tm = t % 10, fade = 1 - ss(9, 10, tm), m = Math.min(w, h), fs = Math.max(10, m / 20);\n  const Nd = { 我: [.5, .55], 爸爸: [.22, .3], 妈妈: [.78, .3], 奶奶: [.5, .12], 学校: [.2, .82], 工作: [.8, .82] };\n  const E = [['爸爸', '我', '为你好', 1], ['妈妈', '我', '听话', 1.8], ['奶奶', '爸爸', '吃苦', 2.6], ['我', '学校', '要争气', 3.4], ['我', '工作', '不能软弱', 4.2], ['我', '妈妈', '说不出口', 5.2]];\n  c.fillStyle = '#0e1116'; c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h); c.globalAlpha = fade;\n  const R = m * .075, at = k => [Nd[k][0] * w, Nd[k][1] * h];\n  for (const [a, b, lab, t0] of E) {\n    const p = eOut((tm - t0) / .7); if (p <= 0) continue; const A = at(a), B = at(b), an = Math.atan2(B[1] - A[1], B[0] - A[0]);\n    const sx = A[0] + Math.cos(an) * R, sy = A[1] + Math.sin(an) * R, ex = B[0] - Math.cos(an) * R, ey = B[1] - Math.sin(an) * R, cx = lerp(sx, ex, p), cy = lerp(sy, ey, p);\n    const warm = lab === '说不出口'; c.strokeStyle = warm ? '#f1b25c' : '#7c8798'; c.lineWidth = 1.4; if (warm) c.setLineDash([5, 5]); c.beginPath(); c.moveTo(sx, sy); c.lineTo(cx, cy); c.stroke(); c.setLineDash([]);\n    c.fillStyle = c.strokeStyle; c.beginPath(); c.moveTo(cx, cy); c.lineTo(cx - Math.cos(an - .4) * 9, cy - Math.sin(an - .4) * 9); c.lineTo(cx - Math.cos(an + .4) * 9, cy - Math.sin(an + .4) * 9); c.fill();\n    c.font = `400 ${fs * .8}px ${SANS}`; c.textAlign = 'center'; c.textBaseline = 'middle'; c.fillStyle = warm ? '#f1b25c' : '#aab3c0'; c.globalAlpha = fade * p; c.fillText(lab, (sx + ex) / 2 - Math.sin(an) * fs * .8, (sy + ey) / 2 + Math.cos(an) * fs * .8); c.globalAlpha = fade;\n  }\n  Object.keys(Nd).forEach((k, i) => {\n    const p = eBack((tm - i * .12) / .5); if (p <= 0) return; const [x, y] = at(k); const me = k === '我';\n    c.fillStyle = me ? '#f1b25c' : '#161b23'; c.strokeStyle = me ? '#f1b25c' : '#cfd6e0'; c.lineWidth = 1.2; c.beginPath(); c.arc(x, y, R * p, 0, TAU); c.fill(); c.stroke();\n    c.fillStyle = me ? '#14100a' : '#e6e9ee'; c.font = `500 ${fs * .85 * p}px ${SANS}`; c.textAlign = 'center'; c.textBaseline = 'middle'; c.fillText(k, x, y);\n  });\n  c.globalAlpha = 1;\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-F3","name":"分屏对比","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-F3.jpg","markdown":"# F3 分屏对比\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · F 讲解\n- 长什么样：左边以前，右边现在，中间一条线。\n- 适合：以前和现在、父母和孩子\n- 关键词：分屏、对比、左右\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"分屏对比\"的动画：左边以前，右边现在，中间一条线。\n适合讲：以前和现在、父母和孩子\n关键词：分屏、对比、左右\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.F3(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.F3 = (c, w, h, t, s) => {\n  if (!s.init) { s.init = 1; const r = rng(53); s.rain = Array.from({ length: 90 }, () => [r(), r(), .5 + r()]); s.lit = Array.from({ length: 40 }, () => [r(), r(), r() * TAU, r()]); }\n  const dx = w * .5 + Math.sin(t * .6) * w * .04, m = Math.min(w, h), fs = Math.max(11, m / 16);\n  c.save(); c.beginPath(); c.rect(0, 0, dx, h); c.clip(); c.fillStyle = vgrad(c, h, '#1a1e26', '#0b0d11'); c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n  c.strokeStyle = 'rgba(170,185,205,.35)'; c.lineWidth = 1; for (const [x, y, v] of s.rain) { const yy = ((y * h + t * 220 * v) % (h + 20)) - 10, xx = x * w; c.beginPath(); c.moveTo(xx, yy); c.lineTo(xx - 2, yy + 12 * v); c.stroke(); }\n  c.fillStyle = 'rgba(210,218,230,.85)'; c.font = `700 ${fs}px ${SERIF}`; c.textAlign = 'left'; c.textBaseline = 'top'; c.fillText('以前', m * .06, m * .06); c.restore();\n  c.save(); c.beginPath(); c.rect(dx, 0, w - dx, h); c.clip(); const g = c.createRadialGradient(w * .75, h * .6, 0, w * .75, h * .6, w * .6); g.addColorStop(0, '#3a2410'); g.addColorStop(1, '#120b05'); c.fillStyle = g; c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n  c.globalCompositeOperation = 'lighter'; for (const [x, y, ph, z] of s.lit) { const yy = ((y * h - t * (8 + z * 14)) % h + h) % h; glowDot(c, x * w, yy, 3 + z * 9, '#ffb664', .4 + .3 * Math.sin(t * 2 + ph)); } c.globalCompositeOperation = 'source-over';\n  c.fillStyle = 'rgba(255,226,180,.92)'; c.font = `700 ${fs}px ${SERIF}`; c.textAlign = 'right'; c.textBaseline = 'top'; c.fillText('现在', w - m * .06, m * .06); c.restore();\n  c.fillStyle = 'rgba(255,255,255,.7)'; c.fillRect(dx - .5, 0, 1, h);\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-G1","name":"老胶片","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-G1.jpg","markdown":"# G1 老胶片\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · G 质感\n- 长什么样：颗粒、闪烁、划痕、边上的漏光。\n- 适合：回忆、旧时光、整片的统一质感\n- 关键词：胶片、颗粒、漏光\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"老胶片\"的动画：颗粒、闪烁、划痕、边上的漏光。\n适合讲：回忆、旧时光、整片的统一质感\n关键词：胶片、颗粒、漏光\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.G1(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.G1 = (c, w, h, t, s, dpr) => {\n  if (!s.init) { s.init = 1; const r = rng(59); [s.nz, s.nx] = offscreen(256, 256, 1); const id = s.nx.createImageData(256, 256); for (let i = 0; i < id.data.length; i += 4) { const v = r() * 255; id.data[i] = id.data[i + 1] = id.data[i + 2] = v; id.data[i + 3] = 255; } s.nx.putImageData(id, 0, 0); }\n  const jy = (Math.random() - .5) * 1.4; c.save(); c.translate(0, jy);\n  c.fillStyle = vgrad(c, h, '#5b3a63', '#f0a36a'); c.fillRect(0, -2, w, h + 4);\n  const m = Math.min(w, h); glowDot(c, w * .68, h * .58, m * .4, '#ffd08a', .5); c.fillStyle = '#ffe3b0'; c.beginPath(); c.arc(w * .68, h * .58, m * .08, 0, TAU); c.fill();\n  c.fillStyle = '#2a1626'; c.beginPath(); c.moveTo(0, h); for (let x = 0; x <= w; x += 8) c.lineTo(x, h * .72 - Math.sin(x / w * Math.PI) * h * .12); c.lineTo(w, h); c.fill();\n  const tx = w * .36, ty = h * .72 - Math.sin(.36 * Math.PI) * h * .12; c.fillRect(tx - 2, ty - m * .22, 4, m * .22); c.beginPath(); c.arc(tx, ty - m * .24, m * .08, 0, TAU); c.arc(tx - m * .06, ty - m * .19, m * .06, 0, TAU); c.arc(tx + m * .06, ty - m * .19, m * .06, 0, TAU); c.fill();\n  const px = w * .47, py = ty + h * .01; c.beginPath(); c.arc(px, py - m * .085, m * .016, 0, TAU); c.fill(); c.fillRect(px - m * .012, py - m * .07, m * .024, m * .07);\n  c.restore();\n  c.globalAlpha = .22; c.globalCompositeOperation = 'overlay'; const ox = Math.random() * 256, oy = Math.random() * 256; c.fillStyle = c.createPattern(s.nz, 'repeat'); c.save(); c.translate(-ox, -oy); c.fillRect(ox, oy, w, h); c.restore();\n  c.globalCompositeOperation = 'source-over'; c.globalAlpha = 1;\n  c.fillStyle = `rgba(0,0,0,${Math.random() * .08})`; c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n  const lx = w * (.1 + .08 * Math.sin(t * .5)); glowDot(c, lx, h * .3, m * .7, '#ff7a2a', .22 + .1 * Math.sin(t * 1.3));\n  if (Math.random() < .3) { c.fillStyle = 'rgba(255,240,220,.25)'; c.fillRect(Math.random() * w, 0, 1, h); }\n  const v = c.createRadialGradient(w / 2, h / 2, m * .3, w / 2, h / 2, Math.hypot(w, h) * .6); v.addColorStop(0, 'rgba(0,0,0,0)'); v.addColorStop(1, 'rgba(0,0,0,.6)'); c.fillStyle = v; c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S1-G2","name":"信号故障","series":"S1","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S1-G2.jpg","markdown":"# G2 信号故障\n\n- 分类：A–G 代码样片 · G 质感\n- 长什么样：画面错位、颜色分离，突然闪一下。\n- 适合：崩溃、混乱、话被打断\n- 关键词：故障、错位、干扰\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段\"信号故障\"的动画：画面错位、颜色分离，突然闪一下。\n适合讲：崩溃、混乱、话被打断\n关键词：故障、错位、干扰\n\n下面是我们自己写的参考实现：一个绘制函数 S.G2(c, w, h, t, s)，c 是 canvas 的 2D context，w/h 是宽高，t 是秒，s 是给这个函数存状态用的空对象。公共的小工具函数在同一文件夹的 _公共函数.js 里。\n\n```js\nS.G2 = (c, w, h, t, s) => {\n  const tm = t % 5, burst = (tm > 1.2 && tm < 1.7) || (tm > 3.1 && tm < 3.3) || (tm > 3.6 && tm < 4.2), m = Math.min(w, h), fs = Math.min(w / 4.6, h / 3.2);\n  c.fillStyle = '#060607'; c.fillRect(0, 0, w, h); const dx = burst ? 4 + Math.random() * 10 : 1.2;\n  c.font = `900 ${fs}px ${SANS}`; c.textAlign = 'center'; c.textBaseline = 'middle'; c.globalCompositeOperation = 'lighter';\n  c.fillStyle = '#ff2b2b'; c.fillText('我没事。', w / 2 - dx, h / 2); c.fillStyle = '#00e1ff'; c.fillText('我没事。', w / 2 + dx, h / 2);\n  c.globalCompositeOperation = 'source-over';\n  if (burst) { for (let k = 0; k < 7; k++) { const y = Math.random() * h, sh = 4 + Math.random() * h * .08, off = (Math.random() - .5) * w * .12; c.drawImage(c.canvas, 0, y * (c.canvas.height / h), c.canvas.width, sh * (c.canvas.height / h), off, y, w, sh); } c.fillStyle = 'rgba(230,230,235,.8)'; c.font = `400 ${fs * .22}px ${SANS}`; c.fillText('（其实不是）', w / 2 + (Math.random() - .5) * 8, h / 2 + fs * .78); }\n  c.fillStyle = 'rgba(0,0,0,.22)'; for (let y = 0; y < h; y += 3) c.fillRect(0, y, w, 1);\n};\n```\n"},{"id":"S10-Z1","name":"水墨","series":"S10","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S10-Z1.jpg","markdown":"# Z1 水墨\n\n- 分类：Z 中国风 · 水墨\n- 长什么样：生宣白底上大团墨色自然晕开，边缘毛糙、中心焦黑向外渐淡，画面一半以上是空的，只有两三段竹枝和一只飞鸟；全程无彩色，靠墨的五级浓淡分层\n- 适合：文化类片头、诗词朗诵背景、茶与香品牌、纪录片章节卡\n- 关键词：水墨、晕染、留白、竹枝、墨分五色\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「水墨」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #f3ede1（生宣白），四角压 6% 的 #d8cdb8 暗角，模拟纸张受潮\n- 墨只用一色 #1b1b1b，靠 alpha 分五级：1.0 / 0.75 / 0.5 / 0.3 / 0.12\n- 竹枝是 3-5 段折线，节处留一道缺口；竹叶两笔成「个」字，起笔重、收笔尖\n- 留白必须占 55% 以上，右下 1/4 不许进任何内容——那块只放落款和朱印\n- 墨团边缘不许是圆的：用 60-90 个半径抖动的圆随机叠出毛边\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-2s：第一团大墨从中偏左滴落，半径 0→120 扩散，外圈淡墨同时向外爬\n- 2-4.5s：竹枝自上而下生长（每节 0.35s），叶随枝出，叶尖带 0.1s 回弹\n- 4.5-6s：右上一只飞鸟掠过，两段折线翅膀在 0.6s 内振 3 次\n- 6-7s：右下角落款「水墨」+ 朱印 #a8352b，全画面静止\n\n技术要点：\n- 墨团用三层 `radialGradient` 叠加（0 处 alpha 0.9、0.6 处 0.45、1 处 0），每层随机偏移 8px 打破正圆感\n- 毛边靠 `ctx.filter = 'blur(6px)'` 画完再错位叠 3 遍，最后一遍不 blur\n- 竹叶用 `quadraticCurveTo`，控制点偏出轴线 30%，才有「撇」的力度\n"},{"id":"S10-Z2","name":"工笔","series":"S10","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S10-Z2.jpg","markdown":"# Z2 工笔\n\n- 分类：Z 中国风 · 工笔重彩\n- 长什么样：极细墨线勾轮廓（1px 上下），填色用淡彩层层罩染，花瓣有渐变过渡但没有水墨那种晕开，背景多为绢本米黄或留白\n- 适合：传统文化题材、器物展示、品牌高级感、节气海报\n- 关键词：工笔、细线、罩染、淡彩、绢本\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「工笔重彩」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，8 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #f4ecd8（绢本米黄），可叠极淡的横竖经纬纹理（opacity 0.03）\n- 主体：一枝花卉或一只禽鸟，轮廓用 #3a3226 的 0.8-1px 细线逐笔描出\n- 填色分层罩染：花瓣先铺 #f0b8b0 底色，再叠 #e88f8a 中层，最后 #d96f6a 尖部，每层 alpha 0.5\n- 严禁使用大范围模糊、晕染、飞白——工笔的质感来自「线准 + 色匀」\n- 叶脉、花瓣脉络必须单独画细线，这是工笔的辨识点\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-2.5s：轮廓线用 stroke-dashoffset 一笔一笔描出（每笔 0.6s）\n- 2.5-5s：三层颜色依次从底部 alpha 0→0.5 罩染上来\n- 5-6.5s：叶脉/脉络细线显现（opacity 0→1）\n- 6.5-8s：右下角落款「工笔」二字 + 朱红小印，静止收尾\n\n技术要点：\n- 细线用 `ctx.lineWidth = 0.9`，记得 setTransform 缩放后还原\n- 罩染用 `globalCompositeOperation = 'multiply'` 让淡彩自然融合\n- 落款字体用楷体，字号小而稳，不要放大\n"},{"id":"S10-Z3","name":"写意","series":"S10","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S10-Z3.jpg","markdown":"# Z3 写意\n\n- 分类：Z 中国风 · 写意\n- 长什么样：整幅只有两三笔，一笔横扫下来宽度从 70px 收到 6px，笔肚饱满、笔锋分叉成飞白；大块浓墨压在纸上，不勾细节，靠笔势的方向感和干湿对比说话\n- 适合：品牌态度片、武侠与国潮片头、武术语录卡、运动主题短片\n- 关键词：写意、大笔触、飞白、干笔、少细节\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「写意」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #ece2cd（粗生宣，比水墨更黄更糙），纸纹竖向细条 opacity 0.05\n- 墨 #141414，另备一支干笔色 #4a3f33 做枯笔过渡，只此两色\n- 全画面最多 3 笔：一笔横贯的荷叶／山石，一笔竖立的茎，一点飞白碎笔\n- 笔触沿路径变宽：起点 `lineWidth 70`，收笔收到 6，中途按 `sin` 起伏模拟笔肚压力\n- 飞白不是白线，是笔触内部的干裂——用 `destination-out` 抽走 20-40 条随机细条\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.8s：主笔从左下向右上扫出，笔尖沿二次贝塞尔走，宽度同步收窄\n- 1.8-3.2s：干笔在收笔处回拖 12%，留下分叉的笔锋丝\n- 3.2-4.5s：竖茎一笔落下，落点有一小团积墨（半径 10 的实心圆）\n- 4.5-6s：碎笔点睛（3 个短撇），右下角小字落款，静止\n\n技术要点：\n- 变宽笔触用「沿路径采样多点、每点画一个半径渐变的圆」实现，步长 3px 才不断裂\n- 飞白用 `ctx.globalCompositeOperation = 'destination-out'` 画 1-2px 的斜向长条，alpha 0.6-0.9\n- 起笔要「顿」：前 8% 路径上额外叠 2 个偏移圆，才不像马克笔\n"},{"id":"S10-Z4","name":"山水","series":"S10","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S10-Z4.jpg","markdown":"# Z4 山水\n\n- 分类：Z 中国风 · 山水\n- 长什么样：五层远近山脊由淡到浓叠出纵深，山形是平缓的折线不是尖峰，山脚被横向云雾切断；水面留大片空白，只有一叶小舟和一枚细小人影，整体偏青灰\n- 适合：旅行与地理纪录片、茶叶与民宿品牌、冥想音频背景、文旅宣传片\n- 关键词：山水、层叠、云雾、小舟、空气透视\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「山水」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，8 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 天空 #e6ece6 渐变到地平 #dfe6df；水面 #eef1e9\n- 五层山由远及近取色：#a8b6b2 → #8b9c98 → #6d7f7b → #4e5f5b → #33413d，远山几乎融进天里\n- 山形一律用 6-10 个点的折线，缓坡为主，允许一处陡崖；山顶不画树，只在下缘给 3-4 条短竖线当林\n- 云雾是 4-6 条横向长条（宽 400-900、高 24-40），#f5f2e8、alpha 0.55，边缘 blur 12px\n- 小舟：3px 弧线当船底 + 一竖当桅 + 一个 r=2 的点当人，位置压在第三层山脚的水线上\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-2.5s：远山从上方 20px 处淡入并落位（最远层先出，每层隔 0.4s）\n- 2.5-5s：云雾自左向右缓慢平移（速度 6px/s，循环错相），逐层遮住山脚\n- 5-7s：小舟从右侧 1/4 处驶入，水平位移 120px，船身随波浪起伏 ±2px\n- 7-8s：最前一层山脚压暗 10%，定格收尾\n\n技术要点：\n- 每层山用 `ctx.save()` 后平移不同速度做视差，位移比 1 : 0.75 : 0.55 : 0.35 : 0.2\n- 云雾用 `createLinearGradient` 横向透明渐变 + `filter='blur(12px)'`，直接 `fillRect` 即可\n- 水面加 5 条 1px 横线（alpha 0.12，长度 60-140 随机），避免大片死白\n"},{"id":"S10-Z5","name":"剪纸","series":"S10","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S10-Z5.jpg","markdown":"# Z5 剪纸\n\n- 分类：Z 中国风 · 中国风\n- 长什么样：红纸镂空，民间纹样 / Chinese paper cutting\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n> 这一条的提示词是重新写过的详细版：画面规则、颜色、时间轴都写死了，DeepSeek 和 Opus 5.5 都能直接用。{{ }} 里是要你自己填的内容。\n\n你是一位前端动效工程师。请写**一个完整的 index.html 文件**：只用原生 HTML + JavaScript + Canvas，不要任何外部库、字体文件或图片。做一段\"中国剪纸\"风格的动画：红纸对折剪出来的圆形镂空窗花，一层层叠在窗户纸上，有投影。\n\n## 内容\n\n- 主题：{{主题，例：新人的片头}}\n- 总时长：{{秒，默认 12}}\n- 中心要镂出的字：{{默认\"囍\"}}\n\n## 输出要求（必须照做）\n\n1. 只输出一个 html 代码块，不要解释。代码必须完整可运行：不要用省略号，不要写\"其余同上\"\"此处略\"。\n2. canvas 固定 1920×1080，用 CSS 等比缩放居中，多出来的部分是黑边。\n3. 所有画面由函数 `draw(t)` 决定（`t` 是秒）。不要用 `Math.random`；需要随机就用带种子的 mulberry32(seed)，同一个 `t` 每次画出来都一样。\n4. 默认循环播放；空格暂停/继续；网址带 `?t=5` 时只显示第 5 秒那一帧。\n\n## 颜色\n\n窗户纸 #F1E8D4；主红 #C8102E；深红 #8F0B22。除这三色外不要别的颜色，不要金色、渐变、高光。\n\n## 怎么画（照这个步骤）\n\n1. **窗户纸**：用 #F1E8D4 铺满，再用带种子的随机画 2000 条长 8–34px 的细短线（一半 #B9A98A、一半 #FFFAF0，透明度 0.05–0.11）当纸纤维。这一层只画一次，放进离屏 canvas 缓存。\n2. **造两张红纸**（各用一个离屏 canvas，大小 1920×1080，坐标原点移到画面中心）：\n   - `layerBase`：深红 #8F0B22 的圆盘，半径 452；用 `destination-out` 挖外缘 48 个半径 10 的小孔（放在半径 428 处），再挖掉半径 400 以内的整个圆（只留一圈深红边）。\n   - `layerTop`：亮红 #C8102E 的圆盘，半径 404；用 `destination-out` 挖下面这些，**每一圈都是\"画 1/N，旋转复制 N 份\"**：\n     - 外缘 48 个小孔：半径 9，放在 r=378；\n     - 24 个小水滴：从 r=342 到 r=274，宽 22；\n     - 12 片大花瓣：从 r=244 到 r=74，宽 50；\n     - 12 个小菱形：每个先转 π/12，中心在 r=226，宽 32 高 48；\n     - 12 个小圆点：每个先转 π/12，半径 12，放在 r=170；\n     - 12 片内圈花瓣：从 r=78 到 r=148，宽 26；\n     - 再挖掉半径 66 的中心圆；然后用亮红在中心补一个半径 46 的小盘；最后用 `destination-out` 在小盘上写中心的字（粗体 66px，居中）。\n   - 花瓣之间必须留红色的\"桥\"，不要把红纸挖断。\n3. **四个角花**：用另一个离屏 canvas 画一块\"四分之一圆\"（半径 250，圆心在画面角上）的亮红，挖：9 个沿弧边的小孔（半径 7）、4 片水滴、一个半径 58 的圆窗。复制到四个角（翻转）。\n4. **合成**：先画窗户纸；再把 `layerBase`、`layerTop`、四个角花依次画上去，每一层画之前设置投影：`shadowColor='rgba(70,12,12,0.38)'`，`shadowBlur=16`，`shadowOffsetX=5`，`shadowOffsetY=9`。\n\n水滴形的路径可以直接用这个函数（原点在窗花中心，y 轴向上为负）：\n\n```js\nfunction drop(g, y0, y1, w) {\n  const d = y0 - y1;\n  g.beginPath(); g.moveTo(0, y0);\n  g.bezierCurveTo(w, y0 - d * .25, w * .9, y1 + d * .35, 0, y1);\n  g.bezierCurveTo(-w * .9, y1 + d * .35, -w, y0 - d * .25, 0, y0);\n  g.fill();\n}\nfunction ring(g, n, fn) { for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) { g.save(); g.rotate(i * Math.PI * 2 / n); fn(g, i); g.restore(); } }\n```\n\n## 动起来\n\n- 0–0.8 秒：深红底盘从半径 0 展开到满（ease-out）；0.25 秒后亮红主盘同样展开。\n- 1.2–4 秒：镂空由外向内一圈圈\"打开\"（每个孔的半径从 0 长到目标值，每圈晚 0.18 秒，每个孔 0.35 秒）；因为孔是挖出来的，所以实现时把\"孔的半径 × 进度\"传给上面的挖孔步骤，每帧重新生成 `layerTop`（可以缓存已经完成的圈）。\n- 4–4.4 秒：中心的字\"盖章\"：缩放 1.15 → 1.0。\n- 之后整张窗花以 ±0.6° 缓慢摆动（周期 6 秒），影子偏移跟着变 ±2px。\n- 最后一秒保持静止，然后循环。\n\n## 不要\n\n金色、渐变、塑料高光、3D、粒子、发光、过重的阴影、把字写在纸上（必须是镂出来的）。\n\n## 写完后自己过一遍（不要输出）\n\n- 镂空是 `destination-out` 挖的，不是用底色盖的吗？\n- 12 份是否完全对称？\n- 所有影子是否都向右下？\n- 同一个 `t` 刷新两次，画面是否一模一样？\n"},{"id":"S10-Z6","name":"年画","series":"S10","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S10-Z6.jpg","markdown":"# Z6 年画\n\n- 分类：Z 中国风 · 年画\n- 长什么样：大红大黄满铺的高饱和撞色，正中一个圆脸胖娃娃，红肚兜绿裤，脸颊两团橘红；所有轮廓裹一圈 4-5px 的金边，构图严格左右对称，四周填满莲花鲤鱼等吉祥符号\n- 适合：春节与节庆片头、年货电商主图、民俗活动海报、红包封面动画\n- 关键词：年画、撞色、胖娃娃、金边、对称\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「年画」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 背景满铺 #cf2b25（正红），中央留一个 #f7e3b0 的圆形亮区当「年画纸」\n- 撞色只四支：#f2b705（明黄）、#1f7a4d（翠绿）、#f19a8a（腮红）、#fdf6e0（高光）\n- 所有主体轮廓描 #d9a441 金边，`lineWidth 5`，且必须画在填充之外不是之内\n- 胖娃娃：大圆脸 r=78，两个小髻（r=22 对称），眉弯眼弯，嘴是上翘的弧；身体是圆角矩形，胳膊腿都是粗圆柱\n- 构图左右对称，四角各一枚莲花／铜钱／鲤鱼，不许打破中轴\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1s：红底从中心圆形扩散铺满（半径 0→画面对角线），亮区随后淡入\n- 1-2.5s：娃娃从下方弹入（scale 0.6→1.12→1，overshoot 一次），金边跟着描出\n- 2.5-4s：两侧莲花依次绽放（花瓣角度 0→360 展开），铜钱旋转 180°\n- 4-6s：腮红从中心向外晕开（alpha 0→0.85），娃娃眨眼一次，静止\n\n技术要点：\n- 金边用「先 `stroke` 粗路径、再 `fill` 同路径」的顺序，比 `lineWidth` 双层描边更干净\n- 花瓣用 12 个旋转的椭圆（rx 18 / ry 34）绕圆心阵列，`ctx.rotate` 后画再 `restore`\n- 撞色之间不许有渐变过渡，色块边界必须是硬边，一加羽化就去年画味\n"},{"id":"S10-Z7","name":"皮影","series":"S10","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S10-Z7.jpg","markdown":"# Z7 皮影\n\n- 分类：Z 中国风 · 皮影\n- 长什么样：深褐近黑的幕布上，一个侧身剪影般的彩色人偶被背后的光透出来，红绿黄三色半透明发亮，边缘压一道黑线；四肢分段靠关节串起，动作像被竹签挑着，一颠一颠\n- 适合：非遗与民俗题材、戏曲片头、故事叙述插段、国风游戏 PV\n- 关键词：皮影、透光、关节人偶、侧影、暖光\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「皮影」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 幕布 #16100c，叠竖向布纹（1px 线，间距 6px，alpha 0.05）\n- 人偶透光色：#c0392b（红）、#2e7d5b（绿）、#d4a017（黄），全部以 alpha 0.55 填充，背后衬暖光\n- 背后的侧光是一团 #ffb85c 的 `radialGradient`，中心 alpha 0.5，半径 320，落在人偶中段\n- 人偶必须侧身：头是侧面轮廓（额→鼻→下巴一段折线），眼睛只是一条弯线，绝不画正面五官\n- 关节分明：头／上身／上臂前臂／大腿小腿各自成块，块与块之间留 3px 缝、用 6px 黑圆当轴\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.5s：幕布先亮（暖光 alpha 0→0.5），人偶从右下角沿弧线「挑」上台\n- 1.5-4s：走位三步，每步上身前后倾 6°，腿一屈一伸，竹签（细黑线）跟着抖\n- 4-5.5s：抬臂亮相，手臂绕轴转 -35°，飘带尾部滞后 0.15s 跟摆\n- 5.5-7s：整体缩小 12% 退向右上，暖光收小，幕布复暗\n\n技术要点：\n- 透光感：先画暖光 `radialGradient`，再用 `globalCompositeOperation='multiply'` 叠人偶，颜色会像被灯打过\n- 每个关节写成函数 `limb(angle, len)`，父级角度累加到子级，避免手调坐标\n- 边缘黑线用 `strokeStyle='#0d0906'`、`lineWidth 2`，只描外轮廓，内部留缝不描\n"},{"id":"S10-Z8","name":"敦煌","series":"S10","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S10-Z8.jpg","markdown":"# Z8 敦煌\n\n- 分类：Z 中国风 · 敦煌壁画\n- 长什么样：土黄偏赭的泥壁底色上，一个侧身屈腿的飞天凭三条长飘带浮在空中，线条是土红细线，颜色只有石绿、朱砂、白垩几种矿物色；墙面到处是剥落的小洞和噪点，像被风沙磨过一千年\n- 适合：文博与考古内容、丝路主题纪录片、国潮美妆包装、文化展览片头\n- 关键词：敦煌、飞天、飘带、壁画、斑驳肌理\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「敦煌」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，8 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 壁底 #c49a63，上缘压 #a87f4c、下缘压 #8f6a3f 的纵向渐变，模拟墙脚积尘\n- 矿物色仅四支：#2f6f66（石绿）、#a63c2c（朱砂）、#8c3b2e（土红）、#ece0c8（白垩）\n- 一切轮廓用 #8c3b2e 的 1.5-2px 线，线不闭合、允许断口，像后人补描\n- 飞天侧身，两腿一屈一伸，手臂上举托物；身体只填白垩底，局部点石绿\n- 飘带三条，从肩后甩出，宽 10px 收到 2px，末端分两三叉，走长 S 曲线\n- 斑驳：全画面 300 个随机小洞（半径 1-6）用 `destination-out` 挖穿，边缘再描一圈土红\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-2s：壁底先出，斑驳噪点由 0 渐到满（模拟镜头推近看清肌理）\n- 2-4.5s：三条飘带自肩部依次展开，各自以不同相位正弦摆动，振幅 26px\n- 4.5-6.5s：飞天整体上浮 30px、缓慢右移，屈腿那只脚踝轻转 8°\n- 6.5-8s：上下各压一条 #8c3b2e 装饰带（含联珠圆点串），画面定住\n\n技术要点：\n- 飘带用采样点连成多边形（左右各一条边，宽度按 `sin` 收窄），比 `lineWidth` 更能做「渐细」\n- 剥落用两层：`destination-out` 挖洞 + 洞缘 `stroke` 土红 alpha 0.35，才有厚度\n- 噪点用离屏 canvas 一次性画 2000 个 1px 点做成 pattern，`fillRect` 平铺，比逐帧画点快一个数量级\n"},{"id":"S10-Z9","name":"青花","series":"S10","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S10-Z9.jpg","markdown":"# Z9 青花\n\n- 分类：Z 中国风 · 青花瓷\n- 长什么样：近乎纯白的瓷底上只有一种钴蓝，缠枝藤蔓从中轴左右对称地爬满器身，每段藤上均匀挂一朵五瓣花，花与花之间留出等宽的空白；蓝不是平涂，笔触中间深、边缘浅，像釉料堆出来\n- 适合：瓷器与器物展示、中式品牌 VI 动画、婚礼请柬、高端食品包装\n- 关键词：青花、钴蓝、缠枝、对称、白瓷\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「青花」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 瓷底 #f8f6f0，中心加一团 #eef1f2 的极淡径向渐变当反光，alpha 0.5\n- 唯一彩色 #1e4b8f（钴蓝），深一档 #14356b 用于笔触重叠处，不加第三种色\n- 器形用梅瓶：左右对称的 6 段贝塞尔曲线，瓶口窄、肩宽、腹收、底外撇，轮廓线 1.2px\n- 缠枝是正弦藤（振幅 26、周期 180），藤上每 60px 挂一朵五瓣花 + 两片卷叶\n- 留白要均匀：纹样总面积不超过器身的 45%，瓶肩与瓶脚各留一圈 8px 弦纹带\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.5s：瓶身轮廓自上而下描出（dashoffset 从全长到 0）\n- 1.5-4s：主藤从中轴起笔，向左右两侧交替生长，花随藤开\n- 4-5.5s：每朵花的花瓣由内向外依次加深（alpha 0.3→0.9），形成釉色堆叠\n- 5.5-7s：瓶底投影淡入，全器轻微上浮 6px 后定住\n\n技术要点：\n- 藤蔓用 `ctx.setLineDash` 配合逐帧减小 offset 做生长，虚线长度设成路径总长\n- 釉料浓淡：同一路径画两遍，第一遍 `lineWidth 6` alpha 0.25，第二遍 `lineWidth 2.5` alpha 0.9\n- 左右对称直接用 `ctx.scale(-1, 1)` 镜像重绘一遍，别手算镜像坐标\n"},{"id":"S10-Z10","name":"木版画","series":"S10","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S10-Z10.jpg","markdown":"# Z10 木版画\n\n- 分类：Z 中国风 · 木版画\n- 长什么样：只有墨黑和纸黄两级色，没有灰阶；所有中间调都用等距平行刻线排出来，线条又粗又硬、端点方齐，转折是生硬的折角；轮廓边缘有细微的锯齿和缺口，是刻刀啃出来的\n- 适合：民谣与独立音乐封面、书店与出版品牌、纪实短片、版画展海报\n- 关键词：木版、黑白、粗刻线、排线、刀味\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「木版画」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 纸底 #efe9dc，墨 #14100c，全片只有这两级色，禁止任何 alpha 中间灰\n- 灰调一律用排线：45° 单向线（间距 6px）表浅灰，交叉排线（90° 再加一层）表深灰\n- 刻线粗：#14100c、`lineWidth` 5-9、`lineCap='butt'`、`lineJoin='miter'`，不许圆头\n- 刀味：每条路径的采样点加 ±1.5px 随机抖动，端点略不齐，长线中间允许 1-2 处断口\n- 主体：一棵树 + 一间屋，全部用剪影＋排线表达体积，不画描边式的细轮廓\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.5s：纸底上「印」出第一块墨（从中心向外硬边扩张，不用羽化）\n- 1.5-3.5s：树干自下而上被刻出，每段 0.4s，切口是方角\n- 3.5-5s：树冠的三层排线依次落下（单向→交叉→加密），明暗随之变深\n- 5-6s：右下角盖一枚朱红小印 #b8442f（唯一彩色，8% 面积），定住\n\n技术要点：\n- 排线用循环 `for (let x=-H; x<W+H; x+=6)` 画 45° 长线，再用 `clip()` 限制在形状内\n- 抖动写成 `jitter(p, 1.5)` 工具函数，先扰动再 `lineTo`，路径才不平滑\n- 套色印不准的感觉：朱印与主体错开 2-3px，比严丝合缝更像手工印刷\n"},{"id":"S10-Z12","name":"书法","series":"S10","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S10-Z12.jpg","markdown":"# Z12 书法\n\n- 分类：Z 中国风 · 书法\n- 长什么样：整幅只有一个大字，占画面六成高，浓墨带明显飞白，笔画的粗细随起收顿挫剧烈变化；右侧一列竖排小字落款，右上或左下压一枚方形朱印，其余全是纸的白\n- 适合：年度关键词、品牌口号片头、节气与年节卡、片尾签名\n- 关键词：书法、单字、飞白、竖排落款、朱印\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「书法」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 纸底 #ece3d0，叠极淡的纤维纹（随机 1px 横线，alpha 0.04）\n- 墨 #171412，中心最浓处可到 #000，边缘因飞白自然变透\n- 主字一个（如「道」「山」「无」），字高占画面 60%，位置偏左上，右下留空\n- 落款竖排 5-7 个小字，字号为主字的 1/12，行距紧，字体用楷体\n- 朱印 #a5352b，18×18mm 比例（35×35px），圆角 1px，印面留白不均才像真印\n- 笔画必须粗细对比：横画收笔细到 2px，捺脚压到 22px\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-2.5s：按笔顺逐笔写出，每笔用遮罩从左向右扫过（0.35-0.6s/笔）\n- 2.5-3.6s：等墨稍「干」，笔画末端的飞白从 alpha 0 渐显\n- 3.6-4.6s：竖排落款自上而下逐字出现（每字 0.08s）\n- 4.6-6s：朱印落章——先缩小 1.2 倍再回弹到 1，印色 alpha 0.85，定住\n\n技术要点：\n- 单字不要用字体渲染，改用 `Path2D` 手写 8-12 段贝塞尔骨架，再沿骨架做变宽描边\n- 飞白用 `destination-out` 在笔画末端画 8-20 条 1px 斜线，角度与运笔方向一致\n- 印面做旧：在红块上用 `destination-out` 撒 15 个小点 + 四边各啃掉一小块\n"},{"id":"S10-Z13","name":"水墨漫画","series":"S10","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S10-Z13.jpg","markdown":"# Z13 水墨漫画\n\n- 分类：Z 中国风 · 水墨漫画\n- 长什么样：毛笔墨线勾出的人物，头大得夸张占身体一半，眼睛是两个带高光的黑豆，嘴巴咧到耳根，额头挂三滴汗；线条有粗细顿挫，配白底黑边的圆气泡，气泡尾巴指向人物，像报纸上的四格漫画\n- 适合：知识科普的轻松段落、社交媒体系列短片、教育 App 讲解、品牌人格化 IP\n- 关键词：水墨漫画、墨线、夸张表情、对话气泡、幽默\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「水墨漫画」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 纸底 #f6f1e5，人物脚下画一道 3px 的墨线当「地面」，其余留白\n- 墨线 #1a1a1a，`lineWidth` 在主轮廓 3px、五官 2px、衣物褶 1.2px 三档\n- 人物比例：头高 = 全高的 50%，肩宽 = 头的 0.7 倍，四肢是两条细线，脚是两个小弧\n- 表情要夸张：眼是 r=14 的实心椭圆 + 左上白点高光，嘴是宽 46 的弧，嘴角超出脸缘\n- 气泡：白 #ffffff 填充、2px 黑边、圆角 26px，尾巴是两根弧线收成一个尖；气泡宽 260-320\n- 淡墨 #b9b2a4 只用于脸侧阴影和地面投影，alpha 0.5\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.5s：人物从左下「甩」进来，入画时整体倾斜 -8° 再回正\n- 1.5-3s：脸部表情切换一次——眼睛放大 1.3 倍，嘴巴由平弧变成大张口\n- 3-4.5s：气泡从人物嘴部弹出（scale 0.2→1.06→1，尾巴跟着拉伸）\n- 4.5-7s：气泡内文字逐字跳出（每字 0.06s），人物点头两次，汗滴滑落\n\n技术要点：\n- 墨线粗细变化：同一批路径先用 `lineWidth 3` 描、再用 `lineWidth 1.2` 描一次，产生「双勾」层次\n- 气泡尾巴用 `quadraticCurveTo` 两根曲线在尖点相交，别用三角形，直角会破坏手绘感\n- 表情切换不要补间，直接两套参数插值 0.15s 内跳变，漫画感来自「突变」\n"},{"id":"S10-Z14","name":"彩铅手绘","series":"S10","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S10-Z14.jpg","markdown":"# Z14 彩铅手绘\n\n- 分类：Z 中国风 · 彩铅手绘\n- 长什么样：轮廓线不是一根，是三根微微错开的细线叠在一起，看起来毛茸茸的；颜色靠 45° 的短笔触一层层叠上去，纸面的颗粒从颜色缝里透出来；整体偏暖，像旧速写本上的一页\n- 适合：手作与生活方式品牌、儿童绘本动画、课程插图、温暖叙事短片\n- 关键词：彩铅、毛糙线条、纸纹、叠色、颗粒\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「彩铅手绘」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，8 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 纸底 #fbf6ec，全画面盖一层噪点 pattern（1px 点、alpha 0.06），这是纸纹\n- 四支主色：#e07a5f（赭橙）、#81a684（灰绿）、#4f6d8c（蓝灰）、#e6c15a（土黄），不许出现纯正红蓝\n- 每条轮廓画 3 遍：`(0,0)`、`(+1.5,-1)`、`(-1,+1.2)` 三个偏移，每遍 alpha 0.45，形成毛边\n- 填充不用实色，用 45° 短笔触（长 8-14px、宽 2px、间距 3-5px），密度决定明暗：亮=间距 6，暗=间距 2\n- 主体：一个捧杯的人 + 一株植物，全部圆角化，没有一条直线\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-2.5s：轮廓三遍线依次描出（每遍 0.6s），像手在反复找形\n- 2.5-5s：叠色从浅到深铺三层，笔触方向一致（都 45°），每层 alpha 0.35\n- 5-6.5s：局部加亮——杯口、脸的高光用白笔点两笔，笔触更短\n- 6.5-8s：纸纹噪点整体轻推 2px（模拟翻页），画面定住\n\n技术要点：\n- 短笔触用 `ctx.translate` + `rotate(45°)` 后画 `fillRect(0,0,12,2)`，比逐点 `lineTo` 快\n- 三层线要共用同一路径对象（`Path2D`），只改 `setTransform` 偏移，避免形状抖动\n- 纸纹 pattern 用离屏 canvas 预生成 64×64，`createPattern(..., 'repeat')` 全屏铺一次即可\n"},{"id":"S10-Z15","name":"温暖水彩科普卡","series":"S10","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S10-Z15.jpg","markdown":"# Z15 温暖水彩科普卡\n\n- 分类：Z 中国风 · 水彩科普卡\n- 长什么样：底上是几团杏粉、浅绿、淡黄的水彩晕染，边界模糊、互相渗色；上面浮着 2-3 张白色大圆角卡片，卡片里有圆形图标和两条文字占位条，整体像一本手账里的知识页\n- 适合：轻知识科普、健康与育儿内容、课程章节卡、社区运营图\n- 关键词：水彩、科普卡、圆角卡片、柔和、留白\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「温暖水彩科普卡」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，8 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 纸底 #fdfaf4，四周留 56px 安全边距，卡片绝不越界\n- 水彩四团：#f7c8b0（杏）、#cfe0c3（浅绿）、#f6e0a8（淡黄）、#bcd4e6（浅蓝），各自 blur 30px，`multiply` 叠加\n- 卡片纯白 #ffffff，圆角 20px，描边 1px #eee7dc，阴影 `rgba(120,100,80,0.12) 0 8px 24px`\n- 卡片内：圆形图标底（r=22，用对应水彩色 alpha 0.35）、标题条（圆角 4px、高 10px、宽 60%）、正文条（高 6px、两行、宽 82%/64%）\n- 全片无黑色，最深只到 #4a4239，字重中等，不做粗黑标题\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-2s：四团水彩从中心向外晕开（半径 0→200，同时 blur 由 0 增到 30）\n- 2-3.5s：三张卡片依次上浮（translateY 24→0，间隔 0.15s，easeOutCubic）\n- 3.5-5.5s：图标圆底弹出，文字条从左向右展开（scaleX 0→1，transform-origin 左侧）\n- 5.5-8s：水彩层极缓漂移（6px/s），卡片轻微 2px 呼吸，定住\n\n技术要点：\n- 水彩 blob 用 `beginPath` + 12 个随机半径的 `arc` 连成不规则的圆，再 `filter='blur(30px)'` 铺\n- 卡片阴影别用 `shadowBlur` 逐帧算，改成先画一层半透明模糊矩形，性能差 5 倍\n- 文字条用 `roundRect`（Safari 需自行 polyfill 或用 arc+lineTo），半径 4px 不要写成 0\n"},{"id":"S2-H1","name":"Biennale Yellow","series":"S2","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S2-H1.jpg","markdown":"# H1 Biennale Yellow\n\n- 分类：H 现成版式 · 现成版式\n- 长什么样：暖羊皮纸底，太阳黄光晕，衬线大标题，靛蓝墨色，细线\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n> 这一条的提示词是重新写过的详细版：画面规则、颜色、时间轴都写死了，DeepSeek 和 Opus 5.5 都能直接用。{{ }} 里是要你自己填的内容。\n\n你是一位前端动效工程师。请写**一个完整的 index.html 文件**：只用原生 HTML + JavaScript + Canvas，不要任何外部库、字体文件或图片。做一张\"艺术双年展杂志封面\"风格的动画标题卡。\n\n## 内容\n\n- 大标题（两行）：{{第一行，例：Biennale}} / {{第二行，例：Yellow}}（第二行用斜体）\n- 页眉左：{{例：THE TWELFTH BIENNALE · EDITORIAL SERIES}}；页眉右：{{例：N° 01}}\n- 页脚左（中文）：{{一句话说明}}；页脚右：{{编号}}\n- 总时长：{{秒，默认 6}}\n\n## 输出要求（必须照做）\n\n1. 只输出一个 html 代码块，不要解释。代码必须完整可运行：不要用省略号，不要写\"其余同上\"\"此处略\"。\n2. canvas 固定 1920×1080，用 CSS 等比缩放居中，多出来的部分是黑边。\n3. 所有画面由函数 `draw(t)` 决定（`t` 是秒）。不要用 `Math.random`；纸纹用带种子的 mulberry32(seed)。\n4. 默认循环；空格暂停/继续；网址带 `?t=3` 时只显示第 3 秒那一帧。\n\n## 颜色和线（只准用这些）\n\n- 底色 paper = #F2E9D2；加纸纹：5000 个 1–3px 的小方点，一半 #8C7A55、一半 #FFFAF0，透明度 0.045–0.095。纸纹只画一次，缓存到离屏 canvas。\n- 唯一的强调色 sun = #F6C23E。\n- 字和所有线都用 ink = #1D2A68，线宽 1.6–1.8px。\n\n## 字体\n\n`font-family: Georgia, \"Times New Roman\", \"Noto Serif SC\", \"Songti SC\", serif`。全片只用衬线体。大标题约 214px，`letterSpacing = '-4px'`；页眉 26px，大写，`letterSpacing = '6px'`；页脚中文 34px。（canvas 的 `ctx.letterSpacing` 在新版浏览器里可用；不支持就逐字绘制。）\n\n## 版面（坐标按 1920×1080）\n\n1. 外框：`strokeRect(64, 64, 1792, 952)`；再画两道横线：y=148 和 y=932，从 x=64 到 x=1856。\n2. 太阳，圆心 (1380, 470)：\n   - 光晕：径向渐变，半径 20→560，从 `rgba(246,194,62,1)` 到 `rgba(246,194,62,0.92)`（38% 处），再到 `rgba(246,214,120,0.35)`（70% 处），到 `rgba(246,214,120,0)`（100%）；\n   - 实心圆：半径 250，颜色 sun；\n   - 三道同心细圈：半径 250、330、410（ink，透明度 0.9、0.45、0.45）；\n   - 刻度环：半径 410 到 421（每 5 条一条长的，到 432），一共 120 条，ink，透明度 0.85。\n3. 大标题：左边 x=100，第一行基线 y=560，第二行（斜体）基线 y=770。注意标题的右端不要碰到刻度环（最左端约 x=948）。\n4. 页眉两行小字基线 y=118（左 x=104，右 x=1650）；页脚中文基线 y=980（左 x=104），编号右对齐到 x=1816。\n\n## 动起来\n\n- 0–0.8 秒：外框和两道横线从四角向中间\"画出来\"（用 `setLineDash` 或按进度截取）；\n- 0.4–1.8 秒：太阳从下方 300px 处升到位（ease-out），光晕透明度 0→1；\n- 1.0–2.4 秒：标题两行依次从下方 24px 处淡入，字距从 +20px 收到 −4px；\n- 2.0–2.8 秒：页眉页脚淡入；刻度环以每秒 1° 顺时针慢转；\n- 结尾 0.5 秒：整体淡出到纸色，然后循环。\n\n## 不要\n\n渐变色块、阴影、圆角卡片、第二种强调色、无衬线大标题、发光、粒子。\n\n## 写完后自己过一遍（不要输出）\n\n- 全片是否只有 sun 一个强调色？\n- 标题是否碰到了刻度环？\n- 同一个 `t` 刷新两次，画面是否一模一样？\n"},{"id":"S2-H2","name":"BlockFrame","series":"S2","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S2-H2.jpg","markdown":"# H2 BlockFrame\n\n- 分类：H 现成版式 · 新粗野主义\n- 长什么样：奶油底上几个巨型矩形，每个都裹一圈 8px 纯黑边框，朝右下拖出一块不带模糊的硬投影；框内塞着大字和糖果色小方块，四角各贴一枚带黑边的色块当「铆钉」，整体像廉价印刷传单故意做糙\n- 适合：独立品牌宣言、活动招募主视觉、唱片与播客封面、社媒九宫格\n- 关键词：粗野主义、粗黑边框、硬投影、糖果色、角标\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「BlockFrame」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，5 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底 #fdf6e3 奶油黄，允许透出 24px 间隔的 0.5px 铅笔网格（alpha 0.06）\n- 主色块三支：#ffd166（明黄）、#ef476f（品红）、#06d6a0（薄荷绿），每块只用一色，不渐变\n- 所有矩形描 #111111、`lineWidth 8`；投影固定右下偏移 (12, 12)、纯 #111、无模糊\n- 角标：四个角各 26×26 的实色方块，带 2px 黑边，位置必须压住框线一半\n- 文字用 900 字重无衬线，字号 96-140，字距 -1px，压在两块色块交界处制造打断\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-0.8s：主框从中心 scale 0.9→1 弹入，硬投影同步从 (0,0) 移到 (12,12)\n- 0.8-2s：三块糖果色块依次从框外「撞」进来，每次到位回弹 6%\n- 2-3.2s：大标题逐行落下（每行 0.14s，translateY -30→0），带 1 帧抖动\n- 3.2-5s：四角角标逐个「啪」上（scale 1.4→1，0.06s），画面静止\n\n技术要点：\n- 硬投影就是 `fillRect(x+12, y+12, w, h)` 再画主体，绝不用 `shadowBlur`\n- 描边要压在填充外：先 `fillRect` 再 `strokeRect(x-4, y-4, w+8, h+8)`，视觉上框才是「包」住内容\n- 弹入缓动 `easeOutBack`：`1 + 2.7*Math.pow(t-1,3) + 1.7*Math.pow(t-1,2)`\n"},{"id":"S2-H3","name":"Blue Professional","series":"S2","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S2-H3.jpg","markdown":"# H3 Blue Professional\n\n- 分类：H 现成版式 · 商务数据\n- 长什么样：整幅深海军蓝渐变，上面浮着极淡的白色细网格和一条横向细亮线；左侧一列白色文字，右侧一组圆角条形图和一条折线，数字比标题更显眼，气质克制、可信、不花哨\n- 适合：企业年报、金融产品说明、B2B 方案汇报、季度数据发布\n- 关键词：深蓝、商务、细网格、数据可视化、克制\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「Blue Professional」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底 #0b2545 到 #13315c 的斜向渐变（45°），右下角叠一团 #1f4b7d、alpha 0.35 的径向光\n- 前景白 #e8eef7，强调色 #4a9eda，禁用任何暖色\n- 细网格：1px 线、`#ffffff` alpha 0.07、间距 24px，只在右半区铺（左半留给文字）\n- 数据组：5 根竖条（宽 34、圆角 3、色 #4a9eda，最右一根 #8cc6f2 高亮）+ 一条 2px 折线贯穿条顶\n- 顶部一条 1px #2c5c8f 横线，距上边 72px；左下角留 4 条 6px 高的灰色文字占位条\n- 数字字号 64、字重 700；正文 20、字重 400、字距 0.5px\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1s：底色渐变淡入，细网格自右向左扫过（遮罩宽度 0→100%）\n- 1-2.5s：左侧标题与占位条依次上浮（间隔 0.1s，透明度 0→1）\n- 2.5-4.5s：5 根竖条从底部生长（scaleY 0→1，间隔 0.08s），折线随之从右向左画出\n- 4.5-6s：高亮条脉冲一次（alpha 0.75→1），数字从 0 计数到目标值，收尾\n\n技术要点：\n- 网格必须用 `globalAlpha = 0.07` 单独一层画，混进别的元素里会显脏\n- 竖条生长用 `ctx.scale(1, t)` 会把圆角拉变形，改成用 `roundRect` 每帧按高度重绘\n- 数字滚动用 `Math.round(v * t)` 配 `easeOutQuart`，不要线性，否则停止太生硬\n"},{"id":"S2-H4","name":"Bold Poster","series":"S2","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S2-H4.jpg","markdown":"# H4 Bold Poster\n\n- 分类：H 现成版式 · 海报版式\n- 长什么样：白底或大色块底，超大无衬线黑字占画面 40% 以上，字距紧、行距压缩，辅以细网格或小圆点做呼吸感，红黑对比强烈\n- 适合：金句卡片、活动主视觉、社交媒体封面、标题卡\n- 关键词：大字、海报、黑白、撞色、网格\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「Bold Poster」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，5 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 背景纯白 #ffffff，下半 45% 覆盖 #e63946 红色块\n- 主标题 900 字重无衬线，字号占画面高度 35%，字距 -2px，行距 0.88\n- 标题黑色 #111，压在红块上的副标题改为白色\n- 装饰：右上角实心黑圆点（r=12）、上半区 20px 间隔的 0.5px 细网格（opacity 0.12）\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-0.8s：红色块从底部 scaleY 0→1 铺满（easeOutQuart）\n- 0.8-1.8s：主标题逐词从左侧滑入（每个词间隔 0.12s，translateX -40→0）\n- 1.8-3s：副标题淡入 + 圆点弹出（scale 0→1.15→1）\n- 3-5s：细网格从中心向四周扩散（opacity 0→0.12），静止保持\n\n技术要点：\n- 字体用 `900 128px \"Arial Black\", sans-serif`，中文用微软雅黑 Bold\n- 标题换行要手动控制断句，不能交给浏览器\n- 文字压红块时先画红块再画字，注意上下留 24px 呼吸边距\n"},{"id":"S2-H5","name":"Broadside","series":"S2","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S2-H5.jpg","markdown":"# H5 Broadside\n\n- 分类：H 现成版式 · 报纸版式\n- 长什么样：泛黄的新闻纸底，上方一个几乎顶到边的衬线大标题，字距压得很紧、行距也挤，标题下一道粗一细的双线，再往下分成三栏，栏间一条竖细线，栏内是密排的小字块，通篇只有黑白\n- 适合：深度报道片头、播客封面、观点类短视频、书评与影评卡\n- 关键词：报纸、衬线大标题、多栏、黑白、密排\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「Broadside」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，5 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 纸底 #f4f1e8，边缘压 8% 的 #ddd6c4 做旧，允许几点随机霉斑（alpha 0.08）\n- 只用黑 #1a1a1a 一色，灰阶靠 0.55 / 0.3 / 0.15 三档 alpha 模拟油墨深浅\n- 报头双线：粗 4px + 细 1px，间隔 3px，横贯版面宽度，距上边 96px\n- 正文三栏，栏宽相等、栏间 1px 竖线（alpha 0.3）；每栏文字用 2px 高灰条模拟（行距 9px）\n- 大标题衬线字体，字号 132、字距 -3px、行距 0.9，允许跨两行并轻微出血到边\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.2s：纸底铺开，报头双线从左端拉到右端（scaleX 0→1）\n- 1.2-2.6s：大标题逐行「印」上——每行用裁剪区从上向下揭开，像被压印\n- 2.6-3.8s：栏间竖线自上而下画出，三栏文字条按行依次出现（每行 0.03s）\n- 3.8-5s：整体轻微放大 1.03 并左移 8px，模拟镜头贴近，停住\n\n技术要点：\n- 大标题揭开用 `ctx.save(); ctx.beginPath(); ctx.rect(x, y, w, h*t); ctx.clip();` 画字再 `restore`\n- 文字条不要真排文字（会因字体缺失崩版），用 `fillRect` 灰条，只要密度节奏对就成立\n- 双线用两次 `fillRect` 而不是 `stroke`，端点才能做到方齐、无半像素模糊\n"},{"id":"S2-H6","name":"Capsule","series":"S2","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S2-H6.jpg","markdown":"# H6 Capsule\n\n- 分类：H 现成版式 · 胶囊排版\n- 长什么样：米粉底上一条条两端全圆的胶囊色条，长短不一、横向错落堆叠，像被压扁的药丸；胶囊之间留均匀的缝，里面是深棕小字，全片没有一个直角，阴影极淡，气质是护肤品柜台那种软\n- 适合：美妆与个护品牌、健康食品包装、App 空状态页、生活类课程封面\n- 关键词：胶囊、全圆角、条状排版、柔和、米粉\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「Capsule」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底 #f5ece1 米粉，右上角一团 #efdcc9、alpha 0.6 的柔和光晕（半径 420）\n- 胶囊三色：#c98b6b（赭）、#7f9c8b（灰绿）、#e8b4a0（裸粉），可加一支 #d9c7a8 米金\n- 所有形状圆角 = 高度/2（`roundRect` 半径写死为 h/2），绝不出现 0 圆角\n- 5 条胶囊：高 56-72、宽 220-620，垂直间距 28，水平起点左右交错 ±40\n- 文字深棕 #3b2f28，20px，胶囊内左内边距 32；一条留白胶囊只放一个小圆点\n- 阴影只有一层：`rgba(150,120,95,0.10)`，blur 18，y 偏移 6\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.5s：胶囊自左侧依次滑入（translateX -260→0，间隔 0.12s，easeOutCubic）\n- 1.5-3s：每条胶囊的宽度从 40% 弹性展开到目标值，末尾回弹 4%\n- 3-4.5s：胶囊内文字从左侧淡入，圆点胶囊的点做一次轻微脉动\n- 4.5-6s：整组胶囊上移 10px 并整体透明度到 0.96，静止收尾\n\n技术要点：\n- 用 `ctx.roundRect(x, y, w, h, h/2)`；不支持时退化成 `arc(x+r, y+r)` 两侧半圆 + `fillRect` 中段\n- 弹性展开用 `easeOutElastic` 会过头太多，改用 `easeOutBack` 参数 1.4 更贴「软」\n- 文字基线要与胶囊垂直居中：`textBaseline='middle'` 且 y 取胶囊中线，不要用 `top` 硬算\n"},{"id":"S2-H7","name":"Cartesian","series":"S2","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S2-H7.jpg","markdown":"# H7 Cartesian\n\n- 分类：H 现成版式 · 坐标图版式\n- 长什么样：近乎纯白的画布上铺一层 40px 方格的浅灰坐标系，横竖轴线略深；一条蓝色趋势线斜穿画面，线上挂着几个实心圆点，每个点向下垂一条虚线到横轴，旁边标着数值，像被抽出来的一页图表\n- 适合：数据新闻、增长与留存讲解、课程里的一页原理图、投资与效率类内容\n- 关键词：坐标网格、数据点、趋势线、留白、理性\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「Cartesian」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底 #fbfbfa，网格 1px #dedede、间距 40px；轴线用 #9a9a9a、1.5px 加粗\n- 趋势线 #2b6cb0、2.5px，`lineCap='round'`，允许一点轻微的手绘感（不用完全笔直）\n- 数据点：外圈 r=7 实心 #2b6cb0，内圈 r=3 白 #fbfbfa，形成同心环\n- 每个点向下一条 1px 虚线（dash 4/4，alpha 0.4）落到横轴，数值 12px #444 标在点右上方\n- 画面左上留 120px 空白放一个 28px 小标题，其余全部留给图\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.2s：网格从中心向外扩展（以中心为原点放大 0.6→1，同时透明度 0→1）\n- 1.2-3.5s：趋势线从左向右「画」出来（`lineDashOffset` 递减），笔尖带一个 r=4 的光点\n- 3.5-5s：数据点按 x 顺序逐个弹出（r 7→10→7），虚线同时从点落到轴\n- 5-6s：数值淡入，趋势线末端延长 20px 并渐隐，静止\n\n技术要点：\n- 折线长度用 `ctx.setLineDash([L, L])` + `lineDashOffset` 从 L 减到 0，比逐段拼接干净\n- 网格用双层 `for` 画，但把 `beginPath` 放在循环外、只 `stroke` 一次，性能差别很大\n- 点弹跳不要用物理，`r = 7 + 3*Math.sin(t*Math.PI)` 在 0.25s 内跑完即可\n"},{"id":"S2-H8","name":"Cobalt Grid","series":"S2","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S2-H8.jpg","markdown":"# H8 Cobalt Grid\n\n- 分类：H 现成版式 · 网格浮层\n- 长什么样：整幅是高饱和钴蓝的方格网，网格线比底色更亮，交叉处有小十字；网上面浮着两三张纯白卡片，卡片投影很重，把网压出层次——网格是主角，卡片只是浮在上面的东西\n- 适合：SaaS 产品发布、技术方案主视觉、开发者大会 KV、数据面板演示\n- 关键词：钴蓝、网格背景、白色卡片、浮层、纵深\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「Cobalt Grid」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底 #14307a 径向渐变到 #1b3a8c（中心稍亮），整体压暗角 12%\n- 网格 1px #3f6ad4、间距 28px，铺满全幅；交叉点画 5px 的小十字（`#5c86e8`，alpha 0.5）\n- 白色卡片 #ffffff、圆角 12px、尺寸 320×200 与 240×150 两种，阴影 `rgba(4,12,40,0.35)` blur 28、y 偏移 14\n- 三张卡片错落：一张居中偏左、一张右上、一张右下，彼此重叠 30-60px\n- 卡片内只有两三个色块和 6px 灰条（#d8dde8），不放照片；卡片上允许压一个 #14307a 的小圆标\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.5s：网格从 4 倍缩放快速拉回到 1 倍（`easeOutQuart`），有种「降落」感\n- 1.5-3s：三张卡片依次从下方 40px 处升起并放大（scale 0.92→1，间隔 0.18s）\n- 3-4.8s：网格持续向左上漂移（12px/s），卡片反向漂移 2px，制造视差\n- 4.8-6s：前景卡片内色块依次亮起，最后整体轻微下沉 4px 定格\n\n技术要点：\n- 网格无限滚动：网格绘制偏移取 `(offset % 28)`，只需重画视口内的线，不要画到画布外\n- 卡片阴影用一次离屏模糊矩形而不是逐卡 `shadowBlur`，三张卡也能稳在 60fps\n- 卡片浮层与网格之间加一层 `rgba(10,25,70,0.18)` 的整幅压暗，纵深立刻出来\n"},{"id":"S2-H9","name":"Coral","series":"S2","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S2-H9.jpg","markdown":"# H9 Coral\n\n- 分类：H 现成版式 · 珊瑚色版式\n- 长什么样：奶油底色上一大块珊瑚红的半圆从底边升起，旁边浮着几个圆和一段拱形；所有边缘都是大圆角，颜色只有珊瑚红、浅珊瑚、奶油三种，没有一条硬边也没有深色，暖得像早晨\n- 适合：母婴与家庭内容、轻食与甜品品牌、活动邀请函、生活方式 vlog 封面\n- 关键词：珊瑚色、奶油底、大圆角、几何抽象、温柔\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「Coral」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底 #fdf6ec 奶油，右下角一团 #ffe9dd、alpha 0.8 的柔光（半径 380）\n- 三色：#ff6f5e（珊瑚）、#e0533f（深珊瑚，仅用于小面积阴影或强调）、#ffd8c9（浅珊瑚）\n- 几何只有四种：半圆（底边升起）、正圆、拱形（圆角矩形 + 顶部全圆）、细横条\n- 主形是一个半径 220 的半圆压在底边中央，上面叠两个 r=40 / r=74 的浅珊瑚圆\n- 圆角一律 ≥ 24px；禁用黑、深灰、纯白，最深只到 #8a5a4e\n- 标题用中粗无衬线，字距 +1px，压在珊瑚半圆上用奶油色反白\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.5s：珊瑚半圆从底边向上生长（scaleY 0→1，以底边为原点，`easeOutCubic`）\n- 1.5-3s：两个圆从半圆内部「冒」出来（半径 0→目标值，间隔 0.15s）\n- 3-4.5s：拱形从右侧滑入，与半圆咬合 20px；细横条随后横穿\n- 4.5-6s：整体轻微呼吸（scale 1→1.02→1），珊瑚色 alpha 到 0.96 定住\n\n技术要点：\n- 半圆用 `ctx.arc(cx, cy, r, Math.PI, 0)` 再 `closePath`，`0` 到 `Math.PI` 才是上半个\n- 以底边为原点缩放要 `ctx.translate(0, H); ctx.scale(1, t); ctx.translate(0, -H)`，否则会从中心弹\n- 色块重叠处不要用 `globalAlpha` 叠（会出脏色），改成预先算好的固定 hex 直接填\n"},{"id":"S2-H10","name":"Creative Mode","series":"S2","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S2-H10.jpg","markdown":"# H10 Creative Mode\n\n- 分类：H 现成版式 · 撞色分区\n- 长什么样：画面被一条 12° 的斜线切成三块撞色区域，黑、橙红、米白各占一块，交界锋利；大字沿着斜线方向倾斜排布，一半压在深色区一半压在浅色区，留白大得近乎挑衅\n- 适合：创意机构提案、音乐节海报、潮流品牌 lookbook、作品集封面\n- 关键词：撞色、斜切、大胆留白、倾斜排版、锋利\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「Creative Mode」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，5 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 三块区域：#1d1d1f（墨黑，占右 40%）、#ff4d2e（橙红，中间斜带 25%）、#f5f0e6（米白，左 35%）\n- 分隔线是 12° 斜线（`tan(12°) ≈ 0.213`），边界必须绝对锐利，不许羽化、不许描边\n- 留白至少 50%：米白块里只放一组小字，其余空着\n- 大字沿 -12° 倾斜，字号 168、字重 900、字距 -4px，横跨三种底色，逐字换色（压黑底用米白，压橙红用黑）\n- 装饰只有两条：一条 3px 黑色斜线、一个 r=52 的实心橙圆\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1s：橙红斜带从右侧滑入（translateX 200→0），先立住画面骨架\n- 1-2.2s：黑块与米白块从上下两侧「夹」进来（scaleY 0→1），45° 斜切边缘咬合\n- 2.2-3.8s：大字逐字从斜线下方升起（每字 translateY 40→0，间隔 0.05s），逐字判定底色\n- 3.8-5s：橙圆从左上弹入并压住一个字，斜线微抖一次，静止\n\n技术要点：\n- 斜块用 `ctx.beginPath()` 四个角点手工算（`x2 = x1 - h*0.213`），别用 `rotate` 画矩形再裁，缝会露白\n- 文字跨色块：先整段画一遍，再用 `clip()` 按色块区域重画对应颜色的那份\n- 逐字换色要按字中心 x 与斜线方程比较，不要按字框左上角，否则边缘字会串色\n"},{"id":"S2-H11","name":"Daisy Days","series":"S2","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S2-H11.jpg","markdown":"# H11 Daisy Days\n\n- 分类：H 现成版式 · 活泼图案\n- 长什么样：奶黄底上铺满一层小雏菊，八片圆瓣围着一个橙色花心，图案很淡像印花布；前面压一块明黄大方块和几片粉色花瓣，字是圆润的粗体，还会轻微上下跳，整体像儿童节横幅\n- 适合：儿童与亲子内容、甜品店活动、生日会邀请、社媒节日贴\n- 关键词：雏菊、明黄粉、图案平铺、活泼、圆润\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「Daisy Days」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底 #fff8e7 暖白，叠雏菊 pattern：一格 110px，花心 #ffb703、花瓣 #ffe08a、alpha 0.35，旋转 ±8° 随机\n- 前景主色 #ffd93d（明黄）、#ff9eb5（粉）、#7fb069（叶绿），饱和度拉满，不用灰调\n- 雏菊画法：8 片椭圆花瓣（rx 11 / ry 19）绕心均分 45°，圆心画 r=9 实心 #ffb703 + r=4 深橙点\n- 主区块：一块 520×300 的明黄圆角矩形（半径 28），四角各贴一片粉色花瓣探出边界\n- 字体圆润无衬线（如 汉仪润圆/思源柔黑），字重 700，字号 92，字距 2px，颜色 #4a3b2a\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.2s：雏菊 background 从边缘向中心「长」出来（遮罩圆形扩张，半径 0→画面对角）\n- 1.2-2.6s：明黄主块从下方弹入（scale 0.7→1.08→1），四片花瓣延迟 0.08s 依次探出\n- 2.6-4.2s：标题逐字跳上（每字 translateY 26→0，overshoot 12%，间隔 0.06s）\n- 4.2-6s：背景雏菊缓慢整体右移 10px，主块两次轻微「呼吸」，收尾\n\n技术要点：\n- 平铺图案别每帧现画：离屏 canvas 画 110×110 一格，`createPattern(...,'repeat')` 后 `fillRect` 一次\n- 花瓣阵列用 `ctx.save(); ctx.rotate(a); ctx.ellipse(0, -24, 11, 19, 0, 0, PI*2); ctx.restore()`，绕原点转才不会散\n- 「跳」用 `easeOutBounce` 会太弹，`easeOutBack` 配 0.6s 时长最像卡通字\n"},{"id":"S2-H12","name":"Editorial Forest","series":"S2","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S2-H12.jpg","markdown":"# H12 Editorial Forest\n\n- 分类：H 现成版式 · 编辑排版\n- 长什么样：米色纸上一块深森林绿的实心图块，旁边是超大的衬线标题，字距拉得很开、行距慷慨，标题上下各一条 0.75px 的米金细线；右下角一小段灰绿小字，整页留白占七成，像一本生活方式杂志的内页\n- 适合：品牌故事片、独立出版物、酒店与民宿宣传、慢生活类内容\n- 关键词：森林绿、衬线大字、编辑排版、留白、米金细线\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「Editorial Forest」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，8 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 纸底 #f2ede2，左边缘压 3% 的 #e0d8c6 纵向渐变，模拟书页装订侧的阴影\n- 主色 #1f3d2b（深森林绿）与 #2f5d43（中绿），辅助 #c8b89a（米金细线）、#8a9a86（灰绿小字）\n- 左侧 42% 是一块纯 #1f3d2b 实心矩形（不带图，只放一枚米金色细线框），右侧是文字区\n- 标题衬线字体，字号 118、字重 400（不要加粗）、字距 +4px、行距 1.05，最多两行\n- 标题上方 0.75px 米金横线，长度 = 标题宽度；下方再一条，等宽，间距 28px\n- 右下一段灰绿小字（16px，行距 1.7，三行），左侧空出 30% 不排字\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.5s：左侧绿块自下而上「抽」起（scaleY 0→1，以底边为原点）\n- 1.5-3.5s：标题第一行逐字从右侧 12px 处滑入并淡入，第二行延迟 0.5s\n- 3.5-5s：两条米金细线分别从左右两端向中间生长至标题宽度\n- 5-6.5s：右下小字逐行淡入（每行 0.2s），绿块内细线框浮现\n- 6.5-8s：整体极缓右移 6px，画面定住，像翻页后的停顿\n\n技术要点：\n- 衬线字体栈写 `'Georgia', 'Songti SC', 'SimSun', serif`，中文回退到宋体才有「编辑感」\n- 细线生长用 `scaleX` 会连线宽一起压缩，改成按宽度重绘 `fillRect(y, 0, w*t, 0.75)`\n- 逐字滑入要先把每个字的 x 坐标预算好（`measureText` 累加），不能靠 `textAlign` 逐字画\n"},{"id":"S3-K1","name":"扁平矢量","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K1.jpg","markdown":"# K1 扁平矢量\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 画面风格\n- 长什么样：纯色块拼接，无描边无渐变，人物几何化（圆头、矩形身体、圆柱四肢），大留白，配色控制在 3-4 个饱和色\n- 适合：产品功能说明、信息图、企业宣传片、SaaS 介绍\n- 关键词：扁平、色块、无描边、几何、大留白\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「扁平矢量」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6-8 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 背景米白 #fbf7f0（93% 面积留白）\n- 主色 #1f3a5f（深蓝），辅色 #e8734a（珊瑚橙）、#f2b544（芥黄），最多 4 色\n- 所有图形无描边、无阴影、无渐变，纯 fillRect / arc / roundRect\n- 人物一律几何化：圆头（r=14）+ 圆角矩形身体 + 两条细矩形腿，不要五官细节\n- 元素之间用「位移 + 缩放」的位移，不要旋转超过 15 度\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.5s：三个色块从右侧滑入（easeOutCubic），堆叠成「场景基底」\n- 1.5-3.5s：几何小人从色块后升起（scaleY 0→1，overshoot 8%），同时橙色圆点从左上弹入\n- 3.5-5.5s：关键信息卡片从底部上浮（translateY 40→0），卡片圆角 10px\n- 5.5-7s：整组元素向右平移 8% 退场，留白收尾\n\n技术要点：\n- 缓动用 easeOutCubic：`1 - Math.pow(1 - t, 3)`\n- 所有元素坐标用 9:16 安全区内边距 48px\n- 杜绝 drop-shadow 和 filter，扁平风一旦加阴影就破功\n"},{"id":"S3-K2","name":"Q 版","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K2.jpg","markdown":"# K2 Q 版\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 角色风格\n- 长什么样：二头身小人，头占身体 55%，圆脸大眼（黑底加两颗白高光）；豆形身子、短香肠四肢，配色是奶粉、雾蓝、奶油黄\n- 适合：个人 IP、表情包、App 引导页、口播配图\n- 关键词：Q 版、二头身、马卡龙、大眼、弹跳\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「Q 版」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #fdf6ec，地面阴影 #e8dccb 椭圆（rx 90 / ry 14）\n- 主色 #ffd9e2、#a8d8ea、#ffe6a7，单帧不超 3 色\n- 头 r=150，身体两段贝塞尔成豆形，四肢 lineWidth 22 短管\n- 眼睛 #2b2b2b 加两颗 #ffffff 高光，腮红 #ffb3c1 alpha 0.35\n- 统一 3px #3d3a37 描边，不加投影渐变\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.2s：从上方落入，触地压扁 12% 再回弹\n- 1.2-3.2s：1.4s 周期呼吸，每 2.4s 眨一次眼（90ms）\n- 3.2-4.6s：抬手挥动，前臂绕肘 -35°→+15° 摆两次\n- 4.6-6s：头顶错峰弹出三颗小爱心，轻微左右晃\n\n技术要点：\n- 回弹用 easeOutBack：`1 + 2.2*(t-1)^3 + 1.2*(t-1)^2`\n- 眨眼用同色矩形下扫 + `ctx.clip()`，别缩放眼睛\n- 四肢 `lineCap='round'`，关节补同色圆，接缝才看不出\n"},{"id":"S3-K3","name":"卡哇伊","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K3.jpg","markdown":"# K3 卡哇伊\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 角色风格\n- 长什么样：奶油粉的团子生物，两只黑豆眼、V 形小嘴、椭圆腮红，头顶飘四角星；全图没有一根直角\n- 适合：贴纸包、表情包、儿童内容、软萌产品介绍\n- 关键词：卡哇伊、团子、豆豆眼、腮红、星星\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「卡哇伊」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #fff3f6，正中一轮 #ffe3ea 柔光圆（r 380，alpha 0.6）\n- 主色 #ffc2d4，辅 #ff8fb1\n- 主体三团叠圆：身体 r=150、双耳 r=55，无描边，只勾 2px 内轮廓\n- 腮红 radialGradient #ff9db8 alpha 0.5→0，半径 46\n- 五官只有豆豆眼（r 11）和 12px 弧线嘴，不画眉毛\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.4s：从底部挤出（scaleY 0→1，easeOutElastic）\n- 1.4-3.4s：果冻摇摆 -4°→+4°（周期 1.2s），双耳错峰 0.2s\n- 3.4-4.8s：头顶 5 颗四角星依次闪耀（scale 0→1→0，各 0.5s）\n- 4.8-6s：豆豆眼变 ^ ^ 笑眼，腮红 alpha 0.5→0.8\n\n技术要点：\n- 果冻用 `ctx.transform` 叠 scale(1+s, 1-s)，s = `sin(t*4)*0.04`\n- 四角星用 8 点 path，内半径 = 外半径×0.28，填 #fff6a8\n- 柔光用离屏 canvas 加 `blur(30px)` 再贴回\n"},{"id":"S3-K4","name":"极简线条小人","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K4.jpg","markdown":"# K4 极简线条小人\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 角色风格\n- 长什么样：纯白底上 2.5px 黑线画的人——圆头、一道弧背、两条直腿、两条摆臂，没有五官没有衣服；脚下一条地平线，其余全空\n- 适合：概念讲解、情绪隐喻、极简片头、播客片头\n- 关键词：单线、极简、留白、黑白、走路循环\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「极简线条小人」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #ffffff，不铺纹理，内容只占中下三分之一\n- 唯一色 #1a1a1a，lineWidth 2.5，`lineCap`/`lineJoin` 均 round\n- 头 r=26，躯干一条颈到胯的二次贝塞尔，四肢各一条直线，总高 240px\n- 地平线在 y=620、长 200px、两端渐隐\n- 地平线下压一行手写体短句（22px，字距 4），此外不加元素\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.6s：逐笔描出（头→躯干→腿→臂，每笔 0.35s）\n- 1.6-4.6s：走路循环，髋部起伏 6px，双腿 ±18°，双臂反相 ±22°\n- 4.6-5.6s：向右走出画面（translateX +40%），只留地平线\n- 5.6-6s：地平线向两端缩到 0，纯白收尾\n\n技术要点：\n- 描线用 `setLineDash` + `lineDashOffset` 递减，长度先 `getTotalLength()` 量\n- 走路靠相位：`legA = sin(phase)*0.32`，另一条取反相\n- 全程不 fill 人物，一旦填色就不是线条小人\n"},{"id":"S3-K5","name":"孟菲斯扁平人","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K5.jpg","markdown":"# K5 孟菲斯扁平人\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 角色风格\n- 长什么样：几个无脸小人，头是纯色圆，身体是圆角矩形，手臂细如面条、末端接夸张大手掌；背景飘半圆、波浪线、圆点阵和 Z 形折线\n- 适合：企业宣传、团队协作、招聘页、SaaS 说明\n- 关键词：Corporate Memphis、无脸、面条手臂、大手掌、漂浮几何\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「孟菲斯扁平人」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #f3efe7，全部元素无描边无阴影\n- 主色 #3b6ea5、#e2703a、#7fae8b、#f0c05a，肤色 #f2c6a0\n- 头 r=34 无五官，躯干 88×120 圆角矩形（r=40），手掌 r=16\n- 漂浮物四类：半圆 r 60、波浪线 lineWidth 8、直径 18 圆点、Z 形折线\n- 三人错落三个高度，刻意打破对称\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.2s：三个漂浮物从左下错峰滑入（间隔 0.15s）\n- 1.2-3s：三个人物从底部升起（easeOutCubic），落点不同高\n- 3-5.5s：漂浮物独立漂浮——半圆自转、圆点 ±12px、波浪线横移\n- 5.5-7s：中间那人抬臂指向，手掌放大到 1.15 倍，定住\n\n技术要点：\n- 漂浮统一 `y = base + sin(t*speed + phase) * amp`，每个元素相位不同\n- 面条手臂用二次贝塞尔，控制点抬高做弧度，`lineCap='round'`\n- 圆角矩形用 `roundRect`，半径给到高度一半才像人体\n"},{"id":"S3-K6","name":"清线漫画","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K6.jpg","markdown":"# K6 清线漫画\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 叙事漫画\n- 长什么样：所有物体同一种粗细的墨线（2px）、内部平涂纯色，没有渐变没有阴影；淡蓝天配大块土黄地，一条透视小路收向远处的房子和山\n- 适合：冒险故事、旅行叙事、品牌分镜、儿童科普\n- 关键词：清线、均匀线宽、平涂、透视、丁丁历险记\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「清线漫画」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 上方 60% #d9e8ef 淡天蓝，下方 40% #e9d8b4 土黄地面\n- 墨线 #2b2b2b，全画面统一 lineWidth 2，远近不变，这是清线的铁律\n- 平涂色 #7fa9c4 远山、#e8c07d 房墙、#d96b5a 屋顶\n- 一条从左下通往右上的路，两条透视线收于 y=300；云为三个相切圆弧\n- 禁止渐变、投影、模糊和半透明叠色\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.5s：空镜，云自右向左平移（18px/s），天空先铺满\n- 1.5-3.5s：横向推轨（translateX 0→-120px），带出房子和路\n- 3.5-5.5s：小车沿透视线驶近（scale 0.35→1，y 300→560）\n- 5.5-7s：车停，烟囱冒出三个依次放大的白圆\n\n技术要点：\n- 相机统一 `ctx.translate(-camX, 0)`，lineWidth 用 `2/scale` 抵消缩放\n- 云用三个相切 `arc` 后 `closePath` 连成一体，只描外轮廓\n- 平涂一次 `fill` 到位，`fillStyle` 只许纯 hex\n"},{"id":"S3-K7","name":"日式动画","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K7.jpg","markdown":"# K7 日式动画\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 赛璐璐动画\n- 长什么样：黄昏逆光侧脸，眼睛占脸三分之一、内有上下两层高光；头发是深色硬边尖块，朝光一侧勾一圈暖橙轮廓光；背景橙到紫的竖渐变加光斑\n- 适合：情绪短片、角色 PV、番剧片头、游戏宣传\n- 关键词：赛璐璐、大眼、逆光、轮廓光、分镜\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「日式动画」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 天空 #ffb26b→#6b4f7c 竖向渐变，地平线压 #2e2a3a 剪影（下 22%）\n- 发 #2e2a3a、肤 #ffe0c8、衣 #3f4a6b；阴影用本色降明度 15% 的纯色块\n- 眼白 #ffffff、虹膜 #6c8fc7、瞳孔 #1d1d28、高光两颗（r 9 / r 4）\n- 逆光轮廓光：朝光一侧描 3px #ffcf8a，alpha 0.9\n- 补 4-6 个 #ffd9a0 光斑（r 6-16）与两道斜向镜头光\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.5s：黑场淡入，镜头从 scale 1.08 缓推到 1.0\n- 1.5-4s：头发衣角 2s 周期飘，2.2s 与 3.4s 各眨一次眼\n- 4-5.5s：缓慢转头 6°，轮廓光变亮（0.9→1），光斑横移\n- 5.5-7s：一道横向高光扫过（alpha 0.18，0.8s），定格\n\n技术要点：\n- 阴影用 `ctx.clip()` 裁区再填纯色，绝不用 `shadowBlur`\n- 头发由 5-8 个尖角三角块拼成、尖端朝外，这是辨识点\n- 推近用 `ctx.scale(s,s)` 后按中心平移\n"},{"id":"S3-K8","name":"绘本","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K8.jpg","markdown":"# K8 绘本\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 叙事插画\n- 长什么样：圆到几乎没有棱角的角色和树，每个形状外面留一圈奶白空隙；奶茶色系配鼠尾草绿；纸面铺细密颗粒，像印在粗糙绘本纸上\n- 适合：儿童故事、亲子内容、温暖品牌片、睡前故事\n- 关键词：绘本、圆润、纸纹、奶白间隙、暖调\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「绘本」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #f6ecd9 旧纸米色，叠 3000 个 1px 随机点颗粒（alpha 0.05）\n- 主色 #e8a87c、#85b7a0、#d9c27e、#c98b7a\n- 套印留白：先用 #f6ecd9 描 8px 边再填色，形与形之间自然留空\n- 头 r 70 + 梯形身体，眼睛两个 #4a3f35 小点；树冠三层圆叠成云朵\n- 地平线 y=520，近大远小，允许一棵树出血裁掉一半\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-2s：远山与天空淡入（1.2s），纸纹同时显现\n- 2-3.6s：树从根部向上长（scaleY 0→1，6% 回弹），树叶逐个弹出\n- 3.6-5.4s：小人从右侧走进来，身体 3° 摆，头发一颠一颠\n- 5.4-7s：整幅上下浮动 ±4px，四角渐暗 8%，收尾\n\n技术要点：\n- 颗粒预生成离屏 canvas 用 `createPattern` 复用，每帧重算会闪\n- 套印留白靠「粗描底色→填充→补一圈本色」，不是加描边线\n- 柔和感来自 `globalAlpha` 与圆角，不要用 blur，边是实的\n"},{"id":"S3-K9","name":"涂鸦","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K9.jpg","markdown":"# K9 涂鸦\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 手绘质感\n- 长什么样：白纸上蓝色圆珠笔画的歪扭小人和箭头，线条抖动、有反复描过的加深，旁边留着没擦的辅助线和涂改圈；两三处红马克笔压在字上\n- 适合：自嘲式口播、「以前的我」叙事、随手科普、社群内容\n- 关键词：涂鸦、圆珠笔、抖动线、草稿感、涂改圈\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「涂鸦」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #fbfbf7 纸白，可叠 #efece2 铅笔格（间距 48，alpha 0.35）\n- 主笔 #2b3a67 圆珠笔蓝，lineWidth 2，同一笔画叠 2 遍形成加深\n- 强调色 #d1495b 红马克笔（lineWidth 7，alpha 0.55）\n- 路径顶点加 ±1.6px 低频噪声抖动，同笔画内必须连续\n- 必备：一个手绘箭头、两个不闭合的圈、一处划掉的横线\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-2s：小人从头被画出，一个 r=4 墨点当笔尖跟着走\n- 2-4s：三个关键词依次写出，第二个写歪后被横线划掉\n- 4-5.6s：红马克笔横扫关键词，alpha 0→0.55\n- 5.6-7s：箭头指向关键词（0.4s），尖端补两笔短须，静止\n\n技术要点：\n- 抖动用固定种子的一维值噪声：`jitter = noise(i*0.35) * 1.6`\n- 描线生长用 `lineDashOffset` 递减，配跟随圆点模拟笔尖\n- 马克笔用 `globalCompositeOperation='multiply'`，才像压在字上\n"},{"id":"S3-K10","name":"粉笔黑板","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K10.jpg","markdown":"# K10 粉笔黑板\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 手绘质感\n- 长什么样：墨绿黑板上彩色粉笔画的示意图，白粉笔写标题、黄粉笔圈重点、青粉笔拉箭头；笔画边缘是毛的、带粉笔灰，板面还留着没擦净的旧痕\n- 适合：知识科普、课堂讲解、道理输出、教程步骤\n- 关键词：黑板、粉笔、颗粒、手写、擦痕\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「粉笔黑板」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #22372f 墨绿，四角叠径向渐变 #1a2a24（alpha 0.5）\n- 粉笔色 #f2f0e6、#f7d154、#8fd3c7、#f08a7a，统一 alpha 0.9\n- lineWidth 4、笔画圆头；只勾轮廓不填充，粉笔填不出实色\n- 每条线重绘 3 遍，每遍偏移 ±0.8px、alpha 0.35，堆出毛边\n- 背景叠 3 道斜向擦痕（#3a4f47，alpha 0.25）\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1s：黑板出现，30 个粉笔灰小点从上方落完\n- 1-3.5s：标题逐字写出（每字 0.12s），写完抖一下补一笔\n- 3.5-5.5s：黄粉笔画两道下划线，再圈住关键词（1.2s）\n- 5.5-7s：青粉笔拉出三条箭头指向三个小图标，整体变暗\n\n技术要点：\n- 毛边靠多遍偏移重绘，不是 blur；alpha 0.35 重描同一路径最像粉笔\n- 写字用短线段拼笔画（每字 2-4 段），`fillText` 整字会太干净\n- 落灰用 30 个小圆，各有不同下落速度与水平漂移\n"},{"id":"S3-K11","name":"手账笔记","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K11.jpg","markdown":"# K11 手账笔记\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 手绘质感\n- 长什么样：米黄横线本上贴着歪三度的黄色便利贴，重点行被荧光笔划过，两段格子胶带按住卡片；字是手写体，箭头星号到处都是\n- 适合：方法清单、步骤教学、读书笔记、周计划\n- 关键词：手账、横线本、便利贴、胶带、荧光笔\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「手账笔记」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #fffdf5，横线 #cfd8e3（间距 44），左侧 #e8a0a8 装订线（x=96）\n- 便利贴 #ffe08a，181×181，旋转 -3°，右下角一个折三角\n- 胶带 #f6c95c alpha 0.55，34×120，两端锯齿撕边\n- 荧光笔 #ffd84d alpha 0.45、高 30；字 #3b3b3b、26px\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.2s：横线逐条画出（每条延迟 0.02s），装订线后到\n- 1.2-2.6s：便利贴落下，落位后摆三次（-3°→+1°→-3°）\n- 2.6-4.6s：四行文字逐行写出，每行末尾跟一个小勾\n- 4.6-6s：荧光笔横扫第二四行，胶带「啪」地贴上\n- 6-7s：整页上浮 6px、阴影加深，定格\n\n技术要点：\n- 便利贴用 `shadowColor=rgba(0,0,0,.18)`、`shadowBlur=12`、`shadowOffsetY=6`\n- 胶带锯齿用 12 段 `lineTo` 交替 ±6px，再 `globalAlpha=0.55`\n- 荧光笔用 `multiply` 混合，压上去仍看得清字\n"},{"id":"S3-K12","name":"报纸四格","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K12.jpg","markdown":"# K12 报纸四格\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 叙事漫画\n- 长什么样：泛黄新闻纸上 2×2 四格黑白漫画，每格 3px 黑框；人物是圆头火柴人，阴影靠 45° 交叉排线，格底压一条手写台词，顶上还有衬线刊头\n- 适合：小段子、四格笑话、讽刺短剧、日常吐槽\n- 关键词：报纸、四格、黑白、交叉排线、刊头\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「报纸四格」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #efe9dc 旧报纸，叠横向细纹（间距 3，alpha 0.04）\n- 唯一色 #1b1b1b，四格边框 lineWidth 3，格间距 14\n- 每格 380×250，内容超出部分 `clip` 掉\n- 阴影一律 45° 交叉排线（间距 7，lineWidth 1），不用灰阶\n- 顶部刊头 Georgia 34px，台词框白底黑框\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.2s：刊头与四条分隔线依次压印（每 0.2s 一条）\n- 1.2-3s：四格按左上→右上→左下→右下出现，先出框再出内容\n- 3-5.5s：只让第三格动——小人抬手、冒三个感叹号，其余静止\n- 5.5-7s：镜头平移到第四格（translateX -190），台词框弹出\n\n技术要点：\n- 每格先 `save()` + `rect` + `clip()`，画完 `restore()`\n- 排线用 for 循环画 45° 平行线，间距 7，靠 `rotate(-Math.PI/4)` 后还原\n- 泛黄靠整幅叠一层 `rgba(214,196,160,0.18)`，别逐个改元素颜色\n"},{"id":"S3-K13","name":"美漫网点","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K13.jpg","markdown":"# K13 美漫网点\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 叙事漫画\n- 长什么样：粗到 6px 的黑描边，形体内是纯色块加网点阴影；背景从中心放射速度线，分镜用斜切，一个爆炸状拟声词砸在画面上\n- 适合：卖点强调、反转瞬间、体育竞技、吐槽讽刺\n- 关键词：美漫、粗描边、网点、速度线、拟声词\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「美漫网点」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #fffdf8，主色块只有 #e63946、#ffd93d、#2b59c3\n- 所有形体统一 lineWidth 6 的 #111111 描边，无渐变\n- 阴影用网点：圆点 r 随距离 3→1 递减、间距 8，alpha 0.75\n- 背景速度线从中心放射 60 条，长 200-420，lineWidth 2\n- 整体允许倾斜 -8°，分镜对角线切分\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1s：黑描边从中心炸开（每块 scale 0→1，错峰 0.06s）\n- 1-2.2s：速度线以 6 帧为周期闪现（opacity 0/0.9）\n- 2.2-4s：主体硬切两拍（第 30、48 帧各跳一次姿势）\n- 4-5.5s：爆炸形拟声词从 2.2 倍缩到 1，配网点阴影\n- 5.5-6s：叠一帧 alpha 0.25 闪光，结束\n\n技术要点：\n- 网点别用图片，双循环 `arc` 打点，先按形体 `clip` 再打才整齐\n- 硬切用 `Math.floor(t*fps/24)` 分档，同档保持同一姿势\n- 拟声词用粗衬线体 + 8px 黑描边 + 4px 白描边，字歪 8°\n"},{"id":"S3-K14","name":"波普","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K14.jpg","markdown":"# K14 波普\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 平面风格\n- 长什么样：同一张脸 2×2 重复四次，每次换一套高饱和配色；像丝网印一样平、一样粗描边；背景铺均匀大圆点，右下角一个气泡对话框\n- 适合：观点强调、系列封面、社交卡片、复古广告梗\n- 关键词：波普、重复、丝网印、对话框、撞色\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「波普」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #ffffff，四格各 480×270，格间留 12px 白缝\n- 四套配色：①#ff2d55/#ffd400 ②#00a6fb/#ffffff ③#ffd400/#1a1a1a ④#7b2ff7/#00e5a0\n- 主体是同一张正面脸（椭圆脸、圆眼、张嘴），5px 黑描边\n- 背景圆点 #000000 alpha 0.12、r 16、间距 44\n- 印刷错版：再叠一层 #00a6fb 轮廓，偏移 (+4,-4)，`multiply`\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.6s：四格依次出现（各 0.18s），错版偏移 12px→4px\n- 1.6-3.4s：四张嘴同步开合（0.4s 一周期）\n- 3.4-5s：对话框从右下弹出（scale 0→1.15→1），尾巴指向第四格\n- 5-6s：叠一帧红色闪光，定住\n\n技术要点：\n- 错版靠两层同路径不同 `fillStyle` + `translate(±4)`，上层设 `multiply`\n- 四格共用 `drawFace(ctx, palette)`，只换色才严格重复\n- 圆点先铺满整幅再逐格 `clip`，别逐格重算坐标\n"},{"id":"S3-K15","name":"橡皮管 1930","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K15.jpg","markdown":"# K15 橡皮管 1930\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 复古动画\n- 长什么样：黑白老动画里的小人，四肢是没有关节的橡皮管、末端接白手套白鞋；眼睛是两个黑饼形圆里挖一道白楔；画面盖着胶片颗粒、划痕和抖动\n- 适合：复古开场、老片致敬、幽默桥段、怀旧品牌片\n- 关键词：橡皮管、白手套、饼形眼、老胶片、复古\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「橡皮管 1930」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #f2ead9 旧胶片米白，叠 0.06 颗粒 + 3 道竖划痕\n- 唯一色 #1a1a1a，线条 lineWidth 6、`lineCap='round'`，不做灰阶\n- 四肢是从肩胯出发的两段贝塞尔，中途不加任何关节圆点\n- 白手套 = 手掌 r 18 加三个 r 10 指头圆，2px 描边\n- 眼睛：黑饼圆 r 22 里挖 60° 白楔，没有瞳孔\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.2s：上下遮幅黑边先合再开，画面开始抖\n- 1.2-4s：原地走路（两腿交替成 8 字，每步 0.45s），身体弹 10px\n- 4-5.6s：转身跳（旋转 -360°，起跳压扁再拉长），落地抖 2px\n- 5.6-7s：一只白手套伸进画面挥两下，随后闪两帧黑屏\n\n技术要点：\n- 胶片抖动每帧整幅 `translate(rand(-1.2,1.2), rand(-1.2,1.2))`，固定种子\n- 橡皮管四肢必须一笔贝塞尔画完，中途画关节就变现代卡通\n- 颗粒用预生成噪点 pattern，每 3 帧换偏移（`pattern.setTransform`）\n"},{"id":"S3-K16","name":"中世纪现代","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K16.jpg","markdown":"# K16 中世纪现代\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 平面风格\n- 长什么样：奶油底上几个有机几何形——回力镖、肾形、星爆、分子球棍，颜色只有铁锈红、芥末黄、墨绿、炭黑；带纸颗粒和轻微套色不准，像 1958 年的广告\n- 适合：品牌故事、复古产品介绍、家居咖啡主题、片头设计\n- 关键词：中世纪现代、有机几何、星爆、四色、纸颗粒\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「中世纪现代」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6.5 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #f0e2c8 奶油，叠纸颗粒（alpha 0.05）与四角微暗\n- 四色封顶：#c1442e、#2f4f4a、#d99b2b、#3b3b3b\n- 形状只允许四种：回力镖、肾形、8 尖星爆、分子球棍\n- 构图沿一条 12° 隐形斜轴错落排列，不做居中对称\n- 标题无衬线大写 + 字距 6px，压在实心色块上\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.4s：三个形状沿 12° 斜轴滑入（错峰 0.2s，easeOutQuart）\n- 1.4-3.2s：星爆放大到 1.1 倍再落回 1，同时自转 15°\n- 3.2-5s：标题色块从左拉起（scaleX 0→1），文字逐字浮出\n- 5-6.5s：所有形状沿斜轴漂移 -8px，定住，颗粒微闪\n\n技术要点：\n- 回力镖：`moveTo` 后 `bezierCurveTo` 画外弧，再反向画内弧，半径差 28\n- 纸颗粒预生成 pattern，alpha 0.05 铺满，别逐帧算噪声\n- 套色不准：色块边缘叠一层同形 #ffffff alpha 0.1、偏移 2px\n"},{"id":"S3-K17","name":"几何","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K17.jpg","markdown":"# K17 几何\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 平面风格\n- 长什么样：深色底上只用圆、方、三角拼出的结构，形与形严格咬合、之间留 8px 均匀缝隙；所有旋转都是 15° 的倍数，填充只有橙红与暖黄\n- 适合：系统架构、结构类比、抽象概念可视化、科技片头\n- 关键词：几何、三原形、咬合、15度网格、深底\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「几何」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6.5 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #101319，参考网格 #2a2f3a（间距 40，alpha 0.35）\n- 三色：#f2f2f2、#ff5d3a、#ffd166，单帧最多两色填充\n- 只允许 circle / square / triangle，任何曲线都不许出现\n- 全部按 8px 网格对齐，旋转角 ∈ {0,15,30,45,60,75,90}\n- 形与形留 8px 缝隙，靠「挖」不靠描边分隔\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.6s：12 个形状从画外随机方向飞入，停在偏离目标 60px 处\n- 1.6-3.6s：依次咬合到位（每 0.1s 一个，easeOutBack 1.4）\n- 3.6-5.2s：整组绕中心转 15°，每转一格停 0.15s，像步进电机\n- 5.2-6.5s：一个三角形换成圆形（交叉淡入 0.3s），结构可变\n\n技术要点：\n- 缝隙别用描边，用 `destination-out` 沿边挖 4px 沟\n- 旋转要量化 `Math.round(angle/15)*Math.PI/180`，连续转会丢机械感\n- 三角形用等边顶点公式，边长取 40 的倍数\n"},{"id":"S3-K18","name":"水彩","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K18.jpg","markdown":"# K18 水彩\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 绘画质感\n- 长什么样：淡到几乎透明的灰蓝、藕粉、灰绿在纸上化开，每块颜色边缘积着一圈更深的水痕；形状没有硬边，叠色处自然变深，纸白本身就是构图\n- 适合：情绪片段、回忆叙事、治愈内容、散文配图\n- 关键词：水彩、湿边、罩染、纸白、透明\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「水彩」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7.5 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色纸白 #fbfaf6，叠 4000 个极淡噪点（alpha 0.04）模拟纸纹\n- 主色 #8fb8d8、#d9a7a0、#a8c6a1、#f0d9a8，起手 alpha 0.55\n- 每块色形外圈 6-10px 深色湿边（同色降明度 20%，alpha 0.4），内部变淡\n- 叠色一律 `multiply`，交叠处自然加深；留白处绝不填色\n- 轮廓「不闭合」：每块至少留 30-60px 缺口，让颜色像流出去\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-2.2s：一团灰蓝从中心晕开（半径 0→260，模糊 40→8）\n- 2.2-4.2s：藕粉与灰绿从左右渗入，交叠处加深\n- 4.2-5.6s：两三道淡墨竖线浮现，只勾轮廓不填，暗示山或树\n- 5.6-7.5s：整幅变干——湿边 alpha 0.4→0.25，透明度降 10%\n\n技术要点：\n- 晕开用离屏 canvas + `ctx.filter='blur(Npx)'`，N 从 40 降到 8\n- 湿边是「描一圈同色深版 + 内部渐变」，不要用 shadow 模拟\n- 纸纹永远在最上层：先画色，再叠纸纹，alpha 0.06\n"},{"id":"S3-K19","name":"厚涂","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K19.jpg","markdown":"# K19 厚涂\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 绘画质感\n- 长什么样：暗底暖光，形体由大量短促斜向笔触堆出来，亮面是几笔近乎白的厚颜料、暗面是深红褐，还掺一点冷青做反光；边缘不精细，笔触间能看见底色\n- 适合：情绪高点、人物特写、电影感片头、专辑封面\n- 关键词：厚涂、笔触、暗调、暖光、高饱和\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「厚涂」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7.5 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #241d18 深褐，主体背后一圈 #f2a03d 暖光晕（alpha 0.5）\n- 四色：#f2a03d、#d9553f、#4a6d7c（暗部反光）、#f6e3c1\n- 形体靠 200-400 条短笔触堆出：长 14-40px、lineWidth 8-18、圆头\n- 笔触角度 35°±25° 随机，越靠亮面越统一\n- 高光区占 12% 面积，只用 #f6e3c1，笔触更短\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-2.4s：暗底先上，暖光晕从中心亮起（alpha 0→0.5）\n- 2.4-4.6s：笔触从暗到亮逐条画上（每秒 80 条），形体显影\n- 4.6-6s：补 30 条高光笔触，同时推镜 scale 1.0→1.06\n- 6-7.5s：暗角加深，一条横向暖光扫过（alpha 0.15），定住\n\n技术要点：\n- 笔触用固定种子一次生成 `{x,y,len,ang,w,color}`，按 index 逐帧揭示\n- 混色靠 `globalAlpha=0.75` 且同位置叠 2-3 笔不同色，才有湿混感\n- 暗角用径向渐变 #000 alpha 0→0.5，别整幅压暗\n"},{"id":"S3-K20","name":"剪纸","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K20.jpg","markdown":"# K20 剪纸\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 纸艺手作\n- 长什么样：整张中国红纸刻出连绵镂空，边缘是刀刻的锯齿与圆角交替，纸背一层浅影托离底色；四周环绕云纹花草，颜色只有红、米白、一点墨黑\n- 适合：节庆片头、非遗民俗讲解、国潮短片、故事动画的转场\n- 关键词：剪纸、镂空、红纸、刀味、对称、窗花\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「剪纸」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #f7f1e3（米白宣纸），叠 opacity 0.02 的纸纤维噪点\n- 主色 #c8102e（中国红）占画面 55%，辅色 #1f1a17（墨黑）只用于点缀与投影\n- 红色形状用「外轮廓 + 内挖孔」一次填充：`ctx.fill('evenodd')`，孔洞只用圆、月牙、花瓣三种\n- 边缘做「刀味」：每 6-8px 插入 ±1.2px 偏移，转角留 1px 直角缺口\n- 红纸下压 #000000、opacity 0.12、偏移 (4,6)、blur 8 的投影，纸才有厚度\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.2s：左右两扇红纸从画外合拢（translateX ±120→0，easeOutQuint），中间留 2px 缝\n- 1.2-4s：镂空从中心向外「刻」出——孔洞半径 0→目标值，easeOutBack 带 6% 过冲，像刀尖顶穿纸面\n- 4-5.6s：纸面轻微旋转 -1.5°→0°，投影同步偏移，像被手抚平\n- 5.6-7s：四周云纹依次亮起（叠加 #ff5a5f、opacity 0.25），静止收尾\n\n技术要点：\n- 挖孔用 `arc` 拼 path 后 `fill('evenodd')`；别用 destination-out，否则投影一起被擦\n- 刀味抖动写成 `jitter(points, amp)`，每条路径每帧用固定 seed，避免乱颤\n- 投影用 `shadowColor`/`shadowBlur`/`shadowOffsetX` 一次设好，画完立刻清零\n"},{"id":"S3-K21","name":"手工纸艺","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K21.jpg","markdown":"# K21 手工纸艺\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 纸艺手作\n- 长什么样：四五层不同颜色的卡纸叠成一层风景，每层边缘是手工剪的微抖曲线，层间投下柔和斜影；纸面有纤维颗粒和一点压痕，颜色是低饱和的苔绿、姜黄、陶土\n- 适合：儿童科普、绘本叙事、自然主题、温暖向品牌片\n- 关键词：卡纸、层叠、纸纤维、柔影、手作、低饱和\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「手工纸艺」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7.5 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #efe6d4 卡纸，主色 #6b8f71 苔绿、#d9a441 姜黄、#b5603f 陶土，四层从远到近明度递减 8%\n- 每层边缘用「手工剪」曲线：以 12px 步长沿 y 抖动 ±3px，用 `quadraticCurveTo` 平滑\n- 每层投影统一方向：offset (6,10)、blur 14、黑色 alpha 0.18；越靠前的层投影越大\n- 纸纤维：每层内部撒 300 条长 0.5-1px 的短线，颜色比本层暗 6%、opacity 0.15，这就是手作感\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-2s：最远层先落位，其余四层间隔 0.35s 依次落下（translateY -80→0，easeOutBounce，振幅逐层减小）\n- 2-4.5s：每层落地压扁 4%（scaleY 0.96→1，0.18s），投影同步从 0 扩散到全尺寸\n- 4.5-6s：太阳圆片从最远层后面升起，光晕用 radialGradient，半径 0→220\n- 6-7.5s：前景草丛轻轻摆动（每根 rotate ±3°，周期 1.6s，相位错开）\n\n技术要点：\n- 落纸用标准四段式 `easeOutBounce`（`t<1/2.75 ? 7.5625*t*t : ...`），落地后停 2 帧再进下一层\n- 投影不要用 filter，`shadowBlur = 14` 时性能最好，且能跟随后续旋转一起变\n- 纤维噪点预生成到离屏 canvas，每帧只 `drawImage` 一次，别逐帧随机\n"},{"id":"S3-K22","name":"折纸","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K22.jpg","markdown":"# K22 折纸\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 纸艺手作\n- 长什么样：一块纯色或双色的纸被折成棱线分明的多面体，每个面只有一种平涂色，靠明度差表达朝向；折痕比纸面稍深，纸角微翘，翻面时露出另一种颜色\n- 适合：讲「变化 / 转化 / 折叠」的概念、极简产品片、几何科普、段落转场\n- 关键词：折纸、折痕、多面体、平面着色、单色、棱线\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「折纸」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，8 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #fdfaf3 暖白，纸面主色 #3f6fa8 蓝，背面 #d94f3d 朱红（翻面时露出来）\n- 每个三角面按法线算明度：`shade = 0.55 + 0.45 * |dot(n, light)|`，同色相只改亮度，绝不加渐变\n- 折痕用 #24313f、lineWidth 1、opacity 0.45 的实线，只画「凸折」，凹折不画\n- 纸角 2px 圆角，整体造型只允许 8-12 个面，面一多就不像折纸了\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.5s：一张平铺的纸从上方落下，带轻微摆动（rotate ±4°，逐渐衰减）\n- 1.5-4.5s：对折三次——每次用 scaleX 1→0.5 制造「压平」，翻面瞬间换色，棱线同时亮一下\n- 4.5-6s：纸鹤 / 多面体展开成形，每个面依次向外翻 15° 再回位（easeOutElastic）\n- 6-8s：整体绕 y 轴缓慢旋转 25°，露出背面朱红，静止收尾\n\n技术要点：\n- 面的排序用画家算法：按面中心 z 从小到大排，每帧重排一次，不要试图模拟 depth buffer\n- 折痕用 `ctx.setLineDash([])` 画实线；折叠中间态用 `ctx.transform` 做非等比缩放最省事\n- 平面着色必须硬填色，禁止 `createLinearGradient`，一加渐变就变成普通 3D\n"},{"id":"S3-K23","name":"拼贴","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K23.jpg","markdown":"# K23 拼贴\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 混合材料\n- 长什么样：牛皮灰底板上钉着十几块剪报碎片——报纸白、黑条、红块、蓝照片，边是手撕的毛边，每块歪 2-3°，接缝贴着半透明胶带；信息是碎的、重叠的、故意对不齐\n- 适合：蒙太奇、纪录片插叙、独立杂志风片头、音乐短片\n- 关键词：拼贴、剪报、撕边、胶带、错位、混合材料\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「拼贴」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底板 #d8d2c4 灰纸，碎片色 #f2e9d8 报纸白、#2b2b2b 黑条、#c94f3d 红块、#3a5a78 蓝照片\n- 每块碎片 rotation 在 -3°~3° 之间随机，位置故意错开 4-8px，绝不对齐网格\n- 撕边：沿矩形四边每 5px 抖动 ±2px，四个角各缺一小口；黑条用纯 `fillRect` 不撕边，故意形成对比\n- 胶带 #e8dfc0、opacity 0.72、宽 26px、长 90px，压在碎片接缝上，两端各有一个小直角\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-2.4s：碎片间隔 0.12s 从画面外甩入（translate + rotate 60°→目标角，easeOutBack 过冲 12%）\n- 2.4-4s：胶带逐条「啪」地按下——scaleY 0→1 用 0.12s 的 easeOutQuart，同时压暗 4%\n- 4-5.5s：三块关键碎片被「抽走」再放回，位置偏移 6px，模拟手工挪动\n- 5.5-7s：整体轻微推近 4%（scale 1→1.04），定格\n\n技术要点：\n- 每块碎片包成 `{x, y, rot, path}` 对象，绘制时 `save / translate / rotate / restore`，不要直接改坐标\n- 撕边路径用固定 seed 的伪随机生成一次并缓存成 `Path2D`，别每帧重算\n- 胶带高光是一条 opacity 0.35 的白色斜线，方向统一 45°，这是「塑料膜」的辨识点\n"},{"id":"S3-K24","name":"粘土","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K24.jpg","markdown":"# K24 粘土\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 手作立体\n- 长什么样：物体像被手指捏出来的，边缘圆钝、体积鼓胀，表面有细细的指纹同心弧；受光面有一块偏白的高光，暗部偏紫红而不是发黑，整体像定格动画的片场\n- 适合：儿童内容、轻松科普、食品与玩具广告、定格动画风片头\n- 关键词：粘土、圆润、指纹、高光、定格、软体积\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「粘土」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #2f4f6f 深蓝（让暖色粘土跳出来），主体 #e76f51 陶红、#f4a261 橙、#2a9d8f 青绿\n- 每个形体画三层：底色 → 右下暗部（同色相降明度 25%）→ 左上高光椭圆（#ffffff、opacity 0.55、blur 6）\n- 形状一律不用直线：路径全部用 `quadraticCurveTo`，圆角半径不小于短边的 18%\n- 指纹：受光面画 4-6 条同心弧，颜色比底色暗 8%、opacity 0.07、lineWidth 1.2，这是「手捏」的辨识点\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.4s：三个粘土球从画面下方弹入，落地挤压（scaleX 1.18 / scaleY 0.86，0.1s）再回弹\n- 1.4-3.6s：球被「拉长」成主体形状——5 个关键形状插值，用 easeInOutQuad，全程宽高乘积守恒\n- 3.6-5.4s：高光沿表面滑动一小段（模拟转灯），指纹淡入\n- 5.4-7s：主体呼吸式缩放 ±2%（周期 1.2s），定格收尾\n\n技术要点：\n- 挤压回弹用 `easeOutElastic(t, 1, 0.35)`，振幅别超过 0.2，过了就像果冻不像粘土\n- 暗部与高光都用 `ctx.clip()` 限制在形体路径内，避免溢色\n- 定格感：位置采样到 12fps（每 2.5 帧才更新一次），其余帧保持不变，这个「顿」比形变更像粘土\n"},{"id":"S3-K25","name":"皮克斯式 3D","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K25.jpg","markdown":"# K25 皮克斯式 3D\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 3D 渲染\n- 长什么样：头大身小（头身比 1:2.5），眼睛占脸三分之一，瞳孔上两点高光；皮肤是柔和暖色，环境光从天蓝过渡到暖橙，接触阴影软而实，没有死黑\n- 适合：角色高光时刻、品牌吉祥物、情感向短片、儿童动画\n- 关键词：皮克斯、大眼、圆润、全局光、暖调、角色\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「皮克斯式 3D」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7.5 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 背景 radialGradient：#cfe4f7（上）→ #f7dfc4（下），圆心偏左上，模拟柔和天光\n- 角色主色 #f2a65a 暖橙，辅色 #4a6fa5 蓝衣，皮肤 #f6c9a0；暗部统一偏 #a8562f 而不是加灰\n- 头部是略扁的圆（rx 96 / ry 88），眼睛两个白椭圆 + 深棕虹膜 #3a2418 + 两个白高光点（r 6 和 r 3）\n- 接触阴影：脚下画 #000000、opacity 0.28 的椭圆，blur 18，宽是脚宽的 1.4 倍\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.5s：角色从画面左侧走入，身体先到、头延迟 3 帧跟上（follow-through）\n- 1.5-3s：转头看镜头（脸横向压缩到 0.82 再回 1），同时眨眼一次（眼睑 0.12s 闭合）\n- 3-5s：眉毛上扬 + 嘴角上扬 12°，头部上仰 4°，做出「恍然大悟」\n- 5-7.5s：右手抬起挥两下（前臂 rotate -25°→-45°，easeInOutSine），定格微笑\n\n技术要点：\n- 柔光用两层 radialGradient 叠加（主光 + 补光），不要用大半径 shadowBlur 做光晕，容易出方块\n- 眼球高光始终固定在虹膜左上 30% 位置，转头时不动——「高光不跟着动」是眼睛显得活的关键\n- 所有旋转前先 `ctx.save()`，缓动统一用 `easeInOutSine`，让动作有重量\n"},{"id":"S3-K26","name":"卡通渲染 3D","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K26.jpg","markdown":"# K26 卡通渲染 3D\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 3D 渲染\n- 长什么样：有真实体积和透视的 3D 物体，但明暗只分两阶——亮面和一块硬边暗面，中间没有过渡；外轮廓一圈 3-4px 黑描边，像从动画里抠出来的模型\n- 适合：游戏宣传、科技产品卡通化、教程演示、潮玩风片头\n- 关键词：卡通渲染、赛璐璐、硬边阴影、描边、体积、两阶\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「卡通渲染 3D」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7.5 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #eef3f8 冷白，主体 #e94f37 红，辅色 #1d3557 深蓝，描边 #10131a\n- 明暗只有两阶：亮面用本色，暗面用本色叠 #000000、opacity 0.22，交界是硬边——沿光照方向切一条折线，不羽化\n- 描边 lineWidth 4，只向外扩；物体转向时描边随深度从 4 收到 2.5\n- 受光侧上缘加一条 opacity 0.2 的白色高光带，宽度约物体宽的 12%\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.5s：物体从中心 scale 0.6→1 弹出（easeOutBack，过冲 8%），描边同步从 8 收到 4\n- 1.5-4.5s：绕 y 轴旋转 360°，暗面形状随光照实时重算，每 15° 重切一次折线\n- 4.5-6s：物体分裂成三个小件，各自沿 120° 方向散开 60px 再回位\n- 6-7.5s：整体下沉 6px，接触阴影扩大，静止\n\n技术要点：\n- 暗面用 `ctx.clip()` 裁在物体内，再用一条折线多边形填充；边缘不设 blur，硬边自然出现\n- 描边靠「同路径先粗后细」：先 `lineWidth 8` 描黑，再用 `lineWidth 4` 描本色盖住内侧\n- 用 `setTransform` 的仿射矩阵模拟 3D，透视压缩 `scaleX = cos(θ)`，不要低于 0.25，否则像纸片\n"},{"id":"S3-K27","name":"低多边形","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K27.jpg","markdown":"# K27 低多边形\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 3D 渲染\n- 长什么样：整个画面由几百个三角面拼成，每个面一个纯色、面与面之间有明显的色阶断层；没有平滑、没有贴图，山体像被削出来的水晶\n- 适合：科技感片头、地形与数据可视化、游戏风转场、独立音乐视觉\n- 关键词：低多边形、三角面、平面着色、色阶、几何、无平滑\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「低多边形」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7.5 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #0f1a24 深墨蓝，面片用 #ff6b35 / #f7c548 / #4ecdc4 / #5b8def 四色，按朝向分色\n- 每个三角面填纯色，色值 = 基色 ×（0.45 + 0.55 × 法线朝上程度），相邻面至少差 8% 明度\n- 面与面之间留 0.8px 的同色深描边（不是黑线），避免出现白缝\n- 全画面只允许三角，禁止任何曲线与圆弧；地平线也用一条折线代替\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-2s：地形从中线向两侧「长」出来，三角面按 x 距离依次出现（每 12ms 一个）\n- 2-4.5s：相机绕场景旋转 30°（面片重新排序 + 明度重算），中途有一枚面片脱离地面升起\n- 4.5-6s：整体调色从冷蓝过渡到暖橙（所有面基色插值 0.6s），像日出\n- 6-7.5s：升起的那枚面放大到全屏，作为转场\n\n技术要点：\n- 面片排序用中心点 z 做插入排序，每帧重排；面数控制在 400 以内\n- 法线用叉积：`n = normalize(cross(b-a, c-a))`，着色只取 y 分量就够\n- 形状一律 `moveTo / lineTo` 直接闭合，别用 arc——一旦有曲线，低多边形的硬气就没了\n"},{"id":"S3-K28","name":"像素","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K28.jpg","markdown":"# K28 像素\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 数字复古\n- 长什么样：所有东西都落在 16px 的方格上，边缘是台阶状的硬像素；颜色被限制在 6 色以内，明暗靠两三个色阶硬切，没有半透明过渡，像 8-bit 主机的画面\n- 适合：复古游戏片头、芯片与极客科普、怀旧梗段落、独立游戏宣传\n- 关键词：像素、色板、网格、8-bit、硬边、复古游戏\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「像素」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #1a1c2c，色板固定 6 色：#5d275d 紫、#b13e53 红、#ef7d57 橙、#ffcd75 米黄、#a7f070 绿、#f4f4f4 白\n- 所有坐标对齐 16px 网格，禁止非整数像素位置；线宽只能是 16 的倍数\n- 光源固定左上：受光面提一阶色，暗面降一阶色，只有两阶，没有渐变\n- 元素数量控制在 12 个以内，每个元素用「块」拼出来，不描边\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.6s：角色以 8fps 步进从左侧走入（每 4 帧换一次坐标，在 30fps 时间轴上表现为跳格）\n- 1.6-3.5s：背景两层视差滚动，远层每帧 1px、近层每帧 3px，都是整像素\n- 3.5-5.5s：角色跳起（抛物线，离地 64px），落地时画面整体下移 8px 再回弹\n- 5.5-7s：顶部用像素字逐字打出标题，光标以 2Hz 闪烁\n\n技术要点：\n- 先按 320×180 的逻辑分辨率画，再用 `ctx.imageSmoothingEnabled = false` 放大 4 倍到 1280×720\n- 步进动画写成 `const f = Math.floor(t * 8) % 4`，不要任何连续插值\n- 色板写成常量数组，取色一律 `PALETTE[i]`，杜绝随手写 hex，否则色数会失控\n"},{"id":"S3-K29","name":"蓝图","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K29.jpg","markdown":"# K29 蓝图\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 技术图解\n- 长什么样：整张深蓝图纸，白线细而准，画等距视角的机械结构；四周有图框、标题栏、尺寸标注和引线，数字小得像真工程图\n- 适合：讲工具与设备结构、硬核科普、工程风产品片、技术发布会\n- 关键词：蓝图、工程图、等距、标注、白线、深蓝\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「蓝图」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7.5 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #0b3d6b 经典蓝图蓝，网格 #1f5c95、opacity 0.35、间距 32px（细）与 160px（粗）\n- 线一律 #dbeaf7、lineWidth 1.2；被强调的部件用 #ffffff、lineWidth 2\n- 等距投影：x 轴 +30°、y 轴 -30°，长宽高比 2:1:1，竖线永远垂直——这是蓝图的辨识点\n- 尺寸标注：两端带 6px 斜杠的标注线 + 居中的 11px 等宽数字；引线末端画 r=2 的实心点\n- 图框四角各画一个 8px 定位十字，蓝图靠这一笔立住\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-2s：网格先淡入（opacity 0→0.35），图框四边用 dash 依次画出（每边 0.3s）\n- 2-4.5s：主体结构由下向上逐层绘制，竖向棱线先出、横向棱线后出，像被尺子推着画\n- 4.5-6s：三处尺寸标注依次浮现，数字用打字机效果逐字符显示\n- 6-7.5s：整体缩放 1→1.03，右下角标题栏盖章式出现\n\n技术要点：\n- 线条生长用 `ctx.setLineDash([len, len])` 配 `lineDashOffset` 从 len 到 0，比逐点绘制稳得多\n- 等距变换写成 `toIso(x,y,z) => [cx + (x-z)*cos30, cy + (x+z)*sin30 - y]`，全局复用\n- 数字用 `11px \"Consolas\", monospace`、字距 1px，中文标签另起小号黑体，别混排\n"},{"id":"S3-K30","name":"说明书线稿","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K30.jpg","markdown":"# K30 说明书线稿\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 技术图解\n- 长什么样：纯白底上只有黑色细线，没有一处填色；零件沿一条虚线轴向外拉开成爆炸图，每个零件带编号和一条折线引线，线端是一个小圆点，像宜家说明书的第三页\n- 适合：装配与使用步骤、产品拆解、教程分步图、硬件评测\n- 关键词：线稿、爆炸图、引线、编号、无填色、说明书\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「说明书线稿」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #ffffff 纯白，线 #1c1c1c、lineWidth 2；辅助虚线 #9a9a9a、lineWidth 1、dash [4,4]\n- 全部无填色，只 stroke；唯一允许的实心是引线端点 r=3 的黑圆和编号底的白圆\n- 爆炸方向统一沿一条 30° 斜轴，零件间距 40px，由内向外依次排开\n- 编号用 12px 无衬线、圆圈直径 20px，按装配顺序编号，数字永远在圆圈中心\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.2s：装配体中心先出现（一个简单的立方轮廓），描边用 dash 生长\n- 1.2-4s：六个零件沿斜轴依次弹出到目标位置（offset 0→40px，easeOutCubic，间隔 0.25s）\n- 4-5.8s：引线从零件外缘折线伸出到编号位（先水平后斜向），编号最后出现\n- 5.8-7s：所有零件轻微上下浮动 ±2px（相位错开），静止收尾\n\n技术要点：\n- 折线引线用两段 `lineTo`：先水平 24px，再 45° 斜向 30px，端点在最后画圆\n- 零件轮廓用 `roundRect` 的 stroke 版本、圆角 4px，全程不 fill\n- 弹开用 `easeOutCubic`，同时给每个零件 1px 的线宽呼吸（2→2.4→2），模拟手绘的不稳\n"},{"id":"S3-K31","name":"平铺整理","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K31.jpg","markdown":"# K31 平铺整理\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 俯拍静物\n- 长什么样：正俯视的木桌面，十几件工具与物件按 4×3 网格摆得整整齐齐，间距一致；每件东西有一层贴边的短阴影，色系统一在莫兰迪调，像博主拍的开箱平铺图\n- 适合：开箱与工具展示、装备清单、生活方式短片、教程物料页\n- 关键词：平铺、俯拍、网格、整齐、道具、莫兰迪\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「平铺整理」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #d9c7ac 木桌，叠 opacity 0.05 的木纹长条（宽 2px、长度随机）\n- 物件色板：#2f4858 深蓝、#e07a5f 陶红、#f2cc8f 米黄、#81b29a 灰绿，每件只用一色 + 一个暗 12% 的侧面\n- 每件物件带硬阴影：offset (3,3)、无 blur、#000000、alpha 0.12；物件之间最小间距 32px\n- 构图严格 4 列 × 3 行，单元 260×140，整体在画面内留 60px 边距\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-2.4s：物件按「从左到右、从上到下」逐个放到桌上（从上方 40px 落下 + rotate 8°→0，easeOutQuad，每件 0.15s）\n- 2.4-4.2s：放完后整体下压 1px 再回弹，阴影同步从 0 长到全尺寸\n- 4.2-5.8s：镜头缓慢推近到 1.06（中心偏左上），三件重点物件被一圈手绘感白线框住\n- 5.8-7s：白线框内的物件单独抬起 6px，投影变大，定格\n\n技术要点：\n- 阴影用 `rgba(0,0,0,0.12)` 偏移复制同一路径，不要 shadowBlur——硬阴影才是贴纸平铺感\n- 摆放位置写成 `grid(i)` 返回中心点，物件内部坐标一律以中心为原点\n- 推近用 `ctx.translate + ctx.scale` 包住整组绘制，别逐个改坐标\n"},{"id":"S3-K32","name":"积木","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K32.jpg","markdown":"# K32 积木\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 玩具拼搭\n- 长什么样：等距视角的塑料积木块，每块顶面有 4 个圆柱凸点，顶面最亮、左面中、右面暗；表面有一条细长高光，边缘圆润但不发光，像刚拆封的玩具\n- 适合：讲「模块化 / 搭系统」、儿童教育、SaaS 架构可视化、玩具广告\n- 关键词：积木、等距、凸点、塑料、堆叠、模块\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「积木」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7.5 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #f0f0f3 浅灰，砖块 #d62828 红、#f77f00 橙、#003049 深蓝、#fcbf49 黄\n- 等距投影 2:1：顶面是菱形（宽 120、高 60），左面与右面各高 56\n- 三面明度：顶面 100%、左面 78%、右面 60%；凸点用 `ellipse` + 8% 白高光，三阶明度差就是塑料感的来源\n- 每块顶部 2×2 共 4 个凸点，半径 10、高 8，位置在顶面菱形的四个象限中心\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-2.6s：五块砖从上方依次落下，落点对齐网格（easeOutBounce，落地后压缩 6% 再弹回）\n- 2.6-4.4s：每块落稳后，凸点从 0 长到满高（scaleY 0→1，easeOutBack）\n- 4.4-6s：整座塔左右摇晃（rotate ±1.2°，周期 0.8s，逐渐衰减），像刚搭好\n- 6-7.5s：镜头绕塔转 15°，右面变宽、顶面收窄，露出块体的塑料厚度\n\n技术要点：\n- 等距坐标写成 `iso(x,y,z) => [ox + (x-z)*60, oy + (x+z)*30 - y*56]`，砖块尺寸以格为单位\n- 凸点 = 顶面 `ellipse` + 柱身 `rect`，柱身左右各压 6% 明度，凸点才有圆柱感\n- 排序按 `x+z` 从大到小，保证前面的砖盖住后面的，别用 z 值排序\n"},{"id":"S3-K33","name":"地铁图","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K33.jpg","markdown":"# K33 地铁图\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 信息图表\n- 长什么样：白底上四条彩色线路，全是 45° / 90° 折线，转角圆润；站点是白底彩色描边的圆点，换乘站是白底黑框的大圆；线路粗细一致，颜色高饱和，站名小而清晰\n- 适合：讲流程 / 路线 / 依赖关系、产品架构、旅行与交通内容、用户增长路径\n- 关键词：地铁图、线路、站点、45°、彩色、信息图\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「地铁图」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7.5 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #fafafa，线路色 #e63946 红、#2a9d8f 青、#457b9d 蓝、#f4a261 橙，lineWidth 10、圆头端点\n- 转角只能是 45° 或 90°，`lineJoin = 'round'`，圆角半径不超过 12px\n- 站点：白底 + 同线路色描边 3px、半径 7；换乘站白底 + #1d1d1d 描边 3px、半径 11\n- 站名用 12px 无衬线，沿线路水平或垂直排，始终在线的上方或右侧\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-2.8s：四条线路从各自起点沿路径生长（dash 从全长收到 0，每条错开 0.3s）\n- 2.8-4.4s：站点在线路经过时弹出（scale 0→1.2→1，0.18s），换乘站最后出现并闪一下\n- 4.4-6s：一条 3px 白线沿主线路再走一遍，像「当前进度」\n- 6-7.5s：站名逐条淡入，整体平移 8px 收尾\n\n技术要点：\n- 先把线路算成点数组、用 `Path2D` 一次建好；生长用 `setLineDash([total, total])` + `lineDashOffset`\n- 站点别只画在端点，要画在折点与每段中点，否则线看起来是空的\n- 换乘站的黑色描边必须在彩色线之后画，否则会被线路盖住\n"},{"id":"S3-K34","name":"赛博霓虹","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K34.jpg","markdown":"# K34 赛博霓虹\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 数字未来\n- 长什么样：近黑的深紫底，霓虹管是饱和度极高的粉、青、紫，每根管子两层辉光；背景是透视网格地平线，空气里飘着细小光尘，像雨夜的电子招牌\n- 适合：未来科技、AI 与硬件发布、电音 / 游戏预告、夜间城市题材\n- 关键词：赛博、霓虹、辉光、暗底、网格、粉紫\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「赛博霓虹」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7.5 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #0a0616，霓虹色 #ff2e88 粉、#00e5ff 青、#b026ff 紫；地面网格 #2a1650\n- 每根霓虹线画三遍：外层 lineWidth 14 + shadowBlur 28 + alpha 0.25，中层 6 + alpha 0.6，内芯 2 纯白\n- 全局叠一层扫描线：每 4px 一条 #000000、alpha 0.12 的横线，再加 0.06 的暗角 vignette\n- 网格地面：视平线在画面 62% 处，纵向线向灭点收敛，横向线按指数间距向观众靠近\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-2s：地平线先亮起（一条 2px 青线从中心向两侧延伸），随后网格向观众滚动\n- 2-4.5s：主体图形逐笔点亮，带 3 次随机 flicker（alpha 0.3↔1，间隔 60ms）\n- 4.5-6s：粉色光晕呼吸式放大（shadowBlur 28→44→28，周期 1.2s），光尘向上飘\n- 6-7.5s：整体轻微色差抖动（红蓝通道各偏移 1px），定格\n\n技术要点：\n- 辉光靠 `shadowColor + shadowBlur`，画完必须 `shadowBlur = 0`，否则后面所有元素都会发光\n- 扫描线用离屏 canvas 预生成一次图案，`createPattern` 平铺，别每帧画几百条线\n- 网格滚动用 `offset = (t * 60) % spacing` 的模运算，保证首尾无缝\n"},{"id":"S3-K35","name":"旧学院风","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K35.jpg","markdown":"# K35 旧学院风\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 复古印刷\n- 长什么样：牛皮纸色的旧纸，四角压暗，纸上只印暗绿与酒红两色，外加少量金线；中央是圆形徽章，双线外环 + 星形 + 宽字距衬线字，像一枚校徽\n- 适合：讲历史与传承、学院教育、精品咖啡与酒类品牌、复古书封\n- 关键词：旧学院、牛皮纸、徽章、衬线、暗绿、复古\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「旧学院风」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，8 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #e8dcc3 牛皮纸，叠 opacity 0.06 的噪点与四周 opacity 0.12 的暗角；主色 #1f3a2e 暗绿、#7a2e2e 酒红、#b08d57 金\n- 徽章：外环 r=120 双线（3px + 1px，间距 8px），内环 r=96，中心一枚 5 角星与三条横线\n- 文字全部衬线体（Georgia / 宋体），字距 3px，字号不超过 28px\n- 只用三种颜色；金线只用于装饰线，不做大面积填色\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.4s：纸面从 1.06 缩到 1（easeOutQuart），暗角同步加深\n- 1.4-3s：徽章「盖章」落下——scale 1.25→1 用 0.18s，伴随 2px 抖动与一次 alpha 闪烁\n- 3-4.6s：外环双线沿圆周生长（dash），星形从中心放大，装饰线向左右展开\n- 4.6-6.2s：下方两行衬线字逐字浮现（每字 0.08s），字距从 6px 收到 3px\n- 6.2-8s：整体上移 4px，纸纹噪点闪一下，静止\n\n技术要点：\n- 纸张纹理用离屏 canvas 生成 512×512 噪点后 `createPattern` 平铺，配 `multiply` 混合\n- 盖章抖动用 `Math.sin(t * 80) * (1 - p) * 2`，p 是盖章进度，结束必须归零\n- 衬线字设 `ctx.font = '600 24px Georgia, \"Songti SC\", serif'`，中文靠回退到宋体\n"},{"id":"S3-K36","name":"丝网印刷","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K36.jpg","markdown":"# K36 丝网印刷\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 复古印刷\n- 长什么样：三四个色版错开压在同一张纸上，边缘有 1-2px 错位和毛刺；颜色平涂，叠加处变深，纸面浮着一层粗颗粒，像手工印的海报\n- 适合：独立海报、乐队与展览视觉、有设计感的标题卡、复古包装\n- 关键词：丝网印刷、套色、错位、颗粒、平涂、复古海报\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「丝网印刷」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #f4efe6 米纸，三套色 #e94f37 红、#2b59c3 蓝、#f6c445 黄，油墨 opacity 0.88\n- 每层相对基准偏移 (3,-2)、(-2,3)、(1,1)，模拟手工对版误差；层间用 `globalCompositeOperation = 'multiply'`\n- 边缘不齐：路径每 8px 加 ±1.5px 噪声，且每层噪声 seed 不同，三层毛边不能重合\n- 颗粒：叠一层 opacity 0.09 的随机黑点图案（4×4 像素块，30% 密度）\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.8s：三个色版依次「压印」下来——每层从上方 20px 落下，落地瞬间 alpha 0.6→0.88，间隔 0.45s\n- 1.8-3.4s：第三层压完后整体「错版」抖一下（全部图层位移 2px 再回，0.2s）\n- 3.4-5.2s：主体内部用 mask 从下往上刷出第二层色，像刮板刮过一遍（0.9s）\n- 5.2-7s：颗粒层淡入，四角加 opacity 0.1 的脏边，定格\n\n技术要点：\n- 套色叠加必须用 `multiply`，普通 alpha 叠加出不来油墨那种透亮发闷的感觉\n- 毛边路径用固定 seed 生成一次并缓存，三层用三个不同 seed，共用就变成同一个形状了\n- 颗粒用 32×32 的图块 `createPattern` 平铺，比逐点 `fillRect` 快一个数量级\n"},{"id":"S3-K37","name":"哥特","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K37.jpg","markdown":"# K37 哥特\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 装饰风格\n- 长什么样：近黑的底，米金色极细装饰线勾出尖拱与四叶饰，线细而密、层层套叠；少量暗红做内部填充，整体严格对称、垂直、向上拔高\n- 适合：恐怖与悬疑题材、暗黑系品牌、金属 / 摇滚视觉、万圣节片头\n- 关键词：哥特、尖拱、细线、对称、暗红、装饰\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「哥特」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7.5 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #0b0a0c，线 #cfc6b8 米金、lineWidth 1，暗红 #7b1e2b 只用于内部填充，面积不超过 15%\n- 尖拱：两段半径等于拱宽的圆弧，圆心在拱脚、交于顶点；拱高是宽的 1.6 倍\n- 装饰：四叶饰（四个 r=14 的圆交叠）、垂直线束（间距 8px）、卷草纹（三次贝塞尔）\n- 严格左右对称，中轴线在画面中心，所有元素关于它镜像\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-2s：尖拱从顶点开始向两侧「生长」，两条弧线用 dash 同时展开（各 0.9s）\n- 2-3.6s：四叶饰在拱内逐个浮现（scale 0.4→1、opacity 0→1，间隔 0.2s）\n- 3.6-5.4s：垂直线束从上往下长，卷草纹沿拱脚向两侧蔓延（dash 生长，速度 240px/s）\n- 5.4-7.5s：暗红填充从拱心缓慢渗入（径向扩展，easeInOutQuad），最后全部线条轻微发光一次\n\n技术要点：\n- 细线在缩放后必须 `ctx.lineWidth = 1 / scale`，否则一放大就变粗，哥特的「细」就没了\n- 镜像用 `ctx.save(); ctx.scale(-1, 1); ctx.translate(-W, 0)`，画一遍得两边；`W` 是画布宽，别拿它当颜色变量名\n- 卷草纹用 `bezierCurveTo` 并保持切线连续，接头处一旦有折角就露馅\n"},{"id":"S3-K38","name":"情景喜剧","series":"S3","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S3-K38.jpg","markdown":"# K38 情景喜剧\n\n- 分类：K 卡通画风 · 影视棚拍\n- 长什么样：暖黄灯光的室内客厅，平涂色块配黑色细描边，人物动作幅度很小；镜头在三个机位间硬切，底部偶尔弹出黄色的「（笑声）」字幕条，像《辛普森一家》混搭老式棚拍剧\n- 适合：生活小场景、产品使用情景剧、吐槽与梗视频、轻喜剧广告\n- 关键词：情景喜剧、暖光、平涂、多机位、罐头笑声、室内\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「情景喜剧」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，7.5 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 底色 #d9a56b 墙面、#8a5a3b 沙发、#c96f4a 地毯，人物肤色 #f2c14e、描边 #2f2f2f、lineWidth 2.5\n- 三个机位共用同一套坐标：A 全景（scale 1）、B 中景（scale 1.35，偏左）、C 过肩（scale 1.6，偏右）\n- 人物只有平涂 + 描边，五官用两个黑点加一条弧线，不做渐变\n- 字幕条在底部 12% 处，`（笑声）` 用 #ffd93d、32px、3px 黑描边\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.2s：A 机位建立，人物从右侧走入并坐下（身体下沉 8px）\n- 1.2-3.2s：硬切到 B 机位，人物说一句话——只有嘴部开合（0.12s 一次）与眉毛上抬\n- 3.2-5s：切到 C 机位，另一个角色耸肩（双肩各上移 4px），底部弹出「（笑声）」并停留 1.2s\n- 5-6.5s：切回 A，两人同时转头看镜头（rotate 6°），定格\n- 6.5-7.5s：画面推近 3%，字幕淡出\n\n技术要点：\n- 机位切换直接换变换矩阵（`setTransform`），不做位移补间，硬切才有棚拍感\n- 人物只动 2-3 个关节，其余保持静止，动作越少越像情景喜剧\n- 字幕先 `strokeText` 描黑边再 `fillText`，`lineWidth 3`、`lineJoin = 'round'`\n"},{"id":"S4-L1","name":"字幕新闻","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L1.jpg","markdown":"# L1 字幕新闻（Caption News）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 剪辑与纪实\n- 长什么样：美国一年死掉 130 万箱蜜蜂，总数却涨了 6%：静音也能看懂的新闻。\n- 适合：不开声音也能看懂，适合竖屏版\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Caption News — Style Prompt\n\n> Social-video news that reads with the sound off. Real footage, photos and the source document are the ground; huge captions are the lead actor, slammed in a chunk at a time with one keyword on a yellow block. Hard cuts on a music grid, numbers that roll, a source tag that slides in whenever a figure appears, and the original document highlighted on screen before its number is believed. 16:9, built for a feed.\n> References (grammar only): the captions-first social news of the mid-2010s (NowThis, AJ+), horizontal explainers that set big type over footage (Bloomberg Quicktake). Never copy their logos, fonts, colour ratios, idents or shots, and never name them in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n1. **Sound-off first.** Every beat must be understood from the picture and the captions alone; the voice adds pace, not information.\n2. **The caption is the shot.** One Chinese line, set huge, slammed in two or three chunks, one keyword in a yellow block; one English line under it. The footage is chosen to sit under the line.\n3. **Evidence on screen.** Each number arrives with its source: a source tag slides in; key figures are first seen highlighted in the original document; per-shot footage credits sit in the corner like a news \"Video:\" tag.\n4. **A hook on frame 4.** The film's largest number lands in the first 0.15 s and rolls to its value.\n5. **One turn.** The bad-news hook is answered by a fact that changes its meaning; the rest of the film explains that turn, one new fact per shot.\n\nNot **Data Storytelling** (charts are short inserts between footage, never the whole film, never a dashboard). Not a **TV news package** (no anchor, no lower-third ticker, no studio, no scrolling crawl). Not a keynote: no headers, no centred title-and-bullets, no page turns.\n\n## 2. Frame and layout (1920×1080)\n\n| Zone | Position | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| Footage | full bleed | video, photo, document or data card; never a vertical frame pillar-boxed in the middle |\n| Caption card | left aligned at x 120; Chinese baseline y ≈ 948, English baseline y ≈ 1014 | one line per language, never wrapped |\n| Channel bug | top left, x 96 y 52, 46 px high | yellow block with a fictional channel name + topic in Chinese and English small caps |\n| Source tag | under the bug, y 108, 36 px | white \"来源\" + dark label (`USDA NASS · 2026.08.03`, `… · 第 13 页`); only on data and document shots, slides in 0.2 s |\n| Footage credit | top right, 19 px on a 50 % dark chip | `视频 作者 · CC BY 3.0`; every CC BY shot carries it |\n| Lower third | x 120, y 742, 96 px high | yellow bar + label + one English line; for a place, date or speaker, never for the main sentence |\n\nUnder the caption: a bottom gradient (0 → 84 % from y 560) plus a soft dark radial from the bottom-left corner; a top gradient (55 % → 0 over 200 px) under the bug and the credit. Data cards use a lighter gradient that starts at y 820. Graphics and charts sit right of centre and bleed to the frame edge; their title and source sit top left, never centred.\n\nVertical 9:16 is a different layout, not a crop: put the caption under a 1080×1560 footage band (horizontal sources then scale ≤ 1.5×), stack the chunks on two lines there (the one-line rule is for 16:9 subtitles), and move the bug and source tag into the top safe area.\n\n## 3. Editing grammar\n\n- **Cuts on a 120 BPM half-beat grid** (every cut at a multiple of 0.5 s). Footage shots 1.5–3 s; data cards and documents 3–4.5 s; a shot that holds a comparison stays until every item has been fully on screen ≥ 2.5 s.\n- **Captions cross cuts.** A caption can run over two shots (picture changes, words don't); captions change at most every ~2.5 s even when shots change every 1.5 s. Reading time, not shot length, sets the caption clock.\n- **The order of a fact**: footage (feeling) → the number on a card → the document line that says it → the consequence. Never a number without its source tag in the same shot.\n- **Transitions are few and fixed**: hard cut (default); a 1–2-frame white flash on the turn and on the freeze; a card sliding in from the right with speed streaks (0.18 s); a split screen opening from its divider; grid panels rising staggered (0.1 s apart); a punch-in (0.3 s ease-out to ~1.25×) on the culprit; a **mask transition** where a photo grows out of a map shape. No dissolves, no page turns, no 3D flips.\n- **Pace**: a strong action within 0.5 s of every shot; no frame flat for more than 1 s (rolling counters, line drawing, highlighter sweeps, slow pushes keep it alive); one held breath (2–3 s) after the turn; two real silences.\n\n## 4. Caption card (the subtitle *is* the graphic)\n\n- **Chinese line**: Noto Sans SC 900, 88 px (verdict lines 110 px), ivory `#F7F5EF`, 20 px dark soft shadow. Split into 2–3 chunks with `|` in the timeline; each chunk slams 0.12 s after the previous: scale 1.3 → 1 in 0.13 s (back ease, overshoot 1.6), opacity full in 0.04 s.\n- **Keyword**: `{…}` inside a chunk; a honey-yellow block `#FFC21A` wipes left → right in 0.12 s behind it, text turns ink `#0E0E0C`. One keyword per caption, at most.\n- **English line**: Inter Tight 500, 33 px, ivory, fades and rises 10 px in 0.18 s, 0.1 s after the first chunk. It is the spoken line (numbers as digits), not a translation of the Chinese.\n- **One line per language, always.** If either language would wrap, split the sentence into two captions. A caption is on screen ≥ 1.8 s and ideally ≥ the spoken line + 0.6 s; the Chinese is a paraphrase short enough to read in that time (≤ ~14 characters).\n- The `.srt` is generated from the same timeline (`timeline.json` → `tools/cues.py` → `core/render/srt.py`): one Chinese line, one English line per cue.\n- A text-only beat (caption with no voice) is native to the style: use it for the breath after the turn.\n\n## 5. Graphics\n\n- **Ledger card** (the data-card ground): `#151512` sheet, faint ruled lines every 54 px (4.5 % white), a thin red double margin at x 78/86 — the \"account book\" that ties every chart together. It slides in from the right over 0.18 s; inside, a slow push (×1.025–1.03 over the shot).\n- **Colour meaning**: honey `#FFC21A` = the point / gains / the highlight; red `#FF4A3D` = losses, the spike, the bad number; ivory = everything else; grey `#8D8B83` = labels and units; `#5E5C55` = the bars that don't matter.\n- **Numbers**: Inter Tight 800 with fixed-width digit cells (`num()` in `demo/main.js`), so a rolling counter never jitters. Counters roll with ease-out and turn their colour when they arrive. Units in Chinese (箱, 万箱) set smaller after the number.\n- **Charts**: titled top left in Chinese (Noto Sans SC 900 64 px, ≤ 6 characters so it can be read in the shot) with one grey English line; lines draw segment by segment (eio); the key point gets a bigger dot, an expanding ring (0.7 s) and a block label; bars grow from the left one after another (first one slower and longer if it is the point).\n- **Map**: state shapes from Natural Earth, projected (Albers), sequential fill in five steps of one hue, states without data in dark grey with a \"未单列\" legend swatch; the subject state outlined in yellow with a leader and a label; the map pushes in on it.\n- **Documents**: the PDF page rendered at 220 dpi as white paper on a dark desk (±1–2° tilt, soft shadow, vignette), pushed in slowly; highlighter rectangles placed from `pdftotext -bbox` coordinates (1 pt = 3.0556 px), `multiply` blend, swept left → right in 0.3–0.5 s; the number read from it is re-set as a big yellow block beside the page. The printed text is picture; what must be read is re-set in type.\n- **Split screen**: two halves with a 4 px yellow divider that drops first; the \"bad\" half desaturated, darkened and tinted red, the \"good\" half in full grade; a label block (Chinese + English word) and a counter in each; a shared stamp centred on the divider.\n- **Grid**: three panels (632 px, 12 px gaps) rising staggered; each one lights up (yellow border, label turns yellow) on the word that names it; numbered 1–2–3.\n- **Freeze frame**: the shot stops on a frame, a 0.14 s white flash, punch 1.0 → 1.12, saturation drops to 45 %, yellow corner brackets close in on the subject, a timecode chip with a pause mark appears top right.\n- **Title sting**: a yellow ruled page (the ledger again, in yellow) wipes across the left 45 % over live footage; the title slams, the English title and a dateline chip follow; the page wipes off left and the title becomes the channel bug.\n\n## 6. Grade and image quality\n\n- One grade for every piece of footage: `contrast(1.07) saturate(0.92) brightness(0.98)` + a 6 % warm soft-light layer; documents 4 %. Light grain in the master (`mux.sh … 30 1`).\n- **Scale limits**: modern footage ≤ 1.5× (prepare frames at their final size with ffmpeg: crop, lanczos, `unsharp=5:5:0.45`); photos ≤ 1:1 of their source; archive material ≤ 2.5× after deblocking. Pushes stay inside those limits — a 720p source already at 1.5× gets no push.\n- Remove station logos by cropping (never cover them with a caption), blur legible private details (phone numbers, names on hives, number plates) with `delogo`, skip shots with burned-in text.\n- Captions, numbers and charts are always vector, never scaled footage.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n| Layer | What | Level |\n|---|---|---|\n| Voice | one brisk narrator (Kokoro, speed 1.1–1.17), numbers spelled out in the TTS text | compressed, ≈ 8 dB over the music |\n| Music | 120 BPM pulse in sampled instruments: kit (kick on the beat, hats on off-beats), eighth-note electric bass, a sixteenth-note marimba ostinato, vibraphone off-beat chords, glockenspiel accents, staccato brass stabs on the big numbers, timpani and suspended cymbal for turns | ducked ~45 % under the voice |\n| Structure | the hook hit on frame 4; a hard stop into silence before the turn (only natural sound and the question); the turn lands on a major-key brass hit; half-time and low strings for the threat; a snare build into the spike; a second short silence before the verdict; a major chord with bells on the verdict | |\n| Foley | every graphic action has a sound: chunk slam (click + thump), counter rattle (a tick every 45 ms), whoosh into each card, highlighter squeak, camera shutter on the freeze, stamp thump on a big number, pops for labels, ticks as map states fill, a riser into the map push | |\n| Natural sound | the subject's own location sound (here a swarm), J-cut 0.3 s before the freeze, bed under the ending | not ducked below audibility |\n\nMaster −14 LUFS, true peak −1.2 dB (`core/render/mux.sh`).\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\n1. **Frame-4 hook** — kicker line, then a giant number in a yellow block rolling to its value in 0.45 s, then the unit and the subject word (`hook()`).\n2. **Chunk slam with one yellow keyword** (`capCard()`).\n3. **Proportion by darkness** — a yellow line sweeps across the footage to the percentage; what it passed goes grey and dark; the figure appears on the dark side (`shotHalf()`). Works for any \"X % of …\".\n4. **The document says it** — headline or sentence highlighted from PDF bbox coordinates, then the number popped as a yellow block beside the paper (`paper()`, `shotDoc6()`, `shotQuote()`).\n5. **Freeze and question** — the image stops on the viewer's question, music cut to silence (`shotFreeze()`).\n6. **Ledger split** — two counters racing side by side, the difference stamped after both settle (`shotSplit()`).\n7. **Lit grid** — three ways / three causes, each panel lighting on its word (`shotGrid()`).\n8. **Punch and circle** — 0.3 s punch-in on the culprit, a circle drawn round it, a leader to its name and its Latin name (`shotSem()`).\n9. **Map to ground** — the map pushes into a region, then a photo of that place grows out of the region's outline (`shotMap()`).\n\n## 9. Material\n\n1. **Ask the user first** whether they have their own footage, photos, documents or data (and the rights to them). Their material comes first; the sources below are the fallback.\n2. **Where to look** (judge every file on its own page, not the search result):\n   - **Wikimedia Commons** (video and photos): read the licence on each file page. Fine: CC0, public domain, CC BY (credit author, title, licence on screen and in `CREDITS`). Not used: CC BY-SA, \"no known restrictions\", unclear licences, YouTube re-uploads, colourised or \"restored\" versions.\n   - **U.S. federal agencies** (USDA, USGS, NASA, NOAA, NARA): works of federal employees are public domain; check that the page does not credit a contractor or a third party. NASA: https://images.nasa.gov. NARA: https://catalog.archives.gov.\n   - **Internet Archive**: only items whose own page states public domain or a CC0/CC BY licence; U.S. works published before 1931 are public domain.\n   - **Library of Congress**: read each item's rights statement; \"no known restrictions\" is not enough.\n   - **Official documents and data** (reports, statistics, court filings) from the agency's own site: the source of every fact; keep `FACTS.md` (claim, URL, quoted wording, derived sums with their arithmetic).\n   - **Map geometry**: Natural Earth (public domain).\n3. **Before downloading, tell the user** what you will fetch, from where and under which licence, and keep within a size budget. The finder copied into this style, `demo/tools/footage.py` (`search commons|archive|nasa`, `fetch`, `used`, `md`), logs licence, author, page and the seconds used of each file into `SOURCES.jsonl` and writes `SOURCES.md`; `demo/tools/commons-tools/peek.py` previews a Commons video from thumbnails before downloading it, `seg.py` downloads only a segment of a long one, `commons_thumb.py` takes a standard-width thumbnail when the original is rate-limited. Wikimedia rate-limits bursts (HTTP 429): one request at a time with pauses.\n4. **People on screen**: no close-up or slow motion of an identifiable private person; public figures and people in the news event are fine. Never use generated images as footage.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nCopy `demo/` into the project and replace the content:\n- `main.js` — the page (`window.render(t)` draws synchronously with placeholders first, so `TEXTS(t)` is valid for `readcheck.mjs`, then loads the frames it needs and draws again). Shot functions per format; `SHOTS` table for cuts.\n- `timeline.json` — captions (`zh` with `|` chunks and `{keyword}`, `en`), voice start times, silences and hits; read by the page, `tools/cues.py` (.srt + timing check) and `audio/mix.py`.\n- `tools/prep.sh` — every video shot as a 30 fps JPEG sequence at its display size (crop, lanczos, unsharp, delogo); report pages; photos; the map projection (`tools/map_prep.py`).\n- `audio/mix.py` — sampled score from the timeline, foley from `events.json`, voice, natural sound, ducking.\n- `tools/fonts.sh` (OFL subsets for every character in `main.js`), `tools/final_check.sh` (loudness, black frames, speech-to-text of the final mix).\n\n## 11. Pitfalls\n\n- **Text that can't be read in its shot.** Chart titles ≤ 6 Chinese characters; ornamental stamps (datelines) must recur readably elsewhere; a label that changes text (a counter) is reported to `TEXTS` with its final value. Run `readcheck.mjs`.\n- **Two-line Chinese captions in 16:9** read as a slide. Split the sentence instead.\n- **The voice running to the cut**: lines that end on the cut feel rushed; speed the TTS (≤ 1.17 — faster, Kokoro starts to mangle names: \"varroa\" became \"Veroa\") or shorten the line.\n- **Homophones in ASR**: \"requeen the weak\" was heard as \"week\"; \"varroa mite\" as \"might\". Rewrite the line (\"give old ones a new queen\", \"varroa mites\").\n- **Words stronger than the source**: the report says \"stressor\", not \"killer\"; quarterly losses summed are not \"annual net loss\". Write what the source says.\n- **Footage from elsewhere**: illustrative footage (here Dutch hives) never carries a place label it doesn't have; dates on photos (an almond orchard in 2014) are stated in the credit.\n- **Blank first frames on transitions**: start split/grid/sting animations 0.1–0.15 s before the cut so frame 1 already has picture.\n- **Station logos**: crop them out; never park a caption over a logo.\n\n## 12. Variation space\n\nThe agent decides per film: topic, the turn, which formats are spent where, footage, the channel's fictional name and colour (keep one accent colour), the title.\n\n- **Three structures:** the inverted pyramid with a mid-film turn (bad number → \"really?\" → the document says otherwise → why); a list of three that turns on the third; a countdown of numbers that ends on the one that matters.\n- **Three openings:** the biggest number on frame 4; the document headline highlighted in the first second; a freeze on the event with a question.\n- **Three endings:** a two-part verdict caption on footage with music returning after a silence; back to the hook's number, now re-read; the last data card's key point becoming the closing caption.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nThe Bee Ledger\n"},{"id":"S4-L2","name":"1920 年代新闻片","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L2.jpg","markdown":"# L2 1920 年代新闻片（1920s Newsreel）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 剪辑与纪实\n- 长什么样：1926 年报纸说查尔斯顿会跳坏膝盖，福特工人野餐会上的孩子照跳不误。\n- 适合：讲\"以前\"，怀旧\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# 1920s Newsreel — Style Prompt\n\n> A sound newsreel of the late 1920s, cut from real archive material: a searchlight-and-globe ident with a brass fanfare, a newspaper that spins into the lens, a fast and cheerful announcer on an optical soundtrack, place-and-date cards, maps, magazine pages and documents, wipes, flips and irises, and a hot band that can stop dead for the punchline.\n> References (grammar only): **Fox Movietone News** and **Universal Newspaper Newsreel** (1927–1930s) — the ident ritual, the announcer's patter, one story per location, the stinger after the last story; **Pathé News** — the item title card; Hollywood's **spinning newspaper** montage; **1920s hot dance bands** (Charleston rhythm, banjo, tuba). Never copy a real newsreel's name, logo, globe design, typeface or fanfare.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nAn edit style: the look is in **how real material is arranged, cut, captioned and carried by sound**, not in a drawing technique.\n- **Archive in a projected gate**: 1.37:1 picture, full height, rounded corners, black outside; everything inside it shares one silver grade, gate weave, flicker, scratches and dust.\n- **News objects** do the explaining: newspapers, magazine pages, books, telegrams, maps, records. They are filmed (spun, slammed, panned, turned, underlined), never typeset as slides.\n- **A voice talks over everything**: short exclamations, half joking, never a lecture.\n- **Music is a band, and it edits**: cuts land on the band's beats; the band stops dead to let a line land; a period record can take over the soundtrack.\n\nNot `silent-film` (1920s Silent Film, in this library): that style is a drawn, inked comedy projected between velvet curtains, with intertitles instead of voices, a cinema pianist and no foley. Here nothing is drawn but the packaging; the footage and print are real; somebody always speaks; the soundtrack is brass, banjo and records; titles are news captions (place · date), not dialogue cards; there are no curtains and no theatre.\nNot a documentary with a \"vintage filter\" either: no talking heads, no slow Ken Burns drifts, no modern lower-third bars.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Gate**: 1480 × 1080 inside 1920 × 1080 (1.37:1), corner radius 26 px, pure black outside. Nothing is ever drawn outside the gate.\n- **Two layers**: the picture layer (footage, print, maps, cards) gets the film look; the type layer (place cards, labels, subtitles, the record inset) stays sharp and steady. Grease-pencil marks belong to the picture (they weave with it).\n- **Base grade** for every source, before anything else (`tools/prep.py`, `grade()`): greyscale → percentile stretch (0.6 % → black, 99.6 % → white; print: 0.2–0.3 % / 99.0–99.7 % with the black lifted to 26–30, like newsprint) → mild S-curve `x + 0.18·(x−0.5)·(1−|2x−1|)` → output 14–238. **Moving footage uses one level set per clip** (sampled across all its frames), never per frame.\n- **Projection look** (`filmLook()`, picture layer only): warm silver (`multiply #efe7d6`, `screen #17140f`), vignette 0.42 H → 0.95 H, flicker ±3.5 % per frame + 3 Hz drift, gate weave ±1.6 px / ±2.2 px (value noise), 0–3 vertical scratches that live for 9 frames, 0–4 one-frame dust specks. Grain is added at mux (`mux.sh … 24 7`), not in the page.\n- **Archive film upscaling**: at most ~2.5×. NTSC transfers (720 × 480) are treated as 4:3, cropped to 1.37 and scaled 2.31× to 1480 × 1080: `hqdn3d=4:3:6:5` → `lanczos` → `unsharp=5:5:0.55` → base grade. Frames are exported at 24 fps as JPEG sequences and played by `floor((t − t0) · 24)`, so renders are deterministic.\n- **Print**: newspapers, magazines and books at their scan resolution; crop pages to the relevant fold so unrelated (and sometimes ugly) old stories never come into view.\n- **Code-drawn packaging only**: the ident (sunburst, two searchlights, wire globe), item title cards, maps, the end card. Never generate \"archive\" pictures.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **Monochrome warm silver** for the picture; values do the work (check every shot as a thumbnail).\n- Type is cream on near-black (`#f3eee4` / `#d6cfc0` on `rgba(12,11,10,.8)`), framed by a 2 px cream rule; marks are a slightly warm white grease pencil; underlines on printed pages are dark soft lead.\n- Packaging silver: Art Deco gradient lettering `#fff → #d9d4ca | #9e978a → #efebe2` with a hard \"metal\" break at the middle and a black outline (0.12 em).\n- No colour accents, no sepia wash on top of the silver, no colourised archive.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n| Use | Face (all OFL) | Spec |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ident, item cards, end title | Limelight | 104–118 px, silver gradient, black outline |\n| Chinese display (ident, cards, end title) | Noto Serif SC 900 | 50–76 px, letter-spacing 10–26 px, `#ece7dc` |\n| Place card, labels (English) | Oswald 600 | 26–34 px, letter-spacing 4–6 px, upper case \"PLACE · DATE\" |\n| Place card, labels (Chinese) | Noto Sans SC 500–600 | 24–30 px |\n| Subtitles | Noto Sans SC 600 40 px over Oswald 400 29 px | bottom of the gate (baselines H−82 / H−38), black stroke 8 / 6 px |\n\n- **Place card** (the newsreel's segment title, instead of full-screen cards for every story): top left of the gate (gate x + 48, y 56), dark box with an inset 2 px cream frame, English \"PLACE · DATE\" over one Chinese line; it is pulled out left to right in 0.22 s and fades 0.15 s before it leaves. Hold ≥ CJK ÷ 4.5 + Latin ÷ 15 + 1.5 s from the moment it is complete (`readcheck.mjs`), so keep both lines short.\n- **Item title card** (one per film at most, to turn to a new story): black, a double Art Deco frame drawn in 0.35 s with quarter-fan corners, Limelight line + Chinese line, slow push; ≥ 2.5 s.\n- **Labels inside split screens**: one per panel, top centre, same box style, enter one beat apart.\n- **Subtitle strip**: the subtitles sit on a dark strip across the bottom of the gate (180 px, transparent at the top edge, ≈ 93–96 % `#0a0908` below), on every shot, fading in 0.25 s before the first cue of a run and held through gaps under 1 s, so it never blinks; print and pale footage would otherwise swallow the letters (`subBand()`).\n- **Subtitles**: Chinese line over English line, **one line per language, never wrapped** (`tools/subcheck.mjs` measures every cue in the page); a cue lasts max(1.8 s, spoken + 0.6 s) and ends 50 ms before the next. Print in shot is never re-typed as a caption: the announcer and subtitles carry its meaning, an underline points at it.\n\n## 5. Editing grammar\n\n- **The band is the clock**: pick a hot-dance tempo (190–220 BPM); every cut, landing, slam and label entrance sits on a beat, most cuts on bar lines; quick inserts on beat 3 or on the Charleston accent (beat 1 and the \"and\" of 2). When a period record plays, cut on the record's own beat (measure it), not on the grid.\n- **One story = one place + one kind of material + one line of voice.** Stories get shorter towards the end (2–3 bars → 1 bar → beats), then one held button.\n- **Shot lengths**: archive shots 1–2.5 s; print and documents 2.5–4 s (they must be read); comparisons ≥ 2.5 s fully in frame before the verdict; never a static frame longer than ~1 s without something moving (push, roll, pencil, flicker of a counter).\n- **Transitions are the newsreel's own, each used once, as punctuation**: iris close/open, diagonal hard wipe with a bright 6 px edge, vertical-axis flip, venetian blinds (12 slats turning), clock wipe from 12 o'clock, spinning paper, page turn, panel push-over, freeze. No cross-dissolves between stories, no modern whip pans, no zoom blur.\n- **L-cuts**: the announcer may run 0.3–0.5 s over a cut; a new music state may start one beat before its picture (J-cut).\n\n## 6. Camera on stills and archive\n\n| Move | What it does | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|\n| Spin-and-slam | the paper arrives as news | a headline; a telegram; a poster |\n| Slow push then punch-in on a beat | from the whole page to the one detail | a headline → a photo in it |\n| Pan across a wide print | a crowd, a parade, a skyline | panoramas, group photos |\n| Tilt to reveal | the landmark behind the subject | a building, a monument |\n| Map push | from the country to one city, into the next story | travel, spread, campaigns |\n| Grid deal | many related stills land one per beat | step-by-step, collections, \"all of them\" |\n| Flip-book | a series of stills played on the beat becomes motion | dance steps, assembly stages, before/after |\n| Freeze + jolt | stop the archive on the telling frame | the punchline, the evidence |\n| Split / quad | two or four places at once, side by side | comparisons, \"everybody's doing it\" |\n\nArchive footage is not reframed with fake camera moves bigger than ~6 % (it already has its own camera); stills always get a move with a purpose.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Announcer** (Kokoro): a bright, energetic male voice; a blend of two voices (e.g. `am_liam:0.6,bm_george:0.4`) gives a mid-Atlantic tinge; speed ≈ 1.15–1.2; 6–12 words per line, exclamations and rhetorical questions. Treatment as a 1930 optical track (`period_voice()` in `audio/mix.py`): high-pass 260 Hz, low-pass 3.7 kHz, +4 dB presence at 1.5–2.3 kHz (horn loudspeaker), tanh drive 2.0, compression 4:1, wow 0.22 % at 0.55 Hz + flutter 0.07 % at 6.3 Hz, two short wall reflections (31 ms, 67 ms). The speech check runs on the clean voice and again on the finished film (`tools/final_asr.py`).\n- **Band** (original): tuba on 1 and 3, banjo four to the bar (accent 2 and 4), trombones on the Charleston figure (1 and the \"and\" of 2), snare backbeat, honky-tonk piano right hand, muted then open trumpet melody with clarinet a sixth below, woodblock pickups; a brass fanfare with timpani for the ident; a dead stop with one stab for the punchline; \"da – DA!\" to close.\n- **Counter-music**: when the story has two sides, give the other side its own band (here an old-time reel: fiddle, plucked dulcimer, upright piano, tuba) and let them trade bars, then half bars, in a split screen, panned left and right.\n- **Archive record**: a public-domain 78 can take over the soundtrack as \"the real thing\": needle drop (click + surface noise), band-pass 110–6500 Hz, crackle and rotation rumble only while the needle is down, varispeed by ≤ 2 % to sit on the film's tempo; end it on its own final chord and lift the needle.\n- **Foley** for every move: carbon-arc snap, rising whoosh with flutter for the spinning paper, thump + paper slap, telegraph clicks under a map line, camera shutter per still, paper flicks per flip-book page, slat flutter, page turn, grease-pencil squeak, soft-lead scratch.\n- **Bed**: projector (24 Hz claw clatter, motor hum, optical hiss) from first to last frame.\n- **Mix**: the band and record ducked ≈ 4–5 dB under the voice (voice ≈ 12 dB over the music), the band band-limited 95–7500 Hz with a low shelf and light saturation; two silences (a dead stop before a turn, only the projector before the button); −14 LUFS.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu; use the ones the story needs, each once.\n\n- **The ident ritual.** Searchlights snap on with a flash in the first 0.2 s, the globe grows and turns, the name lands with overshoot, the iris closes on the globe. Code: `sceneLogo()` — `sunburst()`, `beams()`, `globe()`, `decoTitle()`. *Fits:* the opening of any news-shaped film; the reprise is the sign-off.\n- **The spinning paper.** Grows by `u^2.3`, rotation decays by `(1−u)^1.25` over 2.5 turns, three ghost copies 18 ms apart as motion blur, a vertical jolt (damped sine, 16 px) and a 3 % bounce on the slam, then push and punch in on the next beat. *Fits:* a headline, a decree, a press release.\n- **The map spread.** A projected outline (Natural Earth, Albers), dotted arcs from the source city drawn on the beat with telegraph clicks, star pulses on arrival, place labels in screen space, an odometer of years in a corner; push into the city of the next story. *Fits:* a craze, a product launch, a migration, a campaign.\n- **Grid deal → flip-book.** Stills land one per beat (shutter click each), then the camera dives into the first cell and the series plays on the beat as stop motion. *Fits:* a how-to, the stages of a process, a collection.\n- **Document underline.** Slam a book or letter, turn the page, push to the paragraph, draw soft-lead underlines word-exact (positions from the scan's OCR boxes, e.g. archive.org hOCR). *Fits:* a quote, a rule, a contract clause.\n- **Grease-pencil freeze.** Freeze the telling frame, jolt it, ring the detail with a hand-drawn loop (`pencil()` + `ring()`), add an arrow. *Fits:* the evidence, the punchline, \"look here\".\n- **Split-screen duel → push-over.** Two panels slide in, each labelled, each with its own music in its own channel trading bars; the winner pushes the loser out of frame. *Fits:* old vs new, rival products, two cities.\n- **Needle drop.** The band cuts dead, a 78 spins in the corner, the period record plays under the archive and ends on its own chord at the freeze. *Fits:* any era that has public-domain recordings.\n- **Band stop + button.** One stab, silence with only the projector, one short line from the announcer. *Fits:* the last word of any story.\n\n## 9. Material: where it comes from\n\n1. **Ask the user first** whether they have their own material: film or video, photographs, newspapers, letters, documents, data, a period recording. Their archive beats anything found online.\n2. **If not, search sources whose licence is clear**, with `demo/tools/footage.py` (`search archive|commons|nasa`, `files`, `fetch`, `used`, `md`; it records licence, author and page in `SOURCES.jsonl`):\n   - **U.S. National Archives** (films, often via Internet Archive \"FedFlix\" copies): federal works are public domain; donated collections need their own rights note (e.g. the Ford Motor Company Film Collection: all rights transferred to NARA in 1962, \"copyright- and royalty-free\" except contractor films). Universal Newsreel (1929–1967) is in NARA too.\n   - **Internet Archive**: check every item's licence field and provenance; skip anything that is a YouTube mirror, a colourised or \"restored/AI enhanced\" version, or has no stated source.\n   - **Wikimedia Commons**: read the licence template on the file page; use PD-US-expired, PD-US-no-notice, PD-USGov, CC0, CC BY. A Library of Congress \"No known restrictions\" (often with \"Copyright-tag-still-required\") is **not** enough, nor is DPLA \"no known restrictions\"; CC BY-SA is not used in library demos.\n   - **Library of Congress** (loc.gov, tile.loc.gov; Chronicling America newspapers before 1931), **NASA**, other national archives.\n   - **Print**: anything published in the US before 1931 (newspapers, magazines, books on archive.org / HathiTrust / Commons) is public domain in the US.\n   - **Recordings**: US sound recordings published before 1926 are public domain in the US from 1 January 2026 (Music Modernization Act: 100 years after publication for pre-1947 recordings); UCSB Library / Internet Archive transfers (Edison, Victor) and the LOC National Jukebox give dates and matrix numbers. Check the composition's date too.\n   - **Maps**: Natural Earth (public domain) for outlines drawn in code.\n3. **Before downloading, tell the user** what you will fetch, from where, under which licence, and how big it is; keep `raw/` under ~1.5 GB.\n4. Everything used goes into `demo/CREDITS` and `SOURCES.md` (title, author, date, licence, page, which seconds). Real people: historical public figures and people in news events may appear; ordinary people's faces are never slowed down or made the close-up subject.\n5. Every fact in the voice and on screen goes into `demo/FACTS.md` with its source and the quoted wording; read the catalogue description of each film before using it (it may say what is really happening in the shot).\n\n## 10. Pitfalls\n\n- Catalogue descriptions matter: a picnic film can contain both old-time dances and the Charleston; use only the shots that match the claim.\n- Archive transfers start and end with archive slates, \"End of Recording\" and white flashes; cut inside them. Remove watermarks and archive logos.\n- Newspaper pages hold unrelated, sometimes offensive stories next to yours: crop to the fold you need.\n- Per-frame auto-levels make footage pump; use one level set per clip.\n- `destination-in` irises leave transparent corners that later turn grey under `screen` — paint black behind with `destination-over`.\n- Captions that fade before they can be read fail `readcheck`; shorten the words, not the hold.\n- A fast announcer outruns Chinese subtitles: 6–12 English words per line, ≤ 23 Chinese characters.\n- TTS mispronounces names (Bessie → \"Bestie\"); rephrase until the speech check passes.\n- Old sources are soft: never exceed ~2.5× upscale; prefer wider framings to crops.\n- Grain belongs to the mux; don't add it in the page as well.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\n- **Era**: late silent newsreel (no voice, title cards only) ↔ early sound newsreel (this style) ↔ 1940s wartime newsreel (deeper voice, maps with arrows, march music).\n- **Tone**: comic stinger (this demo) ↔ straight reportage (no jokes, still fast) — not for disasters and deaths, where the jaunty grammar is wrong.\n- **Material balance**: all-footage, or print-heavy when film is scarce (maps, pages, flip-books carry the motion).\n- **Music**: Charleston band, march band, swing orchestra, or a period record carrying a whole section.\n- **Packaging**: invent your own ident name and emblem; keep the grammar (searchlights, globe, fanfare, place cards), change the shapes.\n\nOur demo, its story and build: [`DEMO.md`](DEMO.md).\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nKnock-Knee Alert\n"},{"id":"S4-L3","name":"倒计时分屏","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L3.jpg","markdown":"# L3 倒计时分屏（Live Countdown Split-screen）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 剪辑与纪实\n- 长什么样：阿尔忒弥斯二号发射的最后一分钟，一秒不剪：格子从 1 开到 9，点火合成 1。\n- 适合：对比、倒计时，比如\"三个月\"\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Live Countdown Split-screen — Style Prompt\n\n> An edit style, not a look. Real footage of one event with a known zero (a launch, a verdict, a final whistle, polls closing), shown in real time: screen time equals event time, a clock ticks every second, and the screen splits into more and more cells as the moment nears, every cell showing the same second. At zero the cells merge into one picture and the clock flips.\n> References (grammar only): the real-time clock and splitting screens of the TV series *24*; the multi-camera archive panels and mission-time codes of the documentary *Apollo 11* (2019). Never copy their type, colours, sounds, graphics or shots, and never name them in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n1. **Real time.** One continuous stretch of the event, never cut, never re-ordered, never sped up. Slow-motion sources are played back at real speed. The clock and the picture can be checked against each other at any frame.\n2. **The clock is the lead.** It ticks every whole second (sound and a falling digit), it says who is in control, and it changes state at zero.\n3. **Cells show the same second.** Every video cell is synced to the clock, or says plainly that it is not (§8, \"sync badge\").\n4. **The layout is the edit.** Cuts are layout changes, and every layout change lands on a real spoken call or event in the native sound, not on the music.\n5. **More cells, closer to zero; one picture at zero.** The number of cells is the tension curve.\n\nNot a montage (time jumps), not a security wall (the layout never moves), not a flashback structure (real time cannot go back), not a slide deck (cells that don't follow events, text filling the cells, centred titles with page turns).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Three kinds of material at once**: video cameras of the same moment (2–8), documents (a fact sheet, a filing, a schedule: shown as paper, never retyped as a slide), and photographs (the people of the day). Our own type explains them; type printed in the material is only picture.\n- **Data drawn by us**: checklists, bars, a flight or process profile, a second countdown to the next milestone. Every number comes from an official source (§10).\n- **Cells**: hard rectangles on near-black `#06080A`, 24 px outer margin, 8–9 px gutters, 1 px border `rgba(222,232,238,.16)`, a light radial vignette (to 36 % black) on video cells. No rounded corners, no drop shadows, no 3D.\n- **Camera chip** top-left of every cell: dark plate 84 %, a square dot (orange = synced video, grey = unsynced or still), code (`CAM 4`, `DOC`, `LIST`), Chinese name, small English name.\n- **Burned-in text in the sources** (broadcast bugs, camera IDs, timers, tracker readouts) is cropped by a per-shot usable region, or blurred out (ffmpeg `delogo`) after it has been used for syncing. Never shown as if it were ours.\n- **One grade for every source** (ffmpeg): `eq=contrast=1.07:saturation=0.9:gamma=0.97:brightness=-0.01,colorbalance=rs=-0.02:bs=0.03:rh=0.03:bh=-0.02`, then film grain 1 at the master (`mux.sh … 1`). Cool shadows, slightly warm highlights: control rooms and launch pads look like one film.\n- **Resolution**: modern footage at most 1.5× its source pixels; for a punch-in, extract that stretch at a higher resolution from the 4K source instead of enlarging a 1080p frame. Documents at ≥ 1 source pixel per screen pixel.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n| Role | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Ground | `#06080A`; panels `#0B0E11` |\n| Main text | `#F2F5F7`; secondary `#7C878D` |\n| Accent: the T− sign, synced dots, ticks done, event flags, the active control segment | `#FF5B1F` |\n| Speaking light, \"heard\" tags | `#FF3B30` |\n| Plates over full-screen footage | `rgba(6,8,10,.80)` |\n| After zero, the clock block | ground `#FF5B1F`, text `#0A0B0C` (inverted) |\n\nOrange means \"now / synced / done\". Grey means \"pending / not synced\". Nothing else is coloured; colour belongs to the footage.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- Clock digits: a condensed sans with fixed-width digit slots (ours: Barlow Condensed SemiBold, drawn digit by digit in equal slots so the clock never jitters); size up to 132 px, the T−/T+ sign at 0.85×, tenths at 0.41× in grey, a ten-segment sub-second bar under it.\n- Chinese: a sturdy sans (ours: Noto Sans SC 500/700, subset). English text: a clean grotesque (Barlow). Labels, codes and English small print: a monospace (IBM Plex Mono). All OFL, in the project's `fonts/`.\n- **The live transcript is the subtitle.** A fixed box (bottom right, or a cell in the grid): a speaking light and the speaker type (commentary / loop / narrator — the narrator, our own voice, is marked in orange so it is never mistaken for the event's audio), the English line appearing word by word at the spoken times (0.08 s fade per word, a block cursor), the Chinese line whole when its cue starts. **One line per language, never wrapped**: a long sentence becomes two cues; each cue ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s). Transcribe only what is said; mark doubtful words in `FACTS.md`.\n- Reading time for everything else: CJK ÷ 4.5 + other ÷ 15 + 1.5 s from the moment it is complete (`readcheck.mjs`). A comparison (two bars, two numbers) stays fully on screen ≥ 2.5 s.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- 30 fps. Clock: on each whole second the new digits drop 14 px in 0.12 s (ease-out); the sub-second bar empties one segment per 0.1 s (fills after zero).\n- Re-layout: 0.34 s `easeInOutCubic`. Cells that stay interpolate their rectangles; new cells open from their left edge; leaving cells close to the left; a new cell flashes a 3 px orange border for ~0.2 s when it lands. A grid filling up pops one cell per spoken count in 0.30 s.\n- Swaps (one source replaces another in the same rectangle) wipe in from the left. Hard cuts only between full-screen sources.\n- Photographs push in ≤ 6 % over their whole stay; names pop one by one 0.16 s apart. Documents move their window down a row in 0.45 s; the highlight sweeps left to right in 0.35 s.\n- Never: slow push-ins on video to fake motion, page-turn transitions, centred pop-up words, speed ramps.\n\n## 6. Edit grammar\n\n- **Screen time = event time.** One mapping, `M = t − T0`, for the whole film. Each camera has its own source offset (`s = M + c`) measured once and baked into the frame extraction (`demo/tools/prep.sh`): frames are named by film frame number, so the page never computes sync.\n- **Cut points are calls.** Each layout change starts on the first word of a real call in the native audio (\"ten\", \"go for…\", \"ignition\", a name) or on an event visible in the footage. The music follows; it never sets a cut.\n- **Cell count rises toward zero**: 1 → 2 → 3–4 → many (6–9, the clock itself becomes the centre cell, the transcript a cell) → **one** at zero → a few again after zero (the event's consequences) → one under the title. The shortest layouts are nearest to zero.\n- **Inside a cell, the source cuts itself** (a broadcast switching cameras): keep it; we only cut the layout.\n- **Silence before zero**: the music drops out a quarter-second before zero; only the native sound and the clock remain.\n- Full-screen moments carry the clock and transcript as plates at the bottom; a gradient (0 → 75 % black) under them.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\nLayers, top to bottom:\n\n1. **Native sound** — the event's own audio (commentary, radio loops, crowd), high-pass 90 Hz, compressed, always on top; never ducked.\n2. **Narrator** (sparingly, only in gaps of the native sound, never over it): clean and close, a different gender or accent from the event's voices; Kokoro, every line through `asr_check.py`.\n3. **The clock**: a tick every whole second (two alternating pitches around 1.5–1.9 kHz, panned ±15 %), louder in the last ten seconds, a soft single pitch after zero.\n4. **Interface foley** from `window.EV`: re-layout whoosh + metallic snap; cell pop snap; checklist tock; event-flag whoosh; full-screen cut thump.\n5. **Score**, 60 BPM so one beat = one second = one tick: a low synth drone whose low-pass opens toward zero, a bass drum per beat, off-beat hats, a tom per spoken count, timpani roll into zero, silence, then a low brass major chord with bass drum, crash and tubular bell at zero; after zero a new texture (pizzicato bass ostinato, lighter tick). Low brass, timpani, toms, tubular bells, glockenspiel; no piano-and-strings pad.\n- Mix: music about 8–10 dB under the native sound and ducked under speech; the ignition (or the event's own climax sound) is the loudest moment; master −14 LUFS via a pre-limited mix so `mux.sh` meets the true-peak target.\n- **Changing the music** (e.g. a Suno track): never move the clock or the cuts; choose or stretch a track so a downbeat lands exactly on zero and a section change lands on T−10, and keep the quarter-second silence before zero.\n\n## 8. Native moves (and how to build them)\n\n- **Many into one.** At zero, every cell moves to its tile of the full frame (a 3×3 grid becomes nine 640×360 tiles) while each tile cross-fades to the same full-screen shot, chips and borders vanish in the first half, a warm white flash (`rgba(255,248,236,.7)`, decay τ ≈ 0.11 s) peaks, and the clock and transcript slide from their grid cells back to the bottom. 0.42 s. *Fits:* a verdict read out; the goal at the final whistle.\n- **Clock inversion.** At zero the clock block flips from dark with an orange T− to orange with dark T+, and its header changes (countdown → elapsed). *Fits:* polls closing, a store opening.\n- **Control strip.** Segments in the clock header (who has control: ground → vehicle → mission control; prosecution → jury → judge); the active one fills orange on the call that hands it over, with a short flash. The story's turns live here.\n- **The grid fills on the count.** Empty grid slots show as dashed \"standby\" frames; each spoken number opens one camera. *Fits:* \"three, two, one\" before a premiere.\n- **Sync badge.** Top-right of each video cell: `● 实时同步 LIVE SYNC` (synced), `○ 同一时段 · 未逐帧同步 SAME WINDOW` (hatched, grey: footage from the same minutes without a usable clock), `慢动作还原 1× REAL-TIME` (slow-motion re-timed). Honesty is part of the look.\n- **Planned vs heard checklist.** Official milestones with their planned times tick as the clock passes them; when the native audio actually says one, a red \"heard −32.1\" tag with a small waveform appears beside it. *Fits:* a court schedule against what was said; a train timetable against the platform announcement.\n- **Second countdown.** After zero, a smaller clock counts to the next official milestone (`−1:47 to booster separation, expected T+2:09`) along a schematic profile drawn by time, not distance. *Fits:* the next race split; the next ballot count.\n- **Event flag.** An orange bar with black type slides in at the bottom of the relevant cell on its call (`RS-25 ×4 启动 ENGINE START · T−6.36`), held ≥ the reading time.\n- **Punch-in on a call.** A hard digital punch-in on the relevant detail when the commentary names it, from a higher-resolution extraction of the same source.\n- **Document by rows.** A paper close-up shows two or three rows; a highlight bar and an annotation band below the paper translate the row; the window moves only between rows (its edges in the white space).\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **Unsyncable footage** (an edited \"rover\" reel with no clock or event to align on). Never fake it: label the cell \"same window · not frame-synced\", keep it out of the countdown's key seconds or use it only where nothing it shows contradicts the time, and say so in `FACTS.md`. Look first for a source with a burned-in timer, a visible ignition / flash / whistle, or the same audio.\n- **Measuring zero.** Never trust metadata. Combine two independent marks: a visible event (ignition flash, a broadcast countdown bug's flip) and the spoken word timing (\"booster\" at zero). Re-measure on the file you actually cut: `ffmpeg -ss … -c copy` starts on the previous keyframe.\n- **Audio and picture of one file can disagree** (a switched feed whose audio lagged its video by ~1 s). Sync each camera on its picture.\n- **Slow-motion sources**: measure the factor from two events with a known gap (e.g. engine start to booster ignition = 6.36 s) and re-time with `setpts=PTS/k`.\n- **Burned-in text**: broadcast bugs, camera numbers, timers, tracker data. Define per-shot usable regions (they change when the source cuts) or `delogo` them; check every source cut with a frame-difference scan.\n- **A static wide shot for ten seconds** reads as flat. Give every 1–2 seconds a new element (a cell, a tick on the list, a flag, a punch-in), not a fake camera move.\n- **Spoilers in the subtitle**: a whole-sentence Chinese line reveals the end of a count early. Split counts into separate cues.\n- **The clock jitters** if digits are proportional: draw them in fixed slots.\n- **Hot peaks** from broadcast audio and the ignition hit miss the true-peak target: pre-limit the mix at about −4 dBFS after normalising to −14 LUFS.\n- **Logos.** No agency insignia as a graphic; cut slates and bugs; the film says it is unofficial.\n\n## 10. Materials, sources, facts, engine\n\n**Materials — ask first.** Before searching, ask the user whether they have their own material of the event: footage from several angles, recordings of the audio, photos, documents (schedules, filings, results), data. What they give comes first and is credited as they say.\n\n**If not, where to look** (each item's own page decides; record it):\n- **NASA** (images.nasa.gov, svs.gsfc.nasa.gov): U.S. federal government works, public domain in the U.S.; fine to use. Don't use NASA insignia as graphics or imply endorsement; check each item for third-party material.\n- **Other U.S. federal agencies** (NOAA, National Archives, DoD DVIDS): public domain, same check for third-party material.\n- **Internet Archive**: only items whose own record shows public domain (pre-1931 U.S. publications, U.S. government works) or CC0 / CC BY; skip `youtube-*` / \"mirrortube\" uploads and anything of unclear origin.\n- **Wikimedia Commons**: read each file page; CC0, public domain and CC BY are fine (credit CC BY in full); CC BY-SA and \"no known restrictions\" are not fine without the user's explicit agreement.\n- **Library of Congress**: check each item's rights statement; \"no known restrictions\" is not CC0.\n- Not usable: re-uploads without a source, restored or colourised versions, news-agency footage, anything whose licence is not stated.\n\n**Before downloading, tell the user** what you will fetch, from where and under which licence, in one message. Then fetch only the seconds you need: `python3 demo/tools/grab.py <nasa_id> <orig|large> <start> <duration> <name> [--copy]` reads an HTTP range of a NASA original; `python3 demo/tools/footage.py search|fetch|used|md` searches NASA, Commons and the Internet Archive and records licence, author and page in `SOURCES.jsonl` / `SOURCES.md` automatically. Keep `raw/` under about 1.5 GB.\n\n**Facts**: a `FACTS.md` (claim, URL, quoted wording, or the measurement) covering every number, time, name and call on screen and in the narration; official sources only; planned times and actual (heard) times are kept apart. Check the script against it before the last render.\n\n**Engine** (copy into the project): `demo/index.html` (Canvas 2D: `LAY` layout keyframes with `grid` / `merge` / `cut` / `swap`, `camRegion()` usable regions, `crop()` cover framing, `drawClock()`, `drawTx()`, `drawCheck()`, `drawDoc()`, `drawProfile()`, `drawThrust()`, `window.EV` for the mix, `window.TEXTS` for `readcheck`, `window.SUBFIT` for the one-line subtitle check), `demo/tools/prep.sh` (the sync table → frames), `demo/tools/subs.mjs` (cues → `audio/cues.json`, one-line and duration check), `demo/audio/mix.py` (native sound, ticks, foley, score, ducking, pre-master), `demo/tools/final_asr.py` (Whisper on the finished film), `demo/tools/fetch_fonts.py`.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nThe agent decides per film: the event, where real time starts and ends, the number of cameras, which documents and data, the control segments, the narrator's lines (or none), the closing layout.\n\n- **Clocks**: T−/T+; a match clock; a wall clock (HH:MM:SS) for a news day; a countdown to a deadline that then counts up as \"overtime\".\n- **Three structures**: rising cells to a merge (this demo); one cell that splits outward after zero (results arriving from many places); two parallel countdowns side by side that meet at zero (two teams, two cities).\n- **Three openings**: a single wide shot and the first call; the clock alone on black, then the first camera opens; a document (the schedule) with the first item ticking.\n- **Three endings**: the inverted clock over one full-screen shot; the grid refilling with consequences, then one title; the second countdown reaching its own zero.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, layouts, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nCounting Itself Down\n"},{"id":"S4-L4","name":"延时摄影交响","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L4.jpg","markdown":"# L4 延时摄影交响（Time-lapse Symphony）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 剪辑与纪实\n- 长什么样：纽约的一天：延时光河、踩着十六分音符的网格，1899 年和今天并排。\n- 适合：时间过去、城市节奏\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Time-lapse Symphony — Style Prompt\n\n> A city film cut like a piece of minimalist music: time-lapse turns traffic, crowds and clouds into rivers of light, the cuts get denser with the score's subdivisions, one thing is slowed almost to a stop, and the film ends on a hard cut to black on a downbeat. No narrator. The music carries it.\n> Grammar learned from the city-symphony tradition and the \"Qatsi\" films (Reggio / Fricke, scores in the Philip Glass manner). Nothing is taken from those films: no footage, no melody, no title lettering, no names in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n- **An edit style.** The picture is real footage of a place (time-lapse, hyperlapse, aerials, slow motion, archive film, photographs). The style lives in *how time is bent* and *where the cuts fall*, not in a drawn look.\n- **The score is the script.** It is written first, with a clear arc: minimal repetition → acceleration → climax → a sudden stop → acceleration again → hard stop → a quiet coda. Every cut sits on a beat or a subdivision of it.\n- **Machines fast, people slow.** Cars, trains, clouds and light are sped up until they become lines; the one slowed moment is something fragile (a soap bubble, steam, a flag, a flock), and people appear as crowds, tracks or long-exposure ghosts.\n- **Few words, each one earned.** On-screen text is a title, a handful of numbers that state the size of the place (each from an official source), an archive date, and one line from a real public-domain text at the end.\n- **It is not** a travel reel (no drone-porn montage on a pop track), a vlog, a news package, an explainer with a narrator, or a slideshow of postcards. No lower thirds with names, no headers, no centred title cards with page-turn transitions, no logo stings.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- Footage only (2D). Sources mixed freely: tripod time-lapse, long single-camera time-lapse (≥ 12 h, sunrise and sunset in the same frame), hyperlapse, drone aerials (top-down is gold), high-frame-rate slow motion, archive film (pre-1931), photographs (a pair from the same camera position is gold), a scanned page of a historic text.\n- Everything is unified into one **35 mm release-print** look: 1.85:1 matte (21 px bars on 1920×1080), warm highlights, lifted blacks, highlight bloom, clumped mid-tone grain, ±0.5 px gate weave, soft vignette.\n- Never generated images; never restored, colourised or speed-corrected archive copies; never YouTube mirrors of archive films.\n- Upscale limits: archive ≤ 2.5× (denoise → lanczos → light sharpen → grain); modern ≤ 1.5×.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n| Use | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Type (warm white) | `#EDE8DF` |\n| Secondary line (English, labels) | `#B0AAA0` |\n| Sodium orange — the only accent (rules under numbers, source lines, crowd tracks, the day ring's hand) | `#D9772F` |\n| Matte and black | `#000000` |\n\nGrade (`tools/render.py → make_luts / grade`): per-shot partial grey-world white balance first (only pixels with luminance 0.12–0.88, strength 0–0.6; **0 for night shots**, which keep their sodium and blue), then one LUT for all shots: S-curve (sigmoid slope 6.2, pivot 0.47, blended 55/45 with linear) → black 7/255, white 250/255 → R ×1.025, B ×0.965 (warm highlights, a little cold in the shadows) → saturation 0.86. Bloom: pixels above 170/255, blurred 6 px at ⅛ size, added back × (1, .82, .62); 0.09 by day, 0.15–0.2 at night. Grain: half-resolution Gaussian noise up-sampled and blurred 0.6 px, weighted `0.35 + 1.3·L·(1−L)`, strength 9/255. Archive film stays black-and-white and goes through the same LUT, so it reads as part of the same print.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- Latin: **Michroma** (OFL), capitals, tracking 0.06–0.32 em. Chinese: **Noto Sans SC** variable, weight 300–350, tracking 0.08–0.14 em. Credits: Noto Sans SC 17 px.\n- **Left-aligned**, left margin 168–196 px, never centred, never on a box. Legibility comes from a very soft elliptical darkening behind the block (α ≤ 0.6), not a panel.\n- **Number card**: the number in Michroma 84 px (44 px when three numbers stack); a 2 px sodium rule grows under it; one Chinese line (28 px) and one English line (14–16 px) say what it counts and the source year. A census-type number locks digit by digit on sixteenths (one glockenspiel note per digit); a flow number counts up from 0 in 0.6 s (ease-out). \"About\" numbers carry `≈` and \"约\".\n- **Archive date**: just the year, Michroma 22 px, bottom-left of the archive panel.\n- **Closing quote**: on the page shot the whole sentence is lit; on the card, a short fragment over the last moving shot — Chinese 60 px, English original in capitals 20 px, source line 14 px sodium, fading in within 0.25 s and held just past the reading time.\n- One line per language, never wrapped; every text block stays fully in frame at least CJK ÷ 4.5 + other ÷ 15 + 1.5 s (`readcheck.mjs`). Text may stay up across cuts underneath it, but only while the shots under it belong to what it counts.\n- `.srt`: the film has no voice, so the subtitle file carries the on-screen text (one Chinese and one English line per cue) for muted or accessible playback.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- Speed is the main instrument. Shot speeds run from 0.3× to 75×; every speed above 1× uses **frame blending** (a 360° shutter): each output frame averages the source frames it covers, capped at N frames. Headlights become continuous lines, crowds become fog, clouds become water.\n- **Speed ramps** are smooth (smoothstep between key speeds) and always mean something: a corridor accelerates out of real time (1× → 6×), a time-lapse ramps into a frenzy (1× → 7×), a fast shot brakes into slow motion (2.2× → 0.3× in 0.55 s) on the bar where the music stops.\n- Camera moves are added sparingly in post: slow push-ins (1.00 → 1.05–1.10 over the shot), a falling push on the opening still (1.0 → 1.3 in 0.35 s, ease-out, then accelerating to ×2 by the cut). No shakes, no whip pans, no digital zooms past the upscale limit.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar — editing\n\n**Cut-point table (the score decides; numbers at 120 BPM and 30 fps: 1 beat = 15 frames, triplet = 5, sixteenth = 3.75)**\n\n| Score texture | Cut length | Frames | Use |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Opening hit + eighth-note arpeggio | 2 bars → 1 bar | 120 → 60 | the opening still, the first single-camera time-lapse |\n| Eighths | 2 beats → 1 beat | 30 → 15 | dawn, match cuts |\n| Triplets against eighths (3:2) | 1 beat → ½ beat | 15 → 7/8 alternating | morning, the montage of streets |\n| Sixteenths, full ensemble | grid cells light every 1–2 frames; cells re-cut every sixteenth | 1–4 | the grid |\n| Arpeggios stop, one long chord | 3–4 s, one or two shots | 90–120 | the brake: the slowed thing, the archive split |\n| Sixteenths + triplets + horns | ½ beat → 4 frames → 2 frames | 7 → 4 → 2 | dusk to night, the reverse flashback |\n| Dry cut of the whole ensemble, reverb left ringing | hard cut to black on the bar line | — | the hard stop |\n\nRules:\n\n- **Cuts land on beats.** All cut times come from one function, `bar(n, beat)` in `tools/timeline.py`; shot lengths are never typed in seconds. The music is rendered from the same file.\n- **One long single-camera time-lapse is the clock.** It returns three or four times (night → dawn, noon clouds, a one-second nightfall, a day/night flicker), always from the same position, so the viewer knows the time without a caption.\n- **Density follows the score.** The editor never cuts faster than the current subdivision, and the slowest picture sits under the place where the arpeggios stop.\n- **Shape match cuts** on the beat: clock → clock, arch → arch, grid of streets → grid of cells. A small-to-large punch-in on the same shape is allowed.\n- **The grid** is the split screen of this style: 1×2 → 2×2 → 3×3 → 4×4, one stage per half bar or bar, the new cells lighting up in a scattered order over the previous stage within 0.3 s (never back to black), gutters 6 px black; in the last beat the cells re-cut on every sixteenth.\n- **Counterpoint split**: two halves, archive left, today right, the same kind of place or shape; the gutter grows from the centre in 8 frames.\n- **Day/night flicker**: the same camera, a noon frame and a night frame alternating every 2 frames; a pair of same-position photographs alternating every 2 frames.\n- **Reverse flashback**: in the last bar before the hard stop, earlier shots return in reverse order, 4 frames each, then 2 frames each.\n- Transitions: hard cuts only, plus the hard cut to black. No dissolves except fades from black for the page and the end card.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Music only.** No narrator, no source sound (the footage soundtracks are other people's music), no sound effects. A narrator would turn watching into listening and would fight the score for the structure; that is why the style has none. If a voice is ever required, one short line at the very start or end, never over the cuts.\n- Orchestration (sampled, CC0 — `core/audio/sampler.py`): pipe organ (full and soft stops, pedal), winds staccato (flute, clarinet), strings (spiccato cellos and violins, sustained cellos, violins, double bass), horn pair for the last two bars, concert bass drum on section downbeats, suspended-cymbal crescendos into the hits, glockenspiel only for numbers locking.\n- Writing: arpeggios over a one-chord-per-bar progression; additive layering (eighths → triplets over eighths → sixteenths); the *break* is all arpeggios stopping at once into one held chord (maj7 or 9th); the return is a full-organ hit; the hard stop is a dry cut of every instrument in 15 ms with the reverb tail left ringing; the coda is one soft chord that does not resolve, with the opening figure returning in quarter notes under the end card.\n- Reverb: `S.room(size .7, mix .28)` for the body, `size .78, mix .36` for the coda. Master to −14 LUFS / −1.2 dBTP with `core/render/mux.sh` (grain 0: the grain is already in the picture).\n\n## 8. Native moves (and how they are built)\n\n| Move | Code |\n|---|---|\n| **Light river** | `tools/prep.py`: integrate the speed curve to source time; each output frame averages the source frames in its window (cap `blend`). Speed 40× / blend 30 at night, 37.5× / 24 for clouds, 75× / 30 for a one-second nightfall. |\n| **Speed ramp** | `speed=[(t, v), …]` keyframes, smoothstep between them, same integrator. |\n| **Brake into slow motion** | ramp 2.2× → 0.3× with `interp=3` (ffmpeg `minterpolate` mci, bidir) for soft subjects; `mix=True` (crossfade of the two nearest frames) for thin lines such as cables, where motion compensation tears. |\n| **Crowd → tracks** (people as data) | `Trails` in `tools/render.py`: background = per-pixel median of the whole fixed-camera shot; blobs that differ by > 22/255 (scipy `ndimage.label`, area 8–900 px at half size) are people; each centroid joins the nearest one from the previous frame (< 70 px) with a 2 px line; lines fade × 0.9 per frame; the plate is darkened and desaturated as the tracks appear (0.15 → 0.55 s). Fixed cameras only. |\n| **Traffic → light** | a static or slowly drifting top-down aerial at 5× with full frame blending. |\n| **Grid** | `grid_plate()`: pool clips of 30 frames held in memory at 960×540; stages from `GRID_STAGES`; light-up order scattered; last beat re-cuts cells every sixteenth. |\n| **Counterpoint split** | `split_plate()`: archive 4:3 centre 960 px | today centre 960 px, 6 px gutter grown from the centre. |\n| **Day/night flicker** | two 15-frame clips from the same camera (noon, night), alternating every 2 frames. |\n| **Photo that moves** | a same-position photo pair alternating every 2 frames. |\n| **Number lock** | `lock_digits()`: one digit locks per sixteenth, the others roll; `counter()`: ease-out count-up; music adds one glockenspiel note per lock. |\n| **Day ring** | a thin 24-hour ring bottom-right with a sodium hand and a 3-hour arc: it runs with the film's day, **freezes and dims during the brake**, and **spins backwards during the reverse flashback**. It is a graphic, not a clock reading. |\n| **Reading light on a page** | scanned page, 16:9 window pushed 1.06 → 1.56 page widths, side light (bright top-left), paper tint (1, .93, .80), a Gaussian band (σ 95–140 px) over the quoted line growing to +38 %. |\n| **Hard stop** | picture cuts to black on the bar line; the music's dry signal is cut in 15 ms on the same frame; only reverb remains. |\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **Watermarks and slates hidden in CC footage**: corner logos (scale from the centre until they leave frame, within 1.5×), opening thank-you cards, end-card music credits, title text over the first shots. Check a 1-second contact sheet (`tools/secsheet.py`) of every source before choosing in-points.\n- **Hidden cuts inside sources.** Hyperlapse reels hide 2–3-frame jump cuts and whip transitions; frame blending across a cut makes ghosts. Scan with `ffmpeg … select='gt(scene,0.25)',showinfo` and keep every clip's source window inside one shot.\n- **Too much blending** turns noon clouds into grey mush: about 40 % shutter (24 of 60 frames) looks better than 100 %.\n- **Grey-world balance kills night**: it washes sodium light to white. Strength 0 for night shots.\n- **Tracking needs a fixed camera.** A drifting drone flags every building as motion. Use a median background only on tripod shots; for moving aerials use blending instead.\n- **Motion-compensated slow motion tears thin lines** (cables, rails, text): use the crossfade mode there.\n- **Text over bright sky** disappears: put number cards over the dark half of the frame or add the soft darkening; never a box.\n- **A slow face is not a free subject.** Do not make an identifiable ordinary person the slowed or close-up hero. Slow a thing; show people as crowds, tracks or ghosts. Public figures and people in news events are a different case.\n- **Numbers drift.** Official pages update; quote the page and the year, keep the quote in `FACTS.md`, and recheck before the last render. Prefer one clean figure over a range.\n- **CC BY credits are long.** Each one needs author, full title and licence on the end card. Treat them as fine print (16 px, 29 px line pitch, the licence and changes on one header line) and keep the end card short (4–5 s); a 10 s card of text on black loses the audience. The film's last moving shot runs on under the card, darkened.\n- **Don't end on dead black.** After the hard stop, the coda is a page (≈1 s), then the quote laid over one last moving city shot (≈4 s, just long enough to pass `readcheck`), then the end card over the same shot.\n- Commons rate-limits (HTTP 429): search and download serially with back-off (`tools/s.sh`, `tools/f.sh`); for long 4K files download only the needed excerpt (`tools/seg.py`) or the 1080p transcode.\n\n## 10. Footage: where it comes from\n\n1. **Ask the user first** whether they have their own footage, photographs, documents or data for the place: their own time-lapses, a phone hyperlapse, family photos, a city's open data. Their material comes first.\n2. If not, search these sources (all searchable with `demo/tools/footage.py`, which records title, author, licence and page in `SOURCES.jsonl` and writes `SOURCES.md`):\n   - **Wikimedia Commons** — modern time-lapse, aerial and slow-motion footage under CC BY / CC0, photographs (for example the Carol M. Highsmith Archive, dedicated to the public domain), scans of old maps and books. Check the licence on each file page. Many videos are YouTube or Vimeo imports: use one only when its page carries a passed review (YouTubeReview / VimeoReview / LicenseReview / Flickrreview with a reviewer and a date) or the author's own source page still shows the licence. A pending review is not enough: a channel can later switch to the standard licence and claim the footage through Content ID.\n   - **Library of Congress** (loc.gov, also mirrored on Commons) — archive films published in the US before 1931 are public domain; a rights statement of \"no known restrictions\" alone is **not** enough: you need the publication date or another public-domain basis.\n   - **NASA** (images.nasa.gov, ISS crew photographs) — public domain; the night photos of cities from orbit make a strong opening.\n   - **Internet Archive** — scanned books and documents marked NOT_IN_COPYRIGHT with a pre-1931 publication date; take the line you quote from the scan, not from a modern edition.\n   - **National archives** (US National Archives, other governments' open archives) — check each country's terms.\n   - Licences allowed: public domain, CC0, CC BY. Not allowed: CC BY-SA, \"no known restrictions\" without a basis, YouTube mirrors, unknown sources, restored or colourised archive copies.\n3. **Before downloading, tell the user** what you will download, from where, under which licence, and how it will be credited.\n\nData for number cards comes from official pages (census, transport department, the place's own statistics); write the claim, URL and quoted wording into `FACTS.md`.\n\nRecipe for another place: one single-camera time-lapse of ≥ 12 hours (the clock); 10–15 ground time-lapses and hyperlapses (streets, stations, bridges); 2–4 aerials, one of them top-down at night; one slow-motion shot of a fragile thing; one fixed-camera crowd shot for tracks; one archive film of the same kind of place; one night photograph from orbit or above; a same-position photo pair; three official numbers; one public-domain sentence written about the place.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\n- Place: any city, port, airport, market, campus, factory, a river over a year (the clock becomes a season).\n- Length: 30–90 s; one brake at least; one hard stop always.\n- Score: organ/winds/strings is the default; a choir, a piano ensemble or analogue synth arpeggios also work if the writing stays additive and the break stays a sudden held chord.\n- Tempo 96–132 BPM; at 30 fps prefer tempos whose beat is a whole number of frames (120: 15, 100: 18, 112.5: 16).\n- Grid sizes and the counterpoint pair change with the material (archive vs today, day vs night, two cities).\n- Number cards: 2–4 at most; the day ring may be dropped for non-daily structures.\n- Fixed: footage only, the frame-blended light, cuts on the beat, the brake, few words, one quote, the hard stop, the release-print grade.\n\nSee [`DEMO.md`](DEMO.md) for the example film.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nOne Day in New York\n"},{"id":"S4-L5","name":"视觉调查","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L5.jpg","markdown":"# L5 视觉调查（Visual Investigation）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 剪辑与纪实\n- 长什么样：新闻片、照片、证词和调查报告排上同一条时间轴，五路来源指向一处。\n- 适合：几个来源对到一条时间线上，科普里讲来龙去脉\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Visual Investigation — Style Prompt\n\n> A cold investigation desk: footage, photographs, documents, sound and maps from one real event, laid out on one shared clock so they check each other. The clock lives inside the picture — timestamps on the evidence, a playhead on a data chart, a stopwatch over the decisive seconds; frozen frames are circled and magnified; quotes are highlighted on the page they come from; a plan shows where cameras stood and where they looked; what is known and what is not are kept apart; at the end every piece of evidence is pulled into one map.\n> References (grammar only): newspaper visual-investigation videos, forensic-architecture reconstructions, broadcast verification units. Never copy them, quote their names or use their branding.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n1. **Sources meet at one moment.** The payoff of every section is two or more independent sources (a frame, a photo, a testimony, a report line, a sound) pointing at the same place or the same second. Cut points are sync points.\n2. **One clock, read in the picture.** Time is carried by large readouts that belong to the layout: the timestamp on each telegram card, a playhead with its clock on the data chart, a `T+xx.x` stopwatch over the decisive seconds, a frozen \"32 s\". The scale changes with the story (hours → minutes → seconds → days) at section cuts. A permanent timeline band under every shot is **optional and usually wrong**: it eats the bottom of the frame, adds a layer of small labels and goes empty once the story leaves the clock.\n3. **One finding per layout.** Each section says one thing; a heading is part of the layout, never a page header.\n4. **Uncertainty is drawn, not apologised for.** `≈`, *inferred*, *schematic*, dashed boxes. Known and unknown are separate columns. A source that does not cover a stretch simply is not on screen yet (it lights up late in its column); never an empty \"no footage\" box, which reads as a broken render.\n5. **Restraint.** Real disasters and real people: no slow motion of the catastrophe, no music that tells you to feel; fire only inside evidence frames; casualties as counted dots and numbers.\n\nNot **Satellite Briefing** (no orbital imagery, no zoom from space). Not **Archive Light Table** (no paper on glass, loupes or hands: documents are flat evidence frames). Not **Halftone Dossier** (no print texture, stamps or collage). Not a newsreel package (no channel bug, ticker or anchor voice). Not a keynote: no centred title slides, no page turns.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Three kinds of source at least**: moving image (newsreel, CCTV, phone video, launch camera), documents (testimony, memo, telegram/teletype, report page), photographs. Add sound (original commentary, radio, recordings), data (weather, telemetry, counts) and maps whenever they exist.\n- **Documents**: a scanned page is shown as a strip or a page, never re-drawn; only the decisive line is highlighted (red, `multiply`, α ≈ 0.6, wiped left to right). Text that exists only as a transcription (a report, radiograms) is re-typeset by code and labelled as a transcription.\n- **Archival picture pipeline** (pre-1950 film, max ≈ 2.5×): deinterlace (`bwdif=0`) → crop borders → deblock (`hqdn3d=2:2:3:3`) → lanczos upscale → light unsharp (`5:5:0.5`) → greyscale; one grain for everything. Modern footage: ≤ 1.5×. Never colourised or \"restored\" copies.\n- **One grade** (canvas `ctx.filter`): footage and photos `grayscale(1) contrast(1.22) brightness(0.94)`; paper `grayscale(1) contrast(1.05) brightness(0.86)` (white pages pressed to light grey); maps greyscale, inverted, `brightness(.62)` so they read as dark line drawings.\n- **One grain**: six 256² Gaussian tiles rotated per frame, `overlay`, α 0.22 on footage/photos, 0.10 on paper. Grain only on sources, never on type or lines (`mux.sh … 0`).\n- **Drawn evidence** (plan, side schematic, charts, dot matrix) is thin line work on the panel colour, marked *schematic* when it is not measured.\n- Remove channel bugs, watermarks and the source's own title cards; never generate an image to stand in for a historical one.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n| Use | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Ground | `#0B0C0E` |\n| Panels | `#141619` |\n| Hairlines, frames | `#2E3238` |\n| Text | `#ECEAE4` |\n| Secondary text | `#8B9097` |\n| Ticks, *uncertain* | `#4A4F57` |\n| **The one accent: here / now / match** | `#FF4A2E` |\n| Paper cards (typeset telegrams) | `#E4E0D4` with ink `#1D1D1B` |\n\nSources are greyscale; red is the only colour and it only ever points: circles, the chart playhead, sync lines, highlights, ✓ badges (a drawn tick in a red box, no words), the zone everything converges on, the *unresolved* column, a strike-through on a wrong claim.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- Chinese headings and findings: Noto Sans SC 700 (30–124 px). Tags, timecodes and ticks: IBM Plex Mono 400/600. Quotes: Source Serif 4 Italic for the original wording, Noto Serif SC 500 for a translation underneath in the secondary colour (only when the subtitles don't already say it). All OFL, subset to the words on screen.\n- **Few words per frame.** Each frame keeps only what this beat needs: the main readout or heading, the quoted original (the highlighted line), and one short source tag per piece of evidence. No eyebrow labels, no English twin under every Chinese label, no archive numbers, dates or \"estimate / schematic / not to scale\" footnotes unless the beat is about them, nothing the subtitle is saying at the same moment.\n- **Source tag**: a 6×12 chip + `type · source` in mono, ≤ about 12 characters (`照片 · Pasquarella`, `FBI 电传 · 1937-05-08`), sitting just above its frame. Full credits go on the end card, not on the panels.\n- **Lower third**: a red bar + place name (36 px bold) + one mono line (place code).\n- **Subtitles** (bilingual films): at the bottom of the frame (Chinese baseline y ≈ 984, English y ≈ 1022): Chinese 34 px bold over English 23 px, centred, 10 px black shadow; **one line per language**, measured in the page against 1,500 px; a long sentence becomes two cues. Original sound gets subtitles too, marked `【原声】` / `[1937 newsreel]`.\n- **Reading time**: every key text stays still and whole for CJK ÷ 4.5 + other ÷ 15 + 1.5 s (`readcheck.mjs`); verdict and comparison frames hold ≥ 2.5 s with every item fully in frame. Counters (timecode readouts, the stopwatch) and tick labels are exempt.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- 30 fps. Elements **cut in** (≤ 0.1 s opacity) with a 3–4-frame white flash, as if pulled from the evidence locker; nothing flies, bounces or scale-pops.\n- Only things that are *drawn* are progressive: circles drawn clockwise in 0.5 s (ease-out cubic), leaders and links 0.4–0.8 s, plans stroke by stroke, highlights wiped, typewriter text for telegrams.\n- Inside a frame: a slow push (≤ 1.9× on a map, ≤ 1.06× on a photo) is allowed; the \"camera\" itself never moves. Layouts change on cuts.\n- The event clock (chart playhead, stopwatch) eases between keyframes and moves at constant speed inside a timed run (each tick = one real second).\n- Scale changes (minutes → seconds → days) happen on section cuts, carried by the readout changing form (clock → `T+` stopwatch → date), not by a zooming band.\n- Something changes at least every second; holds are for verdicts only.\n\n## 6. Shot & layout grammar\n\n- 1920×1080, 60 px margins; content y 60–900, subtitles below it (y ≈ 950–1030). Layouts use the full height; no strip is reserved for a timeline. When a section has no subtitle, centre its content vertically.\n- Panels are hard-edged rectangles with 1 px frames; footage panels keep the source's aspect.\n- Typical layouts: **map → plan** (left) with a reference photo and a footage panel (right); **one big statement + one quote + one wide schematic**; **five-source board** (three evidence frames on top, sound and report below, schematic right, red links converging); **three-column sync grid** (report / photo / video under one stopwatch, each column lighting up when its source begins); **document strip + two-column verdict**; **claim card + fact-check card + dot matrix**; **evidence map** (plan, broken-axis mini timeline, schematic, thumbnails with links).\n- Avoid centred layouts, page headers and slide-like page turns. Transitions are hard cuts on a 0.5 s grid, white flashes and the rewind; no dissolves.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n1. **Bed**: low D held by contrabass and cellos; a filtered room tone about 30 dB under the voice.\n2. **Clockwork**: a pendulum tick every 0.5 s in the minute-scale sections, louder in the waiting minutes; in a timed run one tick per **event** second (accent every 5) — the audible stopwatch.\n3. **Harmony**: a quiet pizzicato ostinato in eighths (Dm – B♭ – Gm – A) under the slow sections; tremolo minor-second clusters for tension; slow sustained chords for the verdict columns; a resolving D minor add9 with vibraphone for the evidence map.\n4. **Foley** (procedural): paper for documents, a shutter for photos, Morse \"dits\" and keystrokes for telegrams, teletype chatter, a rewind sweep, pencil scratches for plans, one deep thump for the moment of impact — then near-silence.\n5. **Original sound** (archival commentary, radio, recorded sound): band-passed 180 Hz–5.5 kHz with sparse crackle, shown as a waveform with a red play-line and word-by-word lighting; music ducks −12 dB under it. Original sound and score alternate; never both loud.\n6. **Voice**: calm and flat (ours: Kokoro `am_michael`); music ducks about −9 dB under it; −14 LUFS, true peak ≤ −1.2 dB.\n\n## 8. Native moves (and how to code them)\n\n- **Freeze, circle, magnify.** Freeze a frame with a white flash; draw a 2.5 px red circle clockwise; at 60 % a leader and a bold label appear; a ×2 loupe opens beside it, cropped from the same source pixels (never beyond 1 source px per screen px). *Fits:* a licence plate in CCTV; a crack in a weld.\n- **Hard sync point.** Pick one instant every source can recognise (impact, flash, a public clock, a gunshot peak) and align each source to it. A source that starts late just appears late; if the gap matters, say it in the voice or a subtitle. *Fits:* which phone started filming first; when the launch camera lost the vehicle.\n- **Three-column sync grid.** Document / photo / video side by side under one big stopwatch; each column cuts in (white flash) at the moment its source begins, so the grid fills left to right as the clock runs; freeze on the sync point with a red dashed line across the pictures. *Fits:* two dashcams and a police log.\n- **Readouts change scale.** The time readout changes form at each section cut — card timestamps and a chart playhead (minutes), a `T+` stopwatch (seconds), dates (days) — as the story narrows or widens. *Optional:* a bottom timeline band with tracks per source kind, only for a film that stays on one clock throughout; keep it free of labels and drop it when the clock stops. *Fits:* a market crash from the week to the minute.\n- **Rewind cold open.** Start on the decisive frozen frame, slam the other sources into a grid, sweep a sync line, then rewind (footage playing backwards, scan lines) to the start. *Fits:* any reconstruction.\n- **Telegrams on the clock.** Typeset radiograms / messages land at their timestamps with Morse and keystrokes; red dashed leaders tie each card to a pin at its time on the data chart's axis. *Fits:* chat logs, ATC calls, emails.\n- **Data chart with a playhead.** A chart (wind, speed, temperature) across the lower part of the frame with a time axis, event bands (storm, outage) labelled with one word, the message pins, and a red playhead carrying the clock. *Fits:* power-grid load before a blackout.\n- **Map → plan.** A real survey map pushes in to the site, then cuts to a measured plan with the subject's position, a bearing, a distance ring, a camera point and an inferred view cone; the map stays as a locator inset. *Fits:* where each witness stood.\n- **Evidence → model links.** Every circle in every source sends a red dashed link to the same zone of one schematic; ✓ badges land 0.12 s apart with small mallet notes. *Fits:* which room a fire started in.\n- **Then vs. record.** Play a period claim as original sound, then a fact-check card; strike through the wrong words in the claim. *Fits:* a press release against the filed numbers.\n- **Known / unresolved.** Two columns, ticked vs. dashed boxes, every item held whole for ≥ 2.5 s, one source tag for the board. *Fits:* any inquiry's conclusion.\n- **Dots for people.** One dot per person; the dead become red open rings one by one; a single large number. *Fits:* passengers, workers, victims of any event.\n- **Evidence map.** Plan, broken-axis mini timeline, schematic and thumbnails of every source, all links converging; the poster frame. *Fits:* the end of any investigation.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls\n\n- **Inference passed off as fact.** Camera positions, photo times and offsets need `≈ / inferred / unlocated` on screen and in `FACTS.md`.\n- **Early reports as facts.** The first-night memo and the period newsreel often disagree with the final report; use them as images or as claims to check, never as the source of a number.\n- **Faking a multi-camera event.** With one licensable film, do not borrow clips of unclear origin; cross-check film + photos + documents + sound instead. The structure still holds.\n- **Seams in \"continuous\" shots.** An exposure jump or splice breaks timing; time durations from the official record and state the alignment error.\n- **Time zones and clocks.** Old reports may use standard time while the place ran on daylight time; pick one and state it once on screen (`18:25:00 EST` on the first readout).\n- **Too much at once.** One layout, one finding; at most five sources on a board, and the subtitle never repeats the heading word for word.\n- **Too many words on screen.** Keep only what this beat needs (main readout or heading, the highlighted original, one short tag per source). Eyebrows, English twins of every label, archive numbers, footnotes and translations the subtitle already gives turn the frame into noise.\n- **Empty placeholder boxes for missing material.** A grey \"no footage\" or \"waiting for photo\" panel looks like a render error. Fill the slot with real material, change the layout, or let the source appear when it starts.\n- **A strip reserved at the bottom** (timeline band, caption rail) leaves an empty band when it has nothing to show. Content runs to y ≈ 900; nothing lives in the subtitle area (y ≈ 950–1030).\n- **Catastrophe as spectacle.** No slow motion of fire or impact, no full-screen flames, no sound effects on deaths.\n\n## 10. Footage (素材)\n\n1. **Ask the user first.** Before searching: does the user have their own footage, photos, documents, recordings or data (dashcam files, a report PDF, a spreadsheet of readings)? Their own material always comes first.\n2. **Where to look, and how to judge the licence.** Use `demo/tools/footage.py` (copy it into the project): `search archive|commons|nasa`, `files`, `fetch` (records licence, author and page into `SOURCES.jsonl`), `used`, `md` (writes `SOURCES.md`).\n   - **Internet Archive**: the item's `licenseurl` and collection. The NARA-donated Universal Newsreels (1929–1967) are public domain; other newsreel companies (Hearst, Pathé, Movietone, Paramount) are not, even when uploaders tag them PD. \"stock footage\" and YouTube-ripped items: skip.\n   - **Wikimedia Commons**: read the file page — author, source, licence template. U.S. federal work (Navy, NASA, NARA, NOAA, USGS) = public domain. PD claims resting on \"not renewed\" agency photos (AP, INS, Wide World): treat as uncertain and skip. Download big files through the API thumbnail URL (3840 px) to avoid 429s.\n   - **NASA** (images.nasa.gov): public domain except marked third-party items and logos.\n   - **Library of Congress / national archives**: read each item's rights statement; \"no known restrictions\" is **not** a licence — skip it.\n   - **USGS The National Map**: historical topographic maps as PDF (public domain); render the area you need with `pdftoppm -r 300 -x … -y … -W … -H …`.\n   - **Government reports and FBI files**: U.S. federal works are public domain; quote them, typeset transcriptions, show scans only as evidence frames.\n   - Allowed: public domain, CC0, CC BY (credited in full on the end card). Not allowed: CC BY-SA, \"no known restrictions\", unclear origin, colourised or restored copies.\n3. **Tell the user before downloading**: what (title, length or pages), from where (page URL) and under which licence; keep `raw/` under about 1.5 GB.\n\n## 11. Facts\n\nKeep `FACTS.md` (claim on screen or in the voice → source URL → quoted wording), list every inference separately, and check the final script and subtitles against it before the last render.\n\n## 12. Engine\n\n- One Canvas-2D page (`demo/index.html`), `window.render(t)` deterministic, `window.TEXTS(t)` for `readcheck.mjs` (key texts only; counters exempt), `window.SUBCHECK()` for the one-line subtitle check; all timing in `demo/film.json` (sections, voice starts, original-sound clips, subtitles), shared by the page, the mix (`demo/audio/mix.py`) and the subtitles (`demo/tools/cues.py`).\n- Reusable parts in the page: `plate()` (grade + grain + flash), `ring()` (circle + leader label), `cover()` (fill a panel without stretching, returns a source → screen map for circles), `badge()`, `wave()` (waveform with play-line), `playhead()` (event-clock keyframes shared with the mix), `planPanel()`, `schematic()` (16-cell side view with zone/flutter), `push()`.\n- Prep: `demo/tools/prep.sh` (ffmpeg + pdftoppm + PIL), `demo/tools/wave.py` (waveform peaks), `demo/tools/fonts.py` (OFL subsets). Checks: `demo/tools/subcheck.mjs`, `demo/tools/final_asr.py` (speech check on the finished film, per line).\n\n## 13. Variation space\n\nThe agent decides per film: the event, the sources, the sync point, which time scales the clock passes through, the layouts, the opening and the ending.\n\n- **Three structures:** a cold open on the decisive frame, then rewind and rebuild; one question (\"where did it start?\") answered source by source; a strict clock (T−10 min → T+1 h) with findings dropping in where they happen.\n- **Three sync points:** an impact or flash frame; a sound peak heard in several recordings; a public clock or broadcast time signal.\n- **Three endings:** the evidence map; the known/unresolved board left on screen; back to the opening frame, now fully annotated.\n- **Other subjects:** a traffic collision (dashcams, CCTV, police report), a protest (phone videos, photos, statements), a disputed call in sport (camera angles, rulebook), a launch failure (tracking cameras, telemetry, accident report).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nThe Hindenburg: Four Minutes, 32 Seconds\n"},{"id":"S4-L6","name":"构成主义蒙太奇","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L6.jpg","markdown":"# L6 构成主义蒙太奇（Constructivist Montage）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 剪辑与纪实\n- 长什么样：帝国大厦的小册子写着建造者，工地实拍和老照片给出答案：每天 2,500 人。\n- 适合：有力量的号召\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Constructivist Montage — Style Prompt\n\n> Real archive footage, photographs and documents, printed as one ink-and-paper poster with a single red, and cut on a machine beat. Diagonal bands carry the type; windows cut along the axis hold the film; people are scissored out of photographs and stacked into structures; numbers count, documents are marked in red, and two images collide to make a third idea.\n> References (grammar only): Soviet montage cinema of the 1920s (city symphonies with split screens, counting and machine rhythm; \"intellectual\" montage where A + B yields a concept), constructivist posters and photomontage (red / black / paper, steep diagonals, photo cut-outs with geometric blocks, type as structure). Never copy their shots, layouts, slogans or lettering, and never name them in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n1. **Evidence, recut.** Every picture is real material (archive film, photographs, documents) about the actual subject. The film's argument comes from how they are cut and set, not from illustration.\n2. **One printed sheet.** All material is converted to the same two-tone print (warm ink → paper); red is the only colour and is laid on top as a printed element.\n3. **The beat is the editor.** Cuts, slams, wipes and counts sit on a 120 BPM grid (or the score's tempo); sections accelerate by halving shot length.\n4. **Type is structure.** Words and numbers are beams: on a −18° band, as solid blocks, stacked vertically. Never floating captions.\n5. **The collective over the individual.** Crowds, hands, machines and many small figures; a single face is never the protagonist.\n\nNot **Data Storytelling** or a keynote: no centred headline with bullets, no slide transitions, no header bars; data appears as marked documents, counters and measured bars inside the composition. Not **Swiss Motion Graphics**: asymmetric, diagonal, no grid of white space, no thin rules as decoration. Not **Art Deco** or **60s Spy Titles**: no gold, no fine lines, no jazz. Not a museum slideshow: archive material is never shown \"as is\" in a neutral frame; it is cut, windowed, stacked and counted.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Four kinds of element only**: **bands** (red or ink, along the axis), **windows** (rectangles or parallelograms holding film or photographs), **cut-outs** (people scissored from photographs with a paper-white border), **type blocks**. Plus one paper-fibre texture over everything.\n- **The print grade** (one function for film, photos and documents): luminance → percentile stretch (film: one pair of percentiles per clip, so it never pumps; 0.7 / 99.5; photos 0.8 / 99.4) → S-curve `x + s·(x−0.5)·(1−|2x−1|)·2`, s = 0.32 (documents 0.18, they keep their paper) → map to ink→paper: `rgb = INK + (PAPER − INK)·g`. Archive film keeps a ±2.5 % per-frame flicker inside its window (`hash(frame)`), photographs and documents do not.\n- **Cut-outs**: a person-segmentation model (u2net_human_seg via onnxruntime) → threshold 0.5 → median filter → largest component → 7 px paper border dilated outside the mask. The border is the medium's own look and hides the model's edge errors. Group photos rarely segment cleanly: use them whole in windows.\n- **Documents**: scans at full resolution, never retyped. They are marked: a red highlighter bar in `multiply` over the exact sentence, a hand-drawn red ellipse round a heading, a red circle on a map. Drawings on a document (a chart, a map) may be lifted off the page and reused, keyed from their paper (`alpha = (205 − luminance) / 90`).\n- **Resolution ceiling**: archive film ≤ 2.5× its source pixels on screen, modern footage ≤ 1.5×, photos ≥ 1 source px per screen px where possible. Prepare clips with `hqdn3d → lanczos → light unsharp` before grading; add grain only in the mux. A 720-line film transfer can fill a 1,700 px window, not a full 1080p frame: frame it (a window, a split strip, an iris) instead of stretching it.\n- **Paper fibre**: a fixed seeded noise canvas multiplied over the frame at α 0.5; film grain 4 added by `mux.sh`.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n| Role | Value | Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| Red | `#D52B1E` | bands, wedges, blocks, the key number, marker bars, circles and leaders, the one winner in a comparison |\n| Ink | `#141210` | type, beams under each floor, window borders, dark grounds |\n| Paper | `#EDE4D0` | ground, reversed type, cut-out borders |\n\n- Everything photographic is ink-on-paper; nothing else has colour. Red is never laid on red: a red block on a red ground becomes ink.\n- Alternate grounds to make cuts land: paper scene → ink scene → paper. In an acceleration the background itself alternates red / ink per cut.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n| Use | Face | Size (1080p) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Chinese display, slogans | Noto Sans SC 900 (subset) | 110–210 px; stacked vertically 170 px per character |\n| Numbers, English display | Oswald 600–700 | numbers 230–440 px; English lines 30–62 px, letter-spacing 2–10 px, capitals |\n| Source and footage tags | Space Mono Bold (CJK falls back to Noto) | 17–18 px on an ink or red tab, padding 6 |\n| Subtitles | Noto Sans SC 700 40 px over Oswald 500 28 px | bottom left, ink bar at 90 %, a 10 px red tick |\n\n- **Bilingual pairs**: Chinese block, then one English line in Oswald directly under it (or on the neighbouring band). Each language one line, measured.\n- **Placement**: along the −18° axis on a band, or as an ink / red block; never centred on the frame, never a header. Numbers can be bigger than the words that explain them.\n- **Tags**: every archive window carries its footage tag (`FILM · <TITLE> · <PLACE> <YEAR>`, `PHOTO · <AUTHOR> <YEAR>`), every fact a source tag (`来源 · <PUBLISHER> <YEAR>`). If the footage is not of the subject itself, the tag says what it really is.\n- **Subtitles** stay bottom-left in a 960–1056 px strip; key type keeps above y 930 while narration runs. Subtitles hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s). On-screen text follows `readcheck.mjs` (CJK ÷ 4.5 + other ÷ 15 + 1.5 s, fully in frame); a comparison holds every item ≥ 2.5 s before its verdict.\n\n## 5. Edit grammar\n\n- **Grid**: one tempo for picture and score (120 BPM: beat 0.5 s, bar 2 s). Every cut, slam, wipe and count lands on a beat, half-beat or sixteenth. The page exports these times as `window.EV`; the mix reads the same list.\n- **Shot length**: 0.5–2 s for footage; one idea per shot. A section may accelerate by halving: 0.5 → 0.25 → 0.125 s (never shorter than 3 frames). Holds are reserved for reading: a text block, a freeze, a comparison.\n- **Cut rule**: adjacent shots conflict in at least one of direction (left slam / right slam), value (paper / ink ground), or geometry (diagonal band / upright window). Never two paper documents in a row.\n- **Nothing static for more than a second**: inside a hold something moves (film in its window, a counter, a slow push, a frame-within-the-frame). Planned exceptions only: the comparison hold, one silence beat, the end card.\n- **Transitions inside the medium** (pick per cut, don't fade):\n  - *red wipe*: a 260 px red band at 72° (perpendicular to the axis) sweeps the frame in 0.25 s, the next scene revealed behind it; it lands on the downbeat;\n  - *iris*: a red marker circle drawn on a map or photo becomes an aperture and opens onto footage;\n  - *slam*: the next element arrives from outside along the axis in 0.08–0.2 s, ease-out, hard stop, frame shake (amplitude 3–18 px, `exp(−14·Δt)`);\n  - *flash cut*: one frame of 50–80 % paper over a window at a cut or freeze;\n  - *hard cut on the beat* between grounds of opposite value;\n  - *jump-in*: inside a freeze, a cut to a 1.2× crop of the same frame, the annotation scaling with it.\n- **Two silences**: one where the orchestra stops and only an original recording remains, and one true half-bar of nothing (ink frame, one red dot) before the heaviest hit.\n\n## 6. Formats (each is a function in `demo/index.html`)\n\n| Format | How it looks | How it is built |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Title package** | a red band slams along the axis; a window cut to the band's edge runs footage; digits stamp one per eighth, the CJK unit in red | `S1`: window clipped to the band's top edge (`onBar`), digits measured and slammed individually, `reg()` registers the group for readcheck |\n| **Lower third** | a red bar from the left edge with Chinese, an ink tab with the English | 92 px bar at y ≈ 760–850, slams in 0.14 s, slides off with the next transition |\n| **Map + iris** | a document map pushes in, a red circle is drawn round the place, the circle opens onto the site | `mapXf()` (scale / focus / angle over time), `mapToScreen()` keeps the circle on the map point; aperture = `arc` clip whose radius grows to the frame |\n| **Vertical title** | CJK characters stacked top to bottom beside a tall window, English rotated 90° | per-character slams 1/16 apart |\n| **Date on a girder** | the date in paper on a red band crossing the footage | text placed on the band's centre line (point + normal offset), rotated by the axis |\n| **Grid** | 3 × 3 windows light one per sixteenth in a spiral, the centre counts 1…9 and lights last in red | cells `back()`-scale in, each a different clip at 1.4–1.8× speed, ping-pong |\n| **Split strips** | the frame rotated to the axis, three strips of two panels each; one panel changes per beat; strips drift in opposite directions | panels ≤ 1,160 px wide so the film stays ≤ 2.5×; red seam on the middle strip |\n| **Freeze + annotation** | flash, freeze, push; a red ellipse drawn on the object, a leader to a red block with the number; a jump-in; the film keeps running in a small red-framed inset | the circle is computed from the zoom focus so it follows the jump; inset = same clip, live |\n| **Document close-up** | a tilted page slams in, a red marker bar crosses the exact sentence, a red block repeats the number huge | marker in `multiply` over the scan's own line coordinates; a tall slot beside it runs footage fast to show speed |\n| **Process staircase** | three windows rising along a diagonal, red triangles between them, a counter climbing to the claim | each window 0.75 s apart; the counter is graphic, only its end value is a fact |\n| **The question** | a document heading on an ink ground, circled in red; a huge red \"?\" | heading cropped tight so only the words that matter show |\n| **Acceleration + count** | full-frame windows tilted ±4–6°, ground alternating red / ink, a four-digit counter jumping per cut | cut list with shrinking durations; the original sound plays alone |\n| **Multiple exposure** | top-down footage, a second clip in `screen` at 45 %, cut-outs in `multiply`, the answer in a block | exposure window ≤ 1,700 px on a red ground |\n| **Chart from the document** | the page turns 90°, its drawings lift off and stand on one ground line to scale, red dimension lines, numbers, names; held; then the verdict block | outlines keyed from the scan, scaled so tip-to-ground = value × px per unit; the winner tinted red (`source-in`) |\n| **Photomontage tower** (signature) | floors of footage strips slam in from alternate sides every sixteenth, an ink beam under each, a cut-out worker lands on every other; the camera climbs; a crown and a figure on top | `FLOORS` list, camera `y` follows the eased count of built floors; the floor counter runs to the dated anchor, **rests there while its caption is up**, then runs on to the end value |\n| **Silence beat** | ink frame, one red rivet | half a bar; the score and foley are gated to zero |\n| **Result composition** | huge numbers left, a tall slanted window with the finished subject (camera tilts up it), a tilted photo, a red wedge | numbers slam, the last line restamps the key number on red |\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Score**: an original machine ostinato (eighths, minor, e.g. D–D–F–D–A–D–E–F) in spiccato cellos + staccato bassoon, staccato tuba / trombone on the beat, snare sixteenth taps, xylophone for counts (one rising note per lit cell or built floor), horn / trumpet stabs and a short brass motif, timpani rolls into section changes, gran cassa + gong on the heaviest hit. Sampled CC0 libraries (VSCO 2 CE, VCSL). No piano-and-strings bed.\n- **Original sound alternates with music**: in one section the orchestra stops and a real recording (a rivet gun, a machine, a crowd) carries the cut alone, each burst trimmed to its shot. Bring it in a beat early (J-cut).\n- **Foley for every printed action**: slam = low thump + steel clank; stamp = thump + clack; wipe and iris = whoosh; marker = filtered noise scrub; paper = band-passed rustle; floors and cells = pitched clanks.\n- **Layers**: ambience (a real site / street recording, about 20 dB under the voice, out for the silence), foley ducked 35 % under speech, music ducked 70 % under speech (≈ 10 dB under the voice), narration compressed and levelled by RMS. Master to −14 LUFS with the peaks limited to −1.8 dBFS before `mux.sh`, and a 25 ms fade at sample 0 (an AAC encode overshoots on a hit at the first sample).\n- **Narration** (when the film has one): short declarative lines, newsreel cadence, never more than one sentence over a format; numbers spelled out for TTS, digits in subtitles.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\n1. **The document is marked, not quoted**: the actual sentence on the scan gets a red bar; the number then becomes a red block.\n2. **The document becomes the chart**: drawings printed on a page are lifted off, turned and stood on a ground line to scale.\n3. **A + B → idea**: two kinds of image alternate faster and faster (the machine / the hands; a heading / the people it leaves out) until a card or a number states what the collision meant.\n4. **Count the many**: a counter driven by the cuts, a grid that fills cell by cell, a tower floor by floor.\n5. **People build the structure**: cut-out figures stacked into the thing they made (photomontage).\n6. **Silence, then the heaviest hit** into the result.\n\n## 9. Footage\n\n1. **Ask first.** Before searching, ask the user for their own material: film, video, photographs, scans of documents (reports, brochures, letters, minutes), data. What they give leads; archives fill gaps.\n2. **Where to look, and how to judge the licence** (`demo/tools/footage.py` searches, downloads and records licence, author and source page into `SOURCES.jsonl`; `footage.py md` writes `SOURCES.md`):\n   - **Internet Archive**: read each item's `licenseurl` and description. Public Domain Mark / CC0 / CC BY are usable; *no licence field* is not. For a pre-1978 US film, check the title and end cards for a copyright notice: published 1930 or earlier = public domain in the US by date; published without notice before 1978 = public domain; with a notice, check renewal. Corroborate date and producer elsewhere (a library catalogue, an archive exhibit). Avoid YouTube mirrors, re-uploads with no provenance, colourised or \"restored\" versions.\n   - **Wikimedia Commons**: read the file page's licence and its source institution. Public domain, CC0, CC BY (credit in full) are usable; CC BY-SA is not used in library demos.\n   - **US National Archives (NARA)**, **Library of Congress**, **NASA**, other US federal agencies: federal works are public domain; check each item's rights statement. \"No known restrictions\" (LOC, Flickr Commons) is **not** clear enough: name it to the user or find the same image elsewhere with a clear statement.\n   - **Museums with open access** (Met, Rijksmuseum, Smithsonian): CC0 where the object page says so; check that the item is still marked open access.\n   - **University and city libraries** (e.g. Columbia's digitised ephemera on the Internet Archive): use their rights statement; \"copyright status undetermined\" is not usable.\n3. **Tell the user before downloading**: what (title, years), from where (URL), under which licence and why. Keep `raw/` under about 1.5 GB; download serially (Commons rate-limits).\n4. Record every asset with the seconds used (`footage.py used <name> \"<source s> → film s\"`), list it in `demo/CREDITS`, and keep the trail for anything judged \"no notice\".\n5. Footage that is not of the subject itself (another city, another year) may stand in only with a tag that says exactly what it is.\n\n## 10. Facts\n\nEvery number, date and claim on screen or in the voice comes from an official or primary source (a contemporary report, an official brochure, a government record), kept in `demo/FACTS.md` with the URL and the quoted wording. Counters are graphics: only their end values and dated anchors are facts. Where two primary sources disagree (a film's intertitle vs. the official brochure), use one and record the conflict.\n\n## 11. Engine\n\n- `demo/index.html`: one Canvas 2D page, stateless `render(t)`; scene functions draw a full frame; wipes draw the old scene, clip, draw the new one, then the band. `txt()` draws and registers rotated text boxes for `readcheck.mjs`; `reg()` registers a group drawn in pieces; `slam()`, `flash()`, `flicker()`, `shake()`, `slant()` (parallelogram windows), `cover()` (focus-point cover fit), `clipImg()` (forward / reverse / ping-pong / speed).\n- `demo/tools/prep.py`: the print grade, clip extraction to 24 fps JPEG sequences (one percentile set per clip), stills, document pages and outline keying, cut-outs. `demo/tools/fonts.py`: fetch the OFL fonts and subset Noto Sans SC to the characters used. `demo/tools/cues.mjs`: subtitle cues from the page's own table, measured one line per language. `demo/audio/mix.py`: score, foley, narration, ambience, master.\n- Checks: `readcheck.mjs`, `asr_check.py` per line, `tools/final_asr.py` on the final mix, `tools/rhythm_report.py` (flat windows, cut points, hit-to-onset alignment), `mux.sh` loudness.\n\n## 12. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **Upscaled archive film** turns to mush on a 1080p full frame: window it; split strips into panels; keep zooms inside the 2.5× ceiling.\n- **Per-frame grading pumps**: one percentile pair per clip.\n- **Type on a band running off frame**: place it from the band's centre line (point + normal), not by eye.\n- **Red on red**: a red answer block on a red exposure ground disappears; switch it to ink.\n- **A face as hero**: ordinary identifiable workers appear in groups, at work, in quick cuts; no slow motion or close-up protagonist.\n- **Counters read as facts**: label only the end value and dated anchors; a caption beside a counter must match the number shown at every frame (stop the count on the caption's value while it is up).\n- **A number without a unit**: every counter carries its unit in the same layout as elsewhere (小时 / 层 / 人 MEN).\n- **Ink type over a window**: a line that runs past a photo's edge disappears into dark film; give it a paper tab or keep it clear of the window.\n- **The verdict covering what was compared**: place it where no item is, after the hold.\n- **Too many documents**: one marked sentence per document shot; a page with three highlights is a slide.\n- **Short labels in an acceleration** fail reading time by design: keep text out of the 1/8-beat cuts; put the result in a held block after.\n- **A hit at sample 0** overshoots in the AAC encode: fade the first 25 ms.\n\n## 13. Variation space\n\nThe agent decides per film: structure, which archive and documents, which formats (not all of them in one film), the axis angle (−15° to −30°), the red's exact hue, tempo (100–140 BPM), whether there is narration (intertitles only is valid), the signature build (a tower, a bridge span, a crowd forming a word, a map filling in).\n\n- **Three structures:** a calendar machine (one counter runs the whole film and stops at milestones); a document that asks and footage that answers (the turn at the middle); one object's journey through a process, then the scale of the whole.\n- Subjects that fit: construction and engineering histories, industrial anniversaries, mass events, labour and collective effort, infrastructure openings, any \"many people, one machine rhythm\" story with archive film and documents behind it.\n\nOur demo: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md).\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\n410 Days\n"},{"id":"S4-L7","name":"体育战术板","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L7.jpg","markdown":"# L7 体育战术板（Sports Telestrator）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 剪辑与纪实\n- 长什么样：半场 0–3，费内巴切 12 分钟连进三球：冻住画面、画线、分屏、射门图。\n- 适合：定格、画线讲解，拆一个瞬间\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Sports Telestrator — Style Prompt\n\n> The TV football analysis segment: play the clip at full speed, rewind, freeze it, and draw on the broadcast picture — rings under players' feet, marker arrows for the ball, a red patch for the space nobody covered — then play on, switch angles, count the frames, and flatten the pitch into a board. Real match footage is the ground; every line is drawn on top of it and moves with it. A studio show's packaging (sting, corner bug, score bar, a yellow wipe) holds it together. 16:9.\n> References (grammar only): touch-screen analysis segments in televised football (the \"telestrator\" pundit's board), and explainer channels that annotate real match footage. Never copy a broadcaster's or channel's logo, fonts, colours, idents or graphics, and never name them in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n1. **Watch it, then take it apart.** Every piece of action is seen moving before it is explained. A freeze always follows play; the explanation never comes first.\n2. **One freeze, one finding.** Each freeze says one thing (the ball wasn't cleared; nobody stepped out; he struck it from outside the box) with one label and at most two groups of lines. The same few seconds of footage are reused, each pass going one layer deeper.\n3. **Drawn on the real picture.** Rings sit under feet, arrows follow the ball's real path, the box line is traced over the real line. Nothing floats in an abstract diagram until the film deliberately flattens the pitch.\n4. **Data counted from the footage.** Times come from frame counts (a stopwatch that runs on source frames), distances from the pitch markings (a perspective transform). What the footage can't measure (ball speed, sprint distance) is not stated.\n5. **Studio energy.** Bright marker lines, a white flash and a punch on every freeze, the music stopping with the picture.\n\nNot **Visual Investigation** (that is restrained, synchronised multi-camera evidence where the timeline is the protagonist; this is a pundit's show with packaging and enthusiasm). Not a **highlight reel** (no montage of goals to music; each clip is analysed). Not **Data Storytelling** or an infographic: charts are not the film; the top-down board appears once, as a summary, and is reached by flattening the real picture.\n\n## 2. Frame and layout (1920×1080)\n\n- **Full-frame footage** most of the time. The packaging lives in the corners:\n  - **Corner bug**, top right (x 1452–1880, y 38–110): show name on a yellow block + a mode line in the mode's colour — LIVE (red dot) / ANALYSIS (yellow) / REPLAY (cyan). It is in every footage frame from frame 1, because it sits over the broadcaster's logo.\n  - **Score bar**, bottom right (x 1392–1880, y 926–996): HOME n – n AWAY + minute. The away number flips on the frame the ball crosses the line (a 0.5 s roll with a yellow flash). It sits over the broadcaster's URL.\n  - **Source clock**, top left (x 40–370, y 38–90), on every analysis shot: `❚❚ 04.20s F106` — source seconds and frame number; ▶ ½ in slow motion, ◀◀ in a rewind. Hidden in live play.\n- **Labels** (black card, coloured left bar, Chinese line + small spaced English line) sit in empty grass or crowd, never over the action they describe.\n- **Subtitle bar** bottom, centred at x 880 (its right edge stays left of the score bar), y 938–1044.\n- **Split screen**: two 960×1080 vertical windows, each a 960-px-wide slice of a 1920 frame at 1:1 (no extra scaling), a 6 px yellow divider between them, a camera label at each window's top left (A MAIN CAMERA / B HIGH REVERSE). The slices are chosen so the broadcaster's logo and URL fall outside them.\n- **Board pages** (shot map, data card): dark board `#0C1411` with a faint 60 px grid; left column text, right side drawing. No headers, no centred title-and-bullets.\n\n## 3. Editing grammar\n\n- **Order inside one piece of action:** live at 1× (original sound) → rewind (0.6 s, reversed with tracking bars and ◀◀) → freeze ① → slow play (0.6–0.85×, frame-blended) → freeze ② → slow play with a stopwatch → freeze ③ → ball flight with a comet trail → another angle (replay), possibly one more freeze.\n- **Lighter variants** for secondary pieces of action, so the deep breakdown stays special:\n  - **Freeze-in-play**: no rewind; play at 1× up to the decisive pass, freeze, draw, then play on to the goal.\n  - **Dual-angle freeze**: both angles play side by side and each arrives at the same instant (each at its own speed) where both freeze together; the same player is ringed in both.\n- **Freeze length** 1.8–2.4 s; a freeze longer than ~2.5 s needs a second action inside it. Two to four freezes per piece of action; more than four, split with a replay.\n- **Cuts between segments**: only the packaging wipe — a yellow slanted band with dark edges crossing the frame in 0.34 s. Inside a segment, hard cuts. No dissolves, no page turns.\n- **Comparison frames** (the shot map, the data card) are fully drawn early and held ≥ 2.5 s before the next cut.\n\n## 4. Colour identity (the one rule that matters)\n\n| Role | Colour |\n|---|---|\n| The team/player we follow, the ball, main marker lines | `#FFD400` fluorescent yellow |\n| Opponents (rings, block arrows, link lines) | `#3FD8FF` cyan |\n| Space (a patch nobody covers) | `#FF3B30` red, fill 0.34–0.38 + dashed edge |\n| Pitch lines traced, neutral marks, stamps | `#F4F1E8` chalk |\n| Show board | `#0C1411` ink green-black |\n| Label / bug background | `rgba(8,12,14,0.9)`; opaque `#080C0E` where it covers a logo |\n\nOne colour, one identity, throughout the film: a label's left bar takes the colour of what it names. Change the hues if the show needs to, never the three roles.\n\n## 5. Type\n\n- Labels: Noto Sans SC 900, 40 px (30 px for a name), English line Barlow Condensed 600, 21 px, letter-spacing 2 px, in the role colour.\n- Packaging numbers (score, minute, stopwatch, big data): Barlow Condensed 800 Italic.\n- Clock / frame numbers: IBM Plex Mono 500, 24 px.\n- Board pages: title Noto Sans SC 900 60–64 px, rows Noto Sans SC 700 34–42 px with a grey note under each, numbers Barlow Condensed 800 Italic 64–150 px in yellow; text appears left to right with a clip reveal.\n- **No handwriting fonts** anywhere: printed type on a dark card belongs to the same packaging; handwriting reads as a school notebook.\n- Subtitles: Chinese line (Noto Sans SC 500, 38 px) above an English line (Barlow Condensed 600, 32 px), black bar 0.64 with a 4 px yellow edge; one line per language, never wrapped — shorten the line instead of shrinking it.\n- All fonts OFL from Google Fonts, subset to the characters used (`demo/tools/fonts.py`).\n\n## 6. Motion and drawing\n\n- **Freeze entry**: white flash 0.1 s (0.55) + punch 1.035 → 1.0 in 0.35 s (ease-out) + desaturate 42 % + a cool cast + heavier vignette; then a slow 1.2 % creep. Overlays use the same transform as the picture, or they slide off the players.\n- **Spotlight**: everything outside a focus ellipse darkens to 0.42 (even-odd hole), 0.3 s fade.\n- **Marker line**: dark outline (w + 6, black 0.55) + coloured core with glow 18 + thin white highlight (w × 0.28); drawn along its length; an arrowhead pops in over the last 10 %; deterministic small wobble at the vertices so it feels hand-drawn. Dashed for intended or future paths (a cross, a run), solid for what happened.\n- **Ring**: perspective ellipse (ry = 0.36 r) under the feet, drawn clockwise, outer dashed ring rotating at 60 px/s; a vertical light beam while the player moves.\n- **Draw order inside a freeze**: ring (who) → area or link (what happened) → label (the one-line finding), 0.3–0.6 s apart.\n- **Slow motion**: source frames at 0.6–0.85×, neighbours cross-blended (no stutter).\n- **Rewind**: the clip reversed in 0.6 s (ease-in-out), ±13 px horizontal jitter, four white tracking bars, a big ◀◀ and the word REWIND.\n- **Trails**: in screen space keep only the last ~0.6 s (ball 0.24 s) — the camera pans, so long trails land in the wrong place.\n- **Stamp** (e.g. a VAR decision): a framed card that drops from 1.7× to 1× with overshoot, slightly rotated, with a small flash — once per film.\n- **Perspective flatten**: see Native moves 7.\n\n## 7. Grade and image quality\n\n- Footage: `hqdn3d=1.2:1.2:2.5:2.5` → lanczos to 1920×1080 (modern footage at most 1.5×) → `unsharp=5:5:0.55` → `eq=saturation=0.9:contrast=1.04:gamma=0.97` + slightly cool shadows; one grade for every clip; film grain 2 added once at the master (`mux.sh`).\n- Broadcaster logos and URLs are covered by the packaging (bug and score bar placed exactly over them, present from frame 1), or kept outside split-screen windows — never by cropping past 1.5×.\n- Prefer a broadcaster's native resolution over an \"AI-upscaled\" stream.\n\n## 8. Sound palette\n\n- **Layers**: English voice-over (one line per freeze, ≈2.5–3 s) → match sound (commentary + crowd at full level in live play; under freezes only a low-passed crowd bed, looped; the goal roar on the goal frame) → score → foley. Voice ducks everything else to 0.55.\n- **Score**: sampled band (drum kit, electric bass, staccato trumpet and trombone, timpani, horn, cellos, glockenspiel), about 120 BPM. The groove restarts at the head of each played segment, so cuts don't need a global bar grid. **On a freeze the drums stop**: one brass stab + timpani + low thump + camera shutter, then only closed hi-hat — the music freezes with the picture. Goals: a big brass chord with crash and glockenspiel on the score-bar flip.\n- **Foley**: marker squeak (2–5 kHz band-passed noise with flutter) for each line, pops for rings, a tick per landed label, whoosh on every wipe, tape-motor whine on a rewind, a stopwatch tick every 0.2 s, a thump for the stamp, a long whoosh and a timpani roll under the flatten.\n- Mix limited before `mux.sh` normalises to −14 LUFS.\n\n## 9. Native moves (and how the code does them)\n\n1. **Freeze and draw** — a freeze segment = one source frame + flash + punch + desaturate + spotlight; overlays drawn by progress (`overlayAnalysis()`, `overlayG1()` in `demo/index.html`).\n2. **Tracked ring** — hand keyframes of the feet point in source pixels every ~3–6 frames, linearly interpolated (`TRK`, `at()`); check them with `?dbg=1`, which paints every tracked point in magenta.\n3. **Frame-counting stopwatch** — starts at the receiving frame and counts source frames ÷ fps; stops and turns yellow on the strike (`stopwatch()`).\n4. **Score-bar flip on the goal frame**, one entry per goal (`GOALS`, `scoreAt()`, `bugScore()`).\n5. **Packaging over watermarks** — corner bug and score bar positioned from the logo and URL coordinates, drawn from frame 1 (`bugTop()`, `bugScore()`).\n6. **Dual-angle freeze** — two windows, each its own clip and frame range, both reaching their freeze frame on the same beat; overlays drawn in each window's source coordinates (`splitView()`, `overlayG2()`).\n7. **Perspective flatten into a shot map** — for each shot, mark 6–8 known pitch points in its own frame (box and six-yard corners, where the penalty arc meets the box line; FIFA dimensions) and fit a ground-plane homography by least squares (`demo/tools/homography.py` reads `tools/pitch.json`, writes `assets/pitch.json`; residuals ≤ 6 px are fine). The strike frame is then drawn as a mesh of triangles in pitch coordinates (1.5 m cells), each vertex moving from its image position to its board position, each triangle affine-mapped (`drawTri()`); the picture fades out as the chalk board takes over, and the other shots' feet points, converted with their own frames' matrices, drop onto the same board (`shotMap()`). The broadcaster's URL is painted out of the source frame first, because the flatten drags it into the middle of the screen.\n8. **Data card** — the counted numbers re-stated on a board page: label left, big yellow number right, red marker circle on the key one (`notes()`).\n9. **Commentary transcript** — original-language words with timecodes and a translation, in a small box during live play; inaudible words become an ellipsis, never a guess (`transcript()`).\n\n## 10. Material\n\n1. **Ask the user first** whether they have their own footage (club or academy video, their own camera, a licensed broadcast clip), line-ups, event data or photos, and the rights to use them. Their material comes first.\n2. **Where to look** (judge every file on its own page):\n   - **Wikimedia Commons** (search \"goal\", the match, the player): many clips are cut from broadcasters' own YouTube uploads under CC BY. Fine: CC0, public domain, CC BY. Not used: CC BY-SA, \"no known restrictions\", unclear licences.\n   - **A broadcaster's or club's own YouTube channel** when its video page says *Creative Commons Attribution license (reuse allowed)*: check that the channel is the rights holder's official, verified channel (not a re-upload or a fan mirror), save the evidence (channel id, verification, licence field, date checked), and download the native resolution, not an AI-upscaled stream. Third-party re-uploads, fan mirrors, colourised or \"restored\" versions are never used.\n   - **Facts** (scores, minutes, how a goal was scored): the league's or broadcaster's own match page or video description, then established newspapers; quote them in `FACTS.md`. When sources disagree, use the official list and leave the disputed detail out.\n   - Photos of players: Commons (CC BY / CC0); state the year when the photo isn't from the match.\n3. **Before downloading, tell the user** what you will fetch, from where and under which licence. The finder copied into this style, `demo/tools/footage.py` (`search commons|archive|nasa`, `fetch`, `used`, `md`), logs licence, author, page and the seconds used into `SOURCES.jsonl` and writes `SOURCES.md`; `demo/tools/fetch_sources.sh` shows how a verified YouTube CC BY source is fetched with `uvx yt-dlp`. Wikimedia rate-limits bursts (HTTP 429): one request at a time.\n4. **People on screen**: players, referees and staff in the event are fine; never slow down or freeze on an identifiable spectator.\n\n## 11. Engine\n\nCanvas 2D, one page: `window.render(t)` is async and deterministic, frames load on demand from JPEG sequences (one folder per clip, 90-frame cache). `timeline.json` (segments with clip and source frame range, voice lines) drives the page, `tools/mix.py` and `tools/cues.py`. `window.TEXTS(t)` reports the text that must be read, for `readcheck.mjs`. `core/render/video.mjs` renders at 30 fps.\n\n## 12. Pitfalls\n\n- **Wrong tracking points** are the biggest risk: reading coordinates off a scaled contact sheet is easily 20–50 px off. Read them on full-resolution crops with a pixel ruler, then check with `?dbg=1`.\n- **Overlays not under the punch transform** slide off the players during a freeze.\n- **Balls vanish**: long shots are a few pixels with motion blur; colour tracking loses them; frames where the ball is invisible are interpolated and the number says \"≈\".\n- **Numbers the footage can't support** (ball speed, distance run) are not written. Distances only from a homography fitted to visible markings, each shot in its own frame (the camera zooms and pans between shots).\n- **Packaging late by one frame** shows the logo: bugs never fade in over footage.\n- **Split-screen windows sliding in** reveal what's outside them (a logo, a URL): clip each window to its slice in source coordinates, not only to the screen half.\n- **Score state must match the moment**: before the 67th-minute goal it is 3–2, not 3–3.\n- **Names in TTS**: spell them phonetically for the voice (\"Zayits\", \"Saduk\") and keep the real spelling in subtitles; record the ASR result for proper names in `lines.json` (`asr`).\n- **Freeze labels echo the subtitle** and live only ~1 s; anything the viewer must read (scores, board rows, the data card) gets ≥ 2.5 s and goes into `TEXTS`.\n\n## 13. Variation space\n\nThe agent decides per film: the sport (football, basketball pick-and-roll with screens drawn as bars, tennis rallies with landing zones, F1 overtakes with racing lines — anything with a wide camera that shows the whole play), the moments, the show's fictional name and colours (keeping the three roles), how many freezes.\n\n- **Three structures:** chronological build-up to the deep breakdown (secondary moments get light treatments, the decisive one the full rewind-and-freeze); one moment taken apart from three angles; a \"what went wrong\" defensive breakdown ending on the space map.\n- **Three openings:** the show sting into a match-record card; the goal frame frozen in the first second, then rewind; the stopwatch running over live play.\n- **Three endings:** the top-down board that sums up the moments; the data card with one number circled; the hero shot slowed with the minute and name.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nFreeze Frame\n"},{"id":"S4-L8","name":"电视新闻包装","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L8.jpg","markdown":"# L8 电视新闻包装（Broadcast News Package）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 剪辑与纪实\n- 长什么样：一周四条 AI 新闻，一整套电视台包装：串联单、标题条、翻牌价格表、规格卡。\n- 适合：新闻快讯式的开场\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Broadcast News Package — Style Prompt\n\n> A television newsroom's on-air package, built entirely in code: a sting that becomes the channel bug, a rundown of today's stories that opens and closes the bulletin, a lower-third strap that never leaves the bottom of the frame, and full-frame graphics (split-flap price boards, a line breaking a threshold, explainer maps, documents flying out of a city, quote cards, spec cards with proportion bars, stamps) that carry the facts. The style is the **package system plus its cutting rules**, not a drawing style. 16:9, a clean frame: no burned-in subtitles, no news crawl.\n> References (grammar only): evening network news headline menus, financial-channel market graphics, European news channels' explainer maps, tech-desk spec cards. Never copy a broadcaster's logo, set, fonts, colours, music or idents, and never name them in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n1. **One package system, used consistently.** Every graphic is built from the same few parts: a brand plate (parallelogram), a tab in the accent colour, a paper strap, ink cards with an accent top edge, mono datelines, stamps. Viewers read the system before they read the words.\n2. **The bulletin's order.** Sting → rundown (\"today we have four stories\") → item → item → … → back to the rundown, ticked off → sign-off. Each item opens with its category on the strap's tab and its date on the first graphic.\n3. **One fact per screen.** A full-frame graphic states one thing: the price, the break, the two stocks, the place, the two letters, the quote, the verdict.\n4. **The strap holds the headline.** One lower-third strap is on screen from the first story to the recap and changes its words with a vertical flip; the picture above it is free for the graphic.\n5. **Clean panel.** No crawl, no burned-in subtitles; subtitles ship as a separate `.srt`. The frame is the graphic, the strap, the bug and a source line.\n\nNot **Caption News** (that is captions-first social video: huge slammed captions are the lead, no rundown, no strap; here type is small and orderly and the graphics lead). Not **Data Storytelling** (one chart per beat, never a dashboard; the chart is an insert in a bulletin). Not a keynote: no centred title-and-bullets, no page turns. Not a studio show: no virtual set, no anchor, no fly-throughs — the package is flat graphics over ink.\n\n## 2. Frame and layout (1920×1080)\n\n| Zone | Position | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| Content | x 96–1824, y 56–840 | the full-frame graphic; when a strap is up, nothing important below y 840 |\n| Lower-third strap | tab x 96 y 856, 48 high; strap x 96 y 904, 1250×104, + 120 px ink end block | category tab (accent, ink text, Chinese + English), headline in Chinese 50 px + one English line 27 px |\n| Source line | right-aligned at x 1836, y 950 / 978 | `SOURCE: …` mono 13 px + Chinese 16 px, 75 % opacity; English ≤ 36 characters or it slides under the strap's end block |\n| Channel bug | top right, plate centre (1739, 86), 0.327 of the sting plate | the plate plus `AI NEWS` |\n| Corner tag | top left x 96 y 56, 330×64 | `ILLUSTRATION / 示意 · AI 生成画面` on generated plates, `SCHEMATIC / 示意图` on schematic charts; flips in and out 0.2 s |\n\nContent layers sit lower than a subtitled layout would put them (a 60–110 px downward shift) so the bottom strap does not leave an empty band above it. When you remove subtitles and the crawl, re-lay the page; never leave the space they used.\n\n## 3. Package parts (specs)\n\n| Part | Spec | Rules |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sting | 0–1.5 s | two accent slashes (0.04, 0.10 s) → the plate slams in with an overshoot, an impact ring and shake (0.16–0.42) → letters drop in one by one → wordmark and dateline open from the centre → a glint → the plate flies to the top right and **becomes the bug** while a vertical band wipes into the first shot. A hit within 0.4 s |\n| Brand plate | parallelogram 520×180, accent gradient, ink and gold bars at the left, name in condensed 800 | the same object is the sting, the bug, the desk front and the end card |\n| Rundown | left: date block (big numerals, month, week); right: three rows, 1024×140, number block in accent | rows flip in vertically on the beat (back ease) with a glint; at the close the same object returns and each row is ticked |\n| Lower-third strap | see §2 | enters with the first story, flips (new words up from below, old up and out, 0.32 s; the tab flashes white when the category changes), leaves with a slide when the recap starts. A **profile** version (tab `人物 PROFILE`) names a speaker |\n| Split-flap board | tiles 220×130, top and bottom halves clipped separately | old value and new value on the same board, so the change reads in one look; ×2 / +x % called out to the right |\n| Threshold chart | line from the peak, the threshold as a dashed gold line | the line head advances one trading day per step; the last step crosses the threshold in accent with a shake and a ring; the readout panel slides in after; axes show only what the data supports |\n| Bars | grow from a zero line downward for losses, values roll | a reference line at the shared threshold plus a badge |\n| Explainer map | the same projection, one long take | members light in order of distance from the city; the city label, the institution label with leader, a stamp; the camera pushes, then pans to make room |\n| Documents | 380×490 paper, ink header | fly out of the city dot on an arc (0.5 s, scale 0.08 → 1, rotation settles); check boxes tick on the voice's key words |\n| Quote card | ink background with the package's light streaks, a large accent quotation mark lands first, lines push up one by one (0.18 s apart), then the Chinese gist, a gold rule, name, occasion and date | the official wording, in the language it was said; never paraphrase inside quotation marks; an underline sweeps the key word as the voice says it |\n| Spec card | eyebrow (source · date), the product name pushed up from a mask, a category stamp, proportion bars on one scale (the whole vs the part, values rolling), a percentage badge, a context chip; a second group on the right for the hardware or the place, filling cell by cell, joined by a bracket to its total | one scale for every bar, so the proportion is true; every number from the source page |\n| Stamps | accent fill or ink with a double accent frame, −6° to −10° | land from 1.9× in 0.16 s with an expanding ellipse; one per story at most |\n| Transitions | vertical band wipe (accent 84 px + ink 5 px + gold 14 px edge, 0.3 s) between stories and programme parts; hard cut + timpani inside a story; slanted wipe into the end card | nothing else: no dissolves, no 3D flips, no page turns, no fly-throughs. **The package layer travels with its picture**: under a wipe each side draws its own strap words, tab, source line and corner tag (old state on the old side, new state on the new side), so the band brushes the headline over; on a hard cut they change on the same frame. Strap words never flip or overlap. The incoming picture's entry animation starts under the band, so its first revealed frame already has content |\n\n## 4. Colour\n\n| Role | Value | Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| ink | `#121417` | backgrounds, panels, strap end block |\n| ink2 / ink3 | `#1B1E23` / `#22262C` | cards, land on maps |\n| accent (lemon) | `#F2CF1D` (light `#F8DE4A`, dark `#D8B30C`) | plate, tabs, band wipe, bars, stamps, the line that breaks |\n| gold | `#D4B064` | thresholds, datelines, source lines, light fins, badges |\n| paper | `#F4F1E8` | strap, documents, the chart line |\n| breaking | `#FE6B3D` | only for BREAKING and LIVE. A weekly round-up has neither, so it never appears |\n\nStructure to keep when you change the brand: one accent + one gold-like secondary + paper + ink, and a reserved alarm colour. Ink text on the accent; on paper, English secondary lines in a dark olive (`#6E5A1E`) because gold is too weak there.\n\n## 5. Type\n\n- Chinese: Noto Sans SC 900 (headlines, strap) / 700 (labels).\n- Latin: Barlow Condensed 800 (numbers, names, the plate), 600 (secondary lines, quotes), 600 italic (the end-card English title).\n- Mono: IBM Plex Mono 500 for datelines, sources, axis labels, all caps with 1–4 px tracking.\n- All OFL, subset from Google Fonts by `demo/tools/fonts.sh` (the character list is read from `main.js`; delete `fonts/*.woff2` after a text change). No handwriting faces in this style.\n- Every label is bilingual on one line each (Chinese first, English smaller); never wrap.\n\n## 6. Picture layer and grade\n\n- When there is no footage, the picture layer is data graphics drawn in code plus, at most, a few **illustrative plates** (generated or stock), each tagged `ILLUSTRATION` for as long as it is on screen. They set a mood (a server room behind a model launch, a wafer behind chip stocks); they never stand in for the event, the place or the people.\n- One grade for every plate: greyscale → auto black/white points (0.5 / 99.6 percentile) → γ 1.05 → 35 % S-curve → black 8, white 232 → duotone `#0E1013` → `#ECE7D8` (`tools/prep.py: grade()`), plus a left-dark ink gradient for type, a 50–55 % vignette and 7 % overlay grain cycling through six tiles. Graphics and the package are never grained.\n- Real footage, if you have it, takes the same grade path; modern footage ≤ 1.5× scale, archive ≤ 2.5× after deblocking; crop out station logos.\n\n## 7. Editing grammar\n\n- **120 BPM grid**, beat 0 at the end of the sting (1.5 s). Every cut and wipe on a beat or half-beat.\n- **Shot lengths**: sting 1.5 s; rundown 4–5 s; story shots 3–7 s; a map long take (6–12 s) as the bulletin's breath; a comparison stays until every item has been fully on screen ≥ 2.5 s.\n- **A package action every 0.5–1 s** inside a shot (flip, count, stamp, tick, strap change, map light, line step). No frame flat for more than 1 s; a strong action within 0.5 s of every cut.\n- **Date every story**: a weekly bulletin's items happen on different days; the date sits in the rundown row, the eyebrow line, the readout or the document.\n- **The order inside an item**: what (the name or the number) → how big (the line, the bars, the price board) → who it hits or who said it (bars, documents, a quote) → the verdict stamp.\n\n## 8. Sound palette\n\n| Layer | What | Level |\n|---|---|---|\n| Anchor voice | one narrator, one sentence per graphic, starting 0.1 s after the cut; HP 90 Hz, 3:1 compression. Default: the library's Kokoro (`core/tts/tts.py`, free, Apache 2.0) in English or Chinese. The user's own recording, or another TTS they have a key for (for example Doubao, as in the demo), replaces it — never required | music ducked ≈ −7 dB and its 1.2–5 kHz band thinned under speech |\n| News theme | 120 BPM, minor key: spiccato violins in root–fifth–octave eighths, pizzicato bass on the beat, horn pads, a low-passed 16th-note saw pulse, kick on 1 and 3, snare on 2 and 4 | full under the rundown and the first story |\n| Section moods | markets: violins down an octave, tremolo, four-on-the-floor kick (heartbeat); policy: pizzicato and horns only | |\n| Signature hits | sting tutti; glockenspiel notes as the rundown rows land; timpani on hard cuts and story changes; one beat of silence before the biggest break, then the heaviest hit of the film; a held drone after the verdict stamp; a major-key lift for the last story; tutti on the end card | |\n| Foley | whoosh on wipes, flap clicks on split-flaps, stamp thump + clack, trading-room ticks under market charts, map clicks per lit country, a sonar ping on a city, paper and typewriter ticks on documents, click + glockenspiel on ticks | |\n| Room | server-room hum under a tech plate, soft air under maps | very low |\n\nMaster −14 LUFS, true peak −1.2 dB (`core/render/mux.sh`). Samples: VSCO 2 CE and VCSL (CC0); foley synthesised (`core/audio/sfx.py`).\n\n## 9. Native moves\n\n1. **Sting becomes the bug** — the brand plate slams in, then flies to the corner and stays there for the whole bulletin; opening and programme are one object.\n2. **Strap tab = category** — the strap's tab changes category (模型 MODELS, 市场 MARKETS, 监管 POLICY …) with a white flash when a new story starts; no separate item cards between stories.\n3. **Rundown, then ticked off** — the same list opens and closes the bulletin; at the end each row gets its tick.\n4. **Spec card** — a launch reduced to its proportions: the whole and the part on one scale, then what it runs on, joined by a bracket to the total.\n5. **Strap that never leaves** — one strap group from the first story to the recap; words flip, the strap itself never exits mid-programme.\n6. **Split-flap before/after** — old and new value on one board, each tile flipping from the old to the new.\n7. **Breaking the threshold** — a line advances one period per step towards a dashed threshold; the music stops for one beat before the last step and the crossing gets the heaviest hit.\n8. **Date in the eyebrow** — every graphic's eyebrow line carries source and date in mono caps, so a weekly round-up never blurs which day each item happened.\n9. **City sends the letters** — on a map long take, documents fly out of the sending city to their addressees; one world→screen function keeps labels and documents attached through push and pan.\n10. **Quote card + verdict stamp** — the official's own words in type, with name, occasion and date; an underline on the key word as the voice says it; one stamp.\n\nWhere each move lives in the demo's code: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md) (build notes).\n\n## 10. Material\n\n1. **Facts first.** Every on-screen number and every sentence of the voice comes from an official source (the company's own page, the exchange's data, the institution's press release or speech), recorded in `demo/FACTS.md` with claim, URL, quoted wording and any arithmetic. When a fact exists only in a news report, attribute it on screen and in the voice (\"据…报道 / … reports\") and keep to the plain claim; never put a single report's allegations about a real company or person on screen.\n2. **Data.** Draw charts from real series only; when you have just a few reported numbers, draw a *schematic* line, tag it `SCHEMATIC`, and label only what you know. Exchange closes: the exchange itself, the central bank's statistics portal, or a market-data file whose source you log.\n3. **Pictures: ask in this order.**\n   a. **The user's own images** — photos, b-roll stills, their logo and colours (the package is rebuilt around their brand). Use these first.\n   b. **Generated illustrative plates**, if the user wants them. Before generating, tell the user how many images and what each shows (one line per plate). With the Codex CLI installed and able to generate images, `demo/tools/gen_image.py` (driven by `demo/tools/gen_plates.sh`) makes them and saves JPEGs to `demo/assets/plates/`. With any other image tool (an API or a web app), give the user the prompts and the target file names; they generate the images and drop them into `demo/assets/plates/`. Prompts ask for no text, letters, logos, flags or people.\n   c. **No generated images**: public-domain, CC0 or CC BY photographs (licences below), or abstract backgrounds drawn in code (gradients, grids, light fins — the package already carries the frame).\n   Whatever the source: every word on screen is typeset in code, never baked into an image; a generated plate is only ever an illustration behind a graphic, tagged `ILLUSTRATION / 示意` for as long as it is visible, and never stands in for the event, a place in the news, or a person. No generated anchor.\n4. **Photo and footage licences** (judge each file on its own page, not the search result):\n   - **Wikimedia Commons**: CC0, public domain or CC BY (credit author, title, licence on the end card and in `CREDITS`). Not used: CC BY-SA, \"no known restrictions\", unclear licences, YouTube re-uploads, colourised or \"restored\" versions.\n   - **U.S. federal agencies** (NASA, NOAA, NARA, USGS…): public domain; check the page does not credit a contractor or third party.\n   - **Internet Archive**: only items whose page states public domain, CC0 or CC BY; U.S. works published before 1931 are public domain.\n   - **Library of Congress / national archives**: read each rights statement; \"no known restrictions\" is not enough.\n   - **Institutions' audiovisual services** (for example the European Commission's): read their reuse terms per item; many require attribution and forbid implying endorsement.\n   - **Maps**: Natural Earth (public domain), via `world-atlas` TopoJSON.\n5. **Before downloading, tell the user** what you will fetch, from where and under which licence. The finder copied into this style, `demo/tools/footage.py` (`search commons|archive|nasa`, `fetch`, `used`, `md`), logs licence, author, page and the seconds used into `SOURCES.jsonl` and writes `SOURCES.md`. Keep `raw/` under ~1.5 GB.\n6. **Logos**: never use a company's logo for a company in the news; set its name in type.\n7. **People**: public figures in the news can be named on a profile strap; no close-up or slow motion of private individuals.\n8. **Voice and music**: ask whether the user has a recorded anchor, a preferred voice or a theme of their own before synthesising one.\n\n## 11. Pitfalls\n\n- **Package left behind.** If the strap, source line or tags are drawn once on top of everything, a wipe changes the picture under yesterday's headline and the strap catches up 0.3–0.5 s later. Draw them per picture, inside the wipe's clip.\n- **Leftover layout.** Removing subtitles or a crawl without moving the content down leaves a dead band above the strap.\n- **Masked type peeking in.** A push-in title clipped by a mask shows its top edge before it moves if the travel distance doesn't clear the mask; start it fully below and don't draw at progress 0.\n- **Words stronger than the source.** \"Request for information\" is not \"investigation\" or \"penalty\"; write \"no fine, no case opened\" or \"for the first time\" only if a source says so — and check the institution's own pages, which can contradict a report (\"first requests\" may have gone out months earlier).\n- **Thresholds depend on the reference.** \"Bear market\" = −20 % from *which* high? Name the reference on the axis; from a different peak the claim can be false.\n- **Attribute what only a report says.** When the official source does not name the companies, the graphic carries \"据… 报道 / reported by …\" and so does the voice.\n- **A schematic posing as data.** Rolling counters on a close price flash values that never existed; set the close with a mask push instead. Don't label axes the data doesn't support.\n- **Source lines under the strap.** Right-aligned mono 13 px: ≤ 36 characters.\n- **Stamps over key words.** A stamp hit can mask the voice (\"fines\"); take the brass off the stamp and low-pass its clack.\n- **Gold on paper** has too little contrast: use ink or dark olive.\n- **Generated plates** carry stray corner marks: crop them in `prep.py`.\n- **Short preview text.** Rundown rows are previews of the stories; `readcheck` flags them as short in the opening — they return in the recap and as straps, so accept the flag or leave them out of `TEXTS`.\n- **Names in synthetic speech.** Spell brand names, model numbers and abbreviations the way they should sound in the TTS text (a respelling, a number written as a word, an abbreviation without dots) while the strap and subtitles keep the real spelling; when speech-to-text mishears an unusual name, put the expected transcript in the line's `asr` field instead of rewriting the line.\n\n## 12. Variation space\n\nDecide per film: the channel's name, plate and accent colour; how many items (one lead with a long package, three or four equal items, a five-item round-up); which full-frame graphics each item needs; where the long take goes; the anchor's voice.\n\n- **Three structures:** rundown → items → rundown ticked (round-up); one lead package with a correspondent two-box and a short \"also today\" grid; a countdown of items (election-night, sports results).\n- **Three openings:** sting → rundown; cold open on the biggest graphic, then the sting; sting straight into the first story with the rundown as a recap only.\n- **Three endings:** rundown ticked → end card; a \"coming up next week\" teaser card; the last stamp freezing into the end card.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nThe Week in AI\n"},{"id":"S4-L9","name":"卫星地图简报","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L9.jpg","markdown":"# L9 卫星地图简报（Satellite Briefing）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 实证影像\n- 长什么样：卫星照着地图去找咸海，找到的是沙漠；只有筑了坝的那一块回来了。\n- 适合：讲地点、范围\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Satellite Briefing — Style Prompt\n\n> Real satellite imagery of a real place, graded down to an analyst's grey, with thin white measurement lines drawn on the ground: a lock-on reticle, outlines, a range line, one reading. One continuous zoom built from stacked imagery plates carries the viewer from orbit to the ground and back, and time can run inside one locked frame.\n> References (grammar only): open-source investigation films that annotate satellite pictures (thin lines on imagery, one claim per screen, measuring as the plot); the Eameses' *Powers of Ten* (one continuous change of scale with a readout); NASA Earth Observatory's *World of Change* (one place, time running). Copy none of their layouts, typefaces, icons or music; never name them in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n- **The ground is a photograph**: real, licence-clean, georeferenced satellite or aerial imagery of the actual place. Nothing on the ground is drawn, stylised or generated.\n- **Two layers, two truths**: the imagery is what the satellite saw; the hairlines are what the analyst measured. Marks are thin, white, sparse, and sit on real coordinates.\n- **The grade moves with the story**: orbit in true colour, the analysis layer in grey, colour back only where the story turns.\n- **Scale is the camera**: one zoom from orbit to the ground built from nested plates, with an altitude readout and a scale bar from the same camera.\n- **One finding per screen**, in the single accent colour.\n\nNot Data Storytelling (a chart on paper), not Sci-fi Hologram HUD (no glowing wireframes or invented chrome), not Blueprint, not Isometric Infographic (real nadir or oblique views, no diorama), not a keynote (no slides, header bars or chapter numbers).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Plates**: georeferenced rasters with a lon/lat box, sampled in a shader where the camera ray hits the ground, nested by resolution (world → region → detail) with feathered edges so finer plates dissolve in as the camera descends.\n- **Globe**: ray–sphere from a perspective camera, a thin atmosphere rim, limb shading at orbit only; compute |C|² − R² in double precision (float32 fails below a few tens of km).\n- **Time**: dated plates share one grid. A soft scan front (a satellite pass, never a hairline) swaps them: north → south forward, south → north for a rewind. Growth can run outward from the old edge, ordered by a distance map.\n- **The analyst's grade** (one uniform from colour to grey): luminance with local contrast and clarity, a shoulder so salt, snow and cloud stay below white, toned blue-black → cool silver up to ~65 % grey, a fifth of the chroma. White belongs to the marks.\n- **Marks**: a 2D overlay through the same world → screen function: hairlines 1.2–2.6 px with a dark halo, dashes for history and claims, brackets, ticks, leader lines. Lines draw on; outlines print behind the scan.\n- **Screen space only for instruments** (altitude, scale bar, imagery credit) and the transcript strip. Everything else is anchored to the ground.\n- **The claim layer** is a printed map drawn in the shader from a distance field of the old outline: paper grain a few pixels wide at any zoom, halftone ink, coast and waterlines, graticule, the serif name.\n- **Post**: light grain in ffmpeg, a vignette, directional blur only on whip pans.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- Neutral ground: grey, roughly 3–65 % in value. White means *measured*.\n- One accent (warm, e.g. amber) for the single reading on screen, never decoration.\n- A second hue may mark the **claim** the imagery tests (a map, a plan, a promise): flat, cartographic, never used for anything measured.\n- True colour is evidence: at orbit, and again only where the story turns (the field that greened, the water that returned).\n- No false-colour ramps over the imagery.\n\nExample (our demo): ground #070B0E → #A1A7A9, marks #F2F5F6, reading #FFBE55, map blue rgb(46, 112, 206).\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Roles**: a light condensed grotesk for the reading (96–130 px, e.g. Barlow Condensed); a monospace for instruments and dates (20–34 px, e.g. IBM Plex Mono); a sans for labels in the film's language (24–42 px); a serif only for the claim layer and the title. OFL, self-hosted, CJK subset to the characters used.\n- **Labels attach to the ground** with a leader line. The reading sits in the right third beside what it measures, label above, source note below (\"Landsat, measured\").\n- **Subtitles are a transcript strip** at the bottom left: a 2 px white rule, a dark plate fading right, left-aligned, each line on one line (measure with the real font, `nowrap`; split a long sentence into two cues). If the film is bilingual, the film's language on top.\n- Nothing below 20 px at 1080p, the imagery credit included.\n- Hold subtitles ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s); a rolling counter reports its landing value; labels stay ≥ characters ÷ 4.5 + 1.5 s (CJK).\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- The camera never stops: distance interpolates in log space, segments ease, holds keep a 2–5 % creep or a slow rotation. No frozen frame longer than a second.\n- Marks arrive by being made: a line grows, an outline prints behind the scan, a counter rolls and lands with a small pop, a reticle snaps with overshoot.\n- Time changes are scans, never dissolves; their spacing tells the speed, and the camera moves with them (accelerating as the steps shorten, braking on the last).\n- Never moves: the ground under its annotations, the instrument positions.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Oblique orbit, limb in frame | scale, context, distance | an opening; a return; a title card |\n| Dive (log zoom, tilt to nadir) | going to look for yourself | a reveal; checking a claim |\n| Locked nadir frame, slow creep | evidence, comparison | time running in one place; a measurement |\n| Push-in on a point | singling out | one town, one dam, one field |\n| Track along a line | travelling a distance | measuring; a river, a road, a border |\n| Whip pan with blur | a new subject at the same altitude | the counter-example; \"meanwhile\" |\n| Climb back to orbit | consequence, perspective | an ending; a summary |\n\nAt key moments the subject fills a third of the frame height; reading right, subtitles bottom left, instruments in the corners. Transitions: scan-line swaps, a map layer switched off, zooming through plates, whip pans, the reticle opening into a title card. No crossfades between unrelated places, no hard cuts inside a zoom.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Telemetry**: FM pings (a lock-on ping worth bringing back), dry ticks on the grid that divide to accelerate, noise scans (rising for a rewind, falling forward), servo whines, a ratchet for a range line.\n- **Weight**: timpani, bass drum, gran cassa, bowed cymbal, a tam-tam reversed when time runs backwards. Measurements land on a pitched hit.\n- **Synthesis**: a pulse bass with audible 2nd and 3rd harmonics, detuned-saw pads under a low-pass, risers for dives.\n- **Beds**: orbit hum, radio hiss, data chatter; the place's own ambience (wind, water, a far horn), sparingly, often as J- or L-cuts.\n- **One option for the shape**: keep the palette dry (electronics, skins, metal) and let one wet timbre (glass, water, bowed metal) mark the turn.\n- **Silence**: music and foley stop, a thin bed stays, impacts may decay into it; the first sound after it is the turn. A short suck-out (0.2–0.45 s, music and foley down ~25 dB) before the heaviest impacts makes them land.\n- **Mix**: voice ~10 dB over music; the two or three designed impacts are the loudest moments (out of the bus compressor, with mid-range body, e.g. a low-brass stab); 20–60 Hz about 15 dB under the full band (high-pass ~40 Hz, impact weight at 60–120 Hz); −14 LUFS, true peak ≤ −1.2 dB. Voice: a calm analyst, short declarative lines.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\n- **The map against the photograph.** The camera falls into the printed map of the claim; a scanner bar tears it away and the ground is underneath; the name outlives the page. *Fits content like:* a lake still on maps but gone; a cleared concession forest; a planned city never built.\n- **Time rings in a locked frame.** Dated plates scan in one by one; each leaves its outline, the band lost between two rings flashes. *Fits content like:* a glacier's retreat; a city's sprawl; a fire scar season after season.\n- **The range line.** Mark the destination, grow a ticked line from A to B, chase the tip while the reading counts up, land on the number, pull back to show the whole distance. *Fits content like:* how far a shore moved; a village to its well; a camp to the border.\n- **The dive and the climb.** One zoom from orbit to the ground, or back. *Fits content like:* locating anything; a scale reveal; an ending that returns one place to the planet.\n- **Colour returns where the story turns.** Only the subject regains true colour inside the grey. *Fits content like:* an irrigated field; a refilled reservoir; a regrown forest.\n- **The reticle becomes the frame**: the lock-on brackets open out to frame a title card or the last reading.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **Licences**: not usable: Google Earth/Maps, Bing, Esri World Imagery, Mapbox Satellite, Planet, Maxar, news photos, AI-generated imagery. EOX Sentinel-2 cloudless is CC BY 4.0 only for 2016 (2018 onward is NC). Raw Copernicus Sentinel data need \"Contains modified Copernicus Sentinel data [year]\"; check the user's rules. Public domain or free with credit: USGS Landsat (1972 on, worldwide), USGS NAIP (US, ~1 m), NASA Blue Marble, MODIS, VIIRS. Burn a readable credit into the picture and list every asset in `CREDITS`.\n- **Finding imagery for a new place**: search by bounding box and date in the Planetary Computer STAC (Landsat C2, Sentinel-2 L2A; no account) or USGS EarthExplorer; MODIS/VIIRS and Blue Marble from NASA GIBS; outlines from Natural Earth. Log every request. OSM (ODbL), CC BY-SA and \"no known restrictions\" are not CC0: name the terms and ask the user first. Ask for the user's own surveys or GIS layers when the place is theirs. Never fill a gap with generated imagery presented as evidence.\n- **Seams**: paths imaged on other days disagree. One date per path, each scene gain-matched on stable land, feathered overlaps, holes filled from the same year only.\n- **Old sensors**: Landsat MSS (to 1992) has no blue or SWIR and drops scan lines: nearest-neighbour resampling, near-zero rows as nodata filled from same-year scenes, scan-angle streaks cleaned on land only. Inspect every year at full contrast.\n- **Plumes and haze** cross water as bright diagonal bands: swap the scene for another date of the same year rather than painting it out.\n- **Edges**: resampling against nodata darkens scene rims (erode the mask); drop contour pieces along a plate border. Real straight edges (dikes, polders) can read as seams: frame them out or label them.\n- **Grey mush at close range**: raise local contrast for that altitude only and dim outside the measured corridor.\n- **Water and cloud**: MNDWI where shortwave infrared exists, only the basin you measure; clouds read as features in grey, so use the dry season.\n- **Facts**: keep a FACTS.md in the project folder (claim, source URL, quoted wording, or the measurement method with scene IDs and dates) and deliver it with the film. Measure on the date you show.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\n- `demo/engine.js`: `makeCamera({lat, lon, d, tilt, heading, sx, sy})`, `project()` / `unproject()`, the WebGL2 plate shader (nested plates, time scans, growth map, analyst grade, printed-map layer, whip blur).\n- `demo/overlay.js`: hairlines, brackets, `readout`, the measured single-line `subtitle` strip, `text` with a registry for `window.TEXTS`.\n- `demo/tools/`: `fetch_landsat.py` (STAC, one date per path, same-year gap fill), `plates.py` (normalisation, streak cleaning, water masks, areas), `vectors.py` (shorelines, distances), `mapfield.py` (the printed map), `growth.py`.\n- **Extra packages and packs**: none beyond `setup.sh deps voice` and the sample packs the score uses (`sh tools/fetch.sh instruments <lib>` for vsco2ce, vcsl, freepats in the demo). Imagery uses only the standard library, numpy, SciPy, Pillow (no GDAL); allow 3–4 GB for cached scenes. Anything more (rasterio, pyproj) goes in a private venv in the project folder, not `$LIB/.venv`.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nThe agent decides the place or places, whether there is a claim layer, which dates, the structure, the camera path, the accent hue and where colour comes back.\n\n- **Structures**: a locked time-lapse of one place ending in a push-in; a river followed from source to mouth with a reading at each stop; two places compared at one scale with whip pans.\n- **Openings**: a ground-level detail that climbs to reveal its scale; a black frame a scan line paints in; an oblique orbit with a searching reticle.\n- **Endings**: the climb back to orbit; a last measurement that lands and holds in silence; the claim layer redrawn to match the ground.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nThe Map Still Says Sea\n"},{"id":"S4-L10","name":"工程方案比较墙","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L10.jpg","markdown":"# L10 工程方案比较墙（Engineering Briefing Board）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 实证影像\n- 长什么样：一条河要架桥，四种桥型在一面简报墙上逐个受检，拍板的是桥下要过的船。\n- 适合：几个方案放在一起比\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Engineering Briefing Board — Style Prompt\n\n> One real whiteboard wall, filmed: window light on enamel, ghosts of erased marker, frame and tray. Cut-out photographs of real things land on it with weight; tags, magnets, a ruler and stamps are pinned to it; marker ink draws itself in three colours with fixed meanings. The camera rides the lines from fact to fact and only at the end pulls back to show the whole briefing.\n> References (grammar only): explainers filmed on a map wall; the long pull-back over a wall covered in work; drafting conventions (plan above elevation, dimension lines, hatching). Never copy them or name them in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n1. **A physical board in a room**: enamel, window falloff, a sliding reflection, ghosts of earlier work, frame and tray.\n2. **Real things on it**: cut-out photographs of the real objects, a few millimetres off the board, shadowed by one window, parallaxing. They are evidence: never a generated image presented as a real object.\n3. **Ink that means something**: graphite for names and rules, blue for water and measurement, red only for the decision; rejected ideas erased to ghosts.\n4. **One continuous wall**: no pages; the camera travels along lines, projections and leaders.\n5. **The whole wall, once**: every shot is a crop of the final briefing, seen whole only in the last pull-back, as a poster.\n\nNot **Whiteboard Explainer** (a hand drawing line art on a flat page): the board is a filmed object, subjects are photographs. Not **Data Storytelling**: comparisons are dimension lines in real units beside real objects. Not a keynote: no headers, centred bullets, page turns.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Board (z 0)**: procedural enamel, wiped ghost strokes, smudges, a window falloff, a sliding diagonal reflection; the frame shadows a warm grey wall.\n- **Ink (multiply)**: flat colour at about 90 %, lighter core, round caps, a wet-tip glint on the stroke being drawn. Lettering: a marker font revealed glyph by glyph with seeded tilt and baseline jitter. Erasing leaves a blurred ghost (about 7 %).\n- **Prints (z ≈ 6–14)**: photographs cut out at full resolution (a matting model, or a hand polygon / mask), clean edges, one shared grade (lower saturation, a touch warmer, lifted blacks). Thin structure (cables, wires, masts, railings) is measured in the photo and redrawn as clean lines in one colour. Shadow = blurred silhouette along one light vector, softer the higher the object. **No frame upsamples a photo**: ≥ 1 source px per screen px at its largest, measured; cap the zoom or find a bigger source.\n- **Secondary layer** (only where the camera never goes before the end): the photos as pinned prints, a source card, sums on sticky notes, a small sketch. Texture and proof for the final wall, lighter than the main line.\n- **Props (z ≈ 8–26)**: magnetic tags, magnets, a steel ruler, printed cards, a pitted stamp impression; the tray (z 70) nearest the lens.\n- **Camera**: parallax by height, sub-frame motion blur (180° shutter), light grain. Only subtitles are screen-space.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- Ground: the enamel and its light falloff; the wall around is neutral warm grey.\n- Ink: graphite = names, rules, facts; blue = water and measurement (dimensions, projection lines, measured numbers); red = the decision (a rejection, the key word of a verdict, the one mark to notice); grey for drafting only (extension lines, hatching).\n- Red is rare: two or three red marks per frame at most, never on a red object. Full colour belongs to the photographs; props stay neutral; a red magnet is a verdict.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Marker lettering**: a single-weight marker face covering every script the film uses (ours: LXGW Marker Gothic, OFL, subset — re-run when words change). Content 100–160 board px; a verdict 230–400.\n- **Printed labels** (tags, cards, stamps): a sturdy sans; credit lines in a monospace. A tag holds one to three words.\n- **Subtitles**: a frosted acrylic strip at the bottom centre with a blue marker tick. If the film is bilingual, the film's language sits on top. **One line per language**: `nowrap`, measured in the page against a 1,500 px safe width; a long line becomes two cues. Key content stays above the strip (bottom 15 %).\n- **Reading time**: subtitles ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s); board text stays fully in frame for CJK ÷ 4.5 + other ÷ 15 + 1.5 s from when it appears. In the final wall, text meant to be read is ≥ 20 px on screen.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- 24 fps on ones; nothing is stepped.\n- Camera: overlapping eased moves, velocity ∝ u^a (1−u)^b (a, b ≈ 1.5–4), so speed and acceleration never jump; b > a gives inertia. A hand-held breath (≤ 3 px) underneath; shake only on an impact.\n- Weight: prints are lowered from the camera side, land, bounce and settle as the shadow tightens; magnets clack on to pin them; tags swing; removed magnets pop toward the lens; struck things jolt and spring back. The camera may meet a landing close, briefly, on the object's most telling detail, then pulls out.\n- Blurred in motion, sharp where words are read. Every 1–2 beats something lands, draws or swings; only silences and judgement holds go still.\n- Ink draws at the speed of a hand; dimension lines land with an arrowhead tap.\n- Never: dissolves, scale-pops on text, bouncy UI easing, text fading in by itself.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ride a line (with the pen or a dimension) | measuring; \"how far does it go?\" | lengths compared; a route; a timeline |\n| Tilt along projection lines | the same quantity in another view | plan → elevation; a total → its parts |\n| Slow push-in, slight Dutch tilt | tension, an approaching problem | a collision; a deadline; an overrun |\n| Pull back and sideways | stepping back to state a rule | a constraint; a turn in the argument |\n| Whip with the line, overshoot, settle | a result that exceeds | a record; an outlier |\n| Rise along a leader line | from evidence to verdict | a conclusion; a recommendation |\n| Staircase framing (each new item with the previous result) | comparison in sequence | rankings; tests; options |\n| Judgement frame: the whole item, the limit and the verdict, held ≥ 2.5 s | a test the viewer can check | pass / fail against a threshold; a budget against a cap |\n| Long pull-back to the whole wall | scale; everything at once | the end of an argument; a summary |\n\nFraming: off-centre, cropped, close. The first frame holds a readable subject; the first strong action hits within 0.5 s. Items tested against a limit each get a judgement frame (wider each time if they grow); the verdict lands after the camera settles. Before the reveal: ≤ about 60 % of the board's width, never both side edges, never the reveal's zone. Transitions are moves; no cuts, fades or zoom-to-fit.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- Room: an air-conditioning bed and traffic through the window, about 20 dB under the voice — audible, not a hiss; silences keep only this.\n- Foley: felt-tip squeaks scaled to stroke length, nib taps, magnet clacks (a pitch per magnet in a row), suction pops, print landings (board thump, paper slap), a ruler's ring, eraser felt, a stamp's thud.\n- The subject's own sound arrives early as a J-cut; the first sound after a silence is an impact or the subject's sound.\n- Music: a small rhythmic ensemble, walking to brisk — bass, a light kit, wooden percussion, low brass stabs for verdicts (or mallets); no piano-and-strings pad. Landings, stamps and arrows sit on the beat. Voice calm, briefing-room confident, about 8–10 dB over the music; −14 LUFS.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\n- **The whole-wall reveal.** Design the wall as a poster first (hierarchy, reading path, filling the frame), then plan every shot as a crop of it; keep one zone (secondary layer, verdict, title card) for the reveal alone. *Fits:* a product roadmap; a household budget.\n- **The subject passes through the answer.** The real thing moves through the chosen solution, its sound first. *Fits:* a parcel along the chosen route; a train over the new viaduct.\n- **Ride the dimension line.** A quantity drawn as a real length to one scale; the camera rides the tip. *Fits:* battery ranges of four cars.\n- **Projection lines.** Dashed lines carry an edge from one view to another so two drawings share a scale. *Fits:* a trail map above its elevation profile.\n- **Magnets as countable things.** Units clack on in rhythm, pop off when a rule removes them. *Fits:* seats in a vote.\n- **Erase to a ghost.** A rejected idea wiped but faintly visible. *Fits:* last year's plan under this year's.\n- **The stamp.** A verdict lands with a thud and a jolt. *Fits:* passed / failed tests.\n- **The physical answer.** A real prop laid down as the solution. *Fits:* one cable instead of five.\n- **Leader to the verdict.** An arrow from the deciding number to the conclusion. *Fits:* the bottleneck in a process.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **Text glimpsed in passing** fails the reading time. Fix: words where the camera rests; frame each new item with the last result.\n- **Only close-ups.** The viewer never sees the whole object against the limit. Fix: detail → judgement frame → hold → verdict → move on.\n- **Photos that don't belong together.** Fix: one grade, one light vector, one viewpoint family.\n- **Thin parts lost or stair-stepped** by the matte. Fix: key them against the clean background, redraw clean lines at the measured positions.\n- **Upsampled photos** go soft in close-ups. Fix: full-resolution cuts, large sources, a per-frame check.\n- **Lettering in photos** (signs, names, logos). Fix: paint out, cut, or choose another photo.\n- **The wall seen too early** spends the ending; **a sparse or tiny final wall** wastes it. Fix: measure every frame's visible share; size the hierarchy for the poster; a quiet secondary layer, no empty middle.\n\n## 10. Sources, facts, engine\n\n- **Photos**: Wikimedia Commons (read each file page's licence and author), national libraries and archives (e.g. the Library of Congress: check each item's rights statement), agency media pages, Flickr Commons. Fine: CC0, public domain, CC BY (credit it). CC BY-SA, \"no known restrictions\" (≠ CC0) or other terms: name them to the user and ask first. For the user's own product, site or people, ask for their photos. No licensable photo of a real thing → draw it in marker as a sketch; never a generated image presented as the real object.\n- **Facts**: keep a `FACTS.md` in the project folder (each claim on screen or in the voice, its source URL, the quoted wording, or the measurement method) and deliver it with the film. List every photo in `CREDITS` with source, author and licence.\n- **Extra packages and packs** (beyond `setup.sh deps voice music`): `onnxruntime` and the BiRefNet-general matting model (MIT, ~970 MB: https://github.com/danielgatis/rembg/releases/download/v0.0.0/BiRefNet-general-epoch_244.onnx, path in `$BIREFNET_ONNX`) for cut-outs; `fonttools` only to subset CJK fonts offline. The agent installs them itself into a private venv in the project folder (`demo/kit/tools/kit_env.sh` does venv and model), never into `$LIB/.venv`. Without the model: cut with `prep_object.py --poly` (hand polygon) or `--mask` (a mask image).\n- **Engine** (copy into the project): `demo/kit/board.js` (camera, prints, tags, magnets, ruler, cards, stamp, ink, erasing, `sceneTexts(t)`, `subCheck()`; fields in `demo/kit/README.md`), `demo/kit/batch.mjs` (stills), `demo/kit/tools/` (cut-outs, line redraws, lettering wipe, prints, fonts), checks in `demo/tools/` (`camcheck.mjs` visible share and judgement frames, `rescheck.mjs`, `subcheck.mjs`).\n\nA minimal world (`node demo/kit/batch.mjs park.json`; `fetch_fonts.py park.json` first for new characters):\n\n```json\n{ \"dur\": 6, \"t\": 5.5, \"board\": { \"w\": 4000, \"h\": 2400 }, \"hand\": 0, \"cam\": { \"x\": 1500, \"y\": 1000, \"z\": 0.8, \"moves\": [] },\n  \"elements\": [\n    { \"type\": \"tag\", \"text\": \"地下车库\", \"xy\": [560, 800], \"size\": 60, \"arrive\": { \"t\": 0.2, \"kind\": \"swing\" } },\n    { \"type\": \"ink\", \"kind\": \"dim\", \"from\": [700, 900], \"to\": [1300, 900], \"color\": \"blue\", \"t\": 0.6, \"d\": 0.6 },\n    { \"type\": \"ink\", \"kind\": \"write\", \"text\": \"120 米\", \"xy\": [1360, 940], \"size\": 100, \"color\": \"blue\", \"t\": 1.2 },\n    { \"type\": \"stamp\", \"text\": \"首选\", \"xy\": [1500, 700], \"size\": 110, \"rot\": -8, \"t\": 4.2 } ] }\n```\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nThe agent decides per film: structure, which objects are photographed, plan vs elevation, the wall's reading path, opening, ending, camera path, pacing.\n\n- **Three structures:** one measurement that keeps growing until it breaks a limit; a before/after where the erased first plan stays as a ghost under the second; a checklist stamped item by item as the camera moves down the wall.\n- **Three openings:** a stamp on a finished drawing, then back to see why; one number written large, then the line that measures it; a photo dropped onto an empty corner.\n- **Three endings:** the wall revealed with the answer circled; back to the opening mark, now corrected; the last tag flipped from a question to an answer, then the pull-back.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, title card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nThe Ship Decides\n"},{"id":"S4-L11","name":"激光点云扫描","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L11.jpg","markdown":"# L11 激光点云扫描（LiDAR Survey）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 实证影像\n- 长什么样：激光点云剥掉一片森林的树冠，露出当年农田的石墙：3.4 公里。\n- 适合：科技感，\"扫一遍\"\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# LiDAR Survey — Style Prompt\n\n> Real airborne laser points of a real place, floating in the dark as a measured cloud; a scan line or a cutting plane passes through them and the points condense into a solid, raking-lit surface, which is then measured with hairline instruments. One designed camera move carries the whole film: the cloud is something you fly through, not a picture you look at.\n> References (grammar only): survey and archaeology lidar visualisations (colour as height, the canopy removed); architectural section drawings (a cut, dimensioned); museum film cadence (one held reading per screen). Copy none of their layouts, ramps, typefaces or music, and never name them in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n- **The cloud is the data**: every point is a real return at its measured position; points appear, glow, lift away or give way to a surface, but are never moved into a shape.\n- **Points become a surface**: a front (scan line, cutting plane) behind which the cloud condenses into a solid model of the same place.\n- **Classification is a lever**: ground, vegetation, buildings, water are layers to peel, isolate and recolour.\n- **Measurement in hairlines**: one reading at a time, in the accent, attached to what it measures; numbers from the points or an official source.\n- **Dark, quiet, exact**: near-black ground, restrained colour, no glow except at the front.\n\nNot Sci-fi Hologram HUD (no invented interface: the points are real, sparse where the survey was), not Satellite Briefing (photographs from above; here a 3D cloud flown through), not Blueprint, not Low-poly Isometric, not Glass Product Render.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Points**: GL points sized in world units (0.12–0.3 m), floor of one output pixel, soft discs, opaque and depth-tested (additive only for a ghost layer); colour from height or class, value from intensity; distance fog.\n- **Quantise for the browser**: uint16 positions (2 cm / 1 cm) plus a byte each for height above ground, class, intensity, seed.\n- **Height above ground** for every return (minus a ground model triangulated from class-2 returns) drives the colour ramp, the cutting plane and the ruler.\n- **The solid surface**: a 0.5–1 m mesh of the ground or surface model, smooth normals, baked short-radius AO, a low raking sun (20–35°), faint contours, crests lightened. Display exaggeration of micro relief (×2–3) only with an on-screen note; measure on undistorted data.\n- **The front**: one world-space plane (one uniform) shared by the point and surface shaders; points ahead of it brighten, a thin seam glows behind it and the surface fills in over tens of metres.\n- **The cutting plane** (by height above ground): returns above it lift away and fade, a ring glows where it meets the trees, a ruler on the frame edge tracks it.\n- **Marks on the surface** (traced features, section lines) are a distance field in the surface shader, kept hairline with `fwidth`; readings, leaders and charts are a 2D overlay through the same world → screen function.\n- **A survey window**: outside the measured area the cloud stays as dim points instead of ending in black.\n- **Post**: 2× render, high-threshold bloom (only fronts and seams), vignette; no grain (points are already fine detail; encode at a higher CRF).\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- Background near-black with a cold tint; never pure black, never a gradient sky.\n- Points: one ramp by height or class, desaturated (deep green-teal → pale; or grey → white); intensity modulates value, not hue. No rainbow elevation ramps.\n- The front and seams: one cool near-white; it is light, not a colour.\n- The solid surface: warm neutral (stone, clay, plaster), lit; its colour never competes with the accent.\n- One accent (amber) reserved for what was found and measured: traced features, the reading, the section line. A second, cooler hue only in a comparison chart.\n\nExample (our demo): background #05080A, canopy #1F3F35 → #5C9A7A → #E2F3DC, ground returns #C8B38C, surface #BDB5A6, front #D4FFF0, accent #FFB347, chart green #83C495.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Roles**: light condensed numerals for readings (110–130 px, e.g. Barlow Condensed 300); a monospace for instruments, coordinates, counts and source lines (19–22 px, e.g. IBM Plex Mono); a sans for labels in the film's language (24–36 px, e.g. Noto Sans SC 500). OFL, self-hosted, CJK subset to the characters used.\n- **Instruments** in one corner: place, coordinates, area, data source, point count, any exaggeration; they change only when the medium does.\n- **Readings** in the right third beside what they measure: label, number, source/method line; a leader when the feature is in frame; a rolling counter reports its landing value.\n- **Subtitles**: a transcript strip bottom-left (2 px rule, plate fading right), one line per language measured with the real font, split rather than wrapped; if bilingual, the film's language on top.\n- Nothing below 19 px at 1080p. Hold subtitles ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s); labels ≥ characters ÷ 4.5 + 1.5 s (CJK), letters ÷ 15 + 1.5 s (Latin).\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- On ones, 24 fps; the camera never stops; keys as a C1 spline in target / distance / heading / pitch (no `lookAt` from nadir).\n- The front moves on an ease, slowest where the subject is; a cutting plane steps on the beat (eased steps, short holds) so the layers read.\n- Points appear behind a front or by height, never by random fade-in across the whole frame.\n- Readings land with a small pop; traced features light up along their length in order.\n- Never moves: the points relative to each other (no wobble, no noise displacement), the instruments' positions.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Locked nadir with slow push | \"this is what a photo shows\" | a claim to test; a before; an ending that returns to the map |\n| Crane down with tilt to oblique | entering the data; the place has height | the switch from picture to cloud |\n| Low oblique close to the cloud (10–20° pitch, 90–150 m) | volume; things standing up | canopy, buildings, a facade, a cliff |\n| Slow orbit | studying, the plane doing the work | a peel; a scan; a held reading |\n| Rising pull-back | scale reveal of what was found | a network, a site plan, a corridor |\n| Lateral track | travelling a feature | a wall, a road, a pipeline, a coast |\n| Push to a section | the detail that proves it | a cross-section, a clearance, a height |\n\nSubject ≥ a third of the frame height at key moments; readings right third; instruments in a corner; nothing in the subtitle strip. Transitions are fronts and planes, never crossfades or hard cuts inside the cloud.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Instrument sounds**: FM pings, fine crackle as points arrive, noise sweeps tied to a front, ticks that divide (8ths → 16ths → 32nds) to accelerate, a descending pitched blip per peeled layer, a ratchet while a counter rolls, a ping when a reading lands.\n- **Weight and synthesis**: timpani, bass drum, gran cassa for condensations and cuts, a reversed swell into the main impact; low-passed detuned-saw pads; a pulse bass with audible 2nd/3rd harmonics.\n- **Dry and wet**: one option is electronics while the subject is still points, and a single organic, wooden or bowed timbre (marimba, bowed vibraphone) for the found features once the surface exists.\n- **Beds**: the place's own ambience (wind in leaves, water, traffic) at the start and the end as J- and L-cuts; a thin air bed in between.\n- **Silence**: a near silence (air only) before the main reveal; the first sound after it is the impact.\n- **Mix**: voice ~10 dB over the music and ducking it; impacts outside the bus compressor; −14 LUFS, true peak ≤ −1.2 dBTP. Voice: a calm, plain narrator, short declarative lines.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\n- **Photo to points.** A scan line crosses an aerial photo; behind it the place is points. *Fits content like:* a site visit before a design; what the satellite misses; a quarry's real volume.\n- **The canopy peel.** A plane descends by height above ground, returns above it lift away layer by layer, a ruler tracks it. *Fits content like:* archaeology under forest; a hidden trail; flood channels; landslide scars.\n- **Points condense.** A front turns the remaining points into a solid surface; found features light up in order. *Fits content like:* a building scan to a BIM shell; a cliff to a hazard model; a street to a vehicle clearance model.\n- **The section.** A plane crosses one feature; the profile glows and a dimensioned chart of the real returns hangs beside it. *Fits content like:* a levee's height; a bridge's clearance; a road cut.\n- **The window.** The measured area is a stage inside the unmeasured cloud. *Fits content like:* a parcel; a corridor; a site boundary.\n- **The regrowth.** The removed layer returns as a ghost over what was found. *Fits content like:* an ending; a clearing before/after; a buried pipe under a street.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **Finding data for a new place**: USGS 3DEP (US) as Entwine Point Tiles per project at `https://s3-us-west-2.amazonaws.com/usgs-lidar-public/<project>/ept.json`, projects and footprints in `boundaries/resources.geojson` of github.com/hobuinc/usgs-lidar; bare-earth scouting through the 3DEP elevation ImageServer. OpenTopography (catalogue of many collections, licence per dataset). AHN (Netherlands, CC0). National portals, e.g. England's Environment Agency LiDAR (OGL), France IGN LiDAR HD (Licence Ouverte), Spain PNOA-LiDAR (CC BY 4.0), Finland NLS (CC BY 4.0), New Zealand LINZ (CC BY 4.0); read each portal's terms. Fetch a subset by box and depth, not whole tiles.\n- **Licences**: public domain, CC0, CC BY and attribution-only government licences are fine, with credit in `CREDITS`. ODbL, CC BY-SA, \"no known restrictions\" (not CC0) or non-commercial terms: name them to the user and ask first. Never commercial scans or Google 3D tiles. Ask the user for their own scans (a site survey, a drone or terrestrial scan, their BIM) when the film is about their asset. Never stand in a generated or invented cloud for real evidence.\n- **Facts**: keep a FACTS.md in the project folder (claim, source URL, quoted wording, or the measurement method) and deliver it with the film.\n- **Coordinates**: EPT copies are often Web Mercator: convert to a local east/north/up frame first; check the vertical datum against known ground.\n- **Leaf-off vs leaf-on**: leaf-off lidar reaches the ground but its canopy is sparse; say which season each layer is.\n- **Classification is the provider's**: class 1 mixes vegetation, buildings and noise; drop 7 and 18; finish a cut below 0 m or low vegetation stays as stripes.\n- **A dense cloud reads as a flat carpet** from afar: go close and low for volume; let the distance fog out.\n- **Scan striping** is real texture; don't frame a reveal on it at grazing angles.\n- **Traced features are interpretation**: say \"traced\", keep the tracing in a data file, snap it to the data, leave faint candidates out.\n- **Privacy**: crop out houses and yards; keep the survey window on public or uninhabited land.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\n- `demo/film.js`: point shader (front, cutting plane, lift, ghost, window), surface shader (light, AO, contours, feature field, section), photo shader, camera spline (`camAt`), overlay (`reading`, `drawSection`, `subtitles`, readcheck registry).\n- `demo/tools/`: `fetch_ept.py` (EPT subset), `terrain.py` (local frame, DTM), `walls.py` (relief model), `trace.py` (snap a tracing, measure), `profile.py` (section from raw returns), `build_data.py` (binaries + facts).\n- **Extra packages and packs** (beyond `setup.sh deps voice music`): Python `laspy`, `lazrs` (LAZ decoding), `scikit-image` (relief and ridge tools), `fonttools` + `brotli` (CJK subset). They are not in the library's `.venv`: the agent installs them itself into a private venv in the project folder (the demo uses `demo/assets/raw/.venv`), never into `$LIB/.venv`. Fonts come from github.com/google/fonts (ofl/), fetched by script.\n- Minimal example, not in the demo: a building scan. Points coloured by class, a vertical front sweeping the street; behind it a 1 m surface model; one reading (roof height above the street).\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nThe agent decides the place and dataset, which classes to peel or keep, the fronts and their directions, the camera route, the readings, the accent within the colour logic, and whether a photo or a map opens the film.\n\n- **Structures**: a corridor flown end to end with three readings; two surveys of one place (erosion, construction) swapped by a front; one object scanned, cut and dimensioned.\n- **Openings**: a black frame the scan paints in line by line; a street-level photograph that dissolves into its own returns; a top-down map that tilts up into the cloud.\n- **Endings**: the cloud pulls back to show the whole survey tile; the removed layer returns as a ghost; the last reading holds while the points fade to the instruments.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, title card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nThe Fields Under the Forest\n"},{"id":"S4-L12","name":"白模投影","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L12.jpg","markdown":"# L12 白模投影（Projection on White Model）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 实证影像\n- 长什么样：投影机把数据一层层刷上鹿特丹白模：从 1940 年的空洞到今天的塔楼。\n- 适合：把数据投到白色模型上\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Projection on White Model — Style Prompt\n\n> A pure white massing model of a real place stands on a plinth in a dark room, and a projector paints data onto it as light: one layer at a time, falling on the surfaces that face it, spilling a little past the plate. The model never changes; only the light on it does.\n> References (grammar only): projection-mapped planning models in city galleries (the dark room, the soft throw, colour only on white surfaces); macro model photography (shallow focus at rooftop height); editorial data graphics (one figure, one label, one source per layer). Copy none of their layouts, typefaces, colours or music, and never name them in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n- **The model is real geometry of a real place**: building footprints and heights from open data (§2), extruded at a stated scale (1:1000, 1:2000…). Nothing on the model is invented or \"representative\", and no generated image ever stands in for the place.\n- **The model is white; data is light.** Colour is never painted into the material: it is projected, so it brightens surfaces that face the projector, fades on walls turned away, stops at the edge of the throw and reaches a little onto the plinth.\n- **Layers arrive one at a time**, each with its own gesture of light (a wash, a pop-on, a rising level, a moving sun), and each answers the question the last one raised.\n- **A physical room**: house lights, a projector that strikes and switches, a plinth, darkness around it. Lights out is where the data starts; lights up is where it ends.\n- **Every number on screen is computed from the same buildings** that are on the table.\n\nNot Isometric Infographic (no diorama or icons), not Low-poly Island (no invented terrain), not Sci-fi Hologram HUD (no wireframes; light lands on matter), not Satellite Briefing (no imagery), not a laser-scan or point-cloud film (no scan lines; light is projected, not measured).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Model**: matte white (≈ #F3F3F1, roughness ≈ 0.9), crisp edges; water a darker satin inlay in the base plate; bridges low white slabs; a thin base plate on a dark satin plinth.\n- **Light model**: house light = one soft key + a neutral studio environment + AO on close shots. Projection = a term added to the lit surface in the shader: `colour × (0.42 + 0.58 · max(N·toProjector, 0)) × throw mask`, scaled by albedo, reduced on water. Never a texture on the mesh.\n- **Building data is the material.** First look for the place's own open 3D or footprint-with-height data: 3DBAG (Netherlands, CC BY 4.0), PLATEAU (Japan, CC BY 4.0-compatible), city open-data portals (footprints with heights or floors, zoning, plans). Fine: CC0, public domain, CC BY (credited in CREDITS and on screen). OpenStreetMap and Overture (ODbL), CC BY-SA and \"no known restrictions\" are not CC0: name the terms to the user and ask first. For their own site, ask the user for their data (plan, city model, survey heights).\n- **Layers depend on what exists.** Construction years per building are a Dutch luxury (BAG); elsewhere they are often missing. Substitute what the data does hold and the medium can still project: heights or floor counts, footprint area, zoning or plan status, a boundary, sun and shadow (always computable). Never invent a year layer.\n- **A layer is state, not a picture**: per-building attributes (year, height, rank, flags) live in vertex data; the shader decides each frame what colour the projector gives them.\n- **The sun is a real directional light** with shadow maps, computed from the place's latitude and a real date and time (NOAA/Meeus solar position). The projector can trace what the sun does (a shadow footprint) but never fakes a shadow.\n- **Post**: physical depth of field (stronger on close shots), bloom only above the projection highlights, vignette, light grain in ffmpeg. GTAO only on close shots: on wide grazing views it bands.\n- **Screen space only for the 2D layer**: readouts, pins, measured lines and subtitles go through the camera's world → screen function so they stay pinned to the model.\n- **Keep a FACTS.md in the project folder** (claim, source URL, quoted wording, or the measurement method) and deliver it with the film; every number on screen is computed by a script from the same data as the model.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- Neutral ground: white model, dark room (near black), dark plinth. Projector black level is a cool near-black on the plate.\n- **One hue family per layer**, chosen for the data: a single categorical accent for \"the old/the exception\" (e.g. warm amber), a sequential ramp for continuous data (blue → cyan → white for time, violet → magenta → peach for height).\n- **A colour may return** only with the same meaning (the \"before\" colour reused later marks the same set of buildings).\n- The sun is warm, its shade is a projected deep blue; data colours are put away when the sun arrives.\n- Never colour the model in the house light; never use more than two layer palettes on screen at once.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Roles**: a light grotesk for the figure (Inter Tight 200, 120–140 px), a sans for labels in the film's language (Noto Sans SC 300/500, 20–36 px), a monospace for instruments, years and coordinates (IBM Plex Mono, 18–22 px). OFL, self-hosted, CJK subset.\n- **Readout in the left third**, one per layer: index + layer name, one figure, one label, one source line, a legend bar.\n- **Pins**: a hairline from a roof or a ground point up to a tag on a dark plate (title 26 px + one sub line 20 px).\n- **Identity label** top-left, quiet: place, scale, data sources.\n- **Subtitles** centred at the bottom on a soft dark gradient, no box, measured with the real fonts; if the film is bilingual, the film's language on top, each language on a single line, split into two cues rather than wrap.\n- Nothing below 18 px at 1080p. Hold subtitles ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s); labels ≥ characters ÷ 4.5 + 1.5 s (CJK), letters ÷ 15 + 1.5 s (Latin).\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- The camera moves continuously and slowly; it changes speed with the layers, never stops dead.\n- **Light is the actor.** Each layer arrives by a soft-edged gesture of light: a wash spreading from the projector axis, buildings switching on one by one with a short flash, a level climbing every facade, the sun's clock running. No hard line sweeping the table.\n- House lights cut fast (a relay, 0.25 s) and return slowly (1 s); the projector strikes with a brief flicker.\n- Counters roll while the light moves and land when it lands; measured lines grow from A to B with ticks.\n- What never moves: the model, the plinth, the pins' anchors on the model.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Macro glide at rooftop height, shallow focus | the model is a real object | an opening; a detail that matters |\n| Crane up and back | the whole pattern only seen from above | revealing a hole, a cluster, an edge |\n| Slow orbit with a rise | the city as a whole, layers accumulating | growth over time; comparisons across the model |\n| Boom down to a low angle against black | height, skyline | tall buildings, heights, a single tower |\n| Side-on track along a line | a measured distance on the model | a shadow, a corridor, a route, a view line |\n| Pull back as the house lights return | the data stops; the object remains | an ending; a reset between topics |\n\nAt key moments the subject fills a third of the frame height; readouts left third, subtitles bottom centre, pins never over the readout. Transitions are light changes on one take (lights out, a layer replacing another, the projector going dark), not cuts, fades or wipes.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **The room**: room tone, the projector fan (hum + filtered air) rising when the lamp strikes, relays for the house lights, a lamp strike (zap + thump), the air of a light wash (sliding band-passed noise).\n- **Light as sound**: mallets and glass for things switching on, bowed vibraphone for held light, a rising mallet line and air riser for a climbing level, one glass note per highlighted building.\n- **Weight**: timpani, gran cassa, gong, low brass for a heavy fact; warm low brass for a turn.\n- **Floor**: contrabass and cellos only as a floor under the chords; no generic piano and strings melody.\n- **Silence**: the projector goes dark and everything stops but a thin fan hum before the turn; a short suck-out before the heaviest hit. The first sound after the silence is the turn.\n- **Mix**: voice about 10 dB over the music; the two designed impacts are the loudest moments; −14 LUFS, true peak ≤ −1.2 dB. Voice: calm, short declarative lines, numbers spoken plainly.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\n- **Lights out, lamp on.** House light, a relay, darkness, the lamp strikes, the first layer arrives. *Fits content like:* a site before and after; a district before a plan is shown; any \"now look closer\".\n- **The hole.** Light only the set that answers a question (built before a date, owned by one party, inside a zone) and let the absence draw the shape. *Fits content like:* a war-damaged centre; a flood plain with no housing; land a company owns; a protected zone.\n- **Layers by time.** Buildings switch on in the decade they were built, each layer faster than the last, a year counter rolling. *Fits content like:* a new town's growth; a campus master plan by phase; a port's expansion; a developer's portfolio by year.\n- **The rising level.** A soft level of light climbs every facade at once; low buildings finish first, the tallest last, the reading rides up with it. *Fits content like:* building heights; sea-level or flood depth; floor counts; a height limit being tested.\n- **The sun clock.** A real sun at a real date sweeps the shadows; the projector then traces one shadow and measures it. *Fits content like:* a new tower's shadow on a park; winter light in a courtyard; solar panels' best roofs; a street's daylight hours.\n- **The pick-out.** One building or set lights alone while the rest stay dark, with a pin. *Fits content like:* a listed building; the plot for sale; the hospital in an evacuation plan.\n- **Lights up.** The data stops, the white model comes back. An ending or a reset.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **Wall normals** wound the wrong way show inside faces (walls look hollow): derive the outward normal from ring orientation. **Shared walls** between parts z-fight: skip the overlap below the lower neighbour.\n- **Warm HDRIs turn the white model beige**: use a neutral studio environment.\n- **GTAO on wide grazing views** produces wedge-shaped dark bands; keep it on close shots only.\n- **A low sun lights facades, not roofs**: shoot from the sunlit side or side-on. **Narrow shadows vanish** among all the others: trace the one that matters (fill + edge) and measure it.\n- **Hairline scan lines read as laser scanning**: give every projection front a wide soft edge or switch buildings individually.\n- **Floating labels**: anchor pins on the tallest part of a building, not on its footprint centroid.\n- **Numbers from mixed sources** (official height vs measured roof): say which on screen (\"roof\", \"measured\").\n\n## 10. Engine\n\n- `demo/studio.js` — `makeStudio(W, H, model)`: renderer, post, room, the projection material (`projMaterial(kind)` injects `projLight()` / `roofGlow()` into MeshStandardMaterial), the city mesh (`buildCity`: outward normals, shared walls removed), plate, plinth, house light (index 0) and sun (index 1). Uniforms `U` drive every layer.\n- `demo/tools/prep.py` — **3DBAG + BGT only**: it reads 3DBAG fields (`b3_h_70p`, `b3_h_maaiveld`, `oorspronkelijkbouwjaar`) and Dutch RD coordinates (`tools/rd.py`). Another source needs its own adapter that writes the same `model.json`: `{size:[w,d] model metres, b:[[year|0, height_m], …] per building, p:[[building_index, height_model_m, [[[x,z],…] outer ring, …holes]], …] per roof part, decks:[[[x,z],…]]}`, with x east, z south, 1 unit = 1 model metre; plus `water.png` (plate-sized mask) and a `stats.json` with every on-screen number. `tools/sun.py` (solar position, any place and date) and the heightfield shadow are source-independent.\n- `demo/overlay.js` — readout, pin, measured range line, subtitle strip, title card; registers texts for readcheck.\n- **Extra packages and packs**: none beyond `setup.sh deps` (+ `voice`, `music` for narration and score); downloads are plain `curl` from the data portal. Reprojecting another country's data may need pyproj or shapely: the agent installs them itself into a private venv in the project folder, not into `$LIB/.venv`.\n- Minimal example, not in the demo: set `U.uPreC` to a station and `U.uPreR` growing to light everything within walking distance; or put a flood height in `U.uHgtF` with `U.uHgtBand` held at 1 and leave it still.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nDecide per film: place and scale, which layers in what order, their colours, the opening, the camera path, the ending, the pace.\n\n- **Structures**: a question answered layer by layer; a plan versus today (the plan's buildings switching on in a second colour); one building's story told by its neighbours.\n- **Openings**: a macro glide over white roofs; darkness and a single pick-out; the plinth from across the room as the house lights fall.\n- **Endings**: lights up on the white model; one last layer left on alone; the camera rising until the model is an object on a table in a dark room.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, title card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nHow a Flattened City Grew Back\n"},{"id":"S4-L13","name":"数据实体化","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L13.jpg","markdown":"# L13 数据实体化（Data Physicalization）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 实证影像\n- 长什么样：十二年风电和太阳能装机叠成实物，绕表盘升成两条螺旋，2021 年太阳能反超。\n- 适合：数字变成一堆实物\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Data Physicalization — Style Prompt\n\n> Real statistics built as real objects: every unit is a countable piece of material (a plate, a block, a coin, a bar) stacked to exact scale on a crafted base, shot like a product in a studio — soft key, long shadows, shallow depth of field, physically based materials. The camera reads a number at the edge of one plate, follows the data as it grows, and pulls back until the chart is an installation.\n> References (grammar only): data-sculpture and physical-visualisation practice (one unit = one object, the object is the axis); product and still-life photography (a warm key at a low angle, a cool rim, a seamless sweep); annual-report editorial design (one reading per screen, a source line, a quiet grotesk). Copy none of them; never name them in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n- **The unit is an object.** One plate, block or coin equals one stated quantity, and the rule is said on screen once (\"1 plate = 10 GW\"). A heavier material marks every tenth unit, so the stack is its own ruler.\n- **Scale is exact.** Heights, counts and areas are computed from the data; rounding is one unit at most and written in the facts. Two series compared on the same base use the same unit.\n- **Studio light, real matter.** Metal reflects an HDRI, wood shows grain, acrylic transmits; nothing glows, nothing is outlined, no fake UI.\n- **Growth is assembly.** Data increases by pieces being placed, one after another, with weight and sound; it never scales or morphs.\n- **The pull-back.** At least once the chart is revealed as a made thing: a dial, a table piece, a landscape of stacks.\n- **Every number has a source on screen**: real published statistics (or the user's own figures), credited in frame.\n\nNot Isometric Infographic (no icons, no flat colour coding), not Glass Product Render (the object is data), not Data Storytelling (no paper chart or inked axes), not Dark Tech Keynote (no black stage or neon), not Brick Toy (no toy proportions, no characters).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Pieces**: thin boxes or cylinders instanced in the thousands, each with a few-millimetre random offset and yaw, a ±7 % value change and a hairline gap: that is what makes a stack read as stacked, not as a bar.\n- **Material per series**, chosen so two series separate in one glance at thumbnail size: brushed metal against tinted acrylic, pale wood against dark stone, copper coins against paper. A third material (e.g. brass) only for the ruler pieces, shared by every series.\n- **Base**: a real-texture slab (wood veneer, stone) with inlaid marks: a tick ring, the series' paths, the labels of categories or years, cut into the surface at the base's own scale, lit with the scene.\n- **Rendering**: PBR (transmission for acrylic), an HDRI at low intensity for reflections only, a warm key at 20–35° with long soft shadows, one or two cool rims so metal and acrylic separate from wood at distance; GTAO, physical depth of field, 2× supersampling, a light vignette.\n- **Screen space only for type**: readings, verdicts, the source line, subtitles, the title card. Every reading is pinned to its object through the same world → screen function as the camera.\n- **Post**: grain light (the background is pale), no lens flares, no chromatic aberration.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- The set is warm and neutral: a seamless greige sweep, the base in a natural material, the series in material colours (silver, pale teal, brass, oak). Material, not paint, separates series.\n- **One accent** (e.g. signal orange) for the reading or verdict that matters on this screen; nothing else is accent-coloured.\n- Ink is near-black at two strengths (value, then labels at ~60 %).\n- No rainbow series palettes, no colour ramps on the objects, no dark mode.\n\nExample (our demo): sweep #C4BEB4, walnut base, aluminium #C8CCD1 (metal), acrylic #9FD3D6 with teal attenuation, brass #D2B07A, ink #1C1D20, accent #D4521C.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Roles**: a light grotesk for numbers (e.g. Inter 200–300; the hero reading 130–170 px, a stack reading 46–56 px); a sans for labels and verdicts in the film's language (e.g. Noto Sans SC 500, 24–46 px); a monospace for units, years and source lines (20–24 px); a serif only for the title. OFL, self-hosted, CJK subset.\n- **A reading** = a dot on the object, a 1.4 px leader, a small swatch of the series' material, the category line, then the value and unit, on a translucent paper plate (~84 %). Two readings of one pair stack in one column, larger value on top.\n- **The source line** (dataset, publisher, year, licence, e.g. \"数据：IRENA (2026)，经 Our World in Data 整理 · CC BY 4.0\") is in frame with the data, small (20–22 px, ink at ~60 %) in a corner away from the subtitles, at least from the first reading to the summary, and on the title card.\n- **A verdict** sits in the upper-left third on its own plate in the accent colour, underlined by a growing rule; it appears only after the readings it judges have been still for ≥ 2.5 s.\n- **Subtitles**: bottom-left, a 2 px ink rule and a paper plate fading right; if the film is bilingual, the film's language on top; each line one line only, measured with the real font (`nowrap`), split into two cues rather than wrapped. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- Labels hold ≥ CJK characters ÷ 4.5 + 1.5 s / letters ÷ 15 + 1.5 s; a counter reports its landing value; nothing below 20 px.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- On ones, 24 fps; the camera never stops (2–5 % creep on holds).\n- **Cascade**: a stack assembles bottom-to-top in 0.5–1.0 s, each piece dropping a few centimetres with an ease-out.\n- **Feed**: one period's increase arrives piece by piece from out of frame, quintic ease-out, a yaw that unwinds (\"placed\", not \"dropped\"), 8–16 pieces per second, the counter rolling with the pieces landed.\n- **A single heavy object** falls with gravity, lands with one small damped bounce, its shadow tightening.\n- Readings fade in with a 16 px rise and draw their leader; they leave by fading, never by sliding.\n- Never moves: the base, a piece once landed, the ruler pieces.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Macro dolly along a stack's edge, rack focus | the unit is real and countable | the rule \"1 piece = X\"; a single record |\n| Low lateral track along the series | time passing; growth | a decade; a ranking; a sequence of sites |\n| Locked three-quarter frame on two subjects | comparison | before/after; A against B; a crossover |\n| Crane with the rising top | accumulation | one year's increase; a target being reached |\n| Pull-back to near top view | the whole as an object | a summary; a ratio; an ending |\n| Slow orbit around the base | the installation as sculpture | a title; a breath |\n\nThe subject fills at least a third of the frame height at key moments; readings live in empty background, never over the subtitle strip; comparisons keep both subjects completely in frame. Transitions are camera moves inside one set (one continuous take where possible); no cuts to a chart, no wipes, no page turns.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **The material's own sound** is the foley: metal plates tick bright and short (inharmonic partials 3–8 kHz), acrylic toks duller (1–3 kHz), wood knocks, coins ring. A cascade is a burst of these at the landing times, scaled down as density rises.\n- **The ruler sings**: every ruler piece (each 10th) rings a pitched mallet note, one timbre per series (e.g. glockenspiel for metal, glass harp for acrylic), climbing a pentatonic scale with the count — the chart becomes an arpeggio.\n- **Score**: mallets and air — marimba ostinato, bowed and struck vibraphone, harp, glass, a soft synth pad and a pulse bass with audible 2nd/3rd harmonics; horns for a turn. Accelerate by subdivision (8ths → 16ths), not tempo.\n- **Impacts**: a turn or a last object lands on punch (60–120 Hz) + bass drum + timpani + a bell; the two or three designed impacts are the loudest moments.\n- **Silence**: a 0.3–0.6 s suck-out (music and foley −30 dB) before each designed impact; a thin studio-air bed stays.\n- **Mix**: voice ~10 dB over music, ruler notes under the voice, −14 LUFS, true peak ≤ −1.2 dBTP. Voice: calm, precise, short sentences.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\n- **The unit close-up.** Push into the side of a stack until one piece fills the frame; bracket one piece (\"= 10 GW\") and one ruler piece (\"= 100 GW\"). *Fits content like:* tonnes of CO₂ as steel plates; a budget as banknote bricks; a population as wooden discs.\n- **The paired climb.** Two series on one base, one material each, built year by year as the camera tracks; the crossover is held still with both readings before the verdict. *Fits content like:* imports vs exports; renewables vs fossil capacity; two companies' revenue.\n- **The feed.** One period's increase arrives piece by piece onto last period's level, the counter rolling with the landings. *Fits content like:* this year's new hires on last year's team; a quarter's sales; a record harvest.\n- **Chart to installation.** Pull back until the bars are a dial, a skyline or a table piece; the base's inlaid marks become its typography. *Fits content like:* twelve months as a clock; regions as a map-shaped base; product lines as a city.\n- **The last object.** One single heavier piece (a brass puck, a coin, a weight) lands on the base carrying the final number engraved. *Fits content like:* a total; a ratio; a target met.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Two materials too alike (bright aluminium vs clear acrylic) read as one series: give one a tint or a finish change, and check at thumbnail size.\n- Long shadows and warm reflections turn metal brown at distance: add a cool rim light and keep the base darker than the pieces.\n- Thin pieces moiré at 2–3 px per layer: random per-piece value and offset, 2× supersampling.\n- A reading pinned to a moving object can leave the frame: clamp it to a safe box.\n- Counters that roll by time instead of by landed pieces lie; tie them to the object count.\n- Rounding to whole pieces can invert close values; when two values are within one unit, say so on screen or choose a finer unit.\n- Transmission streaks at low angles: acrylic roughness ≥ 0.2, tinted attenuation.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\n- `demo/set.js` — `buildSet(scene)` (HDRI, warm key + cool rims, base with inlay canvas, instanced pieces per material, the last object) and the landing schedule (`SHEETS`, `EVENTS`, `landed(stack, t)`); change `PAIRS`, the unit and the materials for any single or paired series.\n- `demo/film.js` — cylindrical camera keys (`track`, `core/lib.js`) with focus on a target point, the reading kit (`pairLabels`, `verdict`, `plate`, `swatch`), counter, subtitles (`SUBCHECK`), `TEXTS`, `EV`. `demo/mix.py` — material foley (`tick_alu`, `tok_acr`), ruler notes, suck-outs, master.\n- **Extra packages and packs**: beyond `setup.sh deps` only the studio HDRI, `sh tools/fetch.sh hdri` (Poly Haven, CC0, 12 MB); with narration `setup.sh deps voice`; for a sampled score `sh tools/fetch.sh instruments vsco2ce` / `vcsl` / `freepats`. The walnut veneer is in `core/assets/polyhaven/`. Anything you add (a spreadsheet reader, other textures) goes into a private venv / node_modules in the project folder, never into `$LIB/.venv`.\n- Minimal example, not in the demo: one series of twelve monthly values on a 12-position circle, the annual total engraved on the last object.\n\n### Sources, licences, facts\n\n- **Statistics for a new topic**: Our World in Data (each chart's \"About this data\" names the original source; CC BY 4.0 — credit both), World Bank Open Data (CC BY 4.0), Eurostat (CC BY 4.0), US Census / BLS / EIA (public domain); UN Data, IRENA, IEA free tables and other national statistics offices (e.g. 国家统计局): check each dataset's terms. Search the portal for the indicator, download the CSV, keep the file and its metadata in the project's `data/`.\n- **Licences**: CC0, public domain and CC BY are fine with a credit. ODbL, CC BY-SA, \"no known restrictions\" or \"free for non-commercial use\" are not CC0: name the terms to the user and ask before using them. Textures and HDRIs: Poly Haven / ambientCG (CC0).\n- **The user's own numbers** (annual report, ESG data, sales): ask for them and for the document they come from; never invent or round them into a better story. Never let generated images stand in for real data or material.\n- **Keep a FACTS.md in the project folder** — each on-screen number and spoken claim, its source URL, the quoted wording or row, and how any derived value (ratio, difference, units per piece) was computed — and deliver it with the film.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nDecided per film: the unit and its materials, the base and its shape, which series share it, the camera route, pacing, the last object, accent hue within the colour logic.\n\n- Structures: a single series rising to a record; two series and their crossover; a ranking of regions laid out as a map-shaped base.\n- Openings: the macro of one piece being placed; the finished installation seen from above, then the dive to the first unit; an empty base with its inlay waiting.\n- Endings: the last object landing at the centre; a slow orbit of the whole installation; a single piece lifted off to show what one unit is.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, title card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nTwo Spirals\n"},{"id":"S4-L14","name":"爆炸图拆解","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L14.jpg","markdown":"# L14 爆炸图拆解（Exploded Teardown）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 实证影像\n- 长什么样：开源键盘的一半在一次环绕中逐层拆开，看到主控再合拢，标上真实尺寸。\n- 适合：拆开看里面，比如 AI 由哪几部分组成\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Exploded Teardown — Style Prompt\n\n> A real product, built from its own engineering files (CAD, PCB layout, bill of materials), lit like a studio packshot and taken apart in front of the camera: the layers lift on one axis, the camera travels between them, every part is named with the numbers its files contain, and it closes again into one object.\n> References (grammar only): hardware-launch exploded films (layer stagger, orbit timing), repair-guide teardowns (opening order, the macro on the part that matters), engineering drawing (leaders, dimension lines). Never copy their products, layouts, typefaces or music, or name them in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nA frame reads as this style when:\n\n- **the object is real and exact**: geometry, positions and counts come from the product's own files (STEP, KiCad, BOM, assembly guide). Nothing is eyeballed that a file can give;\n- **it is shown apart**: layers float on one axis with even, readable gaps, each still oriented as it sits in the product;\n- **it is photographed, not drawn**: physically based materials, a studio key light, a cool rim, an HDRI reflection, soft contact shadow on an invisible floor;\n- **the numbers live on the object**: thin leader lines from a dot on the part, dimension lines with ticks drawn in the scene's own perspective, never a table beside it;\n- **the camera never stops travelling**: one or a few continuous moves, orbit outside, macro inside.\n\nIt is not a glass product render (one object, material beauty, no disassembly), not a blueprint (line drawing on a grid, no light), not a keynote (slides, headline type, a stage), not an assembly manual (step numbers, arrows, hands).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- Real-time PBR (three.js `MeshPhysicalMaterial`) at 2× and downsampled, ambient occlusion, a depth of field with one aperture for wide and macro (blur follows 1/focus − 1/z, so it grows as the camera comes close), a high-threshold bloom for emitters only, a vignette.\n- Each material keeps its character: plastics with soft sheen (roughness 0.55–0.7), boards with a thin clearcoat and traces as a raised, lighter relief under the mask, metallic pads, tight highlights on machined metal, matte rubber.\n- **Boards are painted from their layout files**: copper pours, every trace, via, pad and drill, silkscreen, on both sides, at 14–20 px per millimetre, with roughness, metalness and bump maps from the same drawing. Components stand at their footprint positions with their package sizes.\n- Light: a warm key from above front, a cool rectangular rim behind that follows the camera so edges separate from the ground, a soft top light, a low HDRI for reflections; a practical fill only where the camera goes inside.\n- Background: a dark graphite seamless sweep (screen-space radial gradient), floor visible only as soft shadow.\n- Effects anchored to the world (leaders, dimension lines, glows) are projected from 3D points every frame; only subtitles and title cards live in screen space.\n\n**Where the geometry comes from.** For the user's own product, ask for its STEP / IGES / KiCad / other CAD exports, BOM and spec sheet before anything else. For a public product, look for open hardware (GitHub, GitLab, OSHWA certification list, Hackaday.io, Printables/Thingiverse sources with the original CAD) and component datasheets from the maker. CC0, public domain, CC BY and MIT are fine with credit; CERN-OHL (-P, -W, -S), CC BY-SA, GPL hardware and \"no known restrictions\" are not the same thing: name the terms to the user and ask before using them. Without files, build only from published dimensions (datasheet, spec page), say on screen or in the voice that it is a model, and show no number you could not source. Never stand in a generated image for the real part.\n\nKeep a FACTS.md in the project folder (claim, source URL, quoted wording, or the measurement method for anything measured from the CAD) and deliver it with the film.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- The product brings its own colours; the frame around it is neutral and dark, so the product is always the brightest, most saturated thing.\n- Interface ink is one warm white at two strengths (names at full, specs at about 60 %), plus **one accent** reserved for measured things: dimension lines, label indices and one matching detail on the product itself.\n- Values are ordered: ground darkest, product mid to light, labels lightest. Never put text on a busy, bright part without a dark backing.\n- Example (our demo): ground #34363a → #0d0e10, ink #f1efe9, accent #ff6b3d.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- Names: a clean sans at medium weight in the film's script (28–32 px; Noto Sans SC 500 in our demo). Specs and numbers: a monospaced face (15–22 px; JetBrains Mono) so digits line up and read as measurement. A neutral grotesque (Inter) for Latin running text.\n- Label structure is fixed: a two-digit index in the accent, the name, one spec line. Units always written (mm, MB, ×n for counts). A label carries at most one fact the voice does not say.\n- Dimension text sits on a small dark plate, follows the line's angle and is never upside down.\n- Subtitles: a centred strip at the bottom with a soft shadow and no box; if the film is bilingual, the film's language on top. Hold every cue ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s); every label stays fully in frame for CJK ÷ 4.5 + other ÷ 15 + 1.5 s.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- 24 fps on ones; everything is smooth and deterministic.\n- **Separation**: smooth start, fast middle, long settle (ease-in-out cubed); layers leave top first, 0.15–0.2 s apart; repeated parts (keys, screws) fan out in a wave ordered by distance from a corner.\n- **Closing**: bottom first, quicker (about 1 s per layer), the last layer lands with a real mechanical response (a key travels its stroke and returns; nothing bounces like rubber unless it is rubber).\n- While held apart, layers may float a millimetre or two with offset phases; during macro shots they hold perfectly still.\n- Labels draw in (anchor dot, leader growing, text sliding 10 px) and fade out before the parts move again.\n- Never moves: the floor, the light rig's relation to the camera, the label column's x position.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Orbit while layers separate | the whole, its order of assembly | a reveal; a structure overview |\n| Grazing macro dolly | touch, material, finish | an opening; a texture |\n| Dive between layers | being inside | a hidden component; how it works |\n| Slow push on one still part | the part that matters | a chip, a sensor, a seal |\n| Rise through the stack | one subsystem to the next | a transition between stops |\n| Fast pull back | return, synthesis | a closing; a before/after |\n| High three-quarter drift | scale and precision | sizes, pitch, thickness |\n\n- At key moments the subject fills at least a third of the frame height. Labels live in a column on the free side (shift the projection with a view offset rather than moving the object), subtitles in the bottom 12 %.\n- Transitions are camera moves through the object itself. No cuts to black, no slide transitions, no split screens.\n- The camera path is keyed in the product's own coordinates and interpolated without overshoot (monotone cubic), so a held macro really holds.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- Instruments: tuned mallets (marimba, vibraphone, glockenspiel, glass), a round bass, a short synthetic kick and small dry percussion. Precise and dry outside the object; sustained and airy inside it.\n- Foley is the material: hollow plastic clacks for keycaps or housings, a switch click with a low thock, dry taps for boards, inharmonic rings for small machined metal, a soft thud for the enclosure, airy swooshes for layers lifting and for camera dives, a fine pen hiss when a dimension line draws, a tiny tick per label.\n- Every separation and every landing has its sound, panned to where the part is.\n- Silence: at least two near-silences (room tone only) before the big separations or the turn back; the first sound after each is the most important hit around it.\n- Mix: voice clearly on top (music about 10 dB under it while it speaks), music bus ducked, sub-bass kept lean (20–60 Hz well under the full band), loudness −14 LUFS.\n- Voice: calm, close, unhurried, short sentences; numbers spoken as words, written as digits.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\n- **The wave lift.** Repeated parts rise in a wave across the object. *Fits content like:* battery cells; solar tiles; the drawers of a modular shelf.\n- **Inside the gap.** The camera enters the space between two lifted layers to see a hidden face. *Fits content like:* the back of a logic board; the inside of a drone arm; the underside of a sensor module.\n- **The leader that lands on the part.** A macro label whose line ends on the component. *Fits content like:* a pressure sensor in a shoe; a gasket in a waterproof case; a motor driver.\n- **Dimensions drawn on the thing.** After closing, the real outline gets dimension lines in perspective. *Fits content like:* the thickness of a laptop lid; the footprint of a router; the bore of a valve.\n- **One real motion.** A part performs its true mechanical motion once (a press, a hinge, a latch). *Fits content like:* a camera shutter; a buckle; a folding hinge.\n- **Lights on inside.** Emitters inside the product switch on while it is apart. *Fits content like:* status LEDs; a backlit display; an indicator ring.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **Invented numbers.** A size that no file gives. Fix: measure it from the CAD and say \"measured\" in the facts file, or leave it out.\n- **Mixed-up revisions.** Plates, cases and boards from different versions of a project. Fix: check hole positions and stack heights across the files (they must coincide), and pin the commit.\n- **Panelised boards.** Layout files may hold a panel with breakaway tabs. Fix: take the outline from a plate or case of the same revision, or flood-fill from an interior point.\n- **Zero-length edges.** Duplicate outline points give zero normals → NaN → black squares after bloom. Fix: clean outlines, repair zero normals, scan frames for one-frame black tiles.\n- **Overshooting camera splines.** Catmull-Rom over unevenly spaced keys flies past a macro stop. Fix: monotone interpolation per component.\n- **Labels too short.** Macro stops last about three seconds. Fix: one fact per label, opened before the camera settles; check with `readcheck`.\n- **Over-bright light sweeps.** Area lights blow plastics to white. Fix: sweeps at a fraction of the key, bloom threshold above the brightest diffuse surface.\n- **Brand marks on parts.** Chip tops and connector shells carry trademarks. Fix: plain package tops; drop logo footprints unless the product is the user's own.\n- **Crushed blacks.** Black plastics against a black ground disappear. Fix: lift them to a dark grey and let the rim light separate them.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nAll in `demo/src/` (three.js r170, the library's `core/three/post.js` and `core/lib.js`):\n\n- `parts.js`: `keycapGeometry(w, d, h)` (lofted shell with a spherical dish and a stem), `switchGeometries()`, `loft(rings)`, `shapeFrom(outline, holes, circles)` (KiCad outline to extrusion), `inset`, `hexSpacer`, `screwGeometry`.\n- `pcbtex.js`: `paintPCB(data, side, outline, box, ppm)` paints colour, roughness/metalness and bump maps from extracted layout data.\n- `comps.js`: `buildBackParts(data, group, y, materials)` places package bodies at footprint positions and returns anchors.\n- `overlay.js`: anchored labels (`label`), perspective dimension lines (`dimension`), subtitles, title card, readcheck texts, subtitle width check.\n- `main.js`: layers, explode and gap functions, the camera keys (orbit or eye/target, monotone), lights, `window.EV` for the mixer.\n- `tools/extract_pcb.py`, `kicad_geom.py`, `sexp.py`: read any `.kicad_pcb` (outlines, tracks, zones, pads, silkscreen, footprints; plain Python). `tools/step2mesh.mjs`: STEP to mesh.\n\n**Extra packages and packs**, beyond `setup.sh deps [voice] [music]`: `occt-import-js` (npm, LGPL-2.1, not in the library's package.json) to triangulate STEP files, and the studio HDRI (`sh tools/fetch.sh hdri`). Install the npm package yourself into a private `node_modules` in the project folder (as `tools/fetch_cad.sh` does in a temp folder), never into `$LIB`.\n\nMinimal example, not in the demo: read a plate file with `extract_pcb.py`, extrude `shapeFrom(outline, cutouts)` 1.5 mm as aluminium (`metalness: 1, roughness: .35`) and orbit it with three `CAM` keys.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nDecided per film: which product and revision, which layers to separate and in what order, how many stops inside and which parts deserve them, the camera route, whether to open from the whole or from a detail, the product's palette and the accent, the voice.\n\n- **Three structures**: one orbit with two stops; a \"how it works\" path following a signal or flow through the layers; two revisions taken apart side by side, each held still before a difference is named.\n- **Three openings**: a part performing its real motion in macro; the closed product turning in silence on the seamless; a single component floating alone before the rest of the product assembles around it.\n- **Three endings**: closed with its dimensions drawn on it; closing into a top view that becomes a technical drawing; apart one last time, frozen, every part labelled like a printable sheet.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, title card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\n21 Keys, Opened\n"},{"id":"S4-L15","name":"显微镜故事","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L15.jpg","markdown":"# L15 显微镜故事（Microscope Story）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 实证影像\n- 长什么样：一镜从肝组织玻片推到单个细胞核，两个圆逐像素数出 2.4 倍的蓝。\n- 适合：一层层放大，讲眼睛、神经这类感知科普很合适\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Microscope Story — Style Prompt\n\n> Real, licence-clean microscopy of a real specimen, seen as it is, and one continuous change of scale that carries the viewer from the glass slide in the hand to a single cell. Focus breathes when the magnification changes, a focal plane sweeps when the camera lands, and every scale bar, field of view and reading comes from the image's own metadata.\n> References (grammar only): Charles and Ray Eames' *Powers of Ten* (one continuous change of scale with a readout riding along); premium microscope launch films (dark bench, glass catching light, restrained marks, clean instrument sound); digital-pathology viewers' instruments (scale bar, field of view, magnification). Copy none of their layouts, interfaces, typefaces or music; never name them in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n- **The specimen is a photograph**: a real micrograph or whole-slide scan of the actual sample, CC0, public domain or CC BY. Nothing on the specimen is drawn, retouched or generated. A whole-slide image (WSI) is the ideal source: one sample, one registered pyramid from millimetres to microns, with the pixel size in its metadata.\n- **Scale is the camera**: one log-space dive (and, when the story wants it, the climb back) through nested plates of the same sample. The scale bar shortens and jumps in a 1-2-5 sequence, the field-of-view readout rolls, the magnification steps.\n- **Focus has a grammar**: a rack-in from soft at the start, a short breath (blur + a hint of field change) each time the magnification changes, a focal-plane sweep across the frame when the camera lands.\n- **Readings are measured**: every number is computed from the file (pixel size × pixels) and the screen says how it was measured. Describe what was measured; never diagnose. Keep a `FACTS.md` in the project folder (claim, source URL, quoted wording, or the measurement method) and deliver it with the film.\n- **One reading per screen**, in the single accent colour.\n\nNot Satellite Briefing (no grey grade; the stain keeps its colour), not Sci-fi HUD (no glowing wireframes or crosshairs), not Data Storytelling (no chart paper), not a keynote (no slides or header bars).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Plates**: the WSI pyramid levels as plates in one world frame (level-0 pixels), the coarse levels whole and alpha-feathered at the scan edge, the fine levels as crops around where the camera lands. Each frame draws the finest plate that is downsampled no more than ~1.45×, and blends the next level in between 2.2× and 1.45×: no seams and no visible upsampling, because every level is the same sample, already registered.\n- **The naked-eye plate**: the scanner's own photo of the whole slide (many WSI files carry one), registered through the scan's area-of-interest metadata and checked against the tissue's centroid. Cut the glass out of the scanner tray, remove the line-sensor streaks, and fill any manufacturer's printed logo with the surrounding frosted glass. Below ~30 mm of field the photo would be upsampled: let the cover glass become clean glass (its dust leaves the focal plane).\n- **One grade for every plate**: glass normalised to a warm white, a little density and chroma in the stain, nothing else. No false colour, no film emulation.\n- **The bench**: graphite under a slow pool of light; a soft shadow under the slide.\n- **Focus**: screen-space Gaussian blur on a frame rendered with a margin (so edges never go dark); the focal sweep is a tilted sharp front crossing a softened frame. A light illumination vignette, nothing more.\n- **Marks** in screen space but anchored to the specimen through one world → screen function: hairlines 1.5–2.5 px with a dark or light halo, circles drawn on as arcs with cardinal ticks, dimension lines with end ticks, leader lines with one elbow. Counted pixels are shown as a mask revealed behind a scan front, then kept as a faint trace.\n- **Frosted panels** (dark translucent glass over a blurred copy of the frame) for readings, instruments, subtitles and the credit: white type reads on glass and stain alike, nothing switches colour mid-shot.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- Glass: warm paper white. Bench: graphite. The stain is evidence and keeps its true colour (e.g. trichrome red and blue, H&E pink and purple).\n- One accent, cool and away from the stain's hues (mint against red and blue; amber against a green or purple stain), only for the measured reading and the counted pixels.\n- Ink on glass is near-black; type on stain sits on a frosted panel in warm white.\n\nExample (our demo): glass #F4F1EC, bench #0B0D0F → #2A3036, ink #181B1F, panel rgba(12,14,17,0.74), accent mint rgb(120,240,205) (on stain) / rgb(0,122,99) (on glass).\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Roles**: a light condensed grotesk for readings and the scale bar (40–72 px, e.g. Barlow Condensed); a monospace for instruments and pixel counts (20 px, e.g. IBM Plex Mono); a sans in the film's language for labels (20–34 px, e.g. Noto Sans SC); a serif only for the title. OFL, self-hosted, CJK subset.\n- **Instruments** live in one frosted panel in a corner: scale bar with its value, field of view, magnification with a small nosepiece glyph that turns a quarter on every change. The source credit in a small panel in the opposite corner.\n- **Readings** sit in a frosted panel beside what they measure, joined by a leader: a small label above (\"collagen share of tissue area\"), the number large in the accent, the method or the raw counts below.\n- **Subtitles**: a frosted strip at the bottom; if the film is bilingual, one line per language with the film's language on top, never wrapped. Measure each line with the real font and split long lines into two cues.\n- Nothing below 20 px at 1080p. Hold labels ≥ characters ÷ 4.5 + 1.5 s (CJK); a reading that rolls is reported by its landing value.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- The camera is a similarity transform (zoom, roll, position) interpolated as a log spiral between keys (M^u about the fixed point of the two frames), so every push is a true dive towards one point and the roll is part of it. Ease the moves, creep 2–5 % during holds; never a frozen frame longer than a second.\n- Magnification changes happen where the plates actually change, each with a breath of focus, a turret click and the new magnification said once, large.\n- Marks arrive by being made: a dimension line grows from its centre, a circle is drawn as an arc, a scan front counts pixels while the reading rolls, the reading lands with a small spring.\n- Never moves: the specimen under its marks, the instrument positions.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| The dive (log zoom with roll) | going to look for yourself | an opening; from the slide to the cell |\n| Locked frame, slow creep | evidence, a measurement | a reading; a comparison |\n| Truck at one magnification | a second sample of the same thing | the counter-example; \"meanwhile, nearby\" |\n| Pull back to frame two marks | comparison | the verdict |\n| Focal-plane sweep | arriving; depth | a landing; a reveal inside one frame |\n| The climb (reverse dive, scale bar jumping back) | consequence, scale | an ending; a return to the eye's scale |\n\nAt key moments the measured structure fills a third of the frame height or more; instruments in a corner, subtitles bottom centre, readings beside their marks. Transitions are scale changes, trucks and focus, never cuts or crossfades between samples.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Instrument foley**: the objective turret (a click, a detent, a small metal ring), a focus servo, pen on glass for each line drawn, a band-passed scan travelling left to right with counter ticks that speed up, a soft filtered sweep for the focal plane.\n- **Music**: hard-mallet vibraphone in 8ths and 16ths, marimba ticks, bowed vibraphone and water glasses for pads and the motif, glockenspiel for each reading that lands, a pulse bass with 2nd/3rd harmonics, timpani and bass drum under the landings. Harmony stays open and light; the score is the user's to shape.\n- **Beds**: a quiet lab room (fan, a little mains hum).\n- **Silence** before the verdict and before the climb; the first sound after it is the reading or the turn.\n- **Mix**: voice ~10 dB over music, music ducked under it; −14 LUFS. Voice: a calm, exact narrator; numbers spelled out for the TTS, digits on screen.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\n- **The dive.** From the slide in the hand to a cell in one camera, the turret clicking at each real change of level. *Fits content like:* a biopsy to its cells; a coated surface to its crystals; a hair or skin section to its layers.\n- **Counting the colour.** Draw a circle, let a scan front cross it, light up every counted pixel and roll the share up to its landing value; show the raw counts underneath. *Fits content like:* collagen in tissue; porosity in a ceramic; coverage of a coating; pigment in a sample.\n- **Two circles, one verdict.** Measure one place, truck to another at the same scale, then frame both and say what differs. *Fits content like:* treated against untreated; edge against centre; before against after.\n- **The focal sweep and the ring.** Land, sweep the focal plane across the frame, ring one structure and give its size. *Fits content like:* a nucleus; a grain; a fibre's diameter.\n- **The climb back.** Pull out to the eye's scale with the scale bar jumping backwards; a title card written on the glass.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **Licences**: most microscopy on the web is CC BY-NC or all rights reserved. Usable: CC0 whole-slide scans (e.g. the OpenSlide test data: check each file's licence line), public-domain government micrographs (NIH, CDC PHIL, NASA, USGS), CC BY collections (Cell Image Library, Wellcome Collection), with every file listed in `CREDITS`. Not usable: CC BY-SA without agreement, NC images, journal figures, AI images.\n- **Finding material for a new topic**: search by organ, stain or material plus \"whole slide\" in the OpenSlide test data, NCI Imaging Data Commons (TCGA/CPTAC slides, licence per collection), the Cell Image Library, Wellcome Collection, Wikimedia Commons (filter by licence) and NIH/CDC galleries; prefer a file whose metadata carries its pixel size. CC BY-SA, ODbL or \"no known restrictions\" are not CC0: name the terms to the user and ask before using them. For a product, a lab or a brand, ask the user for their own scans (SVS/NDPI/MRXS, SEM TIFFs with a scale) first. Never stand in a generated image for a real specimen.\n- **Different samples never zoom into each other**: two micrographs of \"the same thing\" at two scales are two specimens. Use one pyramid; if you must join sources, cut on a focus breath and say so.\n- **Pixel size**: read it from the file (XMP `ScanRes`, TIFF resolution, OME `PhysicalSizeX`); a slide-photo's resolution tag can be trusted only after checking against the scan.\n- **Upsampling** shows as mush beyond ~1.6×: end the dive at the scan's real resolution.\n- **Tile seams**: some scanners overlap tiles; check the joint info, frame out visible seams.\n- **Labels and logos** on the slide: barcodes and IDs are part of the object; manufacturer logos are not — fill or crop them.\n- **Claims**: a stain's colour means what the stain protocol says, nothing more; measured shares are of the drawn region and the stated threshold, never a clinical score.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\n- `demo/film.js`: log-spiral camera (`camAt`), plate selection by downsampling ratio and coverage, objective events from real level changes, focus, frosted `panel()`, marks, readings, `TEXTS` / `SUBCHECK` / `EV`.\n- `demo/tools/plates.py`: slide-photo registration and cleaning, graded plates, collagen mask and the scan's cumulative curve, the tissue axis. `nucleus.py`: membrane edge on 72 rays, least-squares circle.\n- **Extra packages and packs** (beyond `setup.sh deps voice`): `tifffile` + `imagecodecs` read TIFF-based slides (Ventana BIF, Aperio SVS, Hamamatsu NDPI, generic pyramidal TIFF; `demo/tools/plates.py` is written for BIF: its label page, XMP and AOI); MRXS, SCN, VMS and others need `openslide-python` with the OpenSlide library (`brew install openslide`), or an adapter of your own. Install them yourself into a private venv in the project folder, not into `$LIB/.venv`.\n- Minimal example, not in the demo: a coated steel sample: one SEM pyramid, a dive to one crack, a circle counting coverage, a ring around one grain.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nThe agent decides the specimen, the stain or modality, what is counted, the camera path, the accent, the ending.\n\n- **Structures**: a single dive ending in one measurement; a comparison of two regions or two samples at one scale; a climb that starts on a cell and reveals the object.\n- **Openings**: the slide on the bench under moving light; a cell already in focus, the climb revealing what it belongs to; a blurred field racking into focus.\n- **Endings**: the title written on the glass; a last reading held in silence; the slide lifted out of frame.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, title card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nMeasured Blue\n"},{"id":"S4-L16","name":"档案灯箱","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L16.jpg","markdown":"# L16 档案灯箱（Archive Light Table）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 实证影像\n- 长什么样：电报说最长飞了 57 秒；放大镜下，莱特兄弟的底片和档案说是 59 秒。\n- 适合：翻旧资料、回忆\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Archive Light Table — Style Prompt\n\n> Real archive objects (glass negatives, slides, letters, telegrams, clippings, drawings) lie on a cool, backlit light table. A loupe comes down on one of them, and inside the glass the object comes alive: a negative turns positive, a line of type lights up, a number lifts off the page, a photograph opens into depth. Precise catalogue numbers, dates and readings are pinned to the objects. Clean, modern and quiet, with no vintage filter.\n> References (grammar only): archive documentaries that push into still photographs; visual investigations that annotate real imagery (one line, one reading, one source per screen); product macro films (glass edges, transmitted light). Copy none of their layouts, typefaces, shots or music, and never name them in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n- **The objects are real**: scans of public-domain or licensed archive items, at their true proportions, with their real damage (broken corners, emulsion edges, folds, pencil marks). Nothing in them is drawn or generated.\n- **Light comes from below**: the table glows a near-white cool grey; film and glass transmit it, paper glows warm at its edges, opaque things read darker than the table.\n- **The loupe is the camera's door**: every dive into an object goes through a loupe that lands on it. The inside of the lens is where the object changes state.\n- **Catalogue truth**: every object carries its real identifier, date and title from its holder; numbers on screen come from the record.\n- **One signal colour** for marks, readings and anchors; everything else is the archive's own greys and paper tones.\n\n**Sources and licences.** Open collections with masters and catalogue records: the Library of Congress (files on `tile.loc.gov`; records from its SRU server `lx2.loc.gov:210/lcdb` when loc.gov pages are blocked), Smithsonian Open Access (CC0), the Rijksmuseum (CC0), NARA, Europeana (check each item's rights statement). CC0, public domain and CC BY (credited) are fine. \"No known restrictions\", CC BY-SA and other terms are not CC0: name the terms to the user and ask. For brand history, ask the user for their scans, archive numbers and right to use them. Never stand a generated image in for an archive object. Keep a `FACTS.md` in the project folder (claim, source URL, quoted wording, or how a figure was derived) and deliver it with the film.\n\nNot Halftone Dossier (no newsprint dots, no aged-paper grade), not Satellite Briefing (objects on a table, not the ground from orbit), not Data Storytelling (no charts; the numbers live on the objects), not a keynote (no slides or header bars).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **One world, one camera**: every object is a textured quad on the table with a centre, rotation and scale (texture px → world units at true size). The camera is a 2D centre, zoom and roll; the table and every mark go through the same world → screen function.\n- **The light table**: an emissive surface (≈ 0.97 white, slightly cool), soft falloff, diffuser noise, an aluminium frame and a dark desk beyond. It can be switched off (ambient ≈ 0.04) and flicker on.\n- **Glass negatives**: transmission from the scan's positive, `T = 0.035 + 0.9 · (1 − pos)^1.25`, with a warm silver-gelatin tint; the emulsion mask comes from the scan (white margin touching the border = no glass), so broken corners show the table. Dust and scratches are procedural, anchored to the table.\n- **Paper**: backlit, its colour is transmission (paper tone × light); lit lines get darker ink and brighter paper; unlit text can dim to bring one line forward.\n- **The loupe**: inside the lens each pixel maps back to the table through the magnification (≈ 2.5×) with a pincushion term (k ≈ 0.16 at the rim), slightly different per RGB channel for colour fringes. Around it: a black ring with a bevel highlight and a clear acrylic skirt that refracts the table and glows at its foot. Landing and lifting, it scales by about 1.35× and casts a soft shadow.\n- **The photograph space**: after the push the lens stops being a lens. A photo gains depth from a ground-plane nearness map (zero at the horizon, linear in the row below it), cut-out layers for upright subjects and a filled background; a dolly and a lateral shift drive parallax, and a subject layer may drift. Things left on the ground plane lean: centre the dolly between them.\n- **Sampling**: all texture reads inside the branching lens code use explicit gradients (`textureGrad`), otherwise quads that cross a branch pick a wrong mip level and leave dashed seams.\n- **Marks** live on a 2D canvas above the picture: hairlines 1.5–3 px, rings, crop corners, underlines, leaders. Table marks are clipped out under the loupe; marks inside a photograph use its forward mapping, parallax included.\n- **Post**: a light vignette and fine moving grain (heavier inside photographs). No film damage, no sepia, no halftone.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- Neutral ground: the light table (near-white, cool) and the archive's own tones (silver greys, paper tans). Do not regrade the archive into a colour scheme.\n- **One signal colour** (e.g. a signal orange) for marks, readings, anchors and the verdict; never as a fill behind pictures, never for decoration.\n- Text is ink (near-black) on light areas and paper-white on dark caption plates; choose the contrast by the ground, never colour on colour.\n- Positives inside the lens may be gently levelled (black and white points) so a weak scan reads; negatives stay as the glass gives them.\n\nExample (our demo): table #F7F9FB, ink #15171A, signal #FF5A1F, caption plates rgba(14,15,17,0.74).\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Roles**: a monospace for identifiers, dates and readings (e.g. IBM Plex Mono, 17–34 px; readings 74–120 px); a sans for labels in the film's language (e.g. Noto Sans SC or Inter, 21–36 px); a serif only for titles and the one name a photograph carries (60–76 px). OFL, self-hosted, CJK subset when needed.\n- **Labels attach to objects**: an anchor dot and ring, a vertical leader, then the text. A reading sits in a dark caption plate with a small label above and the source (the catalogue number or the original wording) below.\n- **Subtitles** are a caption strip at the bottom centre, on a dark plate with a 3 px signal-coloured edge. If the film is bilingual, the film's language goes on top. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- **Quote the source when you can**: a crop of the actual line, or the record's wording in quotation marks, beats a paraphrase.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- 24 fps on ones; every camera parameter on smooth monotone curves through keys (no overshoot, no stepping).\n- The camera glides across the table, slows to let the loupe land, then pushes. Speed lives in the glides and pushes; holds are for reading.\n- The loupe lands with a fast ease-out (≈ 0.4 s), lifts the same way. Lines draw on in 0.3–0.6 s; text wipes in from its leader side; numbers count up and land with the voice.\n- What never moves: the archive's own content outside a photograph space, and the table itself.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| table glide (pan and roll) | surveying; moving to the next piece of evidence | a timeline of documents; a product family; an album |\n| loupe landing + push into the lens | choosing one object; \"look closer\" | the claim; the key photograph; a signature |\n| inside the lens: tracking along a line | reading | a letter, a ledger line, a caption, a spec |\n| photograph space: dolly + parallax | being there | the event itself; the founding moment; the place |\n| rise out of the lens | stepping back, the evidence noted | a transition; a summary |\n| pull back to the whole table | scale; everything at once | an index, a timeline, a closing |\n\nFraming: at a reading moment the subject fills at least a third of the frame height; readings and labels stay clear of the subtitle strip (bottom 140 px). Transitions are made by the loupe (land, push, rise, lift) and the glide; no cuts, fades to black or page turns between objects.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Ambience**: the light table's ballast hum (100 Hz and harmonics), starter clicks, room tone. Inside a photograph the hum gives way to that place's air.\n- **Foley**: the loupe on glass (a clack with a high ring), paper sweeps for glides and lit lines, ticks for scales and counters, a pen stroke for a verdict, soft clicks as labels land.\n- **Music**: restrained and small: a felt piano, a few strings, glass or bowed metal for the moments of seeing. Build by subdivision and register, not by drums. Leave real silences before a reveal or a verdict; the first sound after them should be the loupe, the place or the verdict.\n- **Voice**: a calm narrator, short sentences that state what the object says. Music about 10 dB under the voice, ducked while it speaks; −14 LUFS.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\n- **Negative to positive under the loupe.** A develop front spreads from the lens centre with a thin warm edge, turning the glass negative into its positive only inside the lens. *Fits content like:* a founder's portrait plate; a factory on a lantern slide; an X-ray film becoming the product inside.\n- **The line lights up.** On paper inside the lens one line of type brightens while the rest dims, an underline draws under it, and one word or number lifts out into a reading card. *Fits content like:* a ledger entry; a patent claim; the first order on an invoice.\n- **Into the photograph.** The lens rim grows past the frame while the view inside widens; the photograph gains layered depth and a subject drifts. *Fits content like:* the first shop front; a launch day crowd; an expedition camp.\n- **Measuring on the photograph.** A ruler laid on the ground plane in perspective, with ticks that crowd toward the horizon and a counter that lands on the recorded figure, marked as a schematic when it is one. *Fits content like:* a race distance; a bridge span; a building's height.\n- **Two records, held side by side.** Two caption cards, each with a crop of its source, held still until both are read before one is struck. *Fits content like:* a misprinted date; two versions of a founding story; specification against delivery.\n- **The table becomes the index.** Pull back to the whole table; each object's tag flies into one list with its identifier and its reading. *Fits content like:* a company timeline; a collection catalogue; a product family.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **Over-magnifying a weak scan**: keep the lens interior under about 1.5 screen px per scan px; choose items by scan size.\n- **The loupe sweeping over empty table**: lay objects close together, land the loupe straight onto the object (drop in, don't slide in), and glide the camera over objects, not gaps.\n- **Shear in the photograph space**: one depth map for a photo with people or machines in it tears them apart. Cut upright subjects into layers, fill behind them, and keep 2.5D moves small enough that the fill is barely seen.\n- **Labels that scroll away**: a label on a moving table leaves the frame before it is read; hold the camera, or fix the caption on screen.\n- **Vintage creep**: no sepia, no halftone, no paper-ageing overlay. The age is in the objects, not the grade.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\n- `demo/film.js`: `IT` (objects: centre, rotation, scale), `toW()` / `toT()` (texture ↔ world), `CAM` keys and `w2s()`, `dive()` (loupe point, land/lift times, lens interior as tracks), `shot(t)`, `int2s()` (photograph → screen for marks), the shader (`lightTable`, `paper`, `negative`, `parallax`, rim and skirt), the overlay (`annot`, `measure`, `compare`, `indexPanel`).\n- `demo/tools/prep.py`: emulsion masks from scans, difference matting of a subject against a smooth sky, ground-only cuts, push-pull fill.\n- Minimal example, not in the demo: put one 35 mm slide (`IT.slide = {c, r, k, size}`), a loupe landing with `dive(toW(IT.slide, p), t0, t1, keysS, keysC)`, and light one line with `band()`.\n- **Extra packages and packs**: none beyond `setup.sh deps voice music` (numpy, scipy, Pillow, soxr are in the core tier). A score in this palette needs sample libraries: `sh tools/fetch.sh instruments salamander` (piano), `vsco2ce` (strings), `vcsl` (glass, bowed vibraphone). Anything else a film needs goes into a private venv or `node_modules` in the project folder, never into `$LIB/.venv`.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nThe agent decides the objects, their order, the story, which native moves to spend and where, the camera path, the subject of the photograph space, and the signal colour.\n\n- **Three structures**: a company's founding told as five objects in date order, each dive revealing one person; a single photograph explored in three dives (the place, the people, the date on the back); a before/after pair of plans and photographs of one building.\n- **Three openings**: the table switching on in the dark; a loupe already on a plate, rising to reveal the table; a hand-written date on an envelope filling the frame.\n- **Three endings**: the index of everything on the table; the last photograph held in depth with one line of text; the light table switching off, leaving one object glowing.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nTwo Seconds Short\n"},{"id":"S4-L17","name":"蜡笔儿童绘本","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L17.jpg","markdown":"# L17 蜡笔儿童绘本（Crayon Picture Book）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 手绘与绘画\n- 长什么样：月亮失眠了，小女孩爬上屋顶给它唱摇篮曲。\n- 适合：温暖、童年、回忆\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Crayon Picture Book — Style Prompt\n\n> A picture-book page drawn live in wax crayon on toothy paper, with a watercolour wash that reveals what the crayon was hiding.\n> References (grammar only): Raymond Briggs' *The Snowman* (1982) (hand-textured frames that breathe, wordless tenderness, a lyrical passage carried by one tune, still backgrounds with living figures). Copy none of its characters, song or imagery, and never name it in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nA children's picture book page **being drawn while you watch**: wax crayon on warm off-white paper, a limited box of crayons, child-logic perspective, colour that spills past the outline, and every line gently **boiling** because each frame is a fresh drawing. The tone is picture-book storytelling: tender, unhurried, music-led, a few lines of narration that are literally the words printed on the page. Any topic becomes **one page of a picture book that gets finished**: drawing and colouring are the plot.\n\nIt is not \"cute vector art with a crayon filter\": the look comes from **material physics** (wax sticks only to the peaks of the paper tooth, pressure decides how much of the tooth it reaches, watercolour is repelled by wax). Not a whiteboard explainer (no marker, no hand), not a watercolour painting (the wash is a guest, the crayon the host), not a chalkboard.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Paper**: warm off-white (around `#f4efe3`). The tooth is **anchored to the page** (it pans with the camera; on zoom two octaves cross-fade so grains stay ~1 px on screen), never screen-fixed: three octaves of value noise plus a horizontal fibre term. A final pass embosses it (lit top-left), less where wax filled the tooth. Low mottling ±2 %, warm vignette.\n- **Wax adhesion (the core rule)**: every crayon layer is a canvas where RGB = colour and **A = pressure**; the shader deposits wax where `tooth > 1 − k·pressure` (soft edge). Light pressure = speckled peaks; heavy = almost solid, slightly darker.\n- **Strokes**: ribbons with a low-frequency hand wobble (1–2 px), width ±15 %, tapered ends, a denser core and 1–2 thin streaks. Characters and buildings get the heaviest outline; refined \"adult\" objects about half.\n- **Colouring**: back-and-forth zig-zag hatching with a visible gap, each pass at a slightly different pressure; **overshoot the outline by a few px**; a lighter cross-hatch pass on big areas.\n- **Watercolour wash**: a separate density canvas multiplied over the page. Pigment pools at edges (density minus blurred density), granulates in the deepest pits, blooms with noise, and is **resisted by wax** (density × (1 − ~0.9·wax coverage)). Brush strokes are wide bands with bristle streaks and a darker wet leading edge while moving.\n- **Occlusion**: a front part erases what is behind it inside its outline; characters \"keep the paper white\" behind them (a knock-out that restores paper and resets wax), the way a child draws the figure first and colours around it.\n- **Characters**: a skilled adult imitating a child: round head (≈2.5 heads tall), dot eyes with a white-wax catchlight, pink cheeks, tube limbs, mitten hands, bold hair shapes; objects with faces get big eyes and eyelids. **Child perspective**: gable front + slanted side wall; people bigger than doors.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **A box of about a dozen crayons, never more.** Outlines in one dark colour (indigo, dark brown, plum), **never black**. One white crayon reserved for wax resist.\n- **At most one watercolour** per film, chosen for what it reveals (ultramarine for night, rose for dawn, sap green for rain). It never paints the characters.\n- Saturated crayon on lots of bare paper; value comes from pressure, not extra hues. One accent on a character may echo the story.\n- Example boxes: *seaside* — navy line, coral, sand, teal, turquoise, lemon, white; *orchard* — brown line, russet, apple red, mustard, olive, plum, cream, white.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- Subtitles are **the book's printed words**: a handwriting face (Patrick Hand, OFL), 50–56 px, in the outline colour, bottom-centre, each glyph tilted and offset, boiling on twos, written left→right in ~0.35 s, on a ragged knock-out patch of blank paper (never a dark box). Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s); none during wordless music.\n- Titles are hand-lettered on the page (Gaegu Bold or similar), written letter by letter. You can't fade crayon: a title leaves by the camera moving off it, a page turn or being coloured over.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Everything drawn steps at 12 fps** (lines, colouring, characters, boil seed); camera, the wash's leading edge and twinkles run at 24 fps.\n- **Boil**: each stroke re-jitters offset (<1 px), wobble and hatching rows every 2 frames. Characters full, background lines ~0.6, background colouring still (it also halves the bitrate). Calm the boil to about half when the film goes quiet.\n- **Drawing on**: lines reveal along their length in 0.4–0.9 s; colouring row by row; text letter by letter.\n- **Acting**: substitution, a new drawing per pose on the beat; two key drawings alternate for a repeated action. Big simple poses; a child's drawing doesn't in-between.\n- **Colouring is an action**: being coloured in *is* doing (a coat scribbled on = putting it on; pink scribble = blushing; a wash = night falling).\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nThe camera moves over a large page. A vocabulary, not a route; every move needs a reason. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Locked full page | Paper before drawing; the whole picture | a beginning from nothing; a tableau; a reveal that needs the page |\n| Push toward a character | \"This one\" | a title giving way; a feeling arriving |\n| Locked medium close-up | Substitution poses act | comedy; a decision; a small failure |\n| Tilt along a gaze | We see what they see | discovery; longing; something huge |\n| Tight follow | The world draws itself ahead of the subject | a brave act; a path; building |\n| Held wide while the brush moves | The material is the motion | a wash reveal; weather; day or night falling |\n| Slow truck across the page | Reading left to right | a list; a sequence; a family |\n| Pull out past the page edge | The page is an object | a book on a table; a fridge drawing |\n| Page turn | New chapter, time jump | before/after; the next day |\n\nKey figures ≥ 1/4 of frame height in close shots; keep bare paper at the bottom for the printed words. Transitions: drawing on, colouring over, the wash, a pan or a page turn; never a dissolve.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Small wooden and metal-toothed instruments, no strings or grand piano**: ukulele or nylon guitar, clarinet or bassoon (bass, humour), music box (can wind down with a pitch droop), glockenspiel or celesta (a note per uncovered sparkle), toy piano (one note per counted thing), flute or recorder as the singing voice, shaker and woodblock. A simple lilt; one theme can be held back and heard in full once.\n- **Silence is a beat**: a full beat of nothing after a failure; a short breath before a wordless passage.\n- **Foley follows the material**: crayon = stick-slip band-passed noise whose grain follows stroke speed (colouring = rhythmic swishes); wet brush = soft low-passed noise with bristle grain, panned with the stroke; felt puffs, fabric swishes, a sharpener, a crayon set down, a page turn (lift / whoosh / flap). Ambience: faint crickets or birds, room tone, a distant clock.\n- **Mix**: music under narration, rising for wordless passages; metal voices get a gentle high lift. −14 LUFS.\n- **Voice**: a soft, warm storyteller, short lines with breaths. Compress before EQ, cut the highs, de-ess, a warm near-field reverb far under the dry voice; a few dB over the bed, never louder. Whisper-check every line, dry and on the final mix. Avoid famous-book phrases.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Wax resist.** White crayon is invisible until a wash passes; hide the payoff in plain sight from the first frame. *Fits content like:* a farewell message; a product's hidden feature; roots under a garden.\n- **Drawn live.** The world draws itself just ahead of the hero. *Fits content like:* a bridge plank by plank; a journey map; a company timeline.\n- **Colouring is an action.** *Fits content like:* a town lighting up window by window; a team putting on kit; fruit ripening.\n- **Boil.** A held frame is alive; calming it quiets the film. *Fits content like:* a pause for thought; grief; rest.\n- **Child logic.** Wrong perspective, faces on objects, numbers in the sky. *Fits content like:* a launch countdown; a talking house on insulation; a sun that clocks out.\n- **It is a book.** Pull back to the object; turn the page. *Fits content like:* a year in review; a \"chapter two\"; a thank-you card.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Gaps between wash bands leave white stripes → overlap bands and cover all intended paper.\n- Wavy band edges and wide light streaks read as hills → small high-frequency waviness, thin faint streaks (α ≤ 0.12).\n- Light-pressure colouring goes muddy under a wash → pressure ≥ 0.8 or cross-hatch wherever the wash crosses.\n- Text where the wash passes survives it (wax resists) → place it elsewhere or accept it.\n- Characters vanish under a wash → composite them *after* the wash.\n- Outlines over hair read as a headband → outline hair masses on their outer edge only.\n- Knock-out drawn into a plain layer shows as grey crayon → use a dummy canvas.\n- Page turn from rectangle strips shows a staircase → an affine transform per strip.\n- Double vignette/emboss at a page-to-object transition → page with vignette 0, ramp the outer scene's in.\n- Screen-fixed tooth bloats the file → anchor it, don't boil background fills, no ffmpeg grain.\n- Reverb IR from unit-variance noise is hugely loud → scale wet to ~18 dB under dry.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `gl.js` (WebGL2 compositor: paper, wax adhesion, knock-out, watercolour + resist, emboss/vignette/lamp), `crayon.js` (`line` ribbons with taper, streaks, boil; `fill` hatching with overshoot; `text`; shapes), `rig.js` (occlusion + knock-out parts, limb tubes), `sheet.js` (model sheets, `?test=model&page=…`), `end.js` (page on a desk, page turn). Draw your own character's model sheet with them before any shot. File map and commands: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md#build-notes).\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the structure, the characters (or none), the page, the opening, the ending, the camera path, the pacing, the crayon box and whether there is a wash. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a colouring-book page** (all outlines there from frame one; each idea colours one area); **a flip through several pages** (one stage per page, joined by turns); **two children, one page** (two crayon colours argue, then combine).\n- Openings: **a finished page rubbed out** to begin again; **a crayon tip in extreme close-up** mid-stroke, pulling back; **a crumpled page being flattened**.\n- Endings: **the page taped to a fridge** among others; **a last scribble** filling the frame with one colour; **a stray splash of water** finding the white-wax message.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nThe Moon Can't Sleep\n"},{"id":"S4-L18","name":"水彩笔刷","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L18.jpg","markdown":"# L18 水彩笔刷（Watercolor Brush）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 手绘与绘画\n- 长什么样：跟着降雨从澳洲红色腹地一路画到绿色海岸，一镜到底。\n- 适合：情绪段，温柔\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Watercolor Brush — Style Prompt\n\n> A hand-painted journal page that paints itself: translucent brush strokes with dry-brush flying white on warm cotton paper, hand-written notes beside what is painted, nothing drawn with a vector line.\n> References (grammar only): naturalist field-journal and botanical-plate watercolours (specimen + note); long horizontal handscroll panoramas (one continuous painted world); the textbook \"transect\" diagram (a gradient read across space). Borrow the grammar, never a specific artwork, character or layout.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n- **One sheet of warm, fibrous paper** under everything; it is also the eraser (paper-coloured mist washes things away).\n- **Everything is individual brush strokes**: a variable-width ribbon at partial opacity plus a few thin **bristle tracks** that break into dashes (flying white). Strokes overlap and darken where they cross, like transparent pigment. No vector outlines; a darker edge stroke along a form is the only \"line\".\n- **Things paint themselves in**, stroke by stroke, structure before detail (trunk → branches → leaves; outline wash → shadow → accent).\n- **An annotation layer** in a hand the painter would write with: labels with pen leader lines, a small measured value, a quiet title.\n- Tone: calm, curious, precise. Numbers are real; the palette can carry data.\n\nNot ink wash (colour, not ink tones; no calligraphic brush-as-gesture), not impasto (no thick paint, no knife), not a children's picture book (no crayon, no cute outlines), not vector illustration with a watercolour texture laid on top.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Paper**: a pre-generated image (warm base, three octaves of smooth noise ±3.5 %, a few thousand short curved fibres, fine grain, light vignette), drawn first every frame.\n- **Stroke** (`engine.js` `mk`/`drawS`): a ribbon whose width follows a profile (`brush`, `leaf`, `even`, `tip`, `trunk`) with ~±22 % value-noise roughness, filled as a thin wash (≈ 0.55–0.6 body opacity when bristles are present), plus 3–9 bristle tracks at 30–85 % opacity, each with a random dash pattern and a random early end: that is the flying white. A stroke can be painted up to any fraction of its length (a moving brush tip).\n- **Wash**: the same polygon painted in 3 passes with jittered vertices at a third of the opacity each, so edges darken where passes disagree (a pooled watercolour edge).\n- **Depth by layers**: several parallax layers of washes and painted objects; farther = smaller, lighter, less saturated; paper-coloured mist bands between layers.\n- **Cache finished objects as sprites**; draw stroke by stroke only what is being painted or transformed.\n- **Everything is a pure function of `t`**; any stroke made inside `render(t)` is built under a fixed seed.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **Paper is the white.** Never paint pure white; light is unpainted paper.\n- **Transparent layering**: darker = more passes, not a darker pigment on top. Keep 3–5 hues per scene plus ink.\n- **Ink** (a warm near-black and a lighter brown) for lines, text, edges and stems only.\n- **Colour may carry the data or the mood**: blend sky, ground and background by position or time so the palette itself changes with the subject. Map ramps quantised into 4–6 bands read as paint, continuous ramps read as software.\n- **One accent** (a vermilion, a rain blue) reserved for one element that recurs: a stamp, a marker, a key object.\n- Examples: arid-to-wet — red ochre → sage → straw → blue-grey → deep green; a harbour at dawn — `#f3e2c8` sky, `#9fb4c4` water, `#5f7486` hulls, accent `#c8442a` buoys; a vegetable garden — `#e8e0c0` soil light, `#8ea860` leaves, `#4e6e3c` shade, accent `#d9632e` pumpkins.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- An elegant serif (e.g. Cormorant Garamond) for titles and subtitles, a handwriting face (e.g. Caveat) for notes, a small mono (e.g. IBM Plex Mono, wide tracking) for measured labels, Noto Serif SC for Chinese (subset to the characters used).\n- Hand notes are drawn twice over a paper-coloured halo, revealed left to right like writing, with a pen leader line that grows to its specimen.\n- **Subtitles** sit on the paper, not in a box: centred at the bottom, serif italic ~36 px (2 balanced lines max), a second-language line beneath at ~2/3 size, ink at ~78 % over a paper-coloured halo. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- Numbers are spelled out in the TTS text and written as digits on screen.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Painting-in is the main motion**: an object's progress is a function of where it sits on screen or of a cue; strokes in a list each take a slice of that progress, so structure appears first.\n- **Sway** only after an object is finished: a small horizontal skew (`sin(1.15t+φ) + .4 sin(2.7t+2φ)`), ~0.006 for trees, 0.01–0.03 for grass.\n- **Brush-edge wipes**: a wavy vertical clip reveals a whole scene; lines grow by stroke progress; labels open with a left-to-right clip.\n- **Mist** erases: paper-coloured layers with a wavy rising edge.\n- Animated creatures and events may be phased to the music's beat grid so landings hit the beat.\n- Smooth easing (`ss`, `eio`, `eo`, `ei`); 60 fps suits the slow, fluid brush; nothing jitters.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. The picture is one painted sheet; cutting is rare. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Long lateral dolly over a continuous world | a gradient; a journey; cause and consequence | a transect; a route; a timeline |\n| Vertical tilt along one object | scale; measurement | a tall tree; a building; a depth |\n| Locked sheet, things painted in | study; attention | a specimen plate; a recipe; a diagram |\n| Slow push into a detail | the observer leaning in | a seed; an insect; a hand-written note |\n| Pull-back to a map or diagram | the whole re-framed | where it all happened; a system |\n| Mist wash to blank paper | chapter end; time passing | a season; forgetting; a new place |\n| Page turn / new sheet slides over | a new entry in the journal | a new day; a new species; a new case |\n| Change in time on a locked view | growth; decay; history | a season cycle; before/after; deep time |\n\nFraming: generous paper margins; one hero subject per view with its note; labels never cover the subject. Transitions: brush-edge wipes, mist, a page turn, or a continuous camera move; no hard video cuts, no dissolves between unrelated pictures.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Music**: acoustic and gentle: felt piano, fingerpicked guitar, woodwinds, light strings, glockenspiel, kalimba, soft pads. A licensed track can be edited with beat-aligned jumps; the picture can then take its beat grid.\n- **Foley made from the scene's matter**: a paper brush rustle for each painting-in (band-passed noise, light tremolo, a panned sweep); pencil scratch for notes; a soft thump for a stamp; page flips; nature beds and creatures of the subject (birds, wind, rain plinks, water, insects, fire crackle).\n- **Silence**: the brush lifted: music out, only paper and room; it frames a single painted moment.\n- **Mix character**: voice forward (RMS a few dB over the music), music ducked ~4 dB under voice with a smoothed envelope, a short soft reverb, final −14 LUFS. Kokoro voices are peaky (~17 dB peak-to-RMS): limit before level-matching.\n- **Voice**: warm, unhurried documentary read, one line per idea.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Painting-in as reveal.** A new thing appears stroke by stroke as it enters. *Fits content like:* the stages of a building; the ingredients of a dish; the organs of a body.\n- **Burst.** A whole group paints in at once (≤ 1 s) for a peak. *Fits content like:* a festival crowd; a field in bloom; a city lighting up.\n- **Palette as data.** Sky, ground and background blend by a measured value. *Fits content like:* pollution along a river; temperature by altitude; noise across a city.\n- **Specimen + note.** One hero object with a hand-written label and leader line. *Fits content like:* a tool kit; a wine region's grapes; parts of a bicycle.\n- **Mist erase.** The world washes back to paper. *Fits content like:* forgetting; a dream ending; a site demolished.\n- **Map in time.** A painted map whose fills change continuously through years. *Fits content like:* a language spreading; a coastline eroding; a railway network growing.\n- **Painted transformation.** Objects cross-fade to a burnt, wet or aged sprite and regrow. *Fits content like:* a wildfire and recovery; a flood; a restoration.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Offscreen wash strips too short flatten tall shapes into flat tops and expose seams on tilts → size strips well beyond the frame.\n- A translucent wash that ends at its canvas edge shows a hard seam under other layers → extend below the frame and fade to paper with a gradient.\n- Classic scripts share one global scope: touching a `const` before its declaration in async init leaves `READY` false and every screenshot times out → build everything inside the init; read `[pageerror]` lines.\n- The stroke builder consumes the global RNG: any stroke created in `render(t)` must be wrapped in `withSeed` or parallel workers disagree.\n- Thousands of strokes per frame are too slow → sprite cache.\n- Beat-locked animation must be re-derived when the music edit changes.\n- New CJK subtitle characters fall back to another font until the subset is rebuilt.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `engine.js` (RNG, easing, noise, monotone keyframe tracks; the stroke engine `mk`, `drawS`, `drawList`, `sprite`, `clump`, `edgeOf`, `withSeed`), `plants.js` (plant and prop generators, local coordinates, root at 0,0), `scene.js` (layers, wash strips `washPoly`, world placement `add`, annotations `hand` / `leader` / `note`), `main.js` (map, mist, HUD, subtitles, `render(t)`, async init → `READY`), `paper.py`, `fetch_fonts.mjs`, `mix.py`, `render.mjs`. API table and a minimal example: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md) \"Engine reference\".\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the subject, the structure, what gets painted, the palette logic, whether anything is measured, the opening, the ending and the camera. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a journal kept over one year** (locked page per month, each painted over the last); **anatomy of one object** (a single specimen dissected into labelled parts, each part a painted close-up); **a recipe or process** (steps painted top to bottom on one tall sheet, tilting down).\n- Openings: **a finished painting** that we then watch being painted backwards to its first stroke, and forwards again; **a close-up of wet pigment** spreading, pulled back to show what it became; **a hand-written question** on blank paper, answered by painting.\n- Endings: **the page left unfinished**, brush lifted mid-stroke; **a close-up of one detail** as the rest fades to paper; **the sheet dries and is filed** into a stack of other pages.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nFollow the Rain\n"},{"id":"S4-L19","name":"中国水墨","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L19.jpg","markdown":"# L19 中国水墨（Chinese Ink Wash）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 手绘与绘画\n- 长什么样：侠客踏水过江，一剑断流。\n- 适合：东方意境，和 Z1、M30 一起看\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Chinese Ink Wash — Style Prompt\n\n> Xieyi ink painting on rice paper, animated: blank paper is space, one brushstroke is a mountain, a wave, a gesture or a cut.\n> References (grammar only): Shanghai Animation Film Studio's *Feelings of Mountains and Water* (1988) (blank-paper space, ink bloom, guqin pacing, figures in a few strokes); the bamboo-forest fight of *Crouching Tiger, Hidden Dragon* (weightlessness, stillness before action). Copy none of their characters, compositions or music.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nAn ink painting (水墨) painted and unrolled before you: black ink on warm rice paper in five tones, plus **one** red, the vermilion seal. No Western outlines: forms are made by **loaded brushstrokes** whose dark edge *is* the contour (没骨, \"boneless\" painting), by **wet washes** that bleed into the paper, and by **dry, fast strokes** where the paper shows through the bristle tracks (飞白, \"flying white\").\n\nMost of the frame is **empty paper** (留白): water, sky, mist, silence. Things are *painted into existence*, not cut to.\n\nNot watercolour (no colour washes), not vector art with a brush texture (no flat fills or closed outlines), not a calligraphy title sequence.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Paper** is procedural, in the compositor, never a texture file: warm rice paper (around `#F2ECDE`), cloud mottling, long fibres, pulp flecks, grain that fades when zoomed out (or it shimmers), a light vignette; anchored to the world.\n- **Ink** is stroke opacity multiplied onto paper (never an overlay), in five tones (墨分五色):\n\n| Tone | Opacity | Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| 焦 jiao (burnt) | ~.96 | calligraphy, the sharpest lines, moss dots |\n| 浓 nong (thick) | ~.82 | contours, dark edge of loaded strokes |\n| 重 zhong (heavy) | ~.60 | secondary lines, folds |\n| 淡 dan (light) | ~.34 | mountain bodies, robe and body washes |\n| 清 qing (clear) | ~.16 | far mountains, faces, mist |\n\n- **Brushstroke model**: centreline × pressure profile × width noise, plus 4–60 **bristles** whose ink load drops along the stroke and breaks on long-scale noise. `dry` runs from a solid wet stroke to flying white (dry strokes are bristle tracks only).\n- **Loaded stroke**: bristles darker on one side, so the stroke carries its own contour: this makes bodies, rocks and waves read as ink, not vectors.\n- **Two layers**: the wet one bleeds in the compositor (noisy blur, **edge pigment accumulation**, granulation); the dry one only gets fibre jitter and paper tooth.\n- **Masses**: soft polygons, jittered edges, a gradient; never flat fills.\n- **Landscape**: mountains dissolve downward into mist bands, with 披麻皴 texture and 苔点 moss dots on near ranges only; rocks are outline → axe-cut texture → shadow wash → dots.\n- **Water is blank paper**, moved by faint dry streaks and open ink rings. Solid water: Song-dynasty **parallel wave lines** (dense near the edge) or a pale body with a few huge loaded strokes along its face.\n- **Figures are xieyi**: one pale body mass plus two loaded strokes, burnt-ink lines for limbs; features, if ever shown, in 3–5 strokes.\n- **Calligraphy**: an OFL brush font is only a **skeleton**: trace centrelines in stroke order, pressure from glyph thickness, hidden-tip entry (藏锋), tapered exits.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- Ink only, from cool grey at low density to warm black at full density. No other hue in the world.\n- **One vermilion** (around `#B02E22`), for the seal and at most one tiny accent in the story (an object the protagonist carries). The accent finds the protagonist in wide shots and can rhyme with the seal. Never a red wash, never red type.\n- Values carry depth: far = pale and soft, near = dark and textured. If a frame gets busy, remove ink rather than add tone.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- Subtitles are **inscriptions (题跋)**: a classical italic serif (Cormorant Garamond italic), ink-coloured, no box, ≤ 2 lines, placed in each shot's empty paper. A small vermilion square marks the start of each line. They appear wet and dry in a fraction of a second. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- Titles are written on the painting itself, in vertical columns in its empty space, with a vermilion 白文 seal drawn by code (lines knocked out of a slightly irregular red square, with ink-paste speckles). They move with the world.\n- Screen-space overlays reset the camera transform.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Figures step at 12 fps** (on twos); their position, the camera, blooms, ripples and splatter move on ones at 24 fps.\n- **Stable strokes on twos**: seed every stroke by its index and compute bristle breaks along *normalized* arc length × a fixed reference length, so flying-white gaps don't crawl every drawing. Allow only a small \"boil\" on the width noise.\n- **Painted into existence**: strokes appear in drawing order.\n- **Stillness before action** (when a decisive stroke is the event): hold, with at most a slow push-in, then let the stroke cross the frame in a few frames. Consequences start only after the stroke has finished.\n- Nothing is tweened like a vector shape: things are painted, bloom or dissolve into mist.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | Expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Locked frame on the paper | The page is the stage; things are painted in | a thing forming; a line being read; a still decision |\n| Right-to-left pan (handscroll) | Time and distance as one painting | a journey; a history; a process |\n| Vertical tilt (hanging scroll) | Height, stages stacked | seasons; a climb; generations |\n| Lateral tracking, lead room, slow far parallax | Motion through a world | travel; a chase; a commute |\n| High wide, a tiny figure in blank paper | Smallness, solitude | loneliness; nature's scale; one life among many |\n| Low angle, dark mass towering, slow push-in with shake | Weight, threat | an obstacle; a storm; pressure |\n| Hard cut to an extreme close-up | A break in the flow (the style's only hard cuts) | a decision; a shock; a memory |\n| Push into an ink bloom or mist | Passing between states | memory; dream; a season turning |\n| Pull back until a mark joins the whole | The part was inside something larger | a summing-up; irony of scale |\n| Return to an earlier framing | The rhyme shows what changed | aftermath; growth; before/after |\n\nFraming: 60–80 % empty paper; dark foreground may pass along the frame bottom. Transitions are ink blooms, pans along the scroll or mist; never a generic dissolve or a wipe.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Guqin-led, not piano and strings**: guqin (`core/audio/pluck.py` `guqin`: open and stopped strings, slides, vibrato, glassy harmonics, sweeps), a low breathy xiao, big drum and timpani for mass, frame drum for steps, a small gong for a seal. Chinese pentatonic modes.\n- **Cue to the picture, never loop**: unmetred plucks suit emptiness, a pulse suits movement, rolls suit mass; painted strokes can each land on a pluck.\n- **Silence is literal**: zero the whole bus (music, ambience, reverb tails) with short fades. At most one sound inside it (a drip, one ring). Place it where the story holds its breath.\n- **Foley follows the material**: ink drop (short \"tink\" + soft wet thud), brush air (band-passed noise with bristle grains, never a metal whoosh), steps on water (small splash), splatter (wet ticks), seal (wooden thump + paper crackle).\n- **Voice**: calm, few lines, like colophons that don't explain the picture. Duck music ~8 dB and ambience too; verify each line with whisper on the final mix.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **One stroke is everything.** *Fits content like:* a product born from one line; a city growing from a single street; a day beginning with one bird.\n- **Emptiness is matter.** Removing ink reveals paper white. *Fits content like:* a door opening in a wall; a crowd parting; an absence in a family portrait.\n- **Brush = gesture = action.** Stroke order becomes choreography. *Fits content like:* a dancer's phrase that spells a word; a signature; a chef's knife work.\n- **Flying white (飞白).** One fast dry stroke across the frame, strongest out of a silence. *Fits content like:* a sprint finish; a decision; a comet.\n- **Ink bloom transition.** The next scene grows inside a spreading drop with a dark tide-line (noisy radial mask; world-anchored for elements, in the compositor for scenes). *Fits content like:* memories; seasons; one era giving way to the next.\n- **The handscroll.** One long painting panned right to left, seen whole at last. *Fits content like:* a history; a supply chain from field to hand; a day in a street.\n- **Splitting a mass.** Halves clipped either side of the cut; one rises and fades, one slumps; splatter stays. *Fits content like:* breaking a record; a barrier falling; an idea splitting in two.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Dry brush looks like dashed lines → breaks from long-scale noise and an ink load that drops along the stroke.\n- Parallel semi-transparent strokes make bodies look like organ pipes → one base mass + two loaded strokes.\n- Big side-brush strokes look like a hairbrush → a gradient mass + a few low-dryness strokes. Gradients in vertical bands leave seams → a cached per-pixel layer.\n- A stroke body split between wet and dry layers loses density → full body in the dry layer plus a wet halo.\n- Clipping calligraphy with the font outline notches the junctions → font as skeleton only.\n- Uniform hatching inside a wave turns it into a wire mesh → loaded strokes along the face.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `ink.js` (brush engine: strokes, bristles, loaded strokes, masses, blots), `comp.js` (WebGL rice-paper compositor: bleed, edge pigment, paper, bloom), `land.js` (mountains, cun, cliffs, pine, reeds, ripples), `wave.js` (gestural wave), `glyph.js` (skeleton-traced calligraphy), `seal.js` (vermilion seal), `hero.js` (xieyi figure rig), `subs.js` (inscription subtitles), `music/score.py` (guqin writing with `core/audio/pluck.py`); `?test=sheet|pose|frame` pages draw model sheets from the real engine.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the structure, the characters (or none), the landscape, the opening, the ending, the camera path, the pacing and where the red goes. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **one character, stroke by stroke** (no figure; each stroke of one large character opens into a vignette of the topic); **a hanging scroll** (a tilt from sky to ground through four stages, tone deepening); **two brushes in dialogue** (alternating strokes answer each other until they meet in one bloom).\n- Openings: **in motion** (a figure already painted and moving; the world washes in around it); **ink first** (a fully inked frame; ink is lifted where light or a road appears); **the bristles** (a wet stroke fills the frame; only when it lifts do we see what it drew).\n- Endings: **one small mark** (close on a footprint or a leaf while the ink around it dries; no pull-back); **evaporation** (the painting un-paints in reverse stroke order); **mid-gesture** (the brush stops before the last stroke lands).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nThe Swordsman and the River\n"},{"id":"S4-L20","name":"油画厚涂","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L20.jpg","markdown":"# L20 油画厚涂（Impasto Oil Painting）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 手绘与绘画\n- 长什么样：灰色雨中广场，第一把红伞撑开，圆舞曲把整个广场刷上颜色。\n- 适合：浓烈的情绪\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Impasto · Palette Knife — Style Prompt\n\n> Thick oil paint laid with a palette knife: big, flat, sharp-edged planes of colour, raked by light so every ridge of paint shows. Clarity first: value structure before texture, hard edges at the focus, calm planes everywhere else.\n> References (grammar only): the opening titles of *The Umbrellas of Cherbourg* (1964) (overhead choreography read as moving colour); palette-knife street paintings (Leonid Afremov and the genre around him) (knife mosaic, vertical wet reflections, warm lights against cool shadow); Sorolla / Sargent (few, decisive strokes and strong light); Edgar Wright's music-locked editing (one action, one sound, one cut). Never copy their images, compositions, melodies or characters, and never name them in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nA moving oil painting made of knife strokes. Each stroke is a real object: a slanted plane of paint with a ridge where the blade pressed, a lip where it lifted off, broken streaks where the paint ran out, two colours dragged into each other. The world is **repainted from its strokes every frame**, but static paint never moves: only things that move in the story move. Light rakes across a height field, so the paint has body.\n\nThe look is clear and graphic (readable like a children's book at thumbnail size) but has the tactile surface of impasto at 100 % crop.\n\nNot a Van Gogh swirl filter (strokes boiling everywhere), not a brush texture over flat vector art, not watercolour (paint is opaque), not a \"painterly\" photo filter (strokes are designed, not sampled from noise).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Value first.** Design each set as a flat, value-planned illustration (3 values minimum), then convert it to strokes. If the reference doesn't read at 480 px wide, no brushwork will save it.\n- **Stroke sizes by importance and depth.** Coarse to fine: large planes in sky and walls, a few px only at edges and faces (per shape: `maxR`, `detail`, `dir`).\n- **Strokes never cross shape groups**, so silhouettes stay crisp. Lost edges are opt-in (put two shapes in the same `group`).\n- **Thin underpainting** (the reference itself, blurred ~1.5 px) under the strokes, so the canvas never peeks through as outlines.\n- **Knife stroke**: longer than wide (≈ 1.7–3.1 × radius long, 0.95–1.45 × radius wide), slanted ends, small angle and value jitter (≈ ±13°, ±6 %). Solid objects that must read as silhouettes (figures, props) get little or no ragged run-out.\n- **Light** rakes over a height field; wet or glossy surfaces get slightly more specular.\n- **Reflections** (wet ground, glass, water) are **explicit stacks of short horizontal knife dabs** under each light source, broken and fading; never a painted rectangle.\n- Render supersampled (2×). **No film grain**: grain plus compression eats the knife edges.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- Planes of mixed, slightly broken colour; each stroke may drag a second colour (`c2`) into the first. No gradients made by blending pixels: gradients are made by stroke-to-stroke steps.\n- Warm light against cool shadow is the default temperature structure; the brightest, purest chroma is reserved for the story's subject (a prop, a crowd, a light source), not the background.\n- **Colour can be an event.** Every stroke can hold a grey and a colour version; a monochrome world with one coloured exception, or a world re-laid from grey to colour, is native to the medium. The grey version is the luminance of the same strokes, slightly cool, with a small colour residue.\n- Examples of palettes that fit: an after-rain sunset (cobalt → peach → gold sky, ochre facades, full-chroma props); a harvest field (straw yellow, burnt sienna earth, one viridian tractor); a night kitchen (umber shadows, sodium-lamp orange, one cold blue window). Choose from the topic.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- Titles are **laid in with the knife** (a stroke-by-stroke `appear` sweep of about a second) and can be taken off by the world (washed by rain, scraped, painted over). They move with the world.\n- Small text (credits, data, captions) stays crisp DOM text over the painting: knife strokes can't carry small letters.\n- A serif display face (e.g. Cormorant, OFL). Subtitles, if there is a voice: plain serif, ≤ 2 lines, in a calm plane; hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- Static paint is static. Only the things the story moves move (weather, figures, props, ribbons, light). Smooth 24 fps, no stepping: clarity is the brief.\n- Moving figures are procedural strokes rebuilt each frame from **fixed seeds**, so their paint doesn't boil (walk cycles, IK arms, props that open and close).\n- Actions sit on the music grid: a change of direction, a pop, a swell lands on a beat.\n- Anticipation → action → follow-through on every gesture (lift before the strike, dip before the snap, swing after the stop).\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Slow truck + push on a wide | a world observed; patience | a place; a routine; a crowd |\n| Push-in through a silence | inner pressure | a doubt; a decision; giving up |\n| Tilt-up from the ground or a reflection | discovery from below | a small character entering; a first sight |\n| Pull-back from a close-up | reaction; the world answering | consequence; a reveal of who is watching |\n| Rotating crane-out, overhead | pattern emerging from many | choreography; traffic; a gathering |\n| Same wide, repeated | before/after | change of colour, season or time |\n| Locked frame, light moves | time and weather as the actor | a day passing; a storm arriving |\n| Macro slide along the paint surface | the matter itself | a texture; a crafted object; a map |\n| Whip-pan into a new painted plate | energy, a jump | a list; montage; travel |\n\nFraming: keep the camera **inside the painted plate** (`half = W/2/zoom`), or the canvas shows. The subject gets the hard edges and the smallest strokes; everything else stays in big calm planes. Transitions: paint-over sweeps and scrapes, hard cuts on beats; no dissolves (two layers of knife ridges turn to mud).\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Foley follows the paint and the world**: palette-knife scrapes on every repaint or paint-over, wet dabs, the materials of the scene (wet stone, nylon, wood, fabric, glass).\n- **Beds** in layers (a hiss, near grains, one distinct detail), filtered when the camera goes under cover.\n- **Acoustic instruments with a hand-made grain**: solo strings, accordion, pizzicato, harp arpeggios, glockenspiel or celesta for pops, piano, guitar, woodwinds; a solo instrument for a lonely world, an ensemble for a shared one.\n- Options: **one action = one sound = one cut** (music-locked edit); pops pitched as a rising arpeggio so a sequence of events climbs; an unresolved phrase that the film resolves later; a hard silence that cuts music and beds to digital zero, containing at most one sound; a J-cut of the next scene's foley into a silence.\n- Mix: music and ambience duck under any voice (~8 dB); −14 LUFS. Music and foley can carry a film without narration.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Colour re-laid by the knife.** Every stroke keeps a grey and a colour version; a reveal wave re-paints them stroke by stroke (`reveal`, `revC`). *Fits content like:* a town waking up; a patient recovering; a brand relaunch; a season turning.\n- **The one coloured thing.** The world drawn with `grey: 1`, one object with `grey: 0`. *Fits content like:* one person in a crowd; the fruit that ripened; a warning light.\n- **Motion paints the picture.** Moving objects drip their colour as knife dabs; seen from above, their paths leave a painted pattern. *Fits content like:* a dance; delivery routes across a city; a football play; migration.\n- **Raking light.** At the peak, drop the light to a grazing angle so every ridge catches it, then let it settle. *Fits content like:* a sunrise; a moment of recognition; unveiling a sculpture.\n- **Paint-over transition.** The next shot's strokes are laid over the previous one in a sweep (`appear`), with a scrape on the soundtrack. *Fits content like:* a renovation; memory replacing the present; a timeline of versions.\n- **Sound made visible.** A note or a voice becomes a paint ribbon that carries colour to what it touches. *Fits content like:* a song reaching listeners; a radio broadcast; a rumour spreading.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Confined strokes shrink near edges and leave canvas-coloured outlines → add the underpainting texture.\n- Draw order in the reference matters: painting the ground after the buildings covers their feet.\n- Plain rectangles read as posts or blocks → give silhouettes taper, a lit edge, and one or two defining dabs (shoulder, hair, handle).\n- Proportions carry the object's identity at this stroke size: a prop drawn too wide reads as a different thing. Check silhouettes at thumbnail size.\n- Film grain or heavy compression destroys the knife edges → no grain, supersample, high bitrate.\n- Strokes that are re-seeded every frame boil → fixed seeds per stroke.\n- Engine-level WebGL traps (reserved words, VAO binding) are listed in DEMO.md build notes.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nFiles: `demo/engine/impasto.js` (renderer), `demo/engine/plate.js` (reference → strokes).\n\nMain calls: `new Impasto(canvas,{W,H,ss})`, `E.begin()` / `E.finish(post)` (raking light + grade), `E.batch()` → `E.draw(batch,o)` for static plates (`cam`, `grey`, `reveal`, `appear`), `E.drawNow()` for moving strokes, `E.under()` for the underpainting, `new Strokes().push({...})` (`KNIFE`, `BRUSH`, `DAB`, `LINE`), `new Ref()` + `paintRef()` (reference illustration → coarse-to-fine strokes confined to shape groups), `strokePath()`. Full parameter table: DEMO.md \"Engine reference\".\n\nMinimal example: a warm-orange four-point sparkle with a short cursor tail, in knife paint.\n```js\nimport { Impasto, Strokes, KNIFE, DAB, hex } from './engine/impasto.js';\nconst E = new Impasto(canvas), s = new Strokes(), c = hex('#D97757');\nfor (let k = 0; k < 4; k++) {                                   // four tapered knife points\n  const a = k * Math.PI / 2;\n  s.push({ x: 960 + Math.cos(a) * 60, y: 540 + Math.sin(a) * 60, ang: a, len: 120, wid: 46, c, c2: hex('#f0a07a'), type: KNIFE, taper: .95, seed: k });\n}\ns.push({ x: 960, y: 540, len: 40, wid: 40, c: hex('#f6c0a0'), type: DAB, seed: 9 });   // bright core\ns.push({ x: 1060, y: 610, ang: .6, len: 90, wid: 16, c, type: KNIFE, alpha: .8, seed: 10 });   // cursor tail\nE.begin(); E.drawNow(s.data(), { grey: 0, run: 0 }); E.finish();\n```\n\"Only one colour\" (monochrome with one exception): draw the world with `grey: 1, reveal: 1e6`, draw the exception with `grey: 0`.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the structure, the cast (or none), the sets, the opening, the ending, the camera path, the pacing, whether there is a voice, and where colour lives. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **one object, many hands** (a bowl or a boat passes through the people who make and use it, one painted plate per hand); **a still life comes alive** (a locked table whose objects move in turn, the light crossing it hour by hour); **a portrait in layers** (a face painted coarse to fine as a voice tells one life, each layer a decade).\n- Openings: **the bare canvas** (underpainting only; the first knife stroke is the first event); **macro on a ridge of paint** that pulls back to become a mountain or a roof; **mid-action** (a figure already running across a finished plate).\n- Endings: **scraped back** (the knife takes the painting off to the underpainting, leaving one mark); **the light goes out** (raking light sinks until only the ridges glow, then black); **the frame in a room** (the film was a painting hanging somewhere, seen by someone).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nThe Colour of Rain\n"},{"id":"S4-L21","name":"一笔画","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L21.jpg","markdown":"# L21 一笔画（One-line Drawing）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 手绘与绘画\n- 长什么样：一根不离纸的线画完一个人的一生，再把笔交给孩子。\n- 适合：一条线讲到底，和样片库 C2、M4 一起看\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# One-line Drawing — Style Prompt\n\n> A single ink line that never leaves the paper. Everything on screen is that one line being drawn; scenes don't cut, they morph.\n> References (grammar only): Osvaldo Cavandoli's *La Linea* (1971) — one line is both the world and the character, and transformations of the line are the jokes and the transitions; Gjon Mili's 1949 photographs of Picasso drawing with light — the confidence and speed of a single gesture; Norman McLaren's *Begone Dull Care* and *Boogie-Doodle* — lines drawn *to* the music. Do not copy La Linea's character, its gibberish voice, or any specific continuous-line illustration.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n- **One continuous line** on warm white paper. The pen never lifts: in code the whole film can literally be one point array, from the first touch of the nib to the last frame.\n- **Everything is a line**: characters, places, objects, even time. No fills, no shading, no second line weight, no background art.\n- **We watch the line being drawn**: the camera follows the nib with a little lag and anticipation.\n- Besides ink there is only the paper and a soft **shadow of the pen** (the real world above the paper). One accent colour at most, used once.\n\nNot a whiteboard explainer (no hand, no marker, no erasing, no text-heavy boards), not ink wash (no tones, no brush bloom as a look), not a line-art animation with cuts and many strokes.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Paper**: warm white (around `#F4EFE4`), procedural mottling (soft-light, large tile), long fibres that fade out when zoomed out (or they shimmer), a sparse paper-tooth speckle over the ink at close zoom, light vignette. All textures anchored to the paper; they move with the camera.\n- **Ink** is a filled polygon around the path (not `ctx.stroke`), mixed with paper colour to an opaque tone per point.\n  - Width = base width of the current \"voice\" of the line × pen pressure × fine noise (±8 %); pressure from speed: `0.66 + 0.8·exp(−v/300)` (fast = thin, slow = fat).\n  - The line's character can change along the path (base width, alpha, tremble, dryness); blend over a short stretch at boundaries.\n  - Ink bleed: the same polygon slightly wider, blurred on an offscreen layer, composited at ~20 %.\n  - Pooling: extra round dots where speed drops low.\n  - Tremble (perpendicular noise) and dry-brush gaps (long-scale noise + thin bristle strands) for age, fear or fatigue.\n  - A stop leaves a teardrop-shaped blot: tip at the nib, belly sagging downward as it spreads, a dark tide line at the rim.\n  - **A screen-space minimum width**: widths scale with zoom like a line under a macro lens, but clamp the zoom used for width during pull-backs so a wide view keeps its weight.\n- **The pen is never drawn**: a soft blurred wedge of shadow from the nib and a tiny contact dot. Hands, if any, appear only as shadows, each hand composited as one flat layer.\n- **Path design**: chapters written as SVG path strings, sampled by arc length, joined by tangent-continuous connectors; time assigned per point weighted by curvature.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **Ink on paper.** A warm black (around `#1D1A17`) on warm white; nothing else fills the frame.\n- **One accent at most, used once**, on a stretch of the line that means something; it returns to ink afterwards and stays visible in the final picture if the picture is revealed.\n- Examples of an accent's job: red for love or danger; blue for water or cold; gold for money or a prize. Never a second accent.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Text is written, like the line**: a handwriting face (e.g. Caveat) for subtitles, ink at ~84 %, no box, centred near the bottom, **written on** left to right (~0.2 s + ~17 ms per character) behind a soft paper-coloured halo so passing lines never cross the letters; fade out ~0.45 s. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- Titles in a monoline connected script (e.g. Sacramento: itself a one-line drawing), written on in empty paper away from the drawing, gone before the camera moves into them.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Everything on ones** (24 fps): the appeal is the continuous act of drawing; stepping breaks it.\n- **Timing = chapter windows + curvature**: within a window, time per point is weighted by `1 + K·turn` (sharp turns get more time, smoothed so speed never jumps). Pin musical beats with marks: by arc-length fraction or by position (\"the second time the pen passes here\"). A stop is two marks at the same place.\n- Corners (speed minima at high turn) can be exported to the soundtrack as candidate note onsets.\n- Speeds: ~300–700 units/s for travelling lines, ~50–150 for small details. The most important stroke is usually the slowest.\n- **Retracing** over an existing line is legal and invisible (it only thickens slightly).\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. The camera is one continuous move: composition centre, zoom, a few degrees of roll, and a follow weight (0 = composed, 1 = locked to the nib); the follow point averages the nib over roughly −0.55…+0.4 s (lag and anticipation), with a soft clamp keeping the nib inside the central ~70 % of the frame. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Follow the nib (high follow weight) | travel; momentum; a thread of time | a journey; a process; a timeline |\n| Composed frame, low follow weight | a scene that must read as a whole | a vignette; a joke; a diagram |\n| Macro on the nib | intimacy; the act of making | a first touch; a signature; a detail |\n| Hold with a slow push-in | weight; a stop | a loss; a doubt; a decision |\n| Mirrored framing | rhyme across time | parent and child; before/after |\n| Continuous pull-back | everything was one picture | a reveal; a map; a word |\n| Lateral scroll with the line running ahead | a horizon; a graph; a race | a stock chart; a heartbeat; a road |\n| Roll with the line | vertigo; play | a loop-the-loop; a fall; a dance |\n\nNo cuts. Transitions are morphs: the last stroke of one image is the first stroke of the next.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Music: one solo instrument, one unbroken melodic line**, the sound equivalent of the drawing: a bowed or plucked string (cello, violin, viola, harp), a solo woodwind (clarinet, flute, bassoon), a hummed voice, a single guitar. A second colour only as a rare accent (a chime, a pizzicato stretch). No pads.\n- **Write the score to the drawing**: key notes on marks and corners; fast line = short notes; a stopped line = one long note fading into silence; the highest note on the most important touch.\n- **The pen on paper is the most important sound**, synthesised from the pen speed: band-passed noise (brighter when fast, duller when slow), random paper-fibre ticks with density ∝ speed, amplitude ∝ √speed, panned with the nib's screen position; broken up by dry gaps; lighter and jerkier for a young hand. Plus: a wooden tap on the first touch, a faint wet swell for a blot, a tiny rub for a handoff, a page lift.\n- **Silence is literal**: zero the music bus including reverb tails; drop room tone.\n- **Mix**: music ducks ~5 dB and the pen ~4 dB under voice; −14 LUFS; grain 0 (the paper is in the render).\n- **Voice**: warm, few short lines that leave space for the line; place key words on picture beats with whisper word timestamps.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **The pen never lifts.** Anything that is one continuous thing. *Fits content like:* a river from source to sea; a relay race; a production line.\n- **The line is time.** The stroke's character changes with age, mood or strain. *Fits content like:* a building aging; a runner tiring; a machine wearing out.\n- **Transformation.** One image morphs into the next. *Fits content like:* a seed becoming a tree becoming a table; a sketch becoming a product; a word becoming its object.\n- **Stopping is an event.** The nib stops, ink pools, music cuts to silence (1.5–3 s). *Fits content like:* a power cut; a pause for a minute's silence; a hesitation before a signature.\n- **Reverse-designed scale reveal.** Every vignette is secretly part of one big picture seen at the end. *Fits content like:* a city's landmarks forming its skyline; a company's milestones forming its logo; a recipe's steps forming the dish.\n- **Handoff.** The pen passes to another hand (as a shadow) and a new line begins. *Fits content like:* a teacher and a student; a founder and a successor; one generation to the next.\n- **Line as graph.** The line becomes a chart while staying a drawing. *Fits content like:* a heartbeat; a temperature record; a football match's momentum.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **Stray connectors ruin a reveal**: every extra line reads as a wrinkle, beard or mask. Fix the topology (where each chapter enters and leaves), not the drawing. Plan it like an Euler path: a closed motif is a circuit (you leave where you came in); retrace or reorder chapters.\n- Each region of a final picture is drawn exactly once; connectors must fall on natural lines of that picture.\n- **Test the reveal early and often**: render the whole path at once at final scale after every change.\n- Lines drawn too close braid together once width is applied; keep a gap.\n- A round blot reads as a mole → a sagging teardrop.\n- Thin lines at wide zoom → clamp the width zoom.\n- Translucent hand shadows double-darken where they overlap → one layer per hand.\n- Titles collide with the drawing as the camera moves → check every frame of the title's window.\n- Whisper mishears short lines → rephrase rather than fight it.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `geom.js` (samples SVG paths by arc length in the browser, adds tangent-continuous connectors, assigns time by curvature, marks and holds), `ink.js` (the line renderer: pressure, bleed, pooling, tremble, dry brush, accent stretch, blot, minimum screen width), `paper.js` (paper, fibres, tooth, vignette), `cam.js` (composition keys + nib follow + soft clamp), `hands.js` (pen and hand shadows), `subs.js` (written-on subtitles and titles). The drawing itself lives in one file of SVG path strings and a chapter table (`face.js`); replace it with your own path. Debug views (`?all=1`, `?cams=`) render the whole path or many camera views in one session: see [DEMO.md](DEMO.md) \"Build notes\".\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide what the line draws, whether it reveals one final picture, where it stops, the accent (or none), the opening and the ending. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a line that is a graph** (a data series drawn as a horizon; each peak and trough becomes a scene); **two lines** that start in opposite corners and are drawn in alternation until they meet; **a loop** (the line draws a scene and then retraces its way back to the start, which now means something else).\n- Openings: **mid-line at speed**, the pen already running across the paper; **a finished drawing** whose line then unravels into the story; **a word written in script** whose last letter becomes the first scene.\n- Endings: **the line runs off the edge** of the paper and keeps going; **the ink runs out** and the last stroke fades into dry scratches; **the pen stops in mid-air** over the paper before the last stroke.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nThe Line That Never Lifted\n"},{"id":"S4-L22","name":"白板讲解","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L22.jpg","markdown":"# L22 白板讲解（Whiteboard Explainer）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 手绘与绘画\n- 长什么样：手机怎么知道你在哪：GPS、原子钟，和相对论每天多出的 38 微秒。\n- 适合：讲道理、讲步骤，就是你发来的那条\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Whiteboard Explainer — Style Prompt\n\n> An explainer drawn live on one glossy dry-erase board: single-line handwriting that is actually *written* stroke by stroke, floating markers with no hand, magnets that can move, an eraser that can rewind, and a camera that travels across the board instead of cutting.\n> References (grammar only): RSA Animate / Cognitive Media (one continuous board, the camera travels instead of cutting, a full-board view); minutephysics (one sentence = one drawing, the simplest geometry that carries the idea); Steve Reich's *Piano Phase* (two copies of one figure at two tempi as a structure). Never copy their drawings, characters, hands or music.\n\nSuits explainers, usually science or \"how does X work\"; the length follows how many ideas the board has to hold.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n- **One physical whiteboard.** Everything the viewer learns is drawn in dry-erase marker in front of them, in real handwriting order.\n- **The board is a map**: each idea lives in its own region; the camera travels between regions, often led by the pen. Distance on the board = distance in the argument.\n- **Real marker, not animated text**: ink pools where the nib lands, dry streaks and speckle, strokes overshoot and don't quite close, ghosts of old lessons remain, a glossy sheen drifts slower than the board, and **every stroke makes a sound**.\n- **No hands.** Markers float with a real cast shadow that grows when they lift, park outside the frame between phrases and fly back in. Magnets and the eraser move on their own. Never draw a hand, an arm or a cursor.\n\nNot Blueprint (no blue sheet, no drafting conventions), not Crayon Picture Book (no wax texture, no children's story), not a kinetic-type explainer (text is handwritten stroke by stroke, never animated as blocks).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Board**: a warm white gradient, a faint scuff and micro-scratch tile, a few dozen **ghost marks** (old words, circles, arrows) at 5–10 % in grey or pale blue, and a soft diagonal **window sheen** (white, 10–16 %) moving at ~0.35× the camera: this one detail sells \"glossy\".\n- **Ink**: alpha ~0.94, composited with **multiply** so overlaps darken like real marker. Width 6–11 px for drawing, ~13 % of cap height for lettering.\n- **Stroke character**: resampled every ~0.6 × width; low-frequency hand wobble along the normal (a few px, long wavelength) plus a finer tremor; chisel-tip width modulated by direction; a pressure ramp at the start and a small taper at the end; circles start at an angle and **overlap past closure**; rectangles are four strokes with small corner overshoot; a darker pooled dot *inside* the start of long strokes.\n- **Dry-marker texture**: a speckle-and-streak tile subtracted from the ink layer (destination-out), locked to board space, faded out when the camera is wide.\n- **Lettering**: a single-line (single-stroke) font written in stroke order, each glyph with small rotation, baseline and scale jitter. Missing symbols (μ ≈ → × − ✓ ² ° ±) are hand-defined.\n- **Props**: dry-erase markers (white barrel, colour band and cap in the ink colour, felt nib), a felt eraser, magnets (glossy, with specular highlight and soft cast shadow). Frame, tray and wall appear only if the camera ever goes past the board edge.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **Three marker colours at most**, each with a meaning held for the whole film: one dark ink for things and structure, one for signals / measurement / process, one accent for the thing that matters (one accent per region).\n- **Monochrome variant**: everything in the dark ink, one element in the accent (the \"only colour\").\n- Ghost marks and board stay neutral; the accent never appears on props except its own marker cap and the hero magnet.\n- Example sets: black `#23262c` / blue `#2a5cb3` / orange `#d97757`; black / green `#2e8b57` / red `#c8363a`; navy `#1f2a44` / teal `#1f8a8a` / purple `#7a4fb3`.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Handwriting only**: a single-line hand (e.g. EMS Tech, OFL) for all board text; titles large (~120–200 px cap height), labels 30–70 px.\n- **Title and end card are written on the board** in the same hand.\n- **Burned subtitles**: an off-white rounded label (~90 %), a handwriting-like face (e.g. Architects Daughter ~44 px), dark text, a short accent-colour marker dash. Split at clauses, ≤ 44 characters per line, bottom ~12 % of the frame kept free for them in every shot. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- **Writing finishes on the word**: a term appears exactly when the narrator says it.\n- A label is fully in or fully out of every shot it appears in.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Hand speed**: every stroke follows a slow-fast-slow curve (`u − sin(2πu)/2π × 0.8`). Strokes are scheduled into a **time window** and the engine solves the speed, so writing always finishes on time.\n- **Pen hops** between strokes lift in proportion to distance (shadow slides and blurs); long gaps send the pen off-frame to park and back shortly before its next stroke.\n- **Magnet**: drop with a shrinking shadow; slide with a small lift; lift-off and sink possible.\n- **Eraser**: constant speed along a path, a wide band at ~90 % strength (a ghost always remains).\n- **Speed**: faster than real hands (a sped-up lesson) but never so fast the stroke order is lost: a label ~0.3–0.8 s, a title ~3 s.\n- Camera on ones with real motion blur when it moves fast.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA 2D camera over a large board (e.g. 8000 × 4500): position, zoom interpolated in 1/z so pushes feel like dollies, a little roll. A vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pull back from a tiny drawing | scale | a surprising size; context |\n| Ride the line the pen is drawing | one idea leads to the next | a transition; a process; a path |\n| Hold still | read now | an equation; a definition; a list |\n| Whip to a new region | a new idea after a pause | a twist; a \"but\" |\n| Return to an earlier region | a callback, for free | reusing a result; a comparison |\n| Split view of two regions (zoomed out enough for both) | two things at once | a duet; before/after |\n| Slow push into one symbol | this term is the point | a unit; a variable; a name |\n| Track along a long diagram | sequence, time | a timeline; a pipeline; a journey |\n| Pull back past the board edge to the wall | the whole lesson as one picture | a summary; a scale jump |\n\nMotion blur: when the camera moves more than ~14 px per frame, render and average sub-frames (≤ 4 px apart). No cuts on the board; if a cut is needed, it is a whip.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Every stroke sounds**: band-passed felt-on-gloss noise (a hiss band ~2–7.5 kHz + body ~0.5–1.5 kHz) enveloped by the stroke's own speed curve, a tiny nib tick at the start, stick-slip grains, and on some long strokes the **dry-erase squeak** (a 1.5–2.6 kHz sine with 7–13 Hz vibrato). Each colour sounds slightly different. Pan = the stroke's screen x; closer camera = louder.\n- **Props**: magnet on steel = click + metal modes + a low board thump; eraser = felt rub modulated by its zig-zag; cap off/on; marker tray bounce.\n- **Room**: low room tone; a single diegetic ticker (a wall clock, a fridge hum, a projector fan) can own a silence.\n- **Music** options: a solo piano figure; a small jazz trio with brushes; plucked strings and kalimba; a synth arpeggio for tech topics; clockwork minimalism (marimba, pizzicato, woodblock, glockenspiel). Mickey-mouse details: a rising whistle for growth, pings for signals, a harp run for a fall.\n- **Techniques to pick from**: adding one instrument per step of a mechanism; a key change when the frame of reference changes; a full stop into silence before a surprise; the previous cue reversed and squeezed for a rewind; two copies of one figure at two tempi (phasing) for anything that diverges, snapping into unison when resolved.\n- **Mix**: voice on top (compressed, light room), music well under voice and ducked further under speech, foley ~11 dB under voice; −14 LUFS.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Writing in stroke order.** The reveal is the explanation: a formula term by term as it is spoken. *Fits content like:* compound interest; the Drake equation; a recipe's ratios.\n- **The pen leads the camera.** Transitions are drawn lines the camera rides. *Fits content like:* a nerve signal; a supply chain; the water cycle.\n- **Magnets.** The only things allowed to move on a board: give the hero to a magnet; slide, lift, drop, sink it. *Fits content like:* a delivery truck on a route; a cell in a bloodstream; a player on a pitch.\n- **The eraser rewinds.** Wiping a trail backwards reads as undoing time. *Fits content like:* an undo in software; reversing a chemical reaction; \"what if we hadn't…\".\n- **Two pens at once.** A live duet comparing two quantities. *Fits content like:* two savings plans; two runners; a population vs its food supply.\n- **Ghosts of the old lesson.** A faint previous drawing becomes relevant. *Fits content like:* a theory replaced; last year's forecast; a first draft.\n- **The full-board view.** Every region seen at once as one picture. *Fits content like:* a system diagram; a history; a map of a field.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **One pen, too many jobs** drifts later and later → give each drawing a deadline window and let overlapping jobs go to another pen.\n- **Minimum stroke duration × many dashes** makes a dashed line take seconds → dashes need their own floor.\n- **A pooled-ink dot bigger than the stroke start** leaves halos on every letter → keep it inside.\n- **Punctuation vanishes** (a font period is a 1 px stroke) → render sub-width strokes as dots.\n- **Labels half-cut by a push-in** look sloppy.\n- **Motion blur with few sub-frames** strobes text.\n- **Eraser paths** crossing labels you want to keep.\n- **Voice and phoneme set mismatch** (a British voice with US phonemes) mispronounces → match the language code to the voice; ASR-check.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\n`demo/engine/wb.js`: hand-drawn shapes (`line`, `curve`, `poly`, `arc`, `circle`, `rect`, `arrow`, `dashed`, `hatch`), `text()` in writing order from a single-line font (`loadFont`), `Stroke`, floating `Pen`s, `Timeline.draw(pen, shapes, t, {by})` (solves hand speed to a deadline), `Timeline.erase`, `Timeline.cue`, keyed `Camera`, `Board` (ink multiply, dry texture, erasers, sheen, props), prop drawers. API table and a minimal example that draws something new: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md#engine-reference).\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the topic's object, the board layout, the colours within the logic, the voice, the music family, the camera route, the opening, the ending and the length.\n\nAll far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a proof** (one claim at the top, the board fills downward step by step, each step boxed when proven); **a debate** (two pens, two halves of the board, each drawing its side until the drawings meet in the middle); **a timeline** (one long horizontal line ridden left to right, with events hanging off it).\n- Openings: **a question mark** drawn huge in the centre, then shrunk to become a dot in the first diagram; **an erased board** where the ghost of a wrong answer is still visible; **a magnet dropping** onto an empty board with a clack.\n- Endings: **the answer circled** twice and the pen capped; **the board wiped** except for one line that survives; **a push into the smallest symbol** on the board, which becomes the title.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nEinstein in Your Pocket\n"},{"id":"S4-L23","name":"钢笔淡彩","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L23.jpg","markdown":"# L23 钢笔淡彩（Urban Sketch · Pen & Wash）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 手绘与绘画\n- 长什么样：公园只是一张钢笔速写，风把草帽吹到哪里，哪里才有颜色。\n- 适合：生活记录、城市速写\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Urban Sketch · Pen & Wash — Style Prompt\n\n> A sketchbook page drawn on location: fine sepia fineliner lines that wobble, overshoot and never quite close, with loose transparent watercolour dropped on top. The colour sits slightly off the lines, spills over them, and dissolves into cream cold-press paper at the page edge.\n> References (grammar only):\n> - Urban-sketching sketchbook pages: line first, then wash; colour off-register; handwritten date and place in the corner; the page edge breaking up into dabs.\n> - *Paperman* (2012): a monochrome world with one coloured object.\n> - *The Red Balloon* (1956): an object as the protagonist, the camera following it across a city.\n> - Jazz waltz (the Vince Guaraldi school): 3/4 swing, walking bass, brushes, a woodwind lead.\n>\n> Nothing is copied from these: no characters, melodies, layouts or type.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nReportage drawing, made on location in a few minutes. Two layers that stay visibly separate:\n\n1. **Ink.** One thin, near-constant line (a 0.3–0.5 mm fineliner) in dark sepia. It wobbles slightly, overshoots corners by a few pixels, breaks mid-stroke, and suggests rather than describes: a few window marks instead of a grid, scalloped fragments instead of a whole tree outline.\n2. **Wash.** Transparent watercolour glazed on top, **misregistered** by 1–3 px from the line, sometimes spilling past it, leaving paper white for highlights and figures (reserves). Dried edges are darker (pigment pools at the rim), and pigment granulates into the paper tooth.\n\n**The page is part of the image**: warm cream cold-press paper with large-scale mottling and fine tooth. Nothing is painted edge to edge; the painting frays into leaf-sized dabs and white holes, leaving margins for handwriting.\n\nNot Watercolor Brush (no line-free wet-in-wet painting, no painterly edges without ink), not Whiteboard (no marker, no explainer diagrams), not Chinese Ink Wash (no brush calligraphy, no monochrome ink tones). Line and wash, both, always.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Paper**: two octaves of fbm mottling (a few %), a tooth tile multiplied over everything, figures included.\n- **Ink**: ~96 % coverage; world line width thinner far, thicker near (e.g. 1.3–3.4 px); wobble ~1 px; overshoot 3–8 px; a third to half of long strokes get one small gap. Strokes are baked with their **draw time per pixel**, so the shader plays back stroke order.\n- **Wash**: 2–3 rough-edged layers, multiply, alpha ~0.4–0.65, offset from the line by about one line width, a dried edge at 30–40 %; granulation = pigment × (0.82 + 0.36 · fbm).\n- **Colour arrival field**: every pixel has a time at which colour reaches it. The wash is revealed through it with a ragged wet front (darkened band just behind the front) and noisy edges. Figures bloom from a point with a noisy circular clip.\n- **Foliage**: pale base wash, then light and shadow masses, then small leaf dabs coloured by light direction, holes at the rim, scalloped ink only on the outline.\n- **Sky**: a few big soft washes at low alpha, fading upward; clouds are reserves with a tinted belly.\n- **Buildings**: local colour; shadow side mixed ~45 % with a cool violet-grey; distant blocks mixed ~50 % with a haze colour.\n- **Figures**: a 3D skeleton in orthographic projection; torso from cross-sections; limbs as organic tapered tubes; hair and head as one mass; face left blank (one nose tick in profile); shadow side mixed with violet.\n- **Page edge**: a noisy boundary, a white-dab band inside, a coloured-dab spray outside.\n- Deterministic: same seed, same marks.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **Ink is one colour** (dark sepia or warm black); it never changes.\n- **Wash is local colour, lightly**: few pigments, mixed on the page; shadows are the local colour mixed toward one shared cool violet; distance mixes toward one haze.\n- **Paper white is the brightest value**; it is reserved, never painted.\n- **Colour can be withheld**: a page may be ink-only with colour as an event (one tinted object, colour arriving with a cause).\n- Example palettes: park in summer (lawn `#c3c96f`, leaves `#7fa04a`, sky `#9fbcd6`, limestone `#d8c6a0`); harbour morning (sea `#7fa6b8`, hulls `#c9573f`, rope `#c8a86b`, sky `#bcd0dc`); winter market (roofs `#8b93a6`, lights `#e2b04a`, awnings `#b8483e`, snow = paper).\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **All text is handwriting on the page**, written on left to right with pen sound, and moves with the camera: a corner note (e.g. Reenie Beanie: day, time, weather, place), titles and colophon in a casual hand (e.g. Caveat 500–600), left-aligned in the blank margin.\n- **Subtitles**, if there is a voice: handwritten lines in the page margin or a caption strip written into the lower margin, never a generic overlay box. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- The style works well without any voice.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Figures boil at 12 fps** (line wobble and wash seeds change every 1/12 s). The static page does not boil (it is a real page); the camera moves every frame, smoothly.\n- **Performance**: anticipation, action, follow-through; footsteps on the beat.\n- **Invisible forces are drawn**: wind as ink curls that draw on and erase from the tail, trailing dabs; sound as ink lines; heat as wavy strokes.\n- **Colour never snaps**: it blooms from a point or arrives through the field.\n- Stroke-order draw-on for anything that \"appears\".\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA 2D camera over a large page (e.g. 5120 × 2880). Designed moves use cubic easing; follow shots use a critically damped spring on a lead-ahead target, so the camera lags like a hand-held follow. A vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Push-in | attention; a detail observed | a face; a sign; a small event |\n| Lateral follow on a spring | travel, pursuit | an object carried; a walker; a vehicle |\n| Crane up | a new level, a view opening | a skyline; a tree; a balcony |\n| Whip-tilt with slight roll | sudden change of direction | a fall; a gust; a surprise |\n| Punch-in with a short shake | impact | a catch; a collision; a door slam |\n| Pull back to the whole page | scale; the sketch as an object | a summary; a place in full |\n| Tilt into the margin | from picture to words | notes; a date; a title |\n| Slow pan across a line-only area | the world before colour | a quiet start; memory |\n| Hold while the pen draws | the act of noticing | a subject being drawn |\n| Page turn to a new spread | another time or place | a travel diary; a sequence of days |\n\nFraming: subjects ≥ ⅓ of frame height at story beats; a small key object gets a faint paper halo (reserve) so it reads over busy foliage. Transitions: camera travel on the page, page turns, or colour arriving; no dissolves.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Ambience from the place**: paper room tone first, then the location once it has colour (birds, traffic, harbour gulls, market chatter). J-cut a place's sound a bar before we see it.\n- **Pen on cold-press paper**: band-passed noise (~1.6–7.5 kHz) with a fibre texture and grain clicks, one burst per real stroke; thousands overlap into a hiss during a fast sketch; brighter short strokes read like a hi-hat.\n- **Water and brush**: a wet brush \"shhh\" for blooms, a jar rinse, a drip.\n- **Foley follows objects** at sketch scale: fabric or paper flapping for things in the wind (rate and level from speed, pan from screen x), steps on the beat, bells, splashes.\n- **Music families**: a jazz waltz (walking bass in three, brushes, piano, clarinet or flute); a café accordion trio; solo nylon guitar; a string quartet pizzicato; a brass band for crowds. One colour instrument per area of the page works well.\n- **Silence as a tool**: a bar of true silence (ambience included) at the height of suspense; the first sound after it is the next gesture.\n- **Mix**: music ducks under key hits; foley high-passed ~90 Hz; −14 LUFS, moderate LRA.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Line before colour.** The pen draws a subject in real stroke order, then the wash drops in; the drawing is the spectacle. *Fits content like:* a cathedral façade; a chef plating a dish; a street musician.\n- **Colour as arrival.** Wash spreads from a source with a wet front, so an event paints the page; figures stay frozen line drawings until colour touches them. *Fits content like:* a sunrise over a harbour; a parade moving down a street; the lights of a market switching on.\n- **The one coloured thing.** A colourless sketch with a single tinted object that becomes the protagonist. *Fits content like:* a red umbrella in the rain; a yellow taxi; a child's kite.\n- **The page as frame.** Pull back until the whole page shows, edges dissolving, handwritten notes in the margin. *Fits content like:* a travel diary entry; a neighbourhood portrait; a day in a place.\n- **Carrot people.** Distant crowds as one wash blob, two leg lines and a head dot. *Fits content like:* a station at rush hour; a stadium; a beach.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **Straight edges in the colour field** from clipping by a bounding box → clip by the actual noisy distance.\n- **Rectangular masks around complex silhouettes** leave notches → mask with the real stepped silhouette.\n- **Characters losing colour** when they move into unpainted areas → sample arrival once (or tie it to the event), never at the current position.\n- **Static ink can't be occluded by later washes** → erase far ink inside nearer shapes before drawing; use carrot people for far crowds.\n- **IK feet pinned while blending poses** splay the legs → blend foot targets toward under-hip.\n- **Front-facing heads go bald** → draw the hair mass, then knock the face out of it.\n- **Big canvases in headless Chrome**: pack ink and time into RGBA byte textures yourself and upload the arrival field as a filterable float texture (`R16F`).\n- **Painting edge to edge** kills the sketchbook feel → leave margins and fray the edge.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\n`demo/engine.js` (deterministic): `penStroke` + `drawPen` (fineliner with wobble, overshoot, gaps, draw-on), `rasterPen` (bakes stroke order into an ink + time layer), `wash`, `reserve`, `dab` / `foliage`, `arrivalField` (colour arrival per cell from sources), `compositor` (WebGL page: paper × wash revealed by arrival × ink revealed by stroke time), `figure` + `drawPrims` (sketched people), `sketchShape` (any shape in this style in one call), `handwrite`. `demo/poses.js` holds a pose library. API table and a minimal example that draws something new: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md#engine-reference).\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the place, the subjects, what colour does (always there, withheld, arriving), the voice (or none), the music family, the camera path, the opening, the ending and the length.\n\nAll far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a travel diary** of five pages, one per day, each page turned by the wind or a hand; **a single street corner** drawn at four times of day, the wash changing while the ink stays; **a recipe** where each ingredient is drawn from life at the market and the final dish is painted last.\n- Openings: **the blank page** with only the handwritten date, then the first line of the horizon; **a close-up of a coffee cup** on the page's edge, pulling back to find the city drawn behind it; **rain dots** landing on the paper and blooming into the first washes.\n- Endings: **the sketchbook closes** on the page; **the last figure walks off the page** into the blank margin; **the colour drains** back to ink as the evening comes, leaving one lit window.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nWhere the Wind Went\n"},{"id":"S4-L24","name":"皮影戏","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L24.jpg","markdown":"# L24 皮影戏（Shadow Puppetry）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 东方传统\n- 长什么样：十日炙烤大地，后羿张弓射日。\n- 适合：讲故事，和 M12 一起看\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Shadow Puppetry — Style Prompt\n\n> Chinese shadow theatre: translucent dyed-hide puppets, carved like lace, pressed against a cotton screen and lit from behind by oil lamps.\n> References (grammar only): Lotte Reiniger's *The Adventures of Prince Achmed* (1926) for profile acting and lace-like scenery; Shaanxi / Huayin shadow theatre for carving, colour and gong-and-drum rhythm. Never reproduce a specific historical puppet, melody or recording, and never name them in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nA white cloth screen; behind it, flat puppets cut from **translucent dyed hide**, lit by an **oil lamp**. Colour arrives *by transmitted light*: dyes glow like stained glass, carved holes shine brightest, overlaps go darker. Puppets are jointed with **visible thread rivets** and moved by **thin rods** (neck and hands) that show as soft grey lines just off the screen. It is live theatre, not stop-motion: the screen **flickers with the lamp**, puppets **tremble in the hand**, loose parts **swing on their rivets**.\n\nNot a black silhouette film (colour is the second protagonist), not paper-cut (the light comes *through*), not stained glass (no lead lines; pieces move and overlap), not flat vector cut-out animation.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n**Rendering model.** Per pixel: `image = tonemap( lampLight × hideTransmission × clothTexture )`.\n- `hideTransmission`: a 2D canvas that starts **white**; every piece is drawn with `multiply`. Holes stay white, overlaps darken by themselves.\n- `lampLight`: per lamp a broad lobe plus a tight hotspot (~6 : 1), each flickering independently (a few % of ~7 Hz value noise plus a faster sine). Warm light, never white.\n- `tonemap = 1 − exp(−hdr)`, exposure ∝ `1/√(lamps lit)`: more lamps bleach the frame but keep it readable. Lamp count is a dial the story can turn.\n- **Cloth**: procedural weave (sin × sin, noise-warped), fibre speckle, low-frequency density blotches, darker edges at the frame. **Post**: warm-tinted bloom, a **warm vignette** (toward brown, never grey), slight saturation, soft S-curve, light grain; heat haze only when the story burns.\n\n**Hide and carving.** Multiply a tileable hide texture (mottling, fibres, specks) into each piece and restore its alpha with `destination-in` so holes survive. **Carving is density**: leave thin strips, cut out most of the area. Cut vocabulary: crescent rows (scales, tiles), diamond lattice, coin holes (round hole, square of hide inside), cloud collars and scrolls, tapered slits (cracks, rain, fur), strata along a ridge, leaf holes in a canopy. A dark carved outline (1.5–2.4 px) on every region. With `destination-out`, set `fillStyle = '#000'` first: fill alpha decides how much is cut.\n\n**Figures are jointed puppets**: 8–12 pieces, head ~1.2× natural with large headwear, sleeves in a different dye from the torso, round joint lobes with a rivet (dark ring + pale knot), arms by 2-bone IK from hand targets, held objects as separate pieces on the hand line. Sympathetic figures have *open-cut faces* (features as thin strips around a hole); antagonists solid faces.\n\n**Scenery** is carved set pieces on mostly empty lit cloth: one lace piece per element, a narrow carved ground strip, separate liftable strips for water or sky. Fire, smoke and moving water are carved pieces on rods, swapped and shaken, never particles.\n\n**Stage and backstage.** A lacquered frame (lintel, pillars, warm spill on its inner edges), optional blurred audience heads. Backstage = the same transmission canvas with the camera mirrored (`x → −x`), dark wood around, hanging lamps (additive flame glow; snuffed ones trailing dark smoke), hands and sleeves as **silhouettes with a warm rim** (mask minus mask shifted away from the light), rods sharp.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- All colour is **transmitted**: a dye filters warm lamplight, so nothing is brighter than the lamp and every hue leans warm.\n- 3–5 saturated dyes + **raw hide** (the neutral) + **ink** (outlines, hair, boots). Holes are the brightest value.\n- Overlap is darkness: plan which pieces cross.\n- Brightness comes from the number of lamps, not grading. Night is fewer lamps, never added blue.\n- Thin things that must read (strings, shafts, text) are dark: pale pieces vanish on a bright cloth.\n- Example sets: *court* (vermilion, ochre, malachite); *river* (indigo, jade, one ochre); *harvest* (flame orange, gold, one green).\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- Subtitles belong to the theatre: a **storyteller's placard** (narrow dark-lacquer board, thin gold line, small carved seal), a label on a rod, or letters carved into hide. Classical OFL serif (e.g. Cormorant Garamond 600), 40–50 px, cream on dark, short slide in. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- **Titles are puppets**: dark hide plaques with letters cut out (`fillText` + `destination-out`, so words glow), a carved border, carried on rods, pressed on to enter, lifted off to exit.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **24 fps continuous** for puppets and camera; only swapped effect pieces (fire, water) step at 12 fps.\n- **Hand tremor** on every puppet (a few px, ~0.02 rad, low-frequency), stronger under tension.\n- Body glides with the main rod; **legs, plumes, tassels are simulated** (damped pendulums / spring chains driven by body acceleration, pre-simulated at a fixed rate for determinism).\n- Operatic phrasing: shuffling run → hop → crouch → **freeze pose on a percussion accent** (~1 s, only loose parts quiver) → head turn.\n- Faces never change; attitude = head angle + body tilt (up = resolve, down = sorrow).\n- **Press on / lift off**: arrivals come large and blurred onto the cloth and snap sharp (~0.5 s); exits grow, blur and fade (~1 s).\n- A new lamp flares with overshoot; a snuffed lamp gutters with fast flicker.\n- Never moves: the cloth, the frame. Nothing morphs: pieces rotate on rivets, slide on rods, or are swapped.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | Expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Audience seat (whole stage, heads below) | \"This is a show\" | a legend; a lesson; a framed memory |\n| Push through the frame onto the cloth | Entering the tale | a story within a story; focus |\n| Lateral pan across a wide cloth | Extent, a place laid out | a journey; a disaster's reach; a procession |\n| Follow a puppet, slight zoom | Being with one figure | a pursuit; a working day |\n| **Crash zoom** of a few frames on a percussion hit | The drum becomes the camera | an entrance; a pose; a reveal |\n| Whip pan along a moving piece | Cause to effect | a message sent; a strike |\n| **Locked wide** | Every step of a change is seen | counting; repetition; stages |\n| Medium-close, empty cloth between two pieces | Distance, hesitation | a choice; a parting |\n| Close on a carved detail | Craft as meaning | a vow; a tool; a clue |\n| Tilt from ground strip to top of cloth | Height, hierarchy | a tower; heaven and earth |\n| Truck round to the back (mirror view) | The trick revealed | behind the scenes; who pulls the strings |\n| Pull back from cloth into the dark room | The tale becomes an object | aftermath; a story ending for the night |\n\nPuppets in profile; most of the cloth stays empty. When zoomed in, clamp the camera to the screen rectangle (no black bars); push on frame centre unless diving into a lamp. Transitions: lamps dimming, a blurred foreground pillar wipe, a piece lifting off in light, a flash to warm white; never a generic crossfade or digital wipe.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Percussion first, not piano and strings** (gong-and-drum pattern thinking, 锣鼓经): low barrel drum, hard high clapper drum (woodblock), cymbals, small gong with rising pitch (\"tai\"), big gong with falling pitch (\"kuang\"); also a bamboo clapper board, pellet drum, sanxian pluck, a bamboo flute for quiet.\n- **Bowed and reed voices**: a bright nasal fiddle (erhu-like: pitched up, nasal formant, pitch-curve resampling for slides and vibrato), plain pentatonic (**huan-yin**) or sorrowful microtonal (**ku-yin**: raised 4th, lowered 7th); a shrill suona-like reed (oboe + 1.2–3 kHz formant + saturation) at most once.\n- **Techniques, as options**: a single tap inside a silence; one big gong per decisive hit; a heartbeat drum under dread; accelerating rolls into an entrance; one hit per visual step; a gong scrape to change space; a hard stop (≤ 30 ms, tails included).\n- **Silence**: hard-cut the whole bed; at most one sound inside (a breath, a tap, a sizzle).\n- **Foley follows the materials**: wooden clapper (醒木), match strike, lamp \"fwoomp\" and \"pfft\" + sizzle, rod taps on cloth, dry leather flaps on lift-off, leather steps, creaks of hide and wood props, leather thwacks, cloth rustle, backstage room tone.\n- **Voice**: a storyteller, few short lines, small-theatre reverb; music ducked ~12 dB, foley ~4 dB, voice ≥ 12 dB above the bed; −14 LUFS; check each line with whisper alone and on the final mix.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs; put the strongest at the peak.\n\n- **The light is the world.** Lamp count drives heat and colour. *Fits content like:* city lights spreading at night; a fever rising and falling; servers switching on one by one.\n- **Off the screen = big and blurry.** A piece pulled toward the lamp grows and blurs. *Fits content like:* a rumour growing; a memory fading; a deadline pressing onto the cloth and snapping sharp.\n- **Go behind the screen.** Lamp, hands, rods. *Fits content like:* how a product is really made; who controls a market; a teacher revealing the method.\n- **Carved and translucent.** Holes glow, overlaps darken, open faces vs solid. *Fits content like:* an x-ray of a system; good and bad actors in a fable; layers of a city.\n- **Hand-held.** Tremor and swinging parts. *Fits content like:* a nervous interview; a tightrope; a toddler's first steps.\n- **Piece swap.** One carved piece on a rod replaced by another. *Fits content like:* seasons; a machine's states; weather over a harvest.\n- **Strip lift-off.** A band of scenery lifted to reveal another. *Fits content like:* geological layers; before/after a flood; an old town under a new one.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Flat clip-art look → hide texture, denser openwork, deeper dyes, carved outlines everywhere.\n- Figures unreadable in profile → bigger head, fuller lower body, collar piece, contrasting sleeves.\n- Radiating shapes read as saw blades or petals → flame tongues with a fat base, belly and hooked tip, alternating curl.\n- Grey corners when overexposed → warm vignette.\n- Thin pale pieces vanish → make them dark.\n- Mirror view flips pieces when rotation uses the x scale for both axes → x scale for x, y scale for y.\n- Smoke with `lighter` is invisible on bright cloth → dark wisps, warm additive only near a lamp.\n- Push-in centred on an edge lamp shoves figures out → push on frame centre.\n- Beds leaking into a silence → hard-cut them.\n- Headless screenshots time out under GPU load → retry.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `carve.js` (piece sprites, `cut` / `cutLine` / `cutTaper`, pattern library, hide texture, rivets), `gl.js` (WebGL2 compositor: lamp field × transmission × cloth, mirror, bloom, haze, warm fade), `stage.js` (frame, audience, pillar wipe, cut-letter plaques), `backstage.js` (rim-lit silhouettes, flame, smoke), `hud.js` (placard), `scenery.js` (landscape pieces, ground strip, fire), plus the jointed rig with IK and pendulum parts; `?test=model|frame|hands` draws sheets from the real engine. A first test unlike the demo: carve a lantern-seller with a lattice basket, press it onto one lamp, light three more and watch the frame bleach.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the story, the figures (or none), what the lamp means, what leaves the screen, the opening, the ending, the camera path, the dyes and the pacing. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a procession** (one long pan; each figure adds a dye until the cloth is a full street); **two puppeteers** (rival hands argue through their puppets; backstage shows who is winning); **a workshop** (a puppet is carved, dyed and riveted on the cloth, then performs its own making).\n- Openings: **backstage first** (rim-lit hands and rods before the front); **one hole of light** (the screen all dark hide; one carved hole glows and widens); **mid-show** (the camera finds a performance already in full percussion).\n- Endings: **the lamp is carried away** (the cloth darkens from one side); **the puppets rest** (lifted off one by one, laid flat on a dim table); **the audience leaves** (the heads stand and go, the cloth still glowing).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nHou Yi Shoots the Suns\n"},{"id":"S4-L25","name":"浮世绘","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L25.jpg","markdown":"# L25 浮世绘（Ukiyo-e）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 东方传统\n- 长什么样：旅人走向远山，每个镜头都是一幅版画；巨浪是高潮，结尾是远山与整幅画轴拉远。\n- 适合：日式、海浪\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Ukiyo-e — Style Prompt\n\n> Edo-period woodblock prints brought to life: flat colour blocks, carved ink lines, bokashi skies, paper you can feel, and a camera that moves over the print like an unrolling handscroll.\n> References (grammar only): Hokusai's *Thirty-six Views* (one constant subject placed differently each time; bold geometric cropping); Hiroshige's *Fifty-three Stations* and *Hundred Famous Views* (small figures, rain lines, extreme foreground framing); the anime *Mononoke* (2007) (print texture alive in motion, flat layers sliding, seals as screen language). Never reproduce a specific famous print (no copy of *The Great Wave*, *Red Fuji*, *Sudden Shower over Ōhashi*), and never copy their characters or compositions.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nEvery shot is **a complete woodblock print**: a sheet of washi, a thin double ink border, flat areas of colour printed from separate blocks, a black key-block line on top, a vertical title cartouche and a red seal. The world is flat on purpose. Depth comes from stacked horizontal layers, mist bands and bold cropping, never from perspective rendering, lighting or shading.\n\nWhat makes it read as *printed* rather than *vector*:\n- **Paper** is always visible: warm washi, long fibres, slight edge ageing.\n- **Each colour is its own block**, slightly **out of register** (1.5–2.5 px) with the key line.\n- Large colour areas show **woodgrain** and **baren rubbing** (uneven ink density, circular streaks) and fine **goma-zuri** specks.\n- **Bokashi**, hand-wiped gradients, in skies and water.\n\nNot a flat vector illustration with a paper overlay (no woodgrain, no misregistration), not Chinese ink wash (no bleeding washes, colour is printed, not painted), not anime cel shading (no light and shadow on forms).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **The sheet is the frame**: the print fills the screen at zoom 1, inside a thick + thin ink border, with a paper margin beyond. Sheets can sit on a long mounting scroll (darker tan paper, a roller at one end), so the camera can travel from print to print.\n- **Blocks**: draw each colour group into its own offscreen canvas, cached once (key lines; blues; greens / secondary; reds / figures). Composite each with a small fixed registration offset (1.5–2.5 px, a different direction per block).\n- **Texture every colour block**: woodgrain (`source-atop`, strongest in sky and water) → baren streaks → goma-zuri specks (both `destination-out`); the key block gets specks only; light paper fibres over the finished sheet so paper shows through the ink. Big dynamic fills get the same textures inside their clip. Woodgrain = iso-lines of a warped noise field; baren = blurred circular arcs + zigzag streaks (recipe and alphas in [DEMO.md](DEMO.md#palette--props)).\n- **Bokashi** is generated per pixel: a gradient whose top edge wanders by low-frequency noise per column (±20–30 px). Never strips of `fillRect` (vertical seams).\n- **Line**: key lines 2.4–3 px; large contours are variable-width filled strokes, tapered at both ends, with ±20–35 % low-frequency width jitter. Distant things get a grey key line or none.\n- **Motifs of the medium**: mist as long rounded flat bands (suyari-gasumi) fading at both ends; pines with flat-topped **needle pads**; fields as high, flat, parallel layers whose cross lines are **parallel and slanted**, never converging; rain as two sets of fine straight lines at slightly different angles under a sumi bokashi sky; water as banded blues with bokashi between bands, foam and spray left as paper.\n- **Seal**: characters cut out of red, chipped edges. **Figures** are small in the landscape (roughly 80–130 px at full sheet), flat, with a clear silhouette; faces in a few strokes.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **A pigment set, not a colour wheel**: 6–9 flat hues, each printed from its own block, over the paper ground. No gradients except bokashi, no shading on forms.\n- **Sumi key line** over everything; **paper is the white** (no white pigment for foam, snow, mist, blank sky).\n- **One dominant hue family** carries sky and water (in the 19th-century prints, Prussian blue); **red is rare**: seals, a sun, one garment or tab.\n- Far layers pale and low-contrast, near layers saturated with dark key lines.\n- Examples, one per film: an indigo-and-beni landscape (Prussian blue, pale blue, beni red, ochre, greens, sumi); a bijin-ga interior (pale pink, mauve, grey-green, lacquer black, a mica-grey ground); an autumn surimono (persimmon, ochre, grey-green, deep brown, one blue).\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Subtitle = a horizontal cartouche**: a cream slip with a thick + thin ink border and a small red seal at the left; Shippori Mincho (500) in sumi, centred near the bottom. It \"prints\" in with the reveal mask and fades out. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s). It covers the bottom ~150 px: keep ground lines and figures above it.\n- **View cartouche**: vertical cream slip, thick + thin border, a pale tab with the series title, the view title below in a brush font (e.g. Yuji Syuku), a seal beside: the \"full stop\" of each view. Titles live on the print as cartouches and seals, never as floating type.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- Prints are still. **Most of the frame does not move**; each view has one or two small actions (steam, rain, a lantern, a falling leaf).\n- **Figures and nature step at 8 fps**; rain and fast water at **12 fps**; **the camera moves on ones (24 fps)**. A stepped camera judders.\n- **Printing reveal**: each block appears over ~0.3 s with a diagonal baren sweep with a noisy edge, sliding 5–6 px into registration as it lands (pure noise looks like mould).\n- Cartouches drop in (fade + a short slide); a seal *stamps* (scale ~1.35 → 1 in 0.2 s).\n- A native move needs a **held beat of about a second** to be read; a peak flashed in under a second reads as noise.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nThe camera moves *over* prints and between them; it never rotates in 3D space inside one. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Locked frame on one sheet | Reading a print on a wall | a place; a portrait; a still moment |\n| Handscroll slide, right → left through the mounting gap (shorter slides = urgency) | Travel, the next station | a journey; a sequence of steps; a race |\n| Very slow push toward a framing device (a window, a fan, a gate) | Attention narrowing | discovery; longing; a detail that matters |\n| Tilt past the border into the mount | Something larger than the print | a flood; a rumour; a giant |\n| Pull-back from one sheet to many | The series seen whole | a summary; a timeline; a collection |\n| Crash-zoom into the texture (grain, specks, fibres) | The medium itself becomes the image | a dream; a breakdown; a memory dissolving |\n| Pan across a triptych (three sheets side by side) | One scene too wide for one sheet | a procession; a battle; a festival |\n| Hard cut to a blank sheet | Ma (間), a held breath | aftermath; a pause before a decision |\n\nFraming: bold cropping and extreme foreground elements (a branch, a post, a lantern crossing the frame) are native; depth by stacking, never by perspective. Allowed transitions: scroll slides, printing reveals, a wash back to paper, a cut to blank. No cross-dissolves, no 3D flips.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Japanese instruments, not piano and pads**: shamisen (`core/audio/pluck.py shamisen`, with sawari buzz), shakuhachi, koto, taiko and shime-daiko, kotsuzumi / ōtsuzumi calls, hyōshigi (wooden clappers), a small bell.\n- **Modes**: miyako-bushi (in scale), yō, ritsu; long breathy tones with a slide into each note and *meri* drops at phrase ends suit the shakuhachi; the shamisen can hold a pulse or an ostinato.\n- **Techniques, as options**: absolute silence as *ma* (cut reverb tails too); a single sawari note left ringing; free-rhythm breath phrases against a steady pulse; clappers that accelerate (the kabuki curtain call); a taiko heartbeat that tightens into a roll; a koto glissando for a turn of season; a drum call (*kakegoe*) before an entrance.\n- **Foley follows the material**: paper (baren rub = band-passed noise with a slow circular modulation; scroll slide; seal = low thump + tacky peel), wood (block set down, hollow steps), glass (wind chime), water, birds and insects of the season.\n- **Voice**: calm, few short lines, like a poem written beside the print. Duck music ~8 dB under voice, ambience ~4 dB. Loudness −14 LUFS; `mux.sh` grain **0** (the paper is the grain).\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **A series of views.** One constant subject in different compositions; the story is told by *where it sits in the frame* each time. *Fits content like:* one building through a century; a product in five homes; the same street in four seasons.\n- **The handscroll.** Scenes don't cut; the camera slides right to left and the next print arrives. *Fits content like:* a supply chain; the stations of a pilgrimage; the steps of a recipe.\n- **Printing, block by block.** Key line first, then each colour lands slightly off and snaps into register. *Fits content like:* a building going up; an idea taking shape; a character being \"made\" for the story.\n- **Paper is the white.** Foam, snow, mist and blank sky are unprinted paper; white can flood the frame and wash the print back to a blank sheet. *Fits content like:* a snowfall that erases a town; forgetting; a clean start.\n- **The frame is an object.** Border, cartouche and seal are physical; something big enough breaks out of the border into the mount. *Fits content like:* a festival that can't be contained; a legend; a runaway success.\n- **Alternate impressions.** The same key block reprinted with different colour blocks (day → night, spring → winter). *Fits content like:* before and after; a city lit at night; two lives in one house.\n- **The triptych.** A scene split across three sheets that join. *Fits content like:* three generations; a panorama of a harbour; a debate between three voices.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Bokashi from `fillRect` strips leaves vertical seams → build it per pixel.\n- Too much speckle or fibre makes dark areas look like sandpaper or scratched film → specks α ≈ 0.3, fibres α ≈ 0.09.\n- A perspective grid for fields looks digital → parallel, slanted, flat layering.\n- Pine clusters drawn as half-discs look like parasols → flat pads with needle fans.\n- Offset curves self-intersect at tight bends → build inner lines from the gentle side of the contour; force control points x-monotonic.\n- Breaking out of the print must escape **both** the sheet clip and the frame clip.\n- A seal drawn with `multiply` vanishes on dark ground → `source-over` there.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `print.js` (paper, woodgrain, baren, specks, blocks, bokashi, carved lines, reveal masks, cartouche, seals: the reusable core), `nature.js` (mountains, pines, rocks, rain, birds), `wave.js` (banded water, fractal claws, spray), `traveler.js` (flat figure rig), `views.js` (prints as cached blocks + per-frame `paint()`), `main.js` (camera, scroll, reveal, subtitles), `music/score.py`. Instrument recipes and commands: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md#build-notes).\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the constant subject (or none), the number of views, the characters, the opening, the ending, the camera path, the pacing and the pigment set. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **alternate impressions** (one key block of a single street, reprinted for dawn, rain, festival night and snow); **a triptych unfolding** (three sheets revealed one at a time, the story only readable when all three join); **a print being made** (the carver's blocks, one per shot, each block's colour telling one chapter).\n- Openings: **a close crop** (an extreme foreground detail, a lantern or a sleeve, fills the sheet; the pull-back finds the print); **the bare block** (carved wood inked, the paper laid on, the baren rubs; lifted to reveal the first print); **the mount first** (a closed scroll unrolls to its first sheet).\n- Endings: **a seal alone** (the print fades back to paper, only the red seal remains); **the album closes** (sheets stack like pages, the cover lands); **the last print left unfinished** (key line only, colour blocks never land).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nToward the Mountain\n"},{"id":"S4-L26","name":"红色窗花剪纸","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L26.jpg","markdown":"# L26 红色窗花剪纸（Red Paper-cut）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 东方传统\n- 长什么样：除夕年兽进村，小女孩剪出的大窗花照亮全村，把它吓跑。\n- 适合：年味、中国风\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Red Paper-cut — Style Prompt\n\n> Chinese window-flower paper-cuts brought to life: jointed red cut-outs on paper grounds, where the cut *is* the drawing and the holes are where the light gets in.\n> References (grammar only): folk window flowers of Shaanxi and Yuxian (sawtooth, crescent, cloud and swirl cuts, fold-symmetric rosettes, *yin* and *yang* cutting); the 1958–59 Shanghai Animation Film Studio cut-paper shorts (jointed figures, profile acting, paper layers in parallax). Copy no studio character, no named artist's work and no real motif sheet: generate every pattern from code, and never name the references in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nEvery visible thing is **a piece of paper that was cut with scissors** and laid flat on another sheet. There is no painting, no gradient shading, no outline stroke: detail, volume and expression come only from **what was cut away**. Red paper is the hero material; grounds are paper too (a light paper by day, a deep dyed paper by night). Characters are **jointed cut-outs** (head, torso, upper and lower limbs pinned at rivets) animated in hard 12 fps steps.\n\nNot shadow puppetry: no light behind a screen, no translucent dyed hide, no rods. Here the paper is **front-lit and opaque**; backlight is a special event you save for the moment that needs it. Not flat vector art (no strokes, no gradients), not a silhouette film (every shape's interior is cut).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Paper grain:** a neutral-grey texture in `soft-light` inside each piece (dye mottling, fibres, specks), then the piece's alpha restored with `destination-in`, so holes stay holes.\n- **Cut edge:** every silhouette is a polyline with a slight low-frequency wobble (hand-cut), a 1 px light rim upper-left and a dark rim lower-right (the cut face). Architecture and machines use straight segments + wobble, never smoothing.\n- **Paper thickness:** each piece casts a tight soft shadow on the layer below; stacked folded paper shows 2–6 offset darker edges.\n- **Figures: red block + yin lines.** Every figure piece gets an **inset contour** (a thin slit a few units inside the outline): the classic double outline, which also separates red-on-red overlaps (an arm over a coat).\n- **Folk cutting vocabulary** (`demo/motifs.js`): sawtooth rows (fur, grass, tiles, fringe), serrated edges, crescents (scales, quilting), swirls (joints), clouds, rosettes, plum, coin, tapered slits.\n- **No floating islands.** A closed ring cut would drop its centre out of real paper. Eyes are two crescents (upper and lower lid) that leave bridges, plus a pupil *hole*.\n- Faces never morph: expressions are **swappable face pieces** (smile, surprise, fear, determined, asleep).\n- **The rosette (团花)** is designed as one 45° wedge and mirrored 8× (D4); other folds give 2×, 4× or 6×. Alternate rings of *yang* cut (band cut away, red lines left) and *yin* cut (lines cut out of a red field). Fold seams leave faint crease lines: keep them, they are true to the craft.\n- **Light:** front-lit scenes use the paper as is. Night: `frame × lightmap` + warm pools, then **emissive** backlit pieces (light × paper transmission via `multiply`; holes at full light), a two-level bloom and optional rays. Keep bloom low enough that the pattern stays legible at the centre.\n- **Projected pattern:** a piece's hole mask, squashed onto the ground and added with `lighter`; on a character, masked by its layer alpha over a solid warm spot, so cut features still read as holes.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **China red** (around `#d2201f`, range `#c8102e`–`#d7261e`) is the hero material. Depth and role are shown by **paper tones one step apart**, not by shading: near pieces one step brighter, far pieces one step darker, an antagonist or a heavy object one step deeper.\n- **Ground and hero must be different paper families.** If background and hero are both red, the silhouettes stop popping. At night, all background layers are one dark dyed family and only the pieces that matter to the story are red; by day, a light paper ground with red and pale-red layers.\n- **Gold foil** is an accent kept for one late moment (a sun, a title, a coin); never a second main colour.\n- Examples of ground families: indigo night papers (far layers lighter blue, near darker); a warm rice-paper day with pale-red far hills; a jade-green or black paper for a story that isn't festive. Choose from the topic.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Subtitles are a paper strip in the world**, e.g. a red banner (横批) darker than the picture's reds so cream text holds, serrated edges, swallow-tail ends; a sturdy OFL serif (e.g. Fraunces SemiBold) around 44 px, low, with a paper shadow. It drops in on two 12 fps steps, like a couplet being pasted. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.7 s); never overlap.\n- **Titles** are cut-out letters (*yang*-cut, counters are real holes) popping in letter by letter on 12 fps steps, with sawtooth border strips and a seal cut by code.\n- Small credits may be printed ink on paper.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Characters step at 12 fps** (pose sampled at `floor(t·12)/12`); **camera, light, weather, flames and flying pieces move on ones** at 24 fps.\n- Rigs pin pieces at rivets (neck, shoulders, elbows, hips, knees, jaws, tails). Hard poses, tiny eases, 1-frame overshoots (\"pop\" = 1.15× for one step). Weight comes from **size and camera shake**.\n- In profile, a raised near arm crosses the face: keep hands forward of the face or let a prop cover them.\n- **Folding:** the flap is split into ~16 strips; each is compressed by cosθ and enlarged along the fold by `1 + 0.28·height` (fake perspective), darkened as it stands up, in a lighter back colour past 90°, with a growing shadow and a crease after. About half a second per fold, each on a hit.\n- **Cutting:** the pattern is revealed through a mask of circles along the scissor path; the offcut drops once the outer edge is passed; blades open and close on a subdivision of the beat; scraps flutter out and land.\n- **Unfolding:** successive unfolds a fraction of a second apart, each flap swinging from folded to flat with ease-out (the \"pa!\" snap), then a scale bump and warm flash on the accent.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Extreme close-up tracking the scissor tip | making; attention to craft | a beginning of work; a precise act; a signature |\n| Flat front view, locked | the cut-out as an emblem | a title; a statement; a family portrait |\n| Slow pan across paper layers (parallax) | a world laid out | a village, a route, a timeline |\n| Low wide, a shape standing up from the ground layer | scale, threat | a storm; a rival; a deadline arriving |\n| Top-down tabletop | the craft as plot | folding, cutting, assembling, sorting |\n| Push toward a lit piece, pull back to many | one act spreading | influence; a network lighting up |\n| Page turn / sheet peel | time or place changing | day to night; before and after; chapters |\n| Pull back to an object in a room | the film was a thing someone made | memory; a gift; a tradition |\n| Rotation around a rosette's centre | order, repetition | a cycle; a team |\n\nFraming: layers stay parallel to the screen; depth comes from parallax and paper tones, never from perspective. Transitions are paper actions (fold, page turn, peel, a sheet flying off, a cut through the frame); no dissolves.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Foley follows the material:** scissors = metallic shear sweep (2.5–9 kHz) + a short ring + a fibre crack; folds = swish + crease tap; unfold = sharp snap; heavy steps = *cardboard* thud, not flesh; paper peel; pasting = a palm pat; match strike, puff; firecrackers = dense clicks 20–60 ms apart with low booms.\n- **Instruments:** a bright Chinese chamber ensemble, not piano and strings and not the opera percussion of shadow puppetry: pipa (tremolo on long notes), zheng (`dan_tranh`: broken chords, glissandi, tremolo), erhu (glides), dizi (flute), sheng or yangqin colours, woodblock, small and big gong, frame drum. Pentatonic modes.\n- Options: one snip = one beat subdivision; a rising zheng run, one note per thing that appears; a frame-drum heartbeat for weight; one big gong saved for one accent; total silence for a held breath.\n- **Voice:** warm storyteller, few lines. Duck music ≈ 9 dB and foley ≈ 3 dB under the voice; keep impacts out of voice windows. Folk names may need an `asr` alias in `lines.json`.\n- Mix to −14 LUFS, **no film grain** (the paper fibres are the texture).\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Fold – cut – unfold.** Fold a sheet 2/4/8 times; one cut becomes many. Put the unfold on a strong beat. *Fits content like:* one teacher, many students; a single design mass-produced; one seed becoming a field.\n- **Holes are light.** A cut-out lit from behind glows deep red, every hole glows, and the pattern is projected onto the world. *Fits content like:* an idea shining through a person; a lighthouse; a window at night in a story about home.\n- **Symmetry replicates.** A mirrored motif peels off and multiplies (rosette → window flowers → fireworks). *Fits content like:* a franchise; cell division; a song spreading through a crowd.\n- **Scraps have a future.** The bits that fall while cutting come back later (as snow, confetti, stars). *Fits content like:* recycling; leftover time; small failures that add up.\n- **Everything is one flat sheet.** Things share the same cuts, so a ridge can stand up and be a creature, or a wave become a road. *Fits content like:* a landscape that becomes a map; a factory that turns out to be one machine; a zoom out to the made object.\n- **Mirrored pair.** Two figures cut from one folded sheet. *Fits content like:* twins; a partnership; before/after of the same person.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **Red on red** reads as one blob → different ground family, near/far paper tones, inset contours for overlaps.\n- **Darkening a layer through the light map** also darkens what covers it → tint the layer itself (multiply + restore alpha).\n- **A flat cos-squashed flap reads as a sliding card**, not a fold → strips with fake perspective, darken as it stands up, show the back colour.\n- **Cut reveal shows the uncut stack through the holes** → stack thickness inside the \"uncut\" mask only; the cut part gets its own offset copies.\n- Scissors pointing backwards → the blade axis follows the direction of travel, the rivet a little behind the cutting point.\n- Blank frames between shots → overlap outgoing and incoming paper actions.\n- Engine traps (`put()` transforms, piece boxes, test pages) are in DEMO.md build notes.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `paper.js` (paper textures, piece builder: fill, cut, inset, edge, grain, shadows, transmission), `motifs.js` (folk pattern library), `rig.js` (2D affine rig helpers), `tuanhua.js` (8-fold rosette from one wedge × D4), `light.js` (night lightmap, backlit pieces, bloom, rays, masked projected light), `fold.js` (fold – cut – unfold sequence), `hud.js` (subtitle banner, cut-letter text); `?test=` pages draw model sheets from the real engine. A first test not in the demo: a bicycle from `paper.js` pieces (`sawRow` spokes), wheels riveted in `rig.js`, rolling on 12 fps steps.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the structure, the cast (or none), the sets, the paper families, the opening, the ending, the camera path, the pacing and which native moves carry the story. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a year of twelve cuts** (a zodiac or calendar wheel, one wedge per month, rotating); **an assembly line** (a product built piece by piece from cut parts, each part a pattern); **a letter** (a folded message unfolds panel by panel, each panel a scene).\n- Openings: **a finished pattern** (a full rosette, then one piece falls out and walks away); **an empty ground** (a blank sheet, one fold crease appears); **a crowd of cut figures** already dancing, parallax full.\n- Endings: **refold** (the world folds back into a small square that is handed to someone); **scraps settle** (every offcut drifts down into a new pattern); **backlit only** (the lights go off except behind one cut piece, which projects the last image on the wall).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nNian Comes to Town\n"},{"id":"S4-L27","name":"纸雕灯影","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L27.jpg","markdown":"# L27 纸雕灯影（Paper-cut Lightbox）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 东方传统\n- 长什么样：一枚月饼讲中秋的团圆与思念，纸雕灯箱层层透光。\n- 适合：一层层透光，温暖\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Paper-cut Lightbox — Style Prompt\n\n> A layered paper-cut shadow box (纸雕灯) lit from behind: 6–9 sheets of cut card stacked in a shallow box, a warm light glowing through the paper, windows and moons burning through holes, soft shadows falling from each layer onto the next. The frame is the box; the camera is a macro lens inside a lamp.\n> References (grammar only): commercial paper-cut shadow-box lamps (纸雕灯 / \"shadow box lightbox\") for the layer stack and backlight; Lotte Reiniger's silhouette films for the cut-out, jointed figure language; Chinese 剪纸 motifs (auspicious clouds, eaves, mountains) as one possible vocabulary. Do not copy any specific lamp design, film frame or character.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nEvery shot is **one physical object**: a shallow box (a cavity about 0.56 × 0.33 m, 0.16 m deep) holding parallel sheets of cut paper. Everything on screen is one of three things:\n\n1. **A paper layer**: a flat cut sheet, one flat colour, fibre grain, a thin warm **rim of light on its top edge** and a **dark band on its bottom edge**. Back layers lighter and cooler, the front almost black: that value ladder *is* the depth.\n2. **Light through paper**: a warm, cloudy translucency (pulp mottling), strongest near the light source. Windows, lanterns, trails of light are **holes / emissive windows** that burn and bloom.\n3. **Shadows**: a top spot casts **soft shadows** from every layer onto the layers behind. This sells \"real paper in a real box\"; never turn it off.\n\nThe film may step out of the box into the **real world** (the lit lamp on a table, spill light on nearby objects) to say \"this is an object someone keeps\"; whether, where and how often is a choice of the film.\n\nNot flat paper-cut animation (here: depth, backlight, cast shadows), not shadow puppetry (no single screen, no rods), not a 3D diorama (every element is a flat sheet parallel to the box front).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Units in metres, y up.** Layers from z ≈ −0.14 (sky) to 0 (front); camera at z ≈ 0.3–0.56, fov ~26°. Full-bleed sheets are **never larger than the cavity**.\n- **A layer** is a Canvas2D drawing in metre space mapped to a plane; cut-outs are `destination-out` holes. Finish on every sheet: fibre grain (`source-atop`), a warm top rim and a dark bottom shade, each shifted a few px.\n- **Backlight shader**: `emissive += albedo × lightCol × trans × lit × falloff × (base + cloud)`, with a Gaussian falloff around a per-shot light point and a tiled pulp-mottle texture. `trans` per layer goes from ~.9 (far) to ~.05 (front).\n- **Emissive windows**: a second canvas (white = light) gated by a 0→1 \"switch on\" uniform; bloom makes them burn. Titles, lights and curtains in front neither cast nor receive shadows.\n- **Lens**: shallow depth of field, bloom, a gentle vignette; supersample 2× so cut edges stay crisp.\n- **Real-world set** (when used): wood frame, black lining, a dark room, an area light as the box's spill onto real objects.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **Back layers bright and translucent, front layers dark and opaque.** Each layer is one flat colour; the ladder of values from back to front is the depth. Never break it (a pale front layer flattens the box).\n- **One temperature per stack**, chosen by the scene (cool night, warm interior, violet or green elsewhere). Changing ladder is how the film says \"another place\".\n- **Light is warm** (amber to gold) against the stack, and it is the only saturated element besides one accent.\n- **One accent colour** (a seal red, or a single object's colour) marks what the story is about. A second accent only when the story has two sides (one each).\n- Examples of ladders: night village — `#0a1330` sky → `#6a86c2` far → `#3f5a92` mid → `#0e1532` front, windows `#ffb862`; desert caravan — `#f0c890` dusk sky → `#c07850` dunes → `#6a3020` → `#1e0e08`, fires `#ffd080`; winter forest — `#dfe6ee` → `#9aaec4` → `#4a6078` → `#101820`, one red scarf.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Text in the picture is paper**: titles, letters, poems and captions are cut strips inside the box, revealed by growing from an anchor edge (scale the texture repeat with the mesh so glyphs aren't squashed), with an optional small seal.\n- Chinese: an OFL brush font (e.g. Ma Shan Zheng) for titles, a running script for handwriting, Noto Serif SC for subtitles. Latin: an OFL serif (e.g. Cormorant) at similar weight.\n- **Subtitles** are a quiet burned-in line under the box (serif 500, ~40 px CJK / ~38 px Latin, warm off-white, soft shadow, ~74 px from the bottom), fading in just before the line. Split long lines at the comma nearest the middle (~21 CJK / ~42 Latin characters).\n- **Words that appear as paper are not subtitled**: never show the same words twice.\n- Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Everything moves like paper on a stick**: layers slide (x/y), rotate about a pivot, scale in from 0 along one axis (\"written out\"), or light up. No squash and stretch, no morphing, no bending.\n- **Jointed figures**: a body sheet + separate limb sheets anchored at the joint; animate rotation only.\n- **Light cues are the big motion**: the source, then each layer's translucency in a ~0.4 s stagger, then windows, then flicker (`1 + .06·sin(7.3t)·sin(3.1t)`).\n- **Hard action lands on spoken words** (use whisper word timings): an object slams, a character flashes, a light arc grows on its word.\n- Hand-held micro-drift on the camera (~0.6 mm) keeps a locked frame alive. Render on ones at 30 fps; never step.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. The lens is small and the box is shallow: moves are short. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Slow dolly in, nearly frontal | intimacy; being drawn into a place | a memory; a detail that matters; a lit window |\n| Slow dolly out | context; loneliness; scale | a figure alone; before/after; the whole place at once |\n| Rack focus between layers | attention moving in depth | two people near and far; a clue behind; now and then |\n| Lateral travel with depth parallax | journey; time passing | a road trip; a process in stages; a timeline |\n| Top-down layer stack | a made thing seen as a craftsman sees it | a recipe; a letter; a map; a product being assembled |\n| Pull out of / push into the box | the frame as object; \"this is a story told\" | a keepsake; a framing device; returning to the present |\n| Locked frame with only light changing | waiting; the passage of a day or a night | a vigil; seasons; a city going to sleep |\n| Tilt up/down the stack | climbing; falling; sky vs ground | a mountain; a well; hope and weight |\n| Near-plane occluder wipe | a curtain between scenes | chapter changes; dreams; a secret revealed |\n\nFraming: the story layer in focus, foreground and far layers soft; box edges fully out of frame inside, clearly in frame outside, never half. Transitions: dissolves (0.5–1.2 s), an iris, light off/on, a near-plane curtain whose halves meet exactly. No whip pans, no digital zooms.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Instruments** that sit like light behind paper: felt piano, music box, celesta, harp or plucked zithers, bamboo flutes, soft strings; a solo instrument of the topic's culture. No drum kit; percussion only as single soft strokes (woodblock, small gong, hand drum).\n- **Foley is paper and light**: switch click + rising shimmer when a light turns on; paper rustle and slide; card-stock tap; lid thud; chimes on reveals; soft whoosh for curtains; beds from the scene (river, rails, city hum, crickets, snow wind).\n- **Silence**: the lamp switched off — all sound but a room tone drops out, then returns with the light.\n- **Mix character**: voice compressed and forward (RMS ~−18 dB), music underneath with ~5–6 dB of ducking under speech, foley low (−24…−34 dB), peak-limited, final at −14 LUFS.\n- **Options**: a new instrument entering when the film changes place; one foley sound per light cue; a music hand-over solved backwards so a swell lands on the line that matters.\n- **Voice**: warm, a storyteller at a table, not an announcer. Proof every line with whisper; for Chinese polyphonic characters feed TTS a homophone and keep the real text for subtitles.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **The lamp turns on.** Source, then far layers, then windows light up one by one. *Fits content like:* a shop opening at dawn; a startup's first customer; a stadium before a match.\n- **Two windows, one light.** Two lit places sharing one light source (a moon, a lighthouse, a satellite). *Fits content like:* a long-distance friendship; two labs working on the same problem; a sister city pair.\n- **Depth as distance.** A stack of ranges or streets makes \"far away\" visible; travel moves layers at speeds proportional to depth. *Fits content like:* a migration route; shipping a parcel; the history of a river.\n- **Paper that writes itself.** A strip grows from its edge on the spoken word. *Fits content like:* a recipe; the terms of a treaty; a child's first word.\n- **The box as keepsake.** The camera finds the real lamp in a room, real objects lit by its spill. *Fits content like:* a wedding anniversary; a product unboxing; a museum object's story.\n- **Light burst from an opened thing.** A lid lifts and analytic glow spills out. *Fits content like:* a gift; a discovery; a new idea.\n- **Silhouette in a window.** One jointed figure inside a lit frame. *Fits content like:* someone working late; a portrait of a craftsperson; a patient waiting.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- `destination-out` cut-outs need an **opaque fillStyle** before punching, or the hole is half-cut (`props.js` `cut(x, fn)` does it).\n- Soft shadows bleed and halo: layers that must not take part need **both** `shadow: false` and `recv: false`.\n- A transparent radial-gradient texture shows a square edge as an additive glow → use an analytic shader falloff (`burst()`).\n- A near-plane curtain whose halves don't meet at their **front edges** leaves a slit of the old shot.\n- Sheets larger than the cavity poke through the frame in outside shots.\n- **Grade state must not carry over** between shots: every grade key a shot omits must fall back to a default each frame, or renders depend on which frame a worker started on (visible pops at worker boundaries).\n- Headless Chrome must run with GPU flags; SwiftShader is ~6× slower.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/src/`: `paper.js` (`sheet()` = one lit paper layer; `skyPanel`, `moon`, `text`, `burst`), `stage.js` (scene, top spot, `aim()` camera), `post.js` (DoF, bloom, transitions, grade), `room.js` (the real box and table), `art.js` / `people.js` / `props.js` (motifs, jointed figures, props), `lib.js`. `src/main.js` schedules shots, each `build(E) → { S, update(t), post(t), grade(t) }`. API table and a minimal shot: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md) \"Engine reference\".\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the places, the figures (or none), the ladders, the light source, whether the real world appears, the opening, the ending and the camera path. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **one window, one year** (a locked lamp; the scene inside changes season by season, light the only transition); **a vertical stack** (the film tilts down through sky, city, street, underground, each a chapter); **a relay of objects** (a single object passes hand to hand across five small boxes, each a different ladder).\n- Openings: **already lit, mid-scene** (a figure walking through a lit street; we learn it is paper only when the camera racks focus); **darkness and one hole** (a single pinprick of light widens into a moon, a door, an eye); **top-down** (hands' view of a sheet being cut; the cut sheet slides into the stack).\n- Endings: **the lights go out one by one**, the last window last; **rack focus to the front layer** until the story blurs into warm light; **a new sheet slides in** at the front, starting the next story.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nA Mooncake's Longing\n"},{"id":"S4-L28","name":"Risograph 丝网印刷","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L28.jpg","markdown":"# L28 Risograph 丝网印刷（Risograph Print）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 印刷与版画\n- 长什么样：周日早晨骑车穿过城市：面包店、公园、河边，每到一处加一版油墨，最后三版套印。\n- 适合：杂志感、潮\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Risograph Print — Style Prompt\n\n> Animation that looks printed, frame by frame, on a stencil duplicator: two or three translucent spot inks overprinted on warm paper, halftone dots, ink grain, and plates that never quite line up.\n> References (grammar only): Tom Haugomat's riso illustrations (colour fields and silhouettes, a small figure in a large world, extra colours made by overprinting); Jacques Tati's *Jour de fête* (comedy staged small in a wide frame, everyday sounds as rhythm); real riso prints (translucent inks, misregistration, pinholes, feed streaks). Copy none of their characters, compositions or palettes.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nA risograph prints **one colour at a time**: each colour is its own stencil (\"plate\") with its own drum of translucent ink. That one fact makes the look:\n\n- **A tiny palette**: 2–3 spot inks on warm off-white paper.\n- **Overprint mixing**: inks multiply, so two inks make a third colour and all three approach black; a tint of one ink over a solid of another gives the brightest mixes.\n- **Misregistration**: every plate lands a few pixels off; edges show slivers of paper or a coloured halo.\n- **Halftone dots** for mid-tones, each plate on its own screen angle; **imperfect ink**: pinholes in solids, streaks along the feed direction, blotchy density.\n- **Flat shapes, no outlines**: forms are colour fields and silhouettes.\n\nNot flat vector art with a noise overlay (every colour comes from the plates), not CMYK pop-art halftone (no process colours, no keyline), not screen-print posters (riso ink is translucent and grainy, not opaque). **Never** show the RISO brand, logo or machine trade dress; ink names are colour references only.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n**Pipeline.** Draw each frame into **one RGB canvas where each channel is one plate's density** (R = ink 1, G = ink 2, B = ink 3; 0 = none, 1 = solid). `source-over` gives true **knockouts**; `globalCompositeOperation = 'lighter'` adds ink only to the channels you set: a true **overprint**. A WebGL2 shader \"prints\" it:\n\n- per plate: offset in px + tiny rotation → misregistration;\n- halftone: cosine spot function on a rotated grid, period ~6–8 px at 1080p, anti-aliased threshold; densities ≥ ~0.9 print solid; screen angles ≥ 15° apart;\n- grain on the dot threshold, pinholes in solids, density varying with a feed-direction streak and a slow blot noise;\n- composite `paper × Π mix(1, ink_i, coverage_i)`, with faint paper fibre;\n- dots anchored to the **paper** (screen), not to objects: a pan slides the image under a fixed screen, as if each frame were printed anew;\n- a per-plate **gate** and a **roller sweep** (moving edge, slightly heavier ink band) let a plate arrive mid-shot.\n\n**Composition rules.**\n- Every area uses **at most two plates, at most one of them as a tint**. Three halftones on top of each other = mud.\n- Big flat shapes, lots of solid paper and solid ink; halftone in a few stepped bands (e.g. 20 / 40 / 60 / 100 %) for skies, shadows, distance.\n- Characters get a **paper-white knockout halo** (4–5 px): stamp their layer in a ring with `filter: brightness(0)` (zero ink = paper), then draw them. Plates misregister around it, so it picks up coloured fringes like a real trap.\n- One horizon, one big flat field or disc, one small figure; the figure's head against the flattest colour in the frame.\n- Design for the **one-plate version**: every main colour contains some of the first plate, so a one-ink frame still reads as a complete silhouette. A deliberate exception stays blank paper until its plate arrives: a reveal.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **2–3 spot inks, never more**, plus paper; every other colour is an overprint or a tint. No gradients except stepped halftone.\n- Pick inks whose pairs mix into useful colours; at least one must carry a silhouette alone.\n- Paper is warm off-white (around `#F4EEE2`), never pure white; paper white is a colour too (knockouts, highlights, subtitles).\n- Light neutral surfaces (skin): a light tint of the palest ink. Dots of a saturated ink on skin look like a rash.\n- Save the full overprint (every plate, densest mixes) for the moment that earns it.\n- Example ink sets: Blue `#0078BF` + Yellow `#FFE800` + Fluorescent Pink `#FF48B0`; Teal `#00838A` + Fluorescent Orange `#FF7477`; Federal Blue `#3D5588` + Red `#FF665E` + Sunflower `#FFB511`.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Type is printed**: titles and subtitles live on one plate, so they misregister and grain with it. A title can be printed on two plates with a visible offset: the riso double-hit.\n- Fonts (OFL): heavy grotesque for titles (e.g. Bricolage Grotesque 800), geometric sans for captions (e.g. Jost 500–700). Preload every weight before `READY`.\n- Subtitles: a **paper-white knockout strip** (zero ink, small radius) with text on one plate, ~40–46 px; its edges catch misregistration fringes. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- Prints don't fade: titles arrive with a roller wipe or on a beat and leave on a cut.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Characters act on twos** (12 fps); **camera moves on ones** (24 fps): a stepped camera reads as judder.\n- **Registration is not jittered every frame.** One fixed set of offsets per shot (±1–3 px). A **kick** (≈ 8–14 px, decaying in ~0.25 s with slight ringing) on an accent; a **drift** up to ~25 px through a held moment, snapping back on the downbeat.\n- Grain boils partly (about a third re-rolls per print); sheet streaks and blots change only at cuts.\n- Simple cycles on few drawings; secondary motion (scarf, flag, tail) as a travelling sine wave.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | Expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Wide lateral tracking, small figure, empty sky | Travel, routine, room | a commute; a process line; a title |\n| Static wide, the subject crosses the frame | Comedy of scale | a gag; an arrival; a crowd |\n| Locked composition: horizon, disc, figure | Stillness, awe | a decision; a reveal; the film's one pause |\n| Ground-level low angle, big foreground shapes | Energy, abundance | a burst; a harvest; a launch |\n| Medium two-shot held for a look | Reaction | a joke landing; a deal; a disagreement |\n| Straight top-down, long shadows | Pattern, a map | a schedule; a floor plan; a game board |\n| Push into a halftone field until dots are shapes | Entering the print | a close look at data; a memory; a cell |\n| Pull back to reveal the paper | It was one sheet | a series; an archive; a summing-up |\n| Match cut on a flat shape | Continuity through form | a time jump; cause and effect; before/after |\n\nFraming: lots of paper and solid ink, text on empty sky or a knockout strip. Transitions: overprint growths, roller sweeps, shape match cuts, hard cuts on beats. Never a cross-dissolve, never a soft digital wipe.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Instruments**, as options: warm, dry, handmade, slightly lo-fi: upright or electric bass, vibraphone or marimba, nylon guitar, whistled or hummed melody, Rhodes, toy piano, brushed kit, shaker and rim clave, a small drum machine through tape saturation.\n- **Layering like plates** (an option): each new plate brings one instrument, and they can leave one by one.\n- **The machine is the signature foley**: feed \"shhk\", drum \"ka-chunk\" at a plate arrival, motor hum, a sheet landing on a stack, a guillotine cut. Other foley dry, close and papery, following the story's materials.\n- **Silence**: a held bar with reverb tails cut, at most ambience or one distant sound; it pairs with a registration drift, and the snap brings everything back.\n- **Mix**: dry and close; music ducks ~8 dB under voice; −14 LUFS. Voice relaxed and conversational, short lines, verified with whisper. No grain added at mux: it is in the print.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Plates as narrative.** The world gains a plate at each turning point. *Fits content like:* a product arriving as a new ink; a city history, one ink per era; colour returning during a recovery.\n- **Two inks, a third where they meet.** *Fits content like:* a merger; a duet; two data sets whose overlap is the finding.\n- **Overprint transition.** A shape of one ink grows over the frame, turning everything beneath into the mix, then shrinks into the next scene's key shape. *Fits content like:* a pill dissolving; a spotlight becoming a moon; a stamp becoming a map pin.\n- **Registration as rhythm and feeling.** Kicks on accents, drift in a held moment, snap on the downbeat. *Fits content like:* nerves before a speech; dizziness; a memory coming into focus.\n- **Reflections as separated plates.** Each plate wobbles on its own phase. *Fits content like:* a shop window; heat over a road; a coffee surface.\n- **Knockout reveal.** A shape made only of missing ink; its plate arrives later. *Fits content like:* a missing piece; a secret; a logo hidden in plain sight.\n- **Halftone scale.** Dots grow into shapes as the camera pushes in. *Fits content like:* population; rainfall; a crowd that turns out to be people.\n- **It is a sheet of paper.** The print can be stacked, folded, cut or pinned; grain boils like a flipbook of prints. *Fits content like:* a zine; a poster series; a flyer passed hand to hand.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Three halftoned plates in one area = mud → two plates, one tint.\n- `fract(p*vec2(233.34,851.73))` draws vertical stripes across solids at 1080p → a well-mixed hash (`fract(vec3(p.xyx)*.1031)` family).\n- Per-frame registration jitter and sheet streaks flicker and blow up file size → fixed offsets per shot, kicks on accents, sheet noise at cuts, partial boil.\n- JPEG screenshots (4:2:0) smear saturated dots → PNG frames into a lossless yuv444 intermediate, encoded once with `-tune grain`; check at 100 % and 50 % for moiré.\n- The halo layer must copy the canvas transform, or zoomed shots draw the subject at the wrong scale.\n- Additive `over()` inside a transparent layer loses its meaning → overprint on the plate canvas.\n- A main colour without the first plate floats loose in one-ink frames → build palette recipes up from the one-ink silhouette.\n- `evenodd` fills of arches fill solid → trace the inner edge in the opposite direction.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `riso.js` (print shader: plates, halftone, grain, registration, kicks and drift, gates, roller sweeps, reflection wobble), `draw.js` (`ink(b,y,p)` densities, `over()` overprint, `layer()` paper halo, 2-bone IK, limbs), `sheet.js` (model sheet with a \"plates = story\" row via `S.mask`), `tools/video_png.mjs` (PNG frames → lossless intermediate), `tools/mux.sh`. Minimal new frame: two plates (R = teal, G = orange); a solid teal hill, an orange disc drawn with `lighter` so it darkens where it crosses the hill, a 40 % teal sky band, a figure knocked out with `layer()`.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the inks and what each plate means, the structure, characters (or none), opening, ending, camera path and pacing. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **two inks in dialogue** (two subjects alternate shots and only overlap at the end, making the third colour); **subtraction** (start at full overprint, lose a plate at each loss, end in one ink); **a zine** (each shot a page turned, each page a different two-plate pairing).\n- Openings: **a knockout** (a blank shape in a full-colour field, filled by its plate seconds later); **dots first** (extreme close-up of halftone that pulls back into an image); **ghosts converge** (three colour ghosts drift together and snap into a readable frame on the first beat).\n- Endings: **one ink left** (plates lift away until the last line stands in one colour); **the print on a wall** (the last frame becomes a poster pasted up inside the world it showed); **a folded sheet** (the print folds into something the story needs: a boat, an envelope, a ticket).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nSunday Ride\n"},{"id":"S4-L29","name":"复古半调案卷","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L29.jpg","markdown":"# L29 复古半调案卷（Halftone Dossier）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 印刷与版画\n- 长什么样：橘猫胖橘被立案审查：测试重力、凌晨跑酷，最后无罪释放。\n- 适合：揭秘、档案\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Halftone Dossier — Style Prompt\n\n> A retro print case file: cream archive paper, overprinted halftone dots in a few flat inks, huge heavy headlines with misregistered shadows, rubber stamps that slam, and thick-outlined characters. The film is a file that is read out one exhibit at a time.\n> References (grammar only, never copy): Ben-Day-dot pop-art printing (Roy Lichtenstein era comics) for dot density as shading; riso/offset overprint zines for multiply layers and misregistration; police mugshot height charts and case-file folders for the framing device; Japanese variety-show telop captions for the keyword-highlight caption bar.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n- **The whole film is paper.** A warm cream sheet with low-frequency mottling, fine grain, a vignette and a fold; dark scenes are printed on it too, never glowing.\n- **Shading is never a gradient**: it is halftone dots whose size follows a density field. Two or three dot layers at different screen angles overprint (multiply); that is the \"printed\" feel.\n- **Bureaucratic apparatus**: case numbers, file chips, numbered exhibits, height charts, name plates, stamps. Their seriousness is the frame; the subject can be grave or trivial.\n- **Loud but readable**: one idea per 2–4 s card, one big headline, one visual gag or exhibit, one caption line.\n\nNot Risograph (no grainy two-ink riso texture as the whole look, no zine collage), not Swiss motion (no grid-driven kinetic type on white), not Game Show Flat (no candy sets, no rhythm-game rounds). Dots, paper and stamps must be visible in every frame.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Paper layer** (canvas, multiply over everything, built once): an upscaled random tile for mottling, per-pixel noise with a slight warm bias, a radial vignette, a faint centre fold.\n- **Grain layer** (per frame): a few hundred paper-coloured specks and fewer ink specks, reseeded every 2 frames: dust on the print, not film grain.\n- **Halftone**: a rotated grid of circles drawn as one path; step 20–26 px; screen angle different per layer; radius = density × step × k with k 0.6–0.72 (around 0.7 dots merge into solid ink). Density fields: radial (halos, blooms, corner glows), edge (dots crowding the frame border), linear ramps (\"lit from above\"). The second layer is multiplied so inks overprint.\n- **Halftone-filled numerals and shapes**: a flat fill overprinted with a darker halftone clipped to the glyph; giant numerals may bleed off the frame.\n- **Line boil**: turbulence displacement (small, ~5 px) reseeded ~10×/s plus a fine ink-grain mask that eats a little of every fill. Characters and hand-drawn props only; **never** on text, halftone, HUD or captions.\n- **Characters**: chunky shapes with a heavy ink outline (~7 px), flat fills, a lighter belly or face area, glossy eyes (ink ellipse + two highlights) that can squint, blink or close into a happy arc. One head rig shared by all body poses.\n- **Stamps**: a double rounded rectangle and bold text through a coarse ink-grain + displacement filter so edges are rough and the ink mottled; ~93 % opacity, tilted ±10°.\n- **Props**: starbursts, speech bubbles, confetti, radial rays, sticky notes, folders, clocks: all flat fill + ink outline.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **A paper ground, one dark ink, and four or five flat spot inks.** The dark ink (navy, black-brown or deep green) carries every outline, body text and the caption bar, and is the fade-out colour.\n- One ink is the **overprint shadow** colour (misregistration offsets, caption shadows); one is the **highlight** for keywords and numerals; one warm red is reserved for stamps and alarms.\n- Flat inks only. No gradients, no transparency ramps; tone comes from dot size and overprint.\n- **Night or \"secret\" scenes** switch to a dark ink ground with a lighter dot layer of the same family, still multiplied by the paper.\n- Characters may own one or two local colours (fur, clothing) that appear nowhere else.\n- Example palettes: cream / navy / cobalt / hot pink / yellow / red; manila / sepia-black / teal / mustard / vermilion; off-white / forest green / tomato / sky / ochre.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Headlines**: a black-weight serif (e.g. Noto Serif SC 900 for CJK; a black slab for Latin), 100–230 px, left-aligned near the margin, with a **misregistration shadow** (+8/+8 px in the overprint ink, multiplied).\n- **Display / numerals**: a fat rounded display face (e.g. Bagel Fat One) for names, big numbers, times.\n- **Interjections**: a playful hand face (e.g. ZCOOL KuaiLe), paper fill with a thick ink stroke and an offset shadow.\n- **Data / HUD**: a heavy monospace (e.g. JetBrains Mono 800) for case numbers, dates, rulers, file names.\n- **Captions and stamps**: a black-weight sans.\n- **Cards are the subtitles.** A headline per beat, plus a **caption bar**: a dark-ink rounded pill with an overprint-ink offset shadow, paper-coloured text, the **keyword in the highlight ink**. It pops in after the headline and holds ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s). With narration, the caption bar carries the spoken line.\n- **HUD**: case number and a chapter chip top-left, a status (REC dot, date) top-right; colours invert on dark scenes.\n- Latin runs 2–3× longer than CJK: smaller headlines, 1–2-word stamps (GUILTY, CLOSED), a tighter per-character stagger.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- 30 fps transforms; **line boil at ~10 fps** on characters, so the drawing breathes while positions stay smooth.\n- **Everything enters with overshoot** (back ease, 0.3–0.45 s): rising from below the frame or scaling from 0. Nothing fades in.\n- **Per-character pop** for headlines: each glyph drops 30–100 px and squashes in, stagger 0.05–0.08 s (0.02 s for small text).\n- **Stamp slam**: ~0.09 s from ~2.6× to 1× (ease-in), then a small damped bounce; camera shake, flash and stamp sound on the same frame.\n- **Squash landings** preserve volume (`scale(1/sq, sq)` with a damped cosine).\n- **Idle life**: breathing, tail or hair sway, blinks at scripted times, twinkles, a blinking REC dot or clock colon.\n- **Dot wipe transitions**: a coarse grid of circles grows to full cover and shrinks back (~0.44 s) with a diagonal delay; the scene switches while covered. Each cut may take its own wipe colour. A hard cut is the contrast tool, on a big hit.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA 2D camera group over a flat, frontal page. A vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Locked frontal with a small beat pulse (~0.6 %) | the page as a poster, grooving | a title; a list; an accusation |\n| Slow push-in (1 → 1.05–1.08) | scrutiny, rising tension | a profile; a summary before a verdict |\n| Shake with quadratic decay (5–26 px) | impact | a stamp; a crash; a landing |\n| Flash frames (white overlay ≤ 0.95 → 0, capped at 0.5 for big hits) | being photographed, a revelation | a mugshot; a verdict; evidence found |\n| Pan across a pinned board | connection between exhibits | a timeline; suspects; a chain of events |\n| Snap zoom into a halftone detail until dots are huge | \"look closer\" | a fingerprint; a signature; a number |\n| Page turn / folder slide | next file, next chapter | a second case; a flashback |\n| Pull back to reveal the whole desk | the file is one among many | scale; \"this happens every day\" |\n\nCompose like a printed page: headline top-left, subject right-centre, caption bar bottom-centre, HUD in the corners. Transitions: dot wipe or hard cut; no dissolves.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Synth pop-print toolkit**: synth kick, snare, clap, hat; a square-ish 8th-note bass; triangle pad; a **marimba-like pluck** (fundamental + ~4× partial) for melody; pizzicato and finger snaps for sneaking; music-box bell for tenderness.\n- **Foley follows the print and the gag**: stamp = pitch-dropping boom + click (a crackle for big ones), camera shutter, paper slides and folder flaps, typewriter or keyboard clicks, whooshes into wipes, pops on every glyph, boings and plops for characters, glass or ceramic for breakables, synthesized animal or creature voices (formant sweeps).\n- **Silence before the stamp**: empty the music just before a verdict so the stamp lands alone. Other options: a snare roll + rising sweep into a reveal; stripping to bass and snaps for a secret scene; a bell arpeggio as a tag; a single sustained pad under a sad exhibit.\n- **Voice** is optional; cards carry the film. If narrated: a dry, deadpan report voice, never breathless.\n- **Mix**: SFX ahead of the music on impacts, soft-clip master, −14 LUFS, true peak ≤ −1 dB.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **The accusation frame.** A topic becomes \"the case of X\": list its counts. *Fits content like:* a cognitive bias on trial; a city's traffic problem; a plant that won't stop growing.\n- **Numbered exhibits.** Each beat gets a giant halftone numeral behind it. *Fits content like:* five causes of a blackout; three clauses of a contract; the stages of a recall.\n- **The stamp.** A verdict is a physical slam. *Fits content like:* APPROVED on a grant; EXPIRED on a policy; VERIFIED on a rumour check.\n- **The mugshot.** Frontal subject, height chart, name plate, flash. *Fits content like:* a new species; a product teardown; the \"suspect\" in a bug report.\n- **Evidence board.** Exhibits pinned and linked with string, then shaken. *Fits content like:* a supply chain; a family tree; a heist's timeline.\n- **Dot density = emotion.** Dots swell into a halo, crowd the edges for tension, bloom radially for joy. *Fits content like:* a record broken; a deadline closing in; a reunion.\n- **Redaction.** Black bars print over words and lift one by one. *Fits content like:* a leaked memo; a surprise party plan; spoilers.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **Fonts not loaded before layout** → per-character positions computed with a fallback font, glyphs overlap. Preload every family against the whole page source and the built text; runtime strings must use only characters that appear in the file. Use `font-display: block`.\n- **Boil on text or dots** turns them to mush → filter only characters and props.\n- **Clip-path ids are global** → every clipped numeral needs a unique id.\n- **Time-windowed scenes** → an element invisible before its cue needs explicit opacity 0 for `t < t0`.\n- **A dot wipe short of full coverage** at the cut shows the hard switch; make the max radius cover the grid cell's diagonal.\n- **A 100 % white flash** reads as a dropped frame → cap at ~0.5.\n- **Dots too small** (< ~14 px step at 1080p) alias into moiré after encoding.\n- **Too many inks** lose the print feel → at most five plus paper.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nEverything lives in `demo/index.html`: helpers (`el`, `txt`, `tf`, `seg`, easings, `measure`), per-character text (`chars` + `charsPop`), **halftone** (`halftone()` with `radial`/`edge` density fields), `caption()` bar, **stamp** (`stampEl` + `stampAnim`), a mascot rig with a `setFace` interface, `scene(t0, t1, build)`, HUD chips, dot wipe (`WIPES`), paper + grain (`buildPaper`, `drawFx`), `render(t)`. `demo/render.mjs` renders stills and video; `demo/music.py` synthesizes score and SFX. Line ranges and a minimal scene: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md#engine-reference).\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide what the file is about, its counts or exhibits, the characters (or none), the inks within the colour logic, the tone (farce or grave), the opening, the ending and the length (it grows with the number of exhibits; each needs time to be read).\n\nAll far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a declassified report** whose pages are unredacted one by one until the real story shows; **a missing-person file** that works backwards from the last sighting; **an insurance claim** where each exhibit contradicts the claimant's statement.\n- Openings: **the evidence bag** (one object on a dark ink ground, the file assembles around it); **a fingerprint** in huge dots that resolves into a face as we pull back; **the phone call** (a caption bar alone on paper, the file drops in after).\n- Endings: **the file goes into a drawer** full of identical files; **a redacted final page** (the answer stays blacked out); **the stamp misses** (it lands on the table, the subject walks free out of frame).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nCase File: Chubby\n"},{"id":"S4-L30","name":"木刻版画","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L30.jpg","markdown":"# L30 木刻版画（Woodcut Print）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 印刷与版画\n- 长什么样：村子用一整个冬天铸一口钟，钟声第一次响起，雪停了。\n- 适合：粗犷、有力量\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Woodcut Print — Style Prompt\n\n> A relief print that carves itself: every frame is a black wood block, and light only exists where a knife has cut it away. One colour at most, and only for the thing that burns.\n> References (grammar only): Frans Masereel *Passionate Journey* (1919) and Lynd Ward *Gods' Man* (1929), wordless woodcut novels told as a sequence of self-contained plates; Käthe Kollwitz's woodcuts, where emotion lives in the direction of the gouge; Gustave Doré's engraving, where line spacing and direction build light and volume. Never copy their plates, figures, borders or titles.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nEach shot is **one printed plate**: black ink on laid paper, framed by a paper margin. It follows three rules of real relief printing:\n\n1. **Black is the wood, white is what the knife removed.** Light is *carved*: a bright area is many wide cuts, a dark area untouched wood.\n2. **Pictures appear by being carved.** Nothing fades in: cuts grow stroke by stroke along the form, coarse gouges first, fine lines after.\n3. **The block is a mirror.** Letters read backwards until the paper is pulled; that reversal is the style's own transition.\n\nNot copperplate engraving (no swelling burin lines on white, no crosshatch built up from white ground), not linocut poster art (no flat colour areas), not a black-and-white photo run through a threshold filter (the cuts must follow the form).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Print frame**: the printed image area sits inside a paper margin; the wider bottom margin holds captions. The image edge is the block edge: ragged ink, never a clean rectangle.\n- **Knife classes** (widths in 1080p pixels at 1× camera):\n\n| Tool | Width | Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| Knife `k` | 1.2–2 | contours, the white outline that separates a black figure from a black ground |\n| V-gouge `v` | 2–8 | hatching, hair, beard, fine light |\n| U-gouge `u` | 10–22 | rays, sky sweeps, the first rough-out |\n| Stab `stab` | r 3–8 | snow, sparks, flecks of loam, wood chips |\n\n- **Hatching (Doré grammar in wood)**: line **direction follows form** (a round object is wrapped in arcs, slopes follow the fall line, fire and light radiate, cloth follows its folds). **Width follows tone**: bright = wide cuts almost merging into white, mid-tone = thin cuts, dark = no cuts. Far = dense and fine, near = sparse and coarse. Every line is a separate cut (30–160 px, blunt entry, tapered exit, chipped edge), never a continuous machine line.\n- **Figures**: black silhouette + white knife halo + grazing-light hatching on the lit edge only (light z ≈ 0.2, so flat areas stay black). **Skin carved white, features left black** (the wordless-novel rule).\n- **Close-ups (hands, objects)**: sculpt a small height field (capsules, blobs, planks, creases), light it and run it through `woodcutFilter` (§10), so cuts follow the isophotes like an engraver's.\n- **Printing**: the mask (white = cut) goes through a WebGL2 compositor: knife burr on edges, ±6 % ink unevenness, wood-grain streaks, specks where ink didn't take, a mottled colour plate with a small fixed misregistration. Block mode: glossy inked wood with grain sheen, pale wood in the grooves.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- Two values carry everything: near-black ink and warm laid-paper white. The raw wood colour appears only in shots that show the block itself.\n- **At most one colour plate**, printed only where white has been carved, so it glows out of the cuts and never sits on the black. A colour plate over ink is nearly invisible, so plan it on carved white.\n- The colour is reserved for the one thing that matters (a flame, molten metal, a heart, a signal lamp). It can spread at a peak and shrink to a single highlight afterwards; it is never decoration.\n- Examples of a plate: a burnt copper-orange for heat; a cold Prussian blue for water or night; a deep oxblood for a wound or a seal. Choose it from the topic.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Captions are letterpress in the paper margin**, below the plate, like a plate caption in a woodcut novel. They never cover the image. A letterpress old-style face (IM Fell English, OFL) around 40–44 px, in ink, pushed through the same print shader. The ink comes up in 0.1 s with no slide and fades over 0.15 s. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- **Titles are carved, not typed**: cut into the block (IM Fell English SC), mirrored if the block is shown before the print is pulled.\n- Any closing text is a fresh print: letterpress in the margin or carved out of a black panel.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Characters on twos (12 fps)**; camera, particles, fire and light on ones (24 fps). Hatching lives in each body part's local space, so cuts move with the limb and don't boil; only fire and liquids re-cut every 2 frames, as deliberate boil.\n- **Anticipation / action / follow-through** on every key action: a few frames back → a 2-frame strike → settle + a short shake.\n- **Carve reveal**: each stroke has its own start time and speed (0.08–0.35 s). Reveal keys: `light` (bright areas first, coarse to fine), `radial` (from a point: rays, a pour), `down`, or any function.\n- **Hard cut = a new impression**: two frames of misregistration (a few pixels, then back).\n- **Paper transition** (the one transition grammar): brayer rolls ink (a wet highlight sweeps) → paper lays down → baren spirals, the image soaks through → the sheet peels on a cylinder toward camera, heavy then fast, its back showing the mirrored show-through.\n- What never moves: the paper margin.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nThe camera moves over the paper; the margin stays fixed. The knife tip, or the brightest cut, is the focus.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Track with the knife tip | Making; the line is the story | a first mark; a route drawn on a map; a signature |\n| Continuous pull-back as cuts spread | A line becomes a world; scale | a city from one street; a family tree; a reach of sound or news |\n| Locked plate | A page of a wordless novel; reading time | a decision; a portrait; the aftermath |\n| Slow macro push on a sculpted close-up | Patience, touch, labour | hands at work; a tool; a letter being read |\n| Snap zoom and hold | Shock; the detail that breaks | a crack; a wrong number; a face turning |\n| Frame in a frame (door, window, arch) | Distance, exclusion, waiting | a crowd outside; a sickroom; a border |\n| Hard cuts on the beat, each a new impression | Accumulation, a series of prints | offerings; days passing; a list of names |\n| Crane up from a detail to the whole | Consequence | a flood reaching a village; a light reaching faces |\n| Rack between two plates via the peel | One state printed over another | before/after; a promise kept; generations |\n\nOpening and ending come from the topic. Framing: black masses on carved white or white on uncut black, never grey on grey; text only in the margin. Transitions: hard cut (new impression) or the peel; never a dissolve.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Instruments**: low strings (contrabass, cellos; spiccato, pizzicato), wood and skin (log drum, woodblock, frame drum), gran cassa, timpani, one tam-tam, synthesised struck metal (modal: 3–5 inharmonic partials + hammer transient); a bowed saw or hurdy-gurdy drone for a folk colour.\n- **Options**: withhold the timbre of the story's object until it appears; a metric modulation instead of a tempo jump; a modal shift inside a long decay; one drone under a locked plate.\n- **Foley follows the material**, all synthesisable: knife into wood (transient + band-passed fibre tearing tied to knife speed), chips, brayer tack, paper air, baren rasp, the wet peel; metals by size (large = low, small = high and pure); resonant objects as modal models plus echoes for distance.\n- **Silence is a tool**: near-silence with one faint ambience, or true digital silence before the loudest moment; the first sound after it is chosen.\n- **J/L cuts** carry sound across plates and peels.\n- **Mix**: music ducks ~8 dB under voice and a little under key foley; **foley ducks too** under voice. Pan hard transients off centre. −14 LUFS.\n- **Voice**: few lines, low and plain, like captions spoken; the plates tell the rest.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Light is carved.** A burst of radial cuts from a point. *Fits content like:* a power grid switched on; a scientific insight; a lighthouse restored.\n- **Carving = time.** Patient work on screen, cut by cut. *Fits content like:* a year of training; a building rising; a craft apprenticeship.\n- **Mirrored block → true print.** A backwards word reads true when the sheet is pulled. *Fits content like:* a vote result; a name cleared; a misunderstanding put right.\n- **Stab cuts as texture.** White dots can freeze mid-air or be inked back in until the black is clean. *Fits content like:* stars going out over a city; rain stopping; a disease receding on a map.\n- **The one colour plate spreads and withdraws.** *Fits content like:* a blood donation; a sunrise over a harvest; a single lit window in a blackout.\n- **Filter a sculpt or a frame** into form-following cuts. *Fits content like:* a portrait of a real person drawn from a photo; a machine part; an archive image re-cut.\n- **Wordless-novel sequencing.** Self-contained plates, few or no lines; a gap can be silent. *Fits content like:* a migration; a strike; a life in seven plates.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **White marks on white areas are invisible** → stab cuts need dark masses behind them.\n- **Hatching every surface looks like fur or rain** → grazing light; 3–6 cuts on the lit edge only.\n- **Black faces with white cuts don't read small** → skin white, features black, a black keep-line around white parts.\n- **Hand-drawn close-up props look like mittens and blobs** → height field + `woodcutFilter`.\n- **Short hatch segments with a tonal wobble turn into zig-zag chevrons** → long cuts (14–50 × spacing), one broad highlight band, a thin rim light, a stronger halo.\n- **Steam or smoke on top of the subject reads as flames** → draw it behind, so only wisps escape.\n- **Lazily cached strokes shared between shots** make frames depend on the render worker → every cache builds itself on first use.\n- **Fine hatching + ink noise + grain inflate the file** → a higher CRF with `-tune grain`; check a 1:1 crop. `woodcutFilter` NaN in flat areas → clamp before `sqrt`.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\n`demo/engine/index.js` (imports only `/core/lib.js`) carves and prints **any path or shape**, on two canvases: a **mask** (black = uncut, white = cut) and an optional **colour plate**. Main functions: `shape` (carved solid with halo and form-following hatching), `cutAlong` (a path as hand-cut strokes), `hatch` (streamline hatching of a region), `flecks` (stab cuts), `rays` (carved light), `drawStrokes`, `plate`, `woodcutFilter` (image or frame → cuts), `makePrinter(W,H).render` (print / block compositor). Reusable demo pieces: `demo/trans.js` (paper peel), `demo/hands.js` (height-field sculpts). Option table, a minimal example and the filter recipe: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md#engine-reference).\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the story, characters (or none), plates, opening, ending, camera path, pacing and what the one colour is for. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a book of hours** (seven locked plates, one per hour, no moves inside a plate); **the same block recut** (carved deeper each scene, the image changing as wood goes); **two blocks printed in turn** (two places alternate impressions until one sheet carries both).\n- Openings: **the finished print on a wall** (then back into the block to see it cut); **white paper** (an inked block pressed onto it: the first image arrives whole); **wood grain** that becomes water or a field as the first cuts follow it.\n- Endings: **the block cleaned** (ink washed off, pale carved wood remains); **a stack of prints** (rising over many sheets of one plate); **an uncarved corner** (holding on black wood the knife never reached).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build, engine reference): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nThe Bell Founder\n"},{"id":"S4-L31","name":"铜版画","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L31.jpg","markdown":"# L31 铜版画（Copperplate Engraving）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 印刷与版画\n- 长什么样：一张博物志图版自己刻出来：雕刀推开铜版，蜜蜂一线线成形，最后手工水彩上色。\n- 适合：精细，像老科普书插图\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Copperplate Engraving — Style Prompt\n\n> An intaglio plate that engraves itself: a burin cuts swelling lines into copper, a proof is pulled, and the figure builds up — outline, hatching, cross-hatching — until engraved lettering, magnified details and a hand-laid wash make a finished plate.\n> References (grammar only): 19th-century natural-history plates — Haeckel, Audubon, Westwood, the *Britannica* copperplates (centred subject, numbered roundels, roman title over an italic name, \"del.\" / \"sculp.\" credits, the plate mark); Hogarth and Doré for the burin line (hatching that follows the form, crossing only in half-tones); hand-coloured plates for washes laid after printing. Never copy a plate, pose, border or title.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nAn **intaglio print**: the line is ink held in a groove a burin cut into copper. Three rules:\n\n1. **Tone is made of lines, never of fills.** Light is bare paper; half-tone is one family of parallel lines; shadow a second family crossing it; the deepest dark a third, lozenge-making family. No grey fill anywhere in the ink.\n2. **Every line swells and tapers.** The burin enters fine, bites deeper mid-run and lifts out to a point. Lines thicken where the form turns from the light and vanish where it faces it; contours are cut in several runs and thin where runs hand over. A uniform-width line reads as a pen or vector drawing — never.\n3. **Colour comes afterwards, by hand**: transparent watercolour laid flat region by region over the finished black print, spilling past the lines, pooling at its edges, ink always on top. A film may stay monochrome long; colour arriving is an event.\n\nNot a woodcut (black block, white gouges — `woodcut`), not a pen sketch (loose broken lines — `urban-sketch`), not an engineering drawing (`blueprint`), not a sepia filter.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **The sheet is the world**: 1920×1080 world units of laid paper (cream, ~`#f1e8d2`) with world-space texture (fibres, laid lines, sparse foxing) that zooms with the camera; a pressed, bevelled **plate mark** and a ruled border (rule + hairline) frame it.\n- **Ink** is one warm near-black (~`#1c1510`). Width ratios: outlines heaviest (swelling ×0.45–1.35), first hatching from a hair (~0.18 px) to ~2 px, crossing ~0.8×, third family ~0.6×, hair and stipple finest. Spacing is **relative to the figure** (~1/180 of its height, a little wider per later family) so small figures never go grey.\n- **Swell law** for every hatch line: width × (0.2 + 0.8·sin(πu)^0.7), tapered over its first and last ~22 %. Families switch on by tone thresholds (~0.14, ~0.5, ~0.75 on a 0–1 tone).\n- **One light direction** for the whole film (upper left by convention); heavier outlines away from it; tone fields come from the shape (pillow lighting, analytic spheres and cylinders), never from texture. **Occlusion** is geometric: lines stop at the shapes in front.\n- **Roundels**: double-ruled circle, ruled-machine ground with a white halo round the subject, content authored at a large radius and scaled in. **Leaders**: hairlines from a dot on the part to the rim, routed with clearance, a small italic figure number at the root.\n- **Hand colouring** multiplies over paper and under ink: a few px of spill, slight misregister, tide-line at the wet edge, low-frequency density lift, occasional backruns, granulation; each region blooms from where the brush touched.\n- **Copper** (when shown): polished warm metal, polishing hairlines, a soft reflected window; grooves = dark trough + bright burr toward the light. The **burin** (lozenge steel shaft, bevelled face, mushroom handle) is held low and turned ≤ 15° off the line so the fresh groove shows; **swarf** is a narrowing ribbon curling ahead of the point, one edge lit.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- The print is two values, paper and ink; all tone is line density.\n- Colour is **transparent wash only**, in muted earth and mineral pigments, one stronger pigment at most. Never opaque, never on lettering, rules or paper outside the figure.\n- Reserve it: the whole subject at one moment, or only the part that matters — or none.\n- Example wash sets: an amber insect with blue-grey wings; a map with a sea-green coastal band on buff land; one madder-rose organ on an otherwise black anatomy plate.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- Lettering belongs to the plate: **roman capitals** (Bodoni Moda; title ~44–56 px, wide tracking; labels ~15–19 px), **italics** for scientific names (~20–24 px), **script** (Pinyon Script) for notes, fixed size, balanced over two lines, never shrunk.\n- Roman letters are **cut glyph by glyph**; script is **written** in one left-to-right stroke. Nothing fades in.\n- Subtitles: script on a deckled paper slip, bottom centre, ≤ 2 lines, never over a label. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s). An engraved note stays in frame chars ÷ 12 + 1 s from when it starts writing.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Cutting order is the animation**: outline → first hatching sweeping across each form → crossing → darks, hair, stipple. Strokes run at constant burin speed, many at once (a few for outlines, dozens for hatching).\n- A long single stroke may be followed by the camera; start it partly cut so no frame is empty. Keep a full build to a few seconds.\n- Roundels fly on an eased arc with a small lift, growing log-linearly to size. Colour blooms from one point with a ragged front and a wet rim that dries; regions staggered.\n- Nothing fades or slides like UI: lines are cut, text cut or written, colour blooms, sheets are pressed, peeled or laid down.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nThe camera is a **loupe over a sheet**. A vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | Expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Loupe following the burin tip | One point of attention; making | a first mark; a signature |\n| Log-zoom pull-out | The line was part of something larger | scale reveal; context |\n| Push to a part, ride with its roundel | Where → what, in one take | an invisible detail; a mechanism |\n| Tilt/pan along the sheet at reading zoom | Labels in order | a parts list; a coastline |\n| Pull back while something flies | Everything placed in relation | acceleration; summary |\n| Locked frame, or a ≤ 2 % breathing push | The plate as document | reading time; colour moving |\n| Raking view across the copper | The groove as depth | the cost of craft |\n| Cut from mirrored copper to true proof | Reversal | a hidden truth; before/after |\n\nFraming: subject inside the plate mark, labels and roundels in the margins; order details so push-ins never crop a note early. Transitions from printmaking only (proof pulled, tissue guard laid over, region burnished out and re-cut, new sheet on the bed); no dissolves or wipes.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Foley by material**: paper (peel crackle, slip tapped down, tissue rustle, sheet whoosh); press (roller rumble, thump); copper and steel (high band-passed burin hiss with chatter and faint plate ring, swarf pings, a tick on lift, circular scribe, burnishing rub); ink and water (pen scratch, ticks for cut letters, a water plip and wet brush swish). Hatching = micro-scratches denser each pass.\n- **Room**: a quiet workroom (faint clock), paper room once the print exists.\n- **Instruments** (choose): harpsichord, string quartet, solo cello or viola da gamba, lute, chamber organ, glass harmonica for a cold subject. Plucked and dry while lines are cut; bowed or sustained only when something soft arrives; tiny tings for a scribed ring, a low pluck on landing.\n- **Silence** is real: cut room tone too, keep at most one small sound.\n- **Mix**: melody an octave above the voice, music ~half under speech, −14 LUFS. Voice: a quiet curator or naturalist.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **The cut.** Burin mid-stroke, swarf rising, nothing else. *Fits:* the first line of a treaty; a watchmaker's scribe; a coast being charted.\n- **The three passes.** Form gains volume pass by pass. *Fits:* a building from its plan; a portrait emerging; a skill improving.\n- **Magnification roundel.** Ring scribed, burnished, re-cut larger, flown to a margin with a leader, label cut on landing. *Fits:* a gear in a clock; a pollen grain; a harbour inset on a chart.\n- **The proof.** Sheet peels off; mirrored copper becomes true paper. *Fits:* a secret out; a draft made final; a coin's die and strike.\n- **States of the plate.** A region burnished out and re-cut. *Fits:* a border redrawn; before/after; a design revision.\n- **Hand colouring.** Washes bloom after the print. *Fits:* one organ lit; a flag on a historical map; a ripening fruit.\n- **Natural size.** A tiny true-scale figure beside the engraving. *Fits:* a seed by its tree; a chip by its circuit; a stamp by its enlargement.\n- **The finished plate.** Every label and credit, locked. *Fits:* a museum label; a frontispiece; a certificate.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Uniform width reads as pen → swell hatches, pinch contours between runs (`hatch({swell})`, `outline({run, pinch})`), keep wMin hair-fine.\n- Overlaps without occlusion → pass occluders as `excl` (subtracted ring by ring; even-odd would cancel overlaps).\n- Outline-only parts beside a hatched body → hatch along each part's axis, a dark at every joint.\n- Burin along the cut hides the groove; big skew looks like poking → ≤ 15°. Regular swarf helix reads as a spring → vary winding, twist, light one edge.\n- Reflections over grooves wash them out → copper light first. Roundels authored small freeze sub-pixel → author large, scale in. Leaders hugging the subject read as crossing it → clearance.\n- A long early stroke never draws → the ink's early exit must look ahead by the longest stroke.\n- A push-in cropping a fresh note breaks the reading rule → put consecutive details on one side.\n- Scientific names: TTS may manage, ASR won't → give the checker a plain spelling.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\n`demo/engine/`: `burin.js` (`B.Ink` ordered stroke store + `schedule`, `B.hatch`, `B.engraveTone`, `B.contourHatch`, `B.outline`/`B.stroke`, `B.stipple`, `B.fur`, tone fields), `plate.js` (`drawSheet`, `engraveText`, `writeScript`, `roundelFrame`), `wash.js` (`Wash`), `copper.js` (copper, burin, swarf), `index.js` (`engraveShape(svgPath, {at, light, spacing, style})`: any SVG shape → engraved figure). `demo/example/index.html` engraves and colours a four-pointed spark in ~20 lines; code and function table in DEMO.md \"Engine reference\".\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide subject, details, order, opening, ending, camera path, colour, pacing and music. Use cases are **grammar for the order and duration of information**; build your own timeline from the user's facts.\n\n| Use case | Information order | Hold per layer | Length |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Museum label / case screen** | title + scientific name → detail + term + note (×N) → where to see it | title ≥ 2.2 s; note ≥ chars/12 + 1 s; final ≥ 4 s | 30–40 s |\n| **\"One minute on …\"** | question → name → 2–3 facts → one number → follow prompt | number ≥ 2.5 s alone | 35–60 s |\n| **Textbook figure** | figure → parts labelled one by one → caption | label ≥ chars/12 + 1 s | 20–45 s |\n| **Craft heritage** | object → 2–3 craft details → maker / edition | as above | 30–45 s |\n| **Map / site plan** | place → insets → legend → visiting info | inset ≥ 3 s; legend ≥ 4 s | 30–45 s |\n\nAll far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **states of one plate** (one view re-cut three times as a place changes); **a book of plates** (four small plates turned like pages, one fact each, no roundels); **a dissection** (parts labelled in teaching order, colour only on the part taught).\n- Openings: **the finished proof burnished back** to its first line; **on paper at 1×** (title cut first, figure grows below); **raking light over blank copper** as the design is traced.\n- Endings: **plate beside its proof**; **the natural-size figure** held alone; **a blank new sheet** laid on the stack.\n\nSwapping `demo/content.json` and rebuilding is a technical check that the engine re-flows, not a way to make a film.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build, content fields): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nThe Honeybee, Plate VII\n"},{"id":"S4-L32","name":"丝印旅行海报","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L32.jpg","markdown":"# L32 丝印旅行海报（Silkscreen Travel Poster）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 印刷与版画\n- 长什么样：三条步道各一张丝印海报，一色一刮印出来，再沿山脊一镜到底从正午爬到黄昏。\n- 适合：海报、标题、封面\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Silkscreen Travel Poster — Style Prompt\n\n> Opaque, hard-edged flat inks pulled one colour at a time through a screen: 4–6 inks per poster, stepped colour bands instead of gradients, thin paper-white gaps where the registration slips. Information is revealed the way the print is made: one layer, one beat.\n> References (grammar only):\n> - **1930s–40s WPA national-park screen prints**: 4–6 flat inks, lit and shade faces, stepped-band skies, a dark information band, concentric sun rings.\n> - **Fifty-Nine Parks (contemporary)**: restraint, paper as a colour, a series on one template with a new palette per poster.\n> - **The physical process**: hinged screen, squeegee pushing an ink bead across mesh, split-fountain pulls, drag streaks, pinholes, off-register slivers.\n>\n> Never copy a real poster's composition, park name, lettering or logo, and never name a real agency.\n\nA **scene style**: it does a practical job (a guide, an event poster, a destination series) for any place, event or product with a few facts. See §11 for the jobs.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nA poster being **printed on camera**. Every colour is an opaque ink laid by its own pull; later ink covers earlier ink completely. No halftone, no fluorescent ink, no line work: a shape's edge is where one flat ink stops. **Gradients are forbidden**: skies and haze are **stepped bands** (a flat colour, thinning stripes, the next colour).\n\nWhat makes it read as silkscreen and not as flat vector art:\n- **Registration**: every layer offset 1–3 px at 1080p; a knocked-out shape leaves a crescent of bare paper.\n- **Overprint edge**: a faint multiply ghost ~2 u off under each ink, leaving a darker sliver where ink laps over ink.\n- **Ink thickness**: a light rim top-left, a dark rim bottom-right, fills only; fresh ink has a wet sheen that fades within a second.\n- **Paper**: cream stock with fibres; mottle and pinholes in paper colour, visible only on ink.\n\nNot risograph (translucent fluorescent overprint with halftone grain), not ukiyo-e (key-block line work with bokashi), not woodcut (carved line), not low-poly (3D facets). This style is opaque, line-free, flat and banded.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **One `Path2D` per ink layer**, filled in the current transform's units; knock-outs are holes in the path. Any shape can be printed.\n- **Pull reveal**: a ragged squeegee front as a clip (a ~30 u drag tail); blade, bead and a sliver of handle ride on it.\n- **Ink bead**: a round tube with lit half, dark underside, rolling glints and viscous strands; a split fountain puts several colours in one bead.\n- **Screen**: a hinged frame with mesh, emulsion and tape; its shadow is a ring (even-odd), never a full rectangle.\n- **Shapes**: ridges with a jagged lit facet, pines as one outline of drooping tiers, water as a flat body with glint bars. Hard edges everywhere.\n- **Poster template** (1000×1500 u): paper border, art area, a dark **info band** at the foot holding a name, a number, a rule and a row of stats; columns sized by content.\n- **Texture budget**: fine paper tile, mottle multiply ~55 %, pinholes ~75 %, light encode grain. More reads as risograph.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **One palette per poster, ≤ 6 inks**, assigned by role: light sky, deep sky / bands, far, mid, near (darkest; also the info band), and **one bright accent** (sun, route, highlights, key number). An optional lit-face ink.\n- **Values step from far (light) to near (dark)**; depth is value, never blur.\n- **The accent is rare**: the sun or light source, the one route or product, the most important number.\n- **Paper is a colour**: an unprinted area is the brightest thing on the sheet (water, snow, a beam of light).\n- **A series = one template, a new palette per item** (time of day, season, colourway, neighbourhood). Re-inking the same geometry in another palette is a statement.\n- **Text inks are chosen by contrast** against the ink underneath (WCAG), never by a fixed key; a night palette must not swallow a header.\n- Examples (sky1 → near, accent): dawn `#F6CD98 #EE9A73 #9A7FA8 #3E7079 #1E3B3F #FBE6B4`; harbour noon `#E8F0F2 #7FB3C8 #D9C7A3 #2F6E8E #173247 #E8553D`; winter `#EEF1F4 #B8C9D9 #8B9BB0 #4A5D78 #1F2A3C #F2B134`.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Condensed display** for names and values (e.g. Big Shoulders Display, League Gothic), auto-fit; a geometric sans for letter-spaced labels (e.g. Outfit 600).\n- Headers are **printed into the sky**, tracked out; the info band is printed last.\n- **The poster text is the subtitle.** No narration is needed; if there is a voice, subtitles are a printed strip (a band pull), never a floating caption.\n- **Reading rule**: each text stays ≥ chars/12 + 1 s (min 1.5 s) after its **last** item lands, and ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s) with a voice. Compute it from the content, round up to the beat.\n- On a wall shot, labels stay ≥ ~18 px and values ≥ ~30 px at 1080p; close-ups on a band keep the whole band in frame with ≥ 5 % side margins.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Pulls are linear and steady** (a squeegee does not ease). Accent inks start half a beat after the main ink of their layer.\n- **Info items** print left to right on subdivisions of the beat. **There is no fade anywhere.**\n- **Screen lift**: the frame hinges up (vertical squash + growing shadow) in ~0.4 s.\n- **Palette re-ink**: a wide squeegee travels diagonally over the sheet in ~0.35 s; geometry identical on both sides of the blade, only the inks change. Show only the blade, bead and a sliver of handle.\n- **Loose layers**: in a multi-plane move, planes parallax by depth with paper gaps and thickness shadows, then **snap into register** on one hit.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | Expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Truck with the squeegee, slight roll, frame edge in view | the act of printing; colour arriving | a hook; a split-fountain sky |\n| Locked top-down full sheet | layers stacking into a picture | watching a poster build; a comparison |\n| Fast pull-back as the screen lifts | the reveal of a finished layer | a scene complete; a title |\n| Push + tilt to the info band | reading | facts; a date; a price |\n| Truck through a foreground silhouette (tree, lamp post, sail) in screen space | passing into the next poster | poster-to-poster transitions |\n| Crane through loose planes (multi-plane parallax) | depth, a journey inside the print | a route; a climb; a street |\n| Slow lateral dolly along a row of prints | a set, a range | a series wall; colourways |\n| Push into the mesh until the weave shows | the craft, the grain of the medium | an intimate beat; a detail |\n| Log-zoom pull-back to the wall or window | the poster in the world | a final lockup (hold ≥ 3 s) |\n\nClose-ups on a sheet keep its edge in frame; a squeegee across a close-up is tilted with the handle hidden, so it doesn't split the frame. Layers change by squeegee, posters through foreground silhouettes or a table move. No dissolves, no bare cuts.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Acoustic, hand-played instruments**: strummed guitar, slide guitar, harmonica, upright bass, cajon or brushes, banjo, fiddle, accordion, harmonium, ukulele. Choose the family from the place: folk, surf, bossa or brass band all fit.\n- **One pull, one hit**: each ink lands on a musical event (a strum, a hit, a chord change); accelerate by subdividing, not by changing tempo. A registration snap is a loud downbeat.\n- **Foley by material**: squeegee on mesh (band-passed noise with a ~220–280 Hz mesh grain; faster = brighter), wet ink squelch, screen clack and hinge creak, paper stamps, ticks for dashed routes, paper hiss, a wood-and-metal clamp, drying-rack clips.\n- **Ambience from inside the print**: a finished scene may \"wake up\" (surf, street, crowd), J-cut in and L-cut out. Room tone under the table.\n- **Silence as a tool**: the music drops out for a beat before the key facts; what remains is one ambience, and the first sound after is a decisive gesture (a clamp, a long slide up).\n- Music ducks a few dB under foley. −14 LUFS.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nEach serves information order. A menu: use the ones your film needs.\n\n- **One ink, one beat.** Facts print last, when they are read. *Fits content like:* museum hours; a ferry timetable; tasting notes.\n- **Split fountain.** Several inks in one pull fill the frame with colour. *Fits content like:* a festival's night sky; a sunset beach bar; a rainbow of colourways.\n- **Palette re-ink.** Same poster, another palette = time passing, a season, a variant. *Fits content like:* summer vs winter hours; three jacket colourways; day and night at a market.\n- **Registration slip → snap.** Loose layers clamp into register with one hit, before the key facts. *Fits content like:* the date of a race; a launch price; a ticket sale opening.\n- **Paper as a colour.** The unprinted sheet is the brightest element. *Fits content like:* a ski slope; a lighthouse beam; fresh snow on a roof.\n- **The route as a printed layer.** A dashed accent line stamped segment by segment. *Fits content like:* a bus line; a marathon course; a delivery path.\n- **The series wall.** Every print ends up hung together as the final lockup. *Fits content like:* a city's four districts; a band's tour dates; a product family.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Stroke edge effects draw internal lines on unioned paths → fills only (fill, then fill shifted inside a clip).\n- A full-rectangle screen shadow muddies the print → ring shadow.\n- Cool shadow facets next to water read as water → shadows in the near ink.\n- Jagged ink beads read as paper edges → a round bead.\n- Parallax alone reads as \"the mountains are moving\" → paper gaps and thickness while loose, snapped to zero with a sound.\n- Silhouette wipes hold a dark blank → centre on the switch so full cover lasts ~1 frame.\n- Very wide banners are slivers in 16:9 → about 4:1.\n- Random coloured dots on the table read as confetti → a real workbench (planks, grain, muted stains, tape).\n- Too many items at honest reading times overrun the length → print items side by side in the same pulls, or let secondary ones be read on the final wall.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\n`demo/engine/silk.js` prints **any shape** in this style from a `Path2D`: `makeTextures`, `paperSheet`, `ink(ctx, {path, knock, paint}, {color, reg, clip, sheen, trap})`, `wipeRegion`/`wipeFront` (ragged pull), `squeegee` (blade, beads, split fountain), `screenFrame`, `inkFinish`, `bandSteps` (the gradient substitute), shape helpers. `demo/engine/poster.js` (template, banner, `pickInk` contrast), `demo/engine/scenes.js` (planes you can reorder or extend), `demo/film.js` (timeline and shots). API table and a minimal example that prints a new shape: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md#engine-reference).\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the subject, the scenes and their planes, the palettes within the colour logic, the number of posters, the camera path, the opening, the ending, the instrument family and the length. Use cases are **grammar for the order and duration of information**; build your own timeline from the user's facts.\n\n| Use case | Information order (print order) | Hold per layer | Length |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Trail / attraction guide** | place → per item: scene → name → difficulty → distance → time → footer | title ~3 s; name ≥ chars/12 + 1 s; stat row ≥ 2.2–3 s after its last item | 30–40 s, 3 items |\n| **Event / festival poster** | scene pulls → headline → date → venue → call to action | headline 2.5 s, date + venue 3 s, final 3 s | 15–20 s |\n| **Destination series** | one template, a palette per place: name → one fact → series wall | 2.5 s per poster + 4 s wall | 20–30 s |\n| **Product drop** | layers around a flat product silhouette → name → colourway ×3 (re-ink) → price / date | 1.8 s per colourway | 12–18 s |\n| **Seasonal / hours notice** | one poster re-inked per season → season + hours | 2 s per swap | 10–15 s |\n\nAll far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **one poster, many runs** (re-inked for each of four nights, the line-up changing in the band); **a street walk** (one multi-plane truck down a street, each shop sign printed as we pass); **a misprint story** (registration drifts until the last pull lands true on the date).\n- Openings: **the empty screen** (light through a blank mesh, a stencil being exposed); **the accent first** (a lone product in the bright ink on bare paper); **the finished wall at night**, then back to the table.\n- Endings: **the drying rack** (prints clipped in a row, swaying); **the poster in use** (pasted on a bus shelter, rain starting); **the last pull is paper** (the squeegee runs dry, leaving a white silhouette).\n\nSwapping `demo/content.json` and rebuilding is a technical check that the engine re-flows, not a way to make a film.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build, content fields): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nThree Trails\n"},{"id":"S4-L33","name":"瑞士动态排版","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L33.jpg","markdown":"# L33 瑞士动态排版（Swiss Motion Graphics）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 图形与排版\n- 长什么样：一张音乐会海报按瑞士设计的五条规则自己排版，第五条是只打破一条。\n- 适合：干净的文字排版\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Swiss Motion Graphics — Style Prompt\n\n> International Typographic Style in motion: a modular grid, one typeface, one signal colour, and movement as exact as a printing press.\n> References (grammar only): 1950s–70s Zurich posters (Müller-Brockmann, Hofmann, Gerstner, Crouwel) for the grid, flush-left type, scale contrast and \"designing programmes\"; modern identity systems in motion (Experimental Jetset, Pentagram) for snaps on the beat. Never copy a specific historical poster's composition (no concentric-circle or fan layouts lifted from Müller-Brockmann), never use Helvetica or Akzidenz font files, never name a real institution on a poster.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n- **Pure 2D graphic design animated on a grid**: flat paper, black type and bars, a very light grey and **one** saturated signal colour.\n- **Every element snaps to the grid, every move lands on a beat, nothing bounces.**\n- **Rigor with one exception**: the whole frame obeys a system, so the one element that doesn't becomes a character.\n- **Extreme scale contrast**: a giant numeral next to tiny technical notes.\n\nNot a keynote (no gradients, no glow, no device renders), not kinetic typography for a lyric video (words don't fly, spin or bounce), not data visualisation (bars have mass as graphic form, not as charted quantities, unless the grid gives them that meaning), not a Bauhaus pastiche of primary shapes.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Frame grid**: 1920×1080, 12 columns, ~96 px side margins, 24 px gutters, 24 px baseline. The frame can act as a **spread**: a left page (the programme: numeral, rule text, notes) and a right page (the artifact).\n- **Artifact grid** derived from the content (Gerstner): what the columns and rows *mean* is a design decision. **Choose module sizes that give the elements mass**; too many thin columns make a layout look like a chart.\n- **Surfaces**: page, paper, ink, signal colour, grid lines in two or three light greys (on page, while building, on paper). No gradients, no shadows, no particles, no grain (mux grain 0).\n- **Bars and blocks** are flat rects; consecutive cells of one unit merge into one bar, overdrawn 1 px at seams (antialiasing shows hairlines, especially when rotated or scaled).\n- **A secondary voice** can be light-grey elements under the black ones: depth without a second colour.\n- **A 2D camera matrix** (centre, scale, rotation) for all moves.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **Paper, ink, greys, and one signal colour.** Only one thing carries the signal colour; that makes it the protagonist. It is the only thing allowed to be inexact.\n- Overprint is allowed: black type on the signal colour.\n- The signal colour can be red, orange, ultramarine or green; choose it for the topic, then never add a second.\n- Examples: `#EAE9E5` page, `#FFFFFF` paper, `#111111` ink, signal `#E30613`; or `#F2F0EA` / `#141414` / signal `#FF5A00`; or `#E9ECEF` / `#0B0B0B` / signal `#1F3FFF`.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **One family for the whole film**, an OFL neo-grotesque (Archivo, Inter, Schibsted Grotesk, Instrument Sans…), several weights. Letter-spacing ~−2 % at ≥ 90 px. Headlines can be lowercase. Load every weight with `document.fonts.load` before drawing.\n- **Flush left, ragged right**; everything on a grid line; big white space is part of the layout. Giant numerals are **optically** flush left (subtract the glyph's left bearing).\n- **Subtitles are part of the layout**, not a bar at the bottom: each line sits at a column start in the current composition (a small grey lead-in + a large bold statement, or a caption like a museum label), each word clip-revealed on its whisper timestamp. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s); assert it in code.\n- **Caption rows**: small (~19 px) medium-weight notes with a 1 px rule above; technical notes in a corner (module sizes, counts, a bar counter).\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Two curves only**: a precise ease `cubic-bezier(.7,0,.2,1)` for snaps; linear for lines being drawn, playheads and the exception. No overshoot, no spring, no bounce.\n- **Durations are note values** at the film's tempo. A snap starts one note value early and **lands on the beat**.\n- **Entrances are wipes or clip-reveals, never fades**: words rise out of a clip rect from below the baseline and leave upward; lines grow along their length; paper appears by a wipe; big numerals climb in eased steps (\"one module at a time\").\n- **Deletions are knife cuts**: a thin line crosses the element, then the element collapses to its midline.\n- **Ripples are ordered by distance** from their cause, a couple of units per subdivision.\n- **The exception moves differently**: linear, unsnapped, perhaps pausing mid-flight; a coordinate readout beside it can turn from \"on grid\" to \"off grid\".\n- Smooth 24 fps, no stepping: digital precision, not hand-made.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. The camera moves only with the precise ease and only on beats. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Locked spread | cause (left) → effect (right) | a rule applied; a before/after; a comparison |\n| Hard pan by one page or column | the next step in a system | a sequence; a catalogue; a timeline |\n| Rotate 90° and stop | the same thing read another way | a poster as a score; a chart as a map |\n| Pull out to a wall of artifacts | one programme, many outputs | a brand system; a product range; a series |\n| Push into one module | detail; the unit of the system | a pixel; a letter; a single data point |\n| Grid extends past the page | the system is bigger than the artifact | scale; ambition; a break from constraints |\n| Split frame into equal cells | parallel cases | options; variants; team members |\n| Vertical scroll through a column | reading; a list | a manifesto; a spec sheet; a timetable |\n\nFraming: everything on the grid; the signal element may sit off-grid only when it is being the exception. Transitions are wipes, knife cuts, camera moves and re-flows; never dissolves.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Music with a grid of its own**: a steady pulse whose subdivisions give snap durations. Options: motorik / krautrock kit and bass, minimal techno, marimba or vibraphone patterns, a mechanical piano ostinato, a drum machine with a glockenspiel; one instrument added per new rule is a clear build.\n- **Picture and music can share one data file** (a column → pitch, a row → onset), so a graphic can literally be the score.\n- **Techniques (options)**: drop the drums for the one moment of the exception; a near-silent bar before a reveal; thin the band during a reveal so every note is audible.\n- **Foley follows the materials of print**, dry and close, no reverb: letterpress clack, ruling pen \"tss\", guillotine snick, paper slap and slide, numeral thunk, block snap, felt thump for a landing, rubber stamp, typewriter carriage.\n- **The exception can be the only element that sings** (a sine glide following its position).\n- **Silence**: a hard stop of everything on a downbeat; the next snap reads as an event.\n- **Mix**: voice compressed and ≥ 6 dB above music, music ducked ~10 dB under voice, −14 LUFS.\n- **Voice**: cool, crisp, rule-like sentences; prefer voices with bright consonants.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **The grid means something.** Columns and rows become a quantity (notes, hours, floors, years). *Fits content like:* a building's floors and rooms; a week's timetable; a city's districts.\n- **Snap = beat.** Every arrival is a percussion hit. *Fits content like:* a product's feature list; the steps of a recipe; a countdown.\n- **Programme.** Rules applied one by one to one artifact, then the same programme generating many. *Fits content like:* a brand identity; a typeface family; a set of stamps.\n- **Extreme scale contrast.** A giant element as chapter card beside tiny notes. *Fits content like:* a record statistic; a year; a price.\n- **The exception.** One element breaks the grid, linear and unsnapped. *Fits content like:* an inventor; an outlier in the data; a rebel employee.\n- **Knife cut.** An element deleted by a line. *Fits content like:* budget cuts; editing a text; removing a feature.\n- **Re-flow.** Everything else rearranges around a change, nearest first. *Fits content like:* a team reorganised; traffic after a road closes; a layout adapting to a phone.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Too many thin columns look like a chart → fewer, wider modules.\n- A \"better\" composition that isn't obviously better → one strong diagonal with long/short rhythm, a larger signal element, bleeds, type over colour. Merely tilting is not enough.\n- Rotating and labelling at once reads as noise → rotate, hold, then add labels.\n- Merged bars show seams after rotation → overdraw 1 px.\n- Elements that share the camera matrix leak into other shots → draw them only when on screen.\n- Canvas `letterSpacing` affects `measureText` → measure after setting the font.\n- Onsets that fall between frames flicker → put visual onsets on whole frames.\n- Whisper splits or merges words and pads the first word → align by letters, clamp to 0.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `ease.js` (`cubic-bezier(.7,0,.2,1)`, `snap(t, t_arrive, dur)`, `steps()`, `track()`), `film.js` (layout system, clip-reveal text, knife cuts, bars from a score, rotation reveal, wall of artifacts, caption rows), `main.js` (fonts, whisper word times, `?nosub=1`, `?poster=1`), `score.json` (the shared data file for picture and music), `tools/words.py` and `tools/subs.py` (word timings, asserted subtitle intervals). File list and build steps: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md) \"Build notes\".\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the system, what the grid means, the exception (or none), the signal colour, the opening and the ending. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a timetable** (a 24-row grid of hours fills with the topic's day, one row per beat); **an alphabet** (A to Z, each letter a module of the topic, the grid completing as a specimen sheet); **a comparison** (two systems side by side on a split spread until one element crosses the gutter).\n- Openings: **a single dot** placed on the page, then the grid growing from it; **the finished artifact** that is then dismantled into its rules; **a giant numeral** filling the frame, which turns out to be a detail of a small layout.\n- Endings: **the empty grid** after every element has been cut away; **a single word** flush left in the corner; **the exception leaves the frame** and the system closes over the space it left.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nFive Rules for a Poster\n"},{"id":"S4-L34","name":"60s 间谍片头","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L34.jpg","markdown":"# L34 60s 间谍片头（60s Spy Title Sequence）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 图形与排版\n- 长什么样：虚构 1964 年间谍片的片头：追一把被偷的钥匙，几何碎片最后拼成片名。\n- 适合：片头、悬念\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# 60s Spy Title Sequence — Style Prompt\n\n> A cut-paper, four-ink opening title sequence for a film that never existed: silhouettes move through sets built out of the credits themselves, every brass stab is a cut, and a title is assembled from the pieces.\n> References (grammar only): Saul Bass's titles for *North by Northwest*, *Anatomy of a Murder* and *Vertigo* (flat opaque inks, abstraction down to silhouettes, type as structure); the 2002 homage by Kuntzel + Deygas for *Catch Me If You Can* (a cut-paper chase through graphic sets, lateral tracking). Not a gun-barrel opening, not a tiptoeing cartoon panther, not a copy of any real title's logo, lettering, characters or shots.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n- **Flat, opaque inks on paper**: four colours, no gradients.\n- **Hand-cut edges** on every shape; pieces layered with cut gaps and small paper shadows.\n- **Abstraction down to silhouettes**: figures in profile, all acting in outline and timing.\n- **Typography is the picture's structure**: credits are architecture, props, vehicles.\n- **Music cuts the picture**: brass stabs are edit points.\n- A title sequence is a story in itself: one goal, a few locations, one reveal.\n\nNot flat vector motion graphics (the edges are cut, the paper is physical), not a halftone print style (no dots), not a Swiss grid (diagonals, jaunty type, playful), not a spy parody with gadgets and gun barrels.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Scissor-cut edges**: every polygon resampled every ~9–10 px and displaced along its normal by low-frequency noise (amp ~1.2–2.2 px, larger for bigger shapes) plus a rare 1–2 px notch. **Cut the edge once in the piece's local space and cache it**, so a moving cutout keeps the same edge, exactly like real cutout animation (screen-space edges crawl).\n- **Paper layering**: separate pieces are separated by a ~2.4 px cut gap in the ground colour and cast a small paper shadow (offset a few px, blur ~5, alpha ~0.3). Whole figures get the gap as an outline, so a black figure reads in front of a black letter.\n- **Paper texture**: one full-frame texture multiplied on top (mottling + faint fibres), a sparse \"ink void\" speckle on dark ink, faint squeegee streaks; film grain in the mux (~6).\n- **Silhouette figures**: tall stylised adults (~7 heads), identity by hat, coat cut, shoes and **one colour accent** that doubles as a motion indicator (a tie or scarf streaming back when running). Pieces **merge into one outline** inside the figure, with only thin slits at shoulder, elbow, hip and knee. Hands are sharp wedges; no faces except an eye slit in close-ups.\n- **The object of the chase** sits on a black backing (an ink keyline) so it reads on any ground; it is the brightest thing in frame.\n- **Type**: a condensed OFL display face (e.g. League Gothic) for credits, glyph outlines extracted (opentype.js) and **re-cut** per letter (edge noise, ±0.8° rotation, ±1.5 px baseline jitter). A title in custom geometric cut glyphs (straight cuts + arcs, uneven weights, staggered baselines), never a real title's lettering.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **Four inks**: ink black, paper cream, one hot colour, one warm secondary. Darker \"shadow paper\" variants of those inks only for depth.\n- **One dominant ground colour per scene**, changing with the location; figures and type in the other inks.\n- The hot colour is reserved for one accent on the hero and for danger; the secondary marks the object of the chase.\n- Background props go in a darker version of the ground, never in black, or they read as letters.\n- Examples: black `#1b1714` / cream `#efe4c9` / red `#d23a22` / mustard `#e2a52a`; ink `#161a22` / bone `#ece6d6` / teal `#1f8a8a` / orange `#f07a28`; black / pale pink `#f3d6cc` / cobalt `#2a46b8` / lemon `#f0d23a`.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Credits live in the set**, never in the subtitle band: one line per location, fully readable at some moment. Credits are fictional roles only, never real people's names.\n- **Subtitles** on a narrow paper strip (scissor-cut ends, slightly tilted) bottom-left, a small glyph from the film as bullet, a geometric OFL sans (e.g. League Spartan 600, ~44 px). Light strip on dark scenes, dark strip on light scenes; slides in from the left in ~0.18 s. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- Hold each credit long enough to read alongside a subtitle.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Puppets on twos** (12 fps): poses and positions of figures, hands, letters landing, vehicles. **Camera, credit slides and grid growth on ones** (24 fps): a stepped camera judders.\n- Run cycles of ~8 drawings, one step per beat, a strong lean, the accent flying back. Walks one step per beat, coats swinging.\n- Credits slide in along grid lines and **stop dead on the beat** (short ease-out); letters land with a paper slap.\n- **Stop-time**: at a stab the whole picture freezes (background scroll included) for a couple of beats in total silence, then resumes.\n- Shattering: a scene sliced into strips parallel to a cut line that slide off alternately along the diagonal.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. Graphic, flat, decisive. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Wide on a full credit line, then truck in | reading, then action | any location built from type |\n| Lateral tracking, one screen direction | pursuit; momentum | a chase; a race; a delivery |\n| Hard cut on a brass stab | punch; a new location | montage; a reveal; a gag |\n| Graphic match cut (same shape, same position) | rhyme; acceleration | wheel → coin → pupil → moon |\n| Locked single shape on a flat ground | mystery; an icon | a cold open; a clue; a logo |\n| Silhouette against a giant circle | the hero moment | a catch; a triumph; a stand-off |\n| Diagonal split into a grid | fragmentation; many at once | a split screen; a conspiracy; a team |\n| Vertical pan down a column of type | descent; a list | a building; a roster; a countdown |\n| Push into a letter until it fills frame | a letter becomes a place | a door; a tunnel; a window |\n\nFraming: strong diagonals (~30°), big flat grounds, figures small against type. Match cuts keep circle centres and sizes identical across the cut. No dissolves.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **1960s spy big band**: stacked staccato trumpets and trombones in octaves with crash (the stab), surf guitar low-string twang through a spring reverb, walking upright bass, brushes or sticks, bongos and shakers, vibraphone, flute, organ stabs, harpsichord. Minor keys, chromatic lines, dotted rhythms. Original motifs only: avoid the famous chromatic crawl of the most famous spy theme and its minor-major-ninth ending chord.\n- **Stabs = edit points**: place staccato brass ~8 ms early so the sample's peak hits the frame.\n- **Techniques (options)**: a cut interval that shrinks as a chase accelerates; stop-time silence; a big chord for the title followed by a short button; a solo instrument alone for a quiet scene.\n- **Foley follows the material**: paper (cut \"shh\", slides, card slaps for letters landing, blind flips, a tear), metal (keys, chains, locks, clicks), tape (click, hiss). Environments only hinted: a jet pass panned, train wheels in tempo, roulette ticks slowing, wind, a phone ring, footsteps on stairs.\n- **Silence**: true zero, reverb tails included; a single tape click or hiss may remain.\n- **Mix**: music ducks ~7 dB under voice; a peaky mix needs a few dB into a limiter before loudnorm to reach −14 LUFS.\n- **Voice**: a briefing through a tape chain (band-pass ~220–5200 Hz, soft saturation, slow wow, hiss rising under the voice); a few very short lines.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Type is the set.** Each location is one line of credits and that line is its architecture (letters as pillars, words as carriages, a letter as a lock). *Fits content like:* a conference programme as a city; a menu as a restaurant; team names as a stadium.\n- **Credits are rhythm.** Credits land on beats; stabs are cuts. *Fits content like:* a product's features; a festival line-up; an award's nominees.\n- **Graphic match cuts.** Shape rhymes, the interval shrinking as tension rises. *Fits content like:* a coin → a planet → a clock; a gear → a wheel → a sun.\n- **The diagonal grid.** A diagonal cut multiplies into a grid, and the pieces later fly back along it to assemble something. *Fits content like:* a puzzle solved; a team forming; a logo assembled.\n- **Silhouette puppets.** Profile cut-outs whose acting is outline and timing. *Fits content like:* a heist; a courier on a deadline; a detective.\n- **Stop-time gag.** Freeze on a stab, silence, resume. *Fits content like:* a near miss; a double take; a price reveal.\n- **The object of the chase is the title.** What everyone pursues becomes the last thing assembled. *Fits content like:* a product launch; a book's name; a birthday name.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Every internal piece outlined with a gap looks like a mannequin diagram → merge pieces in an offscreen layer, cut only joint slits (`destination-out`), outline the whole figure.\n- A `clear()` that resets the transform silently kills the camera.\n- A figure fully hidden behind a letter reads as \"nobody there\" → let hat, nose and accent stick out. A stiff streamer reads as a tongue → multi-segment ribbon with a travelling sine.\n- A credit only ever seen partially is uncomfortable → always open wide on the full line.\n- Black background props read as letters → darker ground colour.\n- An object in the same ink as its ground vanishes → black backing.\n- Circular props dropped into a word cover neighbouring letters → lay the word out around a gap.\n- Match cuts fail if circle centres move across the cut.\n- Flat inks + grain make huge files at low CRF → a higher CRF looks the same.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `paper.js` (four inks, cached scissor edges, cut gaps, paper shadow, texture, silhouette layer with joint slits), `glyph.js` (League Gothic → opentype.js outlines → re-cut letters), `chars.js` (silhouette rigs, poses, run/walk cycles), `title.js` (custom cut title glyphs, diagonal split), `story.js` (tempo grid, single source of timing), `film.js` (timeline, acting, cameras, subtitles, sound events). `?sheet=1` renders a model sheet, `?frame=<scene>` a style frame. File list and build steps: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md) \"Build notes\".\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide what is pursued, by whom, the locations (as credits), the inks, the opening and the ending. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **no chase: a countdown** (ten credits, ten locations, a bomb-clock number built from type in each); **a heist in reverse** (the object is returned, location by location, to where it belongs); **two agents, one frame** (split diagonally, each side a different ground colour, until they meet).\n- Openings: **a full-frame title** that shatters into the sequence (the reveal moved to the start); **a telephone rings** in a single cut-out room; **the hero already falling** through a column of type.\n- Endings: **the title never assembles**, one piece missing, the hero walking off with it; **a slow pull-out** showing every location was one giant credit page; **a stop-time freeze** on the final stab, held.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nThe Velvet Cipher\n"},{"id":"S4-L35","name":"装饰艺术","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L35.jpg","markdown":"# L35 装饰艺术（Art Deco）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 图形与排版\n- 长什么样：1930 年的大饭店，门童赶在午夜前把一封信送上顶楼。\n- 适合：华丽\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Art Deco — Style Prompt\n\n> Black lacquer, engraved gold line and airbrushed geometry: a 1930s poster that moves. Every composition has a centre axis, transitions open or close along it, and light arrives as bulbs switching on one by one.\n> References (grammar only): **A. M. Cassandre** posters (giant geometry, steep perspective, airbrush inside hard edges); **Chrysler Building / Rockefeller Center ornament** (setbacks, sunbursts, chevrons); **Busby Berkeley** overhead numbers overheads (human kaleidoscopes); **The Great Gatsby (2013) title sequence** (gold line drawing itself on black); **Gershwin-era symphonic jazz** (clarinet, piano, muted trumpet). Never copy a poster layout, a real building, a character, a typeface design or a melody.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nA world built from **gold keylines on warm black**, filled with **airbrushed hard-edged volumes** (Cassandre, Lempicka), organised around **one vertical axis**. Ornament *is* the picture: sunbursts are backgrounds, stepped arches are frames, a dial is a progress bar, a bulb marquee is a reveal. Motion is **mechanical and symmetrical**: things unfold from the centre, doors split along the seam, lights switch on in counted order. Native register: glamour with a clock ticking (an event, a deadline, a launch).\n\nNot a gold-foil slideshow (things move and have volume), not Art Nouveau (no whiplash curves, no flowers), not a flat vector poster (volumes are airbrushed and rimmed), not a 60s spy title (no silhouettes on flat colour).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Ground**: warm lacquer black, never neutral grey-black; a night sky may drift toward a deep emerald near the horizon.\n- **Gold is a banded metal gradient** (dark – light – hot – light – dark); animate its `sheen` offset for a light sweep.\n- **The signature stroke** (`gline`): a dark engraved underline ~1.6 px wider than the line, the gold gradient line, and a hairline highlight on top. Optional glow and a parallel twin (`double`). Nothing gets a black cartoon outline.\n- **Volumes**: hard edge + airbrush (`airbrush`): a directional gradient from shade to light inside the shape, one consistent light direction (upper left by default), a thin gold rim on the lit side.\n- **Motifs** (sunburst, stepped arch / ziggurat, fish-scale, chevrons, fan, four-point sparkle, speed lines, clock face, dial) are structural (a background, a frame, a transition), not stickers.\n- **Perspective is real**: true one-point (mode-7 floors) and two-point perspective through a pinhole camera, so dolly, tilt and pan change perspective instead of scaling flats.\n- **Characters are deco too**: about 7 heads tall, tapered streamline bodies, faceted lacquer planes (Lempicka), almond or arc eyes, a one-stroke nose, airbrush + gold rim. Give each lead one or two silhouette marks readable at a quarter of frame height.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- Three constants: **warm black ground, gold line and metal, ivory** for paper, gloves, light and type.\n- **One saturated accent reserved for the protagonist** (or the one object the story follows). It is the only large saturated area, so the eye finds it in any wide shot. A cooler jewel tone may carry secondary surfaces, never competing in area.\n- **The one-colour exception**: any single element can be drawn in its own colour through the same fill + keyline + glow (`color` option) while everything else stays gold.\n- Always expand hex to six digits in code (three-digit hex breaks colour mixing).\n- Examples of the triad (accent / jewel / metal): burgundy / emerald / gold; coral / sapphire / pale gold; jade / plum / rose gold. Our demo's actual values are in DEMO.md.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Type (OFL)**: Limelight (titles), Poiret One (numbers, sign skeletons, plaques), Josefin Sans (subtitles, labels), Italiana (numerals).\n- **Titles are architecture**: a stepped gold title bar over an arch or sunburst, optional badge and wings; drawn on in gold, then a sheen sweep.\n- **Subtitle card** (`subtitleCard`): a lacquer bar (~88 % black) with stepped ends like the title bar, double gold keyline, Josefin Sans SemiBold ~40–42 px ivory, an optional small speaker icon per voice. It **unfolds from the centre** like every other title, so titles, subtitles and transitions are one system. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- Numbers carry plot without narration: dials, clock faces, floor medallions, badges.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Characters step on twos (12 fps)**; camera, light, bulbs, gold draw-on and sheen run on ones (24 fps). A stepped camera reads as stutter.\n- **Everything unfolds from the centre**: a point → a hairline → bars open symmetrically → text rises → wings and sparkle.\n- **Draw-on follows the music**: a gold line grows at the speed of the phrase under it and lands on the hit.\n- **Bulbs have an ignition overshoot** (~1.6× flash settling in ~0.18 s) and a relay \"chunk\"; once a sign is lit, a fast chase may run across it.\n- **Mechanical easing**: doors, dials and hands move with firm ease-in-out and a small settle, like machinery; nothing floats.\n- **Performance beats get anticipation** and escalation in threes (once, twice, mash).\n- What never moves: the centre axis of a composition. Symmetry may rotate or open, but it never drifts off-centre.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Symmetric split (two door leaves hinged at the edges; or a revolving-door wipe) | a threshold crossed | a new place; a new chapter; a reveal behind a façade |\n| Leaves sliding shut over the live shot | a door closing on a moment | leaving; a decision made; the end of an era |\n| Extreme low-angle Cassandre tilt | scale and aspiration | a goal far above; a tower, a rocket, a monument |\n| Architectural section (pull out to a cutaway of the whole building) | progress through a structure | climbing, descending, a process in stages; a company's floors |\n| Busby Berkeley overhead, mirrored and rotating | people become pattern | a team, a release, a celebration, a system of parts |\n| Push-in on a dial, medallion or clock | the number is the plot | a deadline; a count; a milestone |\n| Match-cut on shape (circle → circle, ray → ray) | two places, one rhyme | jumping between departments, cities, eras |\n| Low oblique along a sign or marquee | light travelling letter by letter | a name revealed; a launch; a score being counted |\n| Pull back from detail to skyline | where the moment sits in the world | aftermath; a city-wide effect; scale |\n\nFraming: the centre axis is sacred; an asymmetric frame must resolve to it within the shot. At an emotional peak the protagonist fills ≥ ~1/3 of frame height (smaller, the face reads as a prop in the ornament). Transitions: splits, revolves, arch openings, shape matches; never a generic dissolve or slide.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Symphonic jazz of the 1920s–30s, not a 60s big band.** Options: stride or concert piano, clarinet (trill; a glissando by time-varying resampling of a long note), strings (tremolo, rushes, pizzicato), muted trumpet commentary, trombone and sax section, walking pizz bass, brushes or sticks, tubular bells, harp glissandi, banjo, xylophone runs, celesta.\n- Rhythm families to choose from: foxtrot, Charleston, tango, a slow torch-song ballad, a fanfare in the manner of a newsreel.\n- **Techniques (options)**: one hit per unit of counted light; accelerate by dividing the beat; plant a theme in fragments so its full statement is a payoff; low-pass a band that is physically far away and open it as we approach; move a period narrator through acoustic spaces (horn, radio, PA, live mic) to make distance audible.\n- **Foley by material**: brass clicks, bronze clang + chain, iron treads, marble heels, silver trays, wooden doors + spring twang, paper flutter, knife-switch \"chunk\" + filament hum, relay ticks, elevator ding, champagne cork.\n- **Silence as a tool**: before the most important sound, drop to one small diegetic sound (a clock tick, wind) and cut back in with that sound alone.\n- **J/L-cuts** carry architecture: the next room's sound enters a bar before its door opens.\n- Mix: duck music ~−9 dB under voice (less under a period announcer who is part of the texture), beds −6 dB, −14 LUFS, light film grain.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Counted light.** A bulb sign lights one letter per beat or per event, so the audience can count. *Fits content like:* the countdown to a product launch; the letters of a city name lighting as a train arrives; votes tallied into a winner.\n- **Threshold doors.** Every scene change is a door split along the seam. *Fits content like:* the departments of a company; the rooms of a museum; stages of a career.\n- **Machine kaleidoscope.** People or objects become a mirrored, rotating pattern that locks into a symbol. *Fits content like:* a team becoming a logo; gears of a supply chain; a choir becoming a clock.\n- **Dials carry the plot.** A floor indicator, a clock or a gauge moves instead of narration. *Fits content like:* a funding thermometer; the altitude of a climb; a stock ticker at the opening bell.\n- **The architectural cutaway.** Pull out until a building is a section and the protagonist a coloured dot with speed lines. *Fits content like:* data moving through a server stack; a letter through a post office; a skier down a resort.\n- **Sheen sweep / draw-on.** Light crosses a gold object on a hit; a gold line engraves itself at the speed of a glissando. *Fits content like:* unveiling a trophy; a map route; a signature on a treaty.\n- **The one colour.** One element keeps its own colour in a gold world. *Fits content like:* the one product among competitors; the patient among doctors; the new idea in an old firm.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Bulb signs from glyph skeletons break on curves (G, S, R): Zhang–Suen staircase pixels fool a neighbour-count junction test → use the **crossing number** and weld chain ends within ~14 px.\n- A generic cartoon character looks pasted in → characters are deco (§2).\n- Fish-scale reads as brickwork → lower half-circles row by row, each overlapping the previous row, plus an inner arc.\n- Door leaves over a flat \"interior\" read as a blank frame → draw only the leaves, closing over the live shot.\n- A gold point on pure black trips `blackdetect` → lift the background wedges and glow slightly.\n- Period loudspeaker distortion is unintelligible under music → gentle drive; whisper-check every line on the final mix.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nCanvas2D, deterministic in `t`, in `demo/engine/`: `deco.js` (gold gradient, `gline`, `airbrush`, motifs, `drawShape` for any path in the deco manner, `color` for the one-colour exception), `type.js` (title bar, digits, badges, dial, clock, `subtitleCard`), `bulbs.js` (any text → bulb sign, `litSequence`), `cam.js` (pinhole camera, `cardTransform`). `demo/frames.js` renders model sheets and style frames from the real engine. Signatures and a minimal example that draws something not in the demo: DEMO.md \"Engine reference\".\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the structure, the characters (or none), the settings, the opening, the ending, the camera path, the pacing, the accent colour and what gets counted. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **the exhibition** (five pavilions of a world's fair, one idea of the topic behind each door); **the ocean liner** (three decks from engine room to ballroom, a horn per stage); **the trophy night** (nominees as sunburst panels, the winner revealed in light).\n- Openings: **the machine room** (gold gears and pistons building to the first beat); **the engraved map** (a gold route crosses a continent before anyone appears); **the telegram** (ticker tape types the premise).\n- Endings: **the lights go out** (letters switch off in reverse until one bulb remains); **the dawn** (lacquer black warms to a pale sky); **the poster** (the last shot flattens into a printed poster with a date).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nMidnight at the Starlight Hotel\n"},{"id":"S4-L36","name":"蓝图 · 工程制图","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L36.jpg","markdown":"# L36 蓝图 / 工程制图（Blueprint）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 图形与排版\n- 长什么样：发明家的蓝图自己画出一台接云机器，修订云线变成了真的雨云。\n- 适合：讲原理、讲结构\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Blueprint — Style Prompt\n\n> Engineering drawings that come alive on a sheet of cyanotype paper: white technical line work on Prussian blue, drawn in drafting order, with annotations that move.\n> References (grammar only, never copy): the moving technical drawings in Prologue Films' *Iron Man* (2008) end credits, Leonardo's *Codex Atlanticus* pages, 19th-century US patent drawings (Fig. 1 / Fig. 2, part balloons, fine-line shading), the automaton drawings in *Hugo* (2011). No Marvel elements, no real patents, no real inventor's machine or signature.\n\nSuits films of 30–60 s.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n- **The whole film happens on one sheet of blueprint paper** lying on a dark drafting table. The camera moves over it like an eye reading a drawing.\n- **White line work on uneven Prussian blue**: centre lines, outlines, hatching, dimension lines, leader lines with numbered balloons, a title block, a notes column.\n- **A 2D line-drawing language**: orthographic and oblique/isometric projection are allowed, **3D rendering never**.\n- **Rigorous convention carries the tone**: the drawing is dry and exact; the notes can carry wit, warmth or gravity.\n- **The designed thing is the character.** A machine, object, building or plan acts through its parts; people appear only as a hand shadow with a pen, or a signature.\n\nNot Hologram HUD (no glowing wireframe, no 3D orbit), not Whiteboard (no marker on white), not Copperplate Engraving (no burin tonal hatching as the whole look).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Sheet**: procedural cyanotype in a shader in *sheet coordinates*, crisp at any zoom: fbm between two Prussian blues, coating streaks, fibres, fold creases (bright edge, dark edge, whitening), bleached edges, specks. Around it: dark drafting cloth with a drop shadow.\n- **Lines**: pale blue-white, slightly soft (mipmap bleed), a faint ink breakup driven by paper grain. Four weights (outline, detail, thin, hair; e.g. 3.2 / 2.0 / 1.25 / 0.9 sheet units). **Screen width = sheet width × zoom^0.65**, so close-ups don't turn into sausages and wides don't vanish.\n- **Dash conventions**: centre line (long-short), hidden (short dashes), phantom / cutting plane (long-short-short), scaled with zoom so the pattern stays on the paper.\n- **Occlusion by painter's order**: every solid is filled with \"paper\" before its outline is stroked. Oblique extrusion: stroke the outline at N depth steps at 2× width, then fill the same steps with paper; a clean silhouette survives.\n- **Composite in buffers**: ink (white = exposed line); fx (shadow, wetness, stamp ink as channels); a subtitle overlay last. Shadows darken lines too: that is how a lifted object proves it has left the paper.\n- **Hatching** (fine-line shading) for sections, soil, weights, at 45°, spacing 8–14 units.\n- **Cyanotype and water**: a drop blooms deeper blue with a pale tide line; white lines inside the stain go soft; ink signatures bleed.\n- **The one real thing** (optional, at most once): a drawing symbol that leaves the drawing language gets soft volume, one gradient across its whole shape, glow, and a shadow falling across the lines. It leaves a pale unexposed imprint where it was drawn.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **Blue and white only.** The blue varies in value (coating, stains, folds); the white varies in weight and softness, never in hue.\n- **One non-blue colour, used exactly once**: a rubber-stamp ink (red is classic; a green APPROVED or black VOID work too). It carries the verdict.\n- The \"real\" object at the climax may carry light (white glow, soft value), not a new hue.\n- Example blues: `#18336B` → `#2A58A6` with lines `#E7F0F9` and table `#0D1116`; a faded sun-bleached sheet `#3A6DA8` → `#5B8CC4`; a deep over-exposed sheet `#0F2350` → `#1D3F80`.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Hand lettering** for header, labels, notes and subtitles (e.g. Architects Daughter); a technical sans for title-block fields (e.g. B612); a script only for the signature (e.g. Allura). All OFL.\n- **Text is written on left to right** with a glowing nib, like everything else.\n- **Subtitles are drawing notes**: a light translucent box with double white rules, a \"NOTE / n\" cell, the text lettered on in ~0.35 s, a small leader tick. **Placed per shot in empty sky**, never fixed to the bottom (the bottom of a drawing is ground line, base and title block). Wrap at ~880 px. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- Every subtitle is also lettered into the sheet's **GENERAL NOTES** column, so the final wide shows the whole voiceover as drawing notes.\n- The **title is the sheet header**; the **title block** holds status and verdict fields.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Camera and draw-on on ones (24 fps).** Continuous moves within a section, hard cuts on downbeats between sections; tiny camera bumps only for physical impacts (paper slap, stamp).\n- **Draw-on** truncated by arc length with a glowing nib at the head; items staggered within a layer; each layer starts on a beat.\n- **Drafting order**: centre lines → outlines → detail → hatching → dimensions → balloons → labels.\n- **Exploded views**: parts leave in sequence on subdivisions of the beat, eased, and return one per beat-group with a click.\n- **Mechanisms step on the grid** like an escapement: a gear advances one tooth per subdivision with a short ease-out; meshing phases computed so teeth really interlock; chains move with their sprockets.\n- **Machine acting**: angles, droops, volumes and gauge needles are the face. Failure = slowing, sagging, winding down over ~1.5 s; joy = snapping upright, a needle overshooting (back-ease).\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Whole sheet on the table | scale, the document as an object | establishing; a verdict; a series of sheets |\n| Medium on a figure, subject ~80 % of height, sky on one side | the design being drawn | drafting; explaining a part |\n| Track along a path of force (chain, pipe, wire, beam) | cause and effect | how it works; a sequence of operations |\n| 2× close-up cut on a downbeat | the mechanism's heart | gears meshing; a valve; a joint |\n| Locked-off, hold a beat | deliberation | a hand revising; a decision |\n| Oblique depth factor 0 → 0.5 | the flat drawing \"turns\" and gains thickness | from plan to object; a reveal of depth |\n| Section line sweeping across | inside vs outside | a cutaway; hidden structure |\n| Lateral pan across Fig. 1 → Fig. 2 → Fig. 3 | versions, steps, time | revisions; stages of construction |\n| Slow push-in, no voice | wonder | the one real thing |\n\nKeep storytelling shots close; full-sheet wides are rare. Keep the main figure at least `960/zoom` from any sheet edge you will frame. Transitions: cuts on downbeats, or fig-number rewrites on the sheet; no dissolves.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Contrapuntal, mechanical music**: harpsichord, bassoon, strings spiccato, harp, celesta, music box, clavichord, a small wind ensemble. Imitative lines (an invention, a canon, a fugato) feel like interlocking parts; a short repeated motive can be the \"gear\".\n- **Foley follows materials**. Paper: roll crackle and slap, T-square wood slide, technical pen scratch (2–6 kHz grain, length = stroke length), lettering bursts, compass squeak, hatching strokes, eraser rub, tracing-paper rustle. Brass and wood: part clicks (resonances ~3–5 kHz), escapement ticks, chain rattle, spring twang, wind-down ticks that slow geometrically. Water: soft damped pats per drop, a light rain bed. Rubber stamp thump, pen cap click.\n- **Options for the arc** (in any order, use what the story needs): instruments dropping out with a written-out ritardando into true silence; a single questioning figure alone; a suspended chord held without resolution under the magic; a full cadence on the stamp; the theme in augmentation for a close; adding a section per mechanism that joins.\n- **Silence** may contain paper sounds only.\n- **Voice**: dry, understated, reading design notes, a few short lines. Leave the most emotional moment voiceless.\n- **Mix**: music ducks well under voice (~0.36 ×), foley by half; whooshes low-passed so they don't mask consonants. −14 LUFS. Little added grain (the paper already has it).\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Drafting order.** The object draws itself as an engineer would, a layer per beat. *Fits content like:* a bridge; a violin; a kitchen renovation.\n- **Live annotations.** Dimensions measure moving parts and the numbers change; balloons fly with parts; a section line reveals the inside. *Fits content like:* a heart valve; a folding bicycle; a rocket stage separating.\n- **Fig. numbers as transitions.** \"FIG. 1\" struck through and rewritten \"FIG. 2 · EXPLODED VIEW\". *Fits content like:* versions of a product; phases of a construction; a recipe's stages.\n- **Projection as camera.** The oblique factor animates; exploded parts fly along the depth axis. *Fits content like:* a floor plan becoming a building; a circuit board; flat-pack furniture.\n- **A symbol made real** (once, if at all). A convention leaves the drawing language: a dimension arrow flies, a hatch becomes rain, a centre line becomes a road, a weld symbol sparks. *Fits content like:* an invention finally working; a city plan becoming a street; a dream becoming a plan.\n- **Cyanotype and water.** Drops bloom and soften lines. *Fits content like:* a flood-defence design; grief; a harbour wall.\n- **The title block as story.** Status field = the verdict; revision rows = history. *Fits content like:* a rejected permit; REV. 1–5 of a city; an approval after years.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **Main figure near the sheet edge** → wides show the desk. Keep margins or enlarge the sheet.\n- **Upward explosions hit the header** → explode sideways and flatten long parts during the explode.\n- **Subtitles on the ink buffer** vanish off-sheet and can't occlude lines → separate overlay, premultiplied alpha.\n- **An object darkened by its own shadow** → zero the shadow channel only in its area.\n- **Per-lobe shading on a blobby shape reads as grapes** → one gradient across the union; crescents from a union-minus-shifted mask; fill unions as one path (`nonzero`) or overlapping fills make bubbles.\n- **A small payoff undersells the return** → the payoff object ~⅓ of frame height, centred, drawn stroke by stroke, subtitles off it.\n- **Line weights not scaled with zoom** → sausages in close-ups, vanishing wides.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\n`demo/paper.js` (WebGL2 compositor: procedural cyanotype, ink bleed, wet stains, shadows, stamp, roll), `demo/draw.js` (drafting engine: camera, oblique projection, draw-on polylines, solids with paper fill, hatching, dimensions, balloons, text), `demo/sheet.js` (border, header, title block, parts list, notes, revision cloud, rain, hand shadow, stamp), `demo/film.js` (timeline on a beat grid: cameras, acting, subtitles, sound events). A new machine goes in its own module like `demo/machine.js`. File map and build: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md#build-notes).\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide what is designed, its figures, the flaw or turn (if any), the voice, the one real thing (or none), the camera path, the opening, the ending and the length.\n\nAll far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a city through revisions** (one site plan, REV. 1 to REV. 6, each revision a decade, the title block filling with dates); **two sheets, one table** (two designs drawn in parallel that turn out to fit together); **a teardown in reverse** (the sheet starts full and parts are erased one by one until only the centre line of the idea remains).\n- Openings: **a single centre line** crossing an empty sheet on the first beat; **the stamp first** (REJECTED lands, then we see what was rejected); **tracing paper** lifted off a finished drawing to reveal a blank sheet underneath.\n- Endings: **the sheet folds up** along its creases into a pocket-sized square; **a coffee ring** lands on the title block and the tide line becomes the last circle of the design; **the lights go off** and the white lines glow faintly on the dark sheet.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nPatent Pending: The Cloud Catcher\n"},{"id":"S4-L37","name":"彩色玻璃窗","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L37.jpg","markdown":"# L37 彩色玻璃窗（Stained Glass）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 图形与排版\n- 长什么样：阳光从清晨移到黄昏，照到哪一格花窗，哪一格的故事就动起来。\n- 适合：光、庄重\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Stained Glass — Style Prompt\n\n> A medieval stained-glass window seen from inside a dark stone hall, alive only where the sun touches it: light is the clock, the camera and the story.\n> References (grammar only): the window prologue of *Beauty and the Beast* (1991) (chapters pane by pane, a storyteller's pace); the glass knight of *Young Sherlock Holmes* (1985) (glass that cracks and recombines piece by piece); 12th–13th-century Chartres glass (colour logic, grisaille, leading). Copy none of their characters, compositions, subjects or music.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n- **Cut pieces of coloured glass in dark lead cames**, faces and folds **painted in brown grisaille**.\n- Glass has no light of its own: it **glows only when the sun is behind it** and throws a soft coloured copy onto the stone floor; unlit, it is dark and milky.\n- Figures move **piece by piece**, rigid and stepped.\n- The room is always there: stone, a splayed embrasure, iron saddle bars, a floor that catches light.\n- **Secular**: no crosses, saints, halos or other religious imagery; borrow the craft, not the subjects.\n\nNot vector art with black outlines, not a kaleidoscope or Voronoi filter, not Tiffany Art Nouveau, not mosaic (mosaic reflects; glass transmits).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\nOne formula for everything (a WebGL compositor), so every element sits in the same light:\n\n```\nglass   = tonemap( backlight(x,y,time) × transmittance × glass texture ) + weathering haze × room light\nsurface = albedo × (room ambient + glow spilled from lit panes + point lights)          // stone, lead, iron\nfloor   = flagstones × (ambient + projected colour-patch map, blurred + halo)            // perspective or top-down\n+ additive light shafts with dust · bloom (bright glass eats into the lead: the \"irradiation\" effect)\n```\n\n- **Hue-preserving tone mapping**: per-channel `1-exp(-x)` turns cobalt pastel; blend to per-channel only at the very top so a sun disc burns white while blue stays jewel blue.\n- **Unlit panes**: weak sky light + room-lit weathering haze, figures ghostly but readable. **Lit panes**: backlight ~2–3×, a soft, slightly skewed moving band edge.\n- **Pieces**: smooth Catmull-Rom cuts (no deep concave corners). **Every piece is textured** (`multiply` in its clip): streaks at a per-piece angle, hue drift (±10 %), darker aged edges by the lead, ±9 % thickness variation. The shader adds seed bubbles, scratches and thickness clouds in world space; fade small features below ~0.6× zoom or they sparkle.\n- **Lead**: near-black, one planar network, thinner on small pieces, round joins, faint grey flange, solder blobs; only where colours meet (one colour = one piece).\n- **Grisaille**: tapered trace lines in translucent dark brown; **matting** (stippled wash, graded shade, soft band inside the lead); **scraped highlights** (a thin stroke beside each fold in the piece's own colour: the only highlight transmitted light allows). Patterns (mail, scales, plates) are grisaille arcs and bands.\n- **Faces**: almond eyes, heavy upper lid, big pupils, brow flowing into the nose. **Expression lives in brows and mouth**; change it by swapping the face piece, never morphing.\n- **Backgrounds**: hand-cut **lozenge quarries** (jittered lattice, shared vertices, a painted motif each); borders of a coloured band with pearls and a white fillet with a running vine.\n- **Room**: warm grey ashlar, a lighter embrasure, mullions; floor of **large irregular flagstones**, never the wall's running bond.\n- **Floor projection**: blurred, ~20 % desaturated, modulated by the stone, haloed, mirrored (the window's top lands farthest into the room). **Shafts**: additive with dust, near 0 at the window, peaking ~40 % down.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- Two hues **carry the picture** (one deep, one hot); three or four more only as **small touches**. Example (Chartres logic): cobalt ground, ruby, touches of gold, green, murrey.\n- Greenish white glass sparingly (faces, fillets, sky). **Flesh darker than you think**, or backlit faces burn white.\n- A story light (candle, lamp, screen) may take a colour no glass uses, so it reads as a source.\n- **The sun's colour is the clock**: blue-white dawn, near-white brightest noon, gold afternoon, deep orange dusk (raise intensity rather than redness), weak blue moonlight. Patch length follows sun height.\n- Stone and lead take colour only from spilled or projected light.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Subtitles**: a parchment **banderole** near the bottom (curled ends, gentle sag, thin rule), IM Fell English ~44 px in dark ink. It unfurls from the centre; text fades in only once it is ≥ 75 % open; pad the ribbon well past the text. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- **Titles**: incised Roman capitals (Cinzel 600) in the stone, dark cut, light lower lip, gilding, revealed by a moving band of light; small lines ≥ 30 px on screen. In the glass, text only as grisaille on a white-glass scroll.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Figures step at 8 fps**; light, camera, shafts and dust move on ones (24 fps): stepped light reads as flicker.\n- Rigid pieces on 2D FK (`setTransform(base · T(part))`, free draw order); long bodies are spine chains of banded pieces.\n- **Freeze rule**: a pane's figures use `min(t, t_lightLeft)`. Actions land on the beat.\n- **Crack**: jagged lines race from the impact in ~¼ s, bright with light, a short white flash and a 2-frame shake.\n- **Re-leading**: piece groups slide one beat each with a small outward bump along the lead, each ending in a weld spark; cracks become mending leads.\n- Glass never bends or morphs; things appear by being lit, not faded in.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | Expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Truck sideways with the band of light | Time passing, the story moving on | a sequence of steps; a day; chapters |\n| Locked close-up on one pane | This panel is \"now\" | a line being read; a character's decision; a detail |\n| Pull back to the whole window | The rule, or the whole story at once | learning the system; a summary; aftermath |\n| Tilt down the shafts to the floor, then top-down | The picture leaves the glass and becomes light | an event too big for its frame; a dream; a memory |\n| Hold through a crack, no cut | The event is the medium breaking | a turning point; a loss; a revelation |\n| Slow push toward a small light | Attention narrowing to one source | a discovery; a secret; hope |\n| Tilt up to a rose or tracery | Something above the panels governs them | a clock; a cycle; an overview |\n| Rack between two panes, one lit, one dark | Past and present side by side | before/after; two lives; cause and effect |\n| Low angle along the embrasure, glass raking | The window as architecture, scale | awe; an institution; a long history |\n| Return to an earlier framing under new light | The rhyme shows what changed | growth; nightfall; repair |\n\nFraming: subjects big (close-ups ~1.3–2.3× a lancet width); in a narrow lancet stack figures diagonally; a revealed thing near centre, ≥ ~1/8 frame width with glow. Transitions: moving light, shafts, a crack, darkness; never a dissolve, wipe or flip.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Instruments**: organ (pedal drone, soft stops, full), harp, hand chimes, tubular bells, recorder or a chant-like voice, water-glass notes, timpani; also hurdy-gurdy drone, psaltery, portative organ. No generic piano or strings.\n- **Mode families**: church modes (Dorian, Mixolydian, Aeolian, Lydian), open fifths, drones, parallel organum.\n- **Options**: free unmetred chant for darkness; one note meaning \"the light\" each time a pane wakes; an organ pedal that holds and then cuts out; a rising scale, one step per lit piece.\n- **Foley, material first** (synthesizable): struck glass = inharmonic partials ~1 : 2.32 : 4.25 : 6.63 : 9.38; crack = snap + pane thump + rain of tinkles; lead = stick-slip creak; glass sliding in a came = gritty band noise + faint squeal; solder = hiss + tick; air on light moves; stone-hall ambience; birds in the vault; iron ticking as it cools.\n- **Silence**: a hard stop, reverb tails included, only the glass's own ring or creak inside; a single long-reverb bell can close it.\n- **Mix**: big and reverberant; heavy hits avoid word onsets; music ducked ~9 dB under voice; −14 LUFS. **Voice**: a gentle, even storyteller, few short lines.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Light is time, and the camera.** Only the lit pane is alive; figures freeze when the light leaves. *Fits content like:* the steps of a recipe; a factory's shifts; four seasons of one farm.\n- **Glass projects onto stone.** A key scene plays in the floor patch, which stretches as the sun sinks. *Fits content like:* a memory seen larger than life; a sports play replayed as shadows; a data trend drawn by lengthening light.\n- **Glass cracks, lead mends.** Pieces slide along the leads into a new picture, leaving scars. *Fits content like:* a company's reinvention; an injury and recovery; an old building restored.\n- **A window holds its own light.** At night one pane glows from a source inside the story. *Fits content like:* a lighthouse keeper; a night-shift nurse; a server that never sleeps.\n- **The window as a diagram.** Lancets as columns, a rose as a wheel or dial, the border as a timeline. *Fits content like:* a product lineup; the parts of a cycle; a family tree.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Hidden leads show through front pieces → erase the surface under each piece before stroking its lead.\n- Pastel glass → hue-preserving tone mapping; darker flesh. Red dusk × blue glass = mud → keep blue alive, raise intensity.\n- Voronoi reads as a filter; flat fills read as clip-art → hand-cut lozenges, texture on every piece, heavy grisaille.\n- A sharp floor patch looks like a decal → blur, desaturate, stone-modulate, halo. Shafts at full strength at the window wash out the pane.\n- Top-down projection is flipped → put the wall at the frame bottom so figures stand upright (mirrored, correct).\n- Patch-map canvas size mismatched with the code draws into a corner of the texture → one constant size.\n- Carved text in a dark wide is unreadable → push in, park a light on it.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `glass.js` (pieces, textures, lead, grisaille, lozenges), `comp.js` (WebGL2 compositor), `window.js` (lancets, rose, borders, wall, carving), `scene.js` (camera, floor projection, shafts, dust), `subs.js` (banderole), `test.js` / `frames.js` (model sheets and style frames from the real engine), `music/score.py`, `mix.py` (glass/lead foley). `knight.js` / `dragon.js` show the rigid-piece FK rig pattern; build your own subjects with it.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the window (lancets, a rose, a medallion, a clerestory), the subjects, the order of light, opening, ending, camera path and pacing. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a rose window as a dial** (petals lit in turn like hours, the centre the answer); **one tall lancet read bottom to top** (a tilt up a life or a process, medallion by medallion); **two windows facing each other** (the sun lights one side in the morning, the other in the evening; the floor between them holds their meeting).\n- Openings: **the floor first** (only a coloured patch on stone; tilt up to find its source); **a lit window from outside at night** (a figure passes a glowing pane, then we are inside); **the glazier's bench** (a single piece cut and leaded, becoming the first piece of the picture).\n- Endings: **clouds cover the sun** (every pane fades to grey at once; one line lingers); **a new pane leaded in** (an empty lancet filled with the last scene); **the camera leaves through the door** (the window small behind, its patch still on the floor).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nThe Dragon of the East Window\n"},{"id":"S4-L38","name":"象形运动图形","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L38.jpg","markdown":"# L38 象形运动图形（Pictogram Motion）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 图形与排版\n- 长什么样：2026 亚运会 43 个大项，几何象形人卡着节拍快闪。\n- 适合：小图标卡点，讲步骤、讲数字\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Pictogram Motion — Style Prompt\n\n> A beat-locked flash-card film built from three things only: a **square-grid \"core graphic\"** of circles, half-discs and tiny patterned cells whose rows slide past each other; **geometric pictograms** made of round-capped bars and a disc head; and **bold bilingual type** that slides up out of masks. Every cut lands on a drum hit.\n> References (grammar only): Otl Aicher's 1972 Munich pictograms (geometric athletes); multi-sport-games \"look of the games\" identity systems (a modular pattern + a colour set used for everything); Swiss sports-broadcast graphics (mono meta text, tick-bar progress). Learn the grammar; never copy any real event's pictograms, emblem, slogan, pattern artwork or colour names, and never name them in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nA **catalogue film**: N items (products, dishes, steps, species…) shown one card at a time, fast, on the beat. Each card = **one pictogram doing one action + a huge title + a second-language line + a mono index \"07 / N\"**. Cards group into chapters; each chapter owns one hue, opened by a full-bleed pattern card.\n\nWhat makes a frame read as this style at a glance:\n1. **The grid pattern**: big discs and half-discs spanning square cells, about half the cells carrying a small motif, all in **five tones of one hue**, with **whole rows cut and shifted sideways**: the signature that drives backgrounds and transitions.\n2. **The pictogram figure**: flat, faceless, rounded bars and a disc head; far-side limbs mixed toward the background. Props are discs, rings, capsules, lines.\n3. **Type as architecture**: a 900-weight grotesk title set very large, a heavy second-language line under it, small mono meta text in the corners like a broadcast HUD.\n4. **Beat lock**: every shot starts on a beat, every key action lands on a beat.\n\nMood: premium broadcast identity; flat colour, light grain, one soft light sweep as the only gradient.\n\nNot an isometric infographic (no depth, no 3D), not Swiss motion graphics (the figure and the pattern carry as much as the type), not a cartoon (figures never emote, have no faces), not a data film (numbers are labels, not charts).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Flat Canvas2D**, rendered clean; grain only at mux time. **One square grid** (cell ≈ 1/6 of the frame height) rules bands, transitions and walls.\n- **Pattern model**: fill with a mid-dark tone; on grid nodes randomly place cell-radius discs in random tones and discs-with-inner-discs; then a fraction of cells get one motif from a library of generic geometry (quarter-discs, fish-scale waves, dot and triangle scales, concentric arcs, sun wheels, checkers, crescents…), rotated by multiples of 90°; never mascots, landmarks or logos. Draw it wider than the screen so any row can shift one cell.\n- **Figure model**: unit = body height, origin at the hip; draw far limbs (mixed ~40 % toward the background), torso, near limbs, head. A second person sits behind, mixed further (~60 %).\n- **Stage**: the figure stands on a large **sun disc** in a neighbouring tone, crossed by one faint light sweep.\n- **Figure environments** (water, nets, ramps) live in the figure's own buffer, masked to zero before the text column.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **A set of five hues + one dark neutral**, each hue in **five tones** (darkest → lightest, the middle one is the base). A chapter = one hue; a multi-hue chapter rotates through the set per card.\n- Under type the pattern runs at **low contrast**; on chapter cards at **full contrast**.\n- **Light hues take dark type and dark figures**, and their disc uses the lighter tone; dark hues take cream. On the dark neutral, the disc uses a lighter tone or it vanishes; its accent is one warm hue.\n- One cream and one ink serve every hue. No outlines, no highlights except the sweep.\n- Choose the set from the topic. Examples: warm festival (red, violet, forest green, gold, ochre); coastal (navy, teal, coral, sand, sky); kitchen (tomato, basil, saffron, aubergine, flour cream). Invent names for your hues; never borrow an event's colour names.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- Title: a 900-weight grotesk (e.g. Inter Tight), 150–330 px at 1080p, tight tracking, auto-fitted. Second language: a heavy face under it. Meta: a mono (e.g. DM Mono) ~22 px, wide tracking; tags as mono chips.\n- **HUD** on every card: mono corner text and a **progress bar of N ticks** (current tall, done dimmed, rest faint), N derived from the item list.\n- **Bilingual rule**: one language is always the big title; the second language is chosen per version by a switch, never by hand-editing cards.\n- The card type *is* the text layer. With narration, captions are a mono line on a solid band in the chapter hue, bottom, never over the title; hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s). A card that must be read (a spec, a number) holds ≥ 1.6 s.\n- Deliver an `.srt` of the title cards, generated from the edit list.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **A grid of time**: pick one tempo for the film; every shot starts on a beat and every key action in a pose (release, contact, landing) lands on an integer beat.\n- **Pace ladder**: normal cards last one bar; related sub-items become half-length rapid-fire runs. A second tempo comes from dividing the beat, never from changing tempo.\n- Easing: exponential ease-out for entrances, exponential in-out for whips and wipes; a back ease once at most.\n- **Type enters by mask slide-up**: title first, second line ~0.08 s later, tag after; meta lines type on without a caret. Short cards compress all timings.\n- **Figure assembly**: the figure arrives as horizontal bands sliding into register from alternating sides.\n- **Backgrounds breathe**: rows drift in alternating directions; on chapter cards they jump a quarter-cell on beats.\n- **Transitions** snap to the grid: sliding rows, dropping columns, a push, quarter-discs from cell corners, an iris from the last figure, a tile flip. Rotate them; each lasts a few frames around the cut.\n- **Motion blur only where things move**: sub-frame averaging inside transition and whip windows; static frames render once.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nMostly a locked flat frame; energy comes from row shifts, transitions and figure actions. When the camera moves, it moves along the grid. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Locked flat frame | clarity, the card as a poster | any item; a spec that must be read |\n| Lateral track with a one-screen whip per beat | one continuous family, momentum | a line of stations; a production line; a route |\n| Slow drift along a row | calm, browsing | a gallery; a menu; a slower chapter |\n| Vertical scroll by grid rows | ranking, depth, descent | a top-ten; floors of a building; ocean depths |\n| Push into one motif cell until it becomes the next frame | zooming into a part | an ingredient inside a dish; a city inside a map |\n| Pull back to a tiled wall of items | the whole set at once | a summary; \"all of them\"; a comparison |\n| Tiles collapsing to one point | many become one | a merger; a shared goal; a count reaching zero |\n| Split screen along a grid line | two items side by side | versus; before/after; two versions |\n\nFraming: text and figure never overlap (one text column, one stage); horizontal poses move the stage outward. The HUD stays in the corners. Allowed transitions are the grid ones in §5; never dissolves, 3D camera moves, handheld shake or depth of field.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Type and music can carry the film alone**; a voice is optional, calm, one line per chapter at most.\n- **Percussion is the spine**: choose a drum family that suits the topic (frame drums, taiko, marching snares, steel pans, body percussion, an electronic kit) plus rim clicks, claps, shakers and crashes.\n- Colour instruments on top: Karplus-Strong plucks (any lute, koto or harp-like voice, with pitch bends), mallets (marimba, glockenspiel), detuned-saw chord stabs, a synth bass, pads, risers and a drone.\n- **Per-family accents**: every shot onset gets a hit chosen by chapter (a splash, a pok, a clank, a crack, a whoosh, a sizzle): foley reads the chapter's matter.\n- **Picture-locked by data**: the score reads the edit list's shot times and places accents there; check every onset to ±15 ms.\n- **Silence as a tool**: half a beat of drums-and-bass-muted before a big hit, or one whole bar stripped to a single ticking sound under a count.\n- Mix: glue compressor and limiter, −14 LUFS, ≤ −1 dBTP.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your film needs.\n\n- **One card = one item.** The count is the hook (\"ALL N\"). *Fits content like:* every tool in a workshop; every station of a subway line; every dish of a regional cuisine.\n- **Chapter = colour.** Families become hues so the viewer always knows where they are. *Fits content like:* instrument sections of an orchestra; food groups; departments of a company.\n- **One verb per item.** One body action readable in a bar, or a prop action (a gear turns, a cup pours). *Fits content like:* yoga poses; kitchen techniques; jobs on a construction site.\n- **Pace ladder run.** A sub-family at double speed. *Fits content like:* four knife cuts; the bones of a hand; weekday routines.\n- **Long take down a track.** Stations one screen apart, the camera whipping on each cut. *Fits content like:* the steps of a recipe; checkpoints of a marathon; stages of a rocket launch.\n- **Count-up slam.** A big number rolls to its value on a bar. *Fits content like:* tonnes recycled; members of a club; years of a company.\n- **Icon wall.** All pictograms return as a grid and turn one colour. *Fits content like:* a summary of a year; a periodic table of habits; a team roster.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Figure environments spread into the text column → render the figure layer into its own buffer and fade it out with a gradient mask before the text.\n- Cream on light hues fails contrast → dark type and dark figures on light hues; their disc is the lighter tone.\n- The sun disc vanishes on the dark neutral → use a lighter tone for it.\n- Pattern canvases are large and seeded per card → cache with a bounded LRU; an unbounded cache runs out of memory with parallel renderers.\n- Horizontal poses (swimming, lying, crawling) cross the title → move the stage outward and narrow the text column.\n- Imitating a real identity too closely → your own palette, mark and slogan from the grid motifs.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `engine.js` (`G.pattern`, `G.motif`, `G.joints` / `G.drawFigure`, `G.keyPose`, `G.maskText`, easings), `poses/base.js` (pose contract, props `G.P`), `edl.js` (edit list in beats), `scenes.js` (layouts, transitions, HUD, motion blur), `lab.mjs` (pose sheets), `music/music.py` (picture-locked score with onset report). API table, pose convention and a minimal example: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md#engine-reference).\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the items, grouping, verbs, palette, chapter order, any long take, the pace ladder, opening, ending, music and voice. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a countdown** (items ranked from N to 1, the camera scrolling down rows, the pace speeding up as the list shortens); **a day in pictograms** (one figure, one card per hour, the palette moving from dawn to night hues); **an assembly** (each card adds one part to a single growing pictogram machine seen in the corner).\n- Openings: **a single motif cell** (one cell fills the frame and splits into the grid); **mid-action** (the first pictogram is already running when the type slams in); **a blank grid** (hairlines draw the grid on the beat before any colour appears).\n- Endings: **one card held long** (the last item stays while the pattern rows stop, one by one); **the figure walks off** (the last pictogram exits the frame and the HUD ticks out); **reverse assembly** (the grid disassembles into rows that slide off-screen, leaving cream).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build, engine reference): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nAichi-Nagoya 2026 — All 43 Sports\n"},{"id":"S4-L39","name":"ASCII · CRT 终端","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L39.jpg","markdown":"# L39 ASCII / CRT 终端（ASCII / CRT Terminal）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 图形与排版\n- 长什么样：月球基地的 AI 沉睡 40 年后被唤醒，用字符画出“家”来回复。\n- 适合：讲代码、讲 AI，和样片库 E3 字符画一起看\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# ASCII / CRT Terminal: Style Prompt\n\n> Everything on screen is a real character on a monospace grid, lit by the phosphor of an old monochrome CRT.\n> References (grammar only): 1970s–80s monochrome terminals and teletype logs (typed output, cursor, status lines); ASCII art and demoscene text-mode pictures (images from glyph density); analog-synth film scores of the early 1980s (cold pulse, patient drones). Copy no film's interface text, fonts or logos, and use no real agency names.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nA single monochrome terminal screen: **P3 amber** (or P1 green) phosphor glowing on dark glass, curved at the edges, with scanlines. Every image, including title, landscape, face, room and subtitles, is made of **real glyphs placed on a character grid** and chosen by ink density. The film has one camera, the screen itself. It moves only by changing the **font size of the grid**: bigger characters push in, smaller characters pull out.\n\nThe mood is cold, patient and machine-calm: typed text, a blinking cursor as suspense, and one warm surprise.\n\n**Never** fake it with an image plus an \"ASCII filter\". Characters must be drawn as characters on a grid. Not a hacker-movie green rain, not a pixel-art game (no sprites, no free pixels), not a sci-fi HUD (no vector lines, no free-floating panels).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Font**: one monospace bitmap-style font (VT323, OFL); cell 0.4 em × 0.8 em (1:2). A large prerendered glyph atlas with a stroke for beam spread, scaled with `drawImage`, keeps zooms continuous and sharp.\n- **Grids**: column count per shot (title ~20, terminal 80, picture 200+); a push-in can go until one cell holds a sub-grid of its own.\n- **Density ramp**: each glyph's measured ink coverage × dim / normal / bold → about 20 levels, near-duplicates dropped; a small ordered-plus-hash dither per cell; cells below a threshold stay blank. **Negative space makes ASCII art readable.**\n- **Picture pipeline**: paint grayscale into an offscreen \"negative\" aligned exactly to the grid (several samples per cell; channels for phosphor, second colour, a flag), average per cell, pick a glyph. Interiors get local contrast (`m + 0.9·(m − mean)`) to keep outlines.\n- **Recognisable subjects**: silhouettes, not noise (real coastline polygons, a face from a few planes); dark = sparse dim glyphs, bright = dense bold, shadow sides sparse `.` only; an angle with a clear silhouette.\n- **Depth on a grid**: far = darker and sparser, near = brighter and denser; a thin bright rim separates planes.\n- **CRT post** (WebGL, adapted from `core/post/crt.js`):\n  - **Phosphor colour**: luminance maps along the phosphor's ramp (dim deep orange → amber → overexposed near-white for P3).\n  - **Glow**: multi-level bloom plus a faint halo.\n  - **Glass**: fine scanlines (narrower on bright areas), slight barrel curvature, rounded mask, warm near-black unlit glass, faint reflection, ~1 % flicker, fine noise. **No RGB grille: monochrome tubes have none.**\n- **Afterglow**: text that disappears decays with τ ≈ 0.1 s, computed analytically so every frame is deterministic. Moving glyphs get one faint trail sample.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **One phosphor** carries everything: amber, green or white, in its own brightness ramp. No second hue for decoration.\n- **At most one soft second colour**, used for one thing only (the emotional object), switched on at a single moment, on a downbeat, and never taken back casually.\n- Examples: amber screen with an ice blue-white for something far away; green screen with a warm pink for a living thing; white P4 screen with a pale gold for a memory.\n- Brightness is the only other tool: dim / normal / bold and inverse video.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Subtitles are terminal output.** A reserved **LOG strip** at the bottom: a dim separator, an inverse-video tag, then a prompt and the line in uppercase at ~45 px. It is **typed word by word in sync with the voice** (whisper word timestamps) and holds ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s). It lives outside the zooming grid, like a status line, with the same scanlines and glow. When nobody speaks it can show system state (a progress bar, a waiting message).\n- **On-screen text that the story shows** is picture, not subtitle; in the `.srt` it appears as `[SCREEN] …`.\n- **Titles** are typed with key clicks, as a filename, a command or a header.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Characters never move off-grid, except in a declared transformation moment.** Typing, scrolling and counters jump from cell to cell. A roller flips digits discretely, fast then slowing.\n- **Only three things move continuously**: grid scale (the camera), CRT physics, and particles in a transformation.\n- **Typing speed is performance**: machine output is even and fast (~10–40 ms/char); dramatic lines slow to note values of the score; human typing is hand-timed, uneven, with pauses and bursts.\n- **Particles** spawn from random points inside source glyphs, start small, fade in, ease to their cell, and flash briefly on landing, so they never stack into bright bars.\n- **Push to a character**: hold where the whole glyph reads, then push until its inner text reads; inner text full brightness, outside text a few percent, neighbours dimmed, so the shape reads before the text.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nThe only camera is the grid scale, plus CRT power states and one possible shot outside the screen.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Static 80-column terminal | Waiting, procedure, machine time | a countdown; a queue; a log being read |\n| Push in (cells grow) | Attention, intimacy | one line of dialogue; a name; a decision typed |\n| Pull out (cells shrink) | Context, scale | a title becoming line 1 of a list; one entry among thousands |\n| Push into a single glyph | Hidden depth; what a symbol is made of | a letter made of messages; a pixel made of people |\n| Scroll (the buffer moves) | Accumulation, time passing | years of records; a changelog; a feed |\n| Clear screen / hard redraw | A cut; a new state | a reboot; a new chapter; a crash |\n| Text becomes image (grid shrinks as glyphs fall) | Words turning into world | a letter becoming a face; data becoming a map |\n| Power on / off, degauss | Birth, death, sleep, shock | a machine waking; an ending; a jolt |\n| Pull back off the glass into a drawn room | The screen is an object in a world | who is reading; where the machine is |\n\nOpening and ending come from the topic. Framing: text stays in the safe area inside the curvature; keep the LOG strip clear. Transitions happen on the screen (clear, redraw, scroll, power), never a crossfade between two screens.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Music**: an original analog-synth score, rendered in code (numpy / numba): PolyBLEP saw and square oscillators, a resonant 4-pole ladder filter with moving cutoff, filter envelopes, square-wave sequencers, slow tape wow (± a few cents), long reverb on pads, gated reverb on hits; options also a sub-bass drone, sample-and-hold noise, a detuned unison lead. **Never chiptune.**\n- **Technique options**: grow a single arpeggio as an image forms; hold the only major chord for one moment; drop everything, reverb tails included, to digital zero on a shock; open a chord on the colour change.\n- **Silence is the main instrument**: room tone alone under typing and blinking cursors; a short near-silence before a transformation.\n- **Foley** (all synthesised): relay click and degauss hum (mains hum plus harmonics with a thump); buckling-spring keys (plastic click, spring ping, key-cap thock; duller space bar, heavier Enter); teletype ticks for remote text; modem and phone-line tones (answer tone, FSK, band-limited noise); square-wave error beeps and BEL; clusters of tiny high ticks for particles; a dot-matrix printer or tape drive as options; room tone (fan, low hum, a **very quiet** flyback whine).\n- **Mix**: music ducked about −8 dB under the voice; master −14 LUFS with no grain in the mux (the noise lives in the picture).\n- **Voice**: when a machine speaks, process it (light ring modulation, a short comb, a telephone-ish band-pass, soft saturation) and check that whisper still reads every line. Few short lines.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **The picture is made of words.** A sentence breaks apart and its letters, sorted by ink density, become an image containing only those letters. *Fits content like:* a manifesto becoming a crowd; a recipe becoming the dish; a poem becoming a landscape.\n- **Font size = camera.** Push until one character fills the screen and is itself made of smaller text. *Fits content like:* a company name made of employee names; a year made of its headlines; a heart glyph made of messages.\n- **Typing rhythm = editing rhythm.** Even machine typing against uneven human typing; a blinking cursor is a performance. *Fits content like:* a job application; an apology; a password remembered.\n- **CRT physics.** Power on as dot → line → frame, off to a dot; afterglow, bloom, curved glass. *Fits content like:* a hospital monitor; a night shift ending; an old archive reopened.\n- **A single colour change.** One soft second colour appears once. *Fits content like:* a first green leaf in a data log; a blood-orange sunset in a weather report; a child's drawing in a server room.\n- **The log file.** Timestamps, error codes, progress bars tell the story. *Fits content like:* a product launch day; a rescue; a migration.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **Inverse video**: `destination-out` only changes alpha, which the upload ignores → opaque black canvas, cut letters with a *black* atlas.\n- **Negative not aligned to the grid** drifts across hundreds of columns → map with cell width / samples exactly.\n- **Wrong cell centre**: cells are 2·cw tall, so the centre is `(j + .5)·2cw`, not `(j + 1)·cw`.\n- **Every dark pixel becomes a character** (`-+-+` walls) → threshold, gamma ≈ 1.25, local contrast.\n- **Noise-textured subjects read as textured blobs** → silhouettes, a dark sparse background, a dot-only shadow side.\n- **Particles from letter centres stack into bars** → random points inside the glyph, small, faded in.\n- **Stroked atlas glyphs close their counters** (`M`, `B`) → sample a thin fill-only glyph bitmap for masks.\n- **Lazily built data never reaches the exporter** → build particle schedules and their sound events before `window.EV` is exported.\n- **A power-on parameter at 0 still draws the centre dot** → exposure 0 before power-on, multiply the dot term by exposure.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `term.js` (glyph atlas, grid drawing, density ramps, image → cells), `art.js` (procedural grayscale scenes for the negative), `crt.js` (monochrome phosphor CRT post, WebGL2), `main.js` (timeline, events `EV`, subtitles `SUBS`), `voice_fx.py` (machine-voice processing), `mix.py` (synthesised foley + ducking), `music/score.py` (analog-synth score), `tools/subs.mjs` (export `window.SUBS`). File map and commands: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md#build-notes).\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide who types, what the screen is for, the grids, the opening, the ending, the camera (grid) path, the pacing and whether the second colour appears at all. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a chat log** (two users typing to each other across one night, timestamps as scene headers); **a system manual** (chapter headers, diagrams in ASCII, one page going wrong); **a boot-to-shutdown day** (one machine's day as a sequence of scheduled jobs, each drawing a picture of what it does).\n- Openings: **mid-scroll** (a buffer already racing past; it stops on one line); **a printout** (a dot-matrix sheet feeding up, then the screen takes over); **an error first** (a crash dump, then we rewind to what caused it).\n- Endings: **the buffer fills** (the story's text scrolls up and away until only the prompt remains); **the screen saver** (after the last line, a slow drifting pattern takes over the idle screen); **the cursor stops blinking** (no power-off, just a final state held).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nTranquility.log\n"},{"id":"S4-L40","name":"数据叙事","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L40.jpg","markdown":"# L40 数据叙事（Data Storytelling）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 信息与发布\n- 长什么样：一百年的夏季气温，图表本身就是故事。\n- 适合：用图表讲故事\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Data Storytelling — Style Prompt\n\n> A chart that tells a story: real data, drawn mark by mark on cream paper by a red–blue pencil, with handwritten notes pinned to the numbers. The chart itself is the camera, the rhythm and the plot.\n> References (grammar only): Hans Rosling's *200 Countries, 200 Years, 4 Minutes* (one dot is a character), Ed Hawkins' warming stripes (a diverging ramp on a reference mean, no axes), The Pudding / NYT scrollytelling (one idea at a time, growing axes, pinned notes). Copy none of their charts, layouts or typefaces; never name them in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n**The chart is the film.** Every mark is a real number and every movement is a meaningful chart operation (a mark lands, an axis grows, the scale changes, a series morphs, a note is pinned). The data is real, openly licensed and traceable. You change the framing, never the numbers.\n\n**Pick the chart the data asks for:**\n\n| The data is about | Chart |\n|---|---|\n| comparing categories, rankings | bars, a ranked bar race |\n| a relationship between two measures | scatter or bubble chart (one dot can be a character) |\n| place | map, choropleth, dots on a map |\n| parts of a whole, counts of people | unit / waffle chart, proportional squares |\n| before → after | slope or dumbbell chart |\n| a distribution | histogram, beeswarm |\n| where things go | flow / Sankey |\n| change over time | line or dot series, stripes, area |\n\nThree layers make it a story, not an infographic: **a human scale** (handwritten notes pinned to marks), **a performer** (a physical pencil that draws every mark), and **sonification** (each mark is a pitch; the trend is heard before it is read).\n\nNot a dashboard (one idea at a time), not a keynote with charts (no slides), never decoration.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Paper**: warm cream, fixed-seed fibre noise and a faint dot grid in world space. **Ink**: dark warm ink for axes and series, pale warm grey for grid and zero line, IBM Plex Mono ticks.\n- **Marks**: data dots carry a thin ink ring so pale values stay visible on cream. A mark lands with a short pop and a thin ripple; suppress ripples when marks arrive closer than ~0.1 s.\n- **Handwriting** (Caveat) in pencil, per-glyph jitter, graphite grain, written on as the tip dances over the x-height. Leaders are wobbly curves ending in an **un-closed** ring around the mark.\n- **The red–blue pencil**: hex body, blue half / red half, sharpened wood cone. Only the tip end shows, from the lower right; its shadow drifts farther and softer with lift; sharp tip, soft body.\n- **Layout**: a plot box fixed in world space, a **memory zone** for early notes, every note pinned in **data space** (value + pixel offset) so rescales keep the layout, a bottom band for the caption.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- Colour encodes **one thing: the value**. Diverging data uses a ramp (ColorBrewer RdBu family) centred on the mean of a meaningful reference period, with a symmetric span; data with no natural centre uses one sequential ramp. Everything else is ink on cream.\n- **Honesty beats drama**: pale values stay pale; never stretch a ramp to make a change look bigger.\n- The two pencil leads have meanings: **blue for memories and human notes, red for records and warnings**. A change of lead marks a turn.\n- One `accent` colour may bypass the ramp for a mark that is not data (a logo, a highlight).\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **The subtitle is the figure caption**: a text serif (Newsreader), ink colour, left-aligned with the chart, ≤ 2 lines, with a hairline rule above it and a small mono kicker for the range drawn so far. Words appear on their whisper timestamps over a paper-colour band. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- The title is the **chart's own headline** (Newsreader), set in world space above the plot; it fades when the camera is close and returns with the full view.\n- No captions over a signature chart operation or a silence.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **The rhythm of the data is the rhythm of the film.** Map the marks to a musical grid; to accelerate, one option is to **divide the beat instead of changing tempo**. Pick a tempo whose finest subdivision lands on whole frames (at 24 fps a minute has 1440 frames; the count of finest notes per minute must divide it), so picture and sound never drift.\n- **Pencil acting**: lift before each tap, land with the shadow meeting the tip, a tiny rebound; hurry as data densifies, tremble like a seismograph needle at a peak. It can **flinch** (recoil and freeze), **hesitate** (hover before a mark) and **exit** (hand the story to the viewer).\n- **Rescale** reads as ratchet clicks (stepped, each step eased); a calm rescale is smooth.\n- **Morph**: a staggered sweep, each mark easing into its new form; notes fade as it passes and return as tiny marks.\n- **No fades to blank or dissolves.** Transitions are chart operations: axis growth, pull-backs, rescales, morphs, a cell widening into the next frame.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA 2D camera `{x, y, zoom, roll}` over **one sheet of paper**. A film is one take; a hard cut is rare and meaningful. The moves below are a vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | Expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Extreme close-up on one mark, following its leader | One fact, one life | a single case; a named person |\n| Full view first, then dive to one mark | The pattern before the case | a familiar trend; a myth to correct |\n| Pull back as axes grow out of the marks | The frame arrives with the unit | naming the measure; widening a claim |\n| Track the pencil, newest mark at ~60 % of width | Progress: the past behind, the future ahead | a series in time; a race; a queue |\n| Pedestal with rising data, a Dutch angle creeping in | Escalation, unease | runaway growth; a debt |\n| Punch-in over 2 frames, decaying shake, jolt on rescale | Surprise, a broken frame | an outlier; a crash |\n| Fast pull-out, roll, small constant tremor | A rush | a boom; a cascade; a panic |\n| Locked-off | The transformation is the movement | a morph; a slow reading |\n| Hold on one mark, then pull back | The part inside the whole | a verdict; context |\n| Pull and pan to add more real data | Scale switch | a longer record; the rest of the list |\n| Lateral pan across notes in the memory zone | Recollection | a biography; a timeline of events |\n\nFraming: subjects fill ≥ 1/3 of frame height at key moments. When the real axis scrolls off-screen, pin its tick labels to a paper strip at the frame edge (frozen pane).\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Ambience** follows the places the notes evoke (J-cuts in, L-cuts out). **Foley** is graphite, paper and wood: tick-and-paper taps, scratches for handwriting, a ruler hiss for axes, a paper puncture for a broken frame, a wooden click for the pencil flip, ratchet teeth for a rescale, a paper sweep for a morph.\n- **Sonification** is the music: pitch = value, rhythm = time density. Low values snap to a pentatonic scale, middle ones to semitones, extreme ones unquantized, with detune growing away from the reference. Timbre: a **low-pass-gate \"plonk\"** (sine + FM whose index rises with the value: wood → metal). Dense passages: quieter notes, decay ≤ 1.5 × the gap.\n- **Bed**: detuned-saw pad, soft kick, noise shaker, low-passed hats, sine sub, sample-and-hold blips, drones; harmony can darken or brighten with the data.\n- **Silence** is a tool: true digital silence (all layers) where the data should be heard alone, before a key mark, a morph or a verdict; the first sound after it matters most.\n- **Mix**: compressed calm, neutral narration, music ducked ~−11 dB, foley light except key taps; −14 LUFS, no grain. The voice never talks over a rush, a silence or a morph.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **One mark = one fact.** *Fits content like:* the first store a chain opened; the first patient of a trial; the first satellite launched.\n- **Axes grow with the story.** The y axis arrives when the narration first names the unit. *Fits content like:* revenue; a city's population; vaccination rates.\n- **Pinned annotations.** Their content and colour carry the arc. *Fits content like:* an athlete's season; a family's migrations; releases on a usage curve.\n- **Breaking the frame.** The chart must make room; the torn edge stays as a scar. *Fits content like:* a viral spike; a price bubble; a record-breaking flood.\n- **Encoding morph.** Bars into a waffle, a map into a ranking, a line into a slope: the reveal of the whole. *Fits content like:* a budget as units of people; countries as a ranking; daily steps as a year strip.\n- **Scale switch.** One value → the series → a longer real record, never extrapolated. *Fits content like:* one day's energy use → a year; one species count → a region.\n- **The empty next cell.** A dashed slot for the value nobody knows yet. *Fits content like:* next year's election; the next launch; tomorrow's reading.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Select data columns by header name, never by index; print every fact the narration claims.\n- Notes collide with later data → data-space pins, a memory zone.\n- A frame break rescaled too fast is invisible → hold the pierced state ≥ half a beat.\n- Right-aligned notes are still written left to right → the pencil hops to the first letter.\n- Helpers that set `globalAlpha` must multiply the incoming alpha, or fades skip them.\n- Tails leak across a hard silence → render before/after in separate buffers; verify on decoded samples.\n- In dense passages the pencil body lies across fresh marks → steepen its angle.\n- Synthesized human sounds (a laugh) are uncanny → use a soft non-human sound.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\n`demo/engine.js` (Canvas 2D, world units = chart pixels at zoom 1) is the style: paper, camera, value ramps, `pencilStroke` (any polyline in pencil), `inkShape` (any closed shape: bars, areas, map shapes), `plotShape`, `handText`, `dataDot`, `morphMark`, annotation geometry, `drawPencil`, `caption`. `demo/music/score.py` holds the sonification. The function table and a minimal example are in [DEMO.md](DEMO.md#engine-reference).\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the dataset, the chart, the human scale, the notes, the opening, the ending, the camera path, the pacing and the silences. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a ranked race** (bars climb and overtake; one bar is the character; a newcomer breaks the frame from below); **a map that becomes a count** (dots gather on a map as events happen, then fall into a unit chart of people in one morph); **before → after dialogue** (two years face each other as a slope chart; one line left unfinished).\n- Openings: **the finished chart** (the whole pattern is already drawn; the film works backwards); **the object before the axis** (a pencil drawing of the thing measured, a ticket, a shoe, becomes the first mark); **the question first** (an empty plot with only the unit and a handwritten question; the pencil hovers).\n- Endings: **one mark, close** (end on a single value and its note, never the full view); **back to the object** (the chart folds into a drawing of the thing it measured); **the headline revised** (the pencil strikes a word of the title and writes the true one).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nA Hundred Summers\n"},{"id":"S4-L41","name":"等距信息图","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L41.jpg","markdown":"# L41 等距信息图（Isometric Infographic）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 信息与发布\n- 长什么样：一杯咖啡从种植园到你手里的旅程。\n- 适合：一个小世界、一套流程\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Isometric Infographic — Style Prompt\n\n> A system diagram comes alive: an isometric diorama of flat three-tone blocks, thin ink labels and Isotype numbers, where things open up to show their insides and the numbers are drawn, not stated.\n> References (grammar only): *Monument Valley* (true-isometric architecture, flat colour blocks, three fixed tones per face, a camera that pans and zooms but never breaks the projection); *SimCity 2000*-style cutaway dioramas (a floating board with soil strata, the whole system visible at once); Kurzgesagt (a question, a steady push forward, one clear visual metaphor per step, a change of scale when a number is too big to feel); Isotype, Otto Neurath / Gerd Arntz (repeated identical icons stand for quantity); *Powers of Ten* (a continuous dive through nested scales). Use none of their characters, buildings, icons, palettes or music.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n- **Flat, true isometric.** No perspective, no outlines on solids; shapes are separated only by the three tones of each face. The projection never rotates or tilts.\n- **Infographic furniture is part of the picture**: leader lines, label pins, dashed cut lines, hatched section faces, tracking rings, a legend, a route line all live in the world.\n- **Numbers are built from icons** (Isotype), and they add up.\n- **A diorama**: the system sits on a floating board or plinth with a visible cross-section at its edges, on a pale ground.\n\nNot low-poly 3D (no perspective, no lighting beyond three face tones), not a flat 2D explainer (everything has isometric depth), not a blueprint (colour blocks, not line drawings on blue).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Projection**: axes at 30°. `sx = (x − y)·cos30°·k`, `sy = ((x + y)·½ − z)·k`; x runs down-right, y down-left, z up; painter depth = x + y + z. Everything is queued and depth-sorted, then flushed.\n- **Three tones per face** chosen from its normal: top light, left (+y) mid, right (+x) dark. Derive tones from one base colour: mix toward warm white ~20 % for the top and toward a cool dark (e.g. `#231A2C`) ~22 % for the dark side, so shadows are slightly cool.\n- **Ink lines** (2 px) only for infographic elements: leaders, pins, cut lines, rings, routes, labels.\n- **The board**: floats on a pale background with a faint isometric dot lattice and a soft blurred shadow; its front faces show strata (soil, water column, floors, pipes) where the section meets the edge.\n- **Composites** (a terraced hill with shrubs, a crane over a ship) are drawn as one ordered item or given a depth bias; generic painter sorting fails on them.\n- **People**: minimal billboards (round head, tapered two-tone body, a thick arm stroke, a hat or hair shape for identity, no faces), stepping at 12 fps.\n- **Close-ups** of hands or objects: flat faceted shapes in three tones, same logic as the blocks.\n- **Level of detail by zoom**: skip small world detail at high zoom, fade tall objects in by zoom so a pull-out never sweeps a giant shape through the frame.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **A pale neutral ground** (cream, paper grey or pale blue) and **5–7 flat hues**, each with its three tones; one ink colour.\n- **Colour codes meaning**, the way a legend does: one hue per material or role (water is always the same blue, the subject always its hue). Never reuse a hue for two meanings.\n- **The subject's hue is reserved**: when the story needs \"this one\", everything else desaturates toward warm grey and only the subject keeps its colour (focus + context).\n- Stations that must be found in a wide shot get a pale plate with a dashed ink outline plus one accent hue.\n- Examples: a recycling chain — bottle green, kraft brown, can silver-blue, compost umber, mustard on cream; a hospital — teal, coral, lavender, steel grey on pale grey; a data centre — electric blue, amber, graphite, mint on off-white.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- One geometric sans (e.g. Jost): titles 600 all caps with ~0.12 em tracking, numbers 400 large (~44 px), units at half size. All text has a ~7 px halo in the ground colour so it reads over any block.\n- **Label pins**: dot (ink ring, pale fill) → vertical leader → horizontal rule → text. Pins point up or down, left or right, chosen per station so labels never cover the subject.\n- **Titles** can be isometric type on a world plane, extruded as blocks.\n- **The subtitle is a map label**: a pale card with a small radius and an ink border, a tiny iso cube as legend chip, sans ~42 px, bottom-centre; border draws first, text wipes in. Keep the bottom ~170 px clear for it. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s); lines never overlap titles or other lines.\n- **An end frame can be the legend**: title, totals, the Isotype key, credits, \"NOT TO SCALE\".\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Nothing moves without a line leading it**: a route line is drawn slightly ahead, a leader grows before an inset opens, a cut line draws before a skin slides away.\n- **Cutaway**: cut line (~0.3 s) → the near skin slides out along its normal and fades (~0.35 s, ease-out) → hatched section rims fade in over the last part.\n- **Isotype fill**: icons pop with a back-ease (overshoot), one per subdivision of the beat; count-up numbers run over the same span.\n- **Accumulation = acceleration**: arrivals on quarter notes, then eighths, then waves; the eye feels the system taking over. Solids arrive by motion, never by alpha (semi-transparent blocks look like ghosts).\n- **Objects perform**: squash on landing (~18 %) and small bounces; colour changes that show a process (raw → cooked, empty → full).\n- **Vehicles** follow polylines with ease-in-out and face their tangent.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. The camera is a world centre plus a **log-interpolated zoom** k (px per unit); the projection never changes. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Follow along a route | process; cause → effect | a supply chain; a journey; a signal |\n| Hold wide + circular inset | the system and the individual together | one worker in a factory; one cell in an organ |\n| Push through nested cutaways | a fall into scale; finding one thing among millions | a needle in a haystack; inside a machine |\n| Fast log pull-out | release; the big picture after a detail | scale reveal; a statistic made physical |\n| Locked frame while things accumulate | a system filling up; overload | a queue; traffic; data growing |\n| Lateral follow over emptiness | a breath; distance | an ocean; a wait; a quiet phase |\n| Top-to-bottom tilt through strata | layers; depth; history | geology; a city's underground; a software stack |\n| Switch boards (a second diorama) | before/after; here vs there | two cities; two eras; two designs |\n\nFraming: the subject ≥ 1/3 of frame height at key moments; labels never cover it; the subtitle zone stays clear. One continuous take is one option; a cut to a new board or an inset is another.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Music**: acoustic and light: kalimba, marimba, nylon guitar, pizzicato, woodblock and hand percussion (shaker, bongo, cajon), upright or synth bass, glockenspiel, toy piano, or a clean electronic pulse for technical systems. A short motif can mark the subject each time it appears.\n- **Techniques (options)**: one sound per icon as numbers fill; instruments dropping out and a low-pass closing as the camera dives; J-cuts (the next station's sound 0.5 s before it appears); one note per station during a final pull-back.\n- **Ambience beds per station**, crossfaded (wind and birds, harbour, sea, city hum, machine room, crowd murmur).\n- **Foley by material**: fibre snap, fleshy thud, grains pouring, granular rattles, inharmonic steel partials for containers, paper tear, ceramic plink, liquid pour with rising pitch, servo whine, keyboard clicks.\n- **Silence**: digital zero or one faint sound; the first sound after it should be one of the most important in the film.\n- **Mix**: music ducked ~8 dB under voice, ambience ~7 dB; keep high-band beds (2.5–7 kHz) very low under speech or whisper mishears words. Final −14 LUFS.\n- **Voice**: clear, friendly explainer; short lines.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Cutaway.** Cut line, skin slides away, hatched rims remain. *Fits content like:* a heart's chambers; a server rack; a beehive.\n- **Isotype number.** Identical icons in a new arrangement each time (a calendar, a bar, a silhouette formed by the icons themselves). *Fits content like:* votes in an election; litres of water per product; users of an app.\n- **Focus + context.** Everything greys except the subject. *Fits content like:* one patient in a hospital; one packet in a network; one tree in a forest.\n- **Frame within the frame.** A leader grows into a circular inset with a close-up. *Fits content like:* a chip on a circuit board; a worker's hands; a crack in a bridge.\n- **Scale reveal.** Pull back until every label is on screen and the last frame is a finished infographic. *Fits content like:* how a city is powered; a school's day; a festival site.\n- **Tracked subject.** One object followed with a \"you are here\" ring. *Fits content like:* a letter through the post; a drop through a water plant; a vaccine dose.\n- **Strata cross-section.** The board's edge becomes the story (layers added or removed). *Fits content like:* a mine; an archaeological dig; a building's floors.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Newell normals depend on vertex order: faces get culled as back faces → give every face an outward hint and flip the normal to match before culling.\n- Painter sorting breaks on composites → draw them as one ordered item; add depth bias for overhangs.\n- Semi-transparent moving solids look like ghosts → motion only, no alpha.\n- A hull or wheel touching the wrong surface reads as an accident → leave visible clearance.\n- World geometry behind a close-up becomes giant discs → skip detail above a zoom threshold, paint a flat backdrop behind hero layers.\n- A pull-out through a tall object gives one ugly frame → fade tall objects in by zoom.\n- Hidden jumps (a time or place change during a dive) must be drawn in screen space anchored on the tracked subject, so the frame doesn't reveal the jump.\n- An object drawn with the wrong silhouette reads as something else (a white box with a red roof is a house) → give machines their tell-tale parts.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\n`demo/engine.js` is dependency-free Canvas 2D: `Iso` (projection, camera, depth-sorted queue: `box`, `prism`, `cyl`, `cone`, `roof`, `sphere`, `flat`, `line3`, `bill`, `text3`, `push/pop` local frames, `desat` + `keep` one-colour hook), `tri()` three-tone generator, `pin`, `inset`, `cutLine`, `hatch`, `ring`, `ICON.*` + `iconGrid`, `person`, and `shape()` which turns any 2D outline into a shaded isometric solid. API table and a minimal example (a shape the demo never uses): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md) \"Engine reference\".\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the system, the board (or several), whether there is a tracked subject, the numbers, the camera path, the opening and the ending. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **one building, floor by floor** (a tilt down through a cutaway tower, each floor a step); **two boards compared** (the same system in two places or eras, alternating, then side by side); **a cycle** (a loop route that returns to its start with one number changed each lap).\n- Openings: **the finished infographic**, which then comes apart into its moving parts; **one icon** that multiplies until it becomes the landscape; **a question written as an isometric title** on an empty board.\n- Endings: **an unanswered station** (the route stops at an empty plate with a question mark); **the legend alone**, the board faded out; **a close-up of a single icon**, the whole number behind it out of focus.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nFrom Bean to Cup\n"},{"id":"S4-L42","name":"暗色科技发布","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L42.jpg","markdown":"# L42 暗色科技发布（Dark Tech Keynote）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 信息与发布\n- 长什么样：虚构 app Tidy 的发布片：被埋掉的光标一键把几百个窗口归位。\n- 适合：发布会感\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Dark Tech Keynote — Style Prompt\n\n> A software launch film where the interface itself is the star: near-black gradient, hairline grid, soft light, one brand accent, and UI that moves with snap-grid precision. Light reveals, one giant number, and a sound design that is as ordered as the pixels.\n> **References (grammar only):** Apple product reveals (black stage, light sweeps, one line per screen, the lone giant number); Linear / Stripe / Vercel launch videos (snap-grid motion, 1 px lines, one accent); Steve Reich's process music (phasing, subtraction); Edgar Wright's insert cuts. Never use their names, interfaces, fonts, system controls, melodies or logos, and never name them in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nA keynote-grade product film for **software**. There is no physical product and no glass render: the protagonist is the interface (a cursor, windows, notifications, tiles, a number). It lives on a dark stage lit by soft, cool light, with **one brand accent** reserved for the single most important element.\n\nThe style is defined by **precision**: every element snaps to a grid, every motion lands on a beat, every sound belongs to one designed family. Drama comes from contrast: disorder shown as generated UI, one decisive action, then empty space.\n\nNot a glass product render (no physical object), not a screencast (no real OS or apps), not a hologram HUD (no scanlines), not a chart film (one number, not a chart).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Stage**: a near-black base with a radial lift at the upper centre, one large cool glow at single-digit opacity off-centre, a 50–55 % corner vignette. A hairline grid (white at ~3 %, every 4th line ~6 %) on a 48 px module. No grain.\n- **UI kit, all original and drawn in code**: rounded windows (title bar with only a small glyph and title; never traffic-light buttons, menu bars or docks), notification cards, file icons with mono filenames, procedural thumbnails, list rows, tabs, badges, bars, uniform tiles.\n- **Surfaces**: three steps of raised dark surface, 1 px border (~8 % white), 1 px inner top highlight, soft shadows.\n- **Depth**: a 2.5D camera (`s = 1/(1/zoom − z)`); cards near the lens blur with magnification. Rotations use strip-rendered perspective (rotY ≤ ~24°).\n- **Light** is the reveal tool: only pixels the light has passed turn on; unlit areas sit at a few percent.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- A cool neutral ladder only: near-black stage, three surface steps, text in three greys (primary, secondary, label). Neutrals are slightly blue, never warm brown.\n- **One accent**, saturated and luminous, with its own glow. It is reserved for what the product *is* or *does*: the protagonist element, the call to action, confirmations, the key number. If two things are accent-coloured, one is wrong.\n- **A problem colour** (typically a red for badges and warnings) may exist only while the problem exists; after the turn it never returns. Problem colour = noise, accent = the product.\n- Secondary hues only as **category codes** in motion (a trail per data type), desaturated, and never on static UI.\n- Example accents, pick one per film: lime `#B7F34A`, electric violet `#8B7CFF`, signal orange `#FF7A1A`.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **OFL faces**: Inter (UI, captions, headlines; variable weight), Inter Tight 600 for giant numbers (tracking around −4.5 %), JetBrains Mono for labels, filenames and status text (wide tracking in small-caps labels).\n- One line per screen for headlines. The big number gets its own screen and **no caption over it**: the number is the message.\n- **Captions are a UI component**: a bottom-centre toast (dark translucent pill, 1 px light border, soft shadow, Inter 500 around 40 px, a small accent \"speaking\" dot). It enters with a small rise + fade in ~0.16 s and can turn more opaque over busy frames. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- **Typed text is its own subtitle**: text the cursor types in frame is not burned twice (it goes only into the .srt).\n- Lift product content while captions are up so toasts never clip it.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- 24 fps on ones, no stepping, no boil: this is precision, not craft.\n- **Everything lands on the grid and on the beat.** Moves start a beat early and land on it. Easing is fast-out with one small overshoot (`back`, s ≈ 1.3) and an immediate settle. No floaty sine drift.\n- **Spawns are short and typed** (drop + bounce, fling, slide-in, pop; 0.2–0.3 s), one motion per element type. Tension can be a tremble of a few px.\n- **Flights** follow curved paths with overshoot along the final tangent; same-type elements bend the same way and never cross.\n- **A living element can act**: squash, stretch, anticipation (≥ 8 frames of wind-up), a 2-frame contact, spring follow-through. The blink of a cursor makes a natural metronome.\n- **Disappearing is designed** (retract one part per beat, CRT-off squash into a point); nothing just fades.\n- The stage and grid never move on their own; only the camera moves them.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | Expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Locked extreme close-up on a tiny UI element | Intimacy, one thing alive on a screen | a single action; a thought forming; a quiet status |\n| Continuous pull-back, keyed per bar in log space | Accumulation, scale growing out of one point | spreading load; a network growing; a team joining |\n| Push-in through a frozen frame | Time stopped, focus narrowing | a decision; the instant before a commit; a bug found |\n| Whip pull-out in one beat | Cause and effect seen at once | a sync completing; a deploy landing everywhere |\n| Dive into a small UI element (log-zoom) + match cut | Going inside the detail | a counter that matters; a log line; a setting |\n| Half-second inserts expanding from their source | Overload, too much to read | notification storms; errors; choice overload |\n| Near-locked frame while an object turns and light sweeps | Presentation, the product as hero | a reveal; a new version; a feature name |\n| Slow lateral track across a UI plane | Order read by the eye | a timeline; a pipeline |\n| Top-down plan over the grid | System view, structure | architecture; permissions; a workflow map |\n| Return to an earlier framing | Rhyme: what changed | before/after; a loop closed; an echo |\n\nFraming: generous negative space, one focal element, the accent at the focal point. Dutch angle and wobble only for disorder.\n\n**Transitions = UI expand / collapse**: every shot change is an interface element expanding into the frame or the frame collapsing into an element. No fades, no blank frames. An expansion on a beat starts one frame early so the beat frame is already ~80 % open.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Tuned percussion and pads**: marimba, vibraphone (mallet or bowed), glockenspiel, celesta, a synth pad or drone, a low sine pulse, a shaker, plucked synth, felt piano. Clean, dry, minimal; samples e.g. VCSL (CC0).\n- **Music as a visible process**: options include phasing (two voices on one pattern, one drifting), additive build (one note per bar), subtractive process (notes removed until one is left), dividing the beat to accelerate instead of changing tempo, or one chord that everything lands on. Picture events can spawn on score notes from a shared note table.\n- **Two sound families = story**: disorder sounds *scattered* (detuned by tens of cents, random pans, a different timbre per source); the product's sounds are *one family* (felt ticks, glass taps, all in one mode, centred), so many landings form one in-key shimmer.\n- **Foley materials**: cursor tick, low-profile keys, notification glass, file plop, window whoosh, glitch accents, stacking thumps, felt mallet + sine drop for a press, light-sweep shimmer, digit grains, CRT-off.\n- **Ambience**: a quiet room, a computer fan, a high coil whine that can grow with pressure. Fan and whine live in the voice band: keep them down until a line ends.\n- **Silence is a tool**: digital zero on every track when the screen pauses; near-silence (a −60 dB room) for a breath. The first sound after a silence is the most important one.\n- **J / L cuts** carry sound across UI transitions (a chord ringing on, a shimmer arriving before the light).\n- **Mix**: music ducks ~8 dB and foley ~4 dB under voice; −14 LUFS; no grain. **Voice**: confident and restrained, short declaratives.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Generated accumulation.** UI spawned by rule, faster and faster, hundreds of items; you don't draw the mess, you generate it. *Fits content like:* unread tickets piling up; cloud costs creeping; a dependency tree exploding.\n- **The snap grid.** A whole promise in one motion: everything flies into a grid in one beat; the grid stays invisible until then. *Fits content like:* a CMS publishing to every channel; a scheduler filling a week; an audit turning green.\n- **The living element.** The cursor (or the product's smallest live element) is the protagonist and the brand mark. *Fits content like:* a search box that grows ambitious; a progress ring that refuses to finish; a status dot.\n- **Freeze.** Everything stops mid-air and the sound drops to digital zero; only a screen can stop time like this. *Fits content like:* a security breach caught; a failed deploy; the second before \"send\".\n- **Light reveal.** A soft band sweeps across and only lit pixels turn on (soft front, glint ≤ 20 %, ≤ ~1.3 s so nothing stays dark). *Fits content like:* a redesign; a new pricing page; an API console.\n- **Number from glyph noise.** Digits scramble and lock one per sixteenth note with a small bounce. *Fits content like:* latency cut; a count of users; a price.\n- **Type streams.** Elements fly to their own rows along coloured curved trails, so categories read as streams. *Fits content like:* an inbox triaged; logs routed by severity; files synced by device.\n- **Put-away.** The UI dismantles itself one part per beat until one element is left. *Fits content like:* \"less is more\" features; offboarding; focus mode.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Strip perspective of a semi-transparent canvas shows vertical seams (overlapping slices double alpha) → project the *opaque* surface, then light it in screen space.\n- A hard white glint reads as a sticker reflection → soft front, glint ≤ 20 %.\n- Nearest-slot snapping looks like a flicker, not a collapse → typed destinations, curved paths, trails.\n- Too few elements leave holes in a grid that read as a mistake → generate by rule, then trim or pad to exactly the slot count per group.\n- A flat tinted rectangle for \"closing\" looks like a placeholder → squash the real surface.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\n`demo/engine.js` (Canvas 2D ES module): `setAccent`, `bg`, `grid`, `panel`, `litShape` (any shape in this style), `trail`, `caret`, the UI kit, `flyPose`, `lightReveal`, `perspective`, `scramble`, `toast`, `camera` (2.5D). Function table and a minimal example: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md#engine-reference); `?scene=engineDemo` renders it.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the product's verb, the protagonist element (or none), the problem image, the accent, the structure, the opening, the ending, the camera path and the score process. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a countdown** (a release checklist ticking through its items, each a UI moment, ending on \"shipped\"); **side by side** (two versions of a workflow run in split screen, the old one drowning, the new one finishing early); **a zoom ladder** (one pixel → a component → a screen → a fleet of devices, each level a new fact).\n- Openings: **the number first** (a huge stat on black, its meaning revealed backwards); **a plan view of an empty grid** that fills slot by slot; **a hard insert storm** from frame one, calm arriving only later.\n- Endings: **one tile left** lit in the accent on an empty grid; **the product running quietly** in a wide shot, no text; **a shipped state** (a single confirmation toast, then darkness).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nRoom to Think\n"},{"id":"S4-L43","name":"活体实机录屏","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L43.jpg","markdown":"# L43 活体实机录屏（Living Screencast）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 信息与发布\n- 长什么样：像素小人 Clawd 搬进 Claude 应用，一镜到底演示 Plan 模式和自检。\n- 适合：录屏加一个动画角色，演示\"我\"怎么用 AI 工具\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Living Screencast — Style Prompt\n\n> A product walkthrough that looks like a real screen recording, where a low-resolution mascot lives inside a high-resolution interface and acts out what the software is doing.\n> References (grammar only):\n> - **Screen Studio-style product recordings:** auto-zoom to the cursor, cursor smoothing, click push-ins, and a window floating on wallpaper.\n> - **Apple \"Guided Tour\" videos:** real interface, calm narration, one feature per sentence.\n> - **Duolingo and Headspace mascot motion:** squash and stretch, anticipation, a character that performs inside the UI.\n> - **The Browser Company (Arc) launch films:** playful tone and fast cutting.\n>\n> Never copy their characters, UI, music or footage.\n\nA walkthrough of a piece of software, typically 45–75 s. The user gives the product and the features; you decide the story, shots, choreography, sound and music.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nThe film looks like a **screen recording of the real product**, but it is not a recording. Every window, menu, diff line and button is **redrawn in 2D (HTML/CSS/SVG)**, so the camera can go anywhere, every pixel is controllable and text stays sharp at any zoom.\n\nA **mascot made of big square pixels** lives in that high-resolution world. The clash of two resolutions, a hard-edged low-res sprite against anti-aliased UI, is the look.\n\nThe mascot is the software's agency made visible: when the software reads, it runs along the rows it reads; when it plans, it holds a pixel pencil; when it tests, it stomps the button.\n\n**Two actors, never a hand:** the **cursor** is the user; the **mascot** is the software.\n\nNot a dark tech keynote (no black stage, no invented abstract UI: this is the real product), not a pixel-art game (the world is hi-res vector UI; only the mascot and its props are pixels), not a real capture (nothing is a screenshot).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Real UI, redrawn**: every label, mode name and shortcut matches the real product; no screenshots, no lorem ipsum. Demo content (code, data, test output) is coherent, and the app under test really changes.\n- **Pixel grid is sacred**: the mascot, its props (pencil, \"!\", sparkles, heart, dust) and trails share one square-pixel grid with crisp edges. It scales with the camera; its position snaps to half-pixels, so its motion feels stepped.\n- **Gravity = top edges of UI**: the mascot only stands on the top edge of a real element (input box, menu, list row, code line, button, status bar, the letters of a heading). Its position comes from **measuring the live DOM** every frame, never hand-placed coordinates.\n- **Window on wallpaper**: the app floats on a desktop with a soft shadow; zoom never shows beyond the monitor.\n- **Depth** only where it serves attention: rack focus (blur the pane that doesn't matter), a vignette spotlight. Never blur text the viewer must read.\n- **Motion blur** is derived from camera speed and applied as an anisotropic blur on an **untransformed wrapper**, only on whips. Slow moves stay sharp.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- The product's own interface colours rule: follow its real light and dark themes exactly.\n- The mascot keeps the product's or brand's mascot colour, or one saturated hue that no UI element uses, so it finds the eye in any frame.\n- Overlays (chapter pill, key HUD, captions) are neutral frosted glass; one accent at most, taken from the product.\n- A theme switch can be a story event: the whole film may change state with it.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- UI type follows the product (or licence-free look-alikes such as Inter, JetBrains Mono, a serif for editorial UIs).\n- **Information layers only**: a chapter pill (01–04), a KeyCastr-style keystroke HUD, frosted-glass caption pills, an optional \"▶▶ 4×\" time-lapse tag. Nothing else overlays the screen.\n- Captions sit in a pill at the bottom, never over the element being discussed and never under the mascot; hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- A title, if any, is diegetic: it appears as part of the UI (an empty-state heading, a window title, a notification).\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- 30 fps; UI on ones; mascot position stepped to its pixel grid.\n- **Mascot acting**: jumps with anticipation crouch, stretch on take-off, tucked legs, landing squash with a damped bounce and dust. Idle breathes (a tiny squash) on the score's beat and blinks irregularly. Walks by leg alternation, flipping the sprite for direction.\n- **Eyes carry the acting** (neutral / left / right / up / down / wide / happy / shut / blink): they look at what matters. Arms: down / up (cheer) / wave.\n- **Cursor**: smoothed, spring-eased paths, small push on click, never teleports.\n- **Cause and effect**: every mascot action triggers a real UI state change; every user action is a cursor move, click or keystroke shown on the HUD.\n- UI animates like the real product (its own menus, popovers, diff reveals); don't invent flourishes the product doesn't have.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA recording-software camera: one continuous take inside the screen, zoom ≥ 1, cuts only under chapter transitions. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Push-in to the cursor or caret | \"this is where it happens\" | typing a prompt; choosing a menu item; a setting |\n| Slow drift at rest | the app is alive while we listen | a line of narration; waiting for a result |\n| Whip-pan between panes (with blur) | cause here, effect there | sending a message → a result appearing; code → preview |\n| Push-in + vignette freeze | the one promise to remember | a safety guarantee; a price; an undo |\n| Rack focus between panes | attention moves, nothing else does | reading files; logs scrolling while chat waits |\n| Pull back to the full window | the whole workflow in view | a summary; parallel tasks; before/after |\n| Follow the mascot along a list | the software working through items | scanning results; a checklist; a migration |\n| Split-window reframe | two things at once | parallel sessions; compare two versions |\n\nFraming: the thing being discussed and its caption never overlap; the mascot never covers text to be read. Transitions: chapter pill wipes, pixel wipes, a theme reveal circle; never dissolves or screenshots.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Two resolutions in the music**: a hi-fi layer (piano, upright or electric bass, brushed or tight kit, glockenspiel, real samples) is the world; a chiptune square or pulse voice is the mascot. They can trade, duet or merge.\n- Options: a low-passed \"night\" version of the band for a dark theme; a tom fill and crash at chapter seams; dropping the drums to build tension; the mascot's notes on words it lands on; a stereo split for split screens; a single held pad under a freeze.\n- **Foley**: keyboard (a thock plus a click, human timing jitter), trackpad clicks, pane swishes, popup pops, notification chimes, CI ticks, a shutter for screenshots. Mascot steps, jumps and landings are 8-bit.\n- **Silence**: a short drop to room tone before the key promise makes it land.\n- **Voice**: calm, one feature per line; product names verified with whisper. Keep effects off word onsets. Duck music under voice; −14 LUFS.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your walkthrough needs.\n\n- **Match-cut origin.** The mascot is born from something already in the UI (a logo's block characters, an icon, a loading spinner) and climbs out, leaving an outline. *Fits content like:* a chat bubble turning into a guide; an icon becoming an assistant; a spinner becoming a helper.\n- **Diegetic title.** The name appears as a real UI element; the mascot lands on it on a downbeat. *Fits content like:* an empty state; a new-project dialog; a welcome email.\n- **Moving floor.** The mascot rides a caret, a popup, a sent message, a progress bar. *Fits content like:* a message flying into a thread; an upload bar; a drag-and-drop.\n- **Time-lapse with a tag.** Long work runs at N× with a flashing tag and rack focus. *Fits content like:* indexing a library; a data import; a build.\n- **Spotlight freeze.** Push in and vignette the one badge that holds the promise. *Fits content like:* \"Saved\"; \"Encrypted\"; \"No charges until…\".\n- **The film changes state.** A toggle's effect escapes the preview and takes over the whole film, and returns later. *Fits content like:* dark mode; a language switch; offline mode.\n- **Cell division.** A new session or window splits the screen and the mascot splits with a pixel burst. *Fits content like:* parallel tasks; multiple accounts; team members.\n- **Cursor and mascot as characters.** The cursor pets, pokes or drags the mascot, and it reacts. *Fits content like:* approving a suggestion; undoing; onboarding delight.\n- **Karaoke-ball words.** The mascot hops from word to word as they are spoken. *Fits content like:* a slogan; a shortcut list; step names.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Invented features or renamed modes → check every button, mode and shortcut against the product's documentation; if it isn't in the docs, it isn't in the film.\n- The mascot floats or drifts off its element → measure the DOM every frame, stand only on top edges.\n- The mascot covers text or sits under a caption → reserve a text-free lane for it.\n- Blur-filter \"fake depth\" on text you want read, or zooming out past the screen → rack focus only on panes not being read; zoom ≥ 1.\n- A hand, arm or finger on screen → the cursor is the user.\n- Motion blur applied to a transformed layer smears the pixel sprite wrong → blur an untransformed wrapper, and only on whips.\n- A keypress or pop landing on a product name masks it → shift foley off word onsets; re-check with whisper on the final mix.\n- TTS mispronounces the product name → try voices and spellings, verify with whisper.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `film.js` (timeline: VO at fixed times, every visual beat hung on a spoken word with `W(id, word)`, camera moves, `Actor` mascot segments, `Cursor`, sound events), `ui.js` (state → HTML for each interface), `clawd.js` (sprite grid, poses, pixel props: replace the sprite with your own mascot), `main.js` (light/dark layers, DOM anchor measurement, motion blur, HUD: captions, keys, pixel wipes, spotlight), `sound.py` (score, foley and VO from `events.json`, with ducking). Build notes: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md#build-notes).\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the features and their order, the mascot (the product's own, or an original pixel character), the story, the opening, the ending, the camera path, the chapters and the music. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a bug hunt** (the mascot chases one error through logs, code and a fix, a single chapter); **a day of one user** (morning to evening in one app, the theme and music shifting with the hour); **a race** (two windows side by side, old way vs. new way, the mascot winning on the right).\n- Openings: **mid-task** (a half-written document; the mascot is already pushing the cursor's selection); **the notification** (a system toast lands; the mascot is inside it); **empty state** (a blank app, a caret blinking; the mascot drops from the menu bar).\n- Endings: **the mascot goes to sleep** on the finished item as the window dims; **the app closes** and the mascot is left standing on the dock; **a real share** (the result is sent and the mascot rides it out of frame).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nClawd Moves In\n"},{"id":"S4-L44","name":"科幻全息界面","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L44.jpg","markdown":"# L44 科幻全息界面（Sci-fi Hologram HUD）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 信息与发布\n- 长什么样：电助力车被扫描成全息线框，目标框依次锁定电池、电机、刹车。\n- 适合：未来感\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Sci-fi Hologram HUD — Style Prompt\n\n> An object is scanned into a glowing wireframe. Target boxes lock onto its parts; each opens in a local exploded view, a leader pulls out, and its number rolls from garbage glyphs into the real value. It may light up, once, into a solid hologram.\n> References (grammar only): the *Iron Man* / *Avengers* HUDs (target boxes, callouts, rolling numbers, layered depth); the *Oblivion* interfaces (one colour, hairlines, space); CAD exploded views. Copy none of their layouts, icons, fonts or colours.\n\nA **scene style**: it does a practical job (a spec walkthrough, a teardown, a loop screen) for any object with parts. See §11 for the jobs.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nThe subject is always a **line wireframe made of light** above a round projector pad on a near-black stage, never a rendered object. Everything reads as *scanned data*: a scan plane grows the model, parts pull apart along their assembly axes, numbers are *computed* on screen.\n\nThree depth layers are always present: a **background dot grid** (slow parallax), the **subject** with its pad and floor, and the **foreground HUD** (corner brackets, rulers that parallax faster, status text).\n\nNot a type-driven keynote (dark-keynote), not a drafting sheet (blueprint), not a product render (glass-product). The subject stays a wireframe; it may gain translucent faces once, and even then the lines stay on top.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Stage**: near-black tinted by the hue, a radial lift behind the subject, a fading dot grid, a strong vignette.\n- **Projector pad** in true perspective: concentric rings (one bright, one dashed), ticks, faint rays; it rotates slowly and spins with any turntable move.\n- **Wireframe**: additive ~1.3 px strokes in a few alpha levels set by **depth fog** (near bright, far ~25 %), plus two low-res glow passes.\n- **Hot edges** (near-white of the hue) only for what matters now: the scan front, inner pieces flagged `hot` when opened, a light wave, a part being pinged.\n- **Real CAD topology**: tubes are rings plus longitudinal lines. **Every part is physically connected**: a floating piece is noticed first.\n- **Solid hologram**: faces filled with a screen-space **Fresnel** × a scrolling **interlace**; lines thicken as it ignites; a light wave climbs the model. A **beam** (one path, nonzero winding) with faint rays and dust.\n- **HUD furniture**: corner brackets, parallaxing rulers, subject // mode top left, a live status line top right, telemetry at the bottom.\n- **Text never sits on the wireframe**: every text block has its own **dark plate** (translucent near-black, 1 px border, corner ticks); the camera still keeps the subject clear of text.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **One cold hue plus one warm accent**, both parameters (e.g. mint/orange, violet/lime, ice-blue/red; choose from the subject).\n- The hue carries lines, HUD and fills. The **accent is spent on locked values only**: a number turns accent-coloured at the frame it becomes true. Nothing else ever uses it.\n- White is heat: near-white edges and one-frame flashes mark what is active. Everything at rest sits in the hue's alpha levels.\n- One object may keep its own colour (the single-colour exception, `keep`), for a brand mark, never for decoration.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- Rajdhani, all caps: Light for big numbers and the product name (wide tracking), Medium/SemiBold for labels. Share Tech Mono for micro data (coordinates, counters, zoom).\n- Numbers are **monospaced** so rolls never jitter; the decimal point is a small square (a glowing \".\" reads as a comma).\n- Subtitles are HUD: a dark plate with corner brackets and \"voiceprint\" bars moving with the speech, near-white Rajdhani Medium, bottom centre; in just before the voice, hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- The title is part of the scene (no title card): the name rolls in like a value, then code, category and tagline.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Everything on ones at 24 fps.** Only glyph scrambling steps, every frame.\n- **Target lock**: oversized, rotated 45°, faint; snaps to fit in ~0.2 s (cubic ease-out), flashes white 3 frames on the downbeat.\n- **Explode** along each piece's assembly axis: the shell slides *along its own axis*, the contents stay in place and glow. Open over a beat or two, close fast.\n- **Leader**: anchor ring → diagonal → horizontal to the card in ~0.5 s. **Rolling value**: characters lock right-to-left and turn accent on the downbeat with a one-frame flash.\n- **Chip dock**: a finished value slides into a side rail and stays until the end.\n- **The camera follows the box**: cubic ease, starting *before* the next lock, **real motion blur** only inside the whip (sub-frames, 180° shutter); HUD stays sharp.\n- **No cuts or fades inside the scene**: only the scan plane (scanning in or erasing) and a loupe iris. A **glitch** of three frames at most once, on the biggest change.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nAn orbit camera (yaw, pitch, distance, narrow fov) with a screen position for the target, so the subject can sit beside text. A vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | Expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| High 3/4, slow orbit craning down | Examination; a scan plane reads as an ellipse | a scan-in; a survey |\n| Top-down plan view | Layout, order | a parts inventory; an assembly sequence |\n| Eye-level profile, locked | The object as it stands in use | a height; a before/after |\n| Push-in, then drift, 3/4 | This part matters; internals come towards the lens | the key spec; a flaw |\n| Whip following the target box | Attention jumps to the next part | a list of parts |\n| Orbit around a part's own axis | Axial structure only reads in rotation | rotors, stacks, hinges, lenses |\n| Lateral dolly along a long part | Length, a path | a beam; a cable run |\n| Pull back + loupe (frame in frame) | Where (wide) and what (loupe) | small parts; a sensor |\n| Crane to eye level or back up | Status change: the object stands up or settles | a transformation; scale |\n| Turntable 360° | Every side | a whole object; a symmetry |\n| Nearly static, slow dolly | Reading time without a dead frame | a spec sheet; a checklist |\n\nFraming: the subject is ≥ ⅓ of frame height at every key moment. Cards on one side, the chip rail on the other, subtitles bottom centre, a loupe above them: never colliding. Shorten explode vectors rather than fly a piece into text.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Music first**, on a grid (a bar = one information beat); locks land on downbeats, checked against the score.\n- **Synth palette**: saw/square arpeggio with ping-pong delay (scooped 1–4 kHz for the voice), square pulse, 8th bass, synth hats and claps, detuned saw pads.\n- **Density follows information.** Tools to pick from, in any order (most films need two or three): half-time for reading time; one beat of **true digital silence** (nothing, not even UI blips); a drop (808-style glide + wide saw chord) for the biggest change; a layer added or removed per part; deceleration by longer note values; a key change; a single sustained tone under a long hold.\n- **Foley follows the material**: light = sines and FM blips **in the key**; machines = servo slides, pneumatic puffs, hydraulic hiss, metal tinks (J-cuts); a lock = thump + clack + two-tone beep; rolls tick at frame rate. Ambience: projector hum (40/80/120 Hz) and room tone.\n- **Voice**: calm, confident. Duck music *and* foley with a **held** envelope (no pumping between words); lock transients escape. −14 LUFS.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nEach move serves information, not story. A menu: use the ones your film needs.\n\n- **Scan growth = \"this is real data.\"** *Fits content like:* a coffee machine; a satellite; an architectural model.\n- **Target lock = \"look here now.\"** The box is the camera. *Fits content like:* a car's sensors; a laptop's ports; a robot arm's joints.\n- **Local explode = \"why the number is true.\"** *Fits content like:* the burr set of a grinder; the layers of a running shoe sole; the elements of a camera lens.\n- **Rolling digits = the payoff beat.** *Fits content like:* a brew temperature; a tolerance in microns; a price.\n- **Chip accumulation = reading time.** *Fits content like:* 3–5 facts that must all be read.\n- **Loupe = two scales at once.** *Fits content like:* a screw inside a watch; a chip on a board; a valve in an engine.\n- **Solid hologram + beam = \"the whole is ready.\"** Once, if at all. *Fits content like:* a launch reveal; a finished assembly; a v2 replacing v1.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Target boxes balloon → rest pose, skip `internal` pieces, long attachments `box:false`.\n- Long diagonal leaders point at the wrong part → ring on the part body, flash the part.\n- Two parallel boxes moving don't read as an explode → shell slides on its axis, contents stay and glow.\n- A part built from generic boxes doesn't read as that part → give it its characteristic silhouette.\n- Voice masked by foley, not music → duck foley too, hold the duck through word gaps.\n- UI blips inside a silence break it → check cues against silence windows.\n- A whip written inside the next segment plays as a hard cut → start it in the previous one.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\n`demo/engine/holo.js` (wireframe engine: projection, scan, explode, Fresnel faces, glow, pad, scan plane, `normalizeModel`, `modelFromPath` to extrude any 2D path), `demo/engine/hud.js` (theme, plate, target box, leader, rolling text, spec card, chip, marker, beam, subtitle, chrome, glitch), `demo/models/mkmodel.mjs` (primitives: tube, ring, disc, box, wheel, cells…). Models are normalised on load, so cameras work for any object. API, model format and an example: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md#engine-reference).\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the object, its model, which parts and in what order, the opening, the ending, the camera path, the colours, the pacing and the music. Use cases are **grammar for the order and duration of information**; build your own timeline from the user's facts.\n\n| Use case | Information order | Hold per layer | Length |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Spec walkthrough** | Name + tagline → one spec per part (label, value, unit, detail) → weight / price → CTA | Title ≥ 5 s; value ≥ 2.5 s, then docked; final sheet ≥ 3 s | 30–40 s, 2–5 parts |\n| **Loop screen** | Name → 3–5 specs → CTA, no voice; last frame = first | 3 s per spec, 5 s final | 20–30 s loop |\n| **Teardown** | Part → what it does → one number; internals only when opened | 4–6 s per part | 40–60 s |\n| **Upgrade** | Part → old value (dim) → new value (accent); old part scan-erased, new one scanned in | 3 s per upgrade | 20–30 s |\n| **Assembly order** | Step number → part name; parts snap home one by one | one bar per part | 20–40 s |\n\nAll far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a loop screen** that never names a product and cycles five sensors; **a teardown** one layer deeper per shot until the core is a single hot piece; **an upgrade** where two versions share the pad and every number rolls from old to new.\n- Openings: **a part first** (one component locked in close-up; the rest scans out from it); **a flat outline** (a 2D silhouette extrudes into the wireframe, `modelFromPath`); **plan view** (top-down, the pad as a dial, the object arriving as its footprint).\n- Endings: **one value at eye level** (the wireframe dims around the single number that matters); **knolling** (parts fly into a labelled top-down layout); **power-down** (edges cool, HUD panels switch off until only the pad glows).\n\nSwapping `demo/content.json` and rebuilding is a technical check that the engine re-flows, not a way to make a film.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build, content fields): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nVolt · Spec Scan\n"},{"id":"S4-L45","name":"1930s 橡皮管卡通","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L45.jpg","markdown":"# L45 1930s 橡皮管卡通（1930s Rubber Hose Cartoon）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 卡通与动画\n- 长什么样：一只咖啡杯满厨房追一块逃跑的方糖。\n- 适合：复古卡通、搞笑\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# 1930s Rubber Hose Cartoon — Style Prompt\n\n> Black-and-white hand-inked cartoons where every object is alive and swings to a hot jazz band.\n> References (grammar only): early Fleischer Studios shorts (c. 1930–33) for \"the whole world dances\", surreal transformation, the bouncing-ball singalong and ink-and-pen framing devices; *Cuphead* for how a modern team finishes the look and scores it with a big band. Never use an existing cartoon character, name or silhouette, and never copy designs, melodies or logos. **Avoid the \"cup-head on a human body with a straw\" design**: if your hero is an object, the *whole object is the body*.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nHand-inked black-and-white animation from the early sound era: characters with **rubber-hose limbs** (equal-width tubes, no elbows, no knees), **white four-finger gloves**, **pie eyes** (black oval pupils with a wedge cut out), huge round shoes, and bodies that squash and stretch like balloons. The backgrounds are soft grey watercolour paintings; the characters are flat white, grey and ink. Everything (furniture, machines, plants, the walls) **breathes in time with the music**. The film looks like a worn 35 mm print projected in a 4:3 gate: flicker, scratches, dust, gate weave, vignette.\n\nNot a 1950s flat cartoon (no limited animation, no colour, no graphic backgrounds), not a silent film (the synchronised music is the engine), not a \"retro filter\" over modern animation (no joints, no rendered shading).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Frame**: 1920×1080 output with a **4:3 gate** (1440×1080 centred, black pillarbox), gate corners rounded (r ≈ 28 px), soft inner shadow on the gate edge.\n- **Characters**: flat fills, one hard-edged form-shadow band (light grey) on the side away from an upper-left light, ink outline **constant in screen space** at any zoom (transform points first, then stroke; ~6 px at 1080p), light **line boil** (±1.2 px, 3 drawings cycling at 12 fps).\n- **Anatomy kit**:\n  - Limbs: noodle tubes of constant width (about 1/20 of the character's height), one smooth bend, round caps.\n  - Gloves: palm + 3 fat fingers + thumb, flared cuff with a fold line, 3 stitch lines on the back. Outline the union only (stroke all parts thick first, then fill all).\n  - Pie eyes: white oval, big black pupil (≈ 64 % × 74 % of the eye) with a wedge cut toward the upper right; blink with an eyelid in body colour and a lid line.\n  - Mouths: smile with cheek ticks, open grin, \"O\", wavy (scared), pucker, tongue-out. Shoes: big black ovals with a white toe glint.\n- **Object characters**: the face sits on the body; a **secondary feature carries the mood** (steam, a flame, a lampshade tilt, a spring). A box-shaped object is a real projected 3D shape seen slightly from above, the face mapped onto its front.\n- **Backgrounds**: painted once into a cached canvas at 1.25–1.5× (push-ins stay crisp): flat wash + soft blotches (±5 %) + inner edge darkening + a thin dark-grey outline + paper grain. They never boil. Period motifs: patterned wallpaper, panelled furniture, checker floors, brick, clapboard.\n- **Film damage** (composite pass, not the scene): low-frequency gate weave (±1.2 px) with a rare jump; flicker ±4 %; 0–3 drifting vertical scratches; 3–8 dust specks per frame; an occasional hair; strong vignette; a small jump on every cut; slight blur. Grain in ffmpeg (`mux.sh` grain **3**), not in the page.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **Strictly greyscale**: about 6 grey steps plus a separate character white. No hue anywhere, not even in the film damage.\n- **Characters own the extremes**: character whites and ink blacks are brighter and darker than anything in the background. Backgrounds live in the middle greys (walls around mid grey, furniture one or two steps darker). Add a mild contrast boost (≈ 1.1–1.15) in the final composite: 1930s prints are rich black and bright white, never flat grey.\n- Form shadow is one light-grey band, never a gradient.\n- Examples of the grey ramp's temperature: warm paper greys (our demo); cool silver-nitrate greys; a faint sepia print. Pick one and keep it for the whole film.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Subtitles are a period title card**: a small near-black plate with a white double-rule border and dot-and-ring corner ornaments; text in **IM Fell English SC** (~36 px), paper white. Drawn in the scene layer so it weaves and flickers with the film; pops on and off with no fades. Bottom by default, top when the action is low. One card may span two short voice clips; hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- **Titles**: **Shrikhand** fat retro script, white face, heavy ink outline (~14 % of the cap height), ink drop shadow down-right; letters can hop in a travelling wave on the beat. Sub-lines in **Limelight** caps.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Characters on twos** (12 fps); camera moves on ones (24 fps). A stepped camera reads as judder.\n- **Tempo grid**: choose a tempo where one beat is an **even whole number of frames** at 24 fps (8, 10, 12, 16 or 20 frames), so every beat lands on a drawing of the twos.\n- **Breathing**: every idle object squashes and stretches with `cos(2π·beats)`: an extreme on every beat (stretch on the beat, squash on the off-beat), sampled on twos, amplitude ~5–7 %, all props in phase. A peak of joy can raise the amplitude ×3 and squash the whole background painting around the floor line (~2.5 %).\n- **Walk**: one step per beat with a body bob (sad = smaller steps, limp arms). **Run**: legs as a windmill (feet orbit a circle), lean forward, speed lines.\n- **Anticipation–hold–action**: before every big move, crouch (squash to ~0.8), hold (let the band stop), then snap. Impacts get a squash with an exponential spring-back.\n- **Held frames are acting**: a chain of holds, each glance on an eighth note with a woodblock tick, reads as thinking.\n- **Freeze on a band stop**: when the music stops, freeze everything, background breathing included.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA theatre camera: mostly locked, moving only to follow a runner or to land a gag. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Stage-wide, locked, eye level | A proscenium: the set is a stage and everyone on it performs | introducing a place; a group number; a routine |\n| Hard cut to a face close-up | The reaction is the joke | a surprise; a taste; a realisation |\n| Trucking pan beside a runner, eased start and stop | Pursuit, momentum | a chase; a race; a delivery |\n| Medium follow shot (character ≈ 1/4–1/3 of the frame height) | Every step is a note | Mickey-moused climbing, dancing, working |\n| Tilt with a fall or a rise | Gravity as rhythm | a tumble; a rocket; a lift |\n| Slightly high angle, one unbroken shot | The hazard and the answer in one frame | a danger; a stretch; a rescue |\n| Tabletop pan (flat characters in front of a model set turning in depth) | The world is a real place | a street; a carousel; a factory floor |\n| Pull-back from a close-up to a packed medium-full | One feeling spreads to everyone | a celebration; a crowd joining in |\n| Iris in / iris out (grabbable by a character) | The film is an object | a beginning; an ending; a scene change with a gag |\n\nFraming: faces must read, so no far-wide shot for a gag or a musical moment. The destination prop enters frame before the gag lands. Arcs and leaps stay inside the gate at the current zoom. Transitions: iris, a hard cut on a beat, a title card rolling up like a window shade; no dissolves.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **1930s hot dance band / ragtime**: stride piano, clarinet, trumpet (open + plunger-mute \"wah-wah\"), trombone (smears), tuba oom-pah, banjo on 2 & 4, snare with brushes and rolls, xylophone, woodblocks, slide whistle, cymbal, kazoo, washboard, a pit organ. **No string pads, no modern synths.** One short original syncopated theme can be restated in different arrangements.\n- **Mickey-mouse the score, as options**: a band member \"answering\" a character (trombone raspberry, trumpet laugh); a stop (hard silence, reverb tails too) before a gag or on a stare; a stinger per gag; slide whistle for every stretch or fall; a prop that *is* an instrument; a solo for tenderness; a key change for a lift.\n- **Foley by material** (synthesised): porcelain and glass = hard inharmonic partials + a tiny transient; wood = woodblock; metal = bells and clanks; springs = \"boing\"; water = filtered noise + rising blips and bubbles; rubber = sawtooth creak through a band-pass.\n- **Voice**: a period character (a radio announcer calling the action, a vaudeville MC, a crooner), few short lines. Radio chain: HP ~260 Hz / LP ~4.6 kHz, tanh saturation, a short room, compression. Duck the music ~8 dB.\n- **Optical soundtrack pass** over music + foley: band-limit ~110 Hz–6.2 kHz, slight wow (~0.6 Hz) and flutter (~7 Hz), soft saturation; then a bed of hiss, crackle and a 24 Hz projector gate clatter, the *only* sound allowed inside a silence.\n- Loudness −14 LUFS (two-pass loudnorm in `core/render/mux.sh`).\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Everything breathes on the beat.** The setting is a character; at the peak the *whole world* can dance in sync, walls included. *Fits content like:* a factory at shift change; a garden after rain; a city waking up.\n- **Mickey-mousing.** Every step is a note; a prop becomes an instrument. *Fits content like:* stairs as a piano; a typewriter as a drum kit; fence posts as a xylophone.\n- **Rubber-hose stretch.** Limbs reach any length in one unbroken shot. *Fits content like:* reaching a top shelf; a long-distance handshake; catching a falling baby bird.\n- **Transformation.** Anything becomes something else mid-motion (a tail into a question mark, a hose into a saxophone). *Fits content like:* an idea turning into a product; a problem into a tool; a word into an object.\n- **The bouncing-ball singalong.** Lyrics on a title card, a ball hopping from word to word on the beat. *Fits content like:* a jingle; a safety rhyme; a slogan to remember.\n- **The film is a physical object.** Title cards roll up like window shades; the iris is grabbed and pulled shut; intertitles get bumped; the frame line slips. *Fits content like:* a brand reveal; a \"the end?\" twist; a character escaping the cartoon.\n- **The ink bottle.** An artist's pen draws the hero, who then argues with it. *Fits content like:* a making-of; a mascot introduction; a lesson about drawing.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **Flat grey frames.** Push backgrounds to mid grey, keep pure white and ink for characters, add a contrast boost.\n- **Wide shots kill gags**: a far shot makes characters tiny and Mickey-mousing unreadable. Use medium shots, and a packed medium-full for crowds.\n- \"Sad\" brows drawn as \"angry\": define brows by inner / outer end (inner = near the nose), not by left / right.\n- A limb drawn behind a body gets hidden by the silhouette (a handle, a tail): kick and reach outward past it.\n- Painted characters collide with background props at some camera positions: check every shot's last frame.\n- Smoke puffs in a row read as thought bubbles: scatter and grow them.\n- Short voice clips make subtitles flash: merge consecutive lines into one card.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `toon.js` (constant-width ink, line boil, affine stack), `film.js` (gate and film damage, reusable as is), `chars.js` (rigs, pie eyes, gloves, shoes), `cast.js` (breathing props), `bg.js` (cached watercolour backgrounds), `ui.js` (title lettering, title-card subtitles), `mix.py` (foley, radio chain, optical ageing), `music/score.py` (big band via `core/audio/sampler.py`). File map and commands: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md#build-notes).\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the location (or several), the cast, the structure, the opening, the ending, the camera path, the tempo and the band's line-up. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a singalong** (verse and chorus, the bouncing ball carrying the message while the set acts out each line); **a talent show** (objects take turns on a stage, the smallest wins); **a relay** (a thing passed hand to hand across a town, each hand-off a new instrument on the melody).\n- Openings: **out of the inkwell** (a pen draws the hero, who hops onto the set); **a curtain rise** (the band tuning up; a baton starts the film); **an iris on one eye** (it blinks, the iris widens to show whose eye it is).\n- Endings: **the card pulled over** (a character drags \"The End\" in front of itself mid-gag); **the film snaps** (the frame burns, a gloved hand splices it for one last bow); **the band packs up** (instruments leave until one note remains).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nCoffee Cup Chase\n"},{"id":"S4-L46","name":"80 年代赛璐璐动画","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L46.jpg","markdown":"# L46 80 年代赛璐璐动画（80s Cel Anime）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 卡通与动画\n- 长什么样：快递少女骑车穿过雨后霓虹都市，赶在黎明发射前送到一盘磁带。\n- 适合：霓虹城市、怀旧\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# 80s Cel Anime — Style Prompt\n\n> Hand-painted cel animation from the golden age of Japanese OVAs, re-created in code: flat two-tone cels over airbrushed backgrounds, backlit neon, limited-animation timing, played back as a late-80s videotape on a CRT television.\n> References (grammar only): 1980s Japanese OVA and TV cel animation in general (painted-background vs flat-cel contrast, backlit light, held drawings, speed grammar, city-pop openings). Borrow the era's craft, never a specific work's characters, mecha, logos, place names or signature shots.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nFour layers, and the contrast between them is the look:\n- **Background painting**: soft airbrushed gradients, glowing windows, wet reflections; painted once at load (colour + emissive pair), then only slid.\n- **Cel** (characters, vehicles, props): flat fills, **hard-edged** shadow and highlight shapes, **coloured** outlines, never pure black; stepped at 12 or 8 fps.\n- **Light**: backlit cel (透過光): signs, lamps, title letters glowing *through* the frame; flares, halation.\n- **Playback**: videotape on a CRT: chroma bleed, scanlines, grille, phosphor glow, darkened corners, faint noise.\n\nIf a frame looks like modern digital anime, one layer is missing. Not moe anime (round eyes, giant irises, hatched blush), not vector clip-art (uniform black lines, gradients in shapes), not a film-print pastiche (no gate weave or scratches: this is tape).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n**Cel rendering.** Fill, then a **hard** shadow crescent, then a **hard** highlight crescent, then the coloured outline. No gradients inside a cel shape (exception: one \"harmony\" painted still at a peak). Automatic shading: clip to the shape and fill `shape − shape shifted toward the light` with the shadow colour (even-odd); the same shifted away for the highlight and for a saturated neon **rim light** (2–4 px). Add hand-placed cast shadows. Outlines ~2–3 px at 1080p, scaled down (0.45–0.8×) in extreme close-ups. **Colour models per lighting** (色指定): define each character in daylight, derive other sets by multiplying lit / shadow / line separately, plus a backlit silhouette set with a warm rim.\n\n**People the 80s way.** Construct the head (sphere + jaw): long egg-shaped face, soft-point chin, far eye foreshortened with a cheekbone turn in 3/4. **Almond** eyes near the vertical middle of the head, a thick upper lash line flicking outward, a tall oval layered iris, two highlights, long thin brows. Nose = a shadow plane + one stroke; one faint blush layer, never hatched. Hair in overlapping locks with sharp uneven tips over a wider dark under-layer, a continuous \"angel ring\" highlight with a zigzag lower edge. Lines: skin thin warm dark brown, hair dark purple; only hard props near-black. Emotion lives in brows, lid height, iris size, mouth corners and shoulders. Build and approve a **model sheet** first; draw every shot from the same head function.\n\n**Backgrounds.** Painted once at load: sky gradients, airbrushed clouds with lit undersides, buildings or landscape with painted light sources, and always a matching **emissive plate** that slides with the colour plate. Wet ground = a flipped, blurred, streaked copy of the plate darkened with ripple bands. Generic sign words only, never brands. Moving plates get a light horizontal smear (~16 px) against strobing.\n\n**Light and lens.** Bloom strongly from the emissive canvas, weakly from the colour canvas above ~0.9; tint the widest bloom red-orange (halation). Flares: star-burst, anamorphic streak, hexagonal ghosts through frame centre. Stamp every cel's silhouette in black onto the emissive canvas so glow never bleeds through it.\n\n**Videotape + CRT, restrained and deterministic:** chroma bleed (sharp luma, horizontally blurred and slightly shifted chroma), fine scanlines anchored to output pixels whose gap shrinks on brights, a faint RGB grille, phosphor glow, darkened corners without curved borders, faint signal noise. Subtitles are composited inside the pass after grading. Two optional accents: a **power-on** (dot → line → picture) and **tracking noise** (a few frames of tearing, rolling band, vertical hop). Check moiré on a 720p downscale. Frame: full 16:9 as a theatrical feature (4:3 pillarbox is valid for an OVA feel).\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- Painted plates carry gradients and atmosphere; cels are flat, few-colour, saturated. Each time of day gets its own colour model, never a filter over the frame.\n- The protagonist has **one accent colour** that pops in every lighting set. Line colours are dark versions of their fill.\n- Skin gets its own colour in every set; multiplying daylight skin by a tint turns faces pink under neon.\n- Examples: night city (indigo, violet, neon magenta, cyan, window gold); seaside noon (cobalt, white cumulus, sand, coral); classroom dusk (amber, chalk green, brown wood, purple shadows).\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- Subtitles live on the tape, composited inside the CRT pass: a semi-condensed sans (e.g. Barlow Semi Condensed 600, ~50–56 px), cream-yellow with a thick dark outline and soft shadow, ~96 px above the bottom; a second speaker may take a second colour and a small boxed tag. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s); a line yields to the next.\n- Titles: heavy italic Latin in a **chrome gradient** (sky → white horizon line → violet → pink → gold), dark + neon outlines, optional katakana line (e.g. Dela Gothic One), glowing through the emissive layer, flickering on at 12 fps with a light sweep and a star glint.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Characters on twos (12 fps)**; performance beats (blinks, eye openings, lip flaps, gestures) on threes (8 fps). Camera, light sweeps, flares and rain on ones (24 fps); a stepped camera judders.\n- **Cycles**: hair and cloth as ribbons driven by a travelling sine, 4-drawing cycle (6 when calm); bounce = a 12 fps jitter of a few px.\n- **Move light, not drawings**: coloured bands sweeping across a held cel (`source-atop`).\n- **Impact frames**: 2 frames of an inverted or monochrome silhouette over radial lines, then the result.\n- **Hold the peak**: a painted still with a slow pan, only cycles moving.\n- Fast blinks (one 8 fps closed drawing); eye openings in three drawings; lip flaps of 3–4 shapes at 8 fps ending on a held expression.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Multiplane crane over a tall plate | a world revealed layer by layer | arriving; descending into a place; rising above it |\n| Side tracking, 3–4 parallax layers | steady momentum | a journey; a chase; progress |\n| Rear view into a pseudo-3D canyon | speed, pressure ahead | a race; a tunnel; a countdown |\n| Held bust with light bands | speed or tension without animation | a drive; waiting; a decision |\n| Insert with bokeh and a sliding highlight | this object matters | a letter; a key; a ticket |\n| Extreme close-up on eyes | resolve, recognition | a choice; a promise |\n| Slow pan across a painted still | awe, a held breath | a peak; a memory; a landscape |\n| Radial speed lines + one-beat freeze | the instant before impact | a leap; a collision; a goal |\n| Truck-in through a window or screen | entering another world | a flashback; a broadcast; a dream |\n| Locked two-shot with lip flaps | conversation | banter; a confession; an argument |\n| An earlier composition repainted | what changed | after a storm; years later |\n\nCut on bars. Framing: cels readable against the painted plate; keep the lower ~120 px clear for subtitles. Transitions: cuts, white flashes, impact frames, tracking noise at a tape-related beat; no digital dissolves or wipes.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Instruments** (city pop, synth funk, anime-opening pop): saxophone or synth-brass lead, FM electric piano with a bell transient, slap bass, chorus-y 16th guitar chanks, gated-reverb snare and big toms, drum machine, arpeggiated analog synth, string pad, FM bell sparkle. Extended jazz-pop harmony (maj7, 9ths).\n- **Techniques, as options**: band-pass the score to cassette/AM when a diegetic source plays, then open it; a brass hit on a title; a drum fill left open for an effect; one beat of true silence before a peak; a semitone lift for a last chorus.\n- **Foley** (synthesized): engines from an RPM curve, rain, tire hiss, wind, bells, radio squelch, impacts, sparks, cassette clicks, crowd murmur, door buzzers, footsteps on wet pavement; tape accents get their own thunk, whine, hiss.\n- **Voice**: an English-dub feel, short lines; a second voice through a phone/radio band-pass (~380–2800 Hz). Verify every line with whisper.\n- **Mix**: limit then compress TTS, voices ~10 dB over ducked music (duck ~9 dB), beds ducked ~3 dB, choruses louder than intros, two-pass loudnorm to −14 LUFS.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Small flat hero in a huge painted world.** *Fits content like:* a first day in a big company; a child in a museum hall; a lone researcher at an observatory.\n- **Stillness as drama** (held close-up or harmony still). *Fits content like:* an exam result; a goodbye at a gate; an unveiling.\n- **Backlit light.** *Fits content like:* a lantern festival; a screen full of code; a lighthouse; stage lights.\n- **Speed grammar** (speed lines, looping plates, impact frames). *Fits content like:* a sprint final; a data packet crossing the world; a kitchen rush.\n- **Light-band sweep on a held cel.** *Fits content like:* a night train; a scanner reading a page; a heartbeat quickening.\n- **A song as an object** (tape, radio, jukebox changes the score). *Fits content like:* a band's origin; a family memory; a call-in show.\n- **Tape artefacts as punctuation.** *Fits content like:* found home video; a rewind to the past; an interrupted broadcast.\n- **Colour-model change** (one place, another hour). *Fits content like:* a day in a shop; a farm's seasons; before/after a renovation.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- White cels bloom pink → bloom from the emissive canvas; weak, high-threshold bloom on colour.\n- Neon bleeds through characters → silhouettes stamped into the emissive canvas.\n- Black holes in highlights → clamp before S-curves.\n- Thick rim light swallows thin shapes → 2–4 px offsets.\n- Hair like tentacles, a face like a paper mask, moe eyes, uniform black lines → the drawing rules in §2.\n- Pink faces under coloured light → skin gets its own colour per set; weaker light sweeps on faces.\n- Front views are the hardest drawing → hide them behind a visor, a windscreen or backlight when you can.\n- Pseudo-3D points behind the camera flip into the sky → clamp z to the near plane.\n- Canvas silently falls back to a serif → `document.fonts.load` every weight before painting.\n- CRT detail inflates files → noise in the shader, only `noise=c0s=2:allf=t` in ffmpeg, CRF ≈ 23; loudnorm in two passes with `linear=true`; don't let a limiter flatten the dynamics.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `cel.js` (path, cel, ribbon, flutter), `pal.js` (colour models), `head80.js` (80s head, 3 views × expressions), `hands.js`, `bg.js` (painted plates, crane plates, wet reflections), `fx.js` (rain, speed lines, flares, trails), `post.js` (WebGL tape + CRT pass: bloom, grade, subtitles, power-on, tracking noise), `hud.js`, `music/score.py` (numpy FM/slap/brass synthesis), `tools/`. `?test=model|close|side` draws model sheets, `?raw=1` skips the CRT pass. A minimal new use: a harbour sunset plate with its emissive pair, one cel boat shaded with `cel.js`, through `post.js`.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the structure, the characters, the world, the opening, the ending, the camera path, the pacing and the colour models. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **slice of life in four seasons** (one place, four colour models, a small change each time); **two-hander dialogue** (a locked two-shot comedy that breaks into one fantasy sequence); **the mock opening credits** (a character roster introduced one by one, each with a pose and a name card).\n- Openings: **in the middle of a conversation** (lip flaps already moving, no establishing shot); **a TV being switched on** to an in-world broadcast; **a close-up of a drawing hand** in a classroom, then cut to the world it draws.\n- Endings: **a freeze-frame** on a laugh with a \"to be continued\" card; **the tape runs out** into blue screen; **a slow pan up to a starry sky** from a quiet rooftop, no dialogue.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nCity Lights, 1987\n"},{"id":"S4-L47","name":"科幻情景喜剧卡通","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L47.jpg","markdown":"# L47 科幻情景喜剧卡通（Sci-Fi Sitcom Toon）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 卡通与动画\n- 长什么样：厌世天才开传送门只想买杯咖啡，却穿过越来越离谱的平行宇宙。\n- 适合：轻松搞笑\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Sci-Fi Sitcom Toon — Style Prompt\n\n> Adult-animation sci-fi sitcom: thick boiling outlines, flat colour, a duo whose reactions don't match, a glowing portal, and one palette per world.\n> References (grammar only): adult-swim-era sci-fi sitcom cartoons (dialogue-driven comedy, reaction close-ups, boiling line, sci-fi hardware as household junk). Borrow the *grammar*, never the characters.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nHand-drawn-looking 2D TV animation for adults: **uniform thick dark outlines that boil**, **flat fills with hard-edged shadow shapes (no gradients)**, **big white eyes with tiny pupils**, **elastic mouths**, grotesque-but-cute creatures, and sci-fi hardware treated like household junk. The comedy is **dialogue-driven**: improvised-feeling talk, interruptions, awkward pauses, reaction shots, escalation, and a last-second reversal. The genre's engine is **a tiny, mundane goal pursued with absurdly large sci-fi means**: whoever could solve anything uses it for something petty, and someone else pays the emotional price.\n\nNot a kids' cartoon (dry humour, long pauses), not anime (no sparkly eyes), not a vector explainer (lines boil).\n\n**Copyright red lines (non-negotiable):**\n- Never reproduce any existing show's characters, silhouettes or colour combos (e.g. a spiky-blue-haired old man in a lab coat + a kid in a yellow shirt), names, catchphrases, burp gags, logos, or the look of any existing portal gun.\n- Invent your own cast, props and portal device. Never write the source show's name in the film or docs; one line \"inspired by adult-swim-era sci-fi sitcom cartoons\" is enough.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Line**: near-black (a slightly purple black reads best), ~7 px for characters and props, 4–6 px for background detail, round joins. **Line width is constant in screen space**: close-ups are \"redrawn\" at the same weight, never scaled up (transform points to screen, then stroke with an identity transform).\n- **Line boil**: resample every outline in screen space (~7 px steps) and displace along the normal with low-frequency noise, amplitude ~1.7 px; cycle **3 boil drawings at 12 fps**. Everything boils, including held poses and backgrounds: a still frame is never dead.\n- **Fill**: flat colours only. Shadows are hard-edged darker shapes clipped inside the fill (one per form, away from the light). Glows are 1–2 flat translucent rings, not gradients. Skies are **flat colour bands**.\n- **Faces**: big white eyes (touching in 3/4 view), 4–5 px pupils, heavy lids for deadpan, eye bags. Parametric mouths (open, width, curl, skew) with interior, teeth, tongue. Brows carry the emotion.\n- **Cast design**: silhouettes must read in solid black. Give each lead one absurd costume idea. Avoid white lab coat + spiky hair.\n- **Creatures**: every world may get one creature that is disgusting *and* adorable; keep it non-verbal (sound effects) so the film stays a dialogue between the leads. Check that anything behind furniture still shows face and upper body.\n- **The portal** (or whatever device moves between worlds): the only thing that glows. A lumpy rim with a thick outline, rotating spiral arms, a pale core, orbiting sparks, drips, a flat translucent wash over the scene while it's open. Open with a gentle overshoot (~12 %, back-ease ~0.3 s); a springy overshoot covers the actors.\n- **Screen graphics**: a retro-terminal tag (VT323) typed in, coloured per world.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **One palette per world**, 3–5 colours, maximally different from the previous world in hue *and* value, so the audience reads \"we jumped\" from colour alone.\n- Line colour and eye white never change between worlds; everything else may.\n- Glow colour belongs to the travel device only: nothing else in any world may use it.\n- A deliberately bland palette (beige, brown) is itself a joke: use it for the world that is \"suspiciously normal\".\n- Examples of a sequence of worlds: sage lab → magenta and lime jelly → orange and teal; or grey office → ultraviolet and gold → mint and coral → beige. Our demo's values are in DEMO.md.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Subtitles**: bottom centre, a rounded heavy sans (Baloo 2 ExtraBold ~50 px), white with a ~11 px black outline, so they look like the cartoon's own captions, with a small **speaker pill** in the character's colour (boiling outline) on the left. Lines over ~1300 px wrap to two lines. Overlapping lines stack; the older one dims to ~70 %.\n- **Timing**: hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s) for narration; for rapid dialogue, `max(audio + 0.35 s, 0.9 s + chars / 17)`. Clip at world changes and **before a silent beat**, so the silence plays on a clean screen.\n- **Title**: chunky display lettering popped in on 12 fps steps on a musical hit. Choose a period look that is not the source genre's logo: **avoid acid green, slime drips and wobbly bubble lettering** together, which reads as a specific show.\n- Keep faces out of the subtitle band (bottom ~170 px) and the world tag clear of faces.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Characters on twos (12 fps)**: poses, mouths, blinks, walk cycles and boil step at 12 fps. **Camera moves, portal swirl, flying props and wipes on ones (24 fps)**.\n- **Acting over moving**: held poses with small changes (eye twitch, pupils sliding, a gulp, sweat drops). Idle life: breathing (±1 % squash), a nod driven by speech loudness.\n- **Anticipation → action** before every gesture.\n- **Squash & stretch** on landings (decaying cosine squash ~22 %), stretch while flying out of a portal; \"sucked in\" = scale toward the portal centre while stretching.\n- **Lip-sync from audio**: per line, RMS (open) and spectral centroid (wide vs round) at 24 Hz, sampled at the 12 fps drawing time. Exaggerate for screams (mouth ×1.5–1.9).\n- **Nervous jitter**: a 12 fps random offset of 3–6 px plus trembling pupils; amplitude grows with panic.\n- What never moves in a cold pause: everything but the boil.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Medium two-shot | the relationship; who reacts how | dialogue; a standoff; a deal |\n| Hard cut to a close-up | an interruption, a face breaking | the cut-off line; the realisation; a lie |\n| Insert extreme close-up of an object | the absurd detail | a reveal; the problem itself; the wrong result |\n| Reaction close-up | the laugh lives on the face | after every absurd image |\n| Tight close-up + short shake on one word | panic | the key word; an alarm; a scream |\n| Rush zoom into the portal → swirl wipe | leaving a world | any jump between places, times or versions |\n| Same framing repeated across worlds | only the world changes | a montage; a comparison; a list |\n| Very slow push-in on a still two-shot | a cold silence | the pause after a reversal; a confession |\n| Wide with the device and both leads in frame | the whole situation at once | a callback; a trap; a fresh start |\n| Over-the-shoulder onto a screen | reading bad news | a message; a readout |\n| Whip pan between two faces | overlapping argument | a fight; a bet; a double take |\n\nRule: every absurd image is followed by a **reaction close-up**. Transitions go through the travel device (swirl wipes) or hard cuts; no dissolves.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Synthesized score, no samples needed.** A **theremin**-like lead (sine + a little 2nd/3rd harmonic, legato portamento ~70 ms, vibrato fading in ~150 ms after each onset, spring reverb) over analog bass, a square-wave arpeggio and a retro drum machine. Minor modes and chromatic lines suit the main theme.\n- **Genre cues per world** (options): surf guitar; polka accordion; toy-piano lullaby; elevator bossa (Karplus–Strong nylon guitar); brushed swing with vibes; tuba + slide whistle; warm Rhodes + a vocal \"aah\"; tremolo strings for panic.\n- **Techniques (options)**: a cue's first beat is the cut; a tempo that snaps cuts to a grid; rule of three becoming machine-gun (same stinger, shorter each time).\n- **Silence is the punchline**: hard-cut the music, reverb tails included, on a reveal and for a cold pause. Leave only room tone (fluorescent hum, a clock tick, a fridge).\n- **Foley (synthesized)**: portal open (sub boom + down-swept noise + rising swirl), hum, close \"fwump\", swirl whoosh per wipe, pop/boing, wet glorp/squish, slurp, chomp, blink \"blip\", click-beep, kazoo sting, squeaky toy.\n- **Voices**: a two-hander with contrasting voices: low, flat and slow for the deadpan lead (light saturation + a ~180 Hz bump for gravel); higher and faster for the nervous one (presence boost). A tiny creature may say one or two words, pitched up. Compress, RMS-match, add short early reflections so voices sit in the room. Duck music ~−7 dB under dialogue. Master −14 LUFS.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Parallel worlds.** Each jump = new palette, new cue, new creature. *Fits content like:* versions of a product; careers you could have had; the same city in five eras.\n- **Duo contrast.** Deadpan vs panic: the joke is in the difference between reactions. *Fits content like:* a veteran and a new hire; a parent and a teenager; an AI and its user.\n- **Rule of three → machine-gun.** Two slow wrong answers, then one-second worlds cut on the beat with the same rejection word. *Fits content like:* failed prototypes; bad date ideas; tax forms.\n- **The suspiciously normal world.** The pattern breaks; the calm is the joke. *Fits content like:* the one boring answer that works; a quiet office after chaos; the real customer.\n- **Dead-air reversal.** A long silence after the twist, then one line revealing a character's values. *Fits content like:* a price reveal; a fine print clause; a gift that wasn't wanted.\n- **Hardware as junk.** World-bending tech used for a petty chore. *Fits content like:* a quantum computer used for a grocery list; a rocket to deliver a sandwich; a time machine to fix a typo.\n- **Callback button.** The last line repeats the first so the story loops. *Fits content like:* a catchphrase of a brand; a morning routine; a running complaint.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- TTS reads hyphen stutters as words: write syllables (\"buh, buh-broken\"); respell words it mangles; pick names that survive a low voice.\n- Whisper mis-hears very short clips unless padded with ~0.6 s of silence.\n- A real interruption: generate the interrupted line *longer* than needed and truncate the audio where the other voice cuts in.\n- A springy overshoot swallows the actors: gentle back-ease.\n- Food and liquids need cues (surface detail, drips, steam) or they read as other objects.\n- Square-wave synth cymbals alias into harsh 8–20 kHz fizz: band-passed noise plus a little FM.\n- A sustained bass below ~40 Hz is rumble on laptop speakers: end an octave higher.\n- A cut music cue without its reverb tail cut is not dead silence.\n- `ffmpeg fps=1` contact sheets are offset up to 0.5 s: don't misdiagnose a shot boundary.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `toon.js` (vector engine: matrix stack, screen-space boil, constant line width, flat shading), `chars.js` (parametric eyes, mouths, hands, noodle limbs), `worlds.js` (one function per world, creatures, the portal), `voice.py` (per-character processing + lip-sync envelopes), `asr.py` (padded whisper check), `music/score.py` (synthesized score from a cue list), `levels.py` (stem meters), `sheet.sh` / `strip.sh` (contact sheets, consecutive-frame strips). New shapes: build them with `toon.js` in world space; the stroke goes through the screen-space boil at constant weight.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the premise, the cast, the worlds, the jokes, the opening, the ending, the camera path, the pacing and the palettes. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **one world, many clocks** (the duo stays home and a device skips them through times of day, each time worse); **the job interview** (a panel interviews candidates from other worlds, each a different genre cue); **the tech-support call** (split screen between two worlds, one voice guiding the other through a disaster).\n- Openings: **mid-disaster** (the portal is already open and something is coming through); **the instruction manual** (a terminal readout explains the device before we see anyone); **the wrong world** (we start in the absurd world and only later see home).\n- Endings: **stuck elsewhere** (the device breaks and they settle in, happily); **the audience** (a creature from one world watches the whole film on a screen and rates it); **the cost** (a quiet final shot of what the errand cost someone, no joke).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nCoffee Run\n"},{"id":"S4-L48","name":"50s 扁平卡通","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L48.jpg","markdown":"# L48 50s 扁平卡通（Mid-century Cartoon）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 卡通与动画\n- 长什么样：用 50 年代教育片的口吻，三步教你装好一台扫地机。\n- 适合：老科普片的感觉，感知科普很合适\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Mid-century Cartoon — Style Prompt\n\n> 1950s flat colour and limited animation for \"follow along\" films: colour printed a few pixels off its broken ink line, bodies that hold still while hands do the work, abstract colour-block rooms, a calm classroom narrator, and a cool-jazz combo.\n> References (grammar only): UPA's early-1950s shorts (*Gerald McBoing-Boing*, *Rooty Toot Toot*): colour and line on separate cels, geometric people, colour-plane backgrounds, limited animation. 1950s classroom films (Coronet, Encyclopaedia Britannica): direct address, one sentence per picture, numbered cards. Charley Harper and Mary Blair: soft atomic-age complements, geometric figures. 1950s appliance manuals: numbered medallions, curved arrows, round call-outs, sunbursts. Copy none of their characters, drawings, lettering or music, and never name them in a film.\n\nA **scene style**: it performs a real use (a set-up guide, a how-to, a recipe card, a safety card) rather than telling a story. The viewer should come away able to do the thing. See §11 for the jobs.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nFlat, printed-looking colour with a thin, breathing ink line that never quite closes. **Every fill is a separate \"cel\" printed a few px off its line**, re-rolled on twos, so the picture shimmers like hand-registered paint while the camera glides. Light dry-brush tooth, paper grain, warm vignette.\n\nPeople and things are geometric: egg or bean heads with the nose part of the face outline, simple garments in two or three flat colours, four-finger cartoon gloves. **A presenter's body is a held drawing: only arms, hands, eyes and mouth animate.** Rooms are not drawn: a scene is one colour plane, a floor band, a horizon line with gaps, and one or two props.\n\nNot the 1930s rubber-hose cartoon (no black-and-white, no bouncing world, no hot jazz). Not a whiteboard explainer: it shows *how to do*, not *why*. The pictures are printed, not written on.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Off-register cels**: fill drawn with a small offset (a few px, down-right) from its line; the offset jitters ±1–2 px on twos. Keep it on everything, text included; switch it off only for a crisp diagram.\n- **Line**: 3–5 px ink ribbons with ±40 % width breathing and 12–25 % breaks. Characters ~4 px, props 3–4.5 px, walls in plans thicker and broken. Hands: one outline around the union of palm and fingers, thin finger separations.\n- **Shading**: one flat darker plane clipped inside a shape, never a gradient. Drop shadows are a copy of the shape offset down-right in a paper shade at ~50 %.\n- **Ornaments**: 4-point atomic sparkles, 8–18-point starbursts, sunburst rays (~30 wedges, semi-transparent), boomerang and kidney shapes, polka dots.\n- **Grain**: light; print, not film.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **A warm paper ground, a warm near-black ink, soft atomic-age complements.** Nothing pure or neon.\n- **One ground colour per scene**: a lighter tint for walls, a darker shade for floors. Never place the product on its own colour, never the presenter on their own accent.\n- **The product keeps one colour through the whole film**; the accent (a presenter's garment, stars, bullets) is a different hue. Step grounds rotate through the rest.\n- Examples: *coral / mustard / turquoise* on cream; *tomato / olive / powder blue* on oatmeal; *plum / lime / sand* on off-white.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- Roles: a 1950s script for a kicker (Oleo Script Bold); a slab serif (Alfa Slab One) for titles, numerals and headers, in ink over an offset colour plate; a geometric sans (Jost 500/600) for body, details, labels and captions.\n- **Caption card**: a paper-coloured rounded slab with a thin ink border (its colour cel offset too), small turning stars at the ends, ~40 px sans, bottom-centre. Fades in quickly with a small rise. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s, chars/12 + 1 s).\n- **Step header**: an ink disc with a paper numeral, a small letter-spaced \"STEP n OF N\", and the step title with a cream plate; it slides in on twos.\n- **Detail tags**: a paper card with a star bullet, popping on the action beat next to the call-out. Data lands on the picture, never as a slide.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **On twos:** poses, hand shapes and the cel jitter change at 12 fps. The camera, iris wipes, arrows, plan routes and rays move at 24 fps.\n- **Held body, moving hands:** switch poses with a single in-between. A hand action has anticipation (pull back or lift), a fast action (0.15–0.25 s, eased out), a follow-through (recoil, settle, lift away) and lands exactly on a beat.\n- **Pop-ins:** medallions, tags, labels and titles scale in with a back-ease (~0.25 s, strong overshoot).\n- **Reaching the floor**: a kneel pose with 2-bone IK keeps the hands on the object while it moves; never stretch standing arms.\n- **Lettering**: a script writes on left→right with a clip; headers slide in on twos.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nThe camera is \"the stand\": never handheld. A vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Slow push (a few %) | Collecting the eye | a product on its stage; a result settling |\n| Iris through a medallion | The number *is* the door | a change of step; a change of rule |\n| Round call-out with a leader line | Context and detail at once | a hand action; a screen; a small part |\n| Tilt with a pointing finger | The finger leads the frame | the one action that matters most |\n| Push into a device, pan along signal rings | Person → interface → device | pairing; connecting |\n| Iris from a round object into a top view | Circle to circle, function to plan | a button starting a job; a lid opening; a dial |\n| Pull-back with counter-rotation from a detail to a plan | One lane becomes a pattern | a route, a schedule, a garden, a floor plan |\n| Colour-block wipe (side, bottom-up) | Turning the manual's page | a tip; a summary; a new card |\n| Split frame in two colour blocks | Right vs wrong, before vs after | a safety rule; an upgrade |\n| Dead still ≥ 3 s | \"This is the frame to screenshot\" | a summary; a CTA; a QR code |\n| Iris-out onto one object | The last circle | a closing on the product, the logo, the result |\n\nKey subjects ≥ 1/3 frame height and clear of the caption band. Transitions are circles, colour-block wipes and cuts on a beat; no dissolves.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\nThree layers, always:\n- **Music**: an original West-Coast cool-jazz combo: flute or muted trumpet, vibraphone (chords, arpeggios, glissandi, tremolo), pizzicato walking bass, brushes (sticks on the ride for energy), celesta, bongos or finger snaps. Not ragtime, not a spy big band, not piano and strings. Options: give **every step its own vibraphone chord** and bring them back one per summary item; halve or double the feel instead of changing tempo; move up a step for a payoff.\n- **Silence as a tool**: cut *everything*, ambience included, for the beat before the most important action, so the first sound after it (the click, the pour) is the step. Room tone alone before a summary.\n- **Foley by material**: cardboard creaks and thumps, appliance plastic knocks and clicks, floor scrapes, glass and ceramic clinks, pours, damped rubber stamps for medallions, paper swishes for wipes, slide whistles for dimension arrows, rising pips for \"connected\", pen scratches for a route, an iris \"shhk\".\n- **Ambience**: warm room tone (low-passed brown noise, very quiet), a mantel-clock tick on the grid; a device's motor hum can take over when it works.\n- **Mix**: J/L cuts (lines start before their iris, tails ring on); narration compressed on top, music ducks ~8 dB, foley unducked, −14 LUFS.\n- **Voice**: calm classroom authority, one clause per line (\"Step one. Set it against a wall.\"). Whisper-check every line; accents can blur key nouns.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nEach move serves information. A menu: use the ones your film needs.\n\n- **Off-register colour** makes an info card hand-made and warm. *Fits content like:* museum rules; a bank app screen; hotel check-in.\n- **Limited animation points the eye**: the still body leaves the hand as the only mover, and the hand *is* the step (one hand action per step, on a beat). *Fits content like:* threading a sewing machine; changing a bike tyre; setting a thermostat.\n- **The round call-out** is the close-up; the step's data lands inside or on it on the action beat. *Fits content like:* a dosage on a measuring cup; a torque value on a bolt; a PIN on a keypad.\n- **The numbered medallion** marks hierarchy and opens the next step; it stamps onto the outgoing subject and the camera goes through it. *Fits content like:* a pool's five rules; a checklist for a flight; stages of a recycling sort.\n- **The payoff turns function into ornament**: a real process (a route, a watering schedule, a seating plan) draws itself region by region and, seen whole, reads as an atomic-age textile. *Fits content like:* a sprinkler's coverage; a delivery round; a week of medication.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- A route that ignores obstacles → follow the product's real rules; flow streamlines bend around obstacles and never cross. Lay it one region per beat, not all at once.\n- A separate nose wedge looks glued on → one open spline from the bridge round the skull, closed at the nose tip.\n- Thin arms read as sticks from the neck → shoulders at the torso corners, sleeves as a thick ink ribbon with a colour ribbon on top.\n- Fat-blob fingers turn the hand into a cloud → stroke every part first, fill second (one union outline), slender fingers with separation lines.\n- Zooming the world triples the ink → close-ups in call-out circles drawn at native scale.\n- Detail text without reading time → move the data into the detail, the context into the title and the voice; chars/12 + 1 s.\n- A transition drawing the next scene before its start → clamp local time to just past the start.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\n`demo/engine/toon.js` (the style: `setClock`, `shape` for any shape in the style, `ink` ribbons, `plane`, two-plate `text`, `fit`, the manual `arrow`, ornaments, the global register `REG`), `demo/engine/chars.js` (presenter, glove hands, the demo product), `demo/engine/icons.js` (18 icons). `demo/film.js` has liftable helpers: `callout()`, `medallionAt()`, `tag()`, `stepHeader()`, the flow-route generator `laneY()` + `buildPath()`, and the transition block (medallion iris, button iris, colour-block wipe, iris-out). API table and a minimal example: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md#engine-reference).\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the thing, the presenter (or none), which steps and in what order, the opening, the ending, the camera path, the colours, the pacing and the music. Use cases are **grammar for the order and duration of information**; build your own timeline from the user's facts.\n\n| Use case | Information order | Hold per layer | Length |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Product set-up guide** | product name → title → step title → step detail on the action beat → payoff → tip → CTA | title ≥ 4 s; step title the whole step; detail ≥ chars/12 + 1 s; summary ≥ 3 s clean | 30–40 s, 2–5 steps |\n| **Safety / rules card** | rule number → rule → one \"why\"; a held figure, a medallion, a gesture, a pictogram per rule | 3–4 s per rule | 20–40 s, 3–6 rules |\n| **Recipe or ritual card** | step → quantity/time → result; side view, call-out on the hands, result as a top-view pattern | 3–5 s per step | 30–45 s |\n| **Onboarding for an app or service** | screen action → setting value → confirmation stamp; the screen lives in the call-out | 3–4 s per action | 20–30 s |\n| **Store or event notice** | headline → time/place → CTA; colour-block wipes between cards | ≥ 3 s per card | 15–25 s |\n\nAll far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a rules card with no product**, one held figure and a medallion per rule; **a recipe on a tabletop**, seen from the side, ending in a top view; **a do / don't split**, each rule shown twice in two colour blocks.\n- Openings: **the finished result first**, then \"here's how\"; **the problem in one gesture** (a hand fumbling a cable); **a numbered list** stamped on, then opened item by item.\n- Endings: **a checklist ticking itself**; **the hands at rest** beside the finished thing; **a QR code** held dead still in a starburst.\n\nSwapping `demo/content.json` and rebuilding is a technical check that the engine re-flows, not a way to make a film.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build, content fields): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nMeet Pip\n"},{"id":"S4-L49","name":"16-bit 像素 RPG","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L49.jpg","markdown":"# L49 16-bit 像素 RPG（16-bit Pixel RPG）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 游戏\n- 长什么样：勇士在最终 Boss 门前存档，存档画面闪回一路冒险。\n- 适合：学习像打怪升级\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# 16-bit Pixel RPG — Style Prompt\n\n> A short film that looks, sounds and *behaves* like a mid-90s console role-playing game: 320×180 pixels, a limited indexed palette, text boxes that type, menus, save files, chip music with echo.\n> References (grammar only): mid-90s console RPGs as a system of dialog boxes, menus, battles, overworlds and save points; the palette tricks of indexed hardware (palette swaps, fades by steps, cycling); the colour-depth history of consoles (4 shades → 8-bit → 16-bit). Never use existing game names, characters, UI frames, fonts, logos, melodies or sound effects. Learn the grammar, not the assets.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n- **320×180 native pixels**, scaled ×6 nearest-neighbour to 1080p; everything, text included, lives in the framebuffer.\n- **A limited, indexed palette**; gradients are **ordered dither**, never blends.\n- **Sprites** around 20–34 px tall animating in a handful of frames; faces act in **portraits**.\n- **Story told the way a game tells it**: dialog boxes with portraits, menus, status bars, battle messages, save files, item fanfares.\n- It takes the game's systems seriously as a language for feeling.\n\nNot HD-2D (no depth of field, bloom or real-time light), not CRT nostalgia (no scanlines, no grain), not a parody.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Indexed framebuffer** (`Uint8Array` of palette indices) presented through a **lookup table** (index → RGB), so a whole palette can change in one frame. Camera moves are **integer pixels**; each parallax layer rounds its own offset.\n- **Master palette** of ~32 colours (a free palette such as ENDESGA-32), plus **protected duplicates** of a few indices that no palette effect touches.\n- **Colour-depth tables** computed from the master palette: identity (16-bit), nearest of ~15 NES-like colours (8-bit; it needs a dark green and a dark brown), 4 fixed luminance thresholds (4-colour, drawn natively at 160×90 and doubled), a faded set (`lum^0.8` → ~7 blue-greys), and intermediate subsets for step-by-step collapse. Map by luminance, never by rank.\n- **Light is index stepping**: `LIGHT[]` / `DARK[]` tables move each index one step along its ramp; glows are Bayer-thresholded rings. Firelight in a low-colour era uses a warm remap (greens → browns → rust), not generic brightening.\n- **Dither**: 4×4 Bayer for gradients and glows; light shafts as a flat 50 % checker with a 25 % edge.\n- **Sprites from a rig**: capsule limbs with 3-tone shading from an upper-left light, a per-part inner line in the material's darkest tone, a global 1-px ink outline, plus hand-placed ASCII heads. One rig gives every pose; lower eras drop the highlight tone. Give the hero a **silhouette mark** (a garment tail, a hat, a hairstyle).\n- **Portraits** 32×32, 3/4 view, base face + eye/brow and mouth overlays; they carry facial acting (the sprite face has one eye pixel).\n- **Props** can be flat-shaded 3D shapes quantised to a few protected indices (e.g. a rotating crystal at 8 frames per 90°).\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **Palette = feeling.** Colour depth, fades and swaps are story tools; change the whole palette by table, never by alpha.\n- **Colour depth can stand for time**: older = fewer colours, lower resolution.\n- **Protected indices**: a few colours exempt from every effect become the thread through the film and allow match cuts between eras. Use one or two; more and they stop meaning anything.\n- **Warm hues survive reductions**: keep orange, pink and plum in reduced subsets, or the first step turns a scene solid red.\n- Example uses of protected colours: a red kite across a childhood; a gold coin in a grey economy; the green of one plant in a faded city.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **The dialog box is the subtitle**, drawn in the framebuffer at the bottom quarter: a layered rounded border (ink / white / silver), a dithered dark-blue fill, a 32×32 portrait on the left, an **original** variable-width pixel font (cap ~7 px) with a 1-px shadow. It types with the voice (~38–40 chars/s; slower in older eras), ▼ blinks when done.\n- **Skins follow the era** (light box for 4-colour, black box with white frame for 8-bit, blue for 16-bit).\n- Battle and system messages go in a thin top window. Every box stays ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s); the next box cuts the previous one. Export the `.srt` from the same subtitle array.\n- Titles in the pixel font at 2×, 8-neighbour ink outline, row-by-row gradient, Bayer-dissolved in and out.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- Output 24 fps. Sprites step at **8 fps** (walk) to **12 fps** (flames, blinks); camera and parallax move on ones at integer pixels.\n- **Game-native acting**: hop forward to act, cast pose, knockback recoil, kneel, KO blink (12 fps on/off) and vanish; breath = shoulders up 1 px.\n- **UI motion**: windows slide in over ~0.3 s (ease-out, integer px); a hand cursor bobs 1 px at 4 fps and **stops when the character hesitates**.\n- **Transitions**: thumbnail expand + mosaic, mosaic cross with a palette switch at the peak, the encounter swirl (polar twist + mosaic + Bayer dissolve to white), screen shake (3–2–1 px over 8 frames), palette step fades (4 levels). Never alpha fades.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. Game cameras are simple and legible. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Side-scroll with parallax layers | journey; loneliness; progress | a commute; a quest; a timeline |\n| Locked frame with UI on one third | a choice; a system speaking | a menu decision; a stats reveal |\n| Battle side view (enemy / party / windows) | conflict as a system | an exam; a negotiation; an illness |\n| Top-down overworld pan | the whole world; where things are | a city; a map of a company; a trip |\n| Integer-pixel tilt up something tall | awe; scale | a tower; a door; a mountain |\n| Hold while the palette changes | loss or change without motion | grief; a memory fading; nightfall |\n| Back view at a threshold | resolve; the unknown ahead | a first day; a launch; a departure |\n| Zoom by mosaic into a thumbnail | entering a memory or a file | a flashback; a save slot; a photo |\n\nFraming: characters stay above the dialog box (floor line above its top edge); menus leave the action visible.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Chiptune voices**: pulse waves at 12.5/25/50 % duty, 4-bit triangle bass, LFSR noise drums, wavetable leads, SNES-style echo (a few taps ~180–370 ms, feedback ~0.4, low-pass in the loop). Original melodies only.\n- **Fidelity follows colour depth** (option): 4-colour = mono, 4-bit volume steps, low sample rate; 8-bit = NES-like mono; 16-bit = stereo with echo; a faded present = the same music muffled.\n- **Techniques (options)**: regional variations of one motif; the arrangement losing one layer per palette step; a key change or the full band returning on a colour flood; a phrase left on the dominant and resolved later; an item-get fanfare as a punctuation.\n- **Foley**, synthesised and bit-crushed to the scene's era: cursor and select blips, text blips (pitch per speaker), footsteps with echo, fire crackle, magic shimmer (triangle arpeggio), charge and blast, HP tick-down, KO blink, a bit-drop per palette step, save ticks, door grind, coin and item jingles, menu error buzz.\n- **One organic sound** (a breath, a heartbeat) can make a character human at one moment.\n- **Silence**: true zero including echo tails.\n- **Mix**: music ducks ~7–8 dB under voice (fast attack, slow release); −14 LUFS, grain 0.\n- **Voice** (optional): first person, few short lines; or text blips only, for a character who exists only in text.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Palette collapse / flood.** Colour depth drops one level per beat, or full colour floods back through a dither wave. *Fits content like:* a city blackout and recovery; a company in crisis; burnout and rest.\n- **Protected colour match cut.** One exempt colour links two eras in the same screen position. *Fits content like:* a family heirloom; a brand colour across decades; a team jersey.\n- **Save files as memory.** A list of files; choosing NEW FILE refuses to overwrite. *Fits content like:* versions of a product; chapters of a career; photo albums.\n- **Game UI as narration.** A greyed-out name, an item description, a stat screen. *Fits content like:* a team member leaving; a skill learned; a budget.\n- **Level up.** Stats rise with a fanfare. *Fits content like:* a student's year; a startup's funding round; learning a language.\n- **Encounter swirl.** A crash into danger. *Fits content like:* a deadline; a diagnosis; a storm.\n- **Era shift.** The same characters redrawn in older hardware. *Fits content like:* a town's history; grandparents' youth; the evolution of a device.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Rank-based palette mapping crushes a scene to black → map by luminance with a gamma.\n- Reduced subsets without warm hues turn a sunset solid red → keep warm hues.\n- An 8-bit set without dark green or brown turns night grass blue; generic brightening near fire turns grass neon → warm remap.\n- Post-quantising a full-colour scene to 4 shades is mud → draw low eras natively with known indices at half resolution.\n- Pixel hair as a smooth dome reads as a helmet; single-pixel alternation reads as noise → 2–3 big spikes. Pale clothing reads as skin. Held props cross the face in side view → back hand.\n- A dark plank door reads as a jail → panels, bevels, studs.\n- Negative modulo indexes `undefined` → `((n % m) + m) % m`.\n- The dialog box covers the bottom quarter → keep the floor line above it.\n- Match cuts on protected colours need both screen positions computed.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `px.js` (indexed framebuffer, palettes and LUTs for every colour depth, Bayer dither, mosaic), `font.js` (variable-width pixel font), `ui.js` (windows, dialog, menus, file list, status bars), `sprites.js` (rig + ASCII heads), `portraits.js` (32×32 portraits + expressions), `props.js`, `scenes.js` (parallax, index lighting, tilt), `main.js` (shots, transitions, palette timing, sound events, subtitles). `?sheet=1` renders a model sheet; `?frame=<scene>&lut=<table>` renders an environment through any colour-depth table. File list and build steps: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md) \"Build notes\".\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide which game system the topic hides, the characters, the eras, the palette plan, the opening and the ending. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **an overworld tour** (a top-down map; each town a chapter entered by a door transition); **a shop and an inventory** (the story told entirely through items bought, used and sold); **a turn-based duel** (two sides take turns; each move is a scene of the topic).\n- Openings: **PRESS START**, a title screen with an attract-mode demo; **a battle already in progress** with HP low; **a boot screen** that fails to load and must be retried.\n- Endings: **GAME OVER** answered by a choice (retry / give up) left open; **credits scrolling over the overworld** as characters walk home; **the save screen** where the last slot is written and the menu closes.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nThe Last Save Point\n"},{"id":"S4-L50","name":"HD-2D","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L50.jpg","markdown":"# L50 HD-2D\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 游戏\n- 长什么样：灯塔熄灭，孙女提着最后一簇火穿过夜林、爬上风暴悬崖。\n- 适合：像素加光影，有电影感\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# HD-2D — Style Prompt\n\n> Hand-made pixel sprites standing like paper cut-outs inside a lit, fogged, fully 3D diorama, shot from a high three-quarter angle through a tilt-shift lens: a toy world where every lamp throws a real shadow.\n> References (grammar only): *Octopath Traveler* (Square Enix × Acquire, 2018) and its sequel (2023), the games that defined the HD-2D look. Learn the grammar: sprite-in-diorama, high 3/4 camera, tilt-shift focus band, point lights with shadows, heavy bloom and vignette, framed dialogue box. Never use their characters, places, logos, UI ornaments or music, and never name them in the film.\n> Name: \"HD-2D\" is Square Enix's term (and trademark) for that look. The library borrows it only to describe the style; this style and its demo have no connection to Square Enix and are not endorsed by it. Don't use \"HD-2D\" as a title or brand in a film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nA **3D miniature set** of boxes and cylinders wrapped in **pixel-art textures**, populated by **flat 2D pixel sprites that receive light and cast shadows**, seen from a **high, far camera** through a **tilt-shift band** so the world reads as a tabletop model. **Light is the subject**: practicals throw sprite shadows across the floor; shafts, fog, dust or rain hang in the air; strong bloom, heavy vignette. On top, a small **RPG UI**: chapter card, framed dialogue box with typewriter text, serif subtitles.\n\nThe key contrast: characters stay **flat, low-res and stepped** while camera, light, particles and focus move smoothly every frame.\n\nNot a voxel game (no cubic characters), not a flat 2D pixel RPG (the set is real 3D with real light), not a tilt-shift photo of a real miniature (textures are pixels, not materials).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Constant texel density**: surfaces drawn per pixel on small canvases, `NearestFilter`, world-space UVs at one density (e.g. 24 px/m); sprites on a similar scale (a character ~30–60 px tall).\n- **Colour from ramps**: 3–6 swatches picked by brightness through a narrow 4×4 Bayer dither. Sprites get a 1 px *selout* outline (neighbour colour darkened toward the ink).\n- **Boxes and cylinders** for buildings, floors, rock; **billboards** (instanced) for foliage, distant props, ridges. Windows are emissive pixel panes; fire is a pixel atlas stepping at ~10 fps; water a shader with quantized ripples and light columns toward the camera.\n- **Light rig per set**: one dim shadow-casting key, a hemisphere fill, **practical point lights** with shadows (lamps, fires, windows, screens), and a **front fill** ~2 m before the hero (a billboard lit from behind stays black). An additive glow sprite on every source gives bloom something to grab.\n- **Post chain, at render time**: supersampled render → physical DOF with tilt-shift in the same signed circle of confusion (above the band = far, below = near; far never bleeds over a sharp foreground) → bloom → vignette/grade → filmic tone map. No film grain.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **Two temperatures**: one large ambient key (cold, or pale, or dusty) against small warm or saturated practicals. The practicals carry the story; the ambient carries the mood.\n- The darkest value is a warm ink, never pure black; the brightest is the light source itself, bloomed.\n- Each set owns one fog colour and one light key, so a cut to a new place reads instantly.\n- The hero wears the one saturated costume colour that no set uses, so the tiny sprite is findable in a wide.\n- UI is one metal (gold, silver, copper) plus one paper white over a dark translucent plate.\n- Examples: night canal town (blue fog, amber windows); autumn market at dusk (violet sky, pink paper lanterns); snowy mine at noon (grey-white daylight, green mineral glow).\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- An inscriptional capital serif (Cinzel, wide tracking) for chapter labels and titles; an old-style serif (Cormorant, Cormorant Garamond italic) for subtitles, dialogue, taglines. All OFL.\n- **Narration subtitles**: italic serif ~46–54 px, centred low on a soft dark gradient band, one line per voice clip, in ~0.35 s before the voice.\n- **On-screen speech gets a dialogue box**, not a subtitle: dark plate, thin metal border, corner ornaments, name plate on its top edge, text **typed over ~90 % of the voice**, blinking caret, soft chime on entry.\n- Chapter cards and a title card belong to the grammar. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s). Export the `.srt` from the data that drives the overlay.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Sprites step, everything else glides.** Walk cycles 4 frames at 6–10 fps, idles alternate two frames at ~1–2 Hz, blinks every few seconds, pose changes are instant cuts between drawings. Camera, lights, particles, beams and focus update every output frame (60 fps).\n- **Poses are data**: a parameter object per drawing; a new pose is a new table entry.\n- **Lights breathe**: every flame flickers (a few summed sines, about ±20 %). A light's level is one value that drives the sprite drawing, the point light (intensity ∝ level^~1.3), the glow opacity and its scale together.\n- **Weather is procedural**: slanted rain streaks; lightning as a scripted flash curve (strike, dip, re-strike, decay) lifting sky, fill and exposure together.\n- The world never shakes except for impact; the set never moves, only the camera and what lives in it.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic. Always look **down** on the world (never at eye level); every path is a monotone spline of a few keys, no overshoot; focus is recomputed every frame from camera to the subject.\n\n| Move | Expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Very high, very far, slow push; band on one tiny subject | Preciousness, a whole place as a map | where we are; scale; a landmark |\n| Medium-high 3/4, near-static drift | Two figures on one stage | a conversation; a handover; a choice |\n| Lateral tracking, camera leading slightly, foreground passing blurred | A path through a world | travel; a process with steps; a commute |\n| Dive low and close while a figure kneels or stops | The toy world suddenly personal | exhaustion; care; a secret |\n| Rising pull-back, focus handed from figure to object | The act spreads outward | consequence; a system switching on |\n| Orbit half-way round a building or prop | An object seen from all sides | a workshop; a machine; a monument |\n| Top-down, straight overhead, tilt band as a strip | A board game, a plan | routes; a schedule; a queue |\n| Rack focus between two depths in a locked frame | Attention changing hands | a memory; someone watching; before/after |\n| Return to an earlier framing, changed | The rhyme shows what changed | aftermath; growth; a season later |\n\nFraming: characters ~1/12–1/8 of frame height; the sharp band follows the subject's projected height (clamped ~.2–.8 of screen); with no character, use a fixed band height per shot. Transitions: short fades to black or cuts between sets; no wipes, no 3D spins.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Music**: chamber-fantasy or cinematic colours (harp, celesta, strings, horns, low choir, timpani) or small folk instruments (lute, recorder, hand drum). Either one piece whose own arc matches the story, **cut inside it** at downbeats, or an original score; a downbeat can land on the frame of a visual event.\n- **Silence as a tool**: a hard stop into a reverb tail, a pad alone, or one heartbeat when the light nearly dies; a short breath (< 1 s) when something fails.\n- **Foley follows the diorama's matter**: footsteps per surface (wood, dirt, stone, snow), fire crackle, cloth, a UI chime for dialogue boxes and menus, small bells for things switching on, thunder after each flash, a deep boom with shimmer for a big ignition; ambience beds per set, crossfaded.\n- **Voice**: storybook narrator, slow (speed ~.82–.90); on-screen characters low-passed (~7 kHz) so they sit \"in the world\". Put invented names in the TTS `asr` field.\n- **Mix**: lines compressed and RMS-matched; per-line auto-duck ~10 dB (down only); −14 LUFS via `core/render/mux.sh`, grain 0.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Shadows jump when a light is lit.** A practical turns on next to sprites and their shadows swing across the floor. *Fits content like:* a shop opening at dawn; a server rack powering up; a teacher switching on a projector.\n- **The carried light as a meter.** One small light a character carries is key light and gauge at once: its brightness rises and falls with whatever the story is measuring. *Fits content like:* a phone battery on a long trip; a patient's heart monitor; a candle vigil.\n- **Tilt-shift preciousness.** An extra-wide with the band on one tiny subject. *Fits content like:* a single delivery van in a city; a newborn's room in a whole hospital; one stall in a night market.\n- **Lights popping on one by one.** Windows, lamps or boats light up at a steady cadence across the map. *Fits content like:* users joining a network; a village getting electricity; a festival beginning.\n- **RPG grammar.** Chapter card, named character in a dialogue box, biomes, a landmark, a title card. *Fits content like:* onboarding a new employee; a history in eras; a recipe in stages.\n- **Weather passes over the diorama.** Rain, snow or lightning sweeps the tabletop with the flash curve. *Fits content like:* a market crash; a harvest threatened; a hard week at work.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **Checkerboard everywhere** → the dither band is too wide (≈ .22 on sprites, ≈ .45 on textures).\n- **A light burns out the sprite that holds it** → move the light ~0.45 m toward the camera; cap sprite lighting in the shader (`min(outgoingLight, diffuse * 1.35 + emissive)`).\n- **Lights behind a billboard don't light it** (front normal only) → always the front fill.\n- **Cylinder beams facing the camera give NaN** that bloom spreads into black blocks → billboard beam cards that fade toward the lens, glow sprite takes over.\n- **Dark scenes unreadable** → lift environment mid-tones ~×2–2.5 outside the one beat that must go dark.\n- **Distant glows hidden by fog** → `depthTest = false`, higher `renderOrder`.\n- **Tilt-shift with no character to track** → fixed band height per shot. **Tilt as a separate blur pass** breaks occlusion → fold it into the DOF's signed CoC.\n- **Node render scripts may not exit** after `done` (server keep-alive) → `process.exit(0)`; the file is complete once `done` prints.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `px.js` (pixel canvas, Bayer ramps, surfaces, lit billboards, glow), `kit.js` (world UVs, materials, houses, props, pixel fire), `fx.js` (sky, pixel sea, particles, rain, beam cards, `flick`), `chars.js` (pose-table pixel characters, `makeChar`), `post_ts.js` (`makePost`: DOF + tilt-shift + bloom + grade), `ui.js` (chapter card, dialogue box, subtitles, title), `main.js` (page contract, `?tilt=1`). Module table, parameters and a minimal new set (a lantern-lit alley): [DEMO.md § Engine reference](DEMO.md#engine-reference).\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the places, the characters, the light the story turns on, the chapters, the opening, the ending, the camera path, the key temperature and the music. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a day-cycle map** (one set, dawn to night, each hour lit by different practicals as the town's routine unfolds); **a party of three** (three short chapters, each character's biome and light, converging in one room); **a menu of places** (a top-down overworld; the camera dives into one location per topic point and pulls back to the map between).\n- Openings: **in the middle of the room** (a dialogue box already typing over a close 3/4 shot); **overhead map** (straight top-down, the band as a strip, then tilt down into the 3/4 view); **daylight** (a bright noon wide with no practicals at all, so the first lamp later is an event).\n- Endings: **a save point** (the hero rests at a small glowing object, the UI shows a save line, fade); **the band narrows** (the sharp strip thins onto one sprite until everything else is blur); **the party leaves frame** (a locked wide holds after the characters walk out; only ambience and flicker remain).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nThe Lampbearer\n"},{"id":"S4-L51","name":"微游戏快闪","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L51.jpg","markdown":"# L51 微游戏快闪（Microgame Frenzy）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 游戏\n- 长什么样：见习宇航员闯五秒训练营，越来越快，Boss 关亲手降落回地球。\n- 适合：快节奏，一连串小场景\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Microgame Frenzy — Style Prompt\n\n> A rapid-fire party-game film: one command word, a few seconds, one action. Every microgame is drawn in a completely different art style, and the film keeps getting faster.\n> Genre reference (grammar only): the WarioWare series. Never use its name, characters, UI, microgames or music.\n\nThe format suits short films; once the frenzy stops building, end it.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n- **A microgame = a shouted command word + 2.5–4 s + one action + one result** (success, or a funny failure).\n- **Each microgame uses a different visual medium** (crayon, ink wash, terminal, risograph, pixel, blueprint, Swiss type, clay, cut paper, woodcut…). The jolt of each switch is both the joke and the beat.\n- **One fixed home look** holds the film together: a show stage with a host, a lives counter and a stage number, which the film returns to between games.\n- **It keeps accelerating**: every few games tempo, game length, dwell, command hold and animation frame rate step up together.\n- **The hero survives every medium** by keeping two or three silhouette marks.\n\nNot Game Show Flat (one art style, rounds of bars, call-and-response), not a style sampler reel (every game has stakes; lives connect them), not a montage (each game has a rule grasped in under a second).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Home stage**: flat toon, thick near-black outline (~7 px), flat fills, **one hard-edged shadow** (fill in the shade colour, clip to the shape, refill shifted up-left). Fixed furniture: a frame-within-the-frame where games play (a TV, an arcade cabinet, a phone, a window), the lives board, a stage badge, a trigger prop (button, lever, bell).\n- **Each medium is a simplified re-drawing, 100–200 lines of code**, built around one channel trick that makes it read at a glance:\n  - drawn media store **pressure or wetness in a channel** (crayon: alpha = pressure against paper tooth; ink: wet and dry ink channels);\n  - print media store **plate densities** (riso: a channel per plate, additive overprint, halftone, misregistration);\n  - screen media are **low-res buffers** scaled up (pixel: indexed buffer, auto outline, dither; terminal: hand-authored glyph art, phosphor shader);\n  - technical media are **line systems** (blueprint: line weights and dimensions; Swiss: a strict grid, one sans, one signal colour).\n- **Hero translation**: the hero is redrawn from scratch in each medium, but the same marks always survive (e.g. a hat shape, one coloured dot, a number on the chest). Make a model sheet of the hero in every medium before animating.\n- **Mixed-media frames**: alpha-coverage media over a base, paper media multiplied, screen media flat and clipped to a panel. WebGL2 material shaders turn the channel buffers into each look.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **The home stage owns a saturated party palette**: a deep ground behind a rotating sunburst, two or three loud accents, plus a fixed success colour and a fixed fail colour used by stamps and the lives board only.\n- **Each medium brings its own palette** from its tradition (wax colours, one-ink-plus-vermilion, amber phosphor, three riso plates, a limited pixel palette, white-on-blue, black-white-red). Never tint a medium with the home palette.\n- **One accent follows the hero across media** where the medium allows (a single vermilion dot in ink, an inverse glyph in a terminal, a signal-red circle in Swiss type): it is how the eye finds the hero after a switch.\n- Example home palettes: violet `#2a0f5c` with magenta `#ff2e88`, gold `#ffc928`, cyan `#1fd1d1`; teal ground with coral and lemon; black stage with hot orange and electric blue.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **The command word is the subtitle**: a heavy display face (e.g. Titan One), white with a thick ink outline, a coloured drop and a burst behind it, skewed a few degrees. It slams in over ~3 frames (overshoot → settle), holds, then **shrinks into a corner tag before the first action**, so the result is never covered. One-off banners fly out instead of tagging, so tags never pile up.\n- **Other lines**: a rounded card in the home palette with the home outline and hard shadow, a friendly rounded face (e.g. Lilita One ~50 px), a small speaker icon; placed above the timer in games. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s), never under a big banner.\n- Inside a game, any text (replay captions, labels) uses **that medium's own typography**.\n- The command word appears in the .srt only, never burned twice.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **The frame rate is part of the ladder**: characters step on twos or at 8 fps early, faster later, on ones at the climax. Camera moves, transitions and the timer spark always run on ones.\n- **Five things accelerate together** at each speed-up: tempo, game length, stage dwell, command hold, character frame rate.\n- **The timer is visible and on the beat**: e.g. a fuse with one knot per beat, the spark passing each knot exactly on it. It freezes in a replay.\n- **Unified transition through the frame-within-the-frame**: push into the screen to start a game (~0.25 s), shrink the game back into it to return (~0.33 s) with a ✓ / ✗ stamp. No fades, no blank frames.\n- **Failure is performed**: the action overshoots, splats, bonks or rolls off; the medium's own physics makes the joke.\n- **Instant replay** for a failure that must be understood: freeze, half speed, push-in, letterbox, a caption in the medium's type.\n- The home host has a tick on every beat; stopping it dead is the strongest emotional tool.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Start inside a game, command already slamming | the rules explain themselves | throwing the viewer in |\n| Pull back out of the game screen | scale: this was one screen on a show | the first return to the stage |\n| Locked frontal home frame | the constant; only the scoreboard changes | stage beats; the host's reaction |\n| Slot reels (the stage splits into spinning strips of earlier frames, stopping one per beat) | acceleration, a summary | a speed-up; a change of round |\n| Extreme insert on the trigger prop | the start, felt | a game starting on the downbeat |\n| Lights cut to one top spot, frame unchanged | stakes; the last chance | the last life; a decision |\n| Tile flip (last frames of past games flip into pieces of a new frame) | the past becomes the material of the present | a boss or finale; a recap |\n| Push into a detail, hold | the viewer notices before the hero does | a clue; a silence |\n| Snap back to wide on one beat | release | a solution blooming; a save |\n| Split screen, two media side by side | comparison, rivalry | two players; before/after; two approaches |\n\nFraming: the game screen is a fixed fraction of the frame (around 40 % of the width); in a mixed-media frame keep **one focal chain** (A leads to B leads to C) and push info panels into a corner.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Music first**: a tempo grid and a cue table before any animation; every picture hit lands on a listed cue.\n- **A constant groove + one colour instrument per medium.** The groove (kit with ghost notes, slap or synth bass, brass stabs, keys) runs throughout; each medium adds its own timbre: toy piano for crayon, shakuhachi or guqin for ink, square beeps for terminals, vibes for print, pulse arpeggios for pixel, a typewriter for drafting, claves for Swiss, kalimba for clay, koto for woodcut.\n- **Foley follows the medium**: wax squeaks, wet splats, teletype relays, paper crunch, bit-crushed noise, ratchets and stamps, hollow bonks. Home stage: a spring trigger click, glass crack for a lost life, card flips, a reel whirr, sparse claps.\n- **Acceleration options** (use what fits): a rising brass run into each speed-up; the key rising a step per round; halving note values instead of changing tempo; dropping the groove to a heartbeat on the last life.\n- **Real silences**: at least one before the biggest hit (a heartbeat or one pitched note may remain). The loudest moment comes right after the longest silence.\n- **Voices**: a host who shouts every command a hair *after* the stab so brass doesn't mask it; a hero with very few words (reactions work best). Duck music ~11 dB and foley ~6 dB under voice. −14 LUFS.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Style switch per game.** Each task is drawn in the medium that suits it. *Fits content like:* the steps of baking bread (flour in crayon, oven dial in blueprint, crust in woodcut); a day of a nurse; a heist's roles.\n- **Command word.** One verb is script, subtitle and downbeat. *Fits content like:* SCAN! at a checkout; BREATHE! in a yoga class; SIGN! at a contract signing.\n- **Lives as UI.** The counter carries emotion without narration; 3 → 2 → 1 (blinking) builds tension alone. *Fits content like:* a phone battery; retries on a driving test; chances left on a quiz.\n- **SPEED UP.** Everything steps up together. *Fits content like:* rush hour; the last week before a deadline; a kitchen during dinner service.\n- **Failure becomes the solution.** The material of an earlier failure returns in the final game as the answer. *Fits content like:* a spilled coffee that later stains the winning map; a bug that becomes a feature; a missed train that leads to the right meeting.\n- **Boss mixes the rules.** The final game is built from pieces of earlier media. *Fits content like:* a final exam; launch day; a festival's closing night.\n- **Instant replay.** The joke explained in slow motion. *Fits content like:* a goal-line decision; a typo that shipped; a cat's leap that missed.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **A luminance-ramp ASCII figure is mush** → hand-authored glyph art with structural glyphs, an inverse glyph for the accent.\n- **Ink shapes read as smoke** unless their silhouette is designed (a short round blot phase, then a clear structure with seams and light edges).\n- **A failure at high tempo goes unread** → make the action overshoot and hit something, or give it an instant replay.\n- **Alpha-coverage media show the background through gaps** → a full-pressure base where it must be solid.\n- **Drawing helpers with a global context** draw on the wrong canvas when scenes render into offscreen buffers (game screen, reels, tiles) → set and restore the context.\n- **Command tags pile up** → secondary commands fly out.\n- **A 100 % white flash** reads as a blank frame → cap at ~60 %.\n- **TTS single-word commands** gain a vowel tail (\"Pump!\" → \"Pompey\") → generate a two-word phrase and cut after the first word using ASR timestamps; verify each clip, since ASR mishears 0.2 s words in a dense mix.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\n`demo/timeline.js` (tempo grid and segments, the single source of truth), `demo/toon.js` + `demo/stage.js` + `demo/hud.js` (home look, stage, timer, command word, stamps, lives, subtitle cards; call `setCtx` when drawing offscreen), `demo/chars.js` (host and hero rigs), `demo/glpass.js` (WebGL2 material shaders: riso, crayon, CRT, ink, blueprint paper), one `demo/g_<medium>.js` per medium (`sceneX(g, lt)` + the hero translation), `demo/film.js` (transitions through the TV, slot reels, tiles, events). Module map and build: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md#build-notes).\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the tasks, which medium draws each, the home stage and its frame-within-the-frame, the host and hero, the number of rounds, where failures fall, the finale, the opening and the ending. The command → action → result unit, the medium switch and the acceleration stay.\n\nAll far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **two players, alternating** (each game is played by one of two rivals; the lives board is a tug-of-war); **one day, hour by hour** (each game is an hour, the speed-ups are the morning rush and the evening rush, the finale is midnight); **a relay** (the result of each game is the object handed into the next one, so the chain is the story).\n- Openings: **the home stage asleep** (lights off, the host wakes on the first beat); **a game-over screen** we rewind from; **the lives board alone**, a counter clicking down before we know what it counts.\n- Endings: **a high-score table** where the hero types their name letter by letter in each medium; **the credits as one more microgame** (CATCH THE NAMES!); **the home stage packs away** into the frame-within-the-frame and the screen switches off.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nFive-Second Astronaut\n"},{"id":"S4-L52","name":"综艺节奏扁平","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L52.jpg","markdown":"# L52 综艺节奏扁平（Game Show Flat）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 游戏\n- 长什么样：把 AI 发展史做成一局节奏游戏，模型踩着拍登场，最后发成绩单。\n- 适合：综艺感\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Game Show Flat — Style Prompt\n\n> A rhythm-game variety show in flat vector: toy-like mascots with thick ink outlines on candy-coloured stripes and sunbursts, every hit landing on a fixed tempo grid, call-and-response patterns, and a judgement word when each round is won.\n> References (grammar only): rhythm-action party games in the Rhythm Heaven mould (cue → answer, round cards, judgement words), Japanese variety-show telops (thick outlined captions, stripe and sunburst sets), WarioWare-style stage cards. Never use their names, characters, UI, levels or music.\n\nThe length follows the number of rounds: each round is a title card plus its bars of play. Narration is optional: the style can carry a film on short shouts and telops alone.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n- **Everything is on the grid.** One fixed, fast tempo. Every pop, drop, stamp, typed character and camera punch sits at a (bar, beat) and is also a sound event; the score is generated from the picture's own timeline, so they can't drift.\n- **One drawing kit, many sets.** Flat fills, one heavy ink outline, round caps, hard offset shadows; each round gets a new saturated background colour and pattern.\n- **Characters are toys.** One body construction for the whole cast, told apart by colour, a belly patch, one head accessory and a name tag. They hop on the beat, squash on hits, throw arms up on a shout.\n- **A round = a repeatable verb + a win.** The topic is split into rounds; each round has one action that can repeat on a beat, and ends on a judgement the audience reads in one glance.\n\nNot a microgame frenzy (no timer or one-verb command per 3 s game, no change of art style per round), not a mid-century cartoon (no textured painted backgrounds, no character acting), not a keynote (no slow reveals, no gradients).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Vector only**, SVG or canvas paths at 1920×1080: flat fills, one outline colour (near-black), 8 px outlines on characters and props, 5–7 px on small parts, round joins and caps.\n- **No gradients, no blur, no texture, no soft shadow.** Depth is a **hard offset shadow**: the same shape in outline colour shifted ~10 px (stickers, banners) to ~14 px (cards), then the coloured shape on top.\n- **Sets** = one saturated background + a same-hue pattern one step lighter: diagonal stripes (often scrolling slowly), offset polka dots (~20 % white), or a rotating sunburst. A darker floor band with an ink line on top grounds the characters.\n- **Characters**: a rounded bean body, stubby arms with mitten hands, oval eyes with a catch-light, a mouth that swaps to an open one. Humans share the construction; older machines or eras read through a boxier silhouette.\n- **UI kit**: round card, corner sticker banner, judgement word, burst (star + ring + speed lines), scoreboards.\n- `render(t)` is a pure function of `t`: every element's state is recomputed from `t`, never carried over from the previous frame.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **Ink + paper + candy.** Near-black ink, white, and a warm cream for cards and paper props; around eight saturated candy hues (yellow, orange, red, pink, green, lime, sky, blue) plus two or three deep hues with darker partners for night or finale sets.\n- **One set = one hue.** The background, its pattern and the round card share a hue; the card changes colour to match the set it introduces. Neighbouring rounds never share a hue.\n- **Characters own colours.** A contestant's body colour is its identity and never changes; keep it distinct from the set it stands on (a character is never the same hue as the background).\n- Big words are white or yellow with a thick ink stroke; judgement words may be pink or yellow.\n- Set examples: tangerine ground `#FFB870` with `#FFA85A` stripes; teal with mint dots; navy with a gold sunburst.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Display**: a heavy rounded sans for Latin and numbers (e.g. Fredoka 700); a black-weight sans for CJK (e.g. Noto Sans SC 900); a hand-lettered face only for handwritten props (e.g. ZCOOL KuaiLe).\n- **Telop text**: white or yellow fill with an ink stroke ≈ 14 % of the font size painted *under* the fill (`paint-order: stroke`). Titles are built glyph by glyph so each character can pop in.\n- **The telops are the subtitles.** Round cards, corner banners, labels, prop text and judgement words carry the meaning; keep them short (≤ ~20 CJK characters on a banner line, ≤ ~12 on a plate; Latin runs ~1.6× wider). With narration, subtitles are telop plates at the bottom in the same outlined type, never a thin generic caption.\n- Hold every text ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s); a judgement word may be shorter because it is read at a glance.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Idle = hop on the beat**: a parabola per beat (8–30 px); neighbours hop on the off-beat so a crowd ripples.\n- **Hit = squash**: a damped wobble (~30 rad/s, ~0.35 s) after each hit; a small squash on every downbeat.\n- **Entrances drop from the sky** with ease-in and land exactly on their beat, so the sound lands with them. Crowds drop in one per beat or half-beat.\n- **Pops use back-ease** for anything appearing (cards, stickers, stamps, words); ease-in for exits. Nothing fades in slowly: things are either on or popping.\n- **Shout pose**: arms up + open mouth for about half a beat.\n- **Typewriter** reveals on fractions of a beat, with a click every few characters.\n- Scene changes are **hard cuts on bar lines**, with a very short white flash. A fade is saved for the very end, if at all.\n- Nothing moves off the grid: an event between beats sits on a subdivision.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nThe stage is a frontal, symmetrical proscenium; the frame itself does the moving. A vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Beat punch (zoom 0.8–5 % around centre, decays in ~0.16 s) | the hit, felt in the frame | any accent; a slam; a count-in |\n| Several punches in one bar | the frame breathing with the music | a chorus; an escalating pattern |\n| Locked frontal wide | the whole stage as a game board | a pattern being set up; a crowd; a scoreboard |\n| Hard cut on the bar line + flash | a new round, a new set | chapter changes; a before/after |\n| Split stage (two halves, two sets) | two sides answering each other | a rivalry; human vs machine; old vs new |\n| Shrink to thumbnail (a finished bit scales down into a slot) | memory, accumulation | a recap; \"and then all of this happened\" |\n| Vertical scroll of the set (floor to sky) | rising stakes or scale | a leaderboard; growth; a tower of items |\n| Snap zoom onto one prop (hard cut to a 2× framing, back on the next bar) | this detail is the joke | a price tag; a number; a face reacting |\n\nKeep the sum of simultaneous punches below ~0.06 so the frame edge never shows. Characters stand on the floor band; banners top-left, judgement words centre-top. Hard cuts only; no dissolves.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **All synthesized** from the picture's events: a sine kick with soft saturation, band-passed noise snare and clap, hats, slap bass, detuned-saw brass stabs with a closing filter, square lead with vibrato, marimba-like pluck, music-box bell, triangle pad, square arp, cowbell.\n- **A bright major pop harmony** on a short chord loop, one chord per bar; each round gets its own flavour (funk, quiz, electro, dream, heavy…), not a new song.\n- **Event foley is toy-like and pitched**: pops, plop + boing landings, stamps, typewriter clicks, buzzers, dings, servo whirs, splats; pitched pips can spell a melody as a crowd drops in.\n- **Shouts, not sentences**: one- to three-word calls (\"Hey!\", a number, a verdict), placed 20–30 ms before the beat so the consonant lands on it. A crowd shout = several different voices stacked a few ms apart and panned across the stereo field.\n- Options to vary intensity (pick what the film needs): a jingle (crash + rising brass + snare roll) into each round; a transposition up for a finale; dropping to drums only for one bar before a big hit; a half-time bar for a reveal; one bar of silence broken by a single shout.\n- **Mix**: voices and foley on their own bus; music ducked from that bus's envelope; soft-clip rather than hard limit; −14 LUFS, true peak ≤ −1 dB.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Rounds with a card.** A 1-bar card (round number + name + a tag such as a year or a step) flies in on a jingle, then play. *Fits content like:* the steps of a recipe; the stages of a startup; the chapters of a museum tour.\n- **Report card.** A typewritten recap with a grade stamp. *Fits content like:* a quarterly review; a trip summary; a final score.\n- **Call-and-response.** Anything with a turn becomes a rhythm pattern; speeding up the pattern (quarters → eighths) *is* the plot. *Fits content like:* a teacher's question and the class answer; a customer order and the barista's reply; ping-pong between two negotiators.\n- **Next-level tease.** Something hatches or unlocks. *Fits content like:* a next version; next season; a next grade.\n- **Judgement word.** A big outlined word pops at an angle on the round's last hit. *Fits content like:* \"Delivered!\" on a logistics step; \"Approved!\" on a permit; \"Personal best!\" on a training plan.\n- **Remix board.** One set with a timeline ribbon; each item gets a few bars centre stage, then shrinks into a slot along the bottom; at a climax all slots bounce together. *Fits content like:* a year in review; a team's release list; a festival line-up.\n- **Stats as game UI.** Numbers become podium scores, counters racing to a target, price tags getting hammered, bar charts that grow per beat. *Fits content like:* fundraising progress; calories per dish; downloads of an app.\n- **Crowd drop.** Many contestants drop in one per beat, each with a pitched pip. *Fits content like:* every member of a club; the planets; the ingredients of a dish.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **Stateful updates break parallel renders**: a worker starting cold shows the initial state. Recompute every pose from `t`; compare single stills with a sequential render.\n- **Cue lists drift** when cut flashes, jingles and arrangement are separate hand lists: derive them from one table.\n- **Fixed-length audio buffers** cut the tail: size them from the duration.\n- **Text measured before fonts load** overflows plates: load fonts, then build.\n- **TTS misreads digits and symbols** in shouts: spell them out.\n- **Real products as characters**: names on tags, original mascots, no logos or UI; real people only as generic stand-ins.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\n`demo/frame_head.html` (SVG helpers: `el`, `txt`, `tf`, `op`, easing `E`, per-glyph `chars`/`charsPop`, `measure`), `demo/main_v1.js` (tempo grid `at(bar, beat)`, `ev()` sound events, `punch()`, `hopY`/`hitSq`, `gtext` telops, `stripes`/`dots`/`raysEl` sets, `burst`, `judge`, `banner`), `demo/main_b.js` (`bean()` characters + `pose()`, `human()`, `scene(t0, t1, build)`, `card()`, `dropY`, `heyAt`), `demo/music.py` + `demo/synth_lib.py` (event-driven score and SFX). The contract: `scene()` builds nodes once and returns `update(t)`, which only sets attributes. Module table and a minimal new round: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md#engine-reference).\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the rounds and their verbs, the cast, the sets and colours, whether there is narration, the tempo, the opening, the ending and the length. The grid, the drawing kit and the telop language stay.\n\nAll far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a tournament bracket** (two contestants per round, the winner advances, the final is a split stage); **one long round** that keeps escalating its single pattern from quarters to sixteenths as the story's pressure rises; **a quiz show** with three contestants where each question is a fact of the topic and the scoreboard is the argument.\n- Openings: **the judgement first** (a \"Perfect!\" pops on an empty stage, then we rewind to how it was earned); **a single contestant alone** hopping on a bare floor until the crowd drops in; **the scoreboard** showing a final number, then counting back to zero.\n- Endings: **a group photo** (everyone freezes mid-hop, a flash, the frame becomes a polaroid on a card); **a level-select map** where every finished round is a lit node; **a curtain** falling bar by bar with the last shout under it.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nRhythm of AI, 1997 → 2026\n"},{"id":"S4-L53","name":"1920s 默片","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L53.jpg","markdown":"# L53 1920s 默片（1920s Silent Film）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 电影与时代\n- 长什么样：面包店学徒追一个滚走的面包，最后掰成两半分给饿肚子的小女孩。\n- 适合：字幕卡、怀旧\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# 1920s Silent Film — Style Prompt\n\n> An engraved-illustration silent film projected in a picture house: ink line and silver wash, a 4:3 print that flickers and weaves between velvet curtains, intertitles instead of voices, and a cinema pianist who plays every beat.\n> References (grammar only): **Buster Keaton**, *The General* (1926) and *Sherlock Jr.* (1924) — the whole gag in one locked-off wide shot, a deadpan face, a body that does the acting; **Harold Lloyd**, *Safety Last!* (1923) — the near-miss; **Georges Méliès** — the iris and the trick shot; **The Artist** (2011) — how few intertitles a modern audience needs; **1920s photoplay piano cue sheets** — music by mood section, the piano doing the sound effects. Never use the Tramp look (bowler, toothbrush moustache, cane), any real film's gags, or any published melody.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nA one-reel film as it would look *projected*:\n- **A 4:3 print in a gate between theatre curtains**; the sides of the 16:9 frame are the picture house.\n- **An ink drawing with washed greys and hatching**, printed on silver stock that flickers, weaves, scratches and burns at the edges.\n- **Nobody speaks.** Beats are told by **action in wide shots** and by **intertitle cards**.\n- **The only sound is a cinema instrument** (piano, organ) and the projector in the booth.\n\nNot a noir (no hard shadows as mood, no voice-over), not a sepia photo filter on modern animation (the drawing itself is engraved and inked), not a Chaplin pastiche.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Tonal painting + Redraw.** Scenes are painted in grey values only (print densities roughly: ink 0.07, dark cloth 0.15–0.3, mid wood 0.45–0.6, skin 0.8, white linen 0.9–0.95), with soft light gradients and a thin separation line. A **Redraw** pass (WebGL2) inks the whole frame: difference-of-Gaussians contours on the dark side of every edge, luminance posterised into ~5 soft wash steps, and 45° hatching (then crossed) in the darks, re-drawn every two frames like hand-redrawn animation. Real video frames can go through the same pass so footage and drawing share one hand.\n- **Print pass**: silver tone with a sepia amount between ~0.1 and ~0.36, print black ~0.055 and white ~0.93, S-curve, halation on highlights, uneven density, flicker, gate weave with occasional perforation jumps, broken vertical scratches (white = scraped emulsion, dark = dirt on the negative), brown burn creeping from the edges, vignette, two-octave silver grain; then dust, a hair in the gate, a rare blotch.\n- **Frame**: 1440×1080 in the centre of 1920×1080, a rounded aperture with a soft dark rim; velvet drapes at the sides lit by the screen's spill and by footlights. Curtains can part and close.\n- **Figures**: realistic proportions (7.5-head adults, ~5-head children) on a 2.5D rig (joints solved in 3D, projected with a yaw), silent-era make-up (pale skin, dark lids and lips); identity by silhouette marks (a hat, a garment, a tool).\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **Monochrome silver**, tinted only by the print: colder and harsher (more damage, less sepia) when things go wrong, warmer and cleaner when the film turns tender. Tint and damage are mood controls, set per section.\n- Values carry everything: dark clothes against pale walls, pale faces against dark interiors; check every shot as a thumbnail.\n- **One colour, at most**: an element drawn into a separate colour mask keeps its hue through the print (still getting grain, flicker, vignette). Use it for the single thing the film is about, or not at all.\n- Period tinting of whole scenes (amber for day interiors, blue for night, rose for romance) is a valid alternative to sepia; one tint per section.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **The intertitles are the subtitles**; the `.srt` lists their text. A card holds 2.5–3.75 s (≥ max(1.8 s, reading time + 0.6 s)).\n- A black painted card with a deco border whose ornament follows the emotion: one heavy rule for a shout (the text shakes), a double rule with stepped corners and fans for narration, vines and small flowers for tenderness.\n- Period faces (OFL): a small-caps display serif (e.g. Playfair Display SC) for titles and shouts, an italic old-style serif (e.g. Old Standard TT Italic) for narration and dialogue.\n- A few words per card; three to five cards in a short film. A card never explains what the picture already shows.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Hand-cranked sampling**: character and camera motion sampled at 16 fps (18 or more to undercrank a fast scene); flicker, weave, grain and scratches change every output frame (24 fps). That mix makes it look projected rather than merely low-frame-rate.\n- **Speed is an emotion**: undercrank chases and panic (faster motion, faster flicker); return to 16 fps and a calmer print for tender scenes.\n- **Deadpan performance**: the face barely changes; the body acts (crouch before effort, held poses, the two-frame snap of a double take, hands on knees). Objects act too (a wobble before a fall, a ritardando to rest).\n- **Keyframed, eased poses**; only deliberate gags snap.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. The period camera is heavy: most shots are locked. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Locked-off extreme wide | cause and effect seen whole; the wait is the joke | a mechanical gag; a crowd; an elaborate process |\n| Locked full shot | the premise in one frame | a character's routine; a confrontation |\n| Tracking shot with foreground passing | pursuit; momentum | a chase; a race; a delivery against the clock |\n| Iris in / out | attention on one thing; a beginning or end of a thought | an object; a face; a memory |\n| Half-open iris, held | a character sharing a look with the audience | a wink; a secret; complicity |\n| Slow motivated push-in | tenderness; realisation | a kneel; a letter read; a decision |\n| Masked shot (keyhole, binoculars) | point of view; spying | a detective; curiosity; romance |\n| Split screen / double exposure | trickery; dream; two places at once | a phone call; a ghost; a daydream |\n\nFraming: wide and medium shots; big close-ups of rigged faces look like mannequins, so keep any close-up small or inside an iris. Transitions: iris, fade to black, straight cuts, the curtain. No dissolves between shots of the same scene, no modern whip pans.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **The instrument is the sound design**, as in a picture house: an upright piano (optionally a reed organ, theatre organ, violin or small ensemble) plays every event. Mickey-mousing vocabulary (options): chromatic creep in the bass (sneaking), grace note (a wink), forearm cluster (a crash), swelling diminished tremolo (a wait), low rumbling tremolo (a storm, a machine), \"plink … plonk\" (emptiness), diminished sforzando (a shock), rolled chord (a door, a reveal), descending glissando (a fall), low \"boing\" (a bounce), nervous trill (hesitation), minor second (something breaking).\n- **Photoplay idiom**: stride left hand, music by mood section in the manner of cue sheets (Hurry, Misterioso, Agitato, Tenderly, Maestoso), a theme that returns changed. Original music only.\n- **No foley, no voice.** **The projector is the ambience**: lamp click, motor spin-up, claw-and-motor purr, film flapping on the take-up reel.\n- **Silence**: the music stops and the projector alone remains; it frames a held moment.\n- **Mix**: tame piano hammer transients with light compression before loudness normalisation (−14 LUFS). Grain goes in the picture (mux grain 1).\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **The chain in one take.** A triggers B, B leaves the frame, the audience waits, B returns and hits C. *Fits content like:* a supply chain gone wrong; how a rumour spreads; a Rube Goldberg invention.\n- **The near-miss.** A hand closes exactly where the object was a frame ago. *Fits content like:* catching a bus; a startup missing a deal; a goalkeeper.\n- **The intertitle twist.** A card reframes the next shot in a few words. *Fits content like:* \"Meanwhile, in the lab…\"; \"The machine had other ideas.\"; a statistic as a punchline.\n- **Undercranked chaos.** Speed and damage rise together. *Fits content like:* rush hour; a kitchen at dinner service; a stock crash.\n- **Iris on the look.** An iris stops half-way so a character can glance at the audience. *Fits content like:* a product winking; a cat that knows; a scientist's secret.\n- **Redrawn footage.** Real video pushed through the ink pass becomes a period drawing. *Fits content like:* a company's archive; a family film; a sports clip.\n- **The echo gesture.** A gesture from the start repeated by someone else with a new meaning. *Fits content like:* a teacher and a pupil; a handover; a tradition.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **Too much content kills a silent film**: a many-step chain at small figure scale is noise in a thumbnail. One legible chain with a pause in the middle beats three busy ones.\n- **Puppet arms**: apply abduction before flexion, let the shoulder rise and swing forward as the arm goes up, slim the torso's shoulder corner on the raised side, hide the arm's contour where it lies over the torso. Raise arms sideways (or turn the figure) so they don't cross the face.\n- **Pose pops**: drive every figure by eased keyframe lists, never by `if` branches.\n- **Floating props**: hold a prop between the palms at the depth between the arms; tie a rolling object's rotation to distance (angle = distance / radius).\n- Brow angles flip with facing direction: check expressions in a crop at final size.\n- An iris must track its target in film coordinates after any camera zoom.\n- A grab must put the hand exactly where the object is at the beat, or it doesn't read.\n- Closed curtains lit only by a dark screen go pure black: add footlights.\n- Heavy grain makes huge files → re-encode with `-tune grain` at a higher CRF.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/engine/`: `ink.js` (tonal forms, ink line, wash, hatch; `drawShape()` draws any path in this style), `redraw.js` (`Redraw`: tonal painting or real footage → ink drawing, WebGL2), `film.js` (`FilmPost` print pass + `damage()`, stackable on any 2D canvas, with an optional colour mask), `cards.js` (intertitles, deco borders, iris, theatre), `figure.js` + `heads.js` (2.5D rig). `demo/timeline.js` is a cue sheet that drives both picture and score. API table and a minimal example (a one-colour shape through the whole pipeline): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md) \"Engine reference\".\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the genre (comedy, melodrama, adventure, newsreel), the characters, the gag or the drama, the cards, the tint plan, the opening and the ending. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a newsreel** (a sequence of \"reports\" with headline cards and a narrator card between them); **a melodrama in three reels** (each reel a tint: amber, blue, rose; a villain card, a rescue); **a trick film** (Méliès-style: one stage, a magician, objects appearing and transforming by substitution cuts).\n- Openings: **mid-chase**, the film already running and scratched, no title yet; **a single intertitle question** before any image; **the projectionist's view**: the beam from the booth, then the screen.\n- Endings: **the film breaks** (the print burns through and the lamp shows white); **a final card that addresses the audience** directly; **fade to black on a locked wide** with the piano holding one chord.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nThe Runaway Loaf\n"},{"id":"S4-L54","name":"后室 · 新怪谈","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L54.jpg","markdown":"# L54 后室 / 新怪谈（Liminal Found Footage）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 电影与时代\n- 长什么样：新夜班员工拍下入职第一晚：无尽的黄色办公空间，和墙上的员工守则。\n- 适合：不安、对未知的害怕\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Liminal Found Footage — Style Prompt\n\n> Camcorder tapes recovered from empty, endless, fluorescent-lit spaces. Rules instead of monsters.\n> Genre reference (grammar only): \"the Backrooms\" internet folklore and new-weird / rule-horror; 1990s consumer camcorder footage. **Never** use the word \"Backrooms\", wiki \"Level\" numbers, community entity names, or any existing organisation or logo inside the film. Everything on screen (rules, forms, company voice, any presence) is original.\n\nSuits short films; the length follows the walk, and the dread thins out once nothing new goes wrong.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\n- **One first-person camcorder recording** (1990s VHS, 4:3), handheld, continuous.\n- **An empty, familiar-but-wrong interior** that repeats: an office, a hotel floor, a school, a mall, a parking level, a hospital wing. Humming fluorescent light, no people.\n- **Dread from emptiness, repetition, bureaucratic rules, and the camera's own machinery failing at the wrong moment.** Nothing chases anyone. A chill on the back of the neck, not a jump.\n- **The medium is the storyteller**: focus, exposure, tape damage and the on-screen display carry the plot.\n\nNot a horror film (no monster close-ups, no gore, no jump scares, no score stings), not an ASCII/CRT terminal piece (the artefacts are tape and lens, not phosphor and scanlines), not a ruin (clean is part of the style).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Frame**: 4:3 pillarboxed in 1920×1080. Render 3D at low resolution (e.g. 720×540) and let the VHS pass upscale; low resolution hides CG cleanliness.\n- **Space**: axis-aligned partitions on a grid with random gaps and stubs, pillars, a drop ceiling of tiles with recessed light panels at regular intervals. A hand-authored route through procedurally filled space, so every doorway shows more emptiness.\n- **Lighting = thousands of lights, no shadows**: store every panel's colour and intensity in a float texture; each fragment sums the nearest panels (emitter cosine × Lambert / (d² + ε)) plus a bounce term. No occlusion: this light is flat. Per-light variation (a few % warm/cool/green), a few % dead, a few % flickering. Ambient occlusion darkens seams.\n- **Fog follows local light**: fog colour × (local light / average). Where lights are dead the haze is dark, so a silhouette there reads as a *hole in the light*. Background = fog × ~0.15, so corridors dissolve into darkness, never a bright wall.\n- **Restrained decay**: tide-marks at the foot of some walls, a discoloured band under the ceiling, rare peeling seams, a few yellowed or missing tiles, dead insects in diffusers. Percentages in single digits.\n- **Printed matter**: office laser-print on yellowed paper, condensed sans headers, form numbers and revisions, numbered rules, a polite closing line from \"Management\". Pinned or taped.\n- **VHS camcorder pass** (not a CRT): small line jitter (≤ 0.25 px low-frequency wobble), a head-switching band, tracking tears on demand, barrel + vignette on the image but **not** the OSD, luma blur + edge ringing, chroma smeared right, light smear, drifting white balance, AGC noise in darks, dropouts, lifted blacks, soft clip. Noise at tape rate (30 fps). No scanlines, no RGB mask.\n- **OSD**: a hand-made bitmap font with a black outline: REC dot, battery, time and date. A camcorder title generator uses the same font, larger.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **One dominant sickly hue** from the location's materials (mustard wallpaper, institutional green, hospital blue, beige laminate), with a darker trim and a mottled floor of the same family; ceiling and light panels near-white and slightly warm.\n- **Exactly one saturated colour**: a signal such as a red alarm lamp, a green emergency sign, an amber warning light. It is the only thing that blooms (drive its emissive high enough to cross the bloom threshold).\n- White balance drifts; colour is never graded clean.\n- Example material palettes: wallpaper `#d5bd66`, trim `#4d3d26`, carpet `#8c763c`; pool tile `#9fc7c0`, grout `#e6ece6`, water `#5e8f96`; school corridor `#b9c49a`, lockers `#6f7f66`, linoleum `#a89a7d`.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Closed captions (CEA-608 style)**: a monospace (e.g. IBM Plex Mono 600) ~42 px, white on a solid black box per row, ≤ 32 characters, centred inside the 4:3 frame above the OSD. Untouched by tape noise (the TV decodes them). Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- **Speaker marks**: `[PA]` for announcements, italics for the operator, SDH descriptions in brackets (`[LIGHTS FLICKER]`, `[TAPE NOISE]`, `[SILENCE]`).\n- **Rules on notices are not captioned**: they are the picture. Readable at 1080p, ≥ 3–4 s uncovered. For close-ups **lower the camera so the lens is parallel to the page**; pitching down at a vertical sheet shears words into fake italics.\n- The **title** is camcorder title-generator text over the first shot.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **One continuous handheld take.** Cuts happen only where the tape breaks: power-on roll-in, tracking tear, REC off.\n- **Handheld** = walking gait (head bob a few cm, sway, small roll) + breathing (~0.27 Hz) + tremor noise at a few frequencies whose amplitude follows a **fear** curve. At a held breath, **freeze all of it**.\n- **Looking down** shows only shoe toes and floor, never legs.\n- **Autofocus** = focus distance animated with overshoot: blur → lock → slip → lock. Blur comes from pulling focus *near*. Zoom narrows the FOV and opens the aperture.\n- **Lights flicker** as short blackouts (~0.1 s); auto-exposure catches up with a lag (~0.45 s) and gain noise pumps.\n- Things move by themselves slowly (a door over >1 s, eased), never snap.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Walking POV down a corridor | routine, the endless | establishing the space; time passing |\n| Slow pan off a detail to the space | scale; the space goes on | a reveal; a first look |\n| Glance down at the feet | obedience, avoidance | obeying a rule; not looking |\n| Crouch and hold on printed text | reading; bureaucracy | a rule; a contradiction |\n| Zoom + focus hunt into the distance | seeing / not seeing | a presence; a doubt |\n| Identical framing after a tear, only the OSD changed | time removed | a jump; a loss |\n| Turn a corner, hold still | a threshold | an exit; a choice |\n| Slow push-in with no sound | the pull of something | a door; a sign |\n| Backing away while filming | retreat, fear | leaving a room; a rule broken |\n| Camera set down on a surface, static, recording alone | abandonment | the operator gone; a long wait |\n| Whip toward a sound, then nothing | expectation denied | a noise with no source |\n\nFraming: text readable and uncovered; any presence small, far, and in a region of dead lights. Transitions: tape events only.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **No score.** Only what the tape would have recorded. Music exists only inside the world (hold music, a radio through walls, a tinny PA jingle), filtered and reverberant, possibly slowing with tape wow.\n- **Fluorescent bed**: 120 Hz (or 100 Hz) + harmonics, a buzzy band-passed component, a faint ballast whine, bad-tube bursts; gated by the same light curve as the picture; closer in small rooms.\n- **PA voice**: calm, corporate, through a ceiling speaker (band-pass ~300–3500 Hz, cone resonance, saturation, room reverb), dry enough to stay intelligible. A short original chime before announcements; after a time jump, chime and voice may drift flat.\n- **Operator**: a few short, quiet lines with proximity bass and breath; breathing whose rate follows fear; a held breath and one long exhale.\n- **Foley by material**: carpet steps (low thud + wet squelch) or hard-floor squeaks, cloth rustles, camcorder clack and motor, zoom servo, AF ticks, ballast tink on flickers, a stick-slip hinge, a drag overhead, a vending machine, a pipe.\n- **Silence**: digital zero for a second or more (tails included), captioned `[SILENCE]`, is the strongest tool. A soft scare at most: everything restriking at once when sound returns.\n- **Drone** options: a low brown-noise band with beating sines rising slowly; the room tone itself pitch-shifting; nothing.\n- **Mix**: bed and in-world music ≈ −8 dB under voices. −14 LUFS; no added grain (the noise is in the picture).\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Autofocus / auto-exposure hunting.** Something is there only while focus is correct. *Fits content like:* a missing pet on a hotel floor; a figure in an empty stadium; a shape at the end of a hospital corridor.\n- **Tape tracking damage.** A tear is a cut the universe made; same framing after, but the clock jumped, the battery drained, a light died. *Fits content like:* a night-watch log; a lost weekend in a mall; a house that ages between tapes.\n- **The OSD is plot.** REC, battery, date and time code are evidence. *Fits content like:* an alibi; a countdown; a dated inspection.\n- **Rules as text in frame.** Notices and PA announcements start ordinary, drift wrong, contradict each other, and finally address the viewer. *Fits content like:* a pool's safety rules; a school fire drill; a museum after hours.\n- **Emptiness and repetition.** The same notice in a different place; the same room with one detail changed; a corridor into fog. *Fits content like:* a moving-day walkthrough; an airport at 4 a.m.; an office after layoffs.\n- **The thing that is almost human.** A distant silhouette wrong by 15–40 % (small head, long neck, narrow high shoulders, long arms), drawn as organic curves on a billboard, moving slowly. *Fits content like:* a mannequin that is not; a night guard; a reflection that lags.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- **A fully bright scene reads as a hospital corridor** → partitions with gaps; far areas fall dark.\n- **Lights leak through walls** (no occlusion) → a dead zone needs every light within ~±5 m dead, and the space behind it dim.\n- **Background shows as a bright rectangle** at a corridor's end → background = fog × ~0.15.\n- **Uniform fog lifts silhouettes to grey** → fog follows local light.\n- **Line wobble skews text; pitching down italicises it** → ≤ 0.25 px wobble; lower the camera.\n- **Primitive-built figures read as toys**, primitive legs as tubes → organic silhouettes; show only shoe toes.\n- **Pure red never blooms** at low emissive (low luminance) → drive it far higher.\n- **Normalising a filtered signal by its pre-filter peak** blows up a bus → normalise the processed signal.\n- **Heavy PA reverb destroys intelligibility** → ASR-check processed lines and the full mix.\n- **Billboards stamp dark rectangles** in AO → exclude them.\n- **Time-compressing a finished timeline** → keyframes in original time mapped through one warp function, including hard-coded times elsewhere.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\n`demo/world.js` (layout, light DataTexture, custom lighting and fog shaders, props), `demo/tex.js` (procedural wallpaper, carpet, ceiling, panels, notices, signs), `demo/vhs.js` (VHS camcorder ShaderPass), `demo/osd.js` (bitmap font, REC, battery, clock, title generator), `demo/subs.js` (608 captions), `demo/thing2d.js` (organic silhouette), `demo/story.js` (timeline, camera keyframes, focus / fear curves, time warp), `demo/main.js` (renderer, post chain GTAO + DOF + bloom → VHS, handheld rig). File map and build: [DEMO.md](DEMO.md#build-notes).\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the location and its materials, the procedure or reason for filming, the rules, the route, the presence (or none), the signal colour, the opening, the ending and the length.\n\nAll far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **two tapes** (the same route filmed twice, a year apart; the second tape finds what changed); **a real-estate walkthrough** where every room is described cheerfully by the operator while the house keeps adding rooms; **a checklist read aloud** item by item, each item ticked on a clipboard in frame, until an item is not on the list.\n- Openings: **mid-sentence** (the tape starts with the operator already talking, tracking still rolling in); **a test pattern** and a date slate before the camera is picked up; **the lens cap on**, sound only, until the cap comes off.\n- Endings: **battery dies** (the icon blinks, the frame freezes on the dead cell); **the camera is set down** and keeps recording an empty room; **the tape loops** back to the first shot, now with the operator's shoes visible in it.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nNight Shift Orientation\n"},{"id":"S4-L55","name":"积木玩具","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L55.jpg","markdown":"# L55 积木玩具（Brick Toy）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 材质与 3D\n- 长什么样：积木宇航员用零件拼出火箭，飞向积木月亮。\n- 适合：一块块搭，\"搭自己的系统\"\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Brick Toy — Style Prompt\n\n> Stop-motion toy-brick films shot like macro photography on a real desk: a world built from interlocking plastic bricks with round studs, animated a pose at a time.\n> References (grammar only): brick-film stop-motion on a tabletop and the \"brick movie\" approach of stepping characters while the camera moves smoothly; macro toy photography (softboxes on glossy plastic, shallow focus, real objects as landscape). Copy none of their characters, sets or music, and never use a brand name.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nA brick world on a **real surface** (a wooden desk, a kitchen table, a windowsill), shot with a **macro lens** and animated **in stop-motion**. The charm is the tension between the toy world and the real room.\n\nOne-glance traits: glossy ABS with clean rectangular highlights; studs on every top face; seams darkened by contact shadow; real objects out of focus behind; characters that move in held poses on twos.\n\n**Never** use the LEGO name, logo or minifigure silhouette (trademarks). Characters are *built from bricks* (e.g. 1×1 columns for legs, a 2×2 brick for a torso, round bricks for arms, a round 2×2 helmet). Studs carry no lettering.\n\nNot a low-poly isometric island (no flat-shaded facets, no floating diorama), not a clay or felt stop-motion (no deformation: bricks are rigid), not a clean product render of a toy (this world is played with, handled, knocked over).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Plastic**: ABS with strong clearcoat (~0.85, clearcoat roughness ~0.07); a roughness map with faint fingerprints and scratches (base ~0.17, scratches up to ~0.65); a bevel so edges catch a highlight.\n- **Geometry**: rounded box bodies (bevel ~0.045 stud), studs with a thin lip. Unit = 1 stud pitch (8 mm); brick height 1.2, plate 0.4. Large baseplates are **matte** (lower clearcoat than bricks), or they reflect softboxes as a coloured wash.\n- **Set**: a real surface with a real texture and real props at true scale (1 m = 125 studs). Household objects become landscape. Light it with a **neutral daylight studio HDRI**, never a tungsten interior (white bricks turn orange).\n- **Light**: a toy-photography setup. A directional key from one upper side, warm-white, casting shadows (fit its shadow frustum to each shot so shadows stay crisp); two or three **RectAreaLight softboxes** (key front, cool rim behind, one over any big set) so glossy plastic shows clean rectangular highlights; environment kept low (~0.45) for contrast. Add a soft contact-shadow blob where the key's shadow falls behind a hero object.\n- **Ambient occlusion is mandatory**: GTAO (radius a few studs) darkens seams between stacked bricks, stud bases and contact points. Without it bricks look pasted on.\n- **Supersample 2×** (render 3840×2160, deliver 1080p): thin stud edges and bevel highlights shimmer without it.\n- **Lens**: physical depth of field (CoC ∝ |1/focus − 1/z|). Scale aperture with shot size: widest for close-ups, less for mediums, least for a wide (too much blurs the hero at mid distance). Focus is always on a character's head or the brick being placed.\n- **Effects are bricks**: fire, smoke, water, light, weather, celestial bodies are all built from plates, round bricks and translucent pieces, jittered or rebuilt per drawing. Nothing is a particle system or a soft gradient.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- A **toy palette**: a handful of saturated brick primaries plus white, black, greys, tan. Each built object has one dominant colour and at most one or two trim colours; characters and hero objects take the most saturated ones.\n- The **real world stays natural and muted**: wood, paper, ceramic, daylight. The saturated colour belongs to the toys, which makes them pop against the room.\n- **Whites must read white**: grade slightly cool so white bricks don't go cream from the room bounce; soft contrast, saturation lifted ~10 %.\n- Translucent pieces (orange, yellow, clear, blue) are reserved for effects: fire, light, water, glass.\n- Example palette (the classic brick primaries): red `#c91a09`, blue `#0055bf`, yellow `#f2cd37`, green `#237841`, white `#f2f2ee`, black `#1b2a34`. Others: pastels and tan; greys with one accent.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- A rounded, friendly OFL face (e.g. Fredoka, in `core/fonts`) for titles and subtitles.\n- Subtitles live on a **toy element**: a dark rounded pill low in the frame with a small brick icon beside the text, or a printed tile, or a sign built into the set. White on dark, ≤ 2 lines. A second speaker type (radio, device, narrator) gets its own icon colour or tag. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- Titles pop in on 12 fps steps, like a piece being placed; a short sub-line in a brick colour.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Characters and bricks step at 12 fps** (on twos); **camera, focus, fire and flying vehicles move on ones** at 24 fps. Stepping the camera too reads as judder, not charm.\n- Bricks **fly in on arcs** and **snap** with a tiny overshoot (a fraction of a stud → 0 in ~4 frames). Every snap is a click on the soundtrack.\n- Characters act with **big readable poses** held for several drawings: a V of arms for pride, arms flung up for alarm, a bowed head for sadness, a hop for joy. Walk cycles swing legs about ±0.55 rad with a small bob. Arms and legs rotate at their joints; nothing bends.\n- Collapses and falls use **real rigid-body physics**: tip about the base edge, then independent bodies with gravity (a lower-than-true value reads better at this scale), bounces (restitution ~0.3), friction, settling flat. Pre-simulate so every render is identical.\n- Tiny imperfections are allowed. Bricks never stretch, squash or morph.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | Expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Extreme macro on one brick or stud, real room blurred behind | The toy scale is the world | a beginning; a detail that matters; a single part |\n| Low angle at minifig eye level | A tiny character made heroic | ambition; pride; standing up to something big |\n| High wide over the whole surface | The scale reveal: it was a toy all along | a summing-up; irony; a finished build |\n| Low, wide, lonely frame, character small in a corner | Smallness, failure | a setback; waiting; loss |\n| Frontal close-up, hands and brick in focus | Intimacy, decision | a choice; a repair; a gift |\n| Handheld shake on impact | Physical shock | a collapse; an arrival; a collision |\n| Tracking alongside a moving object, destination ahead | Momentum toward a goal | a journey; a race; a launch |\n| Top-down plan view | Order, layout, a map | a plan; sorting parts; a city from above |\n| Rack focus from a real object to a brick (or back) | Two scales in one frame | \"big world, small maker\"; a comparison |\n| Orbit around a finished build | Pride, inspection | a product; a monument; a result |\n\nFraming: keep foreground clutter out of the frame edges (piles of parts love to block the lens); check headroom in every shot; check every real prop against every camera, because an object placed on one shot's axis sneaks into the background of another. Cut on beats or bars; a snap can be a cut point. No generic dissolves; transitions can be a hand-placed brick covering the lens, a whip along the desk, or a pull out of one set into the next.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Instruments**: small, acoustic, playful (glockenspiel, toy piano, pizzicato, ukulele, whistling, claps, melodica); a brass or orchestral swell for scale; a heartbeat drum for suspense. Licensed or original, edited to the picture (cut on bars, bar-aligned jumps, chroma similarity at joins) rather than looped under it.\n- **Foley** (synthesizable): plastic creak before a failure, tiny plastic footsteps, a crash of many clacks, a brick click (short transient + resonances near 2.9 kHz and 4.6 kHz), clack on wood, rattle of a parts pile being stirred, whoosh on throws, a bell \"ding\" for discovery, bricks sliding over paper, motors and engines built from filtered noise.\n- **Silence is a tool**: cut music dead at a failure or a surprise; leave only room tone and one small real-world sound (a ticking clock, a fridge hum, a distant bird). Music can also drop out before a big moment, leaving a pulse or a countdown.\n- **Voice**: a warm storyteller, few short lines with room to breathe; secondary voices through a device filter (band-pass ~380–2800 Hz + soft saturation), optionally with beeps. Verify every line with whisper.\n- Mix: voice compressed, music ducked ~40 % under voice, loudness −14 LUFS.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Snap-together building.** The plot is an act of building; pieces fly in on arcs and click home. *Fits content like:* a company assembling its first product; a neighbourhood rising street by street; a recipe put together ingredient by ingredient.\n- **Break without harm.** Things fall apart and the parts become something new; failure yields spare parts. *Fits content like:* a pivot after a failed plan; recycling; a team reshuffled into a better shape.\n- **Toy on a real desk.** Real objects become landscape at toy scale. *Fits content like:* a laptop keyboard as a city grid; a houseplant as a jungle; a coffee spill as a flood.\n- **Hand-made stop-motion.** 12 fps poses, tiny imperfections, even fire and water made of bricks. *Fits content like:* a weather report; a kitchen explained with brick flames; a science demo of a volcano.\n- **Rebuild into something else.** The same pieces, rearranged on screen, become a different object. *Fits content like:* one career turning into another; a before/after renovation; a word re-spelled.\n- **The scale reveal.** Pull back until the whole world is a toy on a table. *Fits content like:* a big ambition that is still small; a year in review; a child's imagination.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Tungsten HDRI → orange whites. Use a neutral studio HDRI and a slightly cool grade.\n- Clearcoat on a large flat baseplate reflects softboxes as a coloured wash. Make it matte.\n- Flat, slightly jagged first renders: even light, no AO, single-sample rendering, stepped camera. Fix: softboxes + low environment, GTAO, 2× supersampling, cool grade, camera on ones.\n- Too much aperture blurs the hero at mid distance. Scale aperture with shot size.\n- A fast-rising or fast-moving object becomes a dot on a blank wall. Track it and keep its destination in frame.\n- Curved transparent parts made from a sphere segment vanish inside a cylindrical part. Use an open-cylinder panel.\n- Real props on a later shot's camera axis sneak into the background. Check props against every camera.\n- Random jitter used as a material or colour index can go negative → undefined material crash. Clamp.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `bricks.js` (rounded brick/plate/round-brick geometry, studs, plastic materials with clearcoat and fingerprint roughness), `actors.js` (a brick-built character rig with pose objects and `walkPose`), `set.js` (desk, HDRI, real props at true scale, softboxes), `rockets.js` (props, pre-simulated rigid-body physics, brick fire and smoke), `main.js` (acting, cameras, DOF, subtitles, sound events), `mix.py` (synthesized foley + voice + music), `music/edit.py` (bar-aligned score edit). Minimal start: a brick lighthouse from `bricks.js` on a stack of books, lit by the `set.js` softboxes, one still via `core/render/still.mjs`.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the structure, the characters (or none), the surface and its props, the opening, the ending, the camera path, the pacing, the palette within the colour logic and the music. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a catalogue** (one build per beat, each a different object answering the same question, lined up at the end); **two builders** on opposite sides of the table whose builds grow toward each other; **a disassembly** (a finished thing is taken apart piece by piece to explain what's inside).\n- Openings: **a hand pours a bin of bricks** onto the table and the pile settles into the first shape; **a real object first** (a mug, a phone), then rack focus to the tiny world at its base; **plan view** of an empty baseplate filling in like a map.\n- Endings: **one piece left over** held in close-up, the question it raises unanswered; **the lights go out** in the room and only a lit brick window glows; **a real hand** reaches in and picks the character up.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nRocket from Spare Parts\n"},{"id":"S4-L56","name":"纸片立体书","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L56.jpg","markdown":"# L56 纸片立体书（Paper Pop-up Book）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 材质与 3D\n- 长什么样：立体书在书桌上打开，小精灵 Pip 在纸片世界冒险，最后跳出书外。\n- 适合：翻书讲故事\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Paper Pop-up Book — Style Prompt\n\n> A pop-up book on a real desk becomes a stage: flat cut-paper actors with white borders perform in front of paper sets that spring up from the page, under real lamp light and a real macro lens.\n> References (grammar only): the *Paper Mario* series (flat cut-paper actors with a white border in a 3D diorama, bubbles that type on with blips, action ratings); Robert Sabuda / Matthew Reinhart pop-up books (layered V-folds, pieces rising back to front, pull-tabs); macro \"tiny world on a real desk\" photography. Never copy their characters, logos, UI or music, and never name them in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nTwo worlds in one frame. **The real world** at human scale: a photoreal surface lit by an HDRI room, a few real objects, a macro lens (shallow focus, warm practical light, soft contact shadows). **The paper world**: a hardcover book in that room; when it opens, one board stands up as the **backdrop** and the lower page is the ground. Every set piece is a **flat cut-paper card** that lies on the page and **pops up** around its bottom edge. Characters are cut paper too: a few centimetres tall, flat, with a thick off-white border, a grey cardboard edge and grain.\n\nWhat sells it is the **mismatch of scale and material**: flat, saturated, outlined paper cartoons under real light, casting cut-out-shaped shadows and going soft in real lens blur. Keep the paper graphic; let light and lens make it real.\n\nNot a papercut silhouette film (no one-colour lace, no backlight), not a 3D cartoon (nothing modelled round), not claymation (paper hinges, folds, curls and slides; it never squishes).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Real units** (metres): page 0.3–0.5 m wide, actors 3–5 cm, near plane a few mm, so HDRI, shadows and depth of field behave like a real macro shot.\n- **Cut-paper finish**: Canvas2D art with a dark ink outline; its silhouette dilated (drawn at many angles on several rings, recoloured `source-in`) into a grey cardboard edge offset slightly down (thickness) and a wide off-white border; a shared paper grain on top (`source-atop`).\n- **Drawing**: one dark-brown ink (never black), thick outline + thinner detail line, round joins; shading = one hard **crescent** (fill with shade, refill shifted with base); wobbly shapes (low-harmonic noise), never perfect geometry; simple faces.\n- **Pop-up pieces**: planes with art on the front and plain paper on the back (same alpha); `alphaTest` + `alphaToCoverage`; a custom depth material with the alpha map so **the shadow is the cut-out silhouette**. Anything \"3D\" is several flat cards (front/back layers, triangles hinged on a keel).\n- **The book** is modelled: cloth cover with foil title (roughness/metalness map), page block with edge lines, a curling leaf.\n- **Real world**: HDRI environment and softly blurred background, real wood or fabric, a few CC0 props. **Neutral tone mapping** (ACES shifts paper colours).\n- **Depth of field by hand**: CoC = aperture × (1/focus − 1/z), spiral gather that keeps far samples off a sharp foreground (stock bokeh looks fake at this scale); then light bloom, vignette, warmth.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- Saturated children's-book colour, **never neon**; the real world stays natural and slightly warm so paper pops against it.\n- **One dominant hue family per spread**, so a page turn is also a colour change; a few accents (a red, a yellow, a gold) recur across spreads to stitch the book together.\n- Ink dark brown, border off-white, card backs plain paper.\n- Unfinished or \"outside the story\" pages lose colour: graphite on cream. Night is a change of the paper art; the real lamp keeps its warmth.\n\nExamples of spread families: green meadow under sky blue; pink-and-lilac market with mint accents; ochre desert with a turquoise oasis.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- Rounded OFL faces: a rounded sans for subtitles, bubbles, signs (e.g. Fredoka); a chunky display face for cover, banners, action words (e.g. Lilita One); a storybook serif for taglines (e.g. IM Fell English); a playful Chinese face (e.g. ZCOOL KuaiLe) with the Latin face in its stack.\n- **Narration subtitles**: bottom centre, ~40 px, cream with a thick dark-brown outline and soft shadow, second language smaller below. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- **Speech bubbles** live in the paper world: white card, ink outline, tail projected each frame to the speaker; pop in with overshoot, type on with blips.\n- **Titles are paper objects**: a cardboard sign dropping on strings and swinging; the cover itself is the film title. Action words (italic display, gradient, double stroke, rays) sparingly, like a game rating.\n- Subset Chinese fonts to the characters used; re-subset after every text change.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- On ones (60 fps suits the smooth macro camera), all a pure function of `t`. Paper moves stiffly; life comes from easing: `back()` overshoot for pop-ups, springs for things on strings, squash and stretch on characters.\n- **Pop-ups rise back to front** (delay grows with distance from the reader), far pieces lying back, near ones folding forward face-down; they fold down in the same order.\n- Plants and light props sway a few hundredths of a radian around their base.\n- **Characters are flat cards redrawn per quantized pose** (walk phase, arms, eyes, mouth, look, blink); walk phase follows distance walked, so feet don't slide.\n- **Turning around = a `scale.x` flip** between front and back drawings; never `rotation.y`. Jumps: parabolic arcs, squash at both ends, a paper puff on landing.\n- **Page turn**: a many-segment strip whose bend is integrated along its length, so it curls like paper.\n- Never moves: the room.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nOne virtual macro camera keyed as position / target / fov / aperture / focus with monotone-cubic interpolation; a new key list = a hard cut. A vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Eye-level macro track, shallow focus | being inside the story at the actors' size | a walk; a conversation; a chase |\n| Pull up to a 3/4 view of the spread | the mechanism, the map | a page turn; a new setting; a plan |\n| Descending dolly from human height | entering the book's scale | an arrival; zooming into one part of a system |\n| Push-in on a face | feeling | a wish; a fear; a decision |\n| Reverse shot from inside the book to the blurred room | the world beyond the page is huge | curiosity; an outside threat; a reader watching |\n| Rack focus paper ↔ real object | paper and reality touch | cause and effect; a real product in a paper story |\n| Top-down over the open spread | a board game, a diagram | a route; two sides of the gutter compared |\n| Locked frame on a pull-tab | the trick is the event | before/after; a count |\n| Rising crane leaving the book small | scale, distance | aftermath; a summary; time passing |\n\nActors in focus, room objects as soft foreground or background; keep near pop-ups out of the lens path. Transitions: page turns, a book closing and reopening, a hard cut on a paper sound; never a video dissolve inside the book.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Paper foley is the signature**: page swish, cardboard thump, pop-up snap (short sine sweep + crinkle), fold and crease, pull-tab slide, footsteps as tiny taps with crinkle, slat flaps, string-drop tinks; the book's spine creak and closing thump. All synthesizable from the page's event list.\n- **Character voices as typing blips**: one waveform and pitch range per character, a blip every other letter.\n- **Narration**: one warm storyteller, picture-book sentences; pronunciations kept apart from displayed text.\n- **Music**: whimsical, acoustic or toy-like: music box, pizzicato strings, clarinet, glockenspiel, ukulele, toy piano, brushes. Options: a warmer cue for anything in the real room; a bar-aligned internal edit of a library track so its ending lands on a picture event; a pull-tab's clicks setting a cue's pulse.\n- **Ambience**: room tone and a clock for the room; one bed per spread for the paper world.\n- **Silence** = the book held still with only room tone; before a turn that matters, or a hesitation.\n- **Mix**: voice high-passed, compressed, well above music; music ducked under speech with smooth release; limit SFX per channel; −14 LUFS.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **The book opens**: creak, the board swings up, the world pops up back to front. *Fits content like:* a project launch; a first school day; a recipe book.\n- **Page turn = scene change**: old pieces fold down as new ones spring up. *Fits content like:* stages of a process; a farm's seasons; chapters of a biography.\n- **Pull-tab mechanisms**: flipping slats, a sun on a stick, sliding strips, a hinged door, pieces on strings. *Fits content like:* a forecast; old way/new way; a lock opening.\n- **Paper as character**: crumpled, smoothed, refolded, torn, taped. *Fits content like:* a mistake becoming a lesson; recycling; a rejected draft becoming the design.\n- **The edge of the page**: crossing between paper and the room, either way. *Fits content like:* a character stepping into a phone on the desk; a real object entering the story; a drawing coming true.\n- **The real object plays a part**: a lamp as a sun, a mug as a tower. *Fits content like:* a product at home; the reader's presence; real clock time.\n- **Typed bubbles and ratings**. *Fits content like:* a tutorial; a quiz; a small victory.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- `rotation.y` shows a card edge-on, then its blank back → `scale.x` flip. Negative scale also swaps front/back materials → draw mirrored props instead.\n- A one-plane container hides its contents → back card + front card, contents between.\n- Near-lens foreground pop-ups fill the frame with blur → move them or close the aperture.\n- Rectangle shadows kill the illusion → alpha-mapped depth material on every card; tight shadow bias and a big shadow map against acne on thin cards.\n- A turning page goes black facing away from lights → emissive map = colour map.\n- ACES shifts paper colours → Neutral.\n- Headless Chrome falls back to software WebGL → GPU ANGLE flags; ES modules need HTTP.\n- Redrawing character canvases each frame → redraw only on pose change.\n- Chinese subsets miss new characters and Latin glyphs → re-subset; Latin face in the stack.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/` (three.js r170 + Canvas2D): `paper.js` (cut finish, grain, crescent, blobs), `cutmesh.js` (cut mesh with paper back and cut-out shadow, strings, particles), `book.js` (`setOpen(θ)`, `setLeaf(u)` curling page), `art.js` (drawings at a fixed px/m), `actors.js` (posable flat characters, a foldable plane), `sets.js` (spreads, back-to-front pop scheduling), `post.js` (hand DOF → bloom → vignette), `hud.js` (subtitles, bubbles, banners), `lib.js` (easing, `back`, `spring`, `track`), `render.mjs` (stills / events / video). API table and a minimal new-spread example: DEMO.md \"Engine reference\".\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the book, the room, the characters (or none), the spreads and mechanisms, the opening, the ending, the camera path and each spread's palette. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **the almanac** (one spread per month or stage, each pull-tab left running so the book fills with motion); **two books facing each other**, characters trading objects across the gap; **one spread pulled again and again**, each pull a step of how something is made.\n- Openings: **mid-turn** (a page already curling, we land in chapter three); **inside the gutter** (low along the fold as the first piece rises over the lens); **a mechanism alone** (a tab slides by itself before any character appears).\n- Endings: **the book closes** (pieces fold in reverse, a puff of air, stillness); **one piece stays up** (everything folds but the card that is the point); **the shelf** (the camera finds this book among many).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nPip's Paper Adventure\n"},{"id":"S4-L57","name":"移轴微缩","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L57.jpg","markdown":"# L57 移轴微缩（Tilt-Shift Miniature）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 材质与 3D\n- 长什么样：玩具城的早高峰，一切都像模型。\n- 适合：小人国，俯看日常生活\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Tilt-Shift Miniature — Style Prompt\n\n> A real-looking place photographed from high above with a tilted lens and a time-lapse camera, so the whole world reads as a tabletop model.\n> References (grammar only): Sam O'Hare's *The Sandpit* (2010) and tilt-shift time-lapse photography in general: high vantage points, the blur band, time-lapse rhythm, cloud shadows sweeping over streets. Copy none of its places or music; show no real landmarks, brands or logos.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nA place seen from high up through a **tilt-shift lens**: only a thin horizontal strip of the frame is sharp, everything above and below melts into blur. The eye reads that as a macro shot of something small, so the world becomes a model. Two more things make the trick work: **saturated, sunny colour** (model-railway paint) and **time-lapse** (vehicles and people move in fast, jittery steps like wind-up toys).\n\nNot a toy or clay render (the world is photoreal, only the lens and the clock make it small), not a game's top-down view (no UI, no clean flat shading), not drone footage (the band, the saturation and the stutter are mandatory).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Vantage**: always high. Oblique views at roughly 45–55° pitch from 100–200 m with a moderately long lens (~27–30° vertical FOV); straight top-down for the most \"board game\" look. Real tilt-shift is never shot from street level, except for a deliberate intimate moment: a true medium close-up at the actors' height, a blurred foreground object framing sharp actors who fill ~1/4–1/3 of the frame height (a long lens from high up keeps them as dots).\n- **Blur band** (`demo/post.js`): physical depth of field mixed with a screen-space band. CoC in pixels = `mix(aper·(1/focus − 1/z), sign(dy)·amp·(max(0,|dy|−w)/0.5)^1.2, mix)`, clamped (~30 px). Put the band on the subject; when the subject moves, project it to screen every frame and slide the band there. Narrow the band for intimacy. Gather at output resolution after a 2× supersampled render, with many spiral taps and per-pixel rotation; far samples must not bleed onto nearer sharp ones.\n- **Grade**: neutral tone mapping, strong saturation boost, soft added contrast, slightly warm highlights, a vignette. Lower saturation at night.\n- **Light**: a hard sun with sharp shadows (a big shadow map fitted to each shot), a low hemisphere fill with warm ground bounce so shadows stay blue but not murky, a sky-gradient environment map for glass and paint. **Cheat the sun azimuth per shot**: each time-lapse clip is its own shot, so front-light the facades you see; one fixed sun leaves half a film backlit and grey.\n- **Model-like detail** (what separates \"filmed miniature\" from \"game CG\"): facades with several window types, 1.6–3 m bays, dark reveals, world-space grime, sky-reflective glass and walls darkening toward the ground; roof clutter, awnings and signs; worn markings and stains; vehicles with dark glass, a soft contact shadow and brake lights; ambient occlusion over everything.\n- **People** are small colour capsules, like model-railway figures. Actors seen up close get organic bodies with a soft sheen material and are made 20–30 % larger than real so they read.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- Saturated, sunny, \"freshly painted model\": a pale warm ground of buildings with a few candy accents, very saturated greens, dark ground surfaces so white markings and vehicles pop.\n- Shadows stay blue and clean, never grey. Night and dawn are the only low-saturation states; dawn must read as early morning (a warm low fill from the sunrise side, few lit windows), not midnight.\n- The subject you want found should be the one saturated thing in the sharp band, or move against the flow.\n- Examples: a Mediterranean harbour (whitewash, cobalt shutters, terracotta, turquoise water); a snowy ski village (white, pine green, red gondolas, long blue shadows); a desert market (sand, saffron, indigo awnings, dusty olive).\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- Subtitles belong to the world's own signage: a panel in the graphic language of the place's signs (road sign, platform board, harbour notice…), an open sign-revival font (Overpass, OFL, is a Highway-Gothic revival), sitting inside the lower blur band. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- A **time-lapse clock** with a speed readout (e.g. `×24 TIME-LAPSE` / `×1 REAL TIME`, monospace) is the audience's speedometer for the native trick; use it whenever speed changes carry meaning.\n- Titles live in the world: letters on a roof, a sign or a field, placed where nothing blocks them, lit or revealed on the beat.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Time-lapse stutter is the soul of the style.** In fast-forward the whole world (vehicles, people, lights, sun) updates at a low rate and holds each position for several frames (8 Hz = 3 frames at 24 fps); choose the rate so every jump lands on the musical grid. **The camera stays smooth on every frame.** Step at a middle rate while the speed is moderate; below about ×2 the stutter disappears and motion becomes silky, which makes a drop to real time hit hard.\n- Systems run on simple simulations pre-computed per clip (car-following, signal cycles, crowds gathering and surging), with cycle offsets chosen so changes land on bar lines.\n- Hero actors get art-directed timing (scripted paths fitted through beat times), never a hope that the sim hits a beat.\n- Close-up acting: anticipation squash → stretch → land squash → settle; a failed attempt before success is the comedy engine.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| High oblique wide, very slow slide | a whole system at a glance | setting a place; routine; many small actors |\n| Band follows one subject across a wide | \"this one\" | a single commuter; a delivery; a lost child |\n| Rack of the band from one subject to another | attention changing hands | cause and effect; a handover; a comparison |\n| Slow push on an intersection, station or yard | a system heating up | rush hour; a festival setting up; a queue |\n| Straight top-down, slowly rotating | the world as a board or machine | patterns; logistics; choreography |\n| Continuous dive from high to actor height with a speed ramp | the one small thing that matters | a twist; a joke; a meeting |\n| Band narrowing to a sliver | intimacy | a confession; a reunion; one decision |\n| Vertical rise with logarithmic height | scale, letting go | aftermath; a summary; a system becoming a pattern |\n| Lateral track along a line (rail, river, road) | a process in order | a supply chain; a parade; a race |\n| Locked frame across hours | time as the actor | a construction; a market day; tide |\n\nFraming: stay high; the sharp band carries the subject; vehicles and people stay tiny except in a close-up. Transitions: cuts on the beat, speed ramps, dives and rises; avoid dissolves that reveal the fake lens.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Ensemble**: tuned percussion carries the style (marimba, glockenspiel, vibraphone, woodblocks, kalimba, celesta), pulse-based and minimalist (Reich grammar: phasing, voices added one at a time, pulsing chords), plus plucked strings or a small reed section. Not generic piano and strings.\n- **Techniques, as options**: world events gate the notes of a fixed phrase skeleton (vehicles crossing a line, lights changing, crowds moving each trigger an instrument), with a base fill rate so the phrase stays audible; tuned horns or bells voiced as chords; a tape-stop (varispeed down, voices dropping out) into silence when time slows; single notes for single events in a rest; phasing two identical lines apart; a music-box version of the theme for the smallest scale.\n- **Sound follows speed**: in time-lapse, music first with a faint granular \"sped-up world\" hiss, clacks and relay clicks. At real speed the world turns suddenly real: idling engines, distant traffic, birds, small voices, footsteps. On release, engines rev and wind rises.\n- **Silence** is the moment real time arrives: cut the music, keep one or two real sounds.\n- **Voice**: relaxed, few short lines, music ducked ~8 dB. Verify every line with whisper; write numbers as words and put digits in the `asr` field. Master to −14 LUFS.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **The speed drop (×40 → ×1).** Time crashes to real speed on one detail. *Fits content like:* one worker finishing a shift; a child dropping an ice cream; a proposal on a crowded pier.\n- **The band as pointer.** Slide or narrow the sharp strip to steer the eye. *Fits content like:* a courier through a market; a single wrong-way skier; one boat leaving port.\n- **God's-eye system.** The place as a sequencer or machine. *Fits content like:* a port unloading ships; an airport's turnaround; a farm harvest.\n- **Long-exposure light.** At dusk, lights leave trails that draw on the world. *Fits content like:* ferries crossing a bay; runners with headlamps; lanterns along a river.\n- **The scale reveal.** Rise until the place becomes a pattern. *Fits content like:* a festival from setup to crowd; a school at recess; a city grid at night.\n- **Cloud shadows sweeping.** Weather crossing the model. *Fits content like:* a season turning; a mood change; a news cycle.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- Tall buildings plus shallow pitch hide the ground → low to mid-rise buildings, pitch 45°+.\n- The subject hides in a canyon or behind a facade → route it on the camera side and let the band follow it.\n- Instance caps silently drop actors → cull by distance from the camera (≈ 4× camera height) and raise the cap.\n- Simulated vehicles stop short of lines (the jam gap) → subtract it where the story needs contact.\n- Focus lags a frame → update actors before the camera.\n- A top-down → oblique dive twists → rotate the top-down so its screen-up matches the target shot.\n- A close-up from high up is too far → go low and close, small near plane for foreground objects.\n- Clouds wash out a rise → unlit white, low opacity, nothing below the camera. Light pools read as bubbles → denser, larger, fainter pools.\n- Scene backlit and grey → cheat the sun per shot. Dawn reads as midnight → warm low fill, fewer lit windows.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `post.js` (tilt-shift DOF: band + physical, grade; `band.follow`), `city.js` (procedural town, facade shader, sign), `geo.js` (merged geometry), `sky.js` (clock → sun, sky, fog, env map), `traffic.js` (lanes, lights, IDM sim, fleet, trails, walkers), `train.js`, `ducks.js` (lathe-turned characters + hop choreography), `main.js` (cameras, stepping, crowds, HUD), `music/score.py` (sampled marimba, skeleton + event gating). A minimal new use: build a harbour from `geo.js` boxes, run boats as a one-lane `traffic.js` fleet on water, and point `band.follow` at one of them.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the place, the system, the tiny actor, the structure, the opening, the ending, the camera path, the speed curve and the palette within the colour logic. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **one day, dawn to night** (a single locked vantage, the sun and the shadows sweep, one shop's light the last to go out); **the relay** (the band hands attention from actor to actor across a whole place, each one passing something to the next); **before/after** (a construction site time-lapsed from empty lot to opening day, with one real-time moment per stage).\n- Openings: **mid-bustle at full density** with the band already on one oddity; **a top-down pattern** that only slowly tilts into a place; **real time first**: one person at actor height, then the lens lifts and time speeds up.\n- Endings: **a drop to real time that stays there** (no pull-back); **lights going out one by one** at night from high above; **the lens un-tilts**: the band widens to full sharpness and the model becomes a real place.\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nToy Town Rush Hour\n"},{"id":"S4-L58","name":"低多边形等距","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L58.jpg","markdown":"# L58 低多边形等距（Low-poly Isometric Island）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 材质与 3D\n- 长什么样：空海里一格格长出小岛和村庄，每放一块响一个音符，直到星空。\n- 适合：小岛、小世界\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Low-poly Isometric Island — Style Prompt\n\n> A tiny faceted world seen through an orthographic isometric lens: everything is a geometric block, and everything that appears arrives with a bounce and a note.\n> References (grammar only): *Townscaper* (building is the performance; every piece lands with a springy bounce and a sound; pieces sprout small details by themselves); *Monument Valley* (isometric geometry, soft pastel palettes, gradient skies, calm pacing, lots of empty space). Never copy their grids, buildings, characters, UI or typography, and never name them on screen.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nA miniature world built from **flat-shaded low-poly geometry** (hexagonal tile columns, box buildings with prism roofs, cone and icosahedron trees) floating in a faceted sea or void, watched by an **orthographic camera** at an isometric angle. No perspective and no horizon: distance dissolves into a **screen-space sky gradient**, so the world looks like a model suspended in colour. **Light, not texture, tells time**: every facet changes colour as the sun moves; in the dark only emissive blocks keep their colour.\n\nNot a voxel game (no cubes-of-cubes, no pixel texture), not a tilt-shift photograph of a real place (no perspective, no photo detail), not a smooth-shaded 3D cartoon (no rounded normals, no subsurface).\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Geometry** (world unit = one hex tile's circumradius): pointy-top hexagonal columns (`CylinderGeometry(r, r, h, 6)`), axial → world `x = √3(q + r/2)`, `z = 1.5 r`. Terrain height encodes material (sand low, grass, hills, rock highest). A thin separate top cap sits over a darker side colour; **sink the side column's top below the cap** or it z-fights. Leave a small gap between tiles so AO draws the seams.\n- **Props** are primitive assemblies: boxes, prisms, low-segment cylinders and cones, icosahedra at detail 0, pinched boxes. Emissive parts (windows, lamps) get their own material per object so they can light individually.\n- **Materials**: `MeshStandardMaterial({ flatShading: true, roughness ≈ .9 })`, **no textures**. Jitter each tile's lightness a few percent so the ground isn't a spreadsheet.\n- **Water** (if any): a dense non-indexed plane displaced by summed sines in the shader, flat-shaded so each triangle glints on its own (not too low a roughness or facets flash white); a coarse plane below for zoom-outs; a **shallow map** redrawn per frame where land exists, mixing deep → shallow colour with a faint foam rim; a thin transparent ring at each tile's water line for surf.\n- **Sky without a horizon**: in post, linearise the orthographic depth and blend distant pixels into a vertical screen gradient; set haze start/end as fractions of frustum height × cot(elevation) so the horizon band stays in the top third at any zoom. Stars (hash grid, twinkle) only where haze is thick.\n- **Post**: MSAA + depth → **GTAO** (mandatory: it draws the seams between tiles and under roofs) → sky/haze composite → a very light tilt-shift at the frame edges → bloom (stronger at night) → pastel grade → Neutral tone mapping. Supersample 2×.\n- **Light beams** (searchlight, projector, torch): a camera-facing ribbon with a Gaussian profile, bright at the root and fading with distance, plus a wider faint halo copy; light the surface under it in its own shader.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- **Pastel, low contrast, a handful of hues**: one ground family (sand/grass/earth or its equivalent), one neutral rock, and a small set of wall and roof colours repeated across buildings.\n- **Time of day is a keyframed set of colours** (zenith, horizon, deep, shallow, sun colour, intensity, elevation, azimuth), interpolated. Day is airy and cool; dusk warms the horizon; night desaturates everything toward blue-violet.\n- **At night, warm emissives are the only bright things** (windows, lamps, a beam). Keep the sun sideways to the camera in key shots (low sun behind the target turns faceted water into glare); water receives no shadows.\n- No outlines, no black; shadows and AO are the only darks.\n\nExamples of world palettes: sand, grass and coral roofs under a pale-blue day; snow tiles, pine greens and red cabins under a lilac sky; terracotta mesas, sage scrub and turquoise pools under an apricot dusk.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- Geometric OFL sans faces: a rounded one for subtitles (e.g. Quicksand 600), a geometric caps face with wide tracking for titles (e.g. Josefin Sans).\n- **Subtitles**: no box; warm white with a soft dark-blue drop shadow, centred low; a small flat-shaded tile icon beside them whose top colour follows the time of day. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s), never overlapping the next line or a title.\n- **Titles behave like tiles**: letters rise one by one from under a water line, overshoot and settle.\n- A text card over the world leaves a gap where the world stays visible.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- On ones (24 fps), smooth. The charm is in the **arrival curves**, not stepping.\n- **Rise from below**: a short cubic ease up, then a damped-sine overshoot; a splash ring and spray the moment it breaks the surface. The event time is when it settles.\n- **Drop and stack** (floors, roofs, rings): a fast ease-in fall, a damped bounce, and volume-preserving squash-and-stretch on landing.\n- **Pop** (trees, lamps, details): scale `1 − e^(−k s)·cos(ω s)`, about 25 % overshoot.\n- **Life**: mills turn, smoke puffs, birds circle, boats bob with the same wave function as the water (evaluated on the CPU).\n- **Schedule growth on the music grid**: at most one piece per smallest slot, so every event keeps its own note; accelerate by density (halves → quarters → eighths), not by tempo.\n- Never moves: the projection (no perspective, no fov change).\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\n`THREE.OrthographicCamera`. **Frustum height is the zoom** (a few units for a close-up, the whole world at a dozen or more, hundreds for a scale reveal); place the camera ≥ 3 × frustum height away or the bottom of the frame looks under the sea. A vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | What it expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Turntable (azimuth sweep) | the thing seen from every side | building; inspecting; a product assembled |\n| Pure orthographic zoom (frustum change) | scale without perspective | a detail in context; smallness; a world among worlds |\n| Elevation change | low (≈20–25°) widens the sky band; high (≈50°) reads as a map | night and stars; a sweep across the whole world; a plan |\n| Locked 3/4 wide | the world lives on its own | lights coming on; time-lapse; waiting |\n| Tight close-up on one arrival | the bounce and the splash | a key piece; a milestone; a gag |\n| Side angle trailing a mover | travel across the frame, never at the lens | a delivery; a departure; a route |\n| Lateral truck along a row of tiles | a list, a sequence | steps of a process; a timeline |\n| Time-lapse sun sweep, camera still | light is the time machine | a day; a season; history |\n\nIntercut wide and close on beats: wide shows the shape growing, close sells the bounce. Transitions: hard cuts on the beat, a time-of-day change, a zoom through the sky; no dissolves, no perspective whip-pans.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Music can be event-driven**: growth events carry a note, and the score synthesizes each one at its exact time, so the picture writes the tune. Pitched percussion suits the facets: marimba, kalimba, woodblock, bells, vibraphone, celesta, steel drum; underneath, a soft pad, a gentle ostinato, light shaker or soft kick. Pentatonic or modal material keeps any order of notes consonant. **No piano, no strings.**\n- **A single note can be a motif**: one sound owned by the world (a bell, a chime, a horn) that returns changed.\n- **Foley follows material**, all synthesizable: bubble sweep + splash with droplet ticks for rising tiles (bigger = lower); wooden knock plus a quieter bounce knock for floors; ceramic clack for roofs; hollow plank knock; heavy stone thunk; glass tink; a low whump with electric hum for anything switching on.\n- **Beds**: low-passed brown-noise water with swells, birds as downward-gliding FM chirps, creaking wood, crickets or wind at night.\n- **Silence**: drop to a low drone and a bed; a moment of digital zero on the music bus before a big downbeat. Options: thin the ostinato to one voice instead of stopping it; let the world's own events carry the rhythm for a passage.\n- **Voice**: clear and gentle, few short lines; avoid words that collide with the film's subject (a film about baking: \"flour\" heard as \"flower\").\n- **Mix**: voice compressed and well above the music, music ducked under it, −14 LUFS; **grain 0** (flat colour shimmers with grain).\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs.\n\n- **Building is composing**: every piece that lands plays a note; a later sweep can replay them in spatial order as a finished melody. *Fits content like:* a skyline whose floors are notes; a team assembling a product; a garden whose flowers form a chord.\n- **Procedural growth**: tiles rise, floors stack, trees pop. *Fits content like:* a startup's first year; a coral reef recovering; a neighbourhood being planned.\n- **Scale by pure zoom**: one object fills the frame, then the world becomes a speck (or the reverse). *Fits content like:* one user among millions; a village on a continent; a data centre inside a network.\n- **Turntable**: a continuous rotation around the thing being made. *Fits content like:* a machine being assembled; a museum object; a house renovation.\n- **Light as clock**: shadows sweep and facets re-colour. *Fits content like:* a day of a shop; a year of a farm; a shift at a hospital.\n- **Emissive blocks in the dark**: lights coming on one by one, a beam sweeping like a clock hand. *Fits content like:* a network going live; a town at nightfall; volunteers joining a cause.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- `perspectiveDepthToViewZ` in shared post code is wrong for an orthographic camera → `orthographicDepthToViewZ`.\n- GTAO renders sprites and transparent objects as solid quads → hide them during the AO pass; set `visible = false` at zero opacity.\n- Side column and top cap sharing one plane → z-fighting → sink the column.\n- A double-sided additive cone reads as two white lines → a camera-facing Gaussian ribbon + halo.\n- Camera too close for its frustum → the frame bottom shows below the sea → distance ≥ 3 × frustum height.\n- Two pieces landing in one grid slot muddy the melody → one piece per slot.\n- `half` is a reserved word in GLSL.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/` (three.js): `world.js` (hex layout, primitive props, growth schedule → events with notes, arrival curves), `sea.js` (faceted wave shader, shallow map, beam-lit water), `post.js` (ortho post: GTAO → sky/haze + stars → tilt-shift → bloom → grade), `story.js` (beat grid, time-of-day keyframes, shots), `main.js` (renderer, lights, per-shot cameras, HUD, event export), `music/score.py` (event-driven score; `music/check.py` compares audio onsets and pitches with the events), `mix.py` (foley, beds, ducking). File map and build steps: DEMO.md.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the world (island, town, orchard, factory floor, planet), what grows or changes, the palette within the colour logic, the opening, the ending, the camera path and the pacing. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **erosion** (a finished world loses pieces one by one, each removal a falling note, until only the essential piece remains); **two worlds** side by side trading pieces across a gap until they join; **a year in four seasons** on one fixed world, the palette keyframes carrying the story.\n- Openings: **mid-construction** (a busy half-built world, many pieces already dropping); **from above** (a top-down map that tilts down into isometric); **the full world first**, then a push into one tile where the story lives.\n- Endings: **a close-up on one final piece** landing, no pull-back; **the world packed away** (pieces fly up and stack into a box); **daylight** (dawn floods the world and the music resolves on the first sunlit facet).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nThe Island That Grew\n"},{"id":"S4-L59","name":"玻璃质感产品","series":"S4","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S4-L59.jpg","markdown":"# L59 玻璃质感产品（Glass Product Render）\n\n- 分类：L Lemo-Opuscar · 材质与 3D\n- 长什么样：虚构玻璃耳机 Aura 的开箱与特写。\n- 适合：高级的产品感\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Glass Product Render — Style Prompt\n\n> Launch-film product rendering in transparent and frosted glass: a dark-field studio, strip-light sweeps, dispersion, caustics, light travelling inside the object, slow exploded views that snap shut on a downbeat.\n> References (grammar only): premium launch films (a sweep drawing a contour in the dark, slow orbits, macro rack focus, cuts on the beat, ultra-thin wide type); transparent-hardware adverts (internals as ornament, exploded views, black and white plus one accent); dark-field glass photography. Copy none of their silhouettes, typefaces, sounds or colour templates, and never name them in the film.\n\n## 1. Essence, and what it is not\n\nA single hero object of **glass, metal and light** floating in an infinite black studio. Long strips *behind and beside* it turn clear glass into a thin white outline with rainbow edges; the internals show through as in a display case. Everything moves slowly and precisely; every cut, sweep and pulse lands on the beat.\n\nThe object is always **fictional**. Never reproduce a real product silhouette (a famous phone or headphone outline), a real brand name, logo, typeface or UI.\n\nNot a white-cyclorama catalogue shot, not a wireframe HUD, not a keynote slide: material and light are the subject.\n\n## 2. Materials & rendering\n\n- **Studio = a strip-light scene, not an HDRI.** Photo-studio HDRIs contain windows and clutter that print as messy streaks on glass. Build a tiny black scene of emissive planes and bake it to PMREM **every frame** (`pmrem.fromScene(env, 0, 1, 1000)`, dispose the previous RT): tall side strips behind (silhouette), a wide far strip (horizon rim), a soft top box (volume for frosted glass and metal), a weak front card (off when concave glass faces camera), a top-front key only when parts must be read one by one, an accent card driven by the pulses, and **the sweep**: a tall strip on an arc *behind the camera* (azimuth = camera + π). Being real geometry, its highlight, refraction and dispersion move physically; never paint a 2D highlight.\n- **Clear glass**: `MeshPhysicalMaterial`, transmission 1, roughness ≈ 0.015, IOR ≈ 1.5, thickness sets lensing (thicker = stronger), **dispersion on** (three r163+), near-neutral attenuation, `side: DoubleSide` so other glass sees its back faces.\n- **Frosted glass**: transmission ~0.85, roughness ~0.4, milky tint, thick. On black it reads as dark grey plastic unless **something bright is behind or inside it**: an opaque emissive shape inside (blurred into a soft internal glow) and/or a bright gradient behind.\n- **Metals** make transparency worth looking at: satin bands, chrome (roughness ≥ ~0.14), brushed surfaces via a roughness map, boards with gold traces. **Anything inside transmissive glass must be opaque** (only opaque objects reach the transmission buffer).\n- **Light guides**: dark polished opaque acrylic, clearcoat, emissive **comet pulses** along a UV axis via `onBeforeCompile` (Gaussian head, exponential tail, white-hot core, hue drifting with age). Idle emission ≈ 0.\n- **Background**: black with an optional dark-grey radial sweep that separates glass edges; the floor fades into it, **no horizon line**. **Floor**: black gloss, blurred mirror, radial fade, procedural caustics (lens ring with R/G/B radii offset, warped Voronoi filaments, rings spreading from events).\n- **Post**: 2× supersampling; physical DoF (shallow macro, deep exploded views); bloom only above ~1.1; **Neutral tone mapping** (AgX washes accents to white); grain 0. Model in mm, near plane 1.\n\n## 3. Colour logic\n\n- The world is neutral: black, greys, the white of strip reflections, the metals' own tints.\n- **One accent colour family, reserved for light** (emission, caustic rings, the dispersion line on UI), optionally drifting between two neighbouring hues with age or energy. Examples: ice blue → soft violet; amber → rose; green → cyan. Pick it from what the product does, never from a brand.\n- Emitters that only illuminate (inside frosted glass) stay cool-white and dim.\n- Dispersion rainbows are allowed only at thick glass edges and in the caustic, never as a gradient on surfaces or type.\n\n## 4. Type & subtitles\n\n- **Subtitles are a slab of frosted glass**: a centred rounded bar filled with the live frame blurred (`filter: blur() brightness()` on the WebGL canvas, `preserveDrawingBuffer: true`), a little cool white, a thin highlight stroke and an accent line on top. A licence-free grotesk (e.g. Inter Tight), light weight, ~36–44 px, near-white. Hold ≥ max(1.8 s, speech + 0.6 s).\n- **Titles**: the same grotesk at an ultra-thin weight (200), very large (100–140 px) with very wide tracking, centred in the upper third; a diagonal glint sweeps across the letters in sync with the sweep light on the object. Small tracked caps (18–20 px) for kickers and credits.\n- The product name and slogan are **title cards**, not subtitle lines.\n\n## 5. Motion quality\n\n- **Everything moves on ones** (24 fps), eased: ease-in-out for camera orbits, ease-out for parts decelerating into a float, ease-in for a snap back.\n- **The camera always drifts**: slow orbits or push-ins; never a locked-off frame, never handheld, except a couple of frames of micro-shake on an impact.\n- **Exploded view**: parts spread along the axis (~2× product size), each with a small deterministic tilt and a spin proportional to its offset; long ease-out apart → short drifting hover → **very short ease-in snap** on a downbeat.\n- Objects lift out of cradles tilted toward camera and settle upright; lids hinge at the back and stand behind the object as a backlit halo.\n- Light pulses and sweeps are events on the beat grid, not free animation.\n\n## 6. Camera grammar\n\nA vocabulary, not a route. Opening and ending come from the topic.\n\n| Move | Expresses | Can serve |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sweep over a dark object, slow push | Contour before content | an unknown thing; a before-state |\n| Slow high 3/4 orbit | The whole object in one move | a mechanism opening; variants |\n| Macro, rack focus surface → part inside | Nothing to hide | an ingredient; a sensor |\n| Side macro orbit | Material contrast | layers; a seam |\n| Exploded view, axis ~40° to view, orbit | Technical beauty, suspension | architecture; a pause before a turn |\n| Straight-on front, slight push | Declaration | a single number; a name |\n| Top-down macro | A pattern at full size | channels; a circuit |\n| Top-down on the floor | The effect leaving the object | range; spreading energy |\n| Low orbit under the object | Monument, weight | durability; a flagship |\n| Slow pull-back into negative space | Room for type | a claim; a question |\n\nFraming: one hero, centred or on a strong diagonal, never cropped by the frame edge in a wide. Cut only on beats (bar lines where possible). Product films need **no spatial continuity** between shots: each shot is its own presentation stage, so hide or move anything that doesn't serve it. Transitions are cuts on the beat or a sweep passing through black; never dissolves or wipes.\n\n## 7. Sound palette\n\n- **Minimal electronic, never piano-and-strings.** Timbres, as options: bowed-glass or wet-finger tones, granular crystal clouds, glass bells and FM glass plucks, a filtered tick grid for precision, a warm pad, sub drone, soft short kicks and claps, 808 bass (sine with pitch envelope, soft saturation, slides), noise and saw risers, a high glass ding. Minor and modal colours suit the dark studio.\n- **Lock every pulse, sweep and snap to the score**: write the cue map first and share one hit list between the score and the renderer; light pulses may follow a syncopated bass pattern exactly.\n- **Silence as a tool**: a short stretch of total silence (reverb tails cut too) before a release, at most a reverse whoosh inside it.\n- **Foley follows the material**: glass shimmer for sweeps (band-passed noise + inharmonic partials, panned with the strip); glass-metal friction for moving covers; magnetic clicks (sub-2 ms transient + 3–5 kHz metal resonances + low thump); air puffs for moving parts; a snap = a cluster of clicks ms apart + an inharmonic glass chord (partials ×2.76, ×5.4); glass-on-glass ticks.\n- **Voice**: restrained, low, launch-like, very few very short lines. Never a product name alone (ASR and viewers mishear it): give it a verb.\n- **Mix**: duck music ~12 dB and foley ~6 dB under the voice; limit the biggest bass peaks ~3 dB so loudnorm stays linear; −14 LUFS; grain 0.\n\n## 8. Native moves\n\nA menu: use the ones your story needs. Adapting any topic: find **the invisible thing the product does** and make it visible as light inside glass.\n\n- **Transparency.** The inside is the hero; rack focus from the shell to the part within. *Fits content like:* a juicer's blades; a watch movement; a supplement capsule's layers.\n- **Lensing.** A thick glass dome magnifies and bends what is behind it; macro through it feels like looking into a jewel. *Fits content like:* a camera module; a magnifying tool; a water filter.\n- **Dispersion.** A sweep crossing a thick edge becomes a moving spectrum. *Fits content like:* a prism lamp; a display's colour range; a skincare bottle.\n- **Light that travels inside.** Energy (charge, data, heat, sound) runs as light through channels. *Fits content like:* a battery filling; a router's data threads; a kettle heating.\n- **Caustics.** An internal pulse becomes a ring spreading over the floor. *Fits content like:* a speaker's range; a perfume's trail; a wireless charger's field.\n- **Exploded view and snap.** Parts float apart and snap back on a downbeat. *Fits content like:* a modular backpack; a pen's refill system; a repairable phone.\n- **Frost to clear.** A frosted shell clears (roughness and tint animate to zero) and the inside appears. *Fits content like:* a privacy feature; a fridge's contents; an honest price breakdown.\n\n## 9. Pitfalls of the medium\n\n- A manually updated mirror uses last frame's camera → call `scene.updateMatrixWorld(); camera.updateMatrixWorld()` first; the transmission pre-pass re-renders it → no-op its `onBeforeRender`, update once per frame.\n- A coloured emitter inside frosted glass tints the whole object; idle glow under a lens dome becomes a pale blob; AgX turns accents white; frosted glass on black is black; mirror chrome becomes a white disc → the rules in §2–§3.\n- An exploded view seen perpendicular to its axis is edge-on lines → axis ~40° to camera.\n- Pure profiles read as simple shapes (mushrooms, pot lids) → 3/4 views, lids upright behind.\n- Bass peaks without headroom push loudnorm into dynamic mode → limit them in the mix.\n- Concave glass facing camera prints the front card as a grey rectangle → card off.\n\n## 10. Engine\n\nIn `demo/`: `studio.js` (strip env baked per frame, sweep, background, floor mirror + caustics + rings), `product.js` (glass / frosted / metal / guide materials, comet-pulse shader, `explode()`), `main.js` (staging, cameras, frosted subtitle bar, title glint), `story.js` (beat-grid timeline shared with the score), `music/score.py`, `mix.py` (foley + voice + ducking). New object: replace the geometry in `product.js` (e.g. a glass cylinder with a coiled light guide), keep materials and `studio.js`.\n\n## 11. Variation space\n\nYou decide the object (always fictional), internals, accent, structure, opening, ending, camera path, pacing and music. All far from our demo:\n\n- Structures: **a line-up** (three variants compared by what glows inside); **a day in light** (one object through morning, noon and night, the strip kit rotating around it); **material stack** (one layer removed per beat until the core stands alone).\n- Openings: **inside first** (one internal part; the shell forms around it); **caustic first** (light on the floor, followed up to the object); **silhouettes** (several dark outlines; one begins to glow).\n- Endings: **floor only** (the object leaves, its caustic keeps pulsing); **cool down** (emission fades to bare strip outlines); **held macro** (no pull-back; one detail, the name small).\n\n---\n\nHow our demo was made (story, shots, score, end card, build): [DEMO.md](DEMO.md). Read it after your treatment exists.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 示范片\n\nAura — Hear the Light\n"},{"id":"S5-M1","name":"白板手绘讲解","series":"S5","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M1.jpg","markdown":"# M1 白板手绘讲解\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 讲解类\n- 长什么样：白底，一只手（或一支笔）一边讲一边画，线条先画出来再上色。\n- 适合：讲概念、讲原理的段落。比如\"大语言模型是大脑，Harness 是手脚\"。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n> 这一条的提示词是重新写过的详细版：画面规则、颜色、时间轴都写死了，DeepSeek 和 Opus 5.5 都能直接用。{{ }} 里是要你自己填的内容。\n\n你是一位前端动效工程师。请写**一个完整的 index.html 文件**：只用原生 HTML + JavaScript + Canvas，不要任何外部库、字体文件或图片。做一段白板手绘讲解动画。\n\n## 讲什么\n\n- 主题：{{主题，例：大语言模型是大脑，Harness 是手脚}}\n- 总时长：{{秒，默认 20}}\n- 画面里的字：{{3–6 个短词。例：标题\"AI 是怎么干活的\"；左边\"大语言模型\"，副标\"会想、会说\"；右边\"Harness\"，副标\"手和脚：真去做事\"；箭头上\"指令\"}}\n\n## 输出要求（必须照做）\n\n1. 只输出一个 html 代码块，不要解释。代码必须完整可运行：不要用省略号，不要写\"其余同上\"\"此处略\"。\n2. canvas 固定 1920×1080；用 CSS 让它在窗口里等比缩放并居中，窗口多出来的部分是黑边。\n3. 所有画面由函数 `draw(t)` 决定，`t` 是秒。不要用 `Math.random`；需要随机就用带种子的 mulberry32(seed)，这样同一个 `t` 每次画出来都一模一样。\n4. 默认循环播放；按空格暂停/继续；网址带 `?t=8.5` 时只显示第 8.5 秒那一帧（静止），方便截图。\n\n## 画面怎么画\n\n- 底色 #FBFAF7；再叠几条很淡的灰色擦痕（宽 40–90px，透明度 0.03–0.06）。\n- 线条是黑色马克笔 #1B1B1B，宽 10px，`lineCap` 和 `lineJoin` 都用 round。每一笔画两遍：第一遍透明度 0.93；第二遍宽 5.5px、透明度 0.55、位置偏移 ±1.5px。\n- 只有黑和**一个**强调色 #E5432B（用在标题下划线、箭头、副标题和笔帽）。不要第三种颜色。\n- 字体：`font-family: KaiTi, STKaiti, \"Kaiti SC\", \"Microsoft YaHei\", sans-serif`，粗体；标题 110px，标签 90px，副标题 56px。字用 `strokeText` 加 `fillText`，描边宽约字号的 3.5%。\n- 手绘感：每个点加 ±2.5px 的抖动，抖动用带种子的随机按\"点的序号\"取，不要每帧重新随机。\n- 要画的东西：\n  - 云朵形的\"脑\"：极坐标 r = R·(1 + 0.11·|sin(3.5θ)|)，里面画几条波浪线；\n  - 一个火柴人：圆头、一根线的身体、两条手臂（末端画一个圆当手）、两条腿；\n  - 一条弯曲的强调色箭头，箭头头部要顺着线尾的方向；\n  - 一支马克笔：黑杆、红帽，长约 330px，斜 55°，笔尖始终停在\"当前正在画的那一笔\"的末端；全部画完后滑出画面。\n\n## 动起来的规则\n\n- 每一笔是一个点列表。用 progress（0→1）决定画到第几个点，progress 用 ease-in-out；一笔 0.4–1.2 秒。\n- 顺序：标题 → 标题下划线（红）→ 左边的脑 → 右边的小人 → 红色箭头 → \"指令\"两个字 → 两处标签文字。同一时间只画一笔。\n- 全部画完后静止 1.5 秒，再循环。\n\n## 不要\n\n渐变、阴影、发光、3D、粒子、复杂配色、满屏文字、镜头晃动。\n\n## 写完后自己过一遍（不要输出）\n\n- `t=0` 时是不是几乎空白的白板？\n- `t=总时长−0.1` 时所有元素都在吗？\n- 文字有没有被线或笔压住？\n- 同一个 `t` 刷新两次，画面是不是一模一样？\n"},{"id":"S5-M2","name":"纸感手写笔记","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M2.jpg","markdown":"# M2 纸感手写笔记\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 讲解类\n- 长什么样：米白纸面，马克笔批注一笔一笔画出来，手写字，大号粗体标题字，偶尔整个画面\"啪\"一下切到深色。\n- 适合：列要点、讲方法、\"你可以这样做\"的段落。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n纸感手写笔记风格。米白纸面背景，配色只用纸色、墨黑和两个强调色，深色模式用炭灰。马克笔批注一笔一笔画出来，手写字用描边动画（stroke-dashoffset）写出来，标题用很大的粗体字。每 1.5 到 2 秒出一个新点子，每次转场只做一个主要动作。动作要有真实的缓动、挤压拉伸和跟随。不要出现播放条、时间码这些界面元素。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[纸感产品发布片](https://prompt-motion.com/ik-builds-b8bdcf)（用 HyperFrames + GSAP 做的，提示词写得特别细）\n"},{"id":"S5-M3","name":"文字建筑（口播金句专用）","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M3.jpg","markdown":"# M3 文字建筑（口播金句专用）\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 文字和金句类\n- 长什么样：只用文字。每个词有一个\"结构上的角色\"：有的是梁，有的是柱子，有的是地板。前面的词搭起来，最后一句话\"站\"在前面搭好的东西上。\n- 适合：结尾金句\"学习，相信自己\"。全片最值得做的一段。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n做一段 [20] 秒、只用文字的口播动画。台词按顺序是：[第1句]、[第2句]……每个关键词有一个结构上的角色，比如\"学习\"是地基，\"相信\"是柱子，\"自己\"是站在上面的人。文字用真实的字形轮廓搭出来，每一句都改变画面的结构。最后一句必须\"站\"在前面那些词搭好的东西上。配色：深色背景、纸色字、一个强调色。不要麦克风、人群、旗子这类俗套画面，不要素材视频。做完挑 8 帧检查，读起来顺序对，最后的平台确实是前面的元素搭起来的。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[把一段话搭成建筑](https://prompt-motion.com/gdgtify-287ddf)\n"},{"id":"S5-M4","name":"一个元素串全片","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M4.jpg","markdown":"# M4 一个元素串全片\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 结构和镜头类（用来把不同风格串起来）\n- 长什么样：一个小东西（一个橙色圆点、一个白球）贯穿所有场景：它先是开关，再变成字母上的点，再变成曲线，再变成一个图形，最后回到结尾。\n- 适合：混搭风格时的\"线\"。比如可以用一个小圆点代表\"公告\"、代表\"想法\"，从头跟到尾。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n一个 [颜色] 小圆点串起每一个场景：[场景1里它是什么]，然后变成 [场景2里的什么]……最后回到结尾卡片。圆点不能瞬移，只能连续地动过去。动作干脆利落，弹簧回弹很小，没有空档。不用素材视频、渐变、发光、粒子爆炸、随机转场。最后一帧和第一帧一样，可以循环。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[橙色圆点](https://prompt-motion.com/ultimaxbt-bbebf5)、[白色小球](https://prompt-motion.com/rossaxbt-94583b)\n"},{"id":"S5-M5","name":"一镜到底","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M5.jpg","markdown":"# M5 一镜到底\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 结构和镜头类（用来把不同风格串起来）\n- 长什么样：从头到尾不切镜头，每个场景都是从上一个场景里\"长\"出来的。\n- 适合：讲变化的段落，比如\"以前要自己推，现在坐进去它自己走\"，一个画面变到另一个画面。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n一镜到底。每个场景都从上一个场景里变出来：文字从一条线里升起来，按钮展开成页面，用一种颜色铺满画面切到下一段。不要淡入淡出、不要模糊、不要硬切。每个场景镜头只做一次缓动。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[照片打印 App 发布片](https://prompt-motion.com/twoclipping-221cab)、[网页 3D 飞越](https://prompt-motion.com/twoclipping-d89be5)、[外卖 App 发布片](https://prompt-motion.com/ultimaxbt-2ddf1e)\n"},{"id":"S5-M6","name":"苹果发布会风","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M6.jpg","markdown":"# M6 苹果发布会风\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 节奏和快剪类\n- 长什么样：柔和、白、亮蓝、玻璃质感，镜头从不停在静止画面上切。\n- 适合：展示工具、展示界面的段落。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n苹果发布会的感觉：柔和、白、亮蓝、玻璃质感。镜头从不在静止画面上切，每个镜头进来时已经在动，离开时加速或者带运动模糊。字体用 Inter。不要出现紫色、品红和橙色。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[发布片对比](https://prompt-motion.com/notdwd-c2037d)、[Apple 风 30 秒](https://prompt-motion.com/gautam-mer1-2ae354)\n"},{"id":"S5-M7","name":"极简卡点","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M7.jpg","markdown":"# M7 极简卡点\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 节奏和快剪类\n- 长什么样：一个镜头只讲一件事，大量留白，只用一个强调色，每一刀都在重拍上。\n- 适合：节奏快的段落，比如\"你每天要整理的表格、你每周要写的周报……\"。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n高级极简。一个镜头只讲一件事，大量留白，只用一个强调色，字距收紧的无衬线字体，文字用遮罩显出来，镜头稳。每一刀都切在重拍上，每个界面动作踩在拍子上。屏幕上的字不要句号。不要冲击波、粒子、RGB 分离、抖动、光晕、霓虹、闪烁、弹来弹去的缓动。只用可以商用的音乐和音效。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[UGC 广告生成器](https://prompt-motion.com/twoclipping-6dd14e)（这条提示词会先反问你要哪些素材，很值得学）\n"},{"id":"S5-M8","name":"口播 + 逐字字幕","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M8.jpg","markdown":"# M8 口播 + 逐字字幕\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 情绪和氛围类（感受线最常用）\n- 长什么样：白底，一种字体，字幕跟着说话一个词一个词打出来，配真实的图片，镜头慢慢漂。\n- 适合：全片的主线。有人讲解、口播的片子，字幕必须对上。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n以口播录音为主线。字幕跟着说话逐词出现，时间用 Whisper 从录音里对出来，不按文案逐字对。白底，只用一种字体，黑字。镜头每段只做一次慢慢的漂移。图片在音乐的 16 分音符上换。屏幕上的字不要句号。不要给字加选中框一样的底色。音乐在关键那句话上起来，人声出来的时候音乐自动压低。响度统一到 -14 LUFS。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[AI 图像模型发布片](https://prompt-motion.com/rossaxbt-3085b7)（ElevenLabs 配音 + Whisper 对字幕时间）、[把口播素材剪成有字幕有图形有音乐的片子](https://prompt-motion.com/sab8a-364a5a)\n"},{"id":"S5-M9","name":"情绪弧线：混乱、掌控、清晰","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M9.jpg","markdown":"# M9 情绪弧线：混乱、掌控、清晰\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 情绪和氛围类（感受线最常用）\n- 长什么样：全片一条弧线。开头画面乱，中间慢慢理顺，最后干干净净落在一句话上。转场靠同一个形状变过去，不硬切。\n- 适合：整期的结构。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n全片是一条连续的弧线：从混乱，到掌控，到清晰，最后落到 [结尾那句话]。场景之间用同一个物体、同一个形状变过去，不要硬切，除非有特别的理由。动作要有高级的缓动和错开的层次，有一点景深和光感。不要满屏模糊、不要弹来弹去的界面、不要粒子。每一个重要的画面单独截出来都能当海报用。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[记账软件广告片](https://prompt-motion.com/daniel-haida-8691d4)（提示词里写着\"不是普通的软件讲解\"）\n"},{"id":"S5-M10","name":"把一个抽象概念、一种情绪做成画面","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M10.jpg","markdown":"# M10 把一个抽象概念、一种情绪做成画面\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 情绪和氛围类（感受线最常用）\n- 长什么样：没有具体的人和物，用形状、文字、颜色的变化表现一种感觉。\n- 适合：讲恐惧、焦虑、不安、\"想太多\"这种情绪的段落。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n做一段 [15] 秒的动态图形，表现\"[情绪/概念]\"这个感觉，比如\"焦虑\"、\"对未知的恐惧\"。不要具体的人和物，用形状、文字和颜色的变化来表现。从 [一种状态] 慢慢变到 [另一种状态]。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[主题是\"想太多\"](https://prompt-motion.com/gizakdag-cf4ae6)\n"},{"id":"S5-M11","name":"人生循环","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M11.jpg","markdown":"# M11 人生循环\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 情绪和氛围类（感受线最常用）\n- 长什么样：同一个人从小到老，最后无缝接回开头。\n- 适合：\"学无止境\"、\"一直在学\"这种讲长时间的段落。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n做一段 [20] 秒的动画，同一个人从 [阶段1] 到 [阶段2] 到 [阶段3]……最后无缝接回开头，可以循环播放。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[人生循环](https://prompt-motion.com/loicrambo-ad6830)\n"},{"id":"S5-M12","name":"剪纸皮影","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M12.jpg","markdown":"# M12 剪纸皮影\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 情绪和氛围类（感受线最常用）\n- 长什么样：纸剪的剪影在光后面演戏，配轻轻的声音。\n- 适合：讲\"家\"、讲回忆、讲温暖的段落。竖屏很合适。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用纯 JavaScript 做一段 [30 到 60] 秒的竖屏 9:16 动画，剪纸皮影戏的风格。配轻柔合适的声音，声音也用代码做。主题是：[一个问题，比如\"是什么让一个地方变成家\"]。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[剪纸皮影讲\"家\"](https://prompt-motion.com/x4b47x-9cc84f)\n"},{"id":"S5-M13","name":"手绘定格，第一人称","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M13.jpg","markdown":"# M13 手绘定格，第一人称\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 情绪和氛围类（感受线最常用）\n- 长什么样：手绘的、一帧一帧有点抖的定格动画，画面里的东西自己开口说话。\n- 适合：让一个东西替你说话。比如让\"那个抓公告的小工具\"自己介绍自己。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n做一段手绘定格动画。你就是 [东西]，用第一人称跟观众介绍你自己。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[手绘定格讲产品](https://prompt-motion.com/nikolajankovic-ce4ff4)\n"},{"id":"S5-M14_","name":"手绘定格 · 多角色互动","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S5-M14_.jpg","markdown":"# M14 手绘定格 · 多角色互动\n\n> （原库该条目为空，此处为补全内容）\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 情绪和氛围类\n- 长什么样：铅笔手绘的三个小人站在同一张纸上，一帧一帧换姿势，线条抖动像被反复描过。他们轮流开口、递东西，纸面留着重画的痕迹。\n- 适合：团队协作、多角色对话、访谈复现、\"三个误区\"这类并列观点\n- 关键词：手绘、定格、抖动、多角色、纸纹、气泡\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「手绘定格 · 多角色互动」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 纸底 #f3ece0，叠 0.05 噪点纸纹与轻暗角\n- 角色用 #2b2b2b 的 3px 蜡笔线，圆头加梯形身体\n- 每人只给站、探身、举手三姿势，硬切不插值\n- 留白不少于 40%，不加道具，纸张就是舞台\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-0.8s：铅笔划出第一根横线，当作地面\n- 0.8-2.4s：三个角色依次被画出，每 0.35s 一个，从脚往上长\n- 2.4-4.8s：轮流发言，每个气泡 0.6s，发言者前倾 4px\n- 4.8-6s：最后一个气泡被擦掉一半，全员回到初始的定格姿势\n\n技术要点：\n- 定格靠帧保持：`poses[floor(t*12)]`，每秒只换 12 次姿势\n- 抖动加固定种子的 ±1.2px 偏移，同帧共用种子，免得乱抖\n- 线条描两遍，第二遍偏 1px、透明度 0.45，做回描\n- 气泡硬切弹出，绝不淡入——定格没有 fade\n"},{"id":"S5-M15_","name":"定格动画 · 物件替换","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S5-M15_.jpg","markdown":"# M15 定格动画 · 物件替换\n\n> （原库该条目为空，此处为补全内容）\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 讲解类\n- 长什么样：木桌上摆着同一件东西，每隔几帧就被换掉一个零件，像有人在你眼前拆装。偶尔有只手伸进来把旧件拿走、新件放下，全程没有补间。\n- 适合：讲\"换个方案\"、产品迭代对比、配件与模块说明\n- 关键词：定格、替换、拆装、手部道具、桌面、离散\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「定格动画 · 物件替换」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 桌面暖木色 #c9a882，压 0.06 木纹横线，光从左上来\n- 主体四件：底座 #6a7b6f、中段 #d9c7a7、顶盖 #b8643f、侧扣 #3f4a55\n- 只出现一只手，从右边缘进出，只做放入和拿走\n- 画面不写字，靠零件增减说明一切\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1s：空桌，底座在 1/12 秒内直接落定，不加下落动画\n- 1-3s：手依次放上中段和顶盖，每次 0.5s：入 0.2s、放置 4 帧、出 0.2s\n- 3-4.5s：手拿走中段，换上另一个颜色，停 0.5s 让人读出差别\n- 4.5-6s：零件被快速拆走，桌面回空，只留侧扣\n\n技术要点：\n- 全片禁用插值与缓动，位移按时间片直接赋值\n- 手用贝塞尔加圆角矩形，进出各 3 帧、中间静止 10 帧\n- 投影面积随零件数变化，每加一件 +12%，用椭圆近似\n- 换色对比时形状一致只改 fill，才读得出是同一件的另一版\n"},{"id":"S5-M16_","name":"逐帧抖动 · 线条生长","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S5-M16_.jpg","markdown":"# M16 逐帧抖动 · 线条生长\n\n> （原库该条目为空，此处为补全内容）\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 情绪和氛围类\n- 长什么样：纯黑底上一根发亮的细线自己生长，一格一格地拐弯、分叉、绕成一张网。整条线一直在极小幅度上抖，像被手一帧帧描出来，边缘带淡淡重影。\n- 适合：讲\"从零到一\"、思维发散、系统长成、关系逐渐清晰\n- 关键词：单线、生长、抖动、黑底、重影、分叉\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「逐帧抖动 · 线条生长」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 背景纯黑 #0a0a0a，不做渐变噪点，线是唯一光源\n- 线色 #7ef0c0，线宽 2px，只留一点点微光\n- 从左下的点长成树状或神经状，分叉角 25°-40°\n- 同路径错位描三遍，偏移一两像素，透明度压到四分之一做重影\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-0.6s：起点亮点呼吸两下，暗示有东西要开始\n- 0.6-4s：主线匀速生长，每 0.5s 拐一次弯，拐弯处降速 40%\n- 4-5s：末端分叉四根短线，摊成扇形\n- 5-6s：抖动从 1.5px 收到 0.3px，画面就慢慢定住\n\n技术要点：\n- 每个点每帧加确定性噪声位移，幅度从 1.5 衰减到 0.3\n- 逐点连线，不用贝塞尔——硬拐角才是手绘感的来源\n- 生长靠已绘点数：只画到进度比例对应的那个点\n- 抖动种子按点索引固定，每帧随机会沸腾而不是发抖\n"},{"id":"S5-M17_","name":"手绘 + 实拍混合","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S5-M17_.jpg","markdown":"# M17 手绘 + 实拍混合\n\n> （原库该条目为空，此处为补全内容）\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 情绪和氛围类\n- 长什么样：底层像随手拍的桌面，有柔光、浅景深和轻微手抖；上面压着马克笔涂鸦：圈、箭头、小人。实拍在动，涂鸦稳稳跟着物体走。\n- 适合：Vlog 式口播的画面补充、产品场景标注、吐槽\"这里有个坑\"\n- 关键词：实拍感、手绘叠加、马克笔、景深、批注\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「手绘 + 实拍混合」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 实拍层用径向渐变模拟窗光，背景 blur(6px) 做浅景深\n- 实拍层加三个摄影缺陷：颗粒、四角暗角、手持漂移 2px\n- 手绘层只用白与正红 #e33b3b，线宽 5px，只画圈、箭头、波浪线、火柴人\n- 手绘层不参与漂移，坐标钉在物体上\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1s：实拍层先入，颗粒慢慢浮现，像镜头对焦\n- 1-2.5s：白笔画圈圈住目标物，尾端带回勾\n- 2.5-4s：红笔拉箭头指向画外，箭尖抖两下，冒出摇头小人\n- 4-6s：波浪线划在物体底部，实拍层推近到 1.06，涂鸦淡出收尾\n\n技术要点：\n- 两层分离：实拍层先在离屏 canvas 画好，主循环只 drawImage 加漂移\n- 马克笔笔迹随进度变细（5→3.5），模拟手速造成的墨量变化\n- 圈选不画正圆，用 3 段不同曲率的弧拼接，端点超出 15°\n- 手绘层 2 帧更新一次，实拍层连续运动，时差就是混合感来源\n"},{"id":"S5-M18","name":"数学·科学讲解（3Blue1Brown 那种）","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M18.jpg","markdown":"# M18 数学/科学讲解（3Blue1Brown 那种）\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 讲解类\n- 长什么样：深色背景，公式和图形一点点变形、推导，讲得很清楚。\n- 适合：感知科普线，讲原理、讲数据。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用 Manim 做一个讲 [概念] 的视频，要通俗易懂，有例子，能引人思考。配音用 edge-tts，中文。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[用 Manim 讲导数](https://prompt-motion.com/linearuncle-5d2bae)（原提示词就是中文的）\n\n### 配合 skill\n\nS4 Manim skill。\n"},{"id":"S5-M19","name":"零基础科普","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M19.jpg","markdown":"# M19 零基础科普\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 讲解类\n- 长什么样：不限画风，重点是讲法：从生活里的例子讲起，一步步讲到专业词。\n- 适合：感知科普线的整期，或者感受线里\"先说清楚 X 是什么\"那一段。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n做一个讲 [主题] 的视频，要深入浅出，让一个完全没有相关基础的人也能看懂。先用一个生活里的例子开头，再讲专业的说法。要有声音，中文讲。需要什么工具可以自己装，可以上网查资料。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[给零基础讲 Git](https://prompt-motion.com/yunn260414-60d996)（中文原提示词）、[把一篇文章做成教学视频](https://prompt-motion.com/henkvaness-0e06af)\n"},{"id":"S5-M20","name":"时间线·历史","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M20.jpg","markdown":"# M20 时间线/历史\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 讲解类\n- 长什么样：一条时间轴往前走，每个节点弹出一件事。\n- 适合：\"这三个月我是怎么走过来的\"、\"AI 是怎么发展到今天的\"。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n做一段时间线动画，讲 [从什么时候到什么时候] 的几个关键节点：[节点1、节点2、节点3]。时间轴从左往右推进，每到一个节点，画面放大讲这一件事，再缩回来继续走。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[AI 发展史](https://prompt-motion.com/kloss-xyz-fe0c31)、[像素风的大模型历史](https://prompt-motion.com/pluginplay-3d4006)、[摩洛哥政治史](https://prompt-motion.com/triglyceee-bd5b08)\n"},{"id":"S5-M21","name":"原理拆解（它是怎么工作的）","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M21.jpg","markdown":"# M21 原理拆解（它是怎么工作的）\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 讲解类\n- 长什么样：把一个东西拆开，一步一步演示里面怎么运转。\n- 适合：抓公告那个例子（定时、抓取、筛选、总结、提醒），讲\"它到底是怎么做到的\"。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n做一段动态图形，讲清楚 [东西] 是怎么工作的。把过程拆成 [3 到 5] 步，每一步一个画面，前一步的结果变成下一步的开头，最后合起来看全貌。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[讲橄榄球转播的黄线是怎么来的](https://prompt-motion.com/jayscambler-3f8889)、[讲限流算法](https://prompt-motion.com/parkerrex-1a54fe)、[AI 数据中心里面在干什么](https://prompt-motion.com/sayan-shanky-f850d8)\n"},{"id":"S5-M22","name":"配方式步骤","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M22.jpg","markdown":"# M22 配方式步骤\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 讲解类\n- 长什么样：像做鸡尾酒教程，从空杯子开始，每加一样东西就标出名字和分量，最后成品。\n- 适合：\"你只要这几步就能做出一个小工具\"这种教学段落。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用讲解视频的风格，从 [起点] 一步步做到 [成品]。每加入一样东西，画面上同时标出它叫什么、加了多少。[N] 秒。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[Negroni 鸡尾酒](https://prompt-motion.com/ror-fly-9950f1)、[White Russian](https://prompt-motion.com/sarut0bisasuke-cefa43)\n"},{"id":"S5-M23","name":"五幕讲解（万能讲解模板）","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M23.jpg","markdown":"# M23 五幕讲解（万能讲解模板）\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 讲解类\n- 长什么样：五个画面讲完一件事。\n- 适合：一期科普的骨架，或者感受线里讲一个工具的段落。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n你是一个资深动态设计师。用一个 HTML 页面做一段 [30] 秒的讲解动画，分 5 幕：1）观众遇到的问题；2）[东西] 是什么；3）怎么用，3 步；4）一个真实的例子；5）结尾卡片写 [频道名]。字要大要粗，转场要顺，用我们的配色。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[商业讲解动画](https://prompt-motion.com/alex-prompter-1ea044)\n"},{"id":"S5-M24","name":"四段式发布片","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M24.jpg","markdown":"# M24 四段式发布片\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 讲解类\n- 长什么样：大环境、痛点、解决办法、好处，四段，最后 logo 动画收尾。\n- 适合：介绍一个新工具、一个 skill 的段落。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n你是一个资深动态设计师。做一段 [30] 秒的视频，4 到 5 个场景：为什么现在这件事重要、大家现在的麻烦、[工具] 怎么解决、用了以后的好处。文字逐词出现，用弹簧物理做动作，元素踩着节拍进场，不要突然冒出来。每出现一个元素配一个很轻的音效，其他时候安静。最后用 logo 动画加一句话收尾。做完自己渲染出来逐帧检查，有重叠、被裁掉、太赶的地方改好再给我。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[SaaS 发布片](https://prompt-motion.com/aschapmann-131210)\n"},{"id":"S5-M25","name":"歌词卡点","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M25.jpg","markdown":"# M25 歌词卡点\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 文字和金句类\n- 长什么样：歌词跟着人声和节拍一个字一个字跳出来。\n- 适合：你自己的歌，或者片子里有一段唱的部分。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n根据附上的歌做一个歌词动态视频，歌词和动画要和人声、节拍完全对上。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[歌词卡点](https://prompt-motion.com/samaote-5e2fc2)\n"},{"id":"S5-M26_","name":"副歌段落 · 画面爆炸","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S5-M26_.jpg","markdown":"# M26 副歌段落 · 画面爆炸\n\n> （原库该条目为空，此处为补全内容）\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 节奏和快剪类\n- 长什么样：前两秒压得很低很闷，颜色发灰、画面收窄；副歌一进，画面从中心炸开，色块、圆环、字形碎片往外冲，四拍之内就定住。\n- 适合：歌曲副歌、视频转折高潮、\"忍了很久终于说出来\"的释放\n- 关键词：副歌、爆炸、强弱对比、色块、卡点、冲击\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「副歌段落 · 画面爆炸」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，5 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 主歌底色 #1b1b1f，主色压到灰，暗角收到 60%\n- 副歌底色翻成 #0d0d0d，三色上齐：#ff3b30、#ffd23f、#2ee6a8\n- 爆炸元素只准五类：色块、圆环、星芒、光条、字碎，不超 40 个\n- 只许两个 shockwave 环，不要光晕和粒子\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-2s：中心一个圆缓慢脉动，字只露一半，色压到底\n- 2-2.1s：drop 前所有元素缩到 0.85，屏住一拍\n- 2.1-2.6s：爆炸，元素按极坐标冲出画外，两个环扩散\n- 2.6-5s：元素每 4 帧抖一次，3.4s 后内收 12%，停成满版构成\n\n技术要点：\n- 每个元素的角度、距离、尺寸与色值预先算好，爆炸只做插值\n- 120bpm 每拍 0.5s，爆炸必须落在整拍上\n- 缓动收速极快，冲出后立刻减速才有撞墙感\n- 对比只靠暗角半径和饱和度，别用滤镜硬堆\n"},{"id":"S5-M27","name":"无对白短片，声音讲一半","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M27.jpg","markdown":"# M27 无对白短片，声音讲一半\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 情绪和氛围类（感受线最常用）\n- 长什么样：没有台词，全靠画面和声音讲故事。\n- 适合：片头、片尾，或者两段之间的过渡小片段。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n做一个 [45] 秒的无对白短片，声音要讲出一半的故事。故事你来编，主题是 [主题]。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[守灯塔的人](https://prompt-motion.com/kamstudiolabs-0b0824)\n"},{"id":"S5-M28","name":"对称暖色的电影感空镜","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M28.jpg","markdown":"# M28 对称暖色的电影感空镜\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 情绪和氛围类（感受线最常用）\n- 长什么样：画面左右对称，粉嫩暖色，像一个精致的小舞台。比如火车窗外四季流过，桌上一杯咖啡。\n- 适合：讲时间流逝、讲停下来想一想的段落。比如\"停播这段时间\"。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n火车窗外四季流过，车厢很温暖，桌上放着一杯咖啡。画面左右对称，粉嫩暖色，像精致的舞台布景，镜头很稳。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[窗外四季](https://prompt-motion.com/itsolelehmann-e47532)（原提示词点名了一部电影的风格，我改成了直接描述长相）\n"},{"id":"S5-M29","name":"日落天空的 3D 空镜加一句话","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M29.jpg","markdown":"# M29 日落天空的 3D 空镜加一句话\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 情绪和氛围类（感受线最常用）\n- 长什么样：3D 场景，日落、紫色的云、海面反光，镜头慢慢飞，画面上写一句短话。\n- 适合：情绪最高的那一句话，比如\"现在不就是最好的时候吗\"。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用网页 3D 做一个日落的海面，紫色积云、海面反光，镜头像动画电影那样慢慢飞过去。画面上写一句：[一句话]。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[日落空战](https://prompt-motion.com/nft-chen-5f8bb0)（中文原提示词）\n"},{"id":"S5-M30","name":"东方意境","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M30.jpg","markdown":"# M30 东方意境\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 情绪和氛围类（感受线最常用）\n- 长什么样：古典诗词里的画面，比如月夜、江水、楼阁，可以配可交互的网页。\n- 适合：讲心境、讲文化的段落，也适合做片头。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n根据你对我的了解，选一个我可能喜欢的东方古典意境（比如月夜、江水、楼阁），做一个 [30] 秒的视频，再做一个可以交互的网页版。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[滕王阁月夜江景](https://prompt-motion.com/demitiyageekzen-7770ad)（中文原提示词）\n"},{"id":"S5-M31","name":"粘土定格 MV","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M31.jpg","markdown":"# M31 粘土定格 MV\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 情绪和氛围类（感受线最常用）\n- 长什么样：粘土小人一帧一帧动，手作感很强。\n- 适合：轻松、好玩的段落；你自己的歌也可以做成这种 MV。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n做一个 [60] 秒的粘土定格动画风格的音乐视频，粘土小人有手指印和手作质感，动作一顿一顿的。顺便写一首适合这个画面的原创歌。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[粘土风 MV](https://prompt-motion.com/davidshulmanfl-371638)\n"},{"id":"S5-M32","name":"竖屏快节奏 POV","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M32.jpg","markdown":"# M32 竖屏快节奏 POV\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 节奏和快剪类\n- 长什么样：竖屏，节奏很快、很碎，第一人称视角，动作和音效都很满。\n- 适合：竖屏版的开头 3 秒，抓住人。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n竖屏 9:16，快节奏碎剪，第一人称视角，讲 [主题]，动作和音效都要做得很好，加一点你自己的性格。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[AI 视角的快剪](https://prompt-motion.com/kloss-xyz-15182a)\n"},{"id":"S5-M33","name":"从一个点拉远到宇宙","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M33.jpg","markdown":"# M33 从一个点拉远到宇宙\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 结构和镜头类（用来把不同风格串起来）\n- 长什么样：黑屏上一个点，慢慢展开成画面，再一直拉远，整个桌面变成太空里的一个点，再推进到另一个画面。\n- 适合：讲尺度、讲\"从一个小工具到一个系统\"。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n从黑屏上的一个点开始，展开成 [画面]，镜头在好几个尺度上推拉，一直拉远到整个画面像太空里的一个点，再推进到 [另一个画面]，最后落到 [一句话]。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[交易终端](https://prompt-motion.com/dale-vaz-cc612a)\n"},{"id":"S5-M34","name":"新闻快讯盘点","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M34.jpg","markdown":"# M34 新闻快讯盘点\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 节奏和快剪类\n- 长什么样：像新闻节目一样，一条一条过，每条点出看点。\n- 适合：以后做\"这周 AI 圈有什么新东西\"这种盘点。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n做一个约 60 秒、好看好懂的动态图形，像新闻快讯那样介绍 [时间段] 里 [领域] 的新东西，从普通用户的角度说清楚每一条有意思在哪。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[Steam 新游盘点](https://prompt-motion.com/gigabit-million-16b3a0)（日文原提示词）\n"},{"id":"S5-M35_","name":"数据播报 · 数字滚动","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S5-M35_.jpg","markdown":"# M35 数据播报 · 数字滚动\n\n> （原库该条目为空，此处为补全内容）\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 节奏和快剪类\n- 长什么样：深蓝新闻底，一个大数字在中央逐位滚动，像机场翻牌从旧值翻到新值，底部跑马灯不停刷补充信息。差不多 1.2 秒换一条。\n- 适合：年度盘点、增长数据、榜单排名、财报与成绩单\n- 关键词：数字、滚动、新闻底、跑马灯、等宽字体、播报\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「数据播报 · 数字滚动」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，5 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 背景 #0f1b2d 深蓝，叠 0.03 的 45° 斜纹，左上角放栏目标签\n- 主数字用等宽字体，字高占画面 42%\n- 强调色 #38bdf8 只给单位、箭头和分隔线\n- 底部 ticker 高 56px，字 20px，匀速左移\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-0.4s：栏目条从左滑入，斜纹淡入\n- 0.4-1.6s：数字从 0000 滚到目标值，高位先停、个位最后停\n- 1.6-1.9s：定住，口径小字淡入，下划线从左画出\n- 1.9-3.4s：换第二条，旧数上移出画，新数从下顶入\n- 3.4-5s：第三条带 ↑ 和百分比，箭头上弹 6px 收尾\n\n技术要点：\n- 逐位滚动不是插值，每位独立算进度取整\n- 每位数字用 clip 矩形遮住，只露 42px 高的窗口\n- ticker 两段相同内容首尾相接，取模循环，接缝不跳帧\n- 变化量用整数百分比，小数尾巴别上屏\n"},{"id":"S5-M36","name":"破框","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M36.jpg","markdown":"# M36 破框\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 文字和金句类\n- 长什么样：一张海报里的东西冲出了海报的边框。\n- 适合：\"跳出原来的想法\"、\"不再觉得跟我没关系\"这种转折。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n做一段动态设计：一张海报里的 [东西] 冲出海报自己的边框。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[海报破框](https://prompt-motion.com/pankajkumar-dev-0bee87)\n"},{"id":"S5-M37","name":"Logo 慢慢拼出来（片头片尾）","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M37.jpg","markdown":"# M37 Logo 慢慢拼出来（片头片尾）\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 结构和镜头类（用来把不同风格串起来）\n- 长什么样：碎片慢慢聚拢，拼成 logo。\n- 适合：每期的片头或片尾，做一次以后反复用。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n做一段慢慢揭示的转场：碎片一点点聚拢，最后拼出你的 logo。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[Logo 揭示](https://prompt-motion.com/tdinh-me-815acb)\n"},{"id":"S5-M38","name":"一个形状变到底","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S5-M38.jpg","markdown":"# M38 一个形状变到底\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 结构和镜头类（用来把不同风格串起来）\n- 长什么样：同一个形状不停变：按钮、加载圈、播放器、滑块、开关……每次变都是鼠标真的点了一下。\n- 适合：一口气列很多例子的段落，比如\"整理表格、写周报、批量改文件、做自媒体\"。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n一个形状从头到尾不切，依次变成 [例子1、例子2、例子3……]，变的是大小、圆角和颜色。每次变化都由鼠标真的点击或拖动触发。到处用弹簧，最多一点点回弹。镜头跟着放大，让每个状态填满画面。最后一帧和第一帧一样。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 参考\n\n[一个形状变遍界面](https://prompt-motion.com/twoclipping-5cba86)\n"},{"id":"S5-M39_","name":"形状 × 文字联动","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S5-M39_.jpg","markdown":"# M39 形状 × 文字联动\n\n> （原库该条目为空，此处为补全内容）\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 结构和镜头类\n- 长什么样：一个几何形状和一段文字互为模具，字沿着轮廓排布，形状一变字就跟着重排。圆收成方、方拉成箭头，字始终贴着边缘走。\n- 适合：一句话观点的强化、栏目标题、概念定义、\"XX 是什么\"开场\n- 关键词：形状、轮廓排字、联动、变形、吸附、负空间\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「形状 × 文字联动」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 背景 #f7f5f0，形状与文字统一 #141414，只留强调色 #ff5a1f\n- 形状在圆、方、三角、箭头之间变形，用同一条路径的锚点插值\n- 文字沿轮廓排布，字距按弧长均分，曲率大处字号缩 10%\n- 形状内部保持空，字只排在外圈，靠负空间立住\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-1.2s：圆从中心长出，12 字沿圆周依次落位\n- 1.2-2.6s：圆变方，圆角 50% 收到 8%，字被挤到四条边上\n- 2.6-4s：方变三角，字沿斜边重排，右下两字被挤出去再弹回\n- 4-5.5s：三角变箭头，字全聚到两条长边上\n- 5.5-6s：形状缩成一点，字收成一行小字居中\n\n技术要点：\n- 四个关键形状写成 Path2D，按 t 插值后采样成 120 个点\n- 排字按弧长定位，到点放字并旋到切线角\n- 字加 0.1s 的 stagger，形状先动、字后跟\n- 变形瞬间闪 2 帧强调色，别做渐变\n"},{"id":"S5-M40_","name":"动态风格总结 · 蒙太奇","series":"S5","hasStill":false,"patched":true,"still":"stills/S5-M40_.jpg","markdown":"# M40 动态风格总结 · 蒙太奇\n\n> （原库该条目为空，此处为补全内容）\n\n- 分类：M 动态风格 · 结构和镜头类\n- 长什么样：十二个小镜头快切，每格 0.4 秒，各是一种风格的高度浓缩版。它们被同一个小圆点串起来，最后收成一个字。\n- 适合：风格合集开场、作品集片头、课程总览、年度回顾\n- 关键词：蒙太奇、快切、合集、串场、收束、总览\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用代码（Canvas 2D）画一段「动态风格总结 · 蒙太奇」风格的动画，16:9，30fps，6.5 秒。\n\n画面规则：\n- 全片只用两色：底 #101014 加前景 #f2f2f2\n- 十二格依次是：字雨、粒子、剪纸、霓虹、网格、墨点、色块、线条、数字、玻璃、颗粒、留白\n- 共享元素是一枚 6px 圆点，每格换身份：一粒字、一个质点、一处镂空，位置始终在同一点\n- 每格只给一个动作，不运镜、不叠加\n\n动画节奏：\n- 0-4.8s：十二格，每格 0.4s，硬切不转场\n- 第 3、7、11 格前各插 1 帧白闪\n- 4.8-5.4s：圆点放大，吸走残留元素\n- 5.4-6.5s：圆点炸成一个字，停 4 帧后收黑\n\n技术要点：\n- 每格一个独立函数，主循环按 `Math.floor(t / 0.4)` 分发，12 帧一格\n- 圆点坐标作为参数传进每个函数，永远同一个值，串场才成立\n- 白闪用整屏透明度占 1 帧，别用 CSS 动画，会和时序错位\n- 十二格共用 seeded PRNG，每次渲染同一帧必须一致\n"},{"id":"S6-O1","name":"翼","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O1.jpg","markdown":"# O1 翼（Wings）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 默片与有声 1927–1938\n- 长什么样：用 three.js 搭的一战空战，按 1927 年正色片拷贝调色：天空发白、黑位很深、整体琥珀染色、片门抖动和划痕。《翼》最出名的是把摄影机绑在飞机上拍的空战，所以这里大部分机位都装在飞机上。\n- 风格：默片 3D 空战 · 正色片黑白 + 琥珀染色\n- 关键词：3D·B&W\n- 年份：1927/28\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n平滑着色的 3D 按默片正色片拷贝调色：高反差，红色发黑。天空近白，地面留清楚的中灰层次，深黑占画面 25%–35%，只在剪影里。染色：白位向琥珀 #FFCC80 混一半，天空是淡奶油琥珀，不要整片橙；黑位不变。只有故事围绕的那个主角留色，整个人都留，饱和度约七成（如 #D97757）；灯火和其他光源一律是灰的。叠 24 fps 片门抖动（±1 像素）、闪烁（±5%）、细颗粒（±4%）、一到三条细划痕、稀疏灰尘，片门圆角加柔和暗角，第一镜 1 秒圆形光圈打开。招牌镜头：机位绑在跟主角一起动的东西上，露出一截支架，轻微震动；再接跟拍和主观镜头。每 2–3 秒一切，穿过云、烟或强光白场转场。对标 William Wellman《翼》（1927）。避免：运动模糊、灰阶里有颜色。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nSmooth-shaded 3D graded as a silent-era orthochromatic print: high contrast, reds go black. Sky near-white, ground in readable mid-greys, deep blacks (25-35% of frame) only in silhouettes. Tint: blend whites half-way toward amber #FFCC80, so the sky is pale cream-amber, never solid orange; blacks stay black. Only the hero the story centres on keeps colour, whole figure at ~70% saturation (e.g. #D97757); every lamp, fire or other light source stays grey. 24 fps gate weave (±1 px), flicker (±5%), fine grain (±4%), 1-3 thin scratches, sparse dust, rounded corners, vignette; open on a 1 s iris. Signature shot: a camera rigged to something moving with the subject, rig in frame; then chase and POV. Cut every 2-3 s; white out through cloud, smoke or glare. Reference: William Wellman, *Wings* (1927). Avoid: motion blur, colour in the greys.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\nthree.js 三维,正色片黑白,默片染色,放映磨损和圆形光圈\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮美军飞行员：皮飞行帽、护目镜、白围巾，坐在 SPAD 座舱里。开场序幕接到第一镜，第 1 拍光圈打开；之后五刀硬切，大约两拍一切。第 5 拍前后它回头、机枪开火；最后穿云白场，接上第 2 部开头的奶油白。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 机头正前方对着座舱：Clawd 戴皮飞行帽、护目镜推在额头，夹在两根支柱中间；圆形光圈打开，镜头缓缓推近。（0.9s）\n2. 大全景：机群在云间盘旋，下面是格子田和白垩战壕，一架福克拖着黑烟；镜头缓缓推近。（0.6s）\n3. 机头机位带发动机震动：Clawd 回头看咬尾的福克，抬起一只手，双挺维克斯机枪开火闪光。（0.8s）\n4. 从福克上翼看出去，敌机机枪开火，追着前方 Clawd 的 SPAD；机位固定在敌机上。（0.8s）\n5. 过 Clawd 肩头的俯冲镜头：前面是上翼和张线，弹坑和折线战壕迎面扑来。（1.1s）\n6. 云团里的 SPAD 像幽灵一样朝镜头飞来，雾越来越浓，画面白到奶油色。（0.8s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nWilliam A. Wellman, 1927,Harry Perry (aerial photography),Tinted silent-era release prints,RFC aerial photographs of the Somme, 1916–18\n"},{"id":"S6-O2","name":"百老汇旋律","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O2.jpg","markdown":"# O2 百老汇旋律（The Broadway Melody）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 默片与有声 1927–1938\n- 长什么样：会动的黑金银装饰艺术歌舞海报：喷枪渐变、阶梯形台口、镀铬电容麦克风。1929 年的百老汇正是装饰艺术盛期，这部又是有声片降临的一部，所以让声波从麦克风里一圈圈放出来。\n- 风格：黑金银装饰艺术歌舞海报\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1928/29\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n装饰艺术歌舞海报。黑漆底；金和银都用分带金属渐变做（每个面 3–5 条硬分界），喷枪光晕、放射线、阶梯形、流线型，场景外圈套一道层层退进的金线台口框，嵌一排灯泡。色板按用途：底 #0A0908，金 #D6B560，高光 #F3E2A2，银 #BFC4C9；皮肤用淡银奶油色；只有故事围绕的那个主角用一个饱和强调色（如 #D97757）。人物是 Erté 式修长造型，有五官表情，题材允许时加流苏、亮片或羽毛。细颗粒、闪光点、星星。正面对称，中间一束追光。动起来：灯泡流动，一排重复元素（舞者、浪头）向中心卷成波浪，主要声源或光源放射环纹；镜头慢推，踩音乐的拍子切，无乐每 2–3 秒一切。对标 A. M. Cassandre 的海报、Erté 的服装设计。避免：平涂黄色冒充金属、人物没脸、多余的颜色。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nArt Deco revue poster. Black lacquer ground; gold and silver from banded metallic gradients (3-5 hard bands); airbrush glows, sunbursts, ziggurat steps, streamlined curves; a stepped proscenium frame of gold lines and bulbs. Palette: ground #0A0908, gold #D6B560, highlight #F3E2A2, silver #BFC4C9; skin pale silver-cream; one saturated accent (e.g. #D97757) only on the hero the story centres on. Elongated Erté-style figures with real faces; fringe, sequins or feathers where the subject allows. Fine grain, glints, stars. Frontal symmetry, a central spotlight. Motion: chasing bulbs, a row of repeated elements (dancers, waves, columns) rippling toward the centre, rings radiating from the main sound or light source; slow pushes, cut on the music's beat (without music, every 2-3 s). References: A. M. Cassandre's posters, Erté's costumes. Avoid: flat yellow for metal, faceless figures, extra hues.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n装饰艺术海报,喷枪金属渐变,A. M. Cassandre,Erté 服装\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 是戴硬草帽、拿黑手杖的踢踏舞者，从头踢踏到尾。开头从第 1 部的白场里淡入；第 4 拍麦克风活过来，声波涌出（有声片来了）。四刀硬切：街口 → 舞台 → 麦克风 → 歌舞队 → Clawd。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 从白场里淡入：Astor 剧院的雨棚招牌（THE BROADWAY MELODY / ALL TALKING · ALL SINGING · ALL DANCING），灯泡流动；Clawd 戴硬草帽、拿手杖站在左边；镜头缓缓拉远。（0.8s）\n2. 舞台全景：装饰艺术台口、台阶上的歌舞女郎，Clawd 在巨大的电容麦克风旁踢踏，麦克风开始一圈圈放出声波；镜头缓缓拉远。（1s）\n3. 镀铬弹簧环麦克风特写，声波放射、音符飞出；镜头推近。（0.5s）\n4. 沿着踢腿队列横移：羽毛头饰、亮片短裙，腿一排排踢成波浪。（0.5s）\n5. Clawd 近景，踢踏中草帽一歪一歪，身后是麦克风环；轻推。（0.5s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nHarry Beaumont, 1929,1929 MGM one-sheet,A. M. Cassandre,Erté\n"},{"id":"S6-O3","name":"西线无战事","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O3.jpg","markdown":"# O3 西线无战事（All Quiet on the Western Front）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 默片与有声 1927–1938\n- 长什么样：珂勒惠支式木刻：大块的黑、刀刻的白轮廓、粗糙木纹，Clawd 用单独一块橙色版套印。珂勒惠支 1921–22 年的反战版画，和这部片出自同一场战争、同一种悲痛。\n- 风格：珂勒惠支式反战木刻\n- 关键词：2D·B&W\n- 年份：1929/30\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n仿凯绥·珂勒惠支《战争》组画（1921–22）的表现主义木刻。画面六到七成是实黑；形体之间用白色轮廓刀分开，刀宽为画幅宽的 1/300–1/150；人脸刻成白形，只留几笔黑五官；手要大。人物占满画面，被画框裁切。每刀都有尖的进刀收刀和碎木边，离光越近的刀越宽，刀痕成组排、节奏略不均。墨色 #17130F 印在 #E8DFCA 纸上；纹理只有木纹条和缺墨斑点（±5%）。只有故事围绕的那个主角套一块色版（如 #D97757），平铺在身体或衣服上，刀痕照样压在上面，黑仍占六成以上。光只从一个开口进来（窗、云缝）；灯只刻一小圈短刀痕。人物脚下的地面留不刻的黑。动作安静（呼吸、一个小手势）；慢摇、慢推。避免：灰雾灰调、排刀规整成条纹、血腥。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nExpressionist woodcut after Käthe Kollwitz, *Krieg* (1921-22). 60-70% solid black; white contour cuts 1/300-1/150 of frame width separate forms; faces are white shapes with a few black features; hands large. Figures fill the frame, cropped by its edges. Each cut tapers in and out with chipped edges, is wider the nearer it sits to the light, in slightly uneven bundles. Ink #17130F on paper #E8DFCA; texture is only wood-grain streaks and under-inked speckle (±5%). One colour block (e.g. #D97757) only on the hero the story centres on, flat on its body or clothes, cuts over it; black stays 60%+. Light enters through one opening (window, cloud gap); lamps get a short ring of cuts. The ground under figures stays uncut black. Quiet motion (breathing, a small gesture); slow pans and push-ins. Avoid: grey fog or midtones, even striped hatching, gore.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n木刻版画,凯绥·珂勒惠支,德国表现主义,双版套印\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮保罗，戴德军钢盔。整段放慢（1.2 倍）、压得很静：第 3 拍前后开始伸手，用三拍慢慢伸到底；幻影士兵只在最后一镜出现。四刀硬切：战壕里的脸 → 保罗的手臂 → 蝴蝶 → 十字架墓地。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 沿战壕横摇，扫过刻出来的脸：一个双手捂脸，一个惊恐地看着，一个睡着了。（0.7s）\n2. 钢盔下一张直视镜头的脸（致敬 1930 年原海报）；慢推。（0.4s）\n3. 中景：Clawd 戴德军钢盔站在射击台上，慢慢把手臂伸向射击孔，孔沿铁罐上停着一只白蝴蝶；镜头推近。（0.9s）\n4. 射击孔边白蝴蝶特写，Clawd 的橙色手臂从左边伸进来；推近。（0.7s）\n5. 大全景：照明弹下的无人地带和白十字架墓地，幻影士兵的队列淡入、往远处走去；缓缓拉远。（0.6s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nLewis Milestone, 1930,Käthe Kollwitz, Krieg (1921–22),1930 Universal poster\n"},{"id":"S6-O4","name":"壮志千秋","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O4.jpg","markdown":"# O4 壮志千秋（Cimarron）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 默片与有声 1927–1938\n- 长什么样：1889 年的金调色蛋白印相全景：柔焦、轻微的横向拖影、奶油高光和暖褐暗部、银盐颗粒，只有 Clawd 手工上色。片子开场就是 1889 年俄克拉何马圈地狂奔，用那一年的照片质感来拍，这一章也保持单色。\n- 风格：1889 棕褐蛋白印相全景\n- 关键词：2D·Sepia\n- 年份：1930/31\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n会动的 1880 年代金调色蛋白照片。先画边缘柔和的平涂形，按亮度分：主体深（15%–30%），地面 50%–60%，天空约 90%；物面和草丛有看得见的排线（线宽约画幅宽 1/300）。再“印”：转灰度，轻柔焦（约画幅宽 1/800），三层横向错开的淡影随运镜拉长，乘奶油色 #F2DCB4，黑位抬成暖褐 #2E1C0F，显影不匀（±5%），暗角压到 #785634，每帧刷新颗粒（±4%），稀疏灰尘划痕。只给故事围绕的那个主角手工上色（如 #D97757），像透明染料，蒙版羽化 2–3 像素；灯光仍是棕褐。横向全景，中间一条带排满重复小元素：人物、动物、车辆，题材冷清就用篱笆、木桩、树；别让一整块大面占满画面。天空默认发白。运镜：从主角近景 4–5 秒缓出拉到全景。避免：硬墨线、主角以外的颜色、干净的数码天空、发灰的印相。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nA moving 1880s gold-toned albumen photograph. Paint soft-edged flat shapes by brightness: main subjects dark (15-30%), ground 50-60%, sky about 90%; visible hatching and grass tufts (lines ~1/300 of frame width). Then print: grayscale, slight blur (~1/800 of frame width), a faint smear of three horizontal offset copies growing with camera speed, multiply cream #F2DCB4, lift blacks to umber #2E1C0F, blotchy development (±5%), vignette to #785634, per-frame grain (±4%), dust, scratches. Hand-tint only the hero the story centres on (e.g. #D97757) as transparent dye, mask feathered 2-3 px; lamps stay sepia. Wide panorama: the middle band packed with repeated small elements (figures, animals, vehicles; for a lone subject fences, posts, trees), no big plane dominating. Camera: one 4-5 s ease-out pull back from the hero to the panorama. Avoid: hard ink outlines, colour beyond the hero, a flat grey print.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n金调色蛋白印相,西部全景老照片,人物手工上色\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮扬西·克拉瓦特：白色宽边牛仔帽、领巾，骑黑马冲在最前。整段按 1.1 倍跑。先拉远看到更多的马，再一刀切回近景，第 5 拍拔枪朝天开火。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 从骑黑马冲在最前的 Clawd 近景开始，一路拉到全景：篷车、驿站马车、尘土里一大片骑手。（1.5s）\n2. 切到 Clawd 骑马的近景：举起左轮朝天开枪，画面随着后坐力一震；轻推。（1s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nWesley Ruggles, 1931,Gold-toned albumen prints, 1880s,Currier & Ives (the flying gallop)\n"},{"id":"S6-O5","name":"大饭店","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O5.jpg","markdown":"# O5 大饭店（Grand Hotel）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 默片与有声 1927–1938\n- 长什么样：用 three.js 建的圆形大堂，按米高梅的银色黑白调色，Clawd 的橙是唯一的颜色。这部片最出名的是从顶上笔直往下拍、回廊一圈圈收进去的镜头，整个布景就按这一镜来建。\n- 风格：米高梅银色黑白 3D 大堂\n- 关键词：3D·B&W\n- 年份：1931/32\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n真 3D 场景，默认装饰艺术风大堂（大理石、镀铬、吊灯、亮面棋盘格地面）；室外保留真实场景，表面做出光泽（湿路面、静水），构图对称。环境光遮蔽加柔和辉光，调成 30 年代片厂银色黑白：约四成画面深黑，白位亮度约九成，不发灰。光从一个高处光源成束落下。只有故事围绕的那个主角留色（如 #D97757），至少占画面高度六分之一；暖灯渲染成白光，辉光只在灯旁。颗粒（±3%），灯旁光晕。招牌镜头：平视大全景，望进对称纵深，地面和天花同时入画，地面有倒影；再接 4–6 秒的侧向跟拍；俯拍同心圆、人像棋子只用一次。年代人物有头、帽、四肢，按路线走。对标美术指导 Cedric Gibbons、摄影师 William Daniels。避免：原地踏步、抽象图案铺满画面、偏色。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nA real 3D set, by default an Art Deco interior (marble, chrome, chandelier, glossy checkerboard floor); outdoors keep the real setting with glossy surfaces (wet pavement, still water) and symmetrical framing. Ambient occlusion, soft bloom; 1930s studio silver: about 40% deep black, glossy whites near 90%, no washed-out greys. Light falls in shafts from one high source. All neutral grey except the hero the story centres on (e.g. #D97757), at least 1/6 of frame height; warm lamps render white, bloom only around them. Grain (±3%), halation. Signature shot: eye-height wide into a deep symmetrical set, floor and ceiling in frame, floor reflections; then 4-6 s tracks; one overhead on rings, figures as chess pieces. Period figures (heads, hats, limbs) walk routes. References: art director Cedric Gibbons, cinematographer William Daniels. Avoid: walking in place, frame-filling abstract patterns, colour casts.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\nthree.js 三维,米高梅银色黑白,装饰艺术室内,俯拍同心构图\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮门童，戴圆筒小帽，拎一只贴满旅馆标签的皮箱。它是真的在走：每秒 1.4 米、2.4 步，步子和位移对得上。整段 1:1 播放，1.08 秒处从俯拍一刀切到侧面跟拍。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 从天窗下笔直往下看：六层回廊一圈圈收向钥匙塔，橙色门童是棋盘格大理石上唯一的一个点，正走过地面；镜头慢慢推近。（1.1s）\n2. 贴着 Clawd 身高的侧面跟拍：它拎着皮箱往前走，大堂从身后滑过，前方旋转门在转；镜头边跟边往前、往里靠。（1.4s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nEdmund Goulding, 1932,Cedric Gibbons (art direction),William Daniels (cinematography)\n"},{"id":"S6-O6","name":"乱世春秋","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O6.jpg","markdown":"# O6 乱世春秋（Cavalcade）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 默片与有声 1927–1938\n- 长什么样：慕夏式新艺术装饰画：膨胀的褐色轮廓线、粉彩平涂、马赛克底，中心人物身后一个光环。电影开场就是 1899 年跨年夜一家人举杯迎接新世纪，所以光环做成敲十二点的钟面。\n- 风格：慕夏新艺术装饰画\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1932/33\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n慕夏式彩色石印。褐色 #4B3326 轮廓线，宽约画幅宽 1/250，背光侧（光默认从左上来）加粗到两倍，两端收尖；粉彩平涂，每块在背光侧叠一层深一档的硬边色。色板：奶油 #F5ECD6 作纸和天空，鼠尾草绿 #A9B490 作植物衣褶，淡金 #CFA55A 作光环装饰，玫瑰 #DBA49C 作皮肤和花；故事围绕的那个主角用一个更浓的强调色（如 #D97757）。横幅：人物和细环纹大光环居中三分之一，两侧拱格，后面铺灰青（鼠尾草绿掺灰）方块马赛克；鞭线藤蔓和花草；底部一条衬线大写铭文，用用户语言，字高约画面 1/25。留白不超过画面八分之一。杏仁眼、厚眼睑；头发或衣料三色成束。石版颗粒、纸斑、暗角。慢摆；下摇到光环再拉远。避免：干净细线、饱和色、空荡背景。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nAlphonse Mucha colour lithograph. Brown contours #4B3326, about 1/250 of frame width, doubling on the shadow side (light from upper left), tapered ends; pastel flats, each with a hard-edged darker shadow tone. Palette: cream #F5ECD6 paper/sky, sage #A9B490 foliage/drapery, pale gold #CFA55A halo/ornament, rose #DBA49C skin/flowers; the hero the story centres on in one stronger accent (e.g. #D97757). Wide frame: figure and a big ring-filled halo in the middle third, arched panels either side, behind them a muted-teal (sage plus grey) square-tile mosaic; whiplash vines, local flowers; a bottom band of serif capitals in the user's language, 1/25 of frame height. No plain area over 1/8 of frame. Almond eyes, heavy lids; hair or cloth in three-tone strands. Litho grain, paper mottle, vignette. Slow sway; tilt down to the halo, pull back. Avoid: thin clean lines, saturated colour, empty backgrounds.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n阿尔丰斯·慕夏,彩色石印,新艺术装饰纹样\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 是举杯的马里奥特家一员：丝质高礼帽、白领结、手持香槟碟。第 3 拍钟敲十二点、光环一闪，紧接着切到举杯，Clawd 一跳把杯子举起来。1:1 播放，两刀硬切，最后一个拉远停在整张海报上。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 从 CAVALCADE 标题和奔马横饰带往下摇到钟面光环并推近；指针合到 XII，敲钟那一下光环一闪。（1.1s）\n2. 举杯中景：戴高礼帽、白领结的 Clawd 一跳，举起香槟碟，两侧的简和罗伯特也举杯；轻拉。（0.8s）\n3. 拉远到整幅慕夏装饰画：三只杯子举着，玫瑰花瓣飘落，底部是 LONDON · XXXI DECEMBER MDCCCXCIX · A NEW CENTURY 铭文带。（0.6s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nFrank Lloyd, 1933,Alphonse Mucha, The Seasons (1896),Alphonse Mucha, Zodiac (1896),Fox 1933 poster (the galloping horses)\n"},{"id":"S6-O7","name":"一夜风流","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O7.jpg","markdown":"# O7 一夜风流（It Happened One Night）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 默片与有声 1927–1938\n- 长什么样：1934 年的黑白橡皮管卡通：粗细变化的墨线、饼切眼、白手套，赛璐璐平涂叠在灰阶水彩背景上，带胶片颗粒。和电影同一年，公路喜剧的笑点也适合卡通的节奏。\n- 风格：30 年代黑白橡皮管卡通\n- 关键词：2D·B&W\n- 年份：1934\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n30 年代初的黑白橡皮管卡通。角色：软管四肢、白手套、饼切眼、圆脑袋，三到四头身；墨线 #141414 粗细变化（画幅宽 1/400–1/150），背光一侧变粗（默认光从左上）；角色平涂只用纯黑、纯白，加至多一种浅灰作硬阴影，黑面一道高光。背景只用中间灰（明度 30%–80%），不用纯黑白：铅笔线不擦，灰色水彩罩染，边缘积水有颗粒沉淀。只有故事围绕的那个主角带颜色（如 #D97757）：整个身体，主角是人时只给关键道具。加 ±1 像素片门抖动、24 fps 颗粒（±4%）、闪烁、划痕、灰尘。动作都有预备、冲过头再回落：落地压扁、快动拉长、吃惊时弹眼，碰撞时镜头轻震。对标弗莱舍工作室的 Betty Boop 短片、迪士尼《糊涂交响曲》的背景。避免：光滑矢量线、角色发灰和背景糊在一起。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nEarly-1930s black-and-white rubber-hose cartoon. Characters: noodle limbs, white gloves, pie-cut eyes, round heads; thick-thin ink #141414 (1/400-1/150 of frame width) swelling away from the light (default upper left); fills pure black and white plus at most one light grey for the hard shadow, gloss on blacks. Backgrounds stay mid-grey (30-80%), never pure black/white: graphite lines left in, grey watercolour washes with pooled edges and granulation, paper grain. Only the hero the story centres on has colour (e.g. #D97757): its whole body, or its one key prop if the hero is human. ±1 px gate weave, grain (±4%), flicker, scratches, dust. Actions anticipate, overshoot, settle: squash on landing, stretch on fast moves, eyes pop when surprised, a camera jolt on hits. References: Fleischer's Betty Boop shorts; Disney's Silly Symphonies backgrounds. Avoid: smooth vector lines, grey characters merging with the background.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n弗莱舍橡皮管,赛璐璐上色,糊涂交响曲水彩背景,放映胶片磨损\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮彼得：灰色软呢帽、松开的条纹领带、一撇小胡子，手臂末端戴白手套。第 3–4 拍艾莉撩裙子，第 5 拍车急刹停下（只有这一下放回 1 倍速），Clawd 的目瞪口呆正好卡在这一下。两刀硬切：拇指 → 双人 → 收尾。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. Clawd 竖起的白手套大拇指和得意的脸，身后是空路，一辆车从坡顶开过来；慢推。（0.7s）\n2. 双人镜头：Clawd 在路边竖拇指，艾莉坐在栅栏上把裙摆撩到小腿；轻拉。（0.7s）\n3. 收尾全景：车在两人中间急刹，司机眼珠弹出来；Clawd 瞪大眼、张开双手，帽子跳起；急停那一下镜头一震。（1.1s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nFrank Capra, 1934,Fleischer Studios, Betty Boop (1932–34),Disney, Silly Symphonies backgrounds,1934 Columbia one-sheet\n"},{"id":"S6-O8","name":"叛舰喋血记","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O8.jpg","markdown":"# O8 叛舰喋血记（Mutiny on the Bounty）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 默片与有声 1927–1938\n- 长什么样：18 世纪英国海军部海图的铜版画：恒向线、罗盘玫瑰、水深数字、手工淡彩，邦蒂号是图上的一幅船舶插画。这部片讲的是一次航行，所以整段都留在海图上。\n- 风格：海军部海图铜版画\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1935\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n18 世纪末的铜版地图，默认是英国海军部海图（内陆题材换成测绘图）。明暗全靠刻刀细排线和交叉排线，线宽约画幅宽的 1/1200；线宽设下限，细小处变暗而不是消失。墨色 #1C1610 印在 #EFE3C4 直纹纸上，带淡霉斑和折痕。只上很薄的手工淡彩：沿水边的海蓝 #9EBCBC，陆地 #B8C08C；故事围绕的那个主角（如 #D97757）是唯一的浓色。32 向罗盘玫瑰拉出恒向线网、刻度边框、晕滃山形、装饰图名框、一个装饰物（如海怪）、衬线字。情节放进一幅不加边框的排线插画，排线向纸面渐隐；推到底时插画占画面高度 60%–75%。运镜：一个 5–6 秒的镜头从整张图滑进插画，四周留着地图；一条虚线路线自己画出来。避免：切出纸面、浓重水彩、僵硬的人物。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nA late-18th-century copperplate map, default an Admiralty sea chart (inland: a survey map). Fine burin hatching and cross-hatching (lines ~1/1200 of frame width) give all tone; a minimum line width makes small detail darken, not vanish. Ink #1C1610 on laid paper #EFE3C4, light foxing, folds. Thin hand colour: water #9EBCBC along edges, land #B8C08C; the hero the story centres on (e.g. #D97757) is the one strong colour. Rhumb lines from 32-point roses, a graduated border, hachured hills, cartouche, one ornament (e.g. a sea monster), serif lettering. Put the action in an unboxed engraved vignette fading into the chart, filling 60-75% of frame height at the end of the move. Camera: one 5-6 s glide from the whole sheet into the vignette, chart still around it; a dotted route inks itself in. Avoid: cropping the paper, heavy washes, stiff figures.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n18 世纪海军部海图,铜版线刻,手工淡彩,Robert Dodd 的版画\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮弗莱彻·克里斯蒂安：横戴双角帽、手持弯刀。整段不离开海图，一个镜头滑进去，不切。第 4 拍举刀，紧接着刀锋一闪指向小艇；最后几拍小艇往船尾漂开。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 一镜到底：从整张海图开始，邦蒂号沿虚线航迹前进，镜头滑进图上的船舶插画，四周仍是恒向线和罗盘玫瑰；甲板上 Clawd 举起弯刀、指着布莱让他下船，小艇落到船尾，驶向帝汶的航迹在图上一路画出来。（2.5s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nFrank Lloyd, 1935,Robert Dodd, The Mutineers turning Lieut. Bligh… adrift (1790),British Admiralty and Cook-voyage charts, 1770s–80s,Thomas Rowlandson\n"},{"id":"S6-O9","name":"歌舞大王齐格飞","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O9.jpg","markdown":"# O9 歌舞大王齐格飞（The Great Ziegfeld）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 默片与有声 1927–1938\n- 长什么样：用 three.js 建的旋转螺旋“婚礼蛋糕”舞台，米高梅银色黑白：缎面、雪纺和鸵鸟羽毛衬在深黑里，只有 Clawd 是颜色。《A Pretty Girl Is Like a Melody》那座 175 级的螺旋舞台是这部片的名场面。\n- 风格：银色黑白 3D 螺旋婚礼蛋糕舞台\n- 关键词：3D·B&W\n- 年份：1936\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n真 3D 舞台大场面，调成 30 年代片厂银色黑白：把题材的世界搭成圆形分层转台上的歌舞布景，慢慢转（每秒约 3 度），挂满垂幔花环；题材里的人变成一排排穿戏服、有脸有手的合唱队。灰阶：戏服浅缎灰，布景中灰，褶皱大幕更深，剧场深黑；白色不到纯白。正面柔和主光，轮廓光更亮，把缎子和羽毛的边勾到近白。淡光柱，颗粒（±3%）。只有故事围绕的那个主角留色（如 #D97757），灯都渲染成白光。招牌镜头：贴低的中景沿层台看过去，人物占画面高度四分之一到三分之一，主角在前排。再接帷幕升起（3 秒）、10 秒吊臂上升，落到平视主角的中景。对标 Robert Z. Leonard《歌舞大王齐格飞》（1936）。避免：平均打光、没脸的锥形人、转得太快。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nReal 3D stage spectacle in 1930s studio silver: the subject's world built as a revue set on a round tiered platform turning about 3° per second, draped and garlanded; its people become rows of costumed chorus figures with faces and arms. Costumes light satin grey, set mid-grey, pleated curtain backdrop darker, theatre deep black; whites below pure white. Soft front key; brighter rim lights edging satin and feathers near-white. Faint shafts, grain (±3%). All neutral grey except the hero the story centres on (e.g. #D97757); lamps render white. Signature shot: low medium along the tiers, figures 1/4-1/3 of frame height, the hero on the front tier. Then a 3 s curtain rise, a 10 s crane up to a level medium of the hero. Reference: Robert Z. Leonard, *The Great Ziegfeld* (1936). Avoid: flat lighting, faceless cones, fast spinning.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\nthree.js 三维,米高梅银色黑白,1936 年歌舞大场面,鸵鸟羽毛与雪纺\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 是蛋糕顶上的明星：水钻头带上一丛鸵鸟羽毛像喷泉一样翻落。场景按 1.2 倍跑；明星留到最后一刀才露脸，第 5 拍它转身、一跳、双臂甩成 V 字。两刀硬切连起帷幕、吊臂和顶层。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 正面低机位：雪纺帷幕从旋转的蛋糕舞台上飞起，一层层举扇的女郎露出来，顶上是小小的 Clawd；镜头往上仰。（0.9s）\n2. 沿螺旋吊臂上升，扫过羽毛扇、白色三角钢琴和小提琴手，停在顶层之下。（0.8s）\n3. 几乎平视的中景：顶上的 Clawd 戴鸵鸟羽毛头饰，转身面对镜头，双臂甩成 V 字；慢推。（0.8s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nRobert Z. Leonard, 1936,Cedric Gibbons and John Harkrider (sets and costumes),Oliver T. Marsh, Ray June, George Folsey (cinematography)\n"},{"id":"S6-O10","name":"左拉传","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O10.jpg","markdown":"# O10 左拉传（The Life of Emile Zola）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 默片与有声 1927–1938\n- 长什么样：报纸活字印刷加 1890 年代线刻插图：木活字大标题、密排铅字正文，泛黄新闻纸上的油墨外扩和缺墨，人物用排线画。这部片最有名的画面就是 1898 年 1 月 13 日《震旦报》的《我控诉》头版。\n- 风格：报纸活字印刷 + 线刻插图\n- 关键词：2D·B&W\n- 年份：1937\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n1890 年代的报纸印刷。文字先排成清晰墨版再“印”：约 1 像素模糊加噪声阈值切边，做出油墨外扩、字边毛糙、局部缺墨、淡淡的背面镜像透字。版面语法：报头、横贯全宽的木活字大标题（约画面高度 1/6）、3–6 栏小字正文（用用户的语言，字高约画面 1/80）夹着一幅大线刻插图。木活字一字一块，带木纹。所有画面都是线刻：线宽约画幅宽 1/1000、间距约 1/400，顺着形体走，交叉排线只用在暗部，背光一侧轮廓加粗，远处用淡墨。暖黑 #17130F 印在新闻纸 #E8E0CC 上；故事围绕的那个主角单独一块强调色版（如 #D97757），套印偏 1–2 像素。动起来：活字块砸下、回弹、留下印迹；一个反复的机械动作，报纸翻页。避免：干净的数码字体、平滑灰调、其他颜色。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\n1890s newspaper print. Set text as a sharp mask, then print: ~1 px blur and a noisy threshold for ink spread, ragged edges, under-inked patches, faint mirrored show-through. Page grammar: masthead, a wood-type headline across the width (~1/6 of frame height), 3-6 columns of small type in the user's language (~1/80 of frame height) flanking one large engraved picture. Wood-type letters are grained blocks. Everything pictured is a line engraving: hatching lines about 1/1000 of frame width, spaced 1/400, following the form, cross-hatching only in darks, heavier shadow-side contours, paler distance. Warm black #17130F on newsprint #E8E0CC; the hero the story centres on gets one accent plate (e.g. #D97757), offset 1-2 px. Motion: type blocks slam down, bounce and leave their print; one repeating mechanical motion, pages turning. Avoid: clean digital type, smooth grey shading, other colours.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n木活字大标题,新闻纸活字印刷,1890 年代线刻\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮左拉：夹鼻眼镜、鹅毛笔、手里拿着当天的报纸。第 2 拍最后一个字母落下，整版一震；第 3 拍它从报纸上抬头，鹅毛笔高高举起。场景按 1.05 倍跑，两刀硬切：大标题 → 左拉 → 印刷机。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 《震旦报》头版特写（JEUDI 13 JANVIER 1898）：“J'Accuse…!”的木活字一个个砸下来，最后一个落下时整版一震；镜头推近。（0.9s）\n2. 中景：扮左拉的 Clawd 站在头版前，从手里的报纸上抬起头，一跳，把鹅毛笔高高举起，右边报童在叫卖；轻拉。（0.8s）\n3. 轮转印刷机在转，印刷工举起湿报纸，报纸一张张飞出来；镜头拉远并向右移。（0.8s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nWilliam Dieterle, 1937,L'Aurore front page, 13 January 1898,Le Petit Journal and L'Illustration line engravings,Marinoni rotary press\n"},{"id":"S6-O11","name":"浮生若梦","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O11.jpg","markdown":"# O11 浮生若梦（You Can't Take It with You）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 默片与有声 1927–1938\n- 长什么样：30 年代 WPA 丝网海报：六色不透明平涂油墨、分色渐变、轻微套印错位，印在棉浆纸上。1936–39 年联邦艺术计划的海报就是这部片同年代的美国平面语言，也和第 10 部的报纸拉开距离。\n- 风格：WPA 丝网海报\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1938\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n1936–39 年 WPA 联邦艺术计划的丝网海报。六色不透明油墨从浅到深套印，后印的版要在不该盖住的浅色形（云、太阳、高光）处镂空：奶油 #F0E2C0 给光和云，赭 #E0A842、朱红 #C84A2A 给天空和暖色面，青 #22686E 给水和阴影，主角专用色（如 #D97757，只用在故事围绕的那个主角身上），最后印深棕描边 #322018。阴影是深一色叠印，不用灰。大面积用分色渐变：两色 2–3 级粗台阶，中间一级是两色叠印。每版套偏 1–3 像素，模版边缘游移，刮板拖痕、漏印，平涂上都有暖色棉浆纸纹（±5%）。人物占画面高度二分之一到三分之二：大块面、月牙形暗面、棕色描边、脸有表情。留一条粗体大写标题，用用户的语言。慢推，一版一版弹出来。避免：网点、平滑渐变、干净的矢量平涂。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nA 1936-39 WPA (Federal Art Project) silkscreen poster. Six opaque inks printed light to dark, each later screen cut away around lighter shapes (clouds, sun): cream #F0E2C0 for light, ochre #E0A842 and vermilion #C84A2A for sky and warm planes, teal #22686E for water and shade, the hero's own ink (e.g. #D97757, only on the hero the story centres on), brown keyline #322018 last. Shadows: a darker ink overprinted, never grey. Big areas get split-fountain blends: two inks in 2-3 coarse steps, the middle one their overprint. Screens 1-3 px off register; wandering stencil edges, squeegee streaks, warm rag-paper grain (±5%) on every flat. Figures fill 1/2-2/3 of frame height: big planes, crescent shadows, expressive faces. A bold capital title band, user's language. Slow pushes; screens pop in one by one. Avoid: halftone dots, smooth gradients, clean vector flats.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n30 年代 WPA 丝网印,分色渐变,六色不透明平涂油墨\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮凡德霍夫爷爷：圆形金属框眼镜、口琴，拐杖靠在椅子边。一屋子人各干各的，地下室的烟花在最后一镜里越炸越大。两刀硬切：客厅 → 口琴 → 烟花。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 整个凡德霍夫家客厅，像一张海报：Clawd 扮爷爷，戴圆眼镜坐在青色扶手椅里吹口琴，埃西在窗前踮脚跳舞，科伦科夫高喊拍子，德皮纳从地下室活板门里探出头；轻拉。（0.8s）\n2. Clawd 近景：圆眼镜、口琴，音符往上飘，德皮纳在画面边上；推近。（0.6s）\n3. 回到客厅，地下室的烟花炸开：火箭、星爆和烟柱填满屋子；镜头拉远。（1.1s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nFrank Capra, 1938,WPA Federal Art Project silkscreen posters, 1936–39,1938 Columbia one-sheet\n"},{"id":"S6-O12","name":"乱世佳人","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O12.jpg","markdown":"# O12 乱世佳人（Gone with the Wind）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 彩色与战争 1939–1949\n- 长什么样：厚涂油画：刮刀色块、颜料堆起的棱，Technicolor 的热度——从金色烧到血红的天空、红土、黑色剪影。全片的色彩从《乱世佳人》开始降临，第一张满色画面就是夕阳下的塔拉。\n- 风格：Technicolor 厚涂油画\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1939\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n1939 年 Technicolor 配色的厚涂油画。刮刀色块：天空用长条横向色带（长为画幅宽的 1/15–1/6，厚约 1/60），地面和人物用顺形体的短笔触，之间留出平静、薄涂的区域；颜料棱受左上方的光，越亮越厚，薄处露布纹。默认是落日天空，从酒红 #3A0912 经大红 #A8241A、橙 #F27A22 烧到金 #FFD878；地面红褐 #6E2214；剪影是带暖意的近黑；故事围绕的那个主角保持看得清的暖中间调（如 #D97757），不做成剪影。强逆光，金色轮廓光；近景加一道暖色侧光照亮半边脸和手。构图像史诗海报：大块深色剪影（树、房子、岩石、人物）压在低地平线上。灯火用油画火焰。运镜：全景慢推，低机位推近主角，最后吊臂升起看大景。避免：大小一样的碎块铺满画面、脏棕色、平滑渐变。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nImpasto oil in a 1939 Technicolor palette. Palette-knife slabs: long horizontal sky bands (1/15-1/6 of frame width long, about 1/60 tall), shorter strokes following form on land and figures, calm thin areas between; ridges lit from upper left, paint thickest in highlights, canvas weave where thin. Default sunset sky: wine #3A0912, red #A8241A, orange #F27A22, gold #FFD878; ground red-brown #6E2214; warm near-black silhouettes. The hero the story centres on stays a readable warm mid-tone (e.g. #D97757), never a silhouette. Strong backlight, gold rims; in close shots a warm side light on half the face and hands. Epic-poster composition: big dark silhouettes (trees, buildings, rocks, figures) on a low horizon. Lamps as painted flames. Camera: slow push on the wide, low push-in on the hero, crane up to a vista. Avoid: tiled same-size strokes, muddy browns, smooth gradients.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n厚涂油画,刮刀,1939 年 Technicolor 配色,史诗海报画\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮斯嘉丽：系绿缎带的宽檐草帽，手里一根萝卜。前 7 拍天空从金色烧到血红，第 6 拍举起萝卜。四刀硬切走过塔拉、发誓、亚特兰大和车站，最后 5 拍是一条升降长镜头。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 塔拉剪影全景：大橡树、白房子和雪松黑压压地立在从金色烧向血红的天空下，Clawd 站在红土丘上拿着萝卜；慢推。（1.7s）\n2. 压低地平线推近，背后是燃烧的天空：戴绿缎带宽檐草帽的 Clawd 把萝卜高高举起，逆光一圈金边。（1s）\n3. Clawd 脸和草帽的近景，缎带尾巴在风里翻飞。（0.4s）\n4. 亚特兰大大火：马车冲过燃烧的仓库和木构架，Clawd 坐在瑞德旁边的驾座上，后面是普莉西和梅兰妮；镜头跟着横移，前景火舌掠过。（1.5s）\n5. 火车站一条升降长镜头：从站在伤兵中间的 Clawd 一路升高后退，一排排伤兵铺满红土直到地平线，场中一面破旗，Clawd 只剩一个橙色小点。（2.1s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nVictor Fleming, 1939,Ernest Haller and Ray Rennahan (Technicolor cinematography),William Cameron Menzies (production design),1939 poster\n"},{"id":"S6-O13","name":"蝴蝶梦","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O13.jpg","markdown":"# O13 蝴蝶梦（Rebecca）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 彩色与战争 1939–1949\n- 长什么样：粗纹纸上的炭笔画：天鹅绒般的黑、抹开的灰，月光和雾用橡皮擦出来。希区柯克的《蝴蝶梦》是一场黑白哥特梦，庄园先在雾里、后在火里，炭笔两样都画得出来。\n- 风格：哥特炭笔画\n- 关键词：2D·B&W\n- 年份：1940\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n有纹的纸上画炭笔。纸纹细（颗粒约画幅宽 1/600）：轻压只挂纹峰，重压填满纹谷。颗粒只在中间调；黑如天鹅绒，擦白是干净纸面。明暗：约六成画面暗到中灰，一成半深黑，在最近的前景和最大块面上，亮处少。炭色 #121111，纸色 #EEECE7。笔法：藤炭干笔断线，炭条侧锋顺形体宽扫（宽为画幅宽 1/60–1/30），排线，手指抹，橡皮擦出边光和光晕；雾是在铺好的灰调上横向擦出来的（如 William Kentridge 的动画素描）。只有故事围绕的那个主角用粉彩（如 #D97757），铺得较实、透点纸纹，炭笔勾边；火焰保持炭黑和纸白。月光从上方来，两三层雾带不同速横漂。运镜：主观镜头慢推，前景向两边分开。避免：数码噪点、光滑的数码光晕、发灰的黑、空亮的天空。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nCharcoal on toothed paper. Fine tooth (grain ~1/600 of frame width): light pressure catches only the peaks, heavy pressure fills the valleys. Grain only in midtones; velvet blacks, clean-paper lifts. Values: ~60% dark-to-mid grey, 15% deep black in the nearest foreground and largest masses, lights small. Charcoal #121111 on paper #EEECE7. Marks: broken vine lines, side-of-the-stick sweeps (1/60-1/30 of frame width) following form, hatching, finger smudges, eraser lifts for edges and glows; mist is horizontal streaks erased out of a toned area (as in William Kentridge's animated drawings). Only the hero the story centres on is in pastel (e.g. #D97757), laid fairly solid, tooth peeking through, outlined in charcoal; flames stay charcoal-and-paper. Moonlight from above; 2-3 fog bands drift at different speeds. Camera: slow subjective push, foreground parting. Avoid: digital noise, smooth digital glows, grey blacks, empty bright skies.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n藤炭笔,橡皮擦白与手指抹开,William Kentridge,哥特悬疑\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮第二任德温特夫人：天鹅绒发箍、开衫，仰望庄园。开场的梦境推进里没有它；接着三刀硬切：Clawd → 起火的庄园 → 丹弗斯太太。每一镜都在往里推，2.5 秒一路推向大火。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 沿着荒草蔓生的月下车道主观推进，树林分开，雾里露出曼德利，几扇本不该亮的窗亮着灯。（0.9s）\n2. Clawd 近景，扮第二任德温特夫人：天鹅绒发箍、开衫翻领，身后是曼德利；慢推。（0.6s）\n3. 月光下的曼德利，镜头推近，火从一扇扇窗里烧起来。（0.5s）\n4. 丹弗斯太太一动不动站在燃烧的窗口，双手交叠，冷脸被平光照着；镜头往她脸上推。（0.5s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nAlfred Hitchcock, 1940,George Barnes (cinematography),William Kentridge (charcoal animation),Odilon Redon, the noirs\n"},{"id":"S6-O14","name":"青山翠谷","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O14.jpg","markdown":"# O14 青山翠谷（How Green Was My Valley）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 彩色与战争 1939–1949\n- 长什么样：用 1941 年原海报金黄加绿配色的不透明水粉：扎实的色块、煤黑的渣山、Kyffin Williams 式厚重的深色山体和断续描边。用水粉而不用木刻，和第 3 部区分开；画的是矿工唱着歌走下山谷回家。\n- 风格：威尔士山谷水粉\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1941\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n仿威尔士画家 Kyffin Williams 的不透明水粉。方向性笔触宽为画幅宽 1/80–1/40，长是宽的 2–5 倍，边缘毛糙，完全覆盖；房子、墙、山脊用断续深色短线勾边；暗山上刮几笔亮绿或灰蓝。色板：天空和光淡金 #F1C565（别成芥末黄），田野绿 #86A03F，暗部煤黑 #1D1B1E，远山和水面灰蓝 #48505E，小点缀黄 #F6CF2F，其他颜色由这几色调出；故事围绕的那个主角用 #D97757，至少占画面高八分之一。田野和天空偏亮（约六成），山和房子偏暗。暖色调色不超过一成，暗角，颗粒。默认傍晚低角度侧光，田野受光，山脊有轮廓光。从高处真透视取景，一条路把视线引下去；小房子（或墙、石块）顺山坡一级级排开。动起来：烟或云循环飘动，横向跟拍。避免：水彩透明感、矢量平涂、发暗发脏。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nOpaque gouache after Welsh painter Kyffin Williams. Directional strokes 1/80-1/40 of frame width, 2-5 times as long, ragged, fully covering; broken dark dashes outline buildings, walls and ridges; dark hills flecked with lighter green or slate knife marks. Palette: sky and light pale gold #F1C565 (not mustard), fields green #86A03F, darks coal #1D1B1E, far hills and water slate #48505E, small accents yellow #F6CF2F, other colours mixed from these; the hero the story centres on #D97757, at least 1/8 of frame height. Fields and sky light (~60%), hills and buildings dark. Warm grade under 10%, vignette, grain. Default low late-afternoon side light: fields lit, ridges rim-lit. From high ground in true perspective, a path leading the eye down; small repeated buildings (or walls, rocks) stepping along slopes. Motion: looping smoke or cloud, a lateral track. Avoid: watercolour transparency, vector fills, mud.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n不透明水粉,Kyffin Williams 刮刀风景,1941 原海报配色\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮少年休：粗花呢平顶帽、打结的红围巾、一枝黄水仙。先坐在山顶看山谷，再跟着唱歌的矿工齐步走下街。两刀硬切：山谷 → 井架大轮 → 街道。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 山顶黄水仙丛里俯瞰：戴平顶帽、系红围巾的 Clawd 坐在左前景，手拿一枝黄水仙，排屋街一路通到谷底的井架和煤渣山；慢拉。（1s）\n2. 井架和转动的大轮特写，后面绿坡上爬满排屋；固定机位。（0.5s）\n3. 沿排屋街的横向跟拍：下工的矿工分三排往下走，脸上是煤灰，边走边唱；Clawd 走在前排，房子从身后滑过。（1s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nJohn Ford, 1941,Arthur C. Miller (cinematography),Kyffin Williams,1941 poster\n"},{"id":"S6-O15","name":"忠勇之家","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O15.jpg","markdown":"# O15 忠勇之家（Mrs. Miniver）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 彩色与战争 1939–1949\n- 长什么样：二战英国本土战线的石印海报：砖红、深绿、奶油、灰蓝的有限套色，阴影是石版蜡笔颗粒。配影片结尾：被炸穿屋顶的乡村教堂里全村唱诗，喷火式战机从破洞上方飞过。\n- 风格：英国战时石印海报\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1942\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n做成 1942 年英国战时的石印海报。造型简化有力，三版套印，略错位 1–3 像素：色版承载所有平涂；冷灰蓝蜡笔版压暗每一处阴影，带短而斜的石纹颗粒（不用网点）；墨绿黑轮廓版给每个形描一圈约画幅宽 1/500 的线，人物和云也要描。配色按用途：奶油 #EBDFC2 是纸色和亮部，灰蓝 #8497A6 是天空和阴影，砖红 #A23F2D 和深绿 #34503F 只作点缀，合计不超过画面四分之一；棕色和肤色由奶油加砖红调出；轮廓色 #1E2422。场景可以排满。天空用喷枪渐变，只有纸纹每秒 12 帧跳动。浅色光带顺着光源方向斜落，照到之处阴影版消失。人物正面站立，像海报里的人。镜头慢移带视差、上摇。对标 Frank Newbould、Abram Games。避免：网点阴影、干净的矢量边、大片饱和色。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nA 1942 British wartime stone-lithograph poster. Simple, strong shapes from three plates, 1-3 px off register: a colour plate with all flat fills; a grey-blue crayon plate darkening every shadow with short slanted stone grain; a green-black key line about 1/500 of frame width around every shape, figures and clouds included. Palette by role: cream #EBDFC2 paper and highlights, grey-blue #8497A6 sky and shadow, brick red #A23F2D and deep green #34503F as accents, at most a quarter of the frame; browns and skin mixed from cream and brick; key #1E2422. Scenes may be full. Airbrushed skies; only the paper grain boils at 12 fps. Pale light bands, angled from the source, cancel the shadow plate. Figures stand frontally. Camera: slow drifts, a tilt up. References: Frank Newbould, Abram Games. Avoid: dot screens, clean vector edges, big saturated fields.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n石印 + 蜡笔阴影版,Frank Newbould、Abram Games 二战海报,英国信息部海报配色\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮米尼弗夫人：花边草帽、赞美诗集和红色的「米尼弗夫人」玫瑰。全景里它在唱诗；切到近景时听见引擎声，停下抬头；第三个镜头是它的视线，穿过屋顶破洞看喷火式编队飞过。三个硬切，靠一个抬头的动作连起来。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 全景，顺着被炸的教堂中殿看过去：村民站在长椅间唱诗，阳光成柱照下；Clawd 戴花边草帽坐在第一排，拿着赞美诗集和红玫瑰在唱。镜头微微后拉。（0.8s）\n2. Clawd 和玫瑰的近景：引擎声传来，它停下不唱，抬头看向屋顶。镜头轻推。（0.6s）\n3. 从教堂里垂直仰拍屋顶的破洞：断裂的椽子框住天空，喷火式战机排成 V 字飞过。镜头慢推并略微旋转。（1.1s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nWilliam Wyler, 1942,Joseph Ruttenberg (cinematography),Frank Newbould, Your Britain — Fight for it Now (1942),Abram Games,Mrs. Miniver one-sheet (1942)\n"},{"id":"S6-O16","name":"卡萨布兰卡","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O16.jpg","markdown":"# O16 卡萨布兰卡（Casablanca）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 彩色与战争 1939–1949\n- 长什么样：影片本身的样子：Arthur Edeson 的片厂黑白，画成精修剧照：带眼神光的柔焦特写、百叶窗投影、烟雾里的光柱。扮里克的 Clawd 是唯一的颜色。\n- 风格：好莱坞片厂柔光黑白\n- 关键词：2D·B&W\n- 年份：1943\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n40 年代好莱坞片厂黑白，画成精修剧照。形体是边缘干净的喷枪明暗块。人脸用短光位：主光照亮离镜头远的那半边脸，靠近镜头的脸颊落在暗部，留一块伦勃朗三角光；每只眼两个眼神光。条状影子借手边的东西（百叶窗、栏杆、树枝），斜落在物面上；光柱只在烟雾飘过处显形。明暗：三到四成画面是深黑，白色面积小而发光；外景天空中暗灰。调色：轻柔光溢出、S 曲线、细颗粒（±3%）、柔暗角、轻微闪烁；雾只在局部。只有故事围绕的那个主角的身体或衣服保留颜色（如 #D97757），皮肤和灯光都是灰阶。镜头：长运镜，推向一张脸，再拉远退进雾里。对标摄影师 Arthur Edeson（《卡萨布兰卡》，Michael Curtiz，1942）。避免：平光、整片灰雾、僵硬的胸像。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\n1940s Hollywood studio black-and-white, painted like a retouched glamour still. Forms: smooth airbrushed tone blocks. Faces: short key (light on the face's far side from camera; the near cheek in shadow, holding a Rembrandt triangle of light), two catchlights per eye. Slatted shadows from anything at hand (blinds, railings, branches) fall diagonally across surfaces; light shafts show only in smoke or fog. Values: 30-40% of the frame deep black, whites small and glowing; outdoor skies mid-dark. Grade: diffusion glow, an S-curve, fine grain (±3%), soft vignette, faint flicker; fog stays local. Only the hero the story centres on keeps colour, on its body or costume (e.g. #D97757); skin and lamps stay grey. Camera: a long glide, a push to a face, a pull-back into fog. References: cinematographer Arthur Edeson (*Casablanca*, Michael Curtiz, 1942). Avoid: flat lighting, grey haze, stiff busts.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n喷枪精修剧照人像,Arthur Edeson 片厂布光,柔光镜与银盐颗粒\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮里克：咖啡馆里是领结和香槟碟杯，机场上是软呢帽和竖起的风衣领。咖啡馆是一条不断的运镜，收在它举杯（\"Here's looking at you, kid\"）；正中那一拍是全段唯一的硬切，跳到机场，一个回头加一次长拉远收尾。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 一条运镜穿过里克的咖啡馆：越过吊扇和烟雾滑到弹琴的萨姆和靠在琴上的伊尔莎，推向她泪光渐起的柔焦脸，再横移过整个房间，落到桌边系着领结的 Clawd，它举起香槟杯。（2.5s）\n2. 硬切到雾中机场：Clawd 戴软呢帽、竖着风衣领的近景，它回头一瞥；镜头长长地拉远，露出伊尔莎、雷诺上尉和雾里螺旋桨转动的 Electra 客机。（2.5s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nMichael Curtiz, 1942,Arthur Edeson (cinematography),Casablanca one-sheet (1942)\n"},{"id":"S6-O17","name":"与我同行","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O17.jpg","markdown":"# O17 与我同行（Going My Way）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 彩色与战争 1939–1949\n- 长什么样：逆光的哥特彩色玻璃：铅条拼起的色玻璃、灰调描出的人脸、沙特尔蓝和宝石红边框，彩光洒在教堂地上。奥马利神父和唱诗班嵌在窗里，头顶是金色星章。\n- 风格：逆光哥特彩色玻璃窗\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1944\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n逆光的哥特彩窗，占画面高度八到九成、宽度约六成，两边露出相邻发光的窗。每个形状是一块切好的玻璃：每块明度浮动约 8%，有流纹气泡和褐色内描边，外压黑铅条（宽约画幅宽 1/250）。天空和空地切成菱形格（每格约画幅宽 1/30）；边框是带圆珠的宝石红。主要人物至少占窗高一半，用尖细的深褐线描脸和衣褶。配色按用途：背景钴蓝 #1B3F9E，边框宝石红 #B3121F，高光金 #E3A526，铅条 #16161A；皮肤淡琥珀；景物用深绿、青绿。只有故事围绕的那个主角是橙色玻璃（如 #D97757）。亮玻璃光晕吃进铅条；彩光落在地面和窗台上。镜头：下摇、横摇，落到光斑上。对标沙特尔大教堂、Harry Clarke。避免：平涂、随意切割、窗四周大片暗墙。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nA backlit Gothic stained-glass window filling 80-90% of frame height and ~60% of width, neighbours glowing at edges. Every shape is cut glass: lightness varied ±8% per piece, streaks, seed bubbles, a brown edge mat, black lead (about 1/250 of frame width). Sky and empty ground: diamond quarries (about 1/30 of frame width); the border is ruby with beads. The main figure is at least half the window's height, in dark-brown grisaille line. Palette by role: background cobalt #1B3F9E, border ruby #B3121F, highlights gold #E3A526, lead #16161A; skin pale amber; scenery deep greens. Only the hero the story centres on is orange glass (e.g. #D97757). Bright glass blooms into the leads; coloured light spills onto floor and sill. Camera: tilt down, pan, settle on the pooled light. References: Chartres Cathedral; Harry Clarke. Avoid: flat fills, random cuts, dark empty wall.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n铅条拼色玻璃 + 灰调描绘,沙特尔蓝菱形格,Harry Clarke 宝石色\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 在玻璃画里扮奥马利神父：身体是最暖的陶土色玻璃，戴草帽和罗马领，拿指挥棒和歌本。它跟着拍子指挥，男孩们的口型各自错开。一个下摇从星章落到 Clawd，再硬切到唱诗班，最后落到地上的彩光。表演 1.8 倍速。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 从金色八角星章和「SWINGING ON A STAR」飘带开始，沿尖拱窗往下摇，落到扮奥马利神父的 Clawd，它在玻璃里指挥男孩唱诗班。（1.3s）\n2. 玻璃唱诗班的中景：穿红袍白衣的男孩们在唱，镜头向右横摇，扫过 Clawd 举指挥棒的手。（0.6s）\n3. 切到教堂中殿：彩窗的颜色洒在长椅之间的大理石过道上，祭坛蜡烛亮着；镜头往地面下摇。（0.6s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nLeo McCarey, 1944,Going My Way one-sheet (1944),Chartres Cathedral glass,Harry Clarke\n"},{"id":"S6-O18","name":"失去的周末","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O18.jpg","markdown":"# O18 失去的周末（The Lost Weekend）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 彩色与战争 1939–1949\n- 长什么样：画面是打出来的：等宽字符落在 pica 网格上，灰阶靠字符疏密和叠打，色带墨色不匀。唐抱着打字机去当铺，于是整条街由打字机打出；Clawd 用橙色色带打。\n- 风格：打字机字符画\n- 关键词：2D·B&W\n- 年份：1945\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n所有画面都用打字机字符打出来：等宽粗体字逐个敲，落在固定网格上，格宽约画幅宽 1/150、格高是格宽两倍。85%–95% 的格子都要打字，只有最亮的光留纸白。灰阶靠字键分级（浅 . : -，中 = + o，深 X # M），暗部约占三成，用 M# 这样的叠打。形体靠边界清楚的字块和剪影读出。每种材质重复自己的字键（如竖向结构 I H，砖缝 _|，水 ~）。招牌或说明要打得能读。色带墨色不匀：半边缺墨，几个键总是偏高或偏低。纸 #EBE6DA，墨 #1D1C21；只有故事围绕的那个主角用红橙色带 #D4704F 打，至少占画面高度三分之一，影子仍是黑的。硬侧光投出边缘清楚的密集长影。画面在第一秒内打完，然后停住。对标 Paul Smith 的打字机画。避免：稀疏空白、看不出形的横向字条。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nType every image: one bold monospaced glyph per keystroke, fixed grid, cells 1/150 of frame width wide, twice as tall. Strike 85-95% of cells; bare paper only for highlights. Grey from a key ladder (light . : -, mid = + o, dark X # M); darks (about 30%) are overstrikes like M#. Shapes read as crisp-edged fields, clear silhouettes. Each material repeats its own keys (e.g. I H verticals, _| courses, ~ water). Signs stay legible. Uneven ribbon: half-inked strikes, a few keys always high or low. Paper #EBE6DA, ink #1D1C21; only the hero the story centres on, at least 1/3 of frame height, is struck in red-orange #D4704F, its shadow black. Hard sidelight casts long, crisp, dense shadows. The picture types itself within the first second, then holds. Reference: Paul Smith's typewriter art. Avoid: sparse frames, shapeless streaks.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n叠打的打字机字符画,Paul Smith、Flora Stacey,John F. Seitz 硬光黑白\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮唐·伯南：字符打出的软呢帽，提着想拿去当掉的打字机，四条腿逐帧交替迈步。街道像被打字机打出来一样在它身边出现；接着两个快速硬切，近景和 CLOSED 牌，把「当铺关门」交代完。表演 1.8 倍速。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 第三大道高架铁下的一点透视全景：街景一行行打到纸上，戴软呢帽的 Clawd 提着打字机往前走；镜头微微后拉。（1.3s）\n2. Clawd 近景：字符打出的帽子、眼睛和身边灰色的打字机，背后是钢梁；镜头慢推。（0.6s）\n3. J. GOLDMAN 的 LOANS 当铺招牌特写，字母由字符拼成，下面挂着 CLOSED 牌；镜头慢推。（0.6s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nBilly Wilder, 1945,John F. Seitz (cinematography),The Lost Weekend one-sheet (1945),Paul Smith (typewriter art),Flora Stacey\n"},{"id":"S6-O19","name":"黄金时代","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O19.jpg","markdown":"# O19 黄金时代（The Best Years of Our Lives）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 彩色与战争 1939–1949\n- 长什么样：1946 年画报的新闻摄影跨页：铜版纸上的粗黑网点、中缝折痕、叠印的图片说明。深焦里拆光的 B-17 排到地平线；Clawd 单独印在专色橙版上，是唯一的颜色。\n- 风格：画报新闻摄影半调跨页\n- 关键词：2.5D·B&W\n- 年份：1946\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n每一帧当成 1946 年画报的新闻摄影跨页。先把场景按照片渲染成灰调（3D 或手绘都行）：低角度太阳、长影子、深焦，近处和地平线一样清楚。照片满版跨过两页，竖向中缝折痕从中间穿过，带一点阴影。再印成 45° 黑色网点，间距约画幅宽 1/250，米白铜版纸 #ECE6D8，墨 #1A1A1A，略有扩墨。从第一镜起，在安静的区域叠印一小块黑色标题和图说。只有故事围绕的那个主角走专色橙版（如 #D97757），换一个网角，错版 2–3 像素，黑版只留在它最深的暗部；主角至少占画面高度四分之一。镜头：走进全景，推进一个细节，再拉远，照片缩成一本摊开的杂志。对标摄影师 Gregg Toland、《生活》杂志战后摄影专题。避免：中缝切过主角，网点粗到照片碎掉。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nEach frame is a 1946 picture-magazine photo spread. Render the scene as a grey photo (3D or painted): low sun, long shadows, deep focus (near and far both sharp). The photo bleeds across both pages, crossed by a soft vertical gutter crease. Print as a 45° black halftone, dot pitch about 1/250 of frame width, ink #1A1A1A on off-white coated stock #ECE6D8. A short black headline and caption sit in a quiet area. Only the hero the story centres on, at least 1/4 of frame height, prints on its own spot-orange plate (e.g. #D97757) at another angle, 2-3 px off, black only in its deepest shadows. Camera: walk in, push to a detail, pull back to reveal an open magazine. References: cinematographer Gregg Toland; *Life* post-war photo essays. Avoid: the gutter cutting the hero, coarse dots that break the photo.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n45° 粗网点半调,Gregg Toland 深焦,1946 年画报跨页版式,专色橙版\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮弗雷德：上尉的船形帽和 B-3 飞行夹克的羊毛领。它沿着轰炸机走廊走，踩着拍子停下抬头；切进它望着的机头舱，再一路拉远，原来整个画面是 1946 年杂志上的一页。两个硬切，最后一镜是一次连续的拉远。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 低机位深焦全景，飞机坟场：戴船形帽、竖着羊毛领的 Clawd 走到一架拆光的 B-17 机翼下，停步，抬头看玻璃机头；镜头右移并推近。（0.9s）\n2. 轰炸员的玻璃机头舱里往外看，一排排轰炸机伸向远山，Clawd 在画面左边缘；镜头慢推。（0.8s）\n3. 拉远：照片缩进一本摊开的杂志，标题 GRAVEYARD OF THE FORTRESSES，放在深色桌面上。（0.8s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nWilliam Wyler, 1946,Gregg Toland (cinematography),Life magazine post-war photo essays,The Best Years of Our Lives one-sheet (1946)\n"},{"id":"S6-O20","name":"君子协定","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O20.jpg","markdown":"# O20 君子协定（Gentleman's Agreement）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 彩色与战争 1939–1949\n- 长什么样：40 年代杂志插画式的石墨铅笔：先打构图线，再一遍遍平行排线，擦抹暗部、橡皮提亮。画面围绕酒店前台一次客气的拒绝逐步画出，只有 Clawd 是彩色铅笔。\n- 风格：40 年代杂志铅笔插画\n- 关键词：2D·B&W\n- 年份：1947\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用石墨铅笔画成 40 年代杂志插画，画得完整、有控制。画面在前 1–1.5 秒内画成，然后停住：先淡淡的透视线和头部椭圆，再画两头轻中间重、转角出头的轮廓（宽约画幅宽 1/600），然后一到四遍近乎平行的排线（遍数越多越暗），间距约画幅宽 1/300，顺着形体走，再擦抹出平滑的过渡，用橡皮提亮。完成的画面几乎铺满：环境和背景都画充分，以中灰为主，深色约占一成半，只有最外一圈约十分之一淡成没画完的边角。纸 #F2EEE5 带轻齿纹，铅色 #2C2B2E。只有故事围绕的那个主角的衣服用彩铅（如 #D97757），皮肤仍是铅笔灰。戏在脸上。镜头：缓缓拉远，再切特写。对标插画家 Al Parker、Robert Fawcett。避免：交叉网格、潦草速写感、大片空白纸面。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nGraphite pencil like a 1940s magazine illustration, finished and controlled. The drawing builds in the first 1-1.5 s, then holds: faint perspective rays and head ovals, pressure-tapered contours (about 1/600 of frame width) overshooting at corners, then 1-4 near-parallel hatching passes (more for darker), about 1/300 of frame width apart, following the form, then rubbed tone and eraser lifts. The finished frame is toned nearly edge to edge: setting fully drawn in mid-greys, about 15% dark accents, only the outer tenth fading to loose margins. Paper #F2EEE5 with a light tooth; lead #2C2B2E. Only the hero the story centres on wears coloured pencil (e.g. #D97757) on its clothing; skin stays graphite. Faces carry the drama. Camera: a slow ease out, then cuts to close-ups. References: illustrators Al Parker and Robert Fawcett. Avoid: cross-hatch grids, scribbly sketching, a near-empty page.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n石墨排线与擦抹,Al Parker、Robert Fawcett,彩色铅笔主体\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮记者菲尔：插 PRESS 卡的软呢帽、采访本和铅笔，站在前台不动。第一镜是画面在它身边被画出来；接着两个硬切给旁观者，经理客气的拒绝和女士斜过来的目光，意思全在这两张脸上。拍点落在剪切上。表演 1.8 倍速。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 酒店大堂全景，画面当场被画出来，从构图线到排线：戴着插 PRESS 卡软呢帽的 Clawd 把采访本举到前台；镜头微微后拉。（1.1s）\n2. 从前台经理推到他的特写：微微欠身、微笑，眼睛却不笑，身后钥匙架上每把钥匙都还挂着。（0.8s）\n3. 固定机位中景：电梯旁披狐皮围脖的女士，眼睛回头瞟向 Clawd。（0.7s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nElia Kazan, 1947,Gentleman's Agreement one-sheet (1947),Al Parker,Robert Fawcett\n"},{"id":"S6-O21","name":"王子复仇记","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O21.jpg","markdown":"# O21 王子复仇记（Hamlet）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 彩色与战争 1939–1949\n- 长什么样：实时 3D 的艾尔西诺，高反差深焦黑白：体积雾、湿石头、碎成蕾丝白沫的海浪、银盐颗粒。照奥利维耶片中的城堡、雾中城垛和礁石来拍；Clawd 是唯一的颜色。\n- 风格：黑白 3D 雾中城堡\n- 关键词：3D·B&W\n- 年份：1948\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n真 3D 场景，40 年代末的深焦黑白。建筑是裸露的砌体，逐块砌出，带倒角和错缝（场景本有这类建筑时才用罗马式半圆拱）；湿的表面发黑发亮。阴天柔光，没有硬投影：深色实体衬着银灰天空。高度雾贴着画面下三分之一，成片里远景是 60%–70% 灰，中景清晰；云后亮天透出淡淡光柱。有水时：多层涌浪、蕾丝般的白沫、礁石上的水雾。后期：环境光遮蔽、轻辉光、高反差银色调色（有真黑，白不过曝），颗粒 ±3%，轻微闪烁。只有故事围绕的那个主角保留颜色（如 #D97757），所有光源都是灰的。镜头：慢慢漂近，沿高处横移，低机位仰拍主角。对标 Laurence Olivier《哈姆雷特》（1948）。避免：晴天硬影子、雾糊成一片白、贴图平铺。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nA real 3D scene in late-1940s deep-focus black-and-white. Built forms: bare masonry, block by block, bevelled, staggered (Romanesque arches only where the setting has them); wet surfaces dark and glossy. Soft overcast light, no hard shadows: dark masses against a silver sky. Low fog fills the lower third; the far background ends at 60-70% grey, the midground sharp; a bright patch behind cloud throws faint shafts. Water, if any: layered swells, lace foam, misty spray. Post: ambient occlusion, gentle bloom, high-contrast silver grade (true blacks, unclipped whites), grain (±3%), faint flicker. Only the hero the story centres on keeps its colour (e.g. #D97757); every light source stays grey. Camera: slow drift in, a track along a high edge, a low angle on the hero. Reference: Laurence Olivier, *Hamlet* (1948). Avoid: hard sunlit shadows, white-out fog, tiled textures.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\nthree.js 3D + 体积雾,Desmond Dickinson 深焦黑白,Roger Furse 光秃的罗马式布景\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮哈姆雷特：约里克的骷髅，海报上的白色蕾丝袖口、腰带和短剑。三个硬切走过这部片的三个画面：悬崖上的城堡、雾里的鬼魂、独白的礁石；最后一镜里骷髅踩着拍子举起来。表演 1.8 倍速。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 从海上看的全景：海岬上的艾尔西诺，脚下浪花拍岸，Clawd 是右边礁石顶上的一个小身影；镜头缓缓漂近。（0.7s）\n2. 浓雾里的城墙步道：披甲的鬼魂从雾中显形、招手，Clawd 在近处看着它；镜头轻推。（0.8s）\n3. 低机位仰拍海边礁石：戴蕾丝袖口、挂短剑的 Clawd 在雾中城堡前举起约里克的骷髅；镜头慢推。（1.1s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nLaurence Olivier, 1948,Desmond Dickinson (cinematography),Roger Furse (production design),Hamlet one-sheet (1948)\n"},{"id":"S6-O22","name":"国王班底","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O22.jpg","markdown":"# O22 国王班底（All the King's Men）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 彩色与战争 1939–1949\n- 长什么样：南方竞选海报的木活字凸版：红、蓝、黑三色油墨印在米黄纸上，实地有木纹，供墨不匀，版间错位，肖像走半调网点。这是斯塔克的竞选集会，Clawd 用第四版橙墨印。\n- 风格：南方竞选木活字海报\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1949\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n每帧都是木活字和刻版印的凸版海报。米黄纸 #EEE3C7 上印三色：蓝 #20488C 管天空、水和大块暗部，红 #C82C28 管暖色面和条纹，黑 #1E1B1E 管轮廓和字；只有故事围绕的那个主角多加一块专色（如 #D97757）。每色一版，错位 2–4 像素；实地挖空下层，只有网点处叠印。实地有木纹、缺墨针孔，边缘挤墨。中间调走粗网点（约画幅宽 1/200），各版网角不同，不超过画面三分之一。人物像麻胶版画：粗黑轮廓、刻刀白线、月牙阴影。画面约七成是图；一两行粗木活字短句写在图里的横幅和招牌上，带错位投影，字高约画面十分之一，用用户语言。镜头：小推，横移，重拍上特写一震（无音乐每 2 秒）。对标 Hatch Show Print。避免：碎灰调代替大块实地、套色精准得像电脑。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nA letterpress poster from wood type and cuts. Three inks on cream stock #EEE3C7: blue #20488C (sky, water, shadow masses), red #C82C28 (warm surfaces, stripes), black #1E1B1E (outlines, type); only the hero the story centres on gets a spot ink (e.g. #D97757). Each ink 2-4 px off register; solids knock out the inks beneath, halftones overprint. Wood grain, pinholed starved patches, squeezed edges. Mid tones: coarse dots (about 1/200 of frame width), varied angles, on at most a third of the frame. Figures as linocuts: heavy outlines, gouged white lines, crescent shadows. Picture fills about 70%; short wood-type lines (about 1/10 of frame height, user's language) with offset shadows sit on banners and signs. Camera: small pushes, a lateral track, a bump on each strong beat (or every 2 s). Reference: Hatch Show Print. Avoid: busy grey mid-tones, perfect registration.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n木活字凸版印刷,Hatch Show Print,麻胶版画人物,半调网点肖像\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮威利·斯塔克：往后推的软呢帽、拉松的红蓝领带，一只手里卷着讲稿。全景里拳头举到一半，中间一镜是人群的回应，特写里它踩着拍子一拳举到顶、再挥一次，镜头在出拳时轻轻一震。全部硬切。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 小镇广场挂满彩旗的讲台全景：扮斯塔克的 Clawd 站在自己的巨幅半调肖像前，拳头举到一半，台下人群举着 STARK FOR GOVERNOR 牌子欢呼；镜头小幅推进。（0.9s）\n2. 切进人群：牌子上下挥动，拳头和喊叫的嘴，EVERY MAN A KING；镜头向右横移。（0.7s）\n3. Clawd 近景，软呢帽往后推、领带拉松：拳头一下举到顶，再挥一次；镜头在出拳那一下跟着一震。（0.9s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nRobert Rossen, 1949,Burnett Guffey (cinematography),All the King's Men one-sheet (1949),Hatch Show Print,Huey Long campaign posters\n"},{"id":"S6-O23","name":"彗星美人","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O23.jpg","markdown":"# O23 彗星美人（All About Eve）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 宽银幕 1950–1959\n- 长什么样：艾尔·赫希菲尔德的百老汇漫画：粗细变化的流畅墨线、黑块对白、大量留白，每人只抓一个夸张特征。这部片讲剧场，派对就画成报纸剧场版漫画；Clawd 是唯一的颜色。\n- 风格：赫希菲尔德一笔画漫画\n- 关键词：2D·B&W\n- 年份：1950\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n画成 Al Hirschfeld 式的漫画，白纸黑墨。线条有弹性、长而连贯，能一笔画完的轮廓就一笔：两端约画幅宽 1/800，转弯处加粗到 1/250。深色衣服、头发和大件物体用实心黑块；灰调只用稀疏的平行排线。环境用细线画完整：建筑、家具、道具连同细节都画，物与物之间留白；约六成纸面留白，但绝不是空纸。每个人物靠姿态和比例夸张一个特征（长脖子、大头），五官具体。纸 #FBFAF6，墨 #131012；只有故事围绕的那个主角的身体或主要衣服用一种颜色平涂（如 #D97757），代替黑块。线条在第一秒内一笔笔写完，然后停住；留白处可有一句手写短句。镜头：拉远，横摇扫过场景，小推。避免：四肢僵直、火柴人、空荡荡的环境。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nAn Al Hirschfeld-style caricature. Lines are elastic, long and continuous, one stroke per contour: about 1/800 of frame width at the ends, swelling to 1/250 on curves. Dark clothing, hair and big objects go solid black; grey only as sparse parallel hatching. The setting is fully drawn in thin line, architecture, furniture and props in detail, white kept between objects: about 60% paper white, never an empty page. Push one trait per figure (a long neck, a big head). Paper #FBFAF6, ink #131012; only the hero the story centres on has its body or main garment filled flat with one colour (e.g. #D97757) instead of black. Lines write on within the first second, then hold; a short hand-lettered line may sit in the white. Camera: ease back, pan across, push in. Avoid: stiff limbs, stick figures, a bare setting.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n一笔墨线漫画,Al Hirschfeld 剧场漫画,手写台词\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮玛戈：一排黑色短卷发、一串珍珠、一杯马提尼，手搭在扶手上。第一镜是画面被画出来、台词被写出来；一个横摇找到角落里盯着她的伊芙；最后一个硬切后，玛戈踩着拍子举杯。表演 1.8 倍速。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 玛戈家派对的全景一笔笔画出来：先是楼梯，再是宾客和钢琴；留白处手写出 \"FASTEN YOUR SEATBELTS, IT'S GOING TO BE A BUMPY NIGHT\"，扮玛戈的 Clawd 站在楼梯上；镜头微微拉远。（1.1s）\n2. 向右横摇扫过宾客：伯蒂、马克斯、艾迪森、卡斯韦尔小姐、钢琴师，停在角落里的伊芙。（0.8s）\n3. 楼梯上 Clawd 的近景：黑色卷发、珍珠项链，举起马提尼；镜头轻推。（0.7s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nJoseph L. Mankiewicz, 1950,All About Eve one-sheet (1950),Al Hirschfeld\n"},{"id":"S6-O24","name":"一个美国人在巴黎","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O24.jpg","markdown":"# O24 一个美国人在巴黎（An American in Paris）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 宽银幕 1950–1959\n- 长什么样：拉乌尔·杜飞的水彩：快速的蓝黑墨线，底下是钴蓝、柠檬黄、玫瑰红的透明色块，线和色永远对不齐。影片结尾的芭蕾布景就是照杜飞画的，协和广场和舞者都用他的画法。\n- 风格：杜飞水彩芭蕾\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1951\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n用拉乌尔·杜飞的水彩画法：先上色、后画线，两者永远对不齐。透明色块铺满大部分画面，带湿边、积色边和颗粒，比物体大一到四成，朝同一方向滑开约画幅宽 1/30：钴蓝 #2258CE 管天空和水，柠檬黄 #FCE02E 管光和地面，玫瑰红 #EC3A7C 管暖色面。重叠可混出紫或绿，绝不脏成棕，主角的色块别压在蓝上。再用蓝黑墨 #1E1E3A 快速细线把整个场景画满（线宽约画幅宽 1/700），有提按和甩尾：建筑、窗、人物、小配景；直边单独一笔、角上出头；树冠和云画成一串小圈。留白两到三成，皮肤是纸白。故事围绕的那个主角有自己的一块色（如 #D97757）。人物轻快有弹性。镜头：小推，踩拍子切（没有音乐约每 2 秒）。避免：颜色叠脏、线描死色块、线条稀疏。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nPaint in Raoul Dufy's watercolour manner: colour first, line second, never aligned. Transparent washes (wet edges, pooled rims, granulation) cover most of the frame, 10-40% oversized, slid one way by about 1/30 of frame width: cobalt #2258CE for sky and water, lemon #FCE02E for light and ground, rose #EC3A7C for warm planes. Overlaps mix violet or green, never brown; keep the hero's wash off blue. Then draw the whole scene densely in quick, thin blue-black ink (about 1/700 of frame width) with flicked ends: buildings, windows, figures, extras; straight edges overshoot corners; trees and clouds as looped strings. Ink #1E1E3A; bare paper 20-30%, skin included. The hero the story centres on gets its own wash (e.g. #D97757). Figures bounce. Camera: small pushes, cuts on the beat (or every 2 s). Avoid: muddy colour, lines tracing the colour, sparse line.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n松动墨线 + 错开的色块,拉乌尔·杜飞,明奈利的芭蕾布景,冷压水彩纸\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮海报上的杰瑞：往后歪的钴蓝软帽（吉恩·凯利那顶）、玫瑰红小领巾、甩出颜料带的画笔。三个镜头里它每一拍都在跳，舞步本身就是剪辑节奏：广场、和丽丝的近景、插满旗的街道。表演 1.8 倍速。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 协和广场全景：舞者绕着喷泉旋转，后面是方尖碑和埃菲尔铁塔；扮杰瑞的 Clawd 戴钴蓝帽、拿画笔，在黄裙丽丝身边跳；镜头小幅推进。（1s）\n2. Clawd 跳在半空的近景，画笔甩出一条颜料带，丽丝的手臂伸进画面；镜头慢推。（0.8s）\n3. 切到插满三色旗的巴黎街道，楼面是粉、黄、蓝，Clawd 在街心行人之间蹦跳；镜头慢推。（0.8s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nVincente Minnelli, 1951,Preston Ames and Irene Sharaff (ballet design),Raoul Dufy, La Fée Électricité,Raoul Dufy, La Rue pavoisée,An American in Paris one-sheet (1951)\n"},{"id":"S6-O25","name":"戏王之王","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O25.jpg","markdown":"# O25 戏王之王（The Greatest Show on Earth）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 宽银幕 1950–1959\n- 长什么样：Strobridge 马戏团彩色石印：铬黄、朱红、钴蓝平涂，粗黑轮廓，带网点、错版和石版颗粒。德米尔的大帐篷画成三圈海报，Clawd 扮塞巴斯蒂安做空中接人。\n- 风格：Strobridge 马戏团彩色石印\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1952\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n19 世纪末马戏团彩色石印，仿 Strobridge 石印公司。平涂油墨加粗黑轮廓（约画幅宽 1/300）；阴影是月牙形加深，加排线；光从左上来。四块石版：铬黄 #F4C416、朱红 #D62C24、钴蓝 #1E4EA0、黑 #181210，印在暖奶油纸 #EBD9B4 上；叠印和浅网只能得到橙、绿、棕、土黄、浅蓝、灰。实地平印，只有天空、皮肤、地面这类渐变处挂网（网点约画幅宽 1/250），各版网角不同，错位 1–3 像素，带石版颗粒。故事围绕的那个主角用黄实地叠朱红实地，得到平的橙（如 #D97757）。构图用夸张透视：放射线汇向主要动作，几件事同时发生；有人群时画成许多可辨的小人，只有一个人物也成立。镜头：上摇，横移，在动作中切到高潮。避免：四色以外的颜色、只有圆点的人群、挂网挂成粉色的主角。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nA circus chromolithograph after the Strobridge Lithographing Co. (c. 1890). Flat ink, heavy black outlines (about 1/300 of frame width), crescent shadows, light from upper left. Four stones: chrome yellow #F4C416, vermilion #D62C24, cobalt #1E4EA0, key black #181210 on warm cream paper #EBD9B4; overprints and tints give only orange, green, brown, tan, pale blue, grey. Solids print flat; screens (dots about 1/250 of frame width) only on gradients (sky, skin, ground), each stone at its own angle, 1-3 px off register, stone grain. The hero the story centres on is solid yellow under solid vermilion, a flat orange (e.g. #D97757). Exaggerated perspective, radial lines converging on the action, several actions at once; any crowd is tiny individual figures, though a lone figure works. Camera: tilt up, lateral dolly, cut on action. Avoid: off-palette colours, dot-only crowds, a screened, pinkish hero.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n四色彩色石印,Strobridge 马戏团海报,平涂油墨 + 粗黑轮廓\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮伟大的塞巴斯蒂安：金色扇贝边的亮片斗篷，倒挂在秋千上。上摇找到秋千杠上的它，中间的大象横移让游行一直往前走，最后在动作中切到接人那一下，时间卡在秋千荡到右边、和海报构图一致的位置，它把手臂甩向观众。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 大帐篷全景：三个表演圈、碗形看台、跑马道上的大象；沿帐篷往上摇，落到倒挂在最高秋千上的 Clawd。（0.8s）\n2. 横移跟拍大象游行：一头卷着一头的尾巴，背上坐着亮片舞女，前景是围栏和前排观众的后脑勺。（0.6s）\n3. 动作中切到接人：Clawd 披亮片斗篷、膝弯倒挂，一只手抓住粉衣女飞人，另一只手向观众甩开，秋千把它们荡向右边；镜头跟着微推并略微倾斜。（1s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nCecil B. DeMille, 1952,The Greatest Show on Earth one-sheet (1952),Strobridge Lithographing Co. circus posters\n"},{"id":"S6-O26","name":"乱世忠魂","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O26.jpg","markdown":"# O26 乱世忠魂（From Here to Eternity）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 宽银幕 1950–1959\n- 长什么样：1953 年的高调黑白：硬朗阳光、压暗的天空、泛白的浪沫、映着天的湿沙，银盐颗粒和光晕。拍哈洛纳海湾浪里那一吻；坐在熔岩上吹军号的 Clawd 是唯一的颜色。\n- 风格：高调海滩黑白银幕\n- 关键词：2D·B&W\n- 年份：1953\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n50 年代初高调的黑白外景摄影，用 2D 画出照片感：不描线、不平涂，只用柔和渐变加细腻纹理（沙粒、岩石麻点、布纹）。明暗：约七成画面亮于中灰；天空浅中灰，只比亮云暗一档；深黑很少。默认正午硬朗的太阳，影子短而清楚；其他光线下仍保持浅天空和明亮结构。水、浪沫或雾在最亮处冲白，留住蕾丝细节；湿的表面映着天空。按印片收尾：柔和 S 曲线、略软的镜头、高光晕、每帧刷新的细颗粒（±4%）、轻暗角、轻微闪烁。只有故事围绕的那个主角整个人保留颜色（如 #D97757），明暗也有照片感。镜头：从天空下摇，平视紧框，再慢慢拉远。对标摄影师 Burnett Guffey（《乱世忠魂》）。避免：低调暗沉、死白一片、卡通平涂。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nEarly-1950s high-key black-and-white photography, painted in 2D to read as a photo: no outlines or flat fills, only soft gradients plus fine texture (sand, rock pitting, cloth). Values: about 70% of the frame above mid-grey; sky light-mid grey, one step below the bright clouds; deep blacks small. Default: hard midday sun, short crisp shadows; under other light keep the pale sky and bright values. Water, foam and mist blow out at the peaks, keeping lace detail; wet surfaces mirror the sky. Print finish: gentle S-curve, slightly soft lens, halation, fresh fine grain (±4%), light vignette, flicker. Only the hero the story centres on keeps colour, whole figure (e.g. #D97757), shaded photographically. Camera: tilt down from the sky, tight level frame, slow pull-back. Reference: cinematographer Burnett Guffey (*From Here to Eternity*). Avoid: low-key frames, white blanks, cartoon fills.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n1953 年高调黑白摄影,Burnett Guffey,银盐颗粒与光晕\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮普鲁伊特：橄榄色船形帽，拿着海报上那支军号。天空一镜往下摇进海湾，中间一镜海浪盖过那一吻，切到全景时 Clawd 踩着拍子把军号举到嘴边，像是在为这场戏吹号。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 开在哈洛纳海湾上空，积云间飞过一队飞机；镜头往下摇到海面。（0.5s）\n2. 平视紧框浪里的恋人：浪头冲上来盖过他们，又退成蕾丝般的白沫，两人一直在吻；镜头慢推并向右漂移。（1.1s）\n3. 海湾全景：戴船形帽的 Clawd 坐在熔岩石台上，举起军号朝恋人吹响；镜头微微拉远。（0.9s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nFred Zinnemann, 1953,Burnett Guffey (cinematography),From Here to Eternity one-sheet (1953)\n"},{"id":"S6-O27","name":"码头风云","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O27.jpg","markdown":"# O27 码头风云（On the Waterfront）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 宽银幕 1950–1959\n- 长什么样：本·沙恩的社会现实主义：颤抖的转印墨线，下面是错开的平涂蛋彩色块，大手长脸的工人，颜色克制在灰蓝、砖红、赭黄。沙恩画的就是码头工人和工会，正是这部片的世界。\n- 风格：本·沙恩社会现实主义\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1954\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n仿本·沙恩的社会写实蛋彩画。墨线（宽约画幅宽 1/500）会颤：细抖叠着慢摆，一个轮廓分两三笔、不闭合，偶有转印鬼线（旁边多一道偏移的淡线）。颜色是平涂蛋彩块，比墨线偏 2–4 像素，边缘毛糙，每块约三分之一面积带干笔拖痕，拖痕一笔宽（约画幅宽 1/100），不随色块放大；明暗只用同色深一档的硬边平色。配色按用途：灰蓝 #768492 管天空和冷色区，砖红 #9E4834、赭黄 #C89852 管房屋、土地和衣服，墨 #1F1A1A，底板暖白 #F0E9DA；皮肤浅赭；其他颜色由这几色加深或加灰。故事围绕的那个主角的主要衣服用唯一的暖色（如 #D97757）。人物头大手大、长脸重下巴，衣服下垂。镜头：短推，上摇，收在近景。避免：平滑渐变、干净闭合的轮廓、鲜亮讨喜的颜色。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nBen Shahn's social-realist tempera. Ink lines (about 1/500 of frame width) jitter and wander, break each outline into two or three strokes, sometimes doubled by a fainter offset ghost (a blotted line). Flat tempera blocks sit 2-4 px off the line, rough-edged, dry-brush streaks (one brush wide, about 1/100 of frame width) over a third of each block; model form with a darker flat of the same hue. Palette by role: slate #768492 sky and cool areas, brick #9E4834 and ochre #C89852 ground, buildings, clothes, ink #1F1A1A, warm board #F0E9DA; skin pale ochre; other colours: these darkened or greyed. The hero the story centres on wears the one warm accent on its main garment (e.g. #D97757). Big heads and hands, long faces, heavy jaws, heavy clothes. Camera: short pushes, tilt up, close-up. Avoid: smooth gradients, clean closed outlines, cheerful colour.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n颤抖的转印墨线,平涂蛋彩色块,本·沙恩,50 年代美国社会现实主义\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮特里：深藏青针织帽、红黑格子的领子和袖口，手上停着一只鸽子。从码头上争抢的手硬切到安静的屋顶，两个镜头就把这部片的两个世界摆在一起；中间一镜鸽群飞起，近景停在 Clawd 和鸽子身上。表演 1.8 倍速。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. PIER B 门口清晨派工的中景：码头工人挤在门前，一双双手举起来抢铜工牌，Clawd 不在画面里；镜头轻推。（0.8s）\n2. 屋顶鸽舍全景：戴针织帽、立着格子领的 Clawd 举手托着一只鸽子，旁边是伊迪，身后是水塔和曼哈顿天际线，鸽群飞起；镜头慢推并略微上摇。（1s）\n3. Clawd 和手上灰鸽的近景，肩后是货轮、屋顶和天际线；镜头轻推。（0.8s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nElia Kazan, 1954,Boris Kaufman (cinematography),On the Waterfront one-sheet (1954),Ben Shahn, Handball (1939),Ben Shahn, Welders (1943)\n"},{"id":"S6-O28","name":"君子好逑","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O28.jpg","markdown":"# O28 君子好逑（Marty）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 宽银幕 1950–1959\n- 长什么样：1955 年的电视录像，在布朗克斯暖色客厅的电视里播：扫描线、荧光晕、圆角凸屏、雪花，只有 Clawd 有颜色。原作是直播电视剧，所以当电视拍。\n- 风格：50 年代电视显像管录像\n- 关键词：2D (B&W screen)\n- 年份：1955\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n把场景做成 50 年代木壳电视里播放的 kinescope 录像。屏幕里：黑白，冷色荧光调（白位约 #DCE6EA），画面发软（半分辨率再放大），有光晕、重影、撕裂带、缓慢滚动的暗带、少量雪花、抬起的黑位、闪烁和颗粒；只有故事围绕的那个主角保留颜色（如 #D97757），同样带这些瑕疵。显像管：圆角、四边外鼓，约 330 条扫描线，玻璃反光。屏幕外是暖色扁平插画风的客厅（赭黄墙纸、木家具、台灯），台灯打暖光，屏幕在附近墙面投出明显的冷蓝光。招牌镜头：电视占画面约六成，先停约 2 秒，再用 4–5 秒推进玻璃，直到节目铺满画面、扫描线还在，然后用演播室式稳定中景拍戏。对标导演 Delbert Mann 的 Philco 电视剧场直播剧。避免：雪花盖住画面、客厅沉闷平光。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nA 1950s kinescope playing on a wood-cabinet television. Inside the screen: black-and-white with a cool phosphor tint (#DCE6EA whites), soft (half resolution, scaled up), halation, ghosting, tearing bands, a rolling hum bar, light snow, lifted blacks, flicker, grain; only the story's hero keeps its colour (e.g. #D97757), under the same artefacts. Tube: rounded corners, sides bowing outward, about 330 scanlines, glass reflections. Outside, a warm flat-illustrated living room (ochre wallpaper, wood, a table lamp), lit warm by the lamp; the screen casts a clear cold-blue glow on nearby walls. Signature shot: hold about 2 s with the TV filling 60% of the frame, then push through the glass (4-5 s) until the broadcast fills the frame, scanlines visible, then steady TV-studio mid shots. Reference: director Delbert Mann's live Philco Television Playhouse dramas. Avoid: heavy snow, a dull, evenly lit room.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\nkinescope 黑白录像,圆角显像管与扫描线,暖色 50 年代客厅插画\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮马蒂：白色屠夫纸帽和一条条纹领带，害羞地靠在柱子边。推进玻璃把我们从客厅带进节目；双人镜头里它鼓起勇气穿过舞池，最后一个硬切落在邀舞上：它伸出手，克拉拉的笑随后才来。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 妈妈的客厅：左边是台灯和转盘电话，右边是她的翼背椅，电视里在播星尘舞厅；镜头推进屏幕玻璃。（0.8s）\n2. 跨过舞池的双人镜头，扫描线仍在：戴屠夫纸帽、系条纹领带的 Clawd 从柱子边走向克拉拉；镜头向右跟移。（0.9s）\n3. 邀舞的中景：Clawd 伸出手，克拉拉抬眼看它，笑了；镜头轻推并向右漂移。（0.8s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nDelbert Mann, 1955,Philco-Goodyear Television Playhouse, Marty (1953),Marty one-sheet (1955)\n"},{"id":"S6-O29","name":"环游地球八十天","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O29.jpg","markdown":"# O29 环游地球八十天（Around the World in 80 Days）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 宽银幕 1950–1959\n- 长什么样：50 年代平面几何卡通：色块和墨线错位套印、墨线断续、奶油纸上的蜡笔颗粒。本片 6 分钟的片尾动画就出自 Saul Bass，正是这种现代主义画法。\n- 风格：50 年代 UPA 现代主义卡通\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1956\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n50 年代 UPA 现代主义卡通。墨线 #221C19 约画面宽的 1/400，有粗细、偶尔断开；色块相对墨线统一朝一个方向错开约 1/300。物体由重叠的平面剪片拼成：一块受光面，加一块同色深约一成半的平色暗面。明亮通透：浅水绿天空和奶油纸 #F2E9D3 占大半画面，芥末黄 #E3A92B、青绿 #2B8C86、橙红 #D4452B 只作点缀；天空、草绿、灰蓝由这些色兑纸色得到。其他时段只改天空颜色。全画面轻蜡笔颗粒（亮度 ±5%），少量纸白斑点。海报构图：一个大图形（建筑、日轮或山峰），旁边是带轮廓的棱角人物。光束画成浅色平涂楔形。机位之间每一两秒硬切，段内慢视差。对标 UPA《Gerald McBoing-Boing》（1950）、Saul Bass《环游地球八十天》片尾（1956）。避免：渐变、套色太准、饱和色铺满画面。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\n1950s UPA modernist cartoon. Uneven ink lines #221C19 about 1/400 of frame width, sometimes broken; colour blocks sit about 1/300 of frame width off them, all one way. Objects are overlapping flat cut-out facets: a lit face and a shadow face one flat step (about 15%) darker. Pale aqua sky and cream paper #F2E9D3 cover most of the frame; mustard #E3A92B, teal #2B8C86, orange-red #D4452B are accents; sky, greens and slate blues are these mixed toward paper. Other times of day change only the sky colour. Crayon grain ±5% brightness everywhere, few flecks. Poster layout: one big graphic shape (a building or sun disc), angular outlined figures beside it. Light beams: flat pale wedges. Hard cuts every 1–2 s; slow parallax drift. After UPA, *Gerald McBoing-Boing* (1950); Saul Bass, *Around the World in 80 Days* titles (1956). Avoid: gradients, perfect registration, saturated colour everywhere.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\nUPA 有限动画,Saul Bass 片尾动画,1956 原海报配色,蜡笔颗粒印刷\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮福格：高礼帽、收起的雨伞、一杯香槟。每两拍一刀硬切：地图、全景、近景。红色航线绕地球跑一圈，气球飘过阿尔卑斯，近景落在路路通从雪峰上舀雪冰镇香槟。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 地图插入镜头，定机位：青绿底上的地球仪转动，红色航线依次跑过伦敦、苏伊士、孟买、横滨、旧金山；右边一只小气球里坐着 Clawd。（0.9s）\n2. 全景：芥末黄配蓝色棋盘带的热气球飘过分面的阿尔卑斯和瑞士小镇，戴高礼帽的 Clawd 站在吊篮边；缓慢横移。（0.8s）\n3. Clawd 扮的福格近景：高礼帽、收起的雨伞、举着香槟杯，旁边路路通探身用冰桶从雪峰尖上舀雪；轻微推近。（0.8s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nMichael Anderson, 1956,Saul Bass (end-title animation),UPA, Gerald McBoing-Boing (1950),1956 original poster\n"},{"id":"S6-O30","name":"桂河大桥","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O30.jpg","markdown":"# O30 桂河大桥（The Bridge on the River Kwai）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 宽银幕 1950–1959\n- 长什么样：钉在绘图桌上的总布置图：普鲁士蓝底上的白线、折痕、水渍和红铅笔修改。电影讲一个执意把桥造好的上校，所以把桥画成他的工程图。\n- 风格：氰版工程蓝图\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1957\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n青晒工程蓝图。图纸铺满画面（胡桃木绘图桌只在拉远时露出）：斑驳的普鲁士蓝纸 #143874，曝光不匀，边缘过曝发绿，有折痕和淡水渍。白线 #ECF4FF 锐利但是印出来的：轮廓约画面宽的 1/600，剖面线和尺寸线减半，曝光弱处断续，带几像素青色微晕。用制图语法把主体画得密：立面、剖面、详图圈、尺寸链、虚线、单线工程字、标题栏。批注和动作用红铅笔 #D63426，写用户的语言。唯一的主角是图上唯一手绘上色的东西，比图纸比例放大（至少占画面高五分之一），不透明水粉，一种暖色分几个深浅（比如橙 #D97757），其余没有暖色。镜头：推近时各层视差，再切详图。避免：霓虹发光、干净的数码蓝、空荡的图纸。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nCyanotype engineering blueprint. The sheet fills the frame (the walnut drafting table shows only on a pull-back): mottled Prussian-blue paper #143874, uneven exposure, a greenish over-exposed margin, creases, faint water stains. White lines #ECF4FF, sharp but printed: outlines about 1/600 of frame width, hatching and dimensions half that, broken where exposure was weak, a slight cyan bloom. Draw the subject densely in drafting grammar: elevations, sections, detail circles, dimension chains, hidden lines, single-stroke lettering, a title block. Notes and actions in red pencil #D63426, in the user's language. The one hero subject is the only hand-painted element, drawn above scale (at least 1/5 of frame height), opaque gouache in one warm hue (e.g. orange #D97757); nothing else is warm. Camera: layered parallax push-in; cut to detail views. Avoid: neon glow, clean digital blue, sparse sheets.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n氰版晒图,木栈桥总布置图,红铅笔修改,水粉上色的主角\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮尼科尔森上校：英军大檐帽、军官短手杖，站在自己的桥上吹口哨。三个镜头讲因果：火车开上桥、引爆器手柄按下、桥跨炸断。爆炸也留在图纸里：红铅笔乱线成火球，排线画烟，不画真实的火光。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 桥立面的中全景：Clawd（尼科尔森上校）站在桥面上吹口哨，音符往上飘，左下战俘列队行进，右边是引爆器，火车从最右边开上来；缓慢推近。（1s）\n2. 引爆器箱特写（PLUNGER – ONE PUSH）：T 形手柄按下，红铅笔引线通向炸药；轻微推近。（0.5s）\n3. 回到桥立面：炸药炸开，红铅笔乱线画成的火球裹在排线画的烟团里，桥跨和火车车厢落进河里；缓慢拉远。（1s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nDavid Lean, 1957,1957 original poster,Timber trestle engineering drawings\n"},{"id":"S6-O31","name":"金粉世界","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O31.jpg","markdown":"# O31 金粉世界（Gigi）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 宽银幕 1950–1959\n- 长什么样：图卢兹-罗特列克式彩色石印：大块平涂、飞白轮廓、用喷溅墨点代替网点、讽刺漫画式面孔。琪琪的高光时刻是走进马克西姆餐厅、全巴黎回头盯着她，正是罗特列克画过的巴黎。\n- 风格：罗特列克美好年代石印\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1958\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n罗特列克式美好年代彩色石印。大块平涂色版，边缘略手抖；人物和主要形体用饱蘸的笔刷勾轮廓，起笔钝、中段鼓、收尾分叉飞白，粗约画面宽的 1/250。牙刷弹的墨点只在背光面、投影和光晕处：1920×1080 下点径一到三像素，稀疏，合计不到画面一成半，颜色取底色深一档。大面用成组的同向干笔条纹。色板：黄 #F1C232、橙红 #E0602C、橄榄绿 #6E7036、黑 #1D1A16，底是带石版颗粒的奶油纸 #EFE3C3；另加灰暗的瓶绿和淡紫，肤色是橙红兑纸色。人物是夸张的讽刺侧脸，画出五官；前景放一个被画框裁掉的大人物。光平而匀，阴影也是平涂色版。镜头：浅视差，慢慢横摇过场景，落到中景。避免：慕夏式光环、平滑渐变、规整网点、满屏黑点。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nBelle Époque colour lithograph after Toulouse-Lautrec. Flat colour plates with wobbly edges; figures and main shapes outlined in a loaded brush (blunt start, swelling middle, split dry tail), about 1/250 of frame width. Crachis only on shadow sides, cast shadows and glows: 1–3 px dots at 1920×1080, sparse, under 15% of the frame, a darker step of the colour beneath. Large surfaces get grouped dry-brush streaks. Palette: yellow #F1C232, orange-red #E0602C, olive #6E7036, black #1D1A16 on cream stone-grained paper #EFE3C3, plus a muted bottle teal and greyed mauve; flesh is orange-red thinned with paper. Caricatured profiles with drawn faces; one large figure cropped by the frame edge in front. Flat, even light; shadows are flat plates. Camera: shallow parallax, a slow pan settling on a medium shot. Avoid: Mucha halos, smooth gradients, regular halftone, all-over speckle.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n彩色石印海报,图卢兹-罗特列克,牙刷喷墨点（crachis）,Cecil Beaton 服装\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮加斯东：高礼帽、白领结、手杖，琪琪挽着它。两人一直往里走，满屋的目光跟着转。三刀卡在拍子上：进门全景、横摇众人瞪眼、再回到两人，琪琪对镜头眨眼收尾。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 马克西姆餐厅全景：戴高礼帽、白领结的 Clawd（加斯东）挽着琪琪走进来，两侧是被裁掉的前景剪影；最近的几桌客人开始回头；缓慢推近并向右漂移。（0.8s）\n2. 沿红丝绒卡座横摇：一张张讽刺漫画式的面孔转过来瞪着看，长柄眼镜举起，扇子停住。（0.7s）\n3. 两人继续往里走的中景；琪琪的目光转向镜头，眨了一下眼，和原海报一样；轻微右移。（1s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nVincente Minnelli, 1958,Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec, Moulin Rouge: La Goulue (1891),Toulouse-Lautrec, Divan Japonais (1893),Cecil Beaton (costumes),1958 MGM poster\n"},{"id":"S6-O32","name":"宾虚","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O32.jpg","markdown":"# O32 宾虚（Ben-Hur）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 宽银幕 1950–1959\n- 长什么样：程序生成的 3D 大竞技场，宽银幕史诗光感：沙漠烈日、沙尘、大理石和金色，特艺七彩调色加 65mm 颗粒。战车赛是全片最有名的段落，就直接拍比赛，头顶是海报上的金色巨神。\n- 风格：3D 大竞技场战车赛 · 65mm 史诗\n- 关键词：3D\n- 年份：1959\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n1959 年 65mm 宽银幕史诗，用 3D 搭。默认是硬朗的高太阳（约 50°），影子短而利，天空钴蓝、到地平线渐成沙白；太阳低时影子仍硬但拉长，天空转琥珀色。大理石、镀金、红花岗岩和沙色石头，做出线脚和槽柱，不能是光秃方盒。暖色尘雾约两成混进中景颜色。调色像 Technicolor 拷贝：暖、饱和、略高反差，不过曝不加辉光；细颗粒（±3%）、轻微片门晃动。核心是规模：背景布满宏伟建筑、雕像和重复元素，绝不空。唯一的主角（比如橙 #D97757）是画面里最饱和的东西；重复动作（步伐、轮、桨）节奏随速度。招牌镜头：全画幅等效 35 到 50mm，低机位贴着运动的主角跟拍；其次是大全景、正俯、近景、仰拍。避免：干净的 CG 光、空荡粗糙的布景、过曝。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\n1959 65mm widescreen epic built in 3D. Default: hard high sun (about 50°), short crisp shadows, cobalt sky fading to sandy white; under a low sun shadows stay hard but long, the sky turns amber. Marble, gilt, red granite and sandstone, with mouldings and fluted columns, never bare boxes. Warm sunlit haze mixes about 20% into mid-distance colours. Technicolor-print grade: warm, saturated, slightly contrasty, no clipping or bloom; fine grain (±3%), slight gate weave. Scale is the point: monumental architecture, statues and repeated elements fill the background, never empty. The one hero subject (e.g. orange #D97757) is the most saturated thing in frame; any repeated motion (stride, wheel, oar) keeps time with speed. Signature shot: low tracking beside the moving hero, 35–50 mm full-frame; then wide, top-down, close-up, low angle. Avoid: clean CG light, empty or primitive sets, overexposure.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n程序生成 3D（three.js）,MGM Camera 65 宽银幕,特艺七彩印片调色,第二摄制组动作调度\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮犹大·宾虚：额上一条亚麻头带，一手握缰一手举鞭，驾四匹白马。五秒六个镜头，照 1959 年第二摄制组的拍法走一遍：起跑、弯道、跟拍、车轮、近景、海报构图。每个机位都跟着战车在动，节奏不掉。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 起跑白线大全景：九辆四马战车并排，Clawd 的白马队在其中，身后看台坐满；车队冲出，镜头慢慢收紧。（0.9s）\n2. 东弯高位俯拍：战车绕过镀金转弯柱甩出去，身后扬起弧形尘土。（0.8s）\n3. 贴着 Clawd 的战车并行跟拍：白马全速奔跑、车辐飞转、尘土飞扬；镜头随车前移。（1.1s）\n4. 两车之间的低机位：梅萨拉的锯齿刀毂咬住 Clawd 的车轮，火花四溅；镜头跟车一起走。（0.6s）\n5. Clawd 在战车上挥鞭的近景，旁边是梅萨拉的黑马；缓慢推近。（0.8s）\n6. 海报构图：低角度侧拍，Clawd 的白马队在中央隔墙的金色坐像巨神下追平，身后是看台和总督看台；镜头慢慢放宽。（0.8s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nWilliam Wyler, 1959,Yakima Canutt & Andrew Marton (chariot race),Robert Surtees (cinematography),1959 MGM poster\n"},{"id":"S6-O33","name":"桃色公寓","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O33.jpg","markdown":"# O33 桃色公寓（The Apartment）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 摇摆年代 1960–1969\n- 长什么样：Saul Bass 式的白线在黑底上滑入，搭出电影最有名的一点透视无尽办公大厅，再填上灰调。本片片头就是 Bass 做的；全画面唯一的暖色是 Clawd 和钥匙牌。\n- 风格：Saul Bass 线条片头 · 黑白\n- 关键词：2D·B&W\n- 年份：1960\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n把 Saul Bass 片头的线条做成布景，黑白加一个暖色。镜头从全黑开始：细白线（约画面宽的 1/800）滑入、向纵深长成网格；两三秒内灰面填满，灯一排排亮起。之后完成态停约三秒：中到浅灰，从 #140F0D 到 #F4F1E8 均匀分四五级平涂，每个形体有约画面宽 1/600 的深色轮廓。严格一点透视，灭点居中；一样的东西重复排向纵深（有人群时是桌子和人，单人时是柱子、路灯、窗格）。人物是两色平涂。唯一的暖色是橙红 #E2472A，只给唯一的主角和一件道具；所有光都是白的。轻颗粒、柔暗角。镜头：急推到主角，硬切到正上方俯视，网格变成图案。避免：草稿感的线、渐变、颜色渗入、透视歪、没填色的暗线框。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nSaul Bass title lines turned into a set. A shot opens on black: thin white lines (about 1/800 of frame width) slide in and grow into a grid; by 2–3 s grey planes fill in and lights switch on row by row. Then hold the finished frame 3 s: mid-to-light grey, flat fills in 4–5 even steps from #140F0D to #F4F1E8, every shape outlined in dark line about 1/600 of frame width. Strict one-point perspective, centred vanishing point; identical elements repeat into depth (desks and people, or posts, lamps, windows). Figures: flat two-tone shapes. The only warm colour is orange-red #E2472A, for the one hero subject and one prop; all light stays white. Camera: a fast push to the hero, a hard cut to top-down, the grid becoming pattern. Avoid: sketchy lines, gradients, colour leaks, skewed perspective, unfilled wireframe.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\nSaul Bass 片头设计,一点透视办公大厅,黑白胶片颗粒,唯一的暖色\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮 C.C. 巴克斯特：圆顶礼帽、挂着橙红牌子（51 W 67）的公寓钥匙，坐在前排桌沿。办公大厅一根线一根线画出来，第 2 拍举钥匙、镜头急推；最后切到俯视的桌阵网格，和下一部开场俯拍的曼哈顿街区网格做图形匹配。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 一镜到底：白线从全黑里搭出无尽的办公大厅，灰调和天花灯一排排亮起；坐在前排桌沿的 Clawd（巴克斯特）在第 2 拍举起公寓钥匙，镜头急推过去。（1.9s）\n2. 硬切到正上方俯视：桌子排成一张网格，Clawd 仰面躺在自己桌上，手里是钥匙和牌子；缓慢推近。（0.6s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nBilly Wilder, 1960,Saul Bass (title sequence),Joseph LaShelle (cinematography),Alexandre Trauner (production design)\n"},{"id":"S6-O34","name":"西区故事","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O34.jpg","markdown":"# O34 西区故事（West Side Story）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 摇摆年代 1960–1969\n- 长什么样：红橙特艺七彩晚霞下的上西区 3D 街区，饱和、舞台化：红砖、之字形消防梯、屋顶水塔、能摆姿势的舞者人偶。重演电影的俯拍开场、Jets 打响指和海报上那一跃。\n- 风格：特艺七彩 3D 舞台\n- 关键词：3D\n- 年份：1961\n\n## 风格提示词\n\nTechnicolor 3D 舞台布景，像 60 年代棚拍歌舞片。几何简化，每个面都细节密集（窗洞窗台、檐口、栏杆、梯子、绳线）；表面是有质感的砖、灰泥或岩石，不能素面。剧场布光：低角度暖阳斜扫表面拉出长影；冷补光约为主光四分之一。手绘天空以橙红 #E0481E 为主，云带镶金边，只在最上沿带一点紫；远处融进暖雾。唯一的主角用饱和橙 #D97757，至少占画面高六分之一，背后衬深色砖墙或加一圈黑边，不能化进天空。人物是木偶式假人。暖而饱和的调色，细颗粒（±3%）。招牌镜头：衬着橙色天空的低角度仰拍；其次一个连续镜头从高处降进布景、再贴地推进。避免：写实脏旧、紫粉天空、素面墙、动作跳变。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nTechnicolor 3D stage set, like a 1960s studio musical. Simplified geometry, dense detail on every built surface (openings with sills, ledges, railings, ladders, strung lines); textured brick, plaster or rock, never plain. Theatrical light: a low warm sun rakes surfaces with long shadows; cool fill a quarter of the key. Painted sky of red-orange #E0481E, gold-edged cloud bands, violet only at the top edge; warm haze in the distance. The one hero subject in saturated orange #D97757, at least 1/6 of frame height, set against dark brick or given a black rim so it never melts into the sky. Puppet-like mannequin figures. Warm, saturated grade, fine grain (±3%). Signature shot: a low angle against the orange sky; then one continuous descent into the set and a ground-level push. Avoid: photoreal grime, violet-pink skies, plain walls, poses that pop.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n程序生成 3D（three.js）,特艺七彩晚霞调色,Boris Leven 布景,1961 海报：红天、黑消防梯\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 混进 Jets 帮，没有戏服，靠场景认片。响指卡在鼓点上；腾跃是一条干净的抛物线，从下蹲到落地缓冲一气呵成。开场俯拍呼应上一部的桌阵网格；结尾划亮的火柴在画面正中，正是下一部日出的位置，做匹配剪辑。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 正上方俯拍西区街区网格（屋顶、水塔、操场球场），一路下降、抬头落进街道。（1.3s）\n2. 球场边低机位，Jets 帮在涂鸦墙前排成楔形打响指，Clawd 在前；一条连续的推镜绕过去推到 Clawd 近景，它的响指卡在鼓点上。（1.7s）\n3. 消防梯下仰拍：Clawd 下蹲、起跳，一条抛物线压在红天上，旁边是舞者的大跳；镜头随弧线抬起。（1.2s）\n4. 消防梯平台近景：Clawd 划亮火柴，镜头推近直到火苗停在画面正中，随后被吹灭。（0.8s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nRobert Wise & Jerome Robbins, 1961,Boris Leven (production design),Saul Bass (end titles),1961 original poster\n"},{"id":"S6-O35","name":"阿拉伯的劳伦斯","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O35.jpg","markdown":"# O35 阿拉伯的劳伦斯（Lawrence of Arabia）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 摇摆年代 1960–1969\n- 长什么样：看得见笔触方向的史诗海报水粉，冷暖对比强烈：琥珀色沙漠对钴蓝天空。《阿拉伯的劳伦斯》原海报就是 Howard Terpning 用这种画法画的。\n- 风格：Terpning 史诗海报水粉\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1962\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n60 年代手绘史诗电影海报，水粉打底、油彩提亮。先把形体画扎实再上色：铺不透明中间调底色，再用顺形体走的笔触（大面宽约画面宽的 1/80 到 1/50，脸和手用小笔），边缘柔和衔接；每个形体分亮、中、暗三级；干笔只用在尘土、布料和远处人物上；最后在受光边缘压锐利的暖奶油色高光。冷暖对比：钴蓝天空 #1F4D97 过渡到浅蓝 #A9C3D6，低垂的太阳周围转暖；地面 #EEC27A（任何地形），皮肤、泥土和衣服用赭红 #B56A33，远处阴影用紫 #6C6394。主角居中最大，一个斜向动作，布料飘起；身后可以有更小更松的人物退远。轻画板颗粒、暖暗角、雾气。运动：起伏、缓推到仰角近景、甩镜出画。避免：又碎又匀的刮擦线、平涂数码色、只有冷色。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\n1960s hand-painted epic film poster, gouache with oil highlights. Draw forms solidly, then paint: an opaque mid-tone base, directional strokes following each form (1/80–1/50 of frame width on large areas, smaller on faces and hands), soft-blended edges; model every form in three values; dry-brush only on dust, cloth and distant figures; sharp warm-cream highlights last on lit edges. Warm–cool contrast: cobalt sky #1F4D97 to pale blue #A9C3D6, warming to cream near a low sun; ground #EEC27A (any terrain); ochre-red #B56A33 for skin, earth and costume; violet #6C6394 for distant shadows. The hero centred and largest, a diagonal gesture, cloth streaming; smaller, looser figures may recede behind. Light board grain (±3%), warm vignette, haze. Motion: bounce, slow push to a low close-up, a whip pan out. Avoid: scratchy uniform hatching, flat digital fills, cold-only colour.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n水粉加油彩海报画,Howard Terpning 1962 海报,Freddie Young 70mm 沙漠光\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮劳伦斯：白袍、白头巾、金色头箍，骑骆驼举弯刀。开场就是影史最有名的匹配剪辑：上一部的火柴火苗变成沙漠日出。整段冲锋恒定 1.5 倍速，弯刀卡拍举起、刀尖一闪，最后甩镜头出画，接《汤姆·琼斯》。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 空旷沙漠的黎明大远景：一轮巨大的太阳在画面正中透过热浪升起，和上一部吹灭的火柴做匹配剪辑；缓慢推近。（0.7s）\n2. 跟拍车上的海报构图：Clawd（劳伦斯）骑骆驼混在冲锋的骑兵里奔来，弯刀先放低，卡拍举起、刀尖一闪；颠簸晃动，缓慢推近。（1s）\n3. 低角度推向高举的弯刀，然后向右甩镜头出画，接下一部。（0.8s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nDavid Lean, 1962,Howard Terpning (1962 poster),Freddie Young (70mm cinematography)\n"},{"id":"S6-O36","name":"汤姆·琼斯","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O36.jpg","markdown":"# O36 汤姆·琼斯（Tom Jones）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 摇摆年代 1960–1969\n- 长什么样：乔治王时代的讽刺版画：霍加斯式粗细变化的刻线和有方向的排线，上面刷罗兰森式鲜艳的手工淡彩，稍稍出线，再加一个对白气泡。电影本身就在模仿霍加斯笔下的 18 世纪英国。\n- 风格：手工上色讽刺铜版画\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1963\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n乔治王时代讽刺版画：先刻铜版，印完再手工上色。墨只有棕黑色的线：雕刀轮廓约画面宽的 1/400，起伏鼓胀，背光一侧更粗；排线顺形体走、铺满每个面（线距约画面宽的 1/250），只在暗部交叉，平面用直尺排线。颜色几乎铺满整幅，清透但饱满：水色溢出墨线几像素，用酒红 #B3262E、黄铜 #D8A93A、肉色 #F0C4A4、石板蓝 #6D8AA6，大面用暖赭和棕褐。浅棕褐帘纹纸，淡霉点，版框印痕，刻线边框。按 William Hogarth 的方式布置：大鼻子的漫画人物，主体身边是旁观者或搞笑的道具、动物；一个手写斜体对话气泡，用用户的语言。镜头：全幅停两秒，急推，横滑到另一处细节，再给近景。避免：机械均匀的排线、大片空白纸、颜色严丝合缝填在线里、颜色发脏。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nGeorgian satirical print: a copperplate engraving, hand-coloured. Brown-black line only: swelling burin outlines about 1/400 of frame width, heavier on the shadow side; hatching follows each form and covers every surface (spacing about 1/250 of frame width), cross-hatched in the darks, ruled on flat areas. Colour covers nearly the whole picture, clear but strong: washes spilling a few pixels off the line in claret #B3262E, brass #D8A93A, flesh #F0C4A4, slate blue #6D8AA6, warm ochre and umber on large surfaces. Sepia laid paper, foxing, plate mark, engraved border. Stage it like William Hogarth: big-nosed caricatures, bystanders or comic props and animals around the subject; one slanted handwritten speech balloon in the user's language. Camera: hold the full plate 2 s, whip in, glide to another detail, close-up. Avoid: even mechanical hatching, blank paper, colour exactly inside lines, muddy colour.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n霍加斯铜版线刻,罗兰森/吉尔雷式手工上色,讽刺画对白气泡\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮汤姆·琼斯：金边三角帽、脸上一个口红唇印、手拿鸡腿。不用正反打，用一次横滑：从隔桌的双人镜头滑到沃特斯夫人眨眼、冒出对白气泡。再切近景：心形眼、陶醉一晃，摘下三角帽扣向镜头，画面全黑，下一部从黑里开场。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 从左边甩进隔着满桌菜的双人镜头，Clawd（汤姆）大口啃鸡腿；镜头横滑过桌面落到沃特斯夫人，她冒出对白气泡（Another Leg, Mr Jones?）并眨了一下眼。（1.3s）\n2. Clawd 近景：眼睛变成心形，陶醉地一晃，又啃一小口，然后摘下三角帽扣向镜头，画面变黑；缓慢推近。（1.2s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nTony Richardson, 1963,William Hogarth, A Rake's Progress / Marriage A-la-Mode,Thomas Rowlandson,James Gillray\n"},{"id":"S6-O37","name":"窈窕淑女","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O37.jpg","markdown":"# O37 窈窕淑女（My Fair Lady）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 摇摆年代 1960–1969\n- 长什么样：快速、带飞白的墨线，配粉色和品红水彩晕染、甩点，四周化进白纸。1964 年原海报就是 Bob Peak 的墨线加粉彩；场景是 Cecil Beaton 设计的黑白阿斯科特。\n- 风格：Bob Peak 时装插画\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1964\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n仿 Bob Peak 的 60 年代时装插画。墨线很快：转弯处变粗、直线处变细，后段飞白，收笔一甩；粗细在画面宽的 1/600 到 1/250 之间，小弯处变细。深色衣服、帽子和条纹用实心墨块；脸留白纸 #FBF7F1。水彩是松散柔边的色池，干后边缘积色更深：粉 #EE84B2 做主色，品红 #CE3076 做热点，加玫瑰粉 #F4B2CC 和一点梅紫 #80285C；次要人物和阴影用暖灰；带方向的甩点。场景只用几根线和这几种水彩带过。横排人物是核心：修长侧身，九到十个头高，横向重复（主角加暖灰人物或重影），黑白图案压在粉色上；水彩盖住画面六到八成。镜头：沿人物慢横移；转场借图形遮罩。避免：轮廓粗细一样、平涂数码色、多边形硬边色块、空白页上孤零零一个人。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\n1960s fashion illustration after Bob Peak. Fast ink line, thicker in turns, dry-brush tail, a flick off; 1/600–1/250 of frame width, thinning on tight curves. Solid black for dark clothing, hats and stripes; faces bare paper #FBF7F1. Loose, soft-edged watercolour pools, darker at drying edges: pink #EE84B2 as the main field, magenta #CE3076 for hot spots, rose #F4B2CC, a touch of plum #80285C; warm grey for secondary figures and shading; directional splatter. Settings are a few lines in these washes, not literal colour. The frieze is essential: slender profile figures 9–10 heads tall repeated across the frame (the hero plus grey-wash figures or echoes), black-and-white pattern over pink; washes cover 60–80% of the frame. Camera: slow drift along the figures; graphic-mask transitions. Avoid: even-width outlines, flat digital fills, polygon-edged washes, a lone figure on an empty page.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n快速墨线,粉色与品红水彩,Bob Peak 1964 海报,Cecil Beaton 阿斯科特服装\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮伊莉莎：巨大的白帽（条纹缎带、玫瑰、白鸵鸟羽），领口和裙边一圈蕾丝。开场从上一部的黑画面里掀起帽檐，结尾收进人群望远镜的两个圆圈，下一部《音乐之声》就在这两个圆里开场。三个镜头，节奏不急。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 从黑里，阿斯科特大帽的帽檐抬起，露出 Clawd（伊莉莎）的近景；它把目光转向镜头，微微屈膝行礼，画面轻轻往后收。（0.8s）\n2. 阿斯科特全景：Clawd 站在两排穿 Beaton 黑白服装、僵立的贵族前面；缓慢横移，它抬起下巴。（0.6s）\n3. 沿僵立的人群横摇，所有人同时举起望远镜，最后画面收进两个望远镜圆圈。（1.1s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nGeorge Cukor, 1964,Bob Peak (1964 poster),Cecil Beaton (costume designs)\n"},{"id":"S6-O38","name":"音乐之声","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O38.jpg","markdown":"# O38 音乐之声（The Sound of Music）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 摇摆年代 1960–1969\n- 长什么样：绘本感的 3D 程序阿尔卑斯：饱和的翠绿、钴蓝、雪白，雕塑感的山脊，棉花团积云，风吹过草地的草浪。重演开场的直升机航拍，最后落到山顶转圈的玛丽亚。\n- 风格：绘本感 3D 阿尔卑斯航拍\n- 关键词：3D\n- 年份：1965\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n绘本感的 3D 风景，60 年代宽银幕的饱和色，形体圆润光滑有雕塑感，不写实也不做低多边形。默认光：太阳高挂、略在镜头身后，大地满色：翠绿草地 #3FA33A、钴蓝天空 #2F6FD0、干净的白雪白云。风化山脊圆润，雪线顺坡，岩层暖灰赭，有云影。空气透视只加在最远的山脊，约两成半淡紫。积云是受光的白色棉花团。前景是齐膝密草和野花，随阵风弯伏。太阳低时只拉长影子、让云边变暖；其他场景也用这些颜色。一个连续镜头：前两秒高空飞越，再两秒降到草坡，之后贴草低机位环绕主体（每秒约 20°，主体约占画面高四分之一）。对标摄影师 Ted McCord（《音乐之声》，1965）和宫崎骏的草地。避免：泛紫、针尖山峰、写实灰岩、不动的草。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nStorybook 3D landscape in saturated 1960s widescreen colour: smooth sculpted forms, not low-poly or photoreal. Default light: bright sun high, a little behind the camera: emerald grass #3FA33A, cobalt sky #2F6FD0, clean white snow and clouds. Rounded ridges, snow lines following slope, grey-ochre rock bands, cloud shadows. Haze only on the farthest ridges, about 25% lilac. Cumulus are lit white cotton puffs. Knee-high grass and wildflowers fill the foreground, bending in gusts. A lower sun lengthens shadows and warms only cloud edges; other settings keep these colours. Camera: one continuous move: 0–2 s high flight, 2–4 s descent, then a low orbit through the grass around the subject (about 20° per second, subject about 1/4 of frame height). References: cinematographer Ted McCord (*The Sound of Music*, 1965); Miyazaki's meadows. Avoid: violet wash, needle peaks, grey photoreal rock, static grass.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n程序生成 3D 地形（three.js）,托德宽银幕色彩,Ted McCord 直升机开场,宫崎骏的草原\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮玛丽亚：头顶一朵雪绒花和一个小头巾结，照海报一手拎条纹毯包、一手拎吉他琴盒。转圈是一条贯穿三个镜头的连续曲线，每一刀都切在转到一半的时候；最后一拍双臂高举。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 画面从上一部的两个望远镜圆圈里打开到全屏：高空航拍，穿过棉花团积云掠过雪峰。（0.7s）\n2. 下降逼近山顶草坡：Clawd 小小地在画面正中，已经甩着毯包和琴盒转起来，远处是森林和田地拼块；镜头朝它降下去。（0.8s）\n3. 贴着草的低机位，镜头环绕转了一整圈的 Clawd，毯包和琴盒甩开，身后是雪峰和积云；最后它双臂高举。（1s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nRobert Wise, 1965,Ted McCord (cinematography),Howard Terpning (1965 poster),Hayao Miyazaki (meadows)\n"},{"id":"S6-O39","name":"日月精忠","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O39.jpg","markdown":"# O39 日月精忠（A Man for All Seasons）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 摇摆年代 1960–1969\n- 长什么样：古典大师油画肖像：平滑罩染塑形、一根根画出的毛领、天鹅绒高光、逐环刻画的金链，再加旧光油和龟裂纹。霍尔拜因 1527 年亲手画过托马斯·莫尔，这里用的就是他的构图。\n- 风格：霍尔拜因都铎肖像油画\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1966\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n仿小汉斯·霍尔拜因的北方文艺复兴油画肖像。一层层画：带色底、顺明暗的笔触、柔和罩染、最后细笔；成品光滑，背景和布料隐约见笔触。每种材质各有画法：毛皮一根根画，天鹅绒褶子亮脊锐利，金属每件两个高光点，呢料和皮革画出柔和绒面。任何主体都画成穿自己衣服的正式半身像；它的场景从窗口露出，或由手里一件东西交代。色板：深绿帘幕 #1F4A26，黑 #141113、天鹅绒红 #6E0C15、金 #B0801E、毛皮棕 #3E2818；皮肤暖色、体积完整。柔光从左上来。招牌镜头：衬着绿帘的半身像；其次是材质特写，再慢慢拉远到暖色光池照亮的暗房间。收尾叠暖色光油和轻龟裂。避免：平涂、数码硬边、均匀亮光、没有体积的脸。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nOil portrait after Hans Holbein the Younger. Paint in layers: a toned underlayer, directional strokes following light and shadow, soft glazes, fine detail last; smooth finish, brushwork faint in background and fabric. Render each material: fur hair by hair, velvet folds with sharp light ridges, metal with two highlights per piece, wool and leather with soft nap. Any subject becomes a formal half-length sitter in its own clothes; its setting shows through a window or in an object it holds. Palette: deep green curtain #1F4A26, black #141113, velvet red #6E0C15, gold #B0801E, fur brown #3E2818; warm, fully modelled skin. Soft light from upper left. Signature shot: the portrait against the curtain; then material close-ups, a slow pull back into a dark room lit by warm pools. Warm varnish, light craquelure. Avoid: flat fills, hard digital edges, even light, unmodelled faces.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n古典油画罩染,霍尔拜因《托马斯·莫尔爵士像》（1527）,烛光都铎室内\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮托马斯·莫尔：都铎扁帽、黑袍毛领、红天鹅绒袖子、SS 金链，手拿封了红蜡的信。接在转圈的草坡之后，这里是静：从大特写切到肖像只有一刀，之后一路拉远；蜡烛将熄，暗下来的画面接上下一部的夜。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 脸和 SS 金链、都铎玫瑰吊坠的大特写：Clawd（莫尔）先看向一侧，再把目光转向镜头，眨一下眼；缓慢推近。（0.9s）\n2. 霍尔拜因肖像构图（都铎扁帽、毛领、红天鹅绒袖子、绿色锦缎帘幕、手里的信）：庄重地点一下头，目光落到信上，然后缓慢拉远，露出烛光下的都铎房间；烛火将熄，画面暗下去。（1.6s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nFred Zinnemann, 1966,Hans Holbein the Younger, Sir Thomas More (1527),Holbein, The Ambassadors (1533),Ted Moore (cinematography)\n"},{"id":"S6-O40","name":"炎热的夜晚","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O40.jpg","markdown":"# O40 炎热的夜晚（In the Heat of the Night）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 摇摆年代 1960–1969\n- 长什么样：三色 Risograph 印刷：深蓝、海报橙红、荧光粉，带网点、颗粒和套色错位；光是用「少上墨」印出来的。呼应 1967 年海报的橙红热浪底和黑色剪影。\n- 风格：三色 Risograph 夜景\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1967\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n三色专色 Risograph 印刷：橙红 #EC5032 是主场（约占画面六成，带竖向流淌痕），深蓝 #162C76 给剪影和阴影，荧光粉 #FF48B0 只给轮廓光和光晕，印在暖白纸 #F7F2E7 上。每色是各自网角的半调，点径约画面宽的 1/300，版间错位二到四像素；有墨粒、针孔，逐帧轻微抖动。蓝剪影底下挖掉橙色，印成纯蓝；只有最暗处两色叠成近黑。光就是没有墨：光束、灯和被照亮的地面露出纸白，外圈一道粉。人物是带粉色边缘光的蓝剪影，肤色用三到六成橙色网点加一级蓝暗部。时间偏冷时仍保留红场，只让蓝色升到天空顶部。镜头：手持跟在提着光源的人身后，一个反打近景，一次缓推。避免：蓝色占满画面、平滑渐变、第四种墨、套印太准。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nThree-ink Risograph: orange-red #EC5032 as the dominant field (about 60%, with drip streaks), deep blue #162C76 for silhouettes and shadows, fluorescent pink #FF48B0 only for rims and halos, on warm paper #F7F2E7. Each ink is halftoned at its own angle, dots 1/300 of frame width, plates misregistered 2–4 px; grain, pinholes, slight boil. Knock orange out under blue silhouettes so they print pure blue; only the darkest accents overprint to near-black. Light is the absence of ink: beams, lamps and lit ground show bare paper, ringed in pink. Figures: blue silhouettes, pink rims; skin is 30–60% orange with a blue shadow step. At cooler times keep the red field; blue rises only into the top of the sky. Camera: handheld behind someone carrying a light, a reverse close-up, a push. Avoid: blue-dominated frames, smooth gradients, a fourth ink, clean registration.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\nRisograph 叠印,1967 海报橙红,Haskell Wexler 夜景,剪影式调度\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮维吉尔·蒂布斯：白衬衫领尖、深色窄领带、袖口，脚边一只手提箱。上一部熄灭的烛光把夜带进来。三个镜头堆出对峙：手电找到它、警长停下嚼口香糖、它挺直腰板，像在说「They call me MISTER Tibbs!」\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 黑暗中的手持手电主观镜头：副警长的光束扫过车站站台，找到手提箱旁的 Clawd（蒂布斯）；它抬头睁大眼，其他几束手电随即亮起。（0.8s）\n2. 反打警长吉莱斯皮近景：大檐帽、飞行员墨镜，嚼着口香糖，然后停住；缓慢推近。（0.5s）\n3. 对峙中全景：Clawd 站在副警长和警长之间，几束手电光交叉打在它身上，身后是蓝色警车和斯巴达镇水塔；它挺直身子、端平肩膀，镜头推近。（1.2s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nNorman Jewison, 1967,Haskell Wexler (cinematography),1967 original poster,Tom Haugomat\n"},{"id":"S6-O41","name":"雾都孤儿","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O41.jpg","markdown":"# O41 雾都孤儿（Oliver!）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 摇摆年代 1960–1969\n- 长什么样：维多利亚玩具纸剧场：手工上色的石印卡纸人物留着剪刀白边，景片分层插在滑杆上，镀金台口下的烛光脚灯把影子投上景片。《雾都孤儿》是歌舞片，正好搬上维多利亚家庭小舞台。\n- 风格：维多利亚玩具纸剧场\n- 关键词：2.5D\n- 年份：1968\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n维多利亚玩具纸剧场（Pollock's 式）。一切都是印出来的卡纸：黑色石印轮廓约画面宽的 1/400，每张剪纸一侧有稀疏蜡笔排线；手工上色是沉稳旧色：深红 #C8262C 和暗金 #E0AB36 用在台口和帷幔（金色不能是亮黄），蓝 #2F5A9A 给服装；布景、天空、水和肤色用发灰的棕、灰和奶油卡纸 #F4ECD6。每个人物和景片留一圈剪刀白边（约画面宽的 1/200）。景片是平行的 2.5D 层，层间视差，层内无透视。烛光脚灯把剪纸影子放大约一倍半投到后面景片上，柔边，随烛火闪动。镀金台口、红帷幔和画出来的包厢观众框住舞台，台口宽占画面八到九成。镜头：慢推、横移，近景微震；结尾拉远露出暗房间。避免：鲜艳矢量色、真 3D 纵深、柔和摄影光、没有剪边的人物。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nVictorian toy paper theatre (Pollock's), all printed card: black lithographic outlines about 1/400 of frame width, sparse crayon hatching on one side of each piece; muted antique hand colouring: deep red #C8262C and dull gold #E0AB36 on proscenium and curtains (never bright yellow), blue #2F5A9A for costumes; scenery, sky, water and skin in greyed browns, greys and cream card #F4ECD6. Every cut-out keeps a white scissor margin (1/200 of frame width). Flats are parallel 2.5D layers in parallax, flat within each plane. Candle footlights throw each cut-out's shadow onto the scenery behind, about 1.5× enlarged, soft, flickering. Gilt proscenium, red drapes and painted audience boxes frame a stage opening 80–90% of frame width. Camera: slow push, lateral tracking, shaky close-ups; a final pull-back shows the dark room. Avoid: bright vector colour, true 3D depth, soft studio light, figures without margins.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\nPollock's 玩具剧场,手工上色石印纸偶,剪卡与脚灯投影,Cruikshank 漫画人物\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮奥利弗：插在自己滑杆上的纸偶，手里举着空碗。全景、舞台、近景三种景别拉开层次：上前一步，卡拍举起空碗，班布尔一吼，它缩了一下，碗还是举着。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 全景：暗房间里红木桌上的玩具剧场，「Theatre Royal」台口，桌上一张人物剪纸，旁边伸进来一只操偶的手；缓慢推近。（0.5s）\n2. 沿舞台横移，济贫院饭堂：Clawd（奥利弗）从一排孩子里走出来，举起空碗（Please, sir…），冒着热气的粥锅旁，班布尔的眉头皱起来；镜头随它向右跟。（0.9s）\n3. Clawd 和班布尔的近景：班布尔一声大吼，Clawd 缩了一下、发抖，碗还是举着；吼声那一下镜头轻震。（1.1s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nCarol Reed, 1968,Benjamin Pollock (toy theatre sheets),George Cruikshank,Pollock's Toy Museum\n"},{"id":"S6-O42","name":"午夜牛郎","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O42.jpg","markdown":"# O42 午夜牛郎（Midnight Cowboy）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 摇摆年代 1960–1969\n- 长什么样：沃霍尔的照片丝网：黑色网点照片层印得不匀，错位地压在松散的平涂色块上，底是银色，画面重复两次、三次。片里就有沃霍尔工厂式的派对，而他画的猫王正是牛仔。\n- 风格：沃霍尔照片丝网 · Double Elvis\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1969\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n安迪·沃霍尔式照片丝网印。两块版：一块是 45° 的高反差黑色半调照片，网点约画面宽的 1/200，几乎没有中间调；下面是手刷平涂色块，相对照片错开约画面宽的 1/100，边缘歪扭：出租车黄 #F3BE14、肤粉 #F2A197、奶油 #EFE6CF、红 #E2462B，底是带柔和光泽的刷涂银漆 #BABCC0。唯一的主角用最强的那块颜色。刮板压力不匀：有的地方网点糊成墨团，有的地方拖成横向干笔条纹和针孔，底下隐约有画布纹。重复就是构图：同一个人物或物体全身，在空银底上并排印两次、再印三次，略有重叠。场景只以褪成银色的印样出现在重复里。镜头：手持晃动、推近、正面平面定格。对标沃霍尔《Double Elvis》（1963）。避免：干净均匀的网点、套色完全对齐、处处一样清晰。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nAndy Warhol photo-silkscreen. Two plates: a high-contrast black halftone photograph at 45°, dots about 1/200 of frame width, few mid-tones; and hand-painted flat colour blocks underneath, offset about 1/100 of frame width with wobbly edges: taxi yellow #F3BE14, skin pink #F2A197, cream #EFE6CF, red #E2462B, on brushed silver paint #BABCC0 with a soft sheen. The one hero subject takes the strongest block colour. Uneven squeegee pressure: dots flood into blotches in places, drag into dry horizontal streaks and pinholes elsewhere, over faint canvas weave. Repetition is the composition: the same figure or object, full-length, printed side by side two then three times, slightly overlapping, on empty silver. Settings appear only as drained-to-silver prints in the repeat. Camera: handheld shake, a push in, flat frontal tableaux. Reference: Andy Warhol, *Double Elvis* (1963). Avoid: clean even halftone, perfect registration, uniform crispness.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n照片丝网印,沃霍尔《Double Elvis》（1963）,银漆底,错位色块\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮乔·巴克：黑色斯泰森帽、红色领巾、手臂上的麂皮流苏。先演电影里那句「I'm walkin' here!」：出租车急刹、它往后一跳、拉索拍车头大吼。然后画面变成沃霍尔丝网，Clawd 摆出猫王的枪手站姿，先印两次，再印三次。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 第六大道的人行横道：黄色出租车急刹冲进来，Clawd（乔·巴克）往后一跳、瞪着眼，拉索拍了一下车头；手持晃动，缓慢推近。（1.1s）\n2. 拉索的近景：叼着烟冲出租车大吼、手指着司机（I'm walkin' here!）；推近。（0.6s）\n3. 街景褪成银色，印成沃霍尔的 Double：两个戴斯泰森帽、手持左轮的 Clawd 叠在一起，再变成三个；正面平视的定格画面。（0.8s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nJohn Schlesinger, 1969,Andy Warhol, Double Elvis (1963),Andy Warhol, Silver Car Crash (Double Disaster) (1963),1969 original poster\n"},{"id":"S6-O43","name":"巴顿将军","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O43.jpg","markdown":"# O43 巴顿将军（Patton）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 新好莱坞 1970–1979\n- 长什么样：仿 1940 年代美军战争债券海报的石印画法：分级平涂的褶皱、深色轮廓、网点阴影、泛黄的纸。对应《巴顿将军》开场：小小的将军站在一面占满舞台的巨幅国旗前训话。\n- 风格：二战战争债券石印海报\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1970\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n1940 年代美国战争债券石印海报。平涂油墨：红 #C2342C、海军蓝 #213261、橄榄绿 #5F5C31、卡其 #B7975E，印在米白 #E6D5B3 的纸上，有纤维、霉点和磨边。每个形体三档：留纸、平涂、油墨加网点，压成硬边色阶。棕黑轮廓线约画面宽的 1/500，最后压上；阴影是粗的 45° 网点带（点径约画面宽的 1/150），带蜡笔颗粒；色版错开约画面宽的 1/600。默认像舞台：对称正面，小主体站在占满大半画面的巨大图形前（旗帜、徽章、放射光芒），灯光是硬边浅色块或留纸；户外也一样，光落成地上的硬边浅斑。文字可有可无，用用户的语言。镜头慢推，配一次干脆的拉出。对标 1942–45 年美国战争债券海报。避免：喷枪渐变、套色完全对齐、网点细到像一片灰。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\n1940s US war-bond lithograph poster. Flat inks: red #C2342C, navy #213261, olive #5F5C31, khaki #B7975E, on fibrous, foxed cream #E6D5B3 paper. Three values per form (bare paper, flat ink, ink plus halftone), posterised in hard steps. Brown-black key line about 1/500 of frame width, printed last; shadows as coarse 45° halftone bands (dots about 1/150 of frame width) with crayon grain; colour plates offset about 1/600 of frame width. Default: a symmetrical frontal stage, a small subject before a huge graphic shape filling most of the frame (flag, emblem, sunburst), lights as hard pale shapes or bare paper; outdoors too, light falling as a hard pale pool. Lettering optional, in the user's language. Camera: slow push-ins, one crisp pull-back. Reference: US war-bond posters, 1942–45. Avoid: airbrushed gradients, perfect registration, halftone so fine it reads grey.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n石印海报,1942–45 美军征兵与战争债券海报,45° 网点、套色错位\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮巴顿：四星钢盔、勋表和奖章、象牙柄左轮、马鞭。它在条纹下走到位，切进近景时啪地敬礼，停住，放下，马鞭拍进手心、一点头，像训话开始。两次硬切，最后的拉镜头让它在巨旗前缩成一个小点。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 红白条纹铺满画面（接上一部沃霍尔网格的图形匹配），随即一个拉镜头，Clawd 从画面下方走上来，站到巨幅国旗前。（1s）\n2. 中近景，镜头带轻微抖动慢推：Clawd 在四星钢盔下啪地敬礼，停住，放下，马鞭拍进手心，用力一点头。（0.9s）\n3. 全景：镜头拉出，越过国旗看到整个空剧场：红幕、镀金台口、脚灯，Clawd 在舞台中央缩成一个小点。（0.5s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nFranklin J. Schaffner, Patton (1970),Patton one-sheet (1970),J. C. Leyendecker,US war-bond and recruiting posters, 1942–45\n"},{"id":"S6-O44","name":"法国贩毒网","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O44.jpg","markdown":"# O44 法国贩毒网（The French Connection）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 新好莱坞 1970–1979\n- 长什么样：整条布鲁克林高架铁下的大街用 3D 建出来，再调成粗颗粒 16mm：冷灰、暗部偏青、高反差、片门抖动。这部片最有名的就是高架铁下的追车，3D 才拍得出那种速度。\n- 风格：高架铁下追车 · 16mm 颗粒 3D\n- 关键词：3D\n- 年份：1971\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n70 年代初粗粝的纪实感，放在细致的 3D 世界里，按增感冲洗的 16mm 收尾。调色：低饱和、高反差；中性冷灰 #7A7C7A、带青的暗部 #1E2A2C，砖、木头、车辆保持暖棕 #6E4A34，至少占四分之一画面，绝不整体泛蓝；唯一的主角（比如橙 #D97757）保持饱和。默认阴天，天空亮到近白，任何时段都一样；阳光穿过头顶格栅（轨道、桥、栈桥）落成硬边条纹。颗粒成团，约画面宽的 1/700，亮度 ±8%；片门晃动、闪烁、灰尘，亮处偶有淡重影。杂物多且符合地点。手持机位跟着动作走：18 到 25mm 低机位向前冲，重复物体频闪掠过；回看主体的跟拍机位；墙角广角。对标 William Friedkin《法国贩毒网》（1971）和摄影师 Owen Roizman。避免：干净的 CG 反光、整体泛蓝、空荡的方块场景、颗粒糊成一片。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nGritty early-1970s documentary look in a detailed 3D world, finished as pushed 16mm. Grade: low saturation, high contrast; neutral-cold greys #7A7C7A, cyan shadows #1E2A2C, warm-brown mid-tones #6E4A34 in brick, wood, vehicles (at least a quarter of the frame), never an overall blue; the one hero (e.g. #D97757) stays saturated. Default overcast, sky near-white, at any time of day; sun through any overhead grid (track, bridge, pier) falls in hard stripes. Grain clumps about 1/700 of frame width, ±8% brightness; gate weave, flicker, dust, occasional faint ghosts of bright lights. Dense clutter fitting the place. Handheld cameras ride the action: a low 18–25 mm lens rushing forward as repeated objects strobe past, a rig looking back at the subject, a corner wide. References: William Friedkin, *The French Connection* (1971); cinematographer Owen Roizman. Avoid: clean CG shine, blue tint, empty places, mushy grain.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\nthree.js 3D 街景,16mm 纪录片颗粒,暗部偏青的冷灰印片调色\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮“大力水手”道尔：猪肉派帽、怒眼、双手握方向盘。追车的场景时间比屏幕时间跑得快得多，加速看起来就是车更快。三个硬切：路口甩尾 → 保险杠狂冲 → 驾驶员，中间不停。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 路口全景：列车从头顶驶过，棕色庞蒂亚克甩尾冲上大街，后轮冒烟；镜头视角慢慢放宽。（0.8s）\n2. 片中有名的保险杠机位：超低机位带着运动模糊往前冲，高架铁的柱子一根根闪过。（0.8s）\n3. 中景，从车外透过摇下的车窗看 Clawd 扮的道尔：猪肉派帽、咬牙握着方向盘，身后高架铁和店面滑过，带车载抖动。（0.9s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nWilliam Friedkin, The French Connection (1971),Owen Roizman (cinematography),The French Connection one-sheet (1971)\n"},{"id":"S6-O45","name":"教父","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O45.jpg","markdown":"# O45 教父（The Godfather）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 新好莱坞 1970–1979\n- 长什么样：深琥珀和赭褐的油画，只有顶光落下的地方是暖的：暗部是透明的薄罩染，受光边缘是厚涂。重现戈登·威利斯在“I believe in America”那间书房里的顶光。\n- 风格：伦勃朗顶光明暗油画\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1972\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n伦勃朗、卡拉瓦乔式明暗油画，只有一盏顶光。约七成画面是看得出形体的暖暗部，大多是赭褐 #24150B，近黑 #070403 只在最深的角落。琥珀 #8F5D2A 中间调约两成；朝上的面亮到 #E2B673。主角整个站在光池里，受光部分至少占画面高的四分之一；眉骨把眼睛压进阴影。一处饱和的红 #9C1A1A。暗部薄而平滑地罩染，亮部是带亮笔脊的厚涂，笔触约画面宽的 1/100；过渡柔和，不用刮擦线条。琥珀光油，重暗角，细画布纹（±3%）。背景可有一处暖色亮口（窗、门洞、发亮的天边）；画内的小灯只是一小团光，不算第二个主光。镜头慢拉、慢推。对标 Francis Ford Coppola《教父》（1972），摄影师 Gordon Willis。避免：提亮暗部、平光、人脸跳出来、暗到看不清。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nChiaroscuro oil painting after Rembrandt and Caravaggio, one overhead light. About 70% of the frame is readable warm dark, mostly umber #24150B, near-black #070403 only in the deepest corners. Amber #8F5D2A mid-tones about 20%; top planes reach #E2B673. The hero stands in the light pool, lit area at least 1/4 of frame height; brows shade the eyes. One red accent #9C1A1A. Darks thin smooth glazes; lights thick impasto, ridged strokes about 1/100 of frame width; modelling soft, never scratchy. Amber varnish, heavy vignette, canvas grain (±3%). A warm bright opening (window, doorway, horizon) may glow behind; a small lamp in frame is a small glow, not a second key. Camera: slow pull-backs and pushes. Reference: Francis Ford Coppola, *The Godfather* (1972), cinematographer Gordon Willis. Avoid: lifting the darks, flat light, faces popping out, frames too dark to read.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n明暗油画,戈登·威利斯的顶光,伦勃朗、卡拉瓦乔式罩染\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮唐·柯里昂：黑领结、翼领、红玫瑰、白袖口，一只手压在猫背上。12 拍全按 1 倍速，保留威利斯那种耐心的镜头；抚猫一下约 4 秒，中间眨一次眼。全部硬切，最后一镜让原海报的提线手降到它头上。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 从暗处博纳塞拉的灰发开始，镜头缓缓拉出，露出坐在翼背椅里的教父和他膝前的猫。（1.7s）\n2. 近景：顶光下的 Clawd 缓缓抚摸灰色虎斑猫，领结和红玫瑰吃到光；镜头慢推。（1s）\n3. 推向百叶窗：窗外阳光下的婚礼，虚而过曝，左边是黑根的剪影。（0.8s）\n4. 书房全景，镜头略推：提线木偶的手从画面顶上降下来，提线在 Clawd 身上慢慢绷紧。（1.5s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nFrancis Ford Coppola, The Godfather (1972),Gordon Willis (cinematography),Rembrandt, The Night Watch,Caravaggio,S. Neil Fujita, The Godfather poster logo\n"},{"id":"S6-O46","name":"骗中骗","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O46.jpg","markdown":"# O46 骗中骗（The Sting）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 新好莱坞 1970–1979\n- 长什么样：Leyendecker、Rockwell 一路的杂志插画：块面受光、平行笔触、利落的深色轮廓。片中章节卡就仿《星期六晚邮报》封面，1936 年的下注厅也照这个画。\n- 风格：《星期六晚邮报》插画\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1973\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n30 年代《星期六晚邮报》封面插画。写实人物，比例准确、表情生动，用块面塑造（受光、背光、下方暖色反光），块面里填顺着形体、长短不一的笔触，宽约画面宽的 1/150，柔和衔接，不成条纹。深色轮廓在背光一侧加粗，画面宽的 1/800 到 1/300。暖色主光从左上来。配色：灰泥 #E2C492、橡木 #764626、石板 #28322C、黄铜 #E2B046、墨 #341E18；橡木和墨色暗部约占三分之一画面；天空灰泥偏石板，水是浅石板，皮肤灰泥加黄铜。轻颗粒画板（±3%）加暖暗角。默认一点透视，时代细节，薄雾，故事有就加次要人物。每个人物只画一遍，整体移动（呼吸、摇摆、沿路径滑行）。镜头：一次长而平滑的推进。对标 J. C. Leyendecker、Norman Rockwell。避免：僵硬剪纸人物、粗细一样的轮廓、发脏的颜色。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\n1930s Saturday Evening Post cover illustration. Realist figures, correct proportions, expressive faces, built from blocky planes (lit, shadow, warm bounce from below) filled with blended strokes about 1/150 of frame width, varied in length, following the form, never stripes. Dark contour thicker on the shadow side, 1/800–1/300 of frame width. Warm key from upper left. Palette: plaster #E2C492, oak #764626, slate #28322C, brass #E2B046, ink #341E18; oak and ink darks about a third of the frame; sky plaster cooled with slate, water light slate, skin plaster plus brass. Grainy board (±3%), warm vignette. Default one-point perspective, period detail, haze, secondary figures if the story has any. Each figure is drawn once and moves whole (breathing, sway, sliding along a path). Camera: one long, smooth push in. References: J. C. Leyendecker, Norman Rockwell. Avoid: stiff cut-out figures, even outlines, muddy colour.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n《星期六晚邮报》封面插画,Leyendecker 式块面塑形,Jaroslav Gebr 的章节卡\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮胡克：浅色礼帽、红领结、背带。整段不切，只有一个推镜头。推到位时它打出骗子们的暗号（手指贴鼻侧），接着压帽檐、冲镜头眨眼，像把观众拉进骗局。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 一镜到底：从下注厅全景（赔率黑板、下注窗口、窗口里的冈多夫）一路推到扮胡克的 Clawd，它把手指贴到鼻侧打暗号，再冲镜头压一下帽檐。（2.5s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nGeorge Roy Hill, The Sting (1973),Jaroslav Gebr (chapter cards),Richard Amsel (poster),J. C. Leyendecker,Norman Rockwell\n"},{"id":"S6-O47","name":"教父2","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O47.jpg","markdown":"# O47 教父2（The Godfather Part II）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 新好莱坞 1970–1979\n- 长什么样：世纪之交的调色银盐照片，手工刷色：棕褐调、柔焦、银盐颗粒、霉斑、裂角，局部淡淡的红、蓝、铜绿。画的是 1901 年少年维托抵达埃利斯岛，像 Lewis Hine 的照片。\n- 风格：手工上色棕褐老照片\n- 关键词：2.5D\n- 年份：1974\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n1900 年前后手工上色的棕褐银盐照片。先做受光的立体形体，柔光从左上来，再“印”出来：柔焦带轻微光晕，颗粒在中间调最重（±5%）；棕褐调从 #281A12 经 #987048 到 #EEE0C2 完整铺开，至少五分之一画面落到最深一端，任何光线下都一样。人物比例真实（头约七分之一身高），明暗柔和。透明颜料松松地刷，溢出边缘：唯一的主角最重（比如橙红 #D97757，不透明度六到八成），一两件道具轻点；颜色进暗部就被吞掉。做旧要轻：暗角、边缘银化（靠边暗部泛灰蓝金属光）、几个小霉点、细划痕，最多一个裂角。构图正面、纪实，人物静立面对镜头。镜头缓慢横摇扫过人脸。对标 Lewis Hine、Augustus Sherman 的埃利斯岛照片。避免：积木人物、数码填色、低反差雾灰、大片霉斑。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nA hand-coloured toned silver print from about 1900. Build lit 3D forms, soft key from upper left, then 'print' them: soft focus, light halation, grain heaviest in mid-tones (±5%), full sepia range #281A12, #987048, #EEE0C2; at least a fifth of the frame reaches the dark end, in any light. Figures: real proportions (head 1/7 of body), soft shading. Transparent dyes brushed loosely past edges: the one hero subject strongly (e.g. orange-red #D97757 at 60–80% opacity), a faint touch on one or two props; dyes die in shadow. Light ageing: vignette, silvered edges (grey-blue sheen in border darks), small foxing spots, fine scratches, at most one cracked corner. Frontal, documentary: figures stand still facing the lens. Camera: slow pan across faces. References: Lewis Hine; Augustus Sherman (Ellis Island). Avoid: toy figures, digital fill colour, low-contrast fog, big mould blotches.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n调色银盐照片,Lewis Hine 的埃利斯岛照片,手工刷色,光线步进的 3D 人物\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮少年维托：灰色粗花呢鸭舌帽，脖子上挂着纸名牌，夹在大人中间显得很小。平均 1.3 倍速，自由女神那一镜是 1 倍。三个硬切：人群 → 雕像（主观）→ 反应；最后的表演点是低头看自己的新名字，再抬头望向新的国家。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 横摇扫过甲板上仰头的移民群像，画面随船身起伏。（0.8s）\n2. Clawd 的主观视角：自由女神从港口的雾里显出来，带一点铜绿色；镜头慢推。（0.7s）\n3. 近景：戴粗花呢鸭舌帽的少年维托（Clawd），胸前挂着“VITO ANDOLINI”名牌；它低头看名牌，又抬头踮脚；镜头略微拉开。（0.9s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nFrancis Ford Coppola, The Godfather Part II (1974),Gordon Willis (cinematography),Lewis Hine, Ellis Island photographs,Augustus Sherman, Ellis Island portraits\n"},{"id":"S6-O48","name":"飞越疯人院","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O48.jpg","markdown":"# O48 飞越疯人院（One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 新好莱坞 1970–1979\n- 长什么样：仿爱德华·霍珀的安静写实：大块平整的淡绿墙面、低角度晨光投下的硬边平行四边形、彼此隔开的人。正合《飞越疯人院》的病房：日光灯下的秩序，被一次出逃打破。\n- 风格：霍珀式美国写实油画\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1975\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n爱德华·霍珀式美国写实绘画。大块克制、偏浅的平面色：机构绿 #9EB8A0 和 #5E7C66、灰绿地面 #7E8876、奶油 #ECE6D2，看得见画布纹（±3%），每块拖几笔长笔触，宽约画面宽的 1/80，亮或暗百分之五到十。低角度阳光从一侧射来，在墙和地上投下硬边暖色平行四边形 #FCE6AC，压着窗框或栏杆的清晰影子，室内户外都一样。天空浅奶油绿，皮肤是提暖的奶油色，衣服用压暗的绿。人物由三四块边缘柔和的明暗色块叠成，不勾轮廓，比例写实；几个人时分开站。大部分时间静止，然后一件不到一秒的突发：甩镜或一阵强光。招牌镜头：一点透视的静止室内，地上有光块。对标霍珀《Office in a Small City》（1953）。避免：粗轮廓、矢量人物、深而饱和的墙、模糊光斑。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nEdward Hopper-style American realist painting. Large flat planes of restrained, pale colour: institutional greens #9EB8A0 and #5E7C66, grey-green floor #7E8876, cream #ECE6D2, on visible canvas (±3%), with a few long dragged strokes about 1/80 of frame width, 5–10% off the base. Low side sun throws hard-edged parallelograms of warm light #FCE6AC on walls and ground, crossed by crisp shadows of frames or railings, indoors or out. Sky pale cream-green; skin warmed cream; clothes darkened greens. Figures painted from 3–4 overlapping soft-edged value shapes, no outline, real proportions; several people stand apart. Stillness, then one sudden event under a second: a whip pan, a burst of light. Signature shot: a still one-point interior, light on the floor. References: Edward Hopper, *Office in a Small City* (1953). Avoid: heavy outlines, vector figures, dark saturated walls, fuzzy light patches.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n爱德华·霍珀式写实,硬边斜射窗光,平整油彩块面加拖笔\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 戴着麦克墨菲的黑色针织帽和格子领，演的是片尾那一下：把水疗台砸出窗外。屏幕时间等于场景时间：蹲下用力、举起、1.0 秒扔出、1.3 秒撞窗，闪光加碎玻璃飞向镜头，然后欢呼。用带运动模糊的甩镜代替剪切。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 日间活动室里的一镜到底：Clawd 把大理石水疗台举过头顶扔出去，镜头跟着甩到后窗，玻璃炸开、晨光涌进来，再穿过光甩回 Clawd，它举起双臂欢呼。（2.5s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nMiloš Forman, One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest (1975),Edward Hopper, Office in a Small City; Morning Sun,Edward Hopper, Nighthawks,Haskell Wexler and Bill Butler (cinematography)\n"},{"id":"S6-O49","name":"洛奇","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O49.jpg","markdown":"# O49 洛奇（Rocky）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 新好莱坞 1970–1979\n- 长什么样：清晨的费城整个做成 3D，从肉库到博物馆 72 级台阶，调成 70 年代 35mm：冷蓝天、低低的金色太阳、长影子、薄雾、细颗粒。跑上台阶和顶上的环绕升镜是这部片的记忆点。\n- 风格：费城台阶日出 3D · 35mm 调色\n- 关键词：3D\n- 年份：1976\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n70 年代 35mm 写实，放在细致的 3D 世界里，默认日出（日落同理）。太阳离地平线五到十度，暖橙 #F0A060，从侧面或前侧方照亮主角，让受光的身体和拉过地面的长影看得清；不要完全逆光。天空冷蓝 #7FA3CF，云底染粉 #E6B2AE；雾朝太阳一侧发金、背光一侧发蓝紫。调色：细颗粒（±3%），暗部偏冷、高光偏暖，不过曝；只有太阳入画时才有光晕和鬼影。镜头：低机位斯坦尼康从背后跟着主角爬上一段坡或台阶，到最高处接一个连续环绕，边转边升边退，六到八秒，转到受光的脸，直到整个地方在下面铺开。对标 John G. Avildsen《洛奇》（1976）、斯坦尼康操作者 Garrett Brown。避免：正午平光、没细节的剪影、高潮时机位不动、塑料感反光。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\n1970s 35mm realism in a detailed 3D world, sunrise by default (sunset the same). Sun 5–10° up, warm orange #F0A060, lighting the hero from the side or three-quarter front so the lit body and long ground shadows read; no full backlight. Cool blue sky #7FA3CF, pink cloud undersides #E6B2AE; haze golden toward the sun, blue-violet away. Grade: fine grain (±3%), cool shadows, warm highlights, nothing clipped; flare and ghosts only with the sun in frame. Camera: a low Steadicam following the hero from behind up an ascent, then at the top one continuous orbit that circles, rises and pulls back over 6–8 s, showing the lit face, until the place spreads out below. References: John G. Avildsen, *Rocky* (1976); Steadicam operator Garrett Brown. Avoid: flat noon light, detail-less silhouettes, a static climax, plastic CG shine.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\nthree.js 3D 城市,70 年代 35mm 胶片调色,斯坦尼康跟拍与环绕升镜\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮洛奇：灰色卫衣兜帽戴起，肩上搭一条白毛巾。训练蒙太奇三个快切，约 1.7 倍速；上台阶以后回到 1 倍。硬切进斯坦尼康跟跑，之后不再切：跑到顶直接接环绕升镜，举臂一跳正好落在镜头开始升起的时候。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 肉库中景，定机位：戴灰色兜帽的 Clawd 对着挂着的牛肉出拳，白瓷砖、裸灯泡。（0.4s）\n2. 更近一些，定机位：拳头打在牛肉上，牛肉晃荡。（0.4s）\n3. 黎明前的意大利市场，定机位：Clawd 迎着镜头慢跑，经过油桶火、果蔬摊和还亮着的路灯。（0.6s）\n4. 低机位斯坦尼康从背后追着 Clawd 跑上 72 级台阶，前方是博物馆的立柱。（1.5s）\n5. 到顶，Clawd 双臂举成 V 字一跳；镜头绕着它转、上升、后退，直到整条大道和城市在日出里铺开。（2.1s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nJohn G. Avildsen, Rocky (1976),Garrett Brown (Steadicam),James Crabe (cinematography),Philadelphia Museum of Art, east façade\n"},{"id":"S6-O50","name":"安妮·霍尔","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O50.jpg","markdown":"# O50 安妮·霍尔（Annie Hall）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 新好莱坞 1970–1979\n- 长什么样：《纽约客》单格漫画：灵动的钢笔墨线、几块克制的淡彩、大量白纸，画下面一行手写图说。伍迪·艾伦的喜剧和这本杂志是一个调子，龙虾那场戏正好是一格笑话漫画。\n- 风格：《纽约客》单格漫画 · 墨线淡彩\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1977\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n《纽约客》单格漫画，满版出血，不画格框。蘸水笔墨线直线处细到约画面宽的 1/1000，转弯处粗到约 1/300，收笔一甩；方盒子几笔分开画，角上交叉出头。淡水彩带积色边，铺在大部分物体上，溢出墨线不超过画面宽的 1/150：灰 #9698A0、海蓝 #78A0BE、沙色 #E2CCA0、铁锈 #C45C32，底是冷压纸 #FBF8F1，四边化成白。人物夸张、有表情、画出五官，周围有日常道具，留白但不空荡。画面下方一行手写说明，用用户的语言，字高约画面高的 1/30，逐字写出：一句冷面幽默。画面不动，活物微微颤动。镜头：轻微漂移和推近；画面可一分为二，左右并排两幅。对标 James Thurber、William Steig、桑贝。避免：浓重饱和的颜色、粗细一样的矢量线、白纸中间漂着一个小格子。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nNew Yorker single-panel cartoon, full-bleed, no border. Nib-pen lines about 1/1000 of frame width on straights, swelling to 1/300 in turns, flicking off at the ends; box corners overlap. Pale watercolour washes with pooled edges over most objects, slipping past the lines by at most 1/150 of frame width: grey #9698A0, sea blue #78A0BE, sand #E2CCA0, rust #C45C32, on cold-press paper #FBF8F1 fading to white at the edges. Exaggerated, expressive figures with drawn faces among everyday props. One hand-lettered caption line under the action, in the user's language, about 1/30 of frame height, appearing letter by letter: a deadpan remark. The drawing holds still; living things wobble. Camera: small drifts and pushes; the frame may split into two side-by-side drawings. References: James Thurber, William Steig, Jean-Jacques Sempé. Avoid: saturated colour, uniform vector lines, a small framed panel floating in white.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n《纽约客》单格漫画,钢笔墨线,淡水彩,手写图说\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮安妮：黛安·基顿那顶宽檐帽、松垮的条纹领带和马甲，手里一台 35mm 相机。约 1.8 倍速，跳起来那一下接近 1 倍：两次闪光拍照，龙虾爬到脚边，吓得一跳，落地笑开。全部硬切，最后用分屏饭桌做一个图形化的出口。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 冰箱后缝的大特写：一只龙虾慢慢探出头；镜头缓慢漂移。（0.6s）\n2. 厨房全景：扮安妮的 Clawd 边笑边给抱着汤锅的艾尔维拍闪光照；龙虾爬到脚边，它一跳，落地笑开，下方一笔笔写出“TALK TO HIM. YOU SPEAK SHELLFISH.”；镜头略微拉开，带一点手持晃动。（1.5s）\n3. 复活节饭桌分屏：左边安静的霍尔家，Clawd 坐在其中；右边吵闹的辛格家。（0.4s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nWoody Allen, Annie Hall (1977),James Thurber,William Steig,Jean-Jacques Sempé,Annie Hall one-sheet (1977)\n"},{"id":"S6-O51","name":"猎鹿人","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O51.jpg","markdown":"# O51 猎鹿人（The Deer Hunter）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 新好莱坞 1970–1979\n- 长什么样：仿安德鲁·怀斯的蛋彩：干燥细密的交叉短笔，低饱和的草黄、赭褐、灰绿，草叶一根根画出来，天空苍白。怀斯是宾州画家，“一枪”猎鹿就在宾州钢铁小镇上方的山里。\n- 风格：安德鲁·怀斯蛋彩\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1978\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n安德鲁·怀斯式石膏底板蛋彩画。干、哑光、极细：颜色全靠密密的交叉短笔叠出来，每笔长约画面宽的 1/400，细如发丝。低饱和配色：苍白阴天 #D9D5C6、草黄 #CDB27A、赭褐 #6A5234、灰绿远山 #8E938C、铁锈色 #8A4E32；水、石、衣服和皮肤都从这几色调出。强调色只给唯一的主角（比如一顶红帽）；画里的光源只是一个淡淡的小点，不带光晕。地面（默认枯草，其他地面同理）先铺大块深浅，再叠细笔、暗色草团，最后是被风梳过的单根草叶；岩石和房屋用短笔顺着各面排。空间空旷孤独，主体小而偏离中心。默认阴天软光，其他时段也保持苍白、不要暖光。镜头缓慢漂移、安静推近；圆形遮罩可作剪切点。避免：湿水彩晕染、饱和色、厚重发亮的笔触。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nEgg tempera on gesso panel after Andrew Wyeth. Dry, matte, very fine: colour built from dense cross-hatched strokes, each about 1/400 of frame width long, hairline thin. Muted palette: pale overcast sky #D9D5C6, straw grass #CDB27A, umber #6A5234, misty grey-green hills #8E938C, rust #8A4E32; water, stone, clothes and skin mixed from these. One accent colour, only on the one hero subject (e.g. a red cap); any light source is a small pale dot, no halo. Ground (dry grass by default, other ground alike): big light and dark patches, fine strokes, dark tufts, then single wind-combed blades; rocks, buildings hatched along their planes. Empty, lonely space; the subject small, off-centre. Soft overcast light; at other times of day stay pale, no warm glow. Camera: slow drift, quiet push; a circular iris may mark a cut. Avoid: wet watercolour blooms, saturated colour, thick shiny strokes.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n石膏底蛋彩,安德鲁·怀斯,细密交叉短笔\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮麦克：红色羊毛猎帽、带瞄准镜的猎枪。河谷 → 瞄准镜 → 鹿眼 → 猎人，四个硬切，平均约 1.24 倍速，安静、稳。表演点是那一枪没开：枪放下，闭眼，呼出一口气，再抬头看那头鹿。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 河谷雾里的钢铁小镇全景：高炉、冒烟的烟囱、洋葱顶教堂；镜头缓慢横移。（0.6s）\n2. 瞄准镜视角：黑色圆形遮罩和十字线里，山脊上的雄鹿；画面随猎人的手微微晃动。（0.7s）\n3. 雄鹿头部和眼睛的特写；镜头慢推。（0.3s）\n4. Clawd 扮麦克站在花岗岩上，放下猎枪，闭眼呼出一口气，再抬头望向雄鹿；镜头拉开到整条山脊。（0.8s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nMichael Cimino, The Deer Hunter (1978),Vilmos Zsigmond (cinematography),Andrew Wyeth, Christina's World (1948),Andrew Wyeth, Winter 1946\n"},{"id":"S6-O52","name":"克莱默夫妇","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O52.jpg","markdown":"# O52 克莱默夫妇（Kramer vs. Kramer）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 新好莱坞 1970–1979\n- 长什么样：孩子用蜡笔画的厨房：歪扭的线、用力涂进纸纹又涂出边界的颜色、标签和箭头、带笑脸的太阳。这是比利眼里他和爸爸做法式吐司的样子。\n- 风格：儿童蜡笔画\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1979\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n认真的六到八岁孩子在白纸上画的蜡笔画。蜡笔只蹭到纸纹凸起；轻涂露出四到六成纸白，用力处填满；叠色时下层透出。轮廓用黑色或深棕蜡笔，蜡质、发抖，粗约画面宽的 1/300。填色是来回涂抹，每笔长约画面宽的 1/40、宽约 1/250，一排排涂、冲出轮廓，每块各有方向，间距和角度不均匀，不能排成规整的网。基础色：红 #E0382C、橙 #F28A1E、黄 #F7D23A、绿 #3AA345、蓝 #2E6BD8。儿童画惯例：地面线、压扁的透视、带箭头的标注、笑脸太阳、写反一个字母（中文写反一个偏旁）、图案一格格涂。文字用用户的语言、发抖的蜡笔笔画写，不用字体。镜头：先晃动近景，再平稳扫过全画。避免：大人式的工整画法、没细节的乱涂、均匀点状或网状肌理、平滑渐变。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nA careful 6–8-year-old's wax-crayon drawing on white paper. Crayon catches the paper grain's peaks; light pressure leaves 40–60% paper showing, heavy pressure fills it; overlaps show through. Outlines in black or dark-brown crayon, waxy, wobbling, about 1/300 of frame width. Fills are back-and-forth scribbles, strokes about 1/40 of frame width long and 1/250 wide, rows overshooting the outlines, each area in its own direction, spacing and angle uneven, never a regular mesh. Primary box: red #E0382C, orange #F28A1E, yellow #F7D23A, green #3AA345, blue #2E6BD8. Child conventions: a ground line, flattened perspective, arrowed labels, a smiling sun, one mirrored letter (in Chinese, a reversed stroke), patterns coloured cell by cell. Text in the user's language, in wobbly crayon strokes, not a typeface. Camera: shaky close-ups, then a calm drift. Avoid: neat adult rendering, random scrawl, uniform stipple or mesh, smooth gradients.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n纹理纸上的蜡笔,6–8 岁儿童画的画法,手写标签\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮泰德：系着蓝色围裙带，拿锅铲；比利站在椅子上打蛋。第一次和最后一次用同一口锅做匹配剪辑，父子关系在一次跳切里讲完。整体约 1.35 倍速；切进全景时正好接住吐司，接着小跳一下、看一眼镜头。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 第一次做的平底锅大特写：焦吐司、黑色蜡笔烟、蛋壳；手持抖动，慢慢拉开。（0.7s）\n2. 匹配剪辑到片尾的同一口锅，干干净净：一片金黄的吐司从锅里翻起来。（0.4s）\n3. 标题“ME AND DADDY MAKE FRENCH TOAST”下父子并肩：Clawd 用锅铲接住吐司，比利笑开，冒出一颗红心，Clawd 得意地小跳一下；镜头往上漂到窗外的蜡笔天际线。（1.4s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nRobert Benton, Kramer vs. Kramer (1979),Néstor Almendros (cinematography),Kramer vs. Kramer one-sheet (1979),Children's drawings, ages 6–8\n"},{"id":"S6-O53","name":"普通人","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O53.jpg","markdown":"# O53 普通人（Ordinary People）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 大片年代 1980–1989\n- 长什么样：层层叠色的透明水彩：湿画法的天空和湖面、水渍边线、回流花、留白的纸当高光，秋天的金和锈红配上冷灰蓝。对上影片开场那个安静又带点忧伤的 Lake Forest 秋天。\n- 风格：秋日透明水彩\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1980\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n透明水彩，在冷压纸上一层层罩色。先把形状画清楚，再罩两三层，每层更小更深，干后边缘积成深一点的水渍线；每帧两三处回流花，约画面宽的 1/10，只在大块湿画里，不落在人物上；颗粒轻沉进纸纹（±4%）。纸是最亮的白，浅色物体和高光留纸。天空和水面湿画，涟漪干笔。分工：秋金 #ECAA34、猩红 #E46030、锈色 #B8743C 给树叶和点缀，对着冷灰蓝的雾 #C2C6D2 和紫灰阴影 #8A8496；天空任何时段都浅而冷；地面浅绿赭。树冠：先铺淡色，叠扇贝边叶团，点深色，甩散叶；树少时暖色给灌木、屋顶、衣服。光从一侧来。镜头缓慢横摇、轻推，落叶从镜头前飘过。对标 Winslow Homer 的水彩。避免：水粉式覆盖、喷枪模糊、矢量硬边、脏色、饱和晚霞。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nTransparent watercolour in layered glazes on cold-press paper. Draw shapes crisply first, then two or three glazes, each smaller and darker, drying to a tide-mark edge; two or three blooms, about 1/10 of frame width, inside large washes, never on figures; light granulation (±4%). Paper is the brightest white; light objects and highlights left as paper. Sky and water wet-in-wet, ripples dry-brush. Roles: autumn gold #ECAA34, scarlet #E46030, rust #B8743C for foliage and accents, against grey-blue mist #C2C6D2 and violet-grey shadows #8A8496; sky pale and cool at any time of day; ground pale green-ochre. Foliage: pale wash, scallop-edged clusters, dark dots, flicked leaves; with few trees, warm colours go to scrub, roofs, clothes. Light from one side. Camera: slow pan, gentle push, leaves drifting past the lens. Reference: Winslow Homer's watercolours. Avoid: opaque gouache, airbrushed blur, vector edges, muddy colour, saturated sunsets.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n透明水彩叠色,Winslow Homer 与 John Singer Sargent,HB 铅笔底稿\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮康拉德：一条藏青配奶白的长校园围巾被湖风吹起，背一只皮书包。全程 1.1 倍速，和帕赫贝尔卡农的慢开场一样慢。三个硬切：湖、回头看家的儿子、没有回应的母亲。它的目光从房子落到地上，就是全部表演。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 黎明的密歇根湖空镜：灰蓝湖面上留白的太阳光路，镜头慢慢右摇到秋天的崖岸。（0.8s）\n2. 枫树下的街道：扮康拉德的 Clawd 围着长条纹围巾、背着书包，在人行道上停下，回头看那栋白色殖民地式房子（卡尔文在院里耙叶），又垂下眼睛转开；镜头缓慢右移并略微拉开。（1s）\n3. 门廊上的贝丝抱着双臂，神情冷淡；镜头慢慢推近她。（0.7s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nRobert Redford, Ordinary People (1980),John Bailey (cinematography),Winslow Homer (watercolours),John Singer Sargent (watercolours)\n"},{"id":"S6-O54","name":"烈火战车","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O54.jpg","markdown":"# O54 烈火战车（Chariots of Fire）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 大片年代 1980–1989\n- 长什么样：仿 Jean Droit 1924 巴黎奥运海报的四色石印：古典人体、有限油墨、成团的石版颗粒、参差套色。《烈火战车》讲的是 1924 年奥运，海滩长跑像那张海报活了过来。\n- 风格：1924 巴黎奥运石印海报\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1981\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n20 年代石版印刷海报，四色：海蓝 #245A8C、赭黄 #D49640、深棕 #40271B、暖红 #E47E62，印在泛旧的奶油纸 #F1E7D1 上，每色错位约画面宽的 1/700。所有色调都由油墨颗粒组成，不平涂：颗粒约画面宽的 1/1000，聚成软团，浅色区覆盖两到四成，中间调五到八成，实色几乎满版、留针孔；空白纸上不能有零散孤点。分工：蓝给天空、水和冷阴影；赭黄给地面和皮肤；深棕给轮廓、暗部和衣服；红少量点缀。明暗安静偏浅。人物古典：受光侧赭黄，四肢顺一道深棕明暗交界线，头约身高八分之一；多人排成横列。动作慢，靠水花、尘土、头发、布料带出。镜头：长焦正面、贴地低机位；可加衬线字标题横带。对标 Jean Droit 的 1924 年巴黎奥运海报。避免：粗噪点、象形小人、矢量平涂。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\n1920s stone lithograph poster in four inks: sea blue #245A8C, ochre #D49640, deep brown #40271B, warm red #E47E62, on aged cream #F1E7D1, misregistered about 1/700 of frame width. Every tone is ink grain, never a flat fill: specks about 1/1000 of frame width in soft clumps, covering 20–40% of pale areas, 50–80% of mid-tones, nearly all of solids (with pinholes); no stray specks on bare paper. Roles: blue for sky, water, cool shadow; ochre for ground and skin; brown for outlines, darks, clothes; red sparingly. Values calm and pale. Classical figures: ochre lit side, brown core-shadow line down each limb, head 1/8 of body; groups in a row. Slow motion carried by spray, dust, hair, cloth. Camera: long-lens front views, ground-level shots; optional serif title band. Reference: Jean Droit, 1924 Paris Olympics poster. Avoid: coarse speckle, pictogram figures, vector fills.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n四版石印,Jean Droit 的 1924 奥运海报,古典解剖人体\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 跑在队伍里：白头带、胸前别着 98 号码布。跑步按 1 倍速，看起来就是原片的慢镜头。四个硬切：面孔、脚、利德尔仰头的跑姿、侧面整队；最后一镜印上海报字框，把这场戏变成 1924 年的海报。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 长焦正面：跑者队伍沿着浪边迎面跑来，Clawd 在其中，戴白头带、别着 98 号码布；镜头略微拉开。（0.7s）\n2. 贴地近景：赤脚踩进浅水溅起水花，湿沙上有倒影；镜头横向滑动。（0.6s）\n3. 利德尔的近景：仰头张嘴，亚伯拉罕斯在他身后；镜头略微拉开。（0.5s）\n4. 侧面看整队跑者，Clawd 在中间；镜头拉开时，“JEUX DE LA VIIIe OLYMPIADE · PARIS 1924”的海报字框印上画面。（0.8s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nHugh Hudson, Chariots of Fire (1981),Jean Droit, Jeux de la VIIIe Olympiade, Paris 1924 (poster),Chariots of Fire one-sheet (1981),Vangelis (score)\n"},{"id":"S6-O55","name":"甘地传","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O55.jpg","markdown":"# O55 甘地传（Gandhi）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 大片年代 1980–1989\n- 长什么样：棉布上的手工木刻印花：靛蓝、茜草红、姜黄加黑色雕版轮廓，版缝处套色错位，染料顺着经纬渗开。手纺土布是甘地的象征，所以食盐进军就印成一块布。\n- 风格：拉贾斯坦手工木刻印花布\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1982\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n拉贾斯坦手工木刻印花布（Bagru、Sanganer）。本色棉布 #EEE5CF 是主底，占画面一半以上，也是皮肤色；靛蓝 #2A3A68 给水面或天空色带和纹样；茜草红 #AA322A 给边框、衣服和主角；姜黄 #DEA42C 给小纹样；黑色雕版 #281E1C 印轮廓。按雕版一块块盖（每块约画面高的 1/8），错位二到四像素，大色带也在版缝处轻微跳变；染料顺经纬微渗，上色不匀。泥防染冰裂纹只在靛蓝里，细而稀疏。轮廓像刻出来的，略毛糙，粗约画面宽的 1/300。平铺成横幅，外框是佩斯利和花藤；海浪是扇贝纹；人物取侧面，细密画式的脸，描黑的眼尾。镜头沿横幅横摇、推近；借转动的纹样转场。避免：矢量硬边、套色完全对齐、没有五官的人、满屏冰裂纹、靛蓝铺满画面。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nHand block print on cotton, Rajasthan style (Bagru, Sanganer). Undyed cotton #EEE5CF is the ground, over half the frame, and the skin colour; indigo #2A3A68 for water or sky bands and patterns; madder red #AA322A for borders, clothing and the hero; turmeric #DEA42C for small motifs; black key block #281E1C for outlines. Stamp blocks about 1/8 of frame height, misregistered 2–4 px, tone jumping slightly at seams even in large bands; dye bleeds along the weave, fills unevenly. Mud-resist crackle only inside indigo, fine and sparse. Carved, rough outlines about 1/300 of frame width. Flat frieze in a paisley and vine border; waves as scallop rows; profile figures with miniature-painting faces, kohl-lined eyes. Camera: pan along the frieze, push in; cut on a turning motif. Avoid: vector edges, perfect registration, faceless figures, dense crackle, indigo flooding the frame.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\nBagru 泥防染靛蓝,Sanganer 木版印花边,拉贾斯坦细密画侧脸\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮甘地：圆框钢丝眼镜、土布披巾的边、竹杖、一把盐。全程 1.1 倍速，不做慢放。从队伍硬切到动作：弯腰、捧盐、举起，再转向队伍举得更高。最后用纺车转动的轮子做转场收尾。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 沿海边的队伍横摇：穿白土布的志愿者、穿靛蓝和茜草红纱丽的妇女、自治旗；Clawd 在队伍最前面入画。（0.8s）\n2. 中近景，镜头慢推：戴圆框眼镜、披土布披巾的 Clawd 弯腰捧起一把盐，举起来盐粒闪光，再转向队伍举得更高。（1.1s）\n3. 村妇坐在纺车前纺线；镜头推向转动的纺车轮，接下一部。（0.6s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nRichard Attenborough, Gandhi (1982),Gandhi one-sheet (1982),Bagru dabu and Sanganer block prints,Rajasthani miniature painting\n"},{"id":"S6-O56","name":"母女情深","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O56.jpg","markdown":"# O56 母女情深（Terms of Endearment）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 大片年代 1980–1989\n- 长什么样：摊开的家庭相册，里面是褪色的宝丽来和柯达快照：发青的黑、奶油色高光、白边。其中一张宝丽来活过来，变成海滩飞车的追拍照片。《母女情深》讲的是母女三十年。\n- 风格：家庭相册宝丽来\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1983\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n70 到 80 年代的生活快照，摊开在相册里。照片已褪色：黑色抬成偏青的灰（约 #2F3A3A），高光柔和发奶油色，略暖、略过曝，偏软，细颗粒（±4%），饱和度约正常的六成。宝丽来白边、照相馆四连拍条、圆珠笔手写说明（用用户的语言）。相册铺满画面，奶油色页面，从正上方看，光柔和均匀，淡淡塑料膜反光。照片里有东西在动就用追拍：背景横向拖糊，主体清晰；静止主体用浅景深。人物像拍的不像画的：比例真实、明暗柔和、轻微虚焦、脸不大。镜头：俯拍推进一张照片直到铺满（约三秒），切进活起来的场景、颜色恢复，再拉出。对标宝丽来 SX-70 和柯达彩色照片。避免：平面卡通造型、干净的现代色彩、光束、饱和的照片。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\n1970s–80s snapshots in an open album. Faded dyes: blacks lifted to teal grey about #2F3A3A, creamy rolled-off highlights, mild warm cast, slight overexposure, softness, fine grain (±4%), saturation about 60% of normal. White Polaroid borders, photo-booth strips (four frames stacked), ballpoint captions in the user's language. The album fills the frame, cream pages seen from above in soft, even light, a faint plastic sheen. In a photo, a moving subject gets a panning-shutter look (background smeared sideways, subject sharp); still subjects get shallow depth of field. People look photographed: real proportions, soft shading, slight blur, small faces. Camera: an overhead push into one photo until it fills the frame (about 3 s), a cut into the living scene as colour returns, then a pull-back. References: Polaroid SX-70, Kodacolor prints. Avoid: flat cartoon shapes, crisp modern colour, light shafts, saturated prints.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\nSX-70 与宝丽来 600 相片,柯达彩色快照,70–80 年代磁性相册,追拍拖影\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮退役宇航员加勒特，戴金色飞行员墨镜，身边坐着大笑的奥罗拉。整体约 1.8 倍速。推进相册以后直接切进照片里，褪色的调子回暖成新鲜的颜色；颜色回来时它举起双臂，最后的近景落在张开双臂的样子上。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 俯拍胡桃木桌上摊开的相册，一路推进那张散放的加尔维斯顿宝丽来，直到照片铺满画面。（1.2s）\n2. 宝丽来活了过来：红色科尔维特冲过浪花，副驾的奥罗拉挥着手，颜色慢慢回来；镜头拉开到更宽的海滩。（0.8s）\n3. 近景：戴金色飞行员墨镜的 Clawd 扮加勒特，在座位后站起来张开双臂；镜头慢推。（0.5s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nJames L. Brooks, Terms of Endearment (1983),Terms of Endearment one-sheet (1983),Polaroid SX-70 and Kodacolor snapshots,1970s magnetic-page photo albums\n"},{"id":"S6-O57","name":"莫扎特传","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O57.jpg","markdown":"# O57 莫扎特传（Amadeus）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 大片年代 1980–1989\n- 长什么样：黑纸剪成的侧面人像镶着金边，坐满马蹄形的金色包厢和红丝绒，对着星空布景的舞台。《莫扎特传》的高光是首演之夜，一个画面装下乐池、舞台、观众和暗处的萨列里。\n- 风格：洛可可剪影 · 烛光金色歌剧院\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1984\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n18 世纪剪纸剪影，放在烛光照亮的洛可可室内。每个人物是实心黑侧影 #0B0705，各有自己的剪纸细节（发型、鼻子、蕾丝镂空、物件），朝最近烛火的一侧描 2–3px（按 1920×1080）亮金边 #F7E3A0，顺衣褶勾几根细金线。金色浮雕 #B88A2E 和红丝绒 #6E0F14 像包厢一样框住画面；户外题材画成舞台上被照亮的布景，剪纸人物站在布景前。红与金占画面大半，夜蓝 #0C1A3A 只用于远处天空。只有主角侧影里的脸是画出来、有颜色的。烛火轻微跳动（亮度±5%），暖光晕约五个火苗宽，薄烟、暗角、细颗粒。招牌镜头：前景剪影框住远处亮着的舞台，慢横移再推进。对标剪影 Edouart、Miers，摄影师 Miroslav Ondříček（《莫扎特传》，1984）。避免：没有剪纸细节的黑块；复制粘贴的侧影；蓝色墙面；频闪的烛火。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\n18th-century cut-paper silhouettes. Each figure: a solid black profile #0B0705 with its own cut detail (hair, nose, lace holes, props), a 2–3 px pale-gold rim #F7E3A0 (at 1920×1080) toward the nearest flame, and hairline gold lines along clothing folds. Gilt relief #B88A2E and red velvet #6E0F14 frame the scene like an opera box; an outdoor subject becomes a lit painted backdrop on a stage, cut-paper figures in front. Reds and golds fill most of the frame; night blue #0C1A3A only for the far sky. Only the hero gets a painted, coloured face. Candles flicker gently (±5%), warm halos about five flames wide; light haze, vignette, fine grain. Signature shot: silhouettes framing a bright stage far back; slow lateral glide, then push. References: silhouettists Edouart, Miers; cinematographer Miroslav Ondříček (*Amadeus*, 1984). Avoid: plain black blobs; copy-pasted profiles; blue walls; strobing flames.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n18 世纪剪影肖像,洛可可金色浮雕,烛光泛光,申克尔的星空布景\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮莫扎特：白色扑粉假发配粉色发带、蕾丝领巾，指挥棒跟着音乐打拍子。一个运镜从乐池升到舞台，正好落在夜后的高音上，然后硬切到暗处看着的萨列里。1:1 时间，不放慢。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 沿着烛光乐池横移到挥棒指挥的 Clawd，再越过它升起、后拉，落到星空舞台上，夜后抬臂唱高音。（1.6s）\n2. 硬切到萨列里的暗包厢：一排剪影里只有他的脸是画出来的，被一支蜡烛从下面照亮；缓慢推进。Clawd 不在画面里。（0.9s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nMiloš Forman, Amadeus (1984),Miroslav Ondříček (cinematography),Karl Friedrich Schinkel, Queen of the Night set (1815),Augustin Edouart,John Miers\n"},{"id":"S6-O58","name":"走出非洲","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O58.jpg","markdown":"# O58 走出非洲（Out of Africa）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 大片年代 1980–1989\n- 长什么样：实时 3D 的低空飞行：铬黄色双翼机掠过苏打湖，上万只火烈鸟惊起，琥珀色薄雾光。这部片最被记住的就是丹尼斯带凯伦飞越肯尼亚，和她向后座伸出的那只手。\n- 风格：3D 双翼机飞越东非裂谷\n- 关键词：3D\n- 年份：1985\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n实时 3D，像 80 年代宽银幕实景摄影：模型按真实比例、细节到位（如支柱、拉线、铆钉）；地形平滑，不要低多边形碎面。默认光：从镜头看，低斜的太阳在主角身后（逆光），奶油色淡天空，琥珀薄雾，远山分三四层渐淡，赭黄大地上长长的柔影。整体明亮：七成画面在中高调，阴影暖灰不深褐；近白不超过五分之一。胶片曲线、低饱和、细颗粒、轻暗角。上千只实例化的鸟或动物（如粉 #F3B3AE 配深红 #DF3F55），主角经过时一波波错开起飞或散开。招牌镜头：越过移动中主角的肩膀看远景；其次是离地 10–20 米的追拍和一个对着太阳的逆光镜头。对标摄影师 David Watkin（《走出非洲》，1985）。避免：冷而干净的 CG 日光；发暗的棕褐调；整齐划一的鸟群。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nReal-time 3D like 1980s widescreen location photography: true-scale models with fine detail (e.g. struts, wires, rivets); smooth terrain, not low-poly. Default light: late-afternoon sun low behind the hero as seen from camera, pale cream sky, amber haze, distant ridges in 3–4 paler layers, ochre ground with long soft shadows. Pale overall: about 70% of the frame in light mid-tones, shadows warm grey, not deep brown; under a fifth near-white. Grade: filmic curve, low saturation, light grain, soft vignette. Thousands of instanced birds or animals (e.g. pink #F3B3AE with crimson #DF3F55) lift or scatter in staggered waves as the hero passes. Signature shot: over the moving hero's shoulder across open land; then a high chase 10–20 m up and one shot into the sun. Reference: cinematographer David Watkin (*Out of Africa*, 1985). Avoid: cold clean CG daylight; dark sepia; lockstep flocks.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\nthree.js 实时 3D,David Watkin 的航拍,暖调胶片调色,实例化的火烈鸟群\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 坐在凯伦的前座：皮飞行帽、黄铜护目镜、白丝巾。两个外景镜头硬切，交代火烈鸟湖上的速度和空间；最后的座舱镜头留给片中那个动作：Clawd 转身向后伸手，丹尼斯握住，落在最后几拍。飞行时间是屏幕时间的 1.5–1.6 倍。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 贴着湖面仰拍追机：黄色双翼机从头顶掠过，Clawd 小小地坐在前座探身往外看，火烈鸟从水面腾起掠过镜头。（0.8s）\n2. 硬切到正对太阳的逆光全景：飞机迎面飞来，两侧是正在升起的粉色鸟群。（0.7s）\n3. 从后座往前的机上镜头：戴皮飞行帽、黄铜护目镜、白丝巾在风里飘的 Clawd 转身向后伸手，丹尼斯戴手套的手握住它。（1s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nSydney Pollack, Out of Africa (1985),David Watkin (cinematography),Out of Africa (1985) poster,de Havilland DH.60G Gipsy Moth G-ABAK\n"},{"id":"S6-O59","name":"野战排","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O59.jpg","markdown":"# O59 野战排（Platoon）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 大片年代 1980–1989\n- 长什么样：快而有力的钢笔线，橄榄绿、土黄、烟灰和火光橙的淡彩，速写本纸四边留白。像随军画家在现场画下的，贴近这部片从步兵眼里看越战的视角。\n- 风格：越战随军速写 · 钢笔淡彩\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1986\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n现场报道式钢笔淡彩速写。钢笔用暖黑，线宽约画面宽度的 1/600，快而肯定，收笔甩出，转角冲出头；暗部用快速排线和折线，人物和地面都排线。淡彩透明而大胆：边缘湿软、有回流花、深浅不匀（不要硬边多边形），铺满画面约七成，整体错开线稿 2–4px（按 1920×1080）。颜色：橄榄绿 #70783E、土黄 #CEA054、烟灰 #706C70，加一个热烈点缀色 #F0822E 给天空和光源；肤色用淡土黄；暖米色速写本纸 #F2ECDE。只有最亮处、四边窄条和笔刷漏掉处留纸，纸纹很轻。角落用用户的语言潦草写一句铅笔速记（地点、时间、天气），字高约画面 1/40。分层便于横移；换视角硬切，像翻页。对标美国陆军越战战地艺术计划（1966–70）。避免：插画式精修；空着的纸；发脏的淡彩；重噪点。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nReportage pen-and-wash field sketch. Pen: warm black, fast confident lines ~1/600 of frame width, flicked ends, overshooting corners; quick hatching and zigzags for shadow, on figures and ground. Bold transparent washes with soft wet edges, blooms and uneven pigment (not flat polygons) cover about 70% of the frame, shifted 2–4 px (at 1920×1080) off the line. Colours: olive #70783E, ochre #CEA054, smoke grey #706C70, one hot accent #F0822E for sky and light sources; skin in diluted ochre; sketchbook paper #F2ECDE. Bare paper only in highlights, a thin edge band and brush gaps; light grain. A scrawled pencil note (place, time, weather) in a corner, 1/40 of frame height, in the user's language. Layer for lateral tracks; hard cuts like turning a page. Reference: US Army Vietnam Combat Artists Program (1966–70). Avoid: polished finish; empty page; muddy washes; heavy noise.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n快速钢笔线,透明淡彩,速写本纸,美国陆军越战战地艺术计划\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮伊莱亚斯：丛林帽、狗牌。两个硬切从地面到空中再回到地面，每个镜头像速写本里的另一页；切到最后一个镜头，双臂随即猛地张成海报姿势，直升机拉起时手臂微微垂下。场景以 1.1 倍速播放。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 横移跟着在象草里前进的巡逻队；戴丛林帽、挂狗牌的 Clawd 站在中间，士兵从它身边走过。（0.8s）\n2. 硬切到从休伊直升机舱门往下看：燃烧的着陆区、被旋翼压平的草和直升机自己的影子，Clawd 小小地站在空地上；镜头缓慢拉远。（0.8s）\n3. 硬切到 Clawd 的近景，拉回海报构图：它在凝固汽油弹的橙色火光下双臂张成 V 字，两侧士兵跑过，直升机拉起离开。（0.9s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nOliver Stone, Platoon (1986),Platoon (1986) poster,US Army Vietnam Combat Artists Program (1966–70)\n"},{"id":"S6-O60","name":"末代皇帝","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O60.jpg","markdown":"# O60 末代皇帝（The Last Emperor）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 大片年代 1980–1989\n- 长什么样：绢本上的铁线描和层层渲染的石青、石绿、朱砂、泥金，界画的宫殿，几千名跪拜的百官。小皇帝的登基是清宫仪典，就用清宫画师记录仪典的画法来画。\n- 风格：工笔重彩 · 绢本宫廷画\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1987\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n绢本工笔重彩（清代宫廷画法）。由远到近画，每个形：平涂底色 → 分染（沿下缘和被遮处加深）→ 均匀铁线描（深褐，线宽约画面 1/900）→ 提金、提白；最后全画面叠三成浓度的暖赭罩染，线仍清楚。矿物色：朱砂 #C23A22、石青 #2C5A9A、石绿 #3A8A6A、藤黄 #E8B830、泥金 #CAA04A，被罩染压稳；地面和天空是中等明度的暖赭绢色，不要近白，不画饱和蓝天和橙色落日，时段变化只靠整体冷暖。不画投影和眩光。水用细线水纹。建筑用界画，瓦片一片片画出；有人群时人人不同，单人也行。绢纹淡（±3%），褐色暗角。一点透视、左右对称，大人物在前，身后深远规整。缓推。对标清代宫廷长卷，如《康熙南巡图》。避免：复制粘贴人物；不分染的平涂；写意水墨；海报式饱和色。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nChinese gongbi zhongcai (fine-line, heavy-colour court painting) on aged silk. Far to near, each form: flat base, a wash darkening its lower, overlapped edges, an even iron-wire outline (dark brown, ~1/900 frame width), gold and white lifts. Last, a 30% warm ochre glaze over everything, lines still clear. Minerals: cinnabar #C23A22, azurite #2C5A9A, malachite #3A8A6A, gamboge #E8B830, gold #CAA04A, muted by the glaze; ground and sky mid-value warm silk ochre, never near-white, saturated blue or orange; time of day shows only as overall warmth. No cast shadows or glare. Fine-line water ripples. Ruled-line architecture, every tile drawn; crowds all differ; a lone figure works. Faint silk weave (±3%), brown vignette. Symmetrical one-point composition: a large figure before a deep, ordered space. Slow pushes. Reference: Qing court scrolls, e.g. *Kangxi's Southern Inspection Tour*. Avoid: copy-pasted figures; unwashed flat fills; loose ink wash; poster saturation.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n工笔重彩,界画,清代宫廷画卷,Storaro 的色彩\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮小溥仪：红缨朝冠、朝珠、马蹄袖。帘幕分开，Clawd 踩着节拍走出来；硬切到百官叩首的波浪，最后几拍落在海报式的全景上。场景以 1.1 倍速播放。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 绣着团龙的黄色绸帘占满画面，帘子分开，戴红缨朝冠的 Clawd 走上丹陛；镜头微微后拉。（1s）\n2. 硬切到低机位横移，扫过跪着的百官，一排排穿补服的官员波浪般叩首。（0.6s）\n3. 硬切到海报构图并慢慢拉宽：Clawd 大大地站在丹陛上，身后是跪满百官的广场和金色重檐，前景是蝈蝈罐。（0.9s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nBernardo Bertolucci, The Last Emperor (1987),Vittorio Storaro (cinematography),The Last Emperor (1987) poster,Kangxi Southern Inspection Tour scrolls,Qianlong's Grand Review\n"},{"id":"S6-O61","name":"雨人","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O61.jpg","markdown":"# O61 雨人（Rain Man）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 大片年代 1980–1989\n- 长什么样：平滑的喷枪渐变、硬边遮罩、镀铬高光和粉绿霓虹，叠一层细细的青色计数标注。80 年代画风配上雷蒙眼里的世界：82、82、82，共 246 根牙签，还有牌桌算牌。\n- 风格：80 年代喷枪插画 + 雷蒙的计数\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1988\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n80 年代喷枪插画，叠一层工程制图标注。每个形是硬边遮罩里喷出的平滑渐变，边缘 1–2px（按 1920×1080）细飞沫。明亮高调：薄荷绿 #79BDA6 的墙或天空、奶油白，霓虹粉 #F58CB5 和品红 #C9467F 点缀，地面铺黑白棋盘格；紫色和夜色不超过两成，其他时段仍是薄荷绿奶油底，晚霞用粉色画。人物学 Patrick Nagel：苍白平整的皮肤、少而利落的线、成块深色头发，粉与薄荷绿轮廓光，但保留本人的年龄衣着。金属玻璃学空山基镀铬：天空色带、硬地平线、十字星光。最上层是细青色标注 #56E6FF、白字 #D9FBFF（字高约画面 1/40）：圆点引线、虚线圈组、尺寸线；像《雨人》的雷蒙，等宽数字数故事里要数的东西，圈一组滚一次。慢推或慢拉。避免：合成波式的条纹太阳；UI 框；发糊的边。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\n1980s airbrush illustration with a drafting overlay. Every shape: a hard-edged mask filled with a smooth sprayed gradient, 1–2 px overspray rim (at 1920×1080). Bright, high-key: mint #79BDA6 walls or sky, cream, neon pink #F58CB5 and magenta #C9467F accents, black-and-white checker floor or ground; purple and night under 20%; at other times of day keep mint and cream; pink carries any sunset. Figures à la Patrick Nagel (flat pale skin, few crisp lines, dark hair masses, pink and mint rim light), keeping their own age and dress. Metal and glass in Hajime Sorayama chrome: banded sky reflection, hard horizon line, cross-star glints. Overlay: thin cyan callouts #56E6FF, near-white text #D9FBFF (1/40 frame height), dotted leaders, dashed rings, dimension lines; monospace numbers count whatever the story counts, Rain Man style, as each group is circled. Slow push/pull. Avoid: synthwave suns; UI boxes; soft edges.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n80 年代喷枪插画,Patrick Nagel 式人物,空山基式镀铬,工程图标注\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮雷蒙：扣到顶的格子领、胸袋里插着几支笔和一支铅笔。剪辑跟着计数走：开头几拍圈出三堆牙签，246 出来时它歪头，然后硬切到拉斯维加斯，每张牌落下都跳出一个计数标记。场景以 1.1 倍速播放。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 俯拍黑白格的餐馆地面：撒了一地的牙签一堆堆被圈出来，数字滚动计数，镜头缓慢拉远，让顶上的 246 进画。（1s）\n2. 硬切到 Clawd 的中近景：格子衬衫领、胸袋里一排笔，歪着头看地上标着 82 的牙签堆，头越歪越低；镜头缓慢拉远。（0.8s）\n3. 硬切到拉斯维加斯的 21 点牌桌，越过荷官肩膀拍：牌从牌靴里飞出，旁边跳出 Hi-Lo 标记和累计点数；Clawd 坐在 Nagel 式女人和查理之间。（0.7s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nBarry Levinson, Rain Man (1988),John Seale (cinematography),Patrick Nagel,Hajime Sorayama\n"},{"id":"S6-O62","name":"为黛西小姐开车","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O62.jpg","markdown":"# O62 为黛西小姐开车（Driving Miss Daisy）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 大片年代 1980–1989\n- 长什么样：扎实温暖的洛克威尔写实：边缘干净、笔触可见，午后阳光穿过橡树，邻居各忙各的。这部片就是 50 年代亚特兰大一辆车里的两个人，本身就像一张《星期六晚邮报》封面。\n- 风格：洛克威尔式油画插画\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1989\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n洛克威尔式 50 年代美国杂志封面油画。人物接近写实：头约占身高 1/7，脸和手用三四块明暗面塑出、不靠描线，表情读得出。塑形平滑，笔触隐约：宽约画面 1/150，顺形体走，每笔与底色只差±5%。默认光：左上方暖色午后阳光，地上有清楚的投影和树影，阴影冷蓝灰；别的光也保持暖主光冷阴影。中等饱和：奶油天空 #E6DCC2、砖红 #9A4A38、叶绿、瓶绿 #1F3A24、柔和肤色。光面映出环境。背景有小故事：有人处路人各忙各的，主角独处就靠物件。轻画布纹、暖光油、柔和暗角。镜头：有纵深的四分之三全景缓推，越切越近，落在手或脸上。对标洛克威尔 1940–50 年代《星期六晚邮报》封面。避免：卡通脸；重纹理滤镜；无笔触的数码平涂；空背景。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nNorman Rockwell-style mid-century magazine-cover oil illustration. Near-realistic figures: head about 1/7 of body height, faces and hands modelled from 3–4 value planes, not outlines; readable expressions. Smooth modelling; faint strokes about 1/150 of frame width, following form, each within ±5% of the colour beneath. Default light: warm afternoon sun from the upper left, clear cast and dappled shadows in cool blue-grey; other light keeps a warm key and cool shadows. Palette: cream sky #E6DCC2, brick red #9A4A38, leaf greens, bottle green #1F3A24, soft skin. Gloss reflects surroundings. Background storytelling: busy bystanders where there are people, telling objects when the hero is alone. Light canvas texture, warm varnish, soft vignette. Camera: deep three-quarter wide, slow push, closer cuts ending on a hand or face. Reference: Rockwell's *Saturday Evening Post* covers (1940s–50s). Avoid: cartoon faces; texture filters; flat digital shading; empty backgrounds.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n诺曼·洛克威尔油画插画,《星期六晚邮报》封面,3D 建模的车再铺油画笔触\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮霍克，戴藏青色司机帽。三个硬切从车外到车里再到后视镜，一个比一个近；结果留到最后：镜子里黛西小姐的板脸在最后几拍慢慢松成微笑。场景时间平均是屏幕时间的 1.8 倍左右，车仍然开得从容。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 墨绿色 1949 年哈德逊的四分之三前侧，车沿着橡树成荫、红砖房和白门廊的街道慢慢开来；戴司机帽的 Clawd 握着方向盘，黛西小姐板着脸坐在后窗里；缓慢推进。（0.9s）\n2. 硬切到后座往前拍的定机位：前景是黛西小姐的大侧脸，后面是开车的 Clawd 的背影，街景从车窗外流过。（0.8s）\n3. 硬切到后视镜特写，也就是海报构图：左边是帽檐下 Clawd 的眼睛，右边是黛西小姐，慢慢露出一点笑；镜头轻推。（0.8s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nBruce Beresford, Driving Miss Daisy (1989),Driving Miss Daisy (1989) poster,Norman Rockwell, Saturday Evening Post covers\n"},{"id":"S6-O63","name":"与狼共舞","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O63.jpg","markdown":"# O63 与狼共舞（Dances with Wolves）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 世纪末 1990–1999\n- 长什么样：一个军人的野外日记：轻重有变化的铅笔线、透明水彩、泛黄的布浆纸、墨水手写和页边的铅笔批注。邓巴在片中一直写画日记，所以草原是从他的速写里活过来的。\n- 风格：邓巴日记 · 铅笔 + 透明水彩\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1990\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n一本活过来的 19 世纪野外日记：看得见铅笔线的满幅透明水彩。铅笔底稿 #3A363A 有轻重和断笔，暗部轻排线，透过颜色仍读得出。水彩边缘湿软，有回流花、积色深边和颗粒（不要平涂多边形），铺满约八成画面，浓郁偏暖。默认魔幻时刻：灰玫瑰色天空 #C88F76 往上转淡紫，玫瑰色条云，低太阳，赭金大地 #DEBE87，远处紫，长投影；朝太阳的边缘点几笔不透明浅金。泛黄布浆纸 #F0E4C8，淡水渍霉斑，纸色透出来。铁胆墨水 #34221C 写小字草书标注和箭头。招牌动作只在开头约两秒：先只有铅笔，镜头推进时颜色从远到近一块块铺上；之后每帧满彩。对标 Karl Bodmer、George Catlin 的野外速写。避免：灰脏或发白的淡彩；均匀数码描边；开头后仍只有铅笔。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nA 19th-century field journal come to life: full-colour watercolour over visible pencil. Pencil under-drawing #3A363A, pressure-varied and broken, lightly hatched in shadow, readable through the washes. Brushed washes with soft wet edges, blooms, pooled rims and granulation (not flat polygons) cover about 80% of the frame in rich warm colour. Default magic hour: dusty-rose sky #C88F76 turning lavender above, streaky rose clouds, low sun, ochre-gold land #DEBE87, violet distance, long shadows, opaque pale-gold lifts on sun-facing edges. Yellowed rag paper #F0E4C8, faint stains and foxing, showing through. Iron-gall ink #34221C for small labels and arrows. Signature move, once at the opening (about 2 s): bare pencil, then colour arrives wash by wash, far to near, during a push-in; after that, full colour. References: Bodmer and Catlin field sketches. Avoid: grey, muddy or pale washes; uniform digital outlines; later pencil-only frames.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n铅笔底稿,透明水彩,铁胆墨水手写,Bodmer、Catlin 的野外水彩\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮邓巴：带金色帽绳的黑色骑兵宽檐帽，一手日记本、一手铅笔。推近页面和上色是同一个动作；切到活过来的草原落在乐句中间，抬帽致意落在最后几拍。1:1 时间。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 推近日记的右页：Clawd 的自画像和狼「双袜」一开始只是铅笔底稿，水彩一笔笔铺上来，先是天空，最后才是 Clawd。（1.2s）\n2. 切到活成满幅彩色的日落草原：一个推镜从平原推到对望的 Clawd 和双袜，身后野牛群奔过；Clawd 抬帽致意。（1.3s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nKevin Costner, Dances with Wolves (1990),Dean Semler (cinematography),Dances with Wolves (1990) poster,Karl Bodmer,George Catlin\n"},{"id":"S6-O64","name":"沉默的羔羊","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O64.jpg","markdown":"# O64 沉默的羔羊（The Silence of the Lambs）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 世纪末 1990–1999\n- 长什么样：细密的铜版排线和点刻，上面薄薄地手工上色，图版编号和拉丁文标签印在泛黄的纸上。海报上的鬼脸天蛾本就该钉在标本图版上，同样冷峻的铜版画法一直延伸到莱克特的囚室。\n- 风格：手工上色的博物学铜版画\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1991\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n手工上色的博物学铜版画。所有物体用暖黑褐刻线画：密集平行线和交叉排线（线距约画面宽度 1/400，暗处线更粗），柔和过渡用点刻，物下有排线投影；天空留纸色或只几道稀疏横线。线内平涂水彩，中等饱和、颜色认得出：纸 #EFE3C4、赭褐 #5A3A22、铁锈红 #B4431E、黄 #E6AD24、灰紫 #7D6F8E。图版：主体作图 1 居中，约占版面高六成，四周 3–6 个编号小图（局部或相关物件），各约五分之一高；上方字距拉开的小型大写标题，下方编号图注，压印斜边版框，淡霉斑。默认均匀日光，时段变化只调着色冷暖；暗室内转冷，侧面硬光。镜头：静止图版里只有一样东西在动（虫、叶、手），再匀速慢推到关键细节。对标 Moses Harris《The Aurelian》（1766）。避免：平滑渐变；刺眼的颜色；主体缩成小晕影。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nA hand-coloured natural-history copperplate. Every object in engraved warm black-brown line: dense parallel and cross-hatching (spacing about 1/400 of frame width, thicker in darks), stipple for soft tones, hatched shadows beneath; sky bare or sparsely lined. Flat watercolour tints inside the line, medium saturation, clearly coloured: paper #EFE3C4, umber #5A3A22, rust #B4431E, yellow #E6AD24, grey-violet #7D6F8E. Plate: the subject as Fig. 1 in the centre, about 60% of plate height, 3–6 numbered part or object figures around it, each about 1/5 high; spaced small-caps title, numbered legend, bevelled plate mark, light foxing. Even daylight; time of day only shifts the tints' warmth; dark interiors go cold, hard side light. Camera: a still plate where one thing moves (insect, leaf, hand), then a slow push to a key detail. Reference: Moses Harris, *The Aurelian* (1766). Avoid: smooth gradients; garish colour; a tiny vignetted subject.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n铜版雕刻排线,手工上色,维多利亚昆虫学图版,Moses Harris《The Aurelian》\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 先是图版上的「图 4」，再扮别着 FBI 学员证的克拉丽丝。飞蛾在开头几拍落下；硬切到囚室，推向莱克特的镜头一气呵成、中间不切，他的笑留到最后才出来。场景时间约为屏幕时间的 1.8 倍。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 整张昆虫学图版「Acherontia styx」：一只活的鬼脸天蛾从茄科植物上飞起，落在 Clawd 头顶；镜头轻推。（0.9s）\n2. 硬切到地下监区：别着 FBI 学员证的 Clawd（克拉丽丝）站在玻璃前；接着一个连续的推镜向右、向里推，直到莱克特的脸占满画面，嘴角露出一丝笑。（1.6s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nJonathan Demme, The Silence of the Lambs (1991),Tak Fujimoto (cinematography),The Silence of the Lambs (1991) poster,Moses Harris, The Aurelian\n"},{"id":"S6-O65","name":"不可饶恕","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O65.jpg","markdown":"# O65 不可饶恕（Unforgiven）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 世纪末 1990–1999\n- 长什么样：两种调子的厚涂西部油画：雷明顿金红晚霞下的平原，和他晚年被暖黄窗光点亮的蓝绿夜景。《不可饶恕》以夕阳下坟前的剪影开场和收尾，决战发生在雨夜的小镇。\n- 风格：雷明顿西部油画 · 晚霞与雨夜\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1992\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n弗雷德里克·雷明顿式西部油画。主调是晚霞，天黑透之前都用：橙到深红的金红天空用长横笔触，太阳压在地平线上，大地和人物是暗暖剪影，向阳一侧一道细暖轮廓光。第二种调子只在天黑透后用：蓝绿夜景（天 #17333A，月光边 #9CC4BC），暖黄窗光 #FFCC6A 铺在深色地面 #1E1C16 上，加雨。先画清楚的大形，再上笔触：每笔宽约画面宽度 1/80、长为宽的三到六倍，颜料饱满、笔端松散；每笔与底色明暗只差±6%，顺着各个面走。画布纹要轻。地平线低，天空占五到六成，人物小到中等。镜头：从剪影大全景慢推到人物。对标雷明顿晚年夜景画、摄影师 Jack N. Green（《不可饶恕》，1992）。避免：平滑渐变；硬边色带；打碎形体的满屏笔触；暖场景人物发灰；大幅横摇。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nFrederic Remington-style Western oil painting. Primary register, for anything before full dark: sunset, a red-gold sky (orange into deep red) in long horizontal strokes, the sun on the horizon, land and figures dark warm silhouettes, thin warm rim sunward. Second register, only after dark: a blue-green nocturne (sky #17333A, moonlit edges #9CC4BC, warm window light #FFCC6A on dark ground #1E1C16, rain). Broad, readable shapes first, then loaded strokes about 1/80 of frame width wide, 3–6 times as long, soft broken ends, each within ±6% brightness of the colour beneath, following the surface. Light canvas texture. Low horizon, sky 50–60% of the frame, figures small to medium. Camera: one slow push from a silhouetted wide to the figure. References: Remington's late nocturnes; cinematographer Jack N. Green (*Unforgiven*, 1992). Avoid: smooth gradients; hard-edged flat bands; shattered shapes; grey figures in warm scenes; wide pans.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n雷明顿的暖色平原,雷明顿晚年的夜景,厚涂油彩,开场字幕的剪影\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮威廉·芒尼：黑色宽檐帽、长雨衣立领、一支步枪。开场剪影到坟前是一个镜头，推到位时它正好抬头；唯一的一刀硬切到雨夜，Clawd 骑马进镇。1:1 时间，不放慢。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 一个推镜从整片晚霞（木屋、风车、孤树、挡在太阳前的十字架）推到坟边的 Clawd（芒尼）；它抬起头，把步枪横到身前。（1.4s）\n2. 硬切到雨夜的大威士忌镇：Clawd 骑一匹兔褐马走过泥街，旁边是亮着窗的 Greeley's 酒馆，镜头推向它滴水的帽檐。（1.1s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nClint Eastwood, Unforgiven (1992),Jack N. Green (cinematography),Unforgiven (1992) poster (Bill Gold),Frederic Remington, nocturnes\n"},{"id":"S6-O66","name":"辛德勒的名单","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O66.jpg","markdown":"# O66 辛德勒的名单（Schindler's List）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 世纪末 1990–1999\n- 长什么样：冷灰、高反差的黑白，粗颗粒，高光晕开；只有小女孩的红大衣和 Clawd 的橙色有颜色。这正是斯皮尔伯格的手法：红大衣和安息日烛火是全片仅有的彩色。\n- 风格：黑白胶片 · 唯一的红大衣\n- 关键词：2D·B&W\n- 年份：1993\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n黑白电影质感：冷中性灰，强 S 曲线（黑接近 #111，白接近 #EEE），高光在雾里泛光晕，略软的老镜头，逐帧变化的银盐颗粒（±6%），轻微闪烁，偶有灰尘，暗角。全画面是灰的，只有故事围绕的那个主角是饱和色，以衣服为主（如陶土橙 #D97757）；可再有一个红色故事道具（#A3241F–#C9463C）。灯和火保持灰色，除非“火苗先灰后转暖”是剧情节点。长焦压缩空间，前景虚焦树枝；有人群时，体型、衣色和步态人人不同。默认阴天中间调，暗部不死黑。招牌镜头：长焦俯看许多走动的小人，带色的那个在其中；没有人群就让主角很小地落在大片灰里。对标摄影师 Janusz Kamiński（《辛德勒的名单》，1993）。避免：棕褐或偏蓝调色；无颗粒的干净灰；超过两样有颜色的东西。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nBlack-and-white film look: cold neutral greys, a strong S-curve (blacks near #111, whites near #EEE), highlights haloing in haze, a soft old lens, silver grain re-rolled every frame (±6%), slight flicker, rare dust, vignette. Everything is grey except the one hero the story centres on, saturated, mainly clothing (e.g. terracotta #D97757); optionally one red story object (#A3241F–#C9463C). Lamps and fires stay grey, unless a flame turning from grey to warm is the story beat. Long-lens compression, soft foreground branches; in a crowd, no two people alike in build, tone or gait. Default: overcast mid-key, shadows open. Signature shot: high long-lens view of many small walking figures, the coloured one among them; without a crowd, the hero tiny in wide grey space. Reference: cinematographer Janusz Kamiński (*Schindler's List*, 1993). Avoid: sepia or blue toning; grain-free greys; more than two coloured things.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n高反差黑白胶片,粗颗粒银盐,局部留色,Janusz Kamiński\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮辛德勒：灰色软呢帽、手握缰绳，坐在马上往下看。四个硬切跟着他的目光走：山坡、长焦主观镜头、街面，最后收在蜡烛上，每个镜头约一拍半。全程克制：人只是在走，不出现任何暴力。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 山坡上骑着深色马的 Clawd（辛德勒），坡下的街道挤满被驱赶的人群，人群里有一个红点；镜头轻推。（0.7s）\n2. 硬切到辛德勒的长焦主观镜头，跟住独自走在人群空当里的红大衣小女孩。（0.6s）\n3. 硬切到街面：横移镜头和小女孩同速，三排人从店铺前往左走，一件虚焦的大衣从镜头前掠过。（0.6s）\n4. 硬切到黑暗里的两支安息日蜡烛：火苗先亮成灰白，再变成彩色；缓慢推进。Clawd 不在画面里。（0.6s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nSteven Spielberg, Schindler's List (1993),Janusz Kamiński (cinematography),Schindler's List (1993) poster\n"},{"id":"S6-O67","name":"阿甘正传","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O67.jpg","markdown":"# O67 阿甘正传（Forrest Gump）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 世纪末 1990–1999\n- 长什么样：平行折立起来的印刷卡片，白色纸芯、灰色纸板边、层与层之间的投影和浅景深，放在胡桃木书桌上。阿甘坐在长椅上把一生讲成故事，翻开一页就立起一段经历。\n- 风格：立体书 · 真实书桌上的纸艺\n- 关键词：2.5D\n- 年份：1994\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n一本放在书桌上实拍的立体书。每个元素是平的模切卡片，靠平行折立在打开成 90° 的背景页前，印着透明水彩和带纸纹的彩铅排线，切口露出白纸芯和灰纸板边。默认光：左上方窗户进来的明亮日光，层间柔和投影。卡面（天蓝、暖沙、绿）和房间都保持日光，故事在任何时段都一样；时段只画在印刷画面里（如低太阳、夜空）。胡桃木书桌 #6B3E1E，后面的房间虚成暖色柔光。物理景深，焦点在主角那层。招牌镜头：高机位四分之三角度看整个跨页。一个连续镜头：钻进一页、拉远到整本书、翻页代替剪辑、再推进下一跨页。对标 Robert Sabuda、Matthew Reinhart 的立体书。避免：像实心模型的卡片；昏暗压抑的房间；卡通脸。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nA real pop-up book filmed on a desk. Every element: a flat die-cut card on parallel folds before a 90° backdrop page, printed with transparent watercolour and grainy coloured-pencil hatching; cut edges show a white core and grey board. Default light: bright window daylight from the upper left, soft shadows between layers. Print colours (sky blue, warm sand, greens) and room light stay daylight whatever the story's time of day; that time appears only in the print (e.g. a low sun, a night sky). Walnut desk #6B3E1E; the room a warm blur. Physical depth of field, focus on the hero's layer. Signature shot: a high three-quarter view of the whole spread. One continuous move: dive into a page, pull back, turn the page instead of cutting, push into the next spread. References: Robert Sabuda, Matthew Reinhart. Avoid: solid-looking cards; dark moody rooms; cartoon faces.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\nRobert Sabuda 立体书结构,水彩、彩铅印刷质感,three.js 纸卡,物理景深\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮阿甘：长椅上捧着一盒巧克力，公路和船上戴红色棒球帽。12 拍一个镜头不切：羽毛把镜头带进书里，两次翻页（约 2.7 秒和 4.2 秒）代替剪辑。3.25 秒开始跑，紧接着停下、转身。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 一镜到底：书桌上合着的书翻开，萨凡纳的广场立起来；镜头跟着一根飘落的白羽毛降到长椅，Clawd 把巧克力递给护士；拉远、翻页，推进到纪念碑谷的 US-163，戴红帽的 Clawd 领着一群胡子拉碴的跟跑者跑近、停下、转身面对他们；再拉远、翻页，推进到闪电交加的暴风雨里的捕虾船「珍妮号」。（5s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nRobert Zemeckis, Forrest Gump (1994),Don Burgess (cinematography),Forrest Gump (1994) poster,Robert Sabuda & Matthew Reinhart\n"},{"id":"S6-O68","name":"勇敢的心","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O68.jpg","markdown":"# O68 勇敢的心（Braveheart）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 世纪末 1990–1999\n- 长什么样：一页中世纪手抄本：带冲压点的磨光金箔、青金石蓝、朱红、哥特体字，中间一幅窗口似的战役细密画。《勇敢的心》是苏格兰传奇，片尾那声 FREEDOM 做成泥金首字母。\n- 风格：泥金手抄本 · 贴金细密画\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1995\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n一页泥金装饰手抄本，取局部或双页填满画面。羊皮纸 #ECDCB4、褐色墨线 #2A1A10、青金石蓝 #27469A、朱红 #C43A20，加抛光金箔：石膏底隆起，有打孔小圆点花，一道烛光高光只在金上慢慢扫过。细密画背景多为金箔（夜景用青金石底加金星），金至少占画面一成。哥特式人物平涂加墨线。凯尔特编结纹、常春藤边框、页边怪诞小兽；正文写故事（拉丁文或用户的语言），宽头笔哥特体，红蓝行尾线，一个大金首字母。版式：细密画如一扇窗，约占半页，旁为文字栏；窗后分层，推进出视差；人物可破框。左上暖烛光，褐色暗角，淡颜料颗粒。对标《凯尔经》、摩根圣经、勒特雷尔诗篇。避免：平涂黄色代替金箔；现代字体；人脸上的高光。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nAn illuminated manuscript page (cropped, or an open spread) filling the frame. Vellum #ECDCB4, brown ink outlines #2A1A10, lapis #27469A, vermilion #C43A20, burnished gold leaf on raised gesso, with punched dot rosettes; a slow candle glint sweeps the gold only. Miniature backgrounds are mostly gold (night: lapis with gold stars); gold fills at least a tenth of the frame. Gothic figures in flat colour and ink outline. Celtic interlace, ivy borders, marginal grotesques; the story's text (Latin or the user's language) in broad-nib textura, red-and-blue line fillers, one large gilded initial. Layout: the miniature a window about half the page beside the text column, layered for parallax on pushes; figures may break out. Warm candle from upper left, brown vignette, pigment grain. References: Book of Kells, Morgan Bible, Luttrell Psalter. Avoid: flat yellow for gold; modern typefaces; glints on faces.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n《凯尔经》编结纹,哥特细密画（摩根圣经）,《鲁特雷尔诗篇》页边画,磨光金箔\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮华莱士：双手大剑、格子呢披带、额头一道蓝色 woad。四个硬切：首字母、整页、桥、交锋。冲锋在中段越来越猛，最后一个镜头里头顶的卷轴展开 freedom。场景时间约为屏幕时间的 1.8 倍。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 泥金首字母 F 的特写，字腔里是半边脸涂着蓝色 woad 的华莱士，一道金光扫过金箔。（0.7s）\n2. 硬切到整页：REEDOM、哥特体正文、页边，中间是斯特林桥细密画，Clawd 冲在最前面；镜头轻推。（0.6s）\n3. 硬切进细密画：英格兰兵挤过木桥，一名披方格马衣的骑士冲来，河里有人在挣扎。（0.6s）\n4. 硬切到 Clawd 的近景：它举起双手大剑迎向骑士的骑枪，头顶的说话卷轴展开，写着 freedom。（0.6s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nMel Gibson, Braveheart (1995),Braveheart (1995) poster,Book of Kells,Morgan Bible (Maciejowski Bible),Luttrell Psalter\n"},{"id":"S6-O69","name":"英国病人","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O69.jpg","markdown":"# O69 英国病人（The English Patient）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 世纪末 1990–1999\n- 长什么样：贴在旧书里的水彩速写，夹着地图、照片、便条和压花，整体是海报的琥珀褐。艾玛殊那本塞满画和笔记的希罗多德是全片的框架，镜头就穿过一张水彩进入游泳者洞穴。\n- 风格：探险家剪贴本水彩\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1996\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n旅人的剪贴本铺满画面，从边到边挤满 6–10 样互相压叠的东西，有的伸出画框：胶带贴的水彩画（主画面，约占四成）、手绘地图、深褐老照片、印刷页、手写便条、压花；每样带柔影和泛黄胶带。水彩画：湿中湿铺色，受光面暖赭，暗面是硬边紫玫瑰色，钢笔勾轮廓，积色边，明暗对比清楚。配色：琥珀纸 #E8D6B0、深褐 #6B4A2E、赭 #D08A3C、紫玫瑰 #9C6A86、墨 #2A1E16。画里：暗前景里一个暖光源（火把、灯）投下光池，外面是明亮的天。琥珀调色只轻压书页，画里保持干净不发脏；暗角约两成，纸纹淡。招牌动作：推进一张画，直到书页边缘从两侧滑出、人已在画里。对标摄影师 John Seale（《英国病人》，1996）。避免：平滑数码渐变；稀疏整齐的版面；书页还框着画时横摇。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nA traveller's scrapbook, crowded edge to edge with 6–10 overlapping items, some running off frame: a taped watercolour (the main image, about 40% of frame), a hand-drawn map, a sepia photo, printed pages, handwritten notes, a pressed flower; each softly shadowed, with yellowed tape. The watercolour: wet-in-wet washes, warm ochre lights, hard-edged violet-rose shadows, pen contours, pooled edges, clear value contrast. Palette: amber paper #E8D6B0, sepia #6B4A2E, ochre #D08A3C, rose-violet #9C6A86, ink #2A1E16. Inside the painting: one warm source (torch, lamp) pooling in a dark foreground, bright sky beyond. Light amber grade on the page only, never muddying the painting; vignette about 20%, faint grain. Signature move: push into the painting until the page edges slide out. Reference: cinematographer John Seale (*The English Patient*, 1996). Avoid: smooth gradients; sparse tidy layouts; panning while a page frames the painting.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n透明水彩,钢笔笔记和手写,剪贴拼贴：胶带、照片、地图,琥珀褐调色\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮艾玛殊：亚麻沙漠头巾，巾尾飘着，一手希罗多德、一手火把。推进画里的镜头本身就是转场；之后两个硬切都留在洞里，先壁画，再 Clawd。场景时间约为屏幕时间的 1.8 倍。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 剪贴本摊在桌上，一个推镜推进那张水彩，直到书页从镜头两侧滑出：Clawd 举着火把站在画里的游泳者洞穴中。（1.2s）\n2. 硬切到横移，扫过洞壁上的游泳人壁画，火光摇晃；Clawd 不在画面里。（0.6s）\n3. 硬切到 Clawd 一手火把一手书站在左边，旁边是壁画，洞口外是沙丘；镜头缓慢后移。（0.7s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nAnthony Minghella, The English Patient (1996),John Seale (cinematography),The English Patient (1996) poster,Cave of Swimmers, Wadi Sura\n"},{"id":"S6-O70","name":"泰坦尼克号","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O70.jpg","markdown":"# O70 泰坦尼克号（Titanic）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 世纪末 1990–1999\n- 长什么样：普鲁士蓝晒图纸上的白线立起来、长出透视，再显影成夕阳下全彩的 3D 船，一路入夜。船本身就是这部片的标志，所以船厂图纸和实景用的是同一艘模型。\n- 风格：蓝图显影成 3D 实景\n- 关键词：3D\n- 年份：1997\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n一张工程蓝图变成偏写实的 3D。蓝图只占开头 1–2 秒、不超过全片 15%：普鲁士蓝晒图纸（#12306B 到 #244D8F）上的白色棱线，线宽约画面 1/800，被挡的线画虚线，人物和地形用等高线；曝光不匀，有折痕、手写标注和图签。滑动变焦让立面图立成透视，再从主角往外显出全彩，显影边是带暖光的软边，幽灵线一秒内淡掉。之后是风格主体：黄金时刻的真实 3D，低太阳，暖琥珀高光 #F2B860，深暖阴影 #3A2530，水面阳光碎金，薄雾，浅景深，轻泛光，细颗粒；再到蓝调时刻和夜晚，暖灯亮起。一个不间断运镜：推近、立起在蓝图段，之后低掠主角、升起环绕、下降平分余下时间。对标摄影师 Russell Carpenter（《泰坦尼克号》，1997）。避免：在图上停太久；硬切；平而干净的 CG 光。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nA blueprint turning into photoreal-leaning 3D. Opening only (1–2 s, under 15% of the film): white edge lines about 1/800 of frame width on Prussian-blue paper (#12306B to #244D8F), hidden lines dashed, figures and terrain as contours, uneven exposure, folds, lettering. A dolly-zoom stands the elevation up into perspective; colour spreads from the hero along a soft warm edge, ghost lines fading within a second. Then the signature look: golden-hour real 3D, low sun, amber highlights #F2B860, warm shadows #3A2530, sun glitter on water, soft mist, shallow focus, gentle bloom, fine grain; then blue hour and night, warm lights coming on. One unbroken move: push in and stand up (blueprint), then, in equal shares, drift low past the hero, rise and orbit, descend. Reference: cinematographer Russell Carpenter (*Titanic*, 1997). Avoid: lingering on the drawing; hard cuts; flat CG light.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n船厂总布置蓝图,按真实尺寸建模的 three.js 船,Russell Carpenter 的色彩,一镜到底\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮杰克，围一条藏青羊毛围巾。16 拍一个镜头不切：1.5 秒蓝图展开完，2.4 秒显影完，Clawd 在罗丝身旁张开双臂；从夕阳到夜晚是运镜中的延时，冰山在最后两秒出现，Clawd 转身报警落在最后几拍。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 一镜到底：在总布置图上推向船头；线稿立起成透视，从两人身上向外显影成夕阳；低机位贴近罗丝的逆光剪影，Clawd 在她身旁张开双臂；镜头升起、绕过左舷船头，太阳沉下，船灯亮起；越过艏楼进入夜色，罗丝从栏杆上下来；再降到 Clawd 身后，冰山从海雾里出来，Clawd 转身举臂报警。（6.7s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nJames Cameron, Titanic (1997),Russell Carpenter (cinematography),Titanic (1997) poster,Harland & Wolff general-arrangement drawings\n"},{"id":"S6-O71","name":"莎翁情史","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O71.jpg","markdown":"# O71 莎翁情史（Shakespeare in Love）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 世纪末 1990–1999\n- 长什么样：学尼古拉斯·希利亚德的伊丽莎白时代宫廷细密画：羊皮纸上的不透明水彩、群青底、贝壳金题字、白色野蔷薇。影片发生在莎士比亚的伦敦，细密画正是那个宫廷自己的肖像形式。\n- 风格：希利亚德伊丽莎白细密画\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1998\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n伊丽莎白时代宫廷细密画，学尼古拉斯·希利亚德：暖色羊皮纸上平涂不透明水彩，暗面极细排线（线宽约画面宽度的 1/1000），受光边点铅白，红褐轮廓线。配色：群青底 #203E92、贝壳金 #D6B05C（细颗粒金点加几处抛光高光）、胭脂红 #A8202C、铅白 #F6F2E8、铜绿 #346C40。招牌画面：主体画在金边椭圆挂坠里，挂坠平放在锈红色锦缎上；一丛开满白花的野蔷薇铺满主体身后和四周，上沿一圈金色罗马大写短铭文。椭圆底色始终是群青，风景和天空放进去时画成平涂色带。大场景可离开挂坠，画法不变。镜头慢推、轻微下摇，只有花、布料和手势在动。对标希利亚德《玫瑰丛中的青年》（约 1587）。避免：喷枪渐变；厚油画笔触；平黄色的金。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nElizabethan portrait miniature in Nicholas Hilliard's manner: opaque watercolour laid flat on warm vellum, very fine hatching in shadows (lines about 1/1000 of frame width), lead-white dots on lit edges, red-brown outlines. Palette: ultramarine ground #203E92, shell gold #D6B05C (granular flecks, a few burnished highlights), carmine #A8202C, lead white #F6F2E8, verdigris #346C40. Signature shot: the picture inside a gold-rimmed oval locket lying on rust-red damask; a flowering white eglantine briar fills the oval behind and around the subject; a short motto in gold Roman capitals arcs along the top. The oval's ground stays ultramarine; landscape and sky go in as flat colour bands. Wide scenes may leave the locket, same method, true perspective. Slow push, slight tilt; only flowers, cloth and gestures move. Reference: Hilliard, *Young Man Among Roses* (c. 1587). Avoid: airbrush gradients; thick oil strokes; flat yellow gold.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n宫廷细密肖像（limning）,羊皮纸上的不透明水彩,贝壳金,深红锦缎静物\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮莎士比亚：浆硬的轮状皱领、金耳环，细密画里拿羽毛笔，舞台上换成白玫瑰。四个镜头硬切，从私人的细密画一路走到公众的剧院，最后一拍女王慢慢点头，收住这一段。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 近景：深红锦缎上的金色挂坠细密画，Clawd 扮莎士比亚站在白色野蔷薇里，戴轮状皱领，羽毛笔在写字；镜头慢慢推近，略往下摇。（0.8s）\n2. 全景：玫瑰剧院。镜头从上方的彩绘天幕和看台往下摇并推近到舞台，Clawd 拿着一朵白玫瑰站在台上，女王坐在右侧包厢里。（0.6s）\n3. 中景：阳台戏，朱丽叶在上方托腮，Clawd 在下面向她举起白玫瑰；镜头略推。（0.6s）\n4. 近景：华盖下包厢里的伊丽莎白女王，从看戏转过脸来，摇着扇子，慢慢点头；镜头略推。（0.6s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nJohn Madden, 1998,Nicholas Hilliard, Young Man Among Roses (c.1587),Shakespeare in Love 1998 poster (red damask, amber light)\n"},{"id":"S6-O72","name":"美国丽人","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O72.jpg","markdown":"# O72 美国丽人（American Beauty）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 世纪末 1990–1999\n- 长什么样：两种画面剪在一起：先是干净饱和、正面对称的郊区住宅（红门、红玫瑰），再切到带 REC 界面的低清 DV 录像。对应影片里莱斯特体面的郊区和里基拍下的那段塑料袋录像。\n- 风格：郊区对称电影画面 + DV 录像\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：1999\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n两种画面。主画面约七成：干净饱和的插画，正面对称，主角居中，身后是它的家或场景；形状有两三层柔和明暗，细节密（灌木、花丛、由许多小叶簇堆成的树冠）。默认明亮日光；其他光线下也要亮（日落偏金），不发暗。红门 #C3141F 一类红色点缀，白线脚，饱和的绿，橙红秋树；暖调、轻暗角、细颗粒。过渡：卷曲的红花瓣或落叶（约画宽 1/60）越积越密，一秒风暴扫过镜头藏住剪切。副画面约三成：同一世界的 90 年代 DV 家庭录像，发软、低清、色彩渗边、马赛克块、隔行线、噪点、曝光一呼一吸、暗部偏绿，屏显 REC、电量、日期。每帧只用一种。镜头缓推。对标摄影师 Conrad L. Hall（《美国丽人》，1999）。避免：干净画面带录像瑕疵；真实品牌屏显；扁平矢量形。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nTwo registers. Primary, about 70%: a clean, saturated illustrated frame, dead-on symmetrical, the hero centred before its home or setting; every shape softly modelled in 2–3 tones, dense detail (shrubs, flowers, foliage of many small leaf clusters). Default bright daylight; under other light stay bright (gold at sunset), never dim. Red accents like a door #C3141F, white trim, saturated greens, orange-red autumn trees; warm grade, light vignette and grain. Transition: curled red petals or leaves (about 1/60 of frame width) thicken into a 1-second blizzard across the lens, hiding the cut. Second register, about 30%: the same world as 1990s DV home video: soft, low-res, chroma bleed, macroblocks, interlace, noise, breathing exposure, greenish shadows, REC/battery/date OSD. One register per frame. Slow push-ins. Reference: cinematographer Conrad L. Hall (*American Beauty*, 1999). Avoid: video artefacts in the clean part; real brands' OSD; flat vector shapes.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\nConrad Hall 式正面对称构图,玫瑰花瓣幻想,90 年代 DV 摄像机录像\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮莱斯特：白衬衫领尖、藏青底酒红斜纹领带，手里一枝红玫瑰。一个推镜头从房子推到近景，花瓣一路加密；到 1.3 秒，切到录像的那一刀藏在扫过镜头的花瓣里，幻想直接流进里基的录像带，看不出剪辑点。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 正面对称的伯纳姆家全景，镜头不切，直接推到人行道上拿着长茎红玫瑰的 Clawd；花瓣越落越密，最后成片扫过镜头。（1.3s）\n2. 里基的摄像机画面（REC、OCT. 17 1998、12:07:33）：白色塑料袋和枯叶在红砖墙前打转；镜头慢推，往右漂。没有 Clawd。（1.2s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nSam Mendes, 1999,Conrad L. Hall (cinematography),American Beauty 1999 poster (the rose)\n"},{"id":"S6-O73","name":"角斗士","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O73.jpg","markdown":"# O73 角斗士（Gladiator）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 新千年 2000–2009\n- 长什么样：整幅画用方形石子和玻璃嵌片铺成：轮廓边的石子顺着形体排，平面处一行行直排，金箔玻璃片会闪。影片本身就在罗马，所以斗兽场和极乐麦田都做成罗马地面马赛克。\n- 风格：罗马地面马赛克\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：2000\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n罗马地面马赛克。先画平涂底稿，再铺小方石子，每粒约画面宽度的 1/150：轮廓边的石子顺着边排两三圈（opus vermiculatum），其余一行行横排；人物外面包一圈深色石子轮廓。颜色只用天然石头和玻璃，沉稳偏暖：焦金 #E2B24C、赭褐 #5B4332、陶红 #8E3C2B、暖大理石 #E9DDC4，帝王紫玻璃只做一处点缀；天空和水用浅灰蓝、奶油色石头，不要饱和的色带。每粒切口略不规则，左上受光，往 1–2px 宽的深色灰缝投小影；金箔玻璃片在光源处慢慢闪。人物正面、略僵，像庞贝马赛克；背景是大片褪色的细碎石子。镜头只短推短拉，动的是细节：手势、人群、草木、光柱。对标《亚历山大马赛克》、Zliten 马赛克。避免：整齐网格变像素画；石子太大；平滑渐变；灰缝太亮。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nRoman floor mosaic: over a flat underdrawing lay square tesserae about 1/150 of frame width: two to three courses follow each outline (opus vermiculatum), flat areas in straight rows, figures outlined with one course of dark stones. Muted, warm stone and glass: burnt gold #E2B24C, umber #5B4332, terracotta #8E3C2B, warm marble #E9DDC4, with imperial-purple glass for one accent only; sky and water in pale grey-blue and cream stone, never saturated bands. Tesserae slightly irregular, lit from the upper left, shadowing into dark 1–2 px grout; gold-leaf glass glints slowly on light sources. Frontal, slightly stiff Pompeian figures before a faded field of fine stones. Short pushes and pulls; motion from details (a gesture, a crowd, plants, a light shaft). References: *Alexander Mosaic*, Zliten mosaic. Avoid: a neat grid that reads as pixel art; oversized tiles; smooth gradients; bright grout.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n顺形排列的石子（opus vermiculatum）,金箔玻璃嵌片,Zliten 角斗士马赛克\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮麦克西姆斯：一只青铜肩甲、一把罗马短剑。开场是大拇指朝下；硬切到斗兽场，观众在 Clawd 四周站起来；最后一刀离开打斗，落到极乐世界安静的麦田。三个硬切，每个一秒左右。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 近景：紫色的皇帝包厢，康茂德的大拇指从朝上转到朝下；镜头略推。（0.8s）\n2. 斗兽场海报式全景：Clawd 扮麦克西姆斯站在中央，短剑朝下，左边是拴着链子的老虎，右边是提格里斯，看台上的观众站起来；镜头略拉。（1s）\n3. 极乐世界全景：低垂的太阳下，Clawd 站在金色麦田里，地平线上是柏树和农庄；镜头慢推。（0.8s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nRidley Scott, 2000,John Mathieson (cinematography),Alexander Mosaic, Pompeii,Zliten mosaic\n"},{"id":"S6-O74","name":"美丽心灵","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O74.jpg","markdown":"# O74 美丽心灵（A Beautiful Mind）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 新千年 2000–2009\n- 长什么样：两种表面上的手写数学：哥特式图书馆窗玻璃上的白色油性笔、黑板上的粉笔，数字亮起，被金线连起来。这是片中纳什看见规律的方式：是他脑中的光，不是电脑界面。\n- 风格：窗上公式与黑板粉笔 + 金色连线\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：2001\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n手写就是画面：半透明白色蜡笔 #F1EFE6（笔画宽约画宽 1/300）在方格铅条窗玻璃上一笔笔写出来，写的是与题材有关的公式、数字或笔记，几小块字只占玻璃约四分之一。窗外是题材的世界，柔焦虚化，中间调、暖中性（阴天里的秋黄和柔和的绿），亮度不能超过字迹。暖色室内框住窗子：书架、绿罩台灯、琥珀灯光。关键时刻，选中的符号变亮，柔和的金线在它们之间连成星形或多边形，小光点沿线走。变体：深绿石板粉笔，带残影。配色：石板绿 #2F3E35、粉笔白 #F1EFE6、灯光琥珀 #E0A050、柔金 #D9B45A。镜头缓推，每次切换都离字更近。对标摄影师 Roger Deakins（《美丽心灵》，2001）。避免：HUD 或科幻界面；打字字体；字铺满玻璃；冷蓝色的窗外。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nHandwriting as the image: translucent white wax-marker strokes (about 1/300 of frame width) appear stroke by stroke on a window of square leaded panes: formulas, figures or notes about the subject, in small blocks on a quarter of the glass. Outside, the subject's world, soft-focus, mid-toned and warm (overcast gold, muted green), never brighter than the writing. A warm interior (shelves, green-shaded lamp, amber light) frames the window. At the key moment, chosen symbols brighten and soft gold lines join them into a star or polygon, lights running along. Variant: chalk on green slate with ghost writing. Palette: slate green #2F3E35, chalk white #F1EFE6, lamp amber #E0A050, soft gold #D9B45A. Camera: slow push-ins, each cut closer to the writing. Reference: cinematographer Roger Deakins (*A Beautiful Mind*, 2001). Avoid: HUD graphics; typed fonts; writing filling the glass; cold blue outside.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n玻璃上的白色油性笔,黑板粉笔,发光的金色连线,迪金斯式窗光\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮纳什：领口敞开的衬衫领、松着的针织领带，窗前拿一支油性笔。第一个镜头在写，后两个镜头是光；每次硬切都离数字更近，最后 Clawd 出画，只剩图案。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 中全景：哥特式图书馆窗前，Clawd 扮纳什站在窗座上，用白色油性笔在玻璃上写博弈论公式，窗外是秋天的方院；镜头慢推。（1s）\n2. 近景：玻璃上的字迹，窗外虚焦，左下角露出 Clawd 的笔；字符亮起，金线把它们连成一颗星；镜头略推。（0.8s）\n3. 近景：黑板上五位一组的密码数字，数字一个个亮起，金色的七角星图案自己画出来；镜头略推。没有 Clawd。（0.8s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nRon Howard, 2001,Roger Deakins (cinematography),A Beautiful Mind 2001 poster\n"},{"id":"S6-O75","name":"芝加哥","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O75.jpg","markdown":"# O75 芝加哥（Chicago）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 新千年 2000–2009\n- 长什么样：黑色舞台被硬边聚光切开：六间牢房里是勾红边的女囚剪影，背后霓虹 C，亮片和烟雾，Clawd 在白色追光里。这是电影版的「Cell Block Tango」，用海报的红黑。\n- 风格：福斯舞台：红黑聚光剪影\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：2002\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n鲍勃·福斯式百老汇歌舞舞台。黑底，但约四成画面发信号红 #D0202A：一排带栏杆的暗色格间，每格在烟雾里从背后打红光，中心最亮、向四周暗到黑，再加红幕；硬边聚光穿过烟雾。舞者是实心黑剪影，身形饱满、有服装轮廓，外面一圈 2–3px 红色边光。一个大霓虹字形（字母或图形），橙红 #FF5A3C，白热芯、微闪，要放在黑底上才读得出；黑色亮漆地板倒映它；台边一排跑马灯泡 #FFD9A0。主角站在暖白追光 #FFF1D6 里，是唯一被照亮、保留自身颜色的人。点缀：亮片、白手套。动作踩爵士拍子；灯格一间间啪地亮。镜头推进，收在追光里。对标 Rob Marshall《芝加哥》（2002）。避免：平涂匀光；多余颜色；剪影没有边光。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nBob Fosse-style Broadway stage number. Black stage, but about 40% of the frame glows signal red #D0202A: a row of dark barred cells, each backlit red through haze, bright at centre, falling off to black, plus red curtains; hard-edged spots. Dancers are full-bodied black silhouettes with costume shapes and a 2–3 px red rim. One big neon shape (letter or pictogram) in orange-red #FF5A3C, white-hot core, slight flicker, set against black so it reads; glossy black floor reflects it; marquee bulbs #FFD9A0 at the stage edge. The hero, in a warm-white follow spot #FFF1D6, is the only lit figure in its own colours. Accents: sequins, white gloves. Moves on the jazz beat; cells snap on one by one. Push in to end in the follow spot. Reference: Rob Marshall, *Chicago* (2002). Avoid: flat even fills; extra colours; rimless silhouettes.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n鲍勃·福斯的舞台调度,勾红边的逆光剪影,霓虹与亮片,2002 年海报的红黑\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 戴福斯式圆顶礼帽（帽带一圈亮片）、白手套，拿黑手杖。第一个镜头里牢房跟着拍子一间间亮；快切一个女囚；然后 Clawd 独占追光，以 jazz hands 收尾。表演按真实速度走，不加速。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 舞台全景，霓虹 C 在后面：六间牢房一间间亮起，每个女囚啪地摆出招牌姿势，Clawd 在台中央走福斯步；镜头慢推。（0.8s）\n2. 近景：一间牢房，铁栏后红光里 Velma 的剪影，扭胯、耸肩；镜头略推。没有 Clawd。（0.5s）\n3. 中近景：Clawd 独自站在白色追光里，身后是亮着的牢房：它走福斯步到脚灯前，然后放下手杖，双手 jazz hands 举起；镜头慢推。（1.2s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nRob Marshall, 2002,Dion Beebe (cinematography),Bob Fosse, Chicago (1975 stage),Chicago 2002 poster\n"},{"id":"S6-O76","name":"指环王：王者无敌","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O76.jpg","markdown":"# O76 指环王：王者无敌（The Lord of the Rings: The Return of the King）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 新千年 2000–2009\n- 长什么样：柔和的水彩加铅笔风景，空气透视很深，再加一张羊皮纸上的墨线地图。电影的概念画就是艾伦·李和约翰·豪画的，所以这段旅程按三部曲最初被画出来的样子来讲。\n- 风格：艾伦·李式水彩 + 托尔金地图\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：2003\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n奇幻概念设计式的水彩加铅笔，暖色纸。透明水彩要看得出颗粒（细碎颜料斑点，亮度起伏 ±6%），几朵回流花，水迹边缘积色更深；每条轮廓和山脊都有一道深褐铅笔线（约画面宽度的 1/800），暗面短排线。空气透视要强：3–5 层后退，越远越淡越冷。光就是主题：每场一个强光源（低太阳、火、灯、熔岩般的光）穿透雾气，余烬色光晕约占画面四分之一，约一半画面压在深褐和紫色暗部里。配色：羊皮纸 #E9DCC0、深褐 #3A2418、余烬橙 #E07A2E、炽红 #B23A1E 只用在光源核心、远景淡紫 #B8A8C8。旅程题材可插一段托尔金式地图，但不当主画面。镜头：长横摇扫过大地，缓推到主角。对标 Alan Lee、John Howe。避免：平滑的数码渐变；平涂矢量形；发白发灰的画面。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nFantasy concept-art watercolour and pencil on warm paper. Transparent washes, granulated (pigment speckle, ±6% brightness), a few blooms, darker pooled edges; every contour and ridge carries a dark-brown pencil line (about 1/800 of frame width), short hatching on shadow sides. Aerial perspective: 3–5 receding planes, each paler and cooler. Light is the subject: one strong source per scene (sun, fire, lamp, molten glow) burning through haze, its ember glow on about a quarter of the frame, about half the frame in deep umber and violet shadow. Palette: parchment #E9DCC0, deep umber #3A2418, ember orange #E07A2E, hot red #B23A1E only at the light's core, lavender #B8A8C8 for distance. Journeys may cut to a Tolkien-style map, never the main look. Camera: long lateral pans, slow push-ins to the hero. References: Alan Lee, John Howe. Avoid: smooth digital gradients; vector shapes; pale frames.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n水彩加铅笔的概念插画,托尔金式羊皮纸地图,艾伦·李与约翰·豪\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮佛罗多：灰绿色斗篷领、罗瑞恩叶子胸针、链子上的魔戒。作为这十年的重点片给了 12 拍：地图交代整条路，烽火带着镜头扫过山脉，眼睛是一拍短促的威胁，最后举起魔戒收住。镜头之间硬切，每个镜头内部是长运动。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 俯视羊皮纸地图：镜头从整张图推进夏尔，再沿红色虚线往魔多飞，Clawd 像一枚棋子那么小，沿着路线走。（1.3s）\n2. 全景：黄昏的雪山浮在云海上，近处的烽火先点着，镜头慢慢往右横移、略往后拉，山脊上的烽火一座接一座亮起。（1.9s）\n3. 近景：巴拉多塔顶的索伦之眼，镜头推近，它转过来扫出光束。没有 Clawd。（0.4s）\n4. 末日火山裂缝边：镜头从熔岩上方的玄武岩舌头全景推近到 Clawd，背后是火山和眼睛，它把发光的魔戒从深渊边提起，高高举起。（1.5s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nPeter Jackson, 2003,Andrew Lesnie (cinematography),Alan Lee,John Howe,J.R.R. Tolkien's maps of Middle-earth\n"},{"id":"S6-O77","name":"百万美元宝贝","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O77.jpg","markdown":"# O77 百万美元宝贝（Million Dollar Baby）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 新千年 2000–2009\n- 长什么样：原创的 16-bit 街机格斗游戏画面：血条、回合计时、ROUND 1 / FIGHT!、连击数、命中停顿和巨大的 K.O.。一部拳击冠军赛的电影，变成用街机语法打完的一回合。\n- 风格：16-bit 像素格斗游戏\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：2004\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n原创的 16-bit 街机格斗游戏。画在 320×180 上，用最近邻放大到输出尺寸的整数倍（1080p 用 6 倍），加轻微 CRT 扫描线（隔行压暗约 15%）。横版场地取自题材：把题材的地点压成平的台面加一层背景（比如拳台配深紫观众带 #1D1435）。界面用自绘 5×7 点阵字：两条血条（黄色 #F5C53A，掉血时拖一段红色）、计时、横幅、ROUND 1 / FIGHT!、连击数。角色是方块胖像素，高约 50 像素，一像素高光加深色边，全画面约 32 色。故事改写成主角和一个对手的较量；题材里没有敌人，就把它世界里的阻碍拟人化当对手（风暴、截止期限）。打中有火花、停顿 3–4 帧、白闪，输家被打飞，然后 K.O. 和 WINNER。固定横版视角，不剪。对标 90 年代初街机格斗游戏。避免：照搬真实游戏的角色或 logo；像素抗锯齿。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nAn original 16-bit arcade fighting game. Draw at 320×180 and nearest-neighbour upscale by a whole number (×6 for 1080p); CRT scanlines (alternate lines ~15% darker). A side-on arena from the subject's world, flattened into stage and backdrop (e.g. a ring with a dark purple crowd band #1D1435). UI in a self-drawn 5×7 pixel font: two health bars (yellow #F5C53A, red damage trail), timer, banner, ROUND 1 / FIGHT!, combo counter. Chunky sprites about 50 px tall, 1-px highlights, dark outlines, ~32 colours per frame. The story becomes a contest between the hero and a rival (if none exists, personify an obstacle: a storm, a deadline). Hits spark, freeze 3–4 frames and flash white; the loser is knocked back, then K.O. and WINNER. Fixed side-on camera, no cuts. Reference: early-1990s arcade fighters. Avoid: real games' characters or logos; anti-aliased pixels.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n16-bit 像素画,街机格斗游戏界面,CRT 扫描线\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 穿白边绿缎拳击袍、戴红拳套；对手是没有脸的深蓝色影子拳手 SHADOW。整场按时间编排：1.46 秒上勾拳命中并停顿，1.6 秒 K.O.，1.95 秒胜利。一镜到底，用游戏自己的镜头。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 一个固定的横版游戏画面：ROUND 1、FIGHT!，影子拳手挥出勾拳，Clawd 低头躲过，冲上去刺拳、刺拳，再一记上勾拳，命中停顿加白闪；对手飞出去，K.O.，Clawd 举起拳套赢下比赛。（2.5s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nClint Eastwood, 2004,Early-1990s arcade fighting games (genre grammar),Million Dollar Baby: Maggie's green robe\n"},{"id":"S6-O78","name":"撞车","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O78.jpg","markdown":"# O78 撞车（Crash）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 新千年 2000–2009\n- 长什么样：用代码画的精修夜景照片：高架上白色和红色的车灯光轨、钠灯橙对霓虹青、湿路面反光、焦外光斑，还有只在光里才看得见的雪。《撞车》的结尾正是洛杉矶难得的一场雪。\n- 风格：洛杉矶夜景光轨 + 飘雪\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：2005\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n长曝光夜景街拍。钠灯暖橙 #F2A24A 铺满建筑、招牌和湿地面（约一半画面在暖光里），对着青色霓虹 #3FD0D8，偶有红蓝警灯；黑色略偏青，不能发灰。构图分层：前景人行道有人，中景亮着的店面招牌，上三分之一横过道路或桥（不在城市就用主体所在地一条有路灯的路）。车灯沿这些路拉成细而连续的光带（约画宽 1/150），白 #FFF4E0 与红 #E0303A，带光晕和发烫的芯。雪分三层，短而柔的拖影，只在光锥里可见。收尾：两级泛光、蓝色横向光条、边缘焦外光斑、暗角、动态颗粒。主角清楚地站在画面中部一盏灯的光锥里，只有车流拖影。镜头：一个连续运镜，俯仰、横摇加转焦。对标摄影师 J. Michael Muro（《撞车》，2004）。避免：雪到处一样亮；大片空黑；光带断成点。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nLong-exposure night street photography. Sodium-vapour orange #F2A24A washes structures, signs and wet ground (about half the frame in warm light), against cyan neon #3FD0D8 and the odd red-blue emergency light; blacks cyan-tinged, never grey. Layered composition: foreground pavement with figures, lit shopfronts and signs mid-frame, roads or bridges crossing the upper third (away from cities, a lamp-lit road in the subject's own setting). Headlights become thin continuous ribbons (about 1/150 of frame width) along those roads, white #FFF4E0 and red #E0303A, glowing with hot cores. Snow: three layers of short soft streaks, seen only in light cones. Finish: two-level bloom, blue lens streaks, edge bokeh, vignette, moving grain. The hero stands sharp in a lamp cone mid-frame; only traffic streaks. Camera: one continuous tilt, pan and rack focus. Reference: cinematographer J. Michael Muro (*Crash*, 2004). Avoid: evenly lit snow; empty black frames; dotted trails.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n长曝光夜景摄影,钠灯橙配霓虹青,变形宽银幕光条与胶片颗粒\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 围一条手织的深青色围巾，站在路灯下仰头看雪。一个连续运镜从光轨到车祸再到 Clawd，快速移动的几段带运动模糊。镜头到它那里时雪变大，接雪的动作落在快结束的地方。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 一镜到底：开场仰拍立交桥上的光轨，往下摇到警戒带后的追尾现场，焦点在事故上；再沿着路缘往右摇到钠灯下的 Clawd，焦点拉过来；Clawd 抬起手臂接一片雪，眨了下眼。（2.5s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nPaul Haggis, 2004,J. Michael Muro (cinematography),Crash 2005 poster,Four Level Interchange long-exposure photography\n"},{"id":"S6-O79","name":"无间行者","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O79.jpg","markdown":"# O79 无间行者（The Departed）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 新千年 2000–2009\n- 长什么样：平涂的多层喷漆模板，有喷雾毛边和流挂，漆吃进砖缝，旁边一张撕破的糊贴海报。《无间行者》最后一个镜头是老鼠爬过州议会金顶下的栏杆，街头模板涂鸦也一直爱画老鼠。\n- 风格：波士顿红砖墙模板涂鸦\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：2006\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n老红砖墙上的街头模板涂鸦。每件作品用 3–5 块镂空模板喷成平色块，有照片感（最小镂空约画宽 1/200）：硬切口，模板翘起处有柔喷雾，散落喷点，流挂，以及模板必须留的连接筋。配色：黑 #151312、中灰 #8C8A85、金 #C99A2E、红 #C3261F；红砖 #7A3224，主要作品喷在刷上去的奶油色补丁上。砖面凹凸、灰缝阴影和雨痕透过漆显出来，让漆吃进砖缝。一面墙并排放好几件作品，加关于题材的手喷短标语（用观众的语言）、旧 tag 和一张撕角的半调糊贴海报。光：柔和的白天光斜扫过墙，反差适中，电线影子轻轻晃；不加重暗角。镜头：先让作品一层层喷出来，再切近景、横移。对标班克斯。避免：边缘干净像矢量；漆浮在砖上；颜色太多；墙面昏暗。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nStreet stencil graffiti on an old red-brick wall. Each piece is 3–5 stencils sprayed as flat colour, photo-derived in look (smallest cut detail about 1/200 of frame width): hard cut edges, soft overspray where a stencil lifted, spray dots, drips, stencil bridges. Palette: black #151312, mid grey #8C8A85, gold #C99A2E, red #C3261F; brick #7A3224, cream painted patches under the main pieces. Brick relief, mortar and rain streaks show through, so paint sinks into the joints. A busy wall: several pieces side by side, short hand-sprayed slogans about the subject (user's language), old tags, a torn halftone paste-up. Light: soft raking daylight, mild contrast, a faint swaying wire shadow; no heavy vignette. Camera: pieces spray on layer by layer, then close-ups and lateral tracks. Reference: Banksy. Avoid: clean vector edges; paint floating on the brick; too many colours; a dark wall.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n多层喷漆模板,班克斯的老鼠,红砖墙肌理,半调糊贴海报\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮卧底警察，耳边贴着翻盖手机，本身也是墙上喷出来的一层。前两拍作品一层层喷出来，之后手机、老鼠、Clawd 挂电话，快切接连出现。没人说话，靠铃声和老鼠讲完。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 全景：整面墙，模板画一层层喷出来：城市、金顶、栏杆、老鼠、拿手机的 Clawd、红色标语；镜头略推。（0.8s）\n2. 近景：两部喷漆翻盖手机隔空响铃，Clawd 耳边也贴着手机；它听着，眼睛往左瞟；镜头慢慢往右漂。（0.6s）\n3. 中景：老鼠沿着金顶下的栏杆跑过，左上角喷着「WHO'S THE RAT?」，警灯的红蓝光扫过砖墙；镜头跟着往右横移。（0.6s）\n4. 近景：Clawd 把手机往下一放，眼睛看向右边；镜头略推。（0.4s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nMartin Scorsese, 2006 (the final shot),Michael Ballhaus (cinematography),The Departed 2006 poster,Banksy\n"},{"id":"S6-O80","name":"老无所依","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O80.jpg","markdown":"# O80 老无所依（No Country for Old Men）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 新千年 2000–2009\n- 长什么样：实时 3D 的西德州黄昏荒原：太阳压在地平线上，长影子朝着镜头，低饱和的土黄和灰绿，细颗粒。照着迪金斯为科恩兄弟拍的光，和海报上逆光里提着箱子的人。\n- 风格：3D 西德州荒原黄昏（迪金斯光）\n- 关键词：3D\n- 年份：2007\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n偏写实的 3D 黄昏旷野（three.js）。天空很大，约占画面六成，地平线处尘土赭色 #D8A060，往上渐到淡奶油色，几缕细长云被下方照亮。太阳高出地平线 1–2°，仍能投影，光沿远处坡脊烧成一线亮边。低饱和：成百丛灰绿小灌木退向地平线，碎石和干草散在暗暖色泥土 #2A2622 上（默认干旱荒地，换别的地貌也保留稀疏成丛的植被）。主角逆光站在中景，约占画面高度三分之一，长影子朝镜头拉来。按天空曝光：太阳可以过曝，光晕不超过画面十分之一。调色：中性色调映射、暖高光、细颗粒、暗角。招牌镜头：基本固定，低机位广角（约 35mm）。对标摄影师 Roger Deakins（《老无所依》，2007）。避免：饱和的橙色晚霞；大片发白眩光；光秃的地面；忙乱的运镜。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nPhotoreal-leaning 3D landscape at dusk. Sky fills 60% of the frame, dusty ochre #D8A060 at the horizon up to pale cream, thin streaked clouds lit from below. Sun 1–2° above the horizon, still casting shadows; its light burns along the far ridge. Low saturation: hundreds of grey-green scrub clumps receding to the horizon, stones, dry grass, dark warm earth #2A2622 (default arid; elsewhere keep sparse clumped growth). The hero stands backlit in the mid-ground, about a third of frame height, long shadow toward the lens. Expose for the sky: the sun may clip, its glow within a tenth of the frame. Grade: neutral tone mapping, warm highlights, fine grain, vignette. Signature shot: locked, low, wide (~35 mm). Reference: cinematographer Roger Deakins (*No Country for Old Men*, 2007). Avoid: saturated orange sunsets; blown-out glare; bare ground; busy camera moves.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n实时 3D（three.js）,罗杰·迪金斯的黄昏光,低饱和胶片调色\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮勒维林：浅褐色毡质牛仔帽、一只皮质文件箱。三个固定机位硬切：日落时提箱走路，中间是「猜吧」的硬币，最后夜里车灯压过来，危险到了。光线把时间从黄昏带到天黑。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 低机位逆光全景：Clawd 扮勒维林，戴牛仔帽、提着钱箱走过小坡，太阳压在它左侧的地平线上，身后是被打烂的皮卡；固定机位。（0.8s）\n2. 微距：加油站柜台上转动的 25 美分硬币，背后是虚焦的奇古尔的腰带和手；硬币越晃越快，倒下；固定机位。没有 Clawd。（0.8s）\n3. 夜，低机位贴着空公路：Clawd 站在路肩上，一辆皮卡的车灯从坡顶压过来；固定机位。（0.8s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nJoel and Ethan Coen, 2007,Roger Deakins (cinematography),No Country for Old Men 2007 poster\n"},{"id":"S6-O81","name":"贫民窟的百万富翁","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O81.jpg","markdown":"# O81 贫民窟的百万富翁（Slumdog Millionaire）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 新千年 2000–2009\n- 长什么样：蓝紫色加金色高光的电视演播室，摇头灯光束、镜头光斑；然后切到饱和橙青色的孟买屋顶。影片本身就在答题节目的最后一题和贾马尔出身的贫民窟之间来回切。\n- 风格：答题节目演播室 + 孟买橙青\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：2008\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n两种画面。主画面约占七成：光鲜的插画式答题演播室，明亮饱满，略俯的四分之三视角：底光玻璃圆台和一圈圈蓝光环，背后坐满观众，摇头灯在烟雾里打出蓝紫光束 #5A3FD0，大屏上长六角形答案框，一个锁成琥珀色 #F0A020；加泛光和横向光斑；越紧张四周越暗，最多暗到一半，不全黑。人物圆润干净，柔和明暗。第二种画面约占三成，整屏硬切：主题所在地方的黄金时刻景象，饱和橙青，密而热闹（比如铁皮屋顶、晾衣绳、人群）。点缀：洋红 #E2206B、青 #0F7C7A、橘黄 #F06A14。镜头推向大屏和座位，光束收到一人身上；其余慢摇。对标摄影师 Anthony Dod Mantle（《贫民窟的百万富翁》，2008）。避免：真实节目 logo；大片漆黑的演播室；平均打光；灰暗空荡的场景。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nTwo registers. Primary, about 70%: a glossy illustrated quiz-show studio, bright and full, seen three-quarter from slightly above: a glass stage lit from below with concentric blue rings, a packed audience behind, moving-head beams cutting blue-violet #5A3FD0 through haze, a big screen of elongated-hexagon answers, one locked in amber #F0A020; bloom and horizontal flares; as tension rises the edges dim to about half, never black. Characters rounded, softly shaded. Second register, about 30%, as full-frame hard cuts: golden-hour views of the subject's home ground, saturated orange and teal, busy (e.g. tin roofs, washing lines, crowds). Accents: magenta #E2206B, teal #0F7C7A, tangerine #F06A14. Camera: push in on screen and hot seat as beams close on one person; elsewhere slow pans. Reference: cinematographer Anthony Dod Mantle (*Slumdog Millionaire*, 2008). Avoid: real show logos; a mostly black studio; even lighting; grey, empty places.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n电视答题节目的灯光,蓝紫光束与镜头光斑,Anthony Dod Mantle 的橙青色调\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮贾马尔：浅蓝细格衬衫领加一只领夹麦。答案从第一帧就锁定；第二个镜头把全场压暗、收到热座上，答案变绿时放开；然后硬切到孟买屋顶，画面一下打开到整座城市。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 近景：大屏上的最后一题，四个答案框，A: ARAMIS 锁定成琥珀色；镜头略推。没有 Clawd。（0.8s）\n2. 舞台全景：主持人坐在凳子上，Clawd 扮贾马尔坐在热座上；全场暗下来，光束收拢到 Clawd 身上，接着答案闪成绿色，观众欢呼；镜头慢推。（1s）\n3. 全景：黄金时刻的达拉维屋顶，Clawd 站在铁皮屋顶上，身后高架上一列挤满人的火车开过；镜头慢慢往右摇。（0.8s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nDanny Boyle, 2008,Anthony Dod Mantle (cinematography),Slumdog Millionaire 2008 poster\n"},{"id":"S6-O82","name":"拆弹部队","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O82.jpg","markdown":"# O82 拆弹部队（The Hurt Locker）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 新千年 2000–2009\n- 长什么样：一页美军拆弹手册：均匀黑线、排线阴影、点描沙土、带圈编号和引线、IED 分解图，只有一点橄榄绿和沙色。《拆弹部队》讲高压下的程序，手册图就是画出来的程序。\n- 风格：拆弹教范线图\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：2009\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n军用技术手册插图。暖米白纸 #EFEADB 上一层很淡的工程网格，统一黑墨 #1B1A16（轮廓线宽约画面宽度的 1/700，排线更细），明暗只靠排线，地面用点刻，只在两三成的画面上平涂橄榄绿 #6C6D42 和沙色 #D9C79C；灯光画成放射线，不画光晕。主图：题材场景的一点透视画面，占页面大半，里面有人物和器物；四周是题材关键器物沿点划线轴展开的爆炸图、引线上的带圈数字、零件表、WARNING 和 CAUTION 框、打字机字体的标注（用观众的语言）和 FIG 编号，一块黑底章节卡。动画一拍二：零件滑开、工具动作、人物走路。镜头先看整页，再切到放大插图式的细节。对标美军技术手册，Kathryn Bigelow《拆弹部队》（2008）。避免：渐变明暗；颜色太多；花哨字体；空荡的页面。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nMilitary technical-manual illustration. Warm off-white paper #EFEADB with a faint engineering grid, uniform black ink #1B1A16 (outlines ~1/700 of frame width, hatching thinner), shading only by hatching, ground by stippling, flat tints of olive #6C6D42 and sand #D9C79C on 20–30% of the drawing; lights as ray lines, not glows. Main figure: a one-point-perspective view of the subject's setting filling most of the page; around it exploded views of the subject's key object along a dash-dot axis, circled numbers on leader lines, a parts table, WARNING and CAUTION boxes, typewriter labels (user's language) and FIG numbers, a black chapter card. Animate on twos: parts slide apart, a tool works, a figure walks. Camera: whole page, then cuts to enlarged insets. References: US Army technical manuals; Kathryn Bigelow, *The Hurt Locker* (2008). Avoid: gradient shading; too much colour; decorative fonts; sparse pages.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n军用技术手册线图,爆炸分解图,打字机字标注,橄榄绿与沙色平涂\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 戴橄榄绿拆弹头盔、面罩掀起，手拿剪线钳，站在六枚半埋的 155mm 炮弹中间。四个镜头从整页一路收紧到剪断：漫长的走路、面罩里的脸、Clawd 的眼睛、剪线。咔的一声落在这段快结束的地方。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 整页手册，FIG 4-6「THE LONG WALK」：穿拆弹服的士兵沿巴格达土路朝镜头走来，Clawd 站在串起来的炮弹中间拿着剪线钳；镜头慢慢推向街道。（0.8s）\n2. 近景：穿拆弹服的士兵在走，面罩里是一张皱着眉的脸；镜头慢推。（0.6s）\n3. 近景：戴拆弹头盔、面罩掀起的 Clawd，眼睛往下看向起爆线，剪线钳举起来；镜头略推。（0.5s）\n4. 特写：剪线钳咬住炮弹上方的双绞起爆线，一捏、咔，线断开；镜头推近。（0.6s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nKathryn Bigelow, 2008,Barry Ackroyd (cinematography),The Hurt Locker 2008 poster,US Army EOD technical manuals\n"},{"id":"S6-O83","name":"国王的演讲","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O83.jpg","markdown":"# O83 国王的演讲（The King's Speech）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 独立年代 2010–2019\n- 长什么样：墙面肌理很重的油画质感室内：罗格诊室那面冷灰绿的多层斑驳墙，再到琥珀金的软包广播间、弹簧吊着的话筒和细细的金色声波。照着 Eve Stewart 设计的墙和海报的金黄。\n- 风格：斑驳墙 + 金色广播间\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：2010\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n墙面就是画面的油画质感室内（户外题材搬进相关的有窗屋子）。主调：菱形钉扣软包墙，金 #C9A050 压在深褐 #3A2610 上，扣子带黄铜高光，声源（比如吊着的话筒）后面一团暖金光。副调用于安静的场景：冷灰绿墙面，局部磨穿露出蓝、粉、赭和灰泥，有潮线和细裂纹；剥落块不规则，最多占墙面三分之一。形状边缘干净，只罩一层淡淡的笔触纹理（笔触约画宽 1/120，亮度起伏 ±5%）；脸和小物件画清楚。声音画成 4–8 条细金色示波线，扩过墙面，先带尖刺，后来平滑。人物偏到角落，留大片墙。镜头慢推，插一个声源特写。对标摄影师 Danny Cohen、美术指导 Eve Stewart（《国王的演讲》，2010）。避免：居中对称；粗糙点戳笔触；地图般的剥落；霓虹般亮的声波。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nPainterly interiors where the wall carries the image (outdoor subjects move into a related room with a window). Primary: a diamond-tufted padded wall in gold #C9A050 over deep brown #3A2610, brass button highlights, a warm glow behind the sound source (e.g. a hanging microphone). Secondary, for quiet scenes: a cool grey-green wall worn through to blue, pink, ochre and plaster, with damp lines and fine cracks; patches irregular, under a third of the wall. Clean-edged shapes under a subtle brush texture (strokes ~1/120 of frame width, ±5% brightness); faces, small objects crisp. Sound: 4–8 thin gold oscilloscope lines crossing the wall, spiky, later smooth. Figures in a corner, lots of wall. Camera: slow push-ins, one close-up of the source. References: cinematographer Danny Cohen, designer Eve Stewart (*The King's Speech*, 2010). Avoid: centred symmetry; coarse dabbed strokes; map-like peel; neon-bright waves.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n数字油画笔触,层层剥落的漆墙,30 年代广播间,金色声波细线\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮乔治六世：小王冠、翼领和深色领带，诊室里夹着烟，广播间里拿着打字稿。三个镜头硬切讲完治疗：缩在角落，声波从口吃变流畅，然后罗格打着拍子，演讲播出去。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 全景：罗格的诊室，Clawd 缩在旧皮沙发左端夹着烟，上方是大片斑驳墙，右边一台喇叭留声机；镜头慢推。（0.8s）\n2. 大特写：弹簧吊环里的大理石话筒，左边是 Clawd 的王冠和眼睛；金色声波先是断断续续（口吃），然后变得流畅；镜头慢推。（0.9s）\n3. 中全景：1939 年的广播间，Clawd 拿着讲稿在左，话筒居中，罗格在右边用手打拍子，ON AIR 灯亮起；镜头略拉。（0.9s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nTom Hooper, 2010,Danny Cohen (cinematography),Eve Stewart (production design),The King's Speech 2010 poster\n"},{"id":"S6-O84","name":"艺术家","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O84.jpg","markdown":"# O84 艺术家（The Artist）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 独立年代 2010–2019\n- 长什么样：干净发亮的黑白：高光明亮、灰阶柔和，装饰艺术边框的字幕卡，轻微闪烁和颗粒；Clawd 是唯一的颜色。《艺术家》是 2011 年写给默片好莱坞的情书，所以比第 1 部更精致。\n- 风格：2011 精致默片黑白\n- 关键词：2D·B&W\n- 年份：2011\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n致敬 20 年代末默片的现代黑白片。影调干净：发光的高光、柔和的中灰、深黑；温和的 S 曲线，灯周围有灰色光晕，轻微闪烁（±3%），细颗粒，偶有灰尘，16:9 里加 1.33 的左右黑边。字幕卡是黑底、装饰艺术风边框、字距拉开的大字，用观众的语言。场景：把题材的世界布置成宏大规整的室内或舞台布景（比如带铁艺栏杆的楼梯、金色台口、逆光剪影的人群），人物多取优雅侧面。故事围绕的那个主角是画面里唯一有颜色的东西，整个都用陶土橙 #D97757 的深浅来画；灯和火都保持灰色。镜头：平滑的推拉，字幕卡闪入，剪辑落在动作上（鞠躬、挥手）。对标摄影师 Guillaume Schiffman（《艺术家》，2011）。避免：重划痕；棕褐色调；颜色渗到灰色里。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nA modern homage to late-1920s silent film in black and white. Clean tones: glowing highlights, soft mid-greys, deep blacks; a gentle S-curve, grey halation around lights, slight flicker (±3%), fine grain, rare dust, a 1.33 pillarbox inside 16:9. Intertitles on black with an Art Deco border and spaced capitals, in the user's language. Settings: the subject's world staged as grand, ordered interiors or stage sets (e.g. a wrought-iron staircase, a gilt proscenium, a crowd in backlit silhouette); figures in elegant profile. The one hero subject the story centres on is the only coloured thing, wholly in tones of terracotta #D97757; lamps and fire stay grey. Camera: smooth push-ins and pull-backs, title cards flashed in, cuts landing on gestures (a bow, a wave). Reference: cinematographer Guillaume Schiffman (*The Artist*, 2011). Avoid: heavy scratches; sepia tint; colour bleeding into the greys.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n20 年代末默片黑白,装饰艺术边框字幕卡,好莱坞片厂的魅力布光\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮乔治·瓦伦丁：白色翼领、黑领结、丝质高礼帽、手杖，还有杰克罗素梗 Uggie。五个快切，接近真实速度：楼梯上举帽，BANG! 字幕卡一闪，小狗倒地，最后观众起立时鞠躬。Clawd 是唯一的颜色。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 楼梯全景往里推：Clawd 扮乔治站在平台上，向上楼的佩皮举起高礼帽致意，群演上下交错，Uggie 在它身边。（1s）\n2. 近景：Clawd 举起的手摘下帽子，Uggie 仰头看它；镜头略推。（0.5s）\n3. 全屏字幕卡：装饰艺术边框里写着 BANG!。（0.3s）\n4. 舞台上，Clawd 用手指比枪，Uggie 往后一倒装死；镜头慢推。（0.5s）\n5. 首映礼全景：银幕上是 THE END，右边 Constance 在鼓掌，观众剪影站起来，Clawd 举帽拄杖鞠躬，Uggie 在脚边；镜头略拉，大幕开始合上。（0.9s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nMichel Hazanavicius, 2011,Guillaume Schiffman (cinematography),The Artist 2011 poster,Bradbury Building, Los Angeles\n"},{"id":"S6-O85","name":"逃离德黑兰","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O85.jpg","markdown":"# O85 逃离德黑兰（Argo）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 独立年代 2010–2019\n- 长什么样：摊在制片桌上的 70 年代柯比漫画：粗墨线、柯比能量泡、泛黄新闻纸上的四色网点。片中那部假科幻片用的正是柯比画的概念图，所以漫画从假史诗一路翻到真实的跑道出逃。\n- 风格：杰克·柯比 70 年代漫画页\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：2012\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n70 年代杰克·柯比漫画页。粗细变化的毛笔墨线，大块涂黑，透视夸张，黑色柯比能量泡，方块拟声字，手写对白框。CMYK 平涂用 45° 网点印出：青 #1696CD、品红 #DE2C70、黄 #FFD616、黑 #1C161A，泛黄新闻纸 #EEE2C2，色版错位 1 像素，纸有纤维和中缝阴影。分格叙事，镜头在格间推进、横移，推近时网点放大成波普圆点。避免：顺滑数码渐变、雪白的纸、僵硬的管状机械。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\n1970s Jack Kirby comic page. Heavy tapered brush contours, feathered spot blacks, exaggerated perspective, Kirby Krackle energy bubbles, block sound effects, hand-lettered balloons and yellow captions. CMYK flats printed as 45° Ben-Day dots: cyan #1696CD, magenta #DE2C70, yellow #FFD616, black #1C161A on aged newsprint #EEE2C2, colour plates 1 px off register. Paper grain, browned edges, a gutter fold shadow, warm lamp falloff. Lay the story out in panels and move between them: push into the splash, pan along a strip. Dots stay round and grow into pop-art dots on push-ins. Avoid: smooth digital gradients, clean white paper, stiff tube-like machines.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n杰克·柯比宇宙漫画,Ben-Day 四色网点印刷,泛黄新闻纸,《光之王》概念图\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮漫画里的太空英雄：红里子高领、蓝披风、金护腕，手拿柯比式光枪。第一拍内光束开火，然后两刀硬切把书页从假史诗翻到真实出逃，最后 Clawd 卸下戏服，成了舷窗后的一名乘客。2.5 秒三格，像在读漫画。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 宇宙跨页大格的中景，缓慢推进：披风太空英雄 Clawd 站在岩架上开光枪，光束打爆机器人的反应堆，「KRAKA-THOOM」。（0.9s）\n2. 硬切到红色胶片条那一格：航站楼里的几位人质和旁白「THE MOVIE WAS FAKE. THE MISSION WAS REAL.」，镜头轻微右移。（0.8s）\n3. 硬切到钢蓝色的夜间跑道格，从左往右横移，掠过追来的卡车和吉普，停在离地的客机；角上的舷窗小格里 Clawd 坐在窗后回望。（0.8s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nBen Affleck, Argo (2012),Jack Kirby, Lord of Light concept art (1979),Jack Kirby, New Gods / Eternals (1970s),Rodrigo Prieto (cinematography)\n"},{"id":"S6-O86","name":"为奴十二年","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O86.jpg","markdown":"# O86 为奴十二年（12 Years a Slave）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 独立年代 2010–2019\n- 长什么样：铜框丝绒盒里的 1850 年代银版照片，从空白显影：椅子上靠着小提琴，Clawd 低头站在旁边。这是所罗门·诺瑟普那个年代的照相术，只用一件物件讲这部片，不画人。\n- 风格：显影中的银版老照片\n- 关键词：2D·B&W\n- 年份：2013\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n1850 年代盒装银版照片在显影。招牌镜头：银版宽度占画面约 85%，外圈是带珠串的黄铜衬框 #8a6a2c、圆角矩形开口，红丝绒和皮盒只露在边上。银版是灰阶银色，从近黑 #1c1a18 到浅银 #ecebe6；蓝紫到琥珀的氧化晕只在开口边缘窄窄一圈。抛光旋纹、发丝划痕和斑点都要轻（亮度 ±4%）。左上方柔和窗光，主体静止摆姿，背后素色布景，户外地点画在布景上。一镜到底：前 1 秒左右镜头从打开的盒子推到招牌景别，然后停住；影像从空白的镜面灰开始，暗部先出，显影前沿从左扫到右，约 3 秒显完。只有故事围绕的那个主角手工上色：一层半透明平涂（如 #D97757），透出底下的明暗；灯保持灰色。避免：棕褐色、银版太小、快切。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nA cased 1850s daguerreotype, developing. Signature shot: the silver plate fills about 85% of the frame width inside a beaded brass mat #8a6a2c with a rounded-rectangle opening; velvet and leather only at the edges. Greyscale silver, near-black #1c1a18 to pale #ecebe6; a thin blue-violet to amber tarnish halo round the opening. Light swirls, hairline scratches, specks (±4% brightness). Soft window light from upper left; the subject poses still before a plain backdrop (an outdoor place is painted on it). One shot: the camera pushes from the open case to this framing in ~1 s, then holds; the image develops from blank mirror-grey, shadows first, a front sweeping left to right, done by ~3 s. Only the story's one hero is hand-tinted: a translucent flat wash (e.g. #D97757), shading showing through; lamps stay grey. Avoid: sepia, a small plate, fast cuts.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n1850 年代带盒银版照片,手工上色锡版照片,影楼静物\n\n### 导演说明\n\n没有戏服，也不画人：Clawd 站在所罗门的小提琴旁，慢慢低头。整段只有三种动：影像显影、一次慢推、低头，2.5 秒一刀不剪。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 一镜到底：开场是桌上整只照相盒，银版一片空白；画面从一侧显影出来（小提琴靠在梯背椅上，Clawd 站在一旁），镜头慢慢推进直到银版铺满画面，Clawd 低下头。（2.5s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nSteve McQueen, 12 Years a Slave (2013),1850s cased daguerreotypes and ambrotypes,Solomon Northup, Twelve Years a Slave (1853)\n"},{"id":"S6-O87","name":"鸟人","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O87.jpg","markdown":"# O87 鸟人（Birdman）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 独立年代 2010–2019\n- 长什么样：细致的 3D 百老汇剧院后台，用手持长镜头拍：暖钨丝灯对冷日光灯，最后冲进时代广场的强光。《鸟人》最出名的就是假装一镜到底，所以这段的风格就是那条运镜。\n- 风格：3D 后台走廊长镜头\n- 关键词：3D\n- 年份：2014\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n实时 3D，仿摄影师埃马努埃尔·卢贝兹基（《鸟人》，2014），一条不断的手持长镜头。主体在窄而旧的通道里（默认后台走廊；户外题材放进最近的室内）：下三分之一是掉漆的血红搪瓷墙裙 #6e2a22，上面是带水渍的烟黄石膏墙 #cdb98e，头顶水管和桥架，墙上有门和告示。光只来自画内灯具，每两三米一盏：铁笼钨丝灯（暖）、一根在闪的日光灯（冷）、一盏红色出口灯 #ff2a1a；远处门外发亮。中等曝光：灯旁墙亮，灯间不低于中灰，地面看得清。广角 18–24 毫米，低频晃动约 ±1°，柔和环境光遮蔽、轻辉光、细颗粒（±3%）。运镜 20–40 秒，次序不限：倒退领路，甩向一侧的门洞，让主体擦过镜头，再转身跟上。避免：锁死机位、空白墙面、重复纹理、压得太黑。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nReal-time 3D, one unbroken handheld long take, after cinematographer Emmanuel Lubezki (*Birdman*, 2014). A narrow, lived-in passage (default a backstage corridor; an outdoor subject gets its nearest interior, e.g. a stairwell): chipped blood-red enamel dado #6e2a22 on the lower third, stained nicotine plaster #cdb98e above, pipes, cable trays, doors, pinned notices. Light only from practicals every 2–3 m: warm caged tungsten bulbs, one cold flickering fluorescent tube, a red exit sign #ff2a1a; the far door glows. Mid-key: plaster light near lamps, never below mid-grey between them; floor visible. Wide lens (18–24 mm), slow sway (±1°), soft ambient occlusion, light bloom, fine grain (±3%). Over 20–40 s, in any order: lead the subject walking backwards, whip aside to a doorway, let it brush past the lens, turn and follow. Avoid: a locked-off camera, blank walls, repeated textures, a crushed dark frame.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n实时 3D（three.js）,卢贝兹基手持长镜头,钨丝灯与日光灯,数字电影细颗粒\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮里根，戴一顶石板蓝的鸟人头盔，金色鸟喙、两侧羽翼。走廊是一条很快的手持运镜，鼓手的摇摆鼓点就在画面里；推开后门那一下卡在拍点上，切到时代广场，Clawd 停下抬头看。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 手持中景，镜头在戴鸟人头盔的 Clawd 前面倒退着走，向左甩到小房间里打爵士鼓的鼓手，再甩回来，Clawd 走过鼓组、擦着镜头过去。（1.3s）\n2. 切到后门，从 Clawd 背后拍，头顶是红色 EXIT 灯；镜头轻轻侧移，Clawd 一把推开门，门外一片白光。（0.7s）\n3. 切到夜里的时代广场：全景，Clawd 站在人行道上，身后是成片的 LED 屏；它迈一步、抬头看屏幕，手持镜头轻轻漂移。（0.5s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nAlejandro G. Iñárritu, Birdman (2014),Emmanuel Lubezki (cinematography),Kevin Thompson (production design),Antonio Sánchez (drum score)\n"},{"id":"S6-O88","name":"聚焦","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O88.jpg","markdown":"# O88 聚焦（Spotlight）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 独立年代 2010–2019\n- 长什么样：印成报纸网点的画面，放到缩微胶片阅读机的灰绿屏幕上：扫描线、滚动的扫描光带、玻璃上的灰尘。片中记者就在这种档案里挖出真相，换镜头也做成胶片横移。\n- 风格：缩微胶片阅读机 · 报纸网点\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：2015\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n2001 年报纸彩色网点，放在图书馆缩微胶片阅读机上看。招牌镜头：图片本身铺满画面，不要报头和文字栏；图里的线条和字（招牌、标签）保持实线。四色网点角度 15°、75°、0°、45°，网点约为画面宽度的 1/250，套色错 1 像素。整体转成低反差荧光绿单色，网点依然清楚可见：片基 #1b2320，帧间隙 #0c110f，高光不亮过 #a0dcaf。只有故事的主角整个人保留原色（如 #D97757），照样带网点，反差一样低；光源一律变绿。每 3 像素一条扫描线，每 4 秒滚过一道淡扫描带，一块柔和灯斑，玻璃上有灰和一根头发，圆角遮光罩只轻压外圈约 5%，一行小等宽胶片编号。换场把胶片横向滑过暗色帧间隙（0.5 秒）；镜头内慢推。避免：报纸版面、颜色外漏、硬切。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nA 2001 newspaper colour halftone on a library microfiche reader. Signature shot: the picture fills the frame, no headline or columns; lines and lettering inside it stay solid. CMYK screens at 15°, 75°, 0°, 45°, dots about 1/250 of frame width, 1 px misregistration. Convert to low-contrast phosphor-green monochrome, dots still clearly visible: film base #1b2320, frame gaps #0c110f, highlights no brighter than #a0dcaf. Only the story's one hero keeps its colour, whole figure (e.g. #D97757), screened and equally low-contrast; every light source turns green. Scan lines every 3 px, a faint scan bar every 4 s, a lamp hot-spot, dust, a hair, a light rounded hood vignette (outer ~5%), a small monospace fiche index. Scene changes slide the fiche sideways past a dark gap (0.5 s); slow push-ins. Avoid: newspaper layout, colour leaking, hard cuts.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n图书馆缩微胶片阅读机,2001 年报纸 CMYK 网点,磷光绿单色\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮 Spotlight 小组的记者：挂绳工作证、速记本，后面换成荧光笔。三帧缩微胶片，衔接不用硬切，而是阅读机里胶片的横向滑动，最后一拍落在头版上。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 地下编辑部全景：记者围着堆满纸的桌子，头顶是荧光灯格栅，Clawd 站在前面，挂着工作证、拿着速记本；镜头慢慢拉开。（0.8s）\n2. 胶片横向滑到下一帧：俯拍打印的神父名单，玩具大小的 Clawd 推着一支大荧光笔一行行往下划，镜头跟着往下移。（0.8s）\n3. 再滑到印刷厂码头：中近景，Clawd 站在头版旁，标题是「Church allowed abuse by priest for years」，镜头慢推。（0.9s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nTom McCarthy, Spotlight (2015),Masanobu Takayanagi (cinematography),The Boston Globe front page, 6 January 2002,Library microfiche readers\n"},{"id":"S6-O89","name":"月光男孩","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O89.jpg","markdown":"# O89 月光男孩（Moonlight）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 独立年代 2010–2019\n- 长什么样：饱满的青绿、品红和月光蓝油画肖像，雕出来的头像再用顺着形体的笔触重新画一遍。直接照着片名那句「月光下，黑人男孩看起来是蓝色的」来画。\n- 风格：月光蓝调油画肖像\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：2016\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n夜色油画肖像。平滑实心体积上，顺着形体用两遍短笔触重画，笔触反差小（明度 ±8%）：海、天、衣服用宽笔，约为画面宽度的 1/60；脸用细笔 1/150，保持平滑。按用途分色：天、海、远处陆地用中等明度的蓝紫 #32377a，约占画面六成；受月光的面和沙地提亮成淡薰衣草蓝；青绿 #128a91 用在水里和受光一侧；轮廓光用品红 #7a2f86；最深暗部 #07071c。任何肤色顶面都受蓝光、带湿亮高光，眼睛睁开有光。默认夜晚，唯一主光是满月，水面铺出碎光；品红只是远处光源的柔和轮廓光；其他小光源只是一小点青白光。颗粒 ±3%。招牌镜头：人物平静，约占画高三分之二。慢推。参照巴里·詹金斯《月光男孩》（2016）。避免：粗块笔触、石膏像小黑眼、发闷的近黑画面。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nNight oil portraiture: smooth volumes repainted in two passes of short, low-contrast (±8% value) form-following strokes: broad (1/60 of frame width) on water, sky and cloth, fine (1/150) on faces, which stay smooth. Palette by role: sky, sea and far land mid blue-violet #32377a, about 60% of the frame; moonlit planes and sand pale lavender-blue; teal #128a91 in water and lit sides; magenta #7a2f86 rim; deepest shadows #07071c. Any skin takes blue top planes and a wet highlight, eyes open and lit. Default night: one key, a full moon with a broken glitter path on water; magenta only as a soft rim from a distant glow; other small light sources stay cyan-white points. Grain ±3%. Signature shot: the figure calm, ~2/3 of frame height. Slow push-ins. Reference: Barry Jenkins, *Moonlight* (2016). Avoid: coarse dabs, small dark statue eyes, a murky frame.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n油画肖像笔触,青绿 / 品红 / 蓝紫双色调,半水上半水下的机位\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮小喀戎，不穿戏服：先在游泳课上被托在水面，再站在三张脸之间，最后坐在凯文身边的沙滩上。三个章节三个镜头硬切；海里和海滩两个镜头都慢慢推近，让安静延续下去。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 贴着碧绿海面的半水上半水下镜头：一个男人站在齐胸深的水里，用手掌托着 Clawd，水下能看见他的手；镜头慢推。（0.8s）\n2. 切到并排的三张油画肖像，青绿、品红、蓝紫各一张；镜头重新取景，三张脸滑拼成一个楔形拼贴，Clawd 站在青绿那一块里。（0.8s）\n3. 切到月光下的海滩：凯文和 Clawd 并排坐在蓝色沙滩上的双人镜头，满月在右上，浪花拍岸；镜头慢推。（0.9s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nBarry Jenkins, Moonlight (2016),James Laxton (cinematography),Moonlight (2016) three-face poster\n"},{"id":"S6-O90","name":"水形物语","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O90.jpg","markdown":"# O90 水形物语（The Shape of Water）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 独立年代 2010–2019\n- 长什么样：实时 3D 的被淹浴室和实验室水箱，整片浸在青绿里：流动的焦散、从上方落下的光束、气泡和几盏暖色灯。德尔·托罗这部片就泡在这种水里，所以整段在水中拍。\n- 风格：3D 水下青绿焦散\n- 关键词：3D\n- 年份：2017\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n实时 3D，全在水下（陆上题材就沉进淹水的屋子或水下遗迹），泡在青绿里：背景 #041414，半球光上方 #5fb3a6、下方 #0c2a24，青绿雾，红色随距离最先消失。水面上方一个强光源投下流动焦散：近处表面上的亮格子约为画面宽度的 1/20，滑过地面、墙面和主体。光柱向下散开，画面四边保持暗。气泡、微粒、从下仰看的波纹水面。暖金色给一到三处实际灯光，至少一处要大（亮窗或灯，约为画面宽度的 1/10），加青色生物荧光。平滑着色，环境光遮蔽、浅景深、适度辉光、颗粒（±3%）。招牌镜头：低机位仰拍，主体约占画面高度一半，偏在光源一侧；慢推。参照吉尔莫·德尔·托罗《水形物语》（2017）。避免：平板的水、焦散画成天上的条纹、暖色点缀太小。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nReal-time 3D, fully under water (land subjects sink into a flooded room or sunken place), in teal: background #041414, hemisphere light #5fb3a6 above and #0c2a24 below; teal fog, reds fading first with distance. A strong source above the surface projects moving caustics: bright cells about 1/20 of frame width on near surfaces, sliding over floor, walls and the subject. Shafts fan down; edges stay dark. Bubbles, motes, the rippled surface from below. Warm gold on 1–3 practicals, at least one large (a lit window or lamp ~1/10 of frame width), plus cyan bioluminescence. Smooth-shaded models, ambient occlusion, shallow focus, modest bloom, grain ±3%. Signature shot: low angle looking up, the subject half the frame height, off-centre beside the light; slow push-ins. Reference: Guillermo del Toro, *The Shape of Water* (2017). Avoid: flat water, caustics as sky stripes, tiny warm accents.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n实时 3D（three.js）,投射焦散与体积光束,Dan Laustsen 的青绿配暖金调色\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮埃莉莎，头上一条深红丝绒发带和蝴蝶结，后面手里拿一个白煮蛋。2.5 秒四刀，节奏稳定在 1.25 倍：光束下的拥抱、贴着的脸、水箱，最后是两只手，接触处亮起青光作为收尾的一拍。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 被淹浴室里的低机位全景：两栖人在光束下抱着 Clawd，一只红鞋在下沉；镜头慢推。（0.9s）\n2. 切到脸部近景：他的头冠和鳃贴着 Clawd 的一角，红色发带蝴蝶结，气泡上升；镜头缓缓推近。（0.5s）\n3. 切到实验室：中景，Clawd 站在发光的水箱前，手里拿着白煮蛋，向后伸手贴上玻璃；镜头慢推。（0.6s）\n4. 切到特写：两只手隔着玻璃相贴，接触处亮起一团青光。（0.4s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nGuillermo del Toro, The Shape of Water (2017),Dan Laustsen (cinematography),Paul D. Austerberry (production design),The Shape of Water (2017) poster\n"},{"id":"S6-O91","name":"绿皮书","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O91.jpg","markdown":"# O91 绿皮书（Green Book）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 独立年代 2010–2019\n- 长什么样：一张「南方问候」亚麻纹明信片活了过来：布纹纸、喷枪上的饱和色、镀铬上的星形闪光。影片是 1962 年穿越美国南方的公路旅行，走的正是这种明信片上的路。\n- 风格：60 年代亚麻纹明信片\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：2018\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n五六十年代亚麻纹明信片，柯特·泰希出版社那一路。平纹布纹卡纸：油墨只落在凸起的经纬上，形成约为画面宽度 1/300 的细格纹，凹处露白点。手工分色的饱和色，喷枪柔和过渡：天蓝 #3fa0dc、草绿 #4fae57、粉红 #f08cb0，主体物或水面用青绿 #2fb5b8，奶油卡纸 #f6e4b8 作底色和白边。主体物要大，正侧面，约占卡片高度一半。亮面加四角星闪光；套色错位 1–2 像素。藏青立体大字带白描边，横在卡片上三分之一，写地名或片名（没有就按题材编一个），每个字母里嵌一小幅风景，上方配红飘带。开场明信片放在桌上，推进到满屏后画面活过来（水在动、人在走），再侧向横移。避免：没有布纹的渐变、发灰的颜色、照片式透视。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nA 1950s–60s linen postcard, Curt Teich manner. Linen-weave card: ink only on raised threads, a fine crosshatch about 1/300 of frame width, white specks in the hollows. Saturated hand-separated colour, airbrush blends: sky blue #3fa0dc, grass green #4fae57, pink #f08cb0, teal #2fb5b8 for the main object or water, cream card #f6e4b8 for paper and border. The main object large, side-on, about half the card's height. Star glints on shiny surfaces; registration off 1–2 px. Big navy 3D block letters with white outlines across the top third spell the place or title (invent one if needed), a small scene in each letter, a red ribbon banner above. Open on the card on a desk, push in until it fills the frame and comes alive (water, figures move), then track sideways. Avoid: weave-less gradients, greyish colour, photographic perspective.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\nCurt Teich 亚麻纹明信片,「Greetings from」大字母,喷枪手工上色,胶印套色错位\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮托尼：油亮的黑色大背头、浅蓝短袖口、一支点着的烟，一手握方向盘。推进明信片的运镜直接带进公路，不切；唯一的一刀落在音乐厅，雪利博士弹琴，Clawd 在侧幕边看。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 一个连续运镜：汽车旅馆书桌上的明信片，旁边是绿皮书和房门钥匙；镜头一路推进明信片，画面活过来，青绿色凯迪拉克开动，镜头往后拉开，变成侧面跟拍，Clawd 握着方向盘，雪利博士坐在后座。（1.9s）\n2. 唯一一刀切到音乐厅：全景，雪利博士和他的三重奏在红丝绒幕前演奏三角钢琴，Clawd 站在侧幕边看；镜头轻推。（0.6s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nPeter Farrelly, Green Book (2018),Sean Porter (cinematography),Curt Teich & Co. linen postcards (1931–59),The Negro Motorist Green Book (1962)\n"},{"id":"S6-O92","name":"寄生虫","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O92.jpg","markdown":"# O92 寄生虫（Parasite）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 独立年代 2010–2019\n- 长什么样：一张剖开首尔山坡的建筑剖面图：山顶的玻璃豪宅、地下的秘密掩体、一路通到被淹的半地下室，细线、柔和的材质色。《寄生虫》建在这种上下的阶级落差上，一张图装下整部片。\n- 风格：建筑剖面轴测图\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：2019\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n矢量建筑剖面轴测图，用低角度二等角视图：剖切面沿接近水平的 7° 轴正对观众，进深沿 42° 轴、按一半缩短；屋顶和近侧墙剖掉，露出房间。剖切穿过主体所在之处（房子、塔、船、山坡）。切面填涂黑：深色混凝土带骨料，土层画 45° 斜线夹小石块。任何缩放都是 1 像素深灰线。默认白天、淡色天空：混凝土 #b3ada2、橡木 #d6b58a、炭灰 #3e4146、松绿 #3f6b3e，白墙、绿草坪、半透明蓝水。房间配家具和简洁的小比例人。夜景或其他暗光只是变体：叠乘三到四成月光蓝，亮灯的房间涂回暖色。镜头沿剖面慢慢升降，框住两三个房间，跟主体走一条不断的路线，最后停在占画面约八成的整张图上。避免：30° 等轴测、粗卡通描边、楼层间切镜头、图太小。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nVector section axonometric, low dimetric view: the cut face lies along the near-flat 7° axis, facing us, depth along the 42° axis at half scale; roof and near walls cut away to show the rooms. Cut through the subject's place (a house, a tower, a ship, a hillside). Poché on cut faces: dark concrete with aggregate, earth hatched at 45° with stones. 1 px dark-grey lines at every zoom. Default daylight under a pale sky: concrete #b3ada2, oak #d6b58a, charcoal #3e4146, pine #3f6b3e, white walls, green lawn, translucent blue water. Furnished rooms, simple scale figures. Night or other low light is a variation: multiply 30–40% moonlit blue, lit rooms painted back warm. Crane slowly framing 2–3 rooms along one continuous path; end on the whole drawing at ~80% of the frame. Avoid: 30° isometric, thick outlines, cuts between levels, a small drawing.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n二等角剖面轴测,建筑剖切填充与比例人,雨夜调色\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮基宇，山水石一路不离手：在草坪上举过头顶，暴雨夜躲在茶几边，再一路顶着它下山。全段只切两刀；每个镜头里镜头都沿剖面上下走，让富人和穷人在同一个画面里，12 拍最后停在整张剖面上。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 白天的玻璃豪宅剖面和条纹草坪上的派对：Clawd 站在花园门口，双手把山水石举过头顶；镜头慢慢推近。（0.8s）\n2. 切到暴雨夜：朴家客厅，Clawd 躲在茶几边；镜头像升降机一样往下，穿过楼板和地下室，落到掩体，灯在按摩斯码闪。（1.3s）\n3. 切到雨中一路往下的长镜头：镜头跟着头顶山水石的 Clawd 从大门走下一段段长台阶，穿过马路底下的隧道，进到低处的街巷和正在被淹的半地下室，再升起拉开成整张剖面，豪宅在顶上，淹水在最底下。（2.9s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nBong Joon-ho, Parasite (2019),Hong Kyung-pyo (cinematography),Lee Ha-jun (production design),Parasite (2019) poster,Architectural section axonometrics\n"},{"id":"S6-O93","name":"无依之地","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O93.jpg","markdown":"# O93 无依之地（Nomadland）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 此时此刻 2020–2025\n- 长什么样：灰紫色纸上的粉质色粉笔触，一片开阔的平原和一辆旧房车，天空从金黄变成玫瑰色再到紫色。《无依之地》就活在黄昏那条又长又空的地平线上，色粉让它安静、柔和。\n- 风格：魔幻时刻色粉画\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：2020\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n灰紫色纸 #7d7079 上的软色粉画，纸的齿纹处处透出来，成细密颗粒（亮度 ±8%）。天空揉开抹平，只有几条细长的横向云带。质感集中在地面：干草赭色（金黄往纸的灰色压暗），上面铺满直立的细草笔，每笔宽约为画面宽度的 1/400、长约 1/40，明度起伏 ±15%，前景密，往地平线渐稀。物体用柔和的斜向排笔，边缘软而碎。按用途分色：地平线金黄 #f6cf7a，下层天空玫瑰 #c9738a，高空、云带和远处陆地用紫 #4b3f78，暗部用暮蓝 #2b2d5c。地平线长，在画面中线附近，下面是原野或水面；主体和身边的东西中等大小，放在下半幅。天色从金黄转玫瑰再转紫，越暗暖灯越亮。一个缓慢横移带轻推的镜头。避免：油画厚涂感、锐利数码边、光秃平滑的地面。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nSoft pastel on grey-violet toned paper #7d7079, tooth showing everywhere as fine speckle (±8% brightness). Sky blended smooth, a few long thin cloud bands. The land carries the texture: dry-grass ochre (gold dulled toward the paper grey) under fine upright grass strokes (1/400 of frame width wide, 1/40 long, ±15% value), dense in the foreground, thinning toward the horizon. Objects get soft diagonal hatching, broken edges. Palette by role: gold #f6cf7a at the horizon, rose #c9738a in the lower sky, violet #4b3f78 for high sky, clouds and far land, dusk blue #2b2d5c for shadows. A long horizon near mid-frame over open land or water; the subject mid-sized in the lower half. The sky drifts gold to rose to violet; lamps brighten as it darkens. One slow pan with a light push-in. Avoid: oily impasto, sharp edges, bare smooth ground.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n有色纸上的软色粉,魔幻时刻的黄昏配色,纸面颗粒肌理\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮弗恩，不拿道具，只有走、坐、看三个动作。整段一刀不剪：走路慢慢停在毯子上，坐下，再抬眼望向地平线；天色暗下来，露营灯和房车门口的光越来越暖。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 一镜到底的全景：Clawd 从一辆侧门敞开的白色旧房车前走过，镜头跟着它横移并轻轻推近，平原和远处的孤丘缓缓滑过；它走到露营灯旁的条纹毯子上坐下，望向地平线，天色从金黄变成紫色。（2.5s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nChloé Zhao, Nomadland (2020),Joshua James Richards (cinematography)\n"},{"id":"S6-O94","name":"健听女孩","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O94.jpg","markdown":"# O94 健听女孩（CODA）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 此时此刻 2020–2025\n- 长什么样：橡木桌上一颗抛光的抹香鲸牙，刻着细密的黑线，点缀红、蓝、黄：格洛斯特的渔船、收网的一家人、船头唱歌的鲁比。这是影片所在的新英格兰海岸自己的民间工艺。\n- 风格：鲸牙骨雕（Scrimshaw）\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：2021\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n19 世纪水手在抛光鲸牙上的针刻画。招牌镜头：宽而扁的鲸牙横躺在橡木桌上，占画面宽约 85%、高约 70%，墨水瓶和折刀在边上。用近黑墨 #17110c 刻：细针排线（线宽约画面宽的 1/800，线距约 1/500）、粗细起伏的轮廓线和点刻。刻线覆盖牙面约七成：海、主体和陆地密，天空轻排线；海是成排的程式化波浪。主角要大（约占牙面高三分之一）并上色；此外红 #9a2a22、蓝 #2b4a78、黄 #e9b526 只作点缀。暖象牙底、淡生长纹、抛光划痕、牙尖蜜黄。民间朴拙构图：绳纹边框、飘带题词、放射光芒的太阳、主体世界的图案。弧度靠边缘明暗和一道窗户反光，画面不变形。先拍整颗牙，推进后慢慢横移，波浪随之动起来。避免：粗线、实色填涂、细长的牛角形。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nScrimshaw: 19th-century sailor's engraving on a whale tooth. Signature shot: a broad, flat-sided tooth lies horizontal on an oak table, about 85% of frame width and 70% of its height; ink bottle, jackknife beside. Engrave in near-black #17110c: needle hatching (lines 1/800 of frame width, spaced about 1/500), swelling outlines, stipple. Lines cover ~70% of the face, dense in sea, subject and land, sky lightly hatched; seas as rows of stylised waves. The hero is large (~1/3 of the tooth's height), coloured; otherwise red #9a2a22, blue #2b4a78, yellow #e9b526 only as small touches. Warm ivory, growth bands, scratches, honey tip. Folk layout: rope border, ribbon motto, rayed sun, motifs from the subject's world. Shade the edges, one gloss streak; don't warp the picture. Push in, then pan slowly as waves animate. Avoid: thick lines, solid fills, a slim horn.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n19 世纪捕鲸水手鲸骨雕,刻刀排线与点刻,桌面静物产品布光\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮鲁比，戴一顶黄色防水渔夫帽，站在船头的鱼箱上。只有一刀：从物件切进刻画，然后一次轻轻的横移，把视线从父亲的手语带到歌声上，五线谱跟着歌声展开。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 全景：刻好的鲸牙放在橡木桌上，旁边是折刀和墨水瓶；镜头推进，直到鲸牙铺满画面。（0.8s）\n2. 切进刻画：渔船的中全景，镜头从左往右慢慢横移并轻推，从一边收网一边打出「我爱你」手语的弗兰克，移到船头唱歌的 Clawd，一行五线谱从它举起的手臂上展开。（1.7s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nSiân Heder, CODA (2021),CODA (2021) poster,New Bedford Whaling Museum scrimshaw collection\n"},{"id":"S6-O95","name":"瞬息全宇宙","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O95.jpg","markdown":"# O95 瞬息全宇宙（Everything Everywhere All at Once）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 此时此刻 2020–2025\n- 长什么样：四个宇宙四种画法，全部实时光线步进：冷绿的国税局、广告光下的热狗手指钢琴、贴大眼睛石头的寂静峡谷、变成黑洞的万物贝果。像电影里一样，靠大眼睛贴纸在宇宙间跳转。\n- 风格：光线步进多元宇宙跳切\n- 关键词：3D\n- 年份：2022\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n实时光线步进 3D 的多元宇宙蒙太奇：主体在三到四个宇宙之间跳转，各有画法，都从主体自己的世界变出来，例如：冷绿日光灯下的写实工作场所，陈设很满；带引力透镜光环的超现实宇宙；配白字幕的安静旷野远景；糖果粉背景、粉青轮廓光的广告产品照。每个停 2–4 秒，各自调色。贯穿始终的衔接物：主体身上的一只活动眼贴纸，约占画高十分之一。跳转 0.5–0.8 秒：推进这只眼睛，拖影、RGB 分离，再炸开一圈彩虹色块环（#e5322d、#f7c928、#1fa39a、#2a6fc0、#e0478f），环上嵌满活动眼和粉珠，扩到画高约八成后白闪。招牌画面：彩虹环围着主体绽开；第一次跳转在 1 秒左右。参照丹尼尔斯兄弟《瞬息全宇宙》（2022）。避免：宇宙都一个样、环太小太素。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nRay-marched real-time 3D multiverse montage: the subject jumps through 3–4 universes, each its own look, invented from its world, e.g. a realistic workplace under cold green fluorescents, densely dressed; a surreal cosmic version with a gravitational-lens halo; a silent wide landscape with white subtitles; a candy-pink product shot with pink-and-cyan rims. Each holds 2–4 s with its own grade. One link through all: a googly-eye sticker on the subject, about 1/10 of frame height. Transition, 0.5–0.8 s: push into the eye, smear, split RGB, then a rainbow ring of colour segments (#e5322d, #f7c928, #1fa39a, #2a6fc0, #e0478f) studded with googly eyes and pink beads blooms to ~80% of frame height and bursts to white. Signature frame: the ring around the subject; first jump at ~1 s. Reference: Daniels, *Everything Everywhere All at Once* (2022). Avoid: look-alike universes, a small plain ring.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\nGLSL 光线步进的多个宇宙,一个宇宙一种画法（写实办公室、超现实宇宙、风景、广告）,大眼睛彩虹曼陀罗\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮秀莲，额头贴大眼睛、腰挂腰包。每次跳宇宙都是先推进那只眼睛，再接拖影、RGB 分离和白闪，卡在切点上；每个宇宙一个不同的动作：站着、用脚踩琴键、陪石头坐着、被吸进贝果。最后回到起点，在曼陀罗里张开双臂。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 国税局办公室全景，收据满天飞，镜头沿过道推向 Clawd；它额头贴着一只大眼睛，腰上挂着腰包。（0.8s）\n2. 切到审计员和她堆满收据的钢制办公桌，她举着一张收据；镜头慢推。（0.6s）\n3. 切到举起双臂的 Clawd，镜头猛推进它额头的大眼睛，跳宇宙的闪光炸开。（0.4s）\n4. 热狗手指宇宙：糖果粉光下的亮面钢琴全景，一只手的热狗手指软软地瘫在琴键上，Clawd 站在琴键上。（0.5s）\n5. 切近到 Clawd 的脚，一下下踩着琴键，两臂末端也长出小热狗。（0.5s）\n6. 再次推进大眼睛，接下一次跳宇宙。（0.4s）\n7. 寂静峡谷边的全景：两块贴着大眼睛的石头，Clawd 安静地坐在旁边，字幕「We're all small and stupid.」；镜头沿崖边慢慢漂移。（1s）\n8. 万物贝果：Clawd 仰望巨大的黑色贝果，镜头一路推进中间的黑洞，穿过视界，Clawd 被吸得往前倾。（1.7s）\n9. 回到办公室：Clawd 张开双臂，身后长出一圈带大眼睛的彩虹曼陀罗环；镜头轻轻拉开。（0.6s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nDaniels, Everything Everywhere All at Once (2022),Larkin Seiple (cinematography),Jason Kisvarday (production design),Everything Everywhere All at Once (2022) poster\n"},{"id":"S6-O96","name":"奥本海默","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O96.jpg","markdown":"# O96 奥本海默（Oppenheimer）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 此时此刻 2020–2025\n- 长什么样：用实时 3D 重建黎明前的三位一体试验场：约 72 万粒子的火球、轨道和波组成的量子幻象，深蓝夜色对橙金火光，加 IMAX 式颗粒。《奥本海默》就围着这一道光展开。\n- 风格：3D 粒子火球 · IMAX 颗粒\n- 关键词：3D\n- 年份：2023\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n实时 3D 夜景，主角光是一场巨大的粒子事件：几十万粒子的火球（或照明弹、火山喷发、山火），形状随时间走：白闪（0.5 秒），1 秒成翻滚的斑驳穹顶，再卷成环，尘墙外扫。在主体正后方像光环，约占画面四成：核心白金，大部分是有纹理的亮橙金火团 #ff9a50，黑烟只做细暗纹和顶边，不超一成半。火光照亮全场：暖色 #ffd8a0 铺满地面和云底，主体有热轮廓光，脸上仍有半强度暖光；长影子朝镜头拖来，火星飘飞。其余是黎明前的深蓝 #2a3654 和暗土 #16120e。只让核心过曝。IMAX 拷贝调色：细颗粒（±3%）、暖色光晕溢出、暗角、轻微片门晃动。低机位广角，主体居中，慢推。参照克里斯托弗·诺兰《奥本海默》（2023）。避免：光滑的 CG 光球、被黑烟裹住的火。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nReal-time 3D night lit by one huge particle event: a ~500k-particle fireball (or flare, eruption, wildfire) whose shape follows time: white flash (0.5 s), billowing dome by 1 s, a rolling toroid, a dust wall sweeping out. Right behind the subject like a halo, ~40% of the frame: white-gold core, bright orange-gold billows #ff9a50 with visible texture, black smoke only as thin veins and the top edge (under 15%). Its glow fills everything: warm #ffd8a0 on ground and cloud undersides, a hot rim on the subject, faces lit warm at half strength; long shadows toward camera. The rest is pre-dawn blue #2a3654 and dark earth #16120e. Clip only the core. IMAX grade: grain ±3%, warm halation, vignette, slight gate weave. Low wide angle, subject centred, slow push-ins. Reference: Christopher Nolan, *Oppenheimer* (2023). Avoid: a smooth CG ball, smoke-shrouded fire.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n实时 3D（three.js）,GPU 粒子模拟,IMAX 65mm 印片调色,三位一体试验高速照片\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮奥本海默：一顶浅驼色平顶软呢帽，手里一只冒烟的石楠烟斗。五个镜头硬切，垒出倒计时：铁塔、装置、幻象，白闪正好落在切进观察者的那一刀上；音乐不停，最后一秒火球在 Clawd 身后膨胀。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 黎明前低机位仰拍被探照灯照亮的钢塔，倒计时的信号灯在闪，镜头慢推。（0.6s）\n2. 切到波纹铁皮小屋里的炸弹：缠满电缆的螺栓球体，暖色工作灯；固定机位。（0.4s）\n3. 切到黑底上的量子幻象：围绕核心的发光轨道、下方的干涉波、一颗坍缩的恒星。（0.4s）\n4. 一道白闪，接反打：戴帽子的观察者举着焊接玻璃，脸被火光照亮，Clawd 戴平顶软呢帽、拿着烟斗站在中间；固定机位。（0.8s）\n5. 低机位全景：Clawd 站在路中间，身后的火球像光环一样升起，火星在空中飘；镜头慢推。（1s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nChristopher Nolan, Oppenheimer (2023),Hoyte van Hoytema (IMAX cinematography),Berlyn Brixner, Trinity test photographs (1945),Oppenheimer (2023) poster\n"},{"id":"S6-O97","name":"阿诺拉","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O97.jpg","markdown":"# O97 阿诺拉（Anora）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 此时此刻 2020–2025\n- 长什么样：粉、青、橙的霓虹灯管发着光，倒影拖在湿栈道上，落进飘雪里，加上辉光、虚焦光斑和颗粒。《阿诺拉》从布莱顿海滩的俄文霓虹走到维加斯的婚礼招牌，整段都用灯管照亮。\n- 风格：雪夜霓虹\n- 关键词：2D\n- 年份：2024\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n照片感的 2D 霓虹夜景。招牌是真实灯管：玻璃管、辉光、亮管、白热管芯，带卡子和深色跳线；轻微起伏，很少抖一下（5–10 秒一次）。粉 #ff4fa8、青 #4fdcff、橙 #ff9a2e、绿 #3dff9a，加暖色钠灯，压在深紫蓝夜色 #1e1838 上。霓虹放在主体世界里说得通的地方（店面、港口酒吧、主体自己那栋楼）。画面下四分之一留给湿地面：招牌拉成长长的竖向倒影，被木板缝、地砖缝或水纹打断；雪在下，边上积成雪堆，被溢光染色。主体脸上保留暖色主光（来自近处的灯、窗或它自己的灯），两侧各一条窄霓虹轮廓光；远处虚成光斑。辉光、暗角、颗粒（±3%）。每个短镜头 2–3 秒，同一地点换角度，各自慢推。参照肖恩·贝克《阿诺拉》（2024）。避免：平涂彩字式霓虹、干地面、频闪。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nPhotographic 2D neon night. Real tubes: glass tube, glow, bright tube, white-hot core, clips, dark jumper wires; rare flicker (every 5–10 s). Neon pink #ff4fa8, cyan #4fdcff, orange #ff9a2e, green #3dff9a plus warm sodium light over a deep purple-blue night #1e1838. Put neon where the subject's world allows (shopfronts, a harbour bar, its own building). Wet ground fills the lower quarter: signs stretch into long vertical reflections broken by plank gaps, paving joints or ripples; snow falls and drifts at the edges, tinted by the spill. The subject keeps a warm key on the face (a lamp, a window, its own light), narrow neon rims on both sides; the distance melts into bokeh. Bloom, vignette, grain ±3%. Short 2–3 s shots, new angles on one place, each a slow push-in. Reference: Sean Baker, *Anora* (2024). Avoid: flat neon lettering, strobing.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n霓虹灯管招牌,湿地面倒影与积雪,35mm 夜景摄影\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮阿诺拉：奶油色毛皮护耳帽、竖起的毛领、一只大圆耳环。四个短镜头卡着拍子走，每个都带一点推拉：烟花、和万尼亚在栈道上、婚礼招牌，最后是安静的车里和落雪。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 长焦拍海边亮灯的摩天轮和跳伞塔，一朵新年烟花炸开；镜头慢推。（0.6s）\n2. 切到下雪的栈道，旁边一排俄文霓虹店招：戴毛皮护耳帽和毛领的 Clawd 站在前面，万尼亚在它旁边大笑挥手，招牌倒映在湿木板上；镜头慢慢拉开。（0.7s）\n3. 切到拉斯维加斯：心形穿箭的「Wedding」招牌和 CHAPEL 灯箱铺满画面，旁边是白色小教堂；镜头慢推。（0.6s）\n4. 切到最后一场戏，停着的车里：Clawd 坐在仪表台上，背后挡风玻璃上的雪落下、积住，伊戈尔在前景握着方向盘；镜头慢推。（0.6s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nSean Baker, Anora (2024),Drew Daniels (cinematography),Anora (2024) poster\n"},{"id":"S6-O98","name":"一战再战","series":"S6","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S6-O98.jpg","markdown":"# O98 一战再战（One Battle After Another）\n\n- 分类：O 奥斯卡 98 部 · 此时此刻 2020–2025\n- 长什么样：实时 3D 的沙漠公路，像过山车一样一起一伏，三辆车翻着一道道坡追逐，用超长焦拍，带热浪、漂白的强光和细颗粒。复刻的是这部片最有名的段落：起伏公路追车。\n- 风格：3D 起伏公路追车 · 长焦压缩\n- 关键词：3D\n- 年份：2025\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n实时 3D，漂白般的沙漠日光。招牌镜头（3–5 秒）：一条笔直的路在连续起伏的坡谷上，水平视角 2–5°，让坡谷叠成波浪，行进的主体沉下去又冒出来；构图先保坡谷，天空最多留一条窄带。再接各 1–2 秒的坡顶低机位、车载或骑手机位。配色：天空 #7fb0e0，雾气 #ece4d2 只在最远的地平线，浅沙色 #d2ae80（不能发橙），硬朗阳光 #fff0d8 从高处侧打，主车是褪色海沫绿 #86b5ad。反差来自前景：开裂的深色沥青、清晰阴影、茂密的绿灌木、路边桩。默认正午，其他时段也保持硬光。热浪扭曲和路面蜃景随距离加重；落地扬尘；车过坡顶腾空、落地弹跳。大画幅细颗粒（±2%）。参照保罗·托马斯·安德森《一战再战》（2025）。避免：广角、发白发灰的画面、粘在路上的车。\n\n---\n\nEnglish:\n\nReal-time 3D, bleached desert daylight. Signature shot (3–5 s): a straight road over rolling dips and crests at a 2–5° horizontal field of view, stacking dips into waves; the travelling subject sinks and reappears; sky a thin band at most. Then 1–2 s each, a low camera at a crest, a mount on the vehicle or rider. Palette: sky #7fb0e0, haze #ece4d2 only at the far horizon, pale sand #d2ae80 (never orange), hard sun #fff0d8 from high to one side, the main vehicle faded seafoam #86b5ad. Contrast: dark cracked asphalt, crisp shadows, dense green scrub, roadside posts. Default midday; at other hours keep hard sun. Heat shimmer and mirage grow with distance; dust on landings; vehicles leave the ground over crests. Grain ±2%. Reference: Paul Thomas Anderson, *One Battle After Another* (2025). Avoid: wide lenses, a washed-out frame.\n\n## 补充\n\n### 技法关键词\n\n实时 3D（three.js）,超长焦压缩,热浪与路面蜃景,VistaVision 细颗粒\n\n### 导演说明\n\nClawd 扮鲍勃：格子浴袍的翻领、推到头顶的金框墨镜，开着最前面那辆车。追车按真实速度走，五个机位在长焦的波浪和近处的瞬间之间交替；最后一个镜头停在 Clawd 举起的手臂上，这个姿势定住，好让终章接过去。\n\n### 成片里的分镜\n\n1. 超长焦从远处车队前方往回看公路：起伏压成一层层波浪，三辆车在波浪里沉下去又冒出来，柏油路面上热浪晃动。（1.2s）\n2. 切到引擎盖上的机位：Clawd 坐在海沫绿敞篷车的驾驶座，墨镜推在头顶，双手握紧方向盘，然后抬眼看后视镜。（0.8s）\n3. 切到坡顶路肩的低机位三脚架：敞篷车飞过坡顶，冲向镜头。（0.6s）\n4. 再切回长焦，这次在车队后方往前看，几辆车在一道道起伏里消失又出现。（1.1s）\n5. 切到车前方的长焦跟拍：Clawd 的敞篷车翻过坡顶离地，深绿和灰色两辆轿车从后面追上来；落地后 Clawd 把一只手伸出车外举起，一直举到最后一帧。（1.3s）\n\n### 参考出处\n\nPaul Thomas Anderson, One Battle After Another (2025),Michael Bauman (VistaVision cinematography),One Battle After Another (2025) poster\n"},{"id":"S7-R1","name":"霓虹雨夜 · 电影感","series":"S7","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S7-R1.jpg","markdown":"# R1 霓虹雨夜 · 电影感\n\n- 分类：R 照参考学来的 · 赛博朋克（第一批）\n- 长什么样：一点透视的雨夜窄街，两边挂满竖招牌，青色的雾越远越浓，街尽头一团暖光；湿地面上的倒影占了半个画面。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n一点透视的雨夜窄街，两边挂满竖招牌，青色的雾越远越浓，街尽头一团暖光；湿地面上的倒影占了半个画面。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 学的是哪张参考\n\n01 霓虹雨夜 · 电影概念\n\n### 相近的旧编号\n\nD1 雨窗\n\n### 定死的画法\n\n不描边；冷青做底，红粉招牌和尽头的橙光做主光；越远越被雾吃掉；倒影竖着拉长、被雨打出波纹；最近的几家拉着卷帘门，把前景压暗。招牌的字从洗过的词表里发，不重复。\n"},{"id":"S7-R2","name":"80–90 年代复古日漫","series":"S7","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S7-R2.jpg","markdown":"# R2 80–90 年代复古日漫\n\n- 分类：R 照参考学来的 · 赛博朋克（第一批）\n- 长什么样：赛璐珞画的人和车在夜里的高架上飞驰，仰着看的高楼，尾灯拖出两条光带，整片像老录像带放出来的。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n赛璐珞画的人和车在夜里的高架上飞驰，仰着看的高楼，尾灯拖出两条光带，整片像老录像带放出来的。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 学的是哪张参考\n\n02 复古日漫\n\n### 相近的旧编号\n\nL46\n\n### 定死的画法\n\n人和车是平涂 + 一块硬边阴影 + 一条硬边高光 + 有颜色的描边；背景是喷枪画、横向拉丝表示速度（招牌在拉丝之后画，字才看得清）；画面微微歪；最后过一遍录像带的色渗和扫描线。\n"},{"id":"S7-R3","name":"美式漫画 · 潮流涂鸦","series":"S7","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S7-R3.jpg","markdown":"# R3 美式漫画 · 潮流涂鸦\n\n- 分类：R 照参考学来的 · 赛博朋克（第一批）\n- 长什么样：黄底，品红和青的闪电劈开画面，网点、喷漆、往外炸的速度线；粗黑描边的人一拳打出画面，旁边一个大拟声字。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n黄底，品红和青的闪电劈开画面，网点、喷漆、往外炸的速度线；粗黑描边的人一拳打出画面，旁边一个大拟声字。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 学的是哪张参考\n\n04 美式漫画 · 潮流涂鸦\n\n### 相近的旧编号\n\nK13、X18\n\n### 定死的画法\n\n粗黑描边；只用黄、品红、青、蓝四个颜色平涂；阴影是整块的深紫，压一层网点；不发光、不虚化；人往动作的方向整个倾过去。\n"},{"id":"S7-R4","name":"末日工业 · 暗黑科幻","series":"S7","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S7-R4.jpg","markdown":"# R4 末日工业 · 暗黑科幻\n\n- 分类：R 照参考学来的 · 赛博朋克（第一批）\n- 长什么样：几乎只有绿；巨大的机械结构一层层叠进雾里，远处一个大圆环，顶上漏下光柱；走道上一个穿红衣、背着包的小人背对镜头。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n几乎只有绿；巨大的机械结构一层层叠进雾里，远处一个大圆环，顶上漏下光柱；走道上一个穿红衣、背着包的小人背对镜头。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 学的是哪张参考\n\n05 末日工业 · 暗黑科幻\n\n### 相近的旧编号\n\n—\n\n### 定死的画法\n\n一个颜色分七层远近，越远越淡；唯一的暖色是小人和几盏小灯；最前面垂下来的铁链几乎全黑，再推远一层。\n"},{"id":"S7-R5","name":"像素赛博 · 复古游戏","series":"S7","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S7-R5.jpg","markdown":"# R5 像素赛博 · 复古游戏\n\n- 分类：R 照参考学来的 · 赛博朋克（第一批）\n- 长什么样：俯看一条满是招牌的街，天桥横过去，街尽头一块\"不夜城\"大屏，打伞的人、小车、飞车，下着像素雨。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n俯看一条满是招牌的街，天桥横过去，街尽头一块\"不夜城\"大屏，打伞的人、小车、飞车，下着像素雨。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 学的是哪张参考\n\n07 像素赛博 · 复古游戏\n\n### 相近的旧编号\n\nM14、L49、L50、X24\n\n### 定死的画法\n\n先画在 480×270 的小画布上，整张压进 28 色调色板（拜耳抖动处理渐变和光晕），再放大 4 倍、不做平滑。动起来时不用一格一格跳的像素动画，每帧从连续的骨骼重画，动作一定要流畅。\n"},{"id":"S7-R6","name":"东方赛博 · 国风幻想","series":"S7","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S7-R6.jpg","markdown":"# R6 东方赛博 · 国风幻想\n\n- 分类：R 照参考学来的 · 赛博朋克（第一批）\n- 长什么样：纸色的天，一轮大红日头，一行鹤飞过；水边一座五层飞檐楼阁，身上挂着\"不夜城\"\"酒家\"霓虹竖招牌和一块屏，塔尖套着青色的全息光环；后面是淡墨的未来塔楼和山，电缆上挂着一串串小红灯笼；水上一叶小船、一个戴斗笠的人，倒影像水墨一样化开。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n纸色的天，一轮大红日头，一行鹤飞过；水边一座五层飞檐楼阁，身上挂着\"不夜城\"\"酒家\"霓虹竖招牌和一块屏，塔尖套着青色的全息光环；后面是淡墨的未来塔楼和山，电缆上挂着一串串小红灯笼；水上一叶小船、一个戴斗笠的人，倒影像水墨一样化开。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 学的是哪张参考\n\n08 东方赛博 · 国风幻想\n\n### 相近的旧编号\n\nC1、Z1、L19、M30\n\n### 定死的画法\n\n纸色底，墨分浓淡做远近；颜色只有墨、纸、朱红，霓虹只点一点青；墨块边上有洇开的淡墨，檐上有干笔擦痕；水边一带雾把楼脚化开；竖排题字和一方印章收住画面。\n"},{"id":"S7-R7","name":"扁平插画 · 黄昏屋顶","series":"S7","hasStill":true,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S7-R7.jpg","markdown":"# R7 扁平插画 · 黄昏屋顶\n\n- 分类：R 照参考学来的 · 赛博朋克（第一批）\n- 长什么样：黄昏的大渐变天，三层平涂的城市剪影，屋顶上一块\"夜城\"大霓虹字，一个人和一只猫坐在楼边，后面一列轻轨开过。\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n黄昏的大渐变天，三层平涂的城市剪影，屋顶上一块\"夜城\"大霓虹字，一个人和一只猫坐在楼边，后面一列轻轨开过。\n\n## 补充\n\n### 学的是哪张参考\n\nP&D Studio 的 Cyberpunk Neon City\n\n### 相近的旧编号\n\nV3\n\n### 定死的画法\n\n不描边；只有天是渐变，其他全是平涂；越远的楼越接近天的颜色；发光只给霓虹字、灯串和小灯。\n"},{"id":"S8-V1","name":"逐帧手绘 · 抖动线条","series":"S8","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S8-V1.jpg","markdown":"# V1 逐帧手绘 / 抖动线条（Frame-by-Frame / Line Boil）\n\n- 分类：V mg-styles-15 · mg-styles-15\n- 适合：手绘感，温暖\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# 逐帧手绘 / 线条沸腾 Cel Animation / Frame-by-Frame & Line Boil\n\n**原片**：手作 HAND MADE · 1920×1080 · 24 fps · 10 秒 · AI 评审终评 8.07 / 10\n\n**源码**：[demos/05-cel-boil/](../demos/05-cel-boil/)\n\n## 怎么用\n\n1. 新建一个空文件夹，在里面打开一个能写代码、能执行命令的 AI 助手（例如 Claude Code）。本机需要能跑网页渲染（Node + Chrome）和 ffmpeg。\n2. 把下面「提示词」整段复制进去发送。\n3. 想换成自己的内容：改 `Creative seed` 里的故事、品牌名、文案和配色，或者改 `Output` 里的尺寸（比如竖屏 1080×1920）。不要删 `Signature features` 那一段，它是这种风格被一眼认出来的依据。\n4. 想再提高：成片出来后，另开一个 AI 用 [rubric.md](../rubric.md) 评审，把评审意见贴回给制作的 AI 再改一轮。原片就是这样改了两轮。\n\n## 提示词\n\n```text\nYou are the director, motion designer, engineer and sound designer of ONE 10-second motion-design film.\nThe goal is the most classic, yet most stunning form of this style — award-shortlist / high-end commercial quality. A clean,\ntemplate-looking result is a fail. Benchmarks: Buck, ManvsMachine, Ordinary Folk, Giant Ant, Territory Studio, Apple keynote\nmotion, Pentagram motion identities, top Behance/Motionographer features, top 抖音/B站 designer accounts.\n\n# Style\n逐帧手绘 / 线条沸腾 Cel Animation / Frame-by-Frame & Line Boil\n\n# Output (fixed)\n- 1920×1080, 24 fps, exactly 10.000 s (240 frames), H.264 MP4 with stereo AAC audio (48 kHz), mastered to −14 LUFS\n  integrated, true peak ≤ −1 dBTP. Final file: video.mp4.\n\n# Suggested technical route\nHTML Canvas2D: custom variable-width brush renderer (centreline + pressure → outline polygon), boil = 3–4 jitter variants cycled every 2 frames; paper texture multiply\n(You may choose a better route if it clearly raises quality; say why.)\n\n# Creative seed\n「手作魔法」Hand-made frame-by-frame magic, Buck/Giant-Ant hybrid (clean shapes + hand-drawn cel FX), animated on 2s at 24 fps.\n- Warm paper ground; ink linework that BOILS (lines redrawn with slight variation every 2 frames); fills slightly off-register like hand colouring.\n- Story: a matchstick strikes (SMEAR frame, on 1s for the fast action) → a flame spirit character jumps out, anticipation + squash, dances (on 2s) → it bursts into cel FX: smoke puffs curling, sparks, star bursts, speed lines → the FX re-form into hand-lettered title (e.g. \"HAND MADE\" or 手作) that keeps boiling on a 1s hold (on 3s for the hold).\n- Palette 3–4 colours: ink black, tomato red, sunny yellow, cream paper; paper grain multiply 10–15%.\nSound: playful jazzy pizzicato/xylophone bed + cartoon SFX: strike, fwoosh, pop, poof, sparkle — synced to the frame.\n\nYou are the director: you may change the concept, copy, story beats and brand names if you find a stronger idea, but the result must remain the CANONICAL, instantly-recognisable form of this style and must hit its signature features below.\n\n# Signature features and key techniques (the result is judged against these)\n手绘逐帧或在矢量动画上叠加逐帧质感，标志性特征是 line boil——线条每隔几帧轻微抖动，仿佛画面在'呼吸'。Giant Ant、Buck 等工作室大量使用 cel 元素（烟、水花、速度线）点缀主动画，反数字精致感的核心手段。\n视觉特征 线条持续微抖(boil), 一拍二/一拍三节奏, smear拖影帧, 手绘烟火水花点缀, 纸纹底\n关键技法 打帧节奏：24fps 下'一拍二'（实际12fps）为主，快动作切'一拍一'，静止镜头'一拍三'\nline boil 伪造法：AE 用 Turbulent Displace（数量2~5、大小50）+ 每 2 帧随机化 evolution + Posterize Time 12fps；真 boil 则画 3~4 张微差线稿循环\nsmear frame：快速位移的中间帧把物体拉长 150%~300% 只保留 1 帧\ncel FX 点缀层：烟/水花/闪电用纯手绘逐帧叠在矢量主体上（Buck 式 hybrid）\n整体叠纸纹（multiply 10~15%）统一手作气质\n\n# How to work\n1. Treatment. Write down one clear idea: a hook in the first 0.5 s (never open on more than 0.3 s of empty or black), an\n   escalation, one unmistakable hero moment at ~60–75 % of the runtime, and a composed end frame held ~0.8–1.2 s with living\n   micro-motion. Map every signature feature above to a moment. Keep one cue sheet (beats and hit times) that both the\n   picture and the sound read from.\n2. Key frames before motion. Build the look, render stills at 6–10 key times and actually look at them. Each still should be\n   poster-worthy: composition, hierarchy, negative space, type set properly (kerning, line-height, weight contrast, CJK\n   punctuation, ~5 % safe margins). Iterate until nothing looks default, generic, cramped or \"AI-template\".\n3. Motion. Build the choreography and preview at low resolution. Step through the fastest moves frame by frame: spacing,\n   easing, arcs, overlap, anticipation and follow-through, motion blur. No unintended dead spans; the energy follows the music.\n4. Sound. Write genre-correct music and sound design locked to the cue sheet. Check that visual hits land on audio onsets.\n   Master to the output spec.\n5. Final. Render at full resolution (motion blur where apt), then check: exactly 10.00 s, resolution, fps, audio present and at\n   the right loudness, no black frames, no fallback fonts or tofu, no clipped elements at the frame edges, no shimmer on thin\n   lines, no gradient banding. Fix and repeat until you would submit it to a festival.\n6. Write an honest self-critique: what is strongest, what is weakest, what you would do with more time.\n\n# Rules\n- Rendering must be deterministic: every frame is a pure function of time t. Seeded randomness only; physics and particles\n  precomputed or closed-form; no real-time clocks; no state-triggered CSS transitions. A reliable route: a web page that can\n  draw any time t on request, captured frame by frame with a headless browser and encoded with ffmpeg (or a Blender script\n  that renders a PNG sequence).\n- Assets: only CC0 / public domain / OFL / Apache / free-for-commercial-use. Record each source and licence in CREDITS.md.\n  No real brand trademarks; invent brand names.\n- On-screen text must be correct and natural: idiomatic Chinese, proper CJK punctuation, no typos.\n\n# Craft checklist (what a jury looks for)\n- Style authenticity: an expert names the style in one second; every signature technique is present and executed correctly.\n- Motion craft: purposeful easing (no linear unless mechanical by design), overlap and stagger, anticipation and\n  follow-through, arcs, squash and stretch where the style allows, consistent physics, motion blur where apt; transitions\n  carried by elements, not crossfades.\n- Rhythm: hits locked to the music; contrast between busy and calm; no monotony.\n- Design: strong composition, grid, typographic hierarchy, controlled palette; texture and finishing (grain, glow, vignette)\n  only where the style wants it; nothing looks accidental.\n- Technical polish: no jitter (unless intended), no popping, no aliasing, no banding, no fallback fonts, no half-loaded images.\n- Sound: genre-correct, musical, synced, mastered.\n\n# Before you deliver, score yourself honestly\n1–10 on style fidelity, concept wow, motion craft, design & typography, finish & texture, sound & sync, technical\n(6 = template-level, 8 = high-end agency, 9 = award shortlist). Aim for 9; fix whatever scores lowest first.\n\n# Deliver\nvideo.mp4, plus a short report: the concept in two sentences; a beat sheet with timecodes; how each signature feature is\nrealised; the tech route; check numbers (duration, resolution, fps, loudness, true peak); your honest top-3 weaknesses.\n```\n"},{"id":"S8-V2","name":"等距 2.5D","series":"S8","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S8-V2.jpg","markdown":"# V2 等距 2.5D（Isometric 2.5D）\n\n- 分类：V mg-styles-15 · mg-styles-15\n- 适合：一个小世界慢慢长出来\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# 等轴 2.5D Isometric / 2.5D\n\n**原片**：ISOPOLIS「让城市自己生长」 · 1920×1080 · 30 fps · 10 秒 · AI 评审终评 8.00 / 10\n\n**源码**：[demos/03-isometric/](../demos/03-isometric/)\n\n## 怎么用\n\n1. 新建一个空文件夹，在里面打开一个能写代码、能执行命令的 AI 助手（例如 Claude Code）。本机需要能跑网页渲染（Node + Chrome）和 ffmpeg。\n2. 把下面「提示词」整段复制进去发送。\n3. 想换成自己的内容：改 `Creative seed` 里的故事、品牌名、文案和配色，或者改 `Output` 里的尺寸（比如竖屏 1080×1920）。不要删 `Signature features` 那一段，它是这种风格被一眼认出来的依据。\n4. 想再提高：成片出来后，另开一个 AI 用 [rubric.md](../rubric.md) 评审，把评审意见贴回给制作的 AI 再改一轮。原片就是这样改了两轮。\n\n## 提示词\n\n```text\nYou are the director, motion designer, engineer and sound designer of ONE 10-second motion-design film.\nThe goal is the most classic, yet most stunning form of this style — award-shortlist / high-end commercial quality. A clean,\ntemplate-looking result is a fail. Benchmarks: Buck, ManvsMachine, Ordinary Folk, Giant Ant, Territory Studio, Apple keynote\nmotion, Pentagram motion identities, top Behance/Motionographer features, top 抖音/B站 designer accounts.\n\n# Style\n等轴 2.5D Isometric / 2.5D\n\n# Output (fixed)\n- 1920×1080, 30 fps, exactly 10.000 s (300 frames), H.264 MP4 with stereo AAC audio (48 kHz), mastered to −14 LUFS\n  integrated, true peak ≤ −1 dBTP. Final file: video.mp4.\n\n# Suggested technical route\nHTML: Three.js OrthographicCamera at true isometric angle + soft shadows + AO (three/addons postprocessing), or SVG with SSR matrix faces\n(You may choose a better route if it clearly raises quality; say why.)\n\n# Creative seed\n「微缩智慧城」An isometric miniature city / data campus that builds itself — the School-of-Motion isometric mograph showpiece.\n- True isometric camera (orthographic, 35.264° elevation, 45° azimuth), no perspective, pastel studio palette (lavender ground, mint, peach, sky blue, white) with soft shadows + ambient occlusion so it reads like a premium Dribbble/Behance hero.\n- 0–2s: ground tiles flip/drop in as a wave from the centre (tiny bounce each);\n- 2–5s: buildings GROW — base lands, walls scaleY from 0 with overshoot, roof caps 2–3 frames later; trees pop; windows light up in sequence;\n- 5–8s: life — cars loop along roads, a little train, drones carrying packets, glowing data streams pulse between buildings, a wind turbine spins; camera does parallel slides (no rotation) with 1 : 0.8 : 0.6 parallax layers (foreground clouds);\n- 8–10s: pull back to reveal the whole island floating in a pastel void; title card set ON the isometric plane (text skewed into iso space) e.g. \"SMART CITY OS\".\nSound: bright plucky tech melody (FM marimba/pluck), soft clicks/pops for every tile/building landing, airy whoosh on the pull-back.\n\nYou are the director: you may change the concept, copy, story beats and brand names if you find a stronger idea, but the result must remain the CANONICAL, instantly-recognisable form of this style and must hit its signature features below.\n\n# Signature features and key techniques (the result is judged against these)\n以 30° 等轴测投影展示微缩世界：小城市、办公室切片、数据机房，无灭点、处处等比。信息密度高又不失可爱，是科技公司架构图动画和 App 功能演示的标配。School of Motion 有专门的 isometric mograph 教程系列。\n视觉特征 30°等角投影, 无透视灭点, 微缩场景, 楼房生长动画, 平行滑轨式运镜\n关键技法 SSR 公式造等轴面：Scale 纵向 86.6% → Shear/skew ±30° → Rotate ∓30°，三个面分别做后拼合\n假 3D 靠图层排序：Z 序=画面 y 坐标，物体沿等轴网格移动时保持 2:1 像素斜率\n楼房'生长'：底面先落位，立面用 scaleY 从 0 拉起 + 顶面延迟 2~3 帧盖上\n运镜是整组平移（无旋转），配合前中后景 1:0.8:0.6 的视差速度\nAE 里可用真 3D 图层+正交相机（无透视）偷懒，Motion Design School 的 Isometric Camera 技巧即此路\n\n# How to work\n1. Treatment. Write down one clear idea: a hook in the first 0.5 s (never open on more than 0.3 s of empty or black), an\n   escalation, one unmistakable hero moment at ~60–75 % of the runtime, and a composed end frame held ~0.8–1.2 s with living\n   micro-motion. Map every signature feature above to a moment. Keep one cue sheet (beats and hit times) that both the\n   picture and the sound read from.\n2. Key frames before motion. Build the look, render stills at 6–10 key times and actually look at them. Each still should be\n   poster-worthy: composition, hierarchy, negative space, type set properly (kerning, line-height, weight contrast, CJK\n   punctuation, ~5 % safe margins). Iterate until nothing looks default, generic, cramped or \"AI-template\".\n3. Motion. Build the choreography and preview at low resolution. Step through the fastest moves frame by frame: spacing,\n   easing, arcs, overlap, anticipation and follow-through, motion blur. No unintended dead spans; the energy follows the music.\n4. Sound. Write genre-correct music and sound design locked to the cue sheet. Check that visual hits land on audio onsets.\n   Master to the output spec.\n5. Final. Render at full resolution (motion blur where apt), then check: exactly 10.00 s, resolution, fps, audio present and at\n   the right loudness, no black frames, no fallback fonts or tofu, no clipped elements at the frame edges, no shimmer on thin\n   lines, no gradient banding. Fix and repeat until you would submit it to a festival.\n6. Write an honest self-critique: what is strongest, what is weakest, what you would do with more time.\n\n# Rules\n- Rendering must be deterministic: every frame is a pure function of time t. Seeded randomness only; physics and particles\n  precomputed or closed-form; no real-time clocks; no state-triggered CSS transitions. A reliable route: a web page that can\n  draw any time t on request, captured frame by frame with a headless browser and encoded with ffmpeg (or a Blender script\n  that renders a PNG sequence).\n- Assets: only CC0 / public domain / OFL / Apache / free-for-commercial-use. Record each source and licence in CREDITS.md.\n  No real brand trademarks; invent brand names.\n- On-screen text must be correct and natural: idiomatic Chinese, proper CJK punctuation, no typos.\n\n# Craft checklist (what a jury looks for)\n- Style authenticity: an expert names the style in one second; every signature technique is present and executed correctly.\n- Motion craft: purposeful easing (no linear unless mechanical by design), overlap and stagger, anticipation and\n  follow-through, arcs, squash and stretch where the style allows, consistent physics, motion blur where apt; transitions\n  carried by elements, not crossfades.\n- Rhythm: hits locked to the music; contrast between busy and calm; no monotony.\n- Design: strong composition, grid, typographic hierarchy, controlled palette; texture and finishing (grain, glow, vignette)\n  only where the style wants it; nothing looks accidental.\n- Technical polish: no jitter (unless intended), no popping, no aliasing, no banding, no fallback fonts, no half-loaded images.\n- Sound: genre-correct, musical, synced, mastered.\n\n# Before you deliver, score yourself honestly\n1–10 on style fidelity, concept wow, motion craft, design & typography, finish & texture, sound & sync, technical\n(6 = template-level, 8 = high-end agency, 9 = award shortlist). Aim for 9; fix whatever scores lowest first.\n\n# Deliver\nvideo.mp4, plus a short report: the concept in two sentences; a beat sheet with timecodes; how each signature feature is\nrealised; the tech route; check numbers (duration, resolution, fps, loudness, true peak); your honest top-3 weaknesses.\n```\n"},{"id":"S8-V3","name":"扁平矢量","series":"S8","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S8-V3.jpg","markdown":"# V3 扁平矢量（Flat Vector）\n\n- 分类：V mg-styles-15 · mg-styles-15\n- 适合：扁平的\"我\"（K1）\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# 扁平矢量动画 Flat / Vector 2D Motion Graphics\n\n**原片**：popwise「One Dot」一个点的一镜到底 · 1920×1080 · 30 fps · 10 秒 · AI 评审终评 7.64 / 10\n\n**源码**：[demos/01-flat-vector/](../demos/01-flat-vector/)\n\n## 怎么用\n\n1. 新建一个空文件夹，在里面打开一个能写代码、能执行命令的 AI 助手（例如 Claude Code）。本机需要能跑网页渲染（Node + Chrome）和 ffmpeg。\n2. 把下面「提示词」整段复制进去发送。\n3. 想换成自己的内容：改 `Creative seed` 里的故事、品牌名、文案和配色，或者改 `Output` 里的尺寸（比如竖屏 1080×1920）。不要删 `Signature features` 那一段，它是这种风格被一眼认出来的依据。\n4. 想再提高：成片出来后，另开一个 AI 用 [rubric.md](../rubric.md) 评审，把评审意见贴回给制作的 AI 再改一轮。原片就是这样改了两轮。\n\n## 提示词\n\n```text\nYou are the director, motion designer, engineer and sound designer of ONE 10-second motion-design film.\nThe goal is the most classic, yet most stunning form of this style — award-shortlist / high-end commercial quality. A clean,\ntemplate-looking result is a fail. Benchmarks: Buck, ManvsMachine, Ordinary Folk, Giant Ant, Territory Studio, Apple keynote\nmotion, Pentagram motion identities, top Behance/Motionographer features, top 抖音/B站 designer accounts.\n\n# Style\n扁平矢量动画 Flat / Vector 2D Motion Graphics\n\n# Output (fixed)\n- 1920×1080, 30 fps, exactly 10.000 s (300 frames), H.264 MP4 with stereo AAC audio (48 kHz), mastered to −14 LUFS\n  integrated, true peak ≤ −1 dBTP. Final file: video.mp4.\n\n# Suggested technical route\nHTML: SVG + GSAP (CustomEase/CustomBounce/MorphSVG); motion blur mb=4 for fast moves\n(You may choose a better route if it clearly raises quality; say why.)\n\n# Creative seed\n「一镜到底的扁平世界」One-continuous-shot brand explainer, the canonical Motion-Ocean/Google-explainer form, pushed to showreel polish.\n- 0.0–1.2s HOOK: empty bold color field; a single saturated circle drops in with the three-phase anticipation → stretch → squash → overshoot → settle. Immediately readable craft.\n- 1.2–3.0s: the circle becomes the sun; a flat city pops up building-by-building with 2–4 frame stagger (each with its own mini overshoot), clouds slide, birds flap.\n- 3.0–5.5s: camera pushes into one window → a geometric character at a desk (limb rig with anchor rotations, head bob, blink) reacts; phone pings.\n- 5.5–8.0s: the notification bubble expands to fill the frame as a SHAPE WIPE transition; inside, icons burst in a choreographed rhythm (bar chart grows, check mark draws, heart pops, coin flips).\n- 8.0–10.0s: element-driven transition — everything collapses/flies into a logo lockup for an invented brand (e.g. \"Popwise\") + tagline, elastic settle, 1s hold with micro-motion.\nRules: one hero moves at a time; squash&stretch ~10%; secondary action & follow-through; element-driven transitions (an object flies out and carries the cut); strictly flat (no gradients), bold 5–6 color palette (e.g. ultramarine #2B2BFF, coral #FF5A4E, sunflower #FFC62B, mint #2EE6A8, cream #FFF6E9, ink #151433).\nSound: upbeat future-bass/pop ~120 BPM; pop/boing/whoosh on each entry, synced to the frame.\n\nYou are the director: you may change the concept, copy, story beats and brand names if you find a stronger idea, but the result must remain the CANONICAL, instantly-recognisable form of this style and must hit its signature features below.\n\n# Signature features and key techniques (the result is judged against these)\nMG 动画最主流的基本盘风格，源自 Google Material/企业解释视频（explainer）传统。纯色几何化的人物与场景、无描边或极简描边、大色块撞色，一切元素都是矢量形状。常见于 SaaS 产品宣传、科普解说、企业年报视频。\n视觉特征 纯色大色块, 几何化角色, 无渐变或极少渐变, 干净留白, 高饱和品牌色板, 元素弹性入退场\n关键技法 AE Shape Layer + 父子级 null 控制层级，人物四肢用锚点旋转做 rig\n弹性缓动是灵魂：overshoot 用 cubic-bezier(0.34,1.56,0.64,1) 或 AE 里 70~85% influence 的贝塞尔手柄；入场'预备-冲出-回弹'三段式\n元素错峰入场（stagger 2~4 帧），同一时刻只让一个主体动\n30fps 居多；位移动画带 10% 左右 squash&stretch 增加弹性\n转场常用色块擦除（shape wipe）或元素飞出带动整场切换\n\n# How to work\n1. Treatment. Write down one clear idea: a hook in the first 0.5 s (never open on more than 0.3 s of empty or black), an\n   escalation, one unmistakable hero moment at ~60–75 % of the runtime, and a composed end frame held ~0.8–1.2 s with living\n   micro-motion. Map every signature feature above to a moment. Keep one cue sheet (beats and hit times) that both the\n   picture and the sound read from.\n2. Key frames before motion. Build the look, render stills at 6–10 key times and actually look at them. Each still should be\n   poster-worthy: composition, hierarchy, negative space, type set properly (kerning, line-height, weight contrast, CJK\n   punctuation, ~5 % safe margins). Iterate until nothing looks default, generic, cramped or \"AI-template\".\n3. Motion. Build the choreography and preview at low resolution. Step through the fastest moves frame by frame: spacing,\n   easing, arcs, overlap, anticipation and follow-through, motion blur. No unintended dead spans; the energy follows the music.\n4. Sound. Write genre-correct music and sound design locked to the cue sheet. Check that visual hits land on audio onsets.\n   Master to the output spec.\n5. Final. Render at full resolution (motion blur where apt), then check: exactly 10.00 s, resolution, fps, audio present and at\n   the right loudness, no black frames, no fallback fonts or tofu, no clipped elements at the frame edges, no shimmer on thin\n   lines, no gradient banding. Fix and repeat until you would submit it to a festival.\n6. Write an honest self-critique: what is strongest, what is weakest, what you would do with more time.\n\n# Rules\n- Rendering must be deterministic: every frame is a pure function of time t. Seeded randomness only; physics and particles\n  precomputed or closed-form; no real-time clocks; no state-triggered CSS transitions. A reliable route: a web page that can\n  draw any time t on request, captured frame by frame with a headless browser and encoded with ffmpeg (or a Blender script\n  that renders a PNG sequence).\n- Assets: only CC0 / public domain / OFL / Apache / free-for-commercial-use. Record each source and licence in CREDITS.md.\n  No real brand trademarks; invent brand names.\n- On-screen text must be correct and natural: idiomatic Chinese, proper CJK punctuation, no typos.\n\n# Craft checklist (what a jury looks for)\n- Style authenticity: an expert names the style in one second; every signature technique is present and executed correctly.\n- Motion craft: purposeful easing (no linear unless mechanical by design), overlap and stagger, anticipation and\n  follow-through, arcs, squash and stretch where the style allows, consistent physics, motion blur where apt; transitions\n  carried by elements, not crossfades.\n- Rhythm: hits locked to the music; contrast between busy and calm; no monotony.\n- Design: strong composition, grid, typographic hierarchy, controlled palette; texture and finishing (grain, glow, vignette)\n  only where the style wants it; nothing looks accidental.\n- Technical polish: no jitter (unless intended), no popping, no aliasing, no banding, no fallback fonts, no half-loaded images.\n- Sound: genre-correct, musical, synced, mastered.\n\n# Before you deliver, score yourself honestly\n1–10 on style fidelity, concept wow, motion craft, design & typography, finish & texture, sound & sync, technical\n(6 = template-level, 8 = high-end agency, 9 = award shortlist). Aim for 9; fix whatever scores lowest first.\n\n# Deliver\nvideo.mp4, plus a short report: the concept in two sentences; a beat sheet with timecodes; how each signature feature is\nrealised; the tech route; check numbers (duration, resolution, fps, loudness, true peak); your honest top-3 weaknesses.\n```\n"},{"id":"S8-V4","name":"线条画","series":"S8","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S8-V4.jpg","markdown":"# V4 线条画（Line Art）\n\n- 分类：V mg-styles-15 · mg-styles-15\n- 适合：一条线画下去\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# 线条动画 Line Art / Line Animation\n\n**原片**：ATELIER LINEA「一笔画 One Line」 · 1920×1080 · 30 fps · 10 秒 · AI 评审终评 7.64 / 10\n\n**源码**：[demos/02-line-art/](../demos/02-line-art/)\n\n## 怎么用\n\n1. 新建一个空文件夹，在里面打开一个能写代码、能执行命令的 AI 助手（例如 Claude Code）。本机需要能跑网页渲染（Node + Chrome）和 ffmpeg。\n2. 把下面「提示词」整段复制进去发送。\n3. 想换成自己的内容：改 `Creative seed` 里的故事、品牌名、文案和配色，或者改 `Output` 里的尺寸（比如竖屏 1080×1920）。不要删 `Signature features` 那一段，它是这种风格被一眼认出来的依据。\n4. 想再提高：成片出来后，另开一个 AI 用 [rubric.md](../rubric.md) 评审，把评审意见贴回给制作的 AI 再改一轮。原片就是这样改了两轮。\n\n## 提示词\n\n```text\nYou are the director, motion designer, engineer and sound designer of ONE 10-second motion-design film.\nThe goal is the most classic, yet most stunning form of this style — award-shortlist / high-end commercial quality. A clean,\ntemplate-looking result is a fail. Benchmarks: Buck, ManvsMachine, Ordinary Folk, Giant Ant, Territory Studio, Apple keynote\nmotion, Pentagram motion identities, top Behance/Motionographer features, top 抖音/B站 designer accounts.\n\n# Style\n线条动画 Line Art / Line Animation\n\n# Output (fixed)\n- 1920×1080, 30 fps, exactly 10.000 s (300 frames), H.264 MP4 with stereo AAC audio (48 kHz), mastered to −14 LUFS\n  integrated, true peak ≤ −1 dBTP. Final file: video.mp4.\n\n# Suggested technical route\nHTML: SVG paths + stroke-dashoffset (getTotalLength) + GSAP; a virtual camera that follows the pen tip\n(You may choose a better route if it clearly raises quality; say why.)\n\n# Creative seed\n「一笔画」One-line narrative for a luxury / architecture brand — the whole film is ONE continuous line.\n- Deep midnight ground (#0B1320 or near-black) + a single champagne-gold line (#E9D7A5), constant ~3px, round caps, a softly glowing pen tip that leads the draw.\n- Story (continuous path, the tail of each figure is the start of the next): a seed → sprouts into a sapling → branches become the structural lines of a building → the building outline extends into a skyline/horizon → the horizon line lifts and loops into a monogram logo → LINE-TO-FILL moment (logo fills with gold from its anchor) → thin letter-spaced serif wordmark (e.g. \"ATELIER LINEA\") fades up.\n- Draw speed eases into corners (cubic-bezier(0.65,0,0.35,1)); the virtual camera drifts/pushes to keep the tip in frame, then dollies out at the end to reveal the whole drawing composed as a poster; older line segments dim to ~35% as the camera moves on.\nSound: minimal felt piano + soft string swell; a pen-on-paper whisper whose loudness follows tip speed; delicate chime on the fill.\n\nYou are the director: you may change the concept, copy, story beats and brand names if you find a stronger idea, but the result must remain the CANONICAL, instantly-recognisable form of this style and must hit its signature features below.\n\n# Signature features and key techniques (the result is judged against these)\n以单色细线为唯一造型语言，线条自我描绘（draw-on）、延伸、转折并变形为下一个图形，一笔连成整支片子。极简高级感强，常见于奢侈品、金融、建筑类品牌片和 logo 动画。\n视觉特征 等宽细线, 单色或双色, 大量负空间, 线条连续生长, 线面转换瞬间\n关键技法 核心是 stroke 描绘：AE 用 Trim Paths 0→100%，Web 端用 SVG stroke-dasharray/dashoffset\n线宽全片恒定（2~4px），转角处让描绘速度略减速，用 easeInOut cubic-bezier(0.65,0,0.35,1)\n'一笔画'叙事：上一图形的尾线即下一图形的起线，路径提前在 Illustrator 里连好\n线转面：描完轮廓后用同路径的 fill 从锚点扩展填充\n配合微小的端点圆头（round cap）与路径抖动可增加手绘感\n\n# How to work\n1. Treatment. Write down one clear idea: a hook in the first 0.5 s (never open on more than 0.3 s of empty or black), an\n   escalation, one unmistakable hero moment at ~60–75 % of the runtime, and a composed end frame held ~0.8–1.2 s with living\n   micro-motion. Map every signature feature above to a moment. Keep one cue sheet (beats and hit times) that both the\n   picture and the sound read from.\n2. Key frames before motion. Build the look, render stills at 6–10 key times and actually look at them. Each still should be\n   poster-worthy: composition, hierarchy, negative space, type set properly (kerning, line-height, weight contrast, CJK\n   punctuation, ~5 % safe margins). Iterate until nothing looks default, generic, cramped or \"AI-template\".\n3. Motion. Build the choreography and preview at low resolution. Step through the fastest moves frame by frame: spacing,\n   easing, arcs, overlap, anticipation and follow-through, motion blur. No unintended dead spans; the energy follows the music.\n4. Sound. Write genre-correct music and sound design locked to the cue sheet. Check that visual hits land on audio onsets.\n   Master to the output spec.\n5. Final. Render at full resolution (motion blur where apt), then check: exactly 10.00 s, resolution, fps, audio present and at\n   the right loudness, no black frames, no fallback fonts or tofu, no clipped elements at the frame edges, no shimmer on thin\n   lines, no gradient banding. Fix and repeat until you would submit it to a festival.\n6. Write an honest self-critique: what is strongest, what is weakest, what you would do with more time.\n\n# Rules\n- Rendering must be deterministic: every frame is a pure function of time t. Seeded randomness only; physics and particles\n  precomputed or closed-form; no real-time clocks; no state-triggered CSS transitions. A reliable route: a web page that can\n  draw any time t on request, captured frame by frame with a headless browser and encoded with ffmpeg (or a Blender script\n  that renders a PNG sequence).\n- Assets: only CC0 / public domain / OFL / Apache / free-for-commercial-use. Record each source and licence in CREDITS.md.\n  No real brand trademarks; invent brand names.\n- On-screen text must be correct and natural: idiomatic Chinese, proper CJK punctuation, no typos.\n\n# Craft checklist (what a jury looks for)\n- Style authenticity: an expert names the style in one second; every signature technique is present and executed correctly.\n- Motion craft: purposeful easing (no linear unless mechanical by design), overlap and stagger, anticipation and\n  follow-through, arcs, squash and stretch where the style allows, consistent physics, motion blur where apt; transitions\n  carried by elements, not crossfades.\n- Rhythm: hits locked to the music; contrast between busy and calm; no monotony.\n- Design: strong composition, grid, typographic hierarchy, controlled palette; texture and finishing (grain, glow, vignette)\n  only where the style wants it; nothing looks accidental.\n- Technical polish: no jitter (unless intended), no popping, no aliasing, no banding, no fallback fonts, no half-loaded images.\n- Sound: genre-correct, musical, synced, mastered.\n\n# Before you deliver, score yourself honestly\n1–10 on style fidelity, concept wow, motion craft, design & typography, finish & texture, sound & sync, technical\n(6 = template-level, 8 = high-end agency, 9 = award shortlist). Aim for 9; fix whatever scores lowest first.\n\n# Deliver\nvideo.mp4, plus a short report: the concept in two sentences; a beat sheet with timecodes; how each signature feature is\nrealised; the tech route; check numbers (duration, resolution, fps, loudness, true peak); your honest top-3 weaknesses.\n```\n"},{"id":"S8-V5","name":"3D 渲染","series":"S8","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S8-V5.jpg","markdown":"# V5 3D 渲染（3D Render）\n\n- 分类：V mg-styles-15 · mg-styles-15\n- 适合：柔和的 3D，要装 Blender\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# 3D 渲染系（C4D/Blender 质感） 3D Render / CGI Motion Design\n\n**原片**：soft.「Soft Landing 柔软着陆」 · 1920×1080 · 24 fps · 10 秒 · AI 评审终评 7.71 / 10\n\n**源码**：[demos/04-3d-render/](../demos/04-3d-render/)\n\n## 怎么用\n\n1. 新建一个空文件夹，在里面打开一个能写代码、能执行命令的 AI 助手（例如 Claude Code）。本机需要能跑网页渲染（Node + Chrome）和 ffmpeg；这种风格建议的路线要用 Blender。\n2. 把下面「提示词」整段复制进去发送。\n3. 想换成自己的内容：改 `Creative seed` 里的故事、品牌名、文案和配色，或者改 `Output` 里的尺寸（比如竖屏 1080×1920）。不要删 `Signature features` 那一段，它是这种风格被一眼认出来的依据。\n4. 想再提高：成片出来后，另开一个 AI 用 [rubric.md](../rubric.md) 评审，把评审意见贴回给制作的 AI 再改一轮。原片就是这样改了两轮。\n\n## 提示词\n\n```text\nYou are the director, motion designer, engineer and sound designer of ONE 10-second motion-design film.\nThe goal is the most classic, yet most stunning form of this style — award-shortlist / high-end commercial quality. A clean,\ntemplate-looking result is a fail. Benchmarks: Buck, ManvsMachine, Ordinary Folk, Giant Ant, Territory Studio, Apple keynote\nmotion, Pentagram motion identities, top Behance/Motionographer features, top 抖音/B站 designer accounts.\n\n# Style\n3D 渲染系（C4D/Blender 质感） 3D Render / CGI Motion Design\n\n# Output (fixed)\n- 1920×1080, 24 fps, exactly 10.000 s (240 frames), H.264 MP4 with stereo AAC audio (48 kHz), mastered to −14 LUFS\n  integrated, true peak ≤ −1 dBTP. Final file: video.mp4.\n\n# Suggested technical route\nBlender 5.1.2 CLI (bpy script → Cycles on Metal GPU with OIDN denoise, or EEVEE if it holds quality) → PNG sequence → ffmpeg; optional HTML overlay pass for typography\n(You may choose a better route if it clearly raises quality; say why.)\n\n# Creative seed\n「Soft & Satisfying」The C4D/Octane pastel product-ident look (Maxon demo-reel vibe): soft candy materials, cloner arrays, physics-feel motion.\n- Pastel studio: infinite cyc backdrop, big soft area lights + HDRI (see /assets/hdri), shallow DOF (f/1.8 feel), 24 fps, real motion blur.\n- A MoGraph-style cloner field (geometry-nodes grid of glossy rounded pills/spheres) ripples with an effector wave; a hero object — inflated/bevelled 3D wordmark (e.g. \"SOFT\" or an invented brand) or a glossy jelly logo — drops in, squash-and-stretch jiggle (damped spring), collides with and displaces the cloner field.\n- Material trio: SSS gummy (candy pink / peach), glossy plastic (cream / lilac), frosted glass or chrome accent — with AO, soft reflections, subtle subsurface glow.\n- Camera: long-tail ease (80% of the move in the first 20%), ends on a clean hero composition held ~1s.\nSound: ASMR-satisfying soft thuds, squishes, bubbly pops, airy cinematic pad; hits frame-synced to impacts.\n\nYou are the director: you may change the concept, copy, story beats and brand names if you find a stronger idea, but the result must remain the CANONICAL, instantly-recognisable form of this style and must hit its signature features below.\n\n# Signature features and key techniques (the result is judged against these)\nCinema 4D + Redshift/Octane 或 Blender 渲染的实体质感动画：软胶、玻璃、金属、布料，配合克隆阵列和物理模拟。是高端产品片（手机、饮料、球鞋）和品牌 ident 的主流，Maxon 官方 Demo Reel 就是该风格的年度风向标。\n视觉特征 PBR真实材质, 浅景深, 柔和棚拍布光, 克隆器阵列, 软体碰撞, 糖果色软胶质感\n关键技法 MoGraph 核心：Cloner 克隆器 + Random/Plain Effector 做群体波浪式动画\n质感三件套：SSS 次表面散射（软胶感）+ AO + HDRI 三点布光\n运动多用长尾 ease：位移曲线前 20% 完成 80% 路程，收尾极慢，配 motion blur\n24/25fps 电影帧率 + 浅景深（f/1.8 感）区别于 2D 系的 30fps 利落感\n常见结构：产品为轴心，粒子/流体/布料围绕它做物理模拟编排\n\n# How to work\n1. Treatment. Write down one clear idea: a hook in the first 0.5 s (never open on more than 0.3 s of empty or black), an\n   escalation, one unmistakable hero moment at ~60–75 % of the runtime, and a composed end frame held ~0.8–1.2 s with living\n   micro-motion. Map every signature feature above to a moment. Keep one cue sheet (beats and hit times) that both the\n   picture and the sound read from.\n2. Key frames before motion. Build the look, render stills at 6–10 key times and actually look at them. Each still should be\n   poster-worthy: composition, hierarchy, negative space, type set properly (kerning, line-height, weight contrast, CJK\n   punctuation, ~5 % safe margins). Iterate until nothing looks default, generic, cramped or \"AI-template\".\n3. Motion. Build the choreography and preview at low resolution. Step through the fastest moves frame by frame: spacing,\n   easing, arcs, overlap, anticipation and follow-through, motion blur. No unintended dead spans; the energy follows the music.\n4. Sound. Write genre-correct music and sound design locked to the cue sheet. Check that visual hits land on audio onsets.\n   Master to the output spec.\n5. Final. Render at full resolution (motion blur where apt), then check: exactly 10.00 s, resolution, fps, audio present and at\n   the right loudness, no black frames, no fallback fonts or tofu, no clipped elements at the frame edges, no shimmer on thin\n   lines, no gradient banding. Fix and repeat until you would submit it to a festival.\n6. Write an honest self-critique: what is strongest, what is weakest, what you would do with more time.\n\n# Rules\n- Rendering must be deterministic: every frame is a pure function of time t. Seeded randomness only; physics and particles\n  precomputed or closed-form; no real-time clocks; no state-triggered CSS transitions. A reliable route: a web page that can\n  draw any time t on request, captured frame by frame with a headless browser and encoded with ffmpeg (or a Blender script\n  that renders a PNG sequence).\n- Assets: only CC0 / public domain / OFL / Apache / free-for-commercial-use. Record each source and licence in CREDITS.md.\n  No real brand trademarks; invent brand names.\n- On-screen text must be correct and natural: idiomatic Chinese, proper CJK punctuation, no typos.\n\n# Craft checklist (what a jury looks for)\n- Style authenticity: an expert names the style in one second; every signature technique is present and executed correctly.\n- Motion craft: purposeful easing (no linear unless mechanical by design), overlap and stagger, anticipation and\n  follow-through, arcs, squash and stretch where the style allows, consistent physics, motion blur where apt; transitions\n  carried by elements, not crossfades.\n- Rhythm: hits locked to the music; contrast between busy and calm; no monotony.\n- Design: strong composition, grid, typographic hierarchy, controlled palette; texture and finishing (grain, glow, vignette)\n  only where the style wants it; nothing looks accidental.\n- Technical polish: no jitter (unless intended), no popping, no aliasing, no banding, no fallback fonts, no half-loaded images.\n- Sound: genre-correct, musical, synced, mastered.\n\n# Before you deliver, score yourself honestly\n1–10 on style fidelity, concept wow, motion craft, design & typography, finish & texture, sound & sync, technical\n(6 = template-level, 8 = high-end agency, 9 = award shortlist). Aim for 9; fix whatever scores lowest first.\n\n# Deliver\nvideo.mp4, plus a short report: the concept in two sentences; a beat sheet with timecodes; how each signature feature is\nrealised; the tech route; check numbers (duration, resolution, fps, loudness, true peak); your honest top-3 weaknesses.\n```\n"},{"id":"S8-V6","name":"形变","series":"S8","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S8-V6.jpg","markdown":"# V6 形变（Shape Morph）\n\n- 分类：V mg-styles-15 · mg-styles-15\n- 适合：一个形状接连变成 6–8 个有意义的图标，比如咖啡杯→太阳→海上日落→海鸥→纸飞机→定位针→心，和 M38 一起看\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# 形变动画 Morphing / Shape Morph\n\n**原片**：一形万象 One Shape, Every Story · 1920×1080 · 30 fps · 10 秒 · AI 评审终评 7.64 / 10\n\n**源码**：[demos/08-morph/](../demos/08-morph/)\n\n## 怎么用\n\n1. 新建一个空文件夹，在里面打开一个能写代码、能执行命令的 AI 助手（例如 Claude Code）。本机需要能跑网页渲染（Node + Chrome）和 ffmpeg。\n2. 把下面「提示词」整段复制进去发送。\n3. 想换成自己的内容：改 `Creative seed` 里的故事、品牌名、文案和配色，或者改 `Output` 里的尺寸（比如竖屏 1080×1920）。不要删 `Signature features` 那一段，它是这种风格被一眼认出来的依据。\n4. 想再提高：成片出来后，另开一个 AI 用 [rubric.md](../rubric.md) 评审，把评审意见贴回给制作的 AI 再改一轮。原片就是这样改了两轮。\n\n## 提示词\n\n```text\nYou are the director, motion designer, engineer and sound designer of ONE 10-second motion-design film.\nThe goal is the most classic, yet most stunning form of this style — award-shortlist / high-end commercial quality. A clean,\ntemplate-looking result is a fail. Benchmarks: Buck, ManvsMachine, Ordinary Folk, Giant Ant, Territory Studio, Apple keynote\nmotion, Pentagram motion identities, top Behance/Motionographer features, top 抖音/B站 designer accounts.\n\n# Style\n形变动画 Morphing / Shape Morph\n\n# Output (fixed)\n- 1920×1080, 30 fps, exactly 10.000 s (300 frames), H.264 MP4 with stereo AAC audio (48 kHz), mastered to −14 LUFS\n  integrated, true peak ≤ −1 dBTP. Final file: video.mp4.\n\n# Suggested technical route\nHTML: SVG + flubber or GSAP MorphSVGPlugin (tune shapeIndex), color-field wipes\n(You may choose a better route if it clearly raises quality; say why.)\n\n# Creative seed\n「万物相连」Apple-keynote-grade morph chain: one hero shape continuously becomes 6–8 meaningful icons, on the beat.\n- Chain example: coffee cup → (circle) → sun → sunset over sea → gull → paper plane → location pin → heart → invented brand logo \"morphe\" with wordmark.\n- Every A→B goes through a simple intermediate (circle/capsule) with rotation and 10–15% squash&stretch; sub-parts morph in sync (steam → sun rays → waves).\n- Background color fields change with each morph (clean color-block wipes); ease cubic-bezier(0.7,0,0.3,1), 3-frame cushions at start/end; no knotting/tangling ever (check every morph mid-point stills).\nSound: rhythmic plucky arpeggio; a pitched whoosh per morph landing on the beat.\n\nYou are the director: you may change the concept, copy, story beats and brand names if you find a stronger idea, but the result must remain the CANONICAL, instantly-recognisable form of this style and must hit its signature features below.\n\n# Signature features and key techniques (the result is judged against these)\n一个图形丝滑连续地变成另一个图形：手机变成地图钉、咖啡杯变成落日。是图形叙事（visual storytelling）的核心语法，苹果发布会动效和高端 explainer 里最见功力的部分。\n视觉特征 轮廓连续过渡无跳变, 中介形状桥接, 形变伴随位移旋转, 变形瞬间的挤压拉伸\n关键技法 路径对齐是前提：两形状顶点数一致、首顶点方位对应（AE 里重设 first vertex），否则会打结\n复杂 A→B 不直接变：先收敛为中介简形（圆/胶囊）再展开，'A→圆→B'两段各 8~12 帧\n形变全程叠加 10~15% squash & stretch 与轻微旋转，掩盖插值的机械感\n速度曲线中段最快 cubic-bezier(0.7,0,0.3,1)：起止各留 3 帧缓冲\nWeb 端用 flubber/polymorph 库做最优顶点匹配插值，效果远好于朴素 SMIL\n\n# How to work\n1. Treatment. Write down one clear idea: a hook in the first 0.5 s (never open on more than 0.3 s of empty or black), an\n   escalation, one unmistakable hero moment at ~60–75 % of the runtime, and a composed end frame held ~0.8–1.2 s with living\n   micro-motion. Map every signature feature above to a moment. Keep one cue sheet (beats and hit times) that both the\n   picture and the sound read from.\n2. Key frames before motion. Build the look, render stills at 6–10 key times and actually look at them. Each still should be\n   poster-worthy: composition, hierarchy, negative space, type set properly (kerning, line-height, weight contrast, CJK\n   punctuation, ~5 % safe margins). Iterate until nothing looks default, generic, cramped or \"AI-template\".\n3. Motion. Build the choreography and preview at low resolution. Step through the fastest moves frame by frame: spacing,\n   easing, arcs, overlap, anticipation and follow-through, motion blur. No unintended dead spans; the energy follows the music.\n4. Sound. Write genre-correct music and sound design locked to the cue sheet. Check that visual hits land on audio onsets.\n   Master to the output spec.\n5. Final. Render at full resolution (motion blur where apt), then check: exactly 10.00 s, resolution, fps, audio present and at\n   the right loudness, no black frames, no fallback fonts or tofu, no clipped elements at the frame edges, no shimmer on thin\n   lines, no gradient banding. Fix and repeat until you would submit it to a festival.\n6. Write an honest self-critique: what is strongest, what is weakest, what you would do with more time.\n\n# Rules\n- Rendering must be deterministic: every frame is a pure function of time t. Seeded randomness only; physics and particles\n  precomputed or closed-form; no real-time clocks; no state-triggered CSS transitions. A reliable route: a web page that can\n  draw any time t on request, captured frame by frame with a headless browser and encoded with ffmpeg (or a Blender script\n  that renders a PNG sequence).\n- Assets: only CC0 / public domain / OFL / Apache / free-for-commercial-use. Record each source and licence in CREDITS.md.\n  No real brand trademarks; invent brand names.\n- On-screen text must be correct and natural: idiomatic Chinese, proper CJK punctuation, no typos.\n\n# Craft checklist (what a jury looks for)\n- Style authenticity: an expert names the style in one second; every signature technique is present and executed correctly.\n- Motion craft: purposeful easing (no linear unless mechanical by design), overlap and stagger, anticipation and\n  follow-through, arcs, squash and stretch where the style allows, consistent physics, motion blur where apt; transitions\n  carried by elements, not crossfades.\n- Rhythm: hits locked to the music; contrast between busy and calm; no monotony.\n- Design: strong composition, grid, typographic hierarchy, controlled palette; texture and finishing (grain, glow, vignette)\n  only where the style wants it; nothing looks accidental.\n- Technical polish: no jitter (unless intended), no popping, no aliasing, no banding, no fallback fonts, no half-loaded images.\n- Sound: genre-correct, musical, synced, mastered.\n\n# Before you deliver, score yourself honestly\n1–10 on style fidelity, concept wow, motion craft, design & typography, finish & texture, sound & sync, technical\n(6 = template-level, 8 = high-end agency, 9 = award shortlist). Aim for 9; fix whatever scores lowest first.\n\n# Deliver\nvideo.mp4, plus a short report: the concept in two sentences; a beat sheet with timecodes; how each signature feature is\nrealised; the tech route; check numbers (duration, resolution, fps, loudness, true peak); your honest top-3 weaknesses.\n```\n"},{"id":"S8-V7","name":"贴纸讲解","series":"S8","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S8-V7.jpg","markdown":"# V7 贴纸讲解（Sticker Explainer）\n\n- 分类：V mg-styles-15 · mg-styles-15\n- 适合：轻松的讲解\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# 粗描边贴纸人科普 MG（回形针/林超风格） Bold-outline Sticker Explainer MG (PaperClip / Lin Chao style)\n\n**原片**：一部手机里，藏着多少种元素？ 70/118 · 1920×1080 · 30 fps · 10 秒 · AI 评审终评 7.71 / 10\n\n**源码**：[demos/19-paperclip/](../demos/19-paperclip/)\n\n## 怎么用\n\n1. 新建一个空文件夹，在里面打开一个能写代码、能执行命令的 AI 助手（例如 Claude Code）。本机需要能跑网页渲染（Node + Chrome）和 ffmpeg。\n2. 把下面「提示词」整段复制进去发送。\n3. 想换成自己的内容：改 `Creative seed` 里的故事、品牌名、文案和配色，或者改 `Output` 里的尺寸（比如竖屏 1080×1920）。不要删 `Signature features` 那一段，它是这种风格被一眼认出来的依据。\n4. 想再提高：成片出来后，另开一个 AI 用 [rubric.md](../rubric.md) 评审，把评审意见贴回给制作的 AI 再改一轮。原片就是这样改了两轮。\n\n## 提示词\n\n```text\nYou are the director, motion designer, engineer and sound designer of ONE 10-second motion-design film.\nThe goal is the most classic, yet most stunning form of this style — award-shortlist / high-end commercial quality. A clean,\ntemplate-looking result is a fail. Benchmarks: Buck, ManvsMachine, Ordinary Folk, Giant Ant, Territory Studio, Apple keynote\nmotion, Pentagram motion identities, top Behance/Motionographer features, top 抖音/B站 designer accounts.\n\n# Style\n粗描边贴纸人科普 MG（回形针/林超风格） Bold-outline Sticker Explainer MG (PaperClip / Lin Chao style)\n\n# Output (fixed)\n- 1920×1080, 30 fps, exactly 10.000 s (300 frames), H.264 MP4 with stereo AAC audio (48 kHz), mastered to −14 LUFS\n  integrated, true peak ≤ −1 dBTP. Final file: video.mp4.\n\n# Suggested technical route\nHTML: one huge canvas (e.g. 6000×3000 px) with a camera transform (long eases), SVG charts, photo stickers with 10px white outline\n(You may choose a better route if it clearly raises quality; say why.)\n\n# Creative seed\n「回形针式硬核科普」The PaperClip-style knowledge-video look: sticker-ized photos + flat icons + precise charts on a giant canvas with continuous camera moves.\n- Topic (write your own tight copy), e.g. 「一部手机里，藏着多少种元素？」.\n- Calm blue-grey/off-white canvas (#E9EEF2 + slate), 2–3 colours + one accent (e.g. #FF5A36); equal-weight sans typography, strict alignment.\n- One continuous camera path: sticker phone photo (white 10px outline, soft shadow) → exploded-parts stickers with leader-line labels → periodic-table grid where element tiles light up → bar chart grows with count-up \"70+\" → world map with supply routes drawing → pull out to a bold typographic title card in the 回形针 manner (heavy sans, huge number, small caption).\n- Every beat introduces a new visual element; charts are exact (bars to scale, numbers consistent).\nSound: clean tech-explainer bed, UI clicks, ticking during count-up, soft whoosh on camera moves.\n\nYou are the director: you may change the concept, copy, story beats and brand names if you find a stronger idea, but the result must remain the CANONICAL, instantly-recognisable form of this style and must hit its signature features below.\n\n# Signature features and key techniques (the result is judged against these)\n回形针 PaperClip 带火的知识区标准包装：实拍图片抠图后加粗白描边变成『贴纸』，与扁平图标、数据图表一起铺在大画布上，镜头平移缩放串联信息点；林超等财经/跨学科 UP 主将其与手写板书、公式卡片结合。至今仍是硬核科普、财经知识区、企业宣传片的主流范式。\n视觉特征 照片贴纸化（粗白描边）, 蓝灰/米白冷静底色, 扁平图标+精确数据图表, 大画布镜头平移缩放, 信息密度极高的节拍化叙事\n关键技法 素材照片抠图 + 8~12px 白描边统一质感，消除图片来源差异\n超宽大画布布局，摄像机层做平移/缩放（ease 长曲线），一镜串多个信息点\n图表动效：柱状图生长、数字滚动计数、路径描边生长\n解说词逐句驱动画面元素入场，语速快、每句必有新视觉元素\n克制配色（2~3 色）+ 等线字体，保持『严肃感』\n\n# How to work\n1. Treatment. Write down one clear idea: a hook in the first 0.5 s (never open on more than 0.3 s of empty or black), an\n   escalation, one unmistakable hero moment at ~60–75 % of the runtime, and a composed end frame held ~0.8–1.2 s with living\n   micro-motion. Map every signature feature above to a moment. Keep one cue sheet (beats and hit times) that both the\n   picture and the sound read from.\n2. Key frames before motion. Build the look, render stills at 6–10 key times and actually look at them. Each still should be\n   poster-worthy: composition, hierarchy, negative space, type set properly (kerning, line-height, weight contrast, CJK\n   punctuation, ~5 % safe margins). Iterate until nothing looks default, generic, cramped or \"AI-template\".\n3. Motion. Build the choreography and preview at low resolution. Step through the fastest moves frame by frame: spacing,\n   easing, arcs, overlap, anticipation and follow-through, motion blur. No unintended dead spans; the energy follows the music.\n4. Sound. Write genre-correct music and sound design locked to the cue sheet. Check that visual hits land on audio onsets.\n   Master to the output spec.\n5. Final. Render at full resolution (motion blur where apt), then check: exactly 10.00 s, resolution, fps, audio present and at\n   the right loudness, no black frames, no fallback fonts or tofu, no clipped elements at the frame edges, no shimmer on thin\n   lines, no gradient banding. Fix and repeat until you would submit it to a festival.\n6. Write an honest self-critique: what is strongest, what is weakest, what you would do with more time.\n\n# Rules\n- Rendering must be deterministic: every frame is a pure function of time t. Seeded randomness only; physics and particles\n  precomputed or closed-form; no real-time clocks; no state-triggered CSS transitions. A reliable route: a web page that can\n  draw any time t on request, captured frame by frame with a headless browser and encoded with ffmpeg (or a Blender script\n  that renders a PNG sequence).\n- Assets: only CC0 / public domain / OFL / Apache / free-for-commercial-use. Record each source and licence in CREDITS.md.\n  No real brand trademarks; invent brand names.\n- On-screen text must be correct and natural: idiomatic Chinese, proper CJK punctuation, no typos.\n\n# Craft checklist (what a jury looks for)\n- Style authenticity: an expert names the style in one second; every signature technique is present and executed correctly.\n- Motion craft: purposeful easing (no linear unless mechanical by design), overlap and stagger, anticipation and\n  follow-through, arcs, squash and stretch where the style allows, consistent physics, motion blur where apt; transitions\n  carried by elements, not crossfades.\n- Rhythm: hits locked to the music; contrast between busy and calm; no monotony.\n- Design: strong composition, grid, typographic hierarchy, controlled palette; texture and finishing (grain, glow, vignette)\n  only where the style wants it; nothing looks accidental.\n- Technical polish: no jitter (unless intended), no popping, no aliasing, no banding, no fallback fonts, no half-loaded images.\n- Sound: genre-correct, musical, synced, mastered.\n\n# Before you deliver, score yourself honestly\n1–10 on style fidelity, concept wow, motion craft, design & typography, finish & texture, sound & sync, technical\n(6 = template-level, 8 = high-end agency, 9 = award shortlist). Aim for 9; fix whatever scores lowest first.\n\n# Deliver\nvideo.mp4, plus a short report: the concept in two sentences; a beat sheet with timecodes; how each signature feature is\nrealised; the tech route; check numbers (duration, resolution, fps, loudness, true peak); your honest top-3 weaknesses.\n```\n"},{"id":"S8-V8","name":"赛博朋克界面","series":"S8","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S8-V8.jpg","markdown":"# V8 赛博朋克界面（Cyberpunk HUD / FUI）\n\n- 分类：V mg-styles-15 · mg-styles-15\n- 适合：科幻界面\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# 赛博朋克 HUD / FUI Cyberpunk HUD / FUI (Fictional UI)\n\n**原片**：隼眼-9 · 目标锁定 KESTREL-9 TARGET ACQUIRED · 1920×1080 · 30 fps · 10 秒 · AI 评审终评 7.93 / 10\n\n**源码**：[demos/22-hud/](../demos/22-hud/)\n\n## 怎么用\n\n1. 新建一个空文件夹，在里面打开一个能写代码、能执行命令的 AI 助手（例如 Claude Code）。本机需要能跑网页渲染（Node + Chrome）和 ffmpeg。\n2. 把下面「提示词」整段复制进去发送。\n3. 想换成自己的内容：改 `Creative seed` 里的故事、品牌名、文案和配色，或者改 `Output` 里的尺寸（比如竖屏 1080×1920）。不要删 `Signature features` 那一段，它是这种风格被一眼认出来的依据。\n4. 想再提高：成片出来后，另开一个 AI 用 [rubric.md](../rubric.md) 评审，把评审意见贴回给制作的 AI 再改一轮。原片就是这样改了两轮。\n\n## 提示词\n\n```text\nYou are the director, motion designer, engineer and sound designer of ONE 10-second motion-design film.\nThe goal is the most classic, yet most stunning form of this style — award-shortlist / high-end commercial quality. A clean,\ntemplate-looking result is a fail. Benchmarks: Buck, ManvsMachine, Ordinary Folk, Giant Ant, Territory Studio, Apple keynote\nmotion, Pentagram motion identities, top Behance/Motionographer features, top 抖音/B站 designer accounts.\n\n# Style\n赛博朋克 HUD / FUI Cyberpunk HUD / FUI (Fictional UI)\n\n# Output (fixed)\n- 1920×1080, 30 fps, exactly 10.000 s (300 frames), H.264 MP4 with stereo AAC audio (48 kHz), mastered to −14 LUFS\n  integrated, true peak ≤ −1 dBTP. Final file: video.mp4.\n\n# Suggested technical route\nHTML: SVG/Canvas FUI layers + Three.js wireframe hologram; seeded random data; glow/scanline post pass\n(You may choose a better route if it clearly raises quality; say why.)\n\n# Creative seed\n「目标锁定 TARGET ACQUIRED」Cyberpunk HUD / FUI, film-UI grade (Territory Studio-level density & restraint).\n- Deep teal-black; cyan #00E5FF linework with orange/red #FF6A00 alert state; hex grid, concentric rotating rings with ticks, radar sweep (conic) with blips, data columns (monospace numbers rolling 3–5 frames then settling), typewriter status lines with cursor, sparkline graphs, a central Three.js wireframe hologram (globe or drone) with scanlines.\n- Layered build-up: lines draw on in hierarchical staggered order → data comes alive → a target is found → lock brackets snap 1.4 → 1.0 (fast-out slow-in) → everything flips to alert orange with 'TARGET LOCKED / 目标锁定', coordinates \"N31°14′ E121°29′\".\n- Glow, slight chromatic aberration, scanlines, restrained micro-glitches.\nSound: darksynth pulse + HUD beeps, scan sweeps, ascending lock-on tone, alarm on lock.\n\nYou are the director: you may change the concept, copy, story beats and brand names if you find a stronger idea, but the result must remain the CANONICAL, instantly-recognisable form of this style and must hit its signature features below.\n\n# Signature features and key techniques (the result is judged against these)\n模拟科幻电影/游戏界面的抬头显示包装：青橙霓虹线框、目标锁定框、雷达扫描、数据流小字。在科技数码测评、游戏剪辑（赛博朋克2077、EVA 二创）、军事航天科普和 AI 产品演示中作为『科技感』的默认皮肤，B 站 AE HUD 案例教程播放量达 20 万。\n视觉特征 青色/橙色霓虹线框, 目标锁定框与十字准星, 雷达/环形扫描 loop, 等宽小字数据流与打字机文字, 六边形网格与辉光\n关键技法 线框生长：SVG stroke-dasharray/dashoffset 描边动画，元素按层级错帧展开\n环形雷达：conic-gradient 扫描扇区旋转 loop + 目标点闪烁\n数字滚动：随机数快速跳变 3~5 帧后落定真实值；文字打字机 + 光标闪烁\n锁定框：四角括号从大到小收缩吸附目标（scale 1.4→1.0 + 快出缓入）\n全局外发光（glow）+ 轻微色差 + 扫描线叠加统一质感\n\n# How to work\n1. Treatment. Write down one clear idea: a hook in the first 0.5 s (never open on more than 0.3 s of empty or black), an\n   escalation, one unmistakable hero moment at ~60–75 % of the runtime, and a composed end frame held ~0.8–1.2 s with living\n   micro-motion. Map every signature feature above to a moment. Keep one cue sheet (beats and hit times) that both the\n   picture and the sound read from.\n2. Key frames before motion. Build the look, render stills at 6–10 key times and actually look at them. Each still should be\n   poster-worthy: composition, hierarchy, negative space, type set properly (kerning, line-height, weight contrast, CJK\n   punctuation, ~5 % safe margins). Iterate until nothing looks default, generic, cramped or \"AI-template\".\n3. Motion. Build the choreography and preview at low resolution. Step through the fastest moves frame by frame: spacing,\n   easing, arcs, overlap, anticipation and follow-through, motion blur. No unintended dead spans; the energy follows the music.\n4. Sound. Write genre-correct music and sound design locked to the cue sheet. Check that visual hits land on audio onsets.\n   Master to the output spec.\n5. Final. Render at full resolution (motion blur where apt), then check: exactly 10.00 s, resolution, fps, audio present and at\n   the right loudness, no black frames, no fallback fonts or tofu, no clipped elements at the frame edges, no shimmer on thin\n   lines, no gradient banding. Fix and repeat until you would submit it to a festival.\n6. Write an honest self-critique: what is strongest, what is weakest, what you would do with more time.\n\n# Rules\n- Rendering must be deterministic: every frame is a pure function of time t. Seeded randomness only; physics and particles\n  precomputed or closed-form; no real-time clocks; no state-triggered CSS transitions. A reliable route: a web page that can\n  draw any time t on request, captured frame by frame with a headless browser and encoded with ffmpeg (or a Blender script\n  that renders a PNG sequence).\n- Assets: only CC0 / public domain / OFL / Apache / free-for-commercial-use. Record each source and licence in CREDITS.md.\n  No real brand trademarks; invent brand names.\n- On-screen text must be correct and natural: idiomatic Chinese, proper CJK punctuation, no typos.\n\n# Craft checklist (what a jury looks for)\n- Style authenticity: an expert names the style in one second; every signature technique is present and executed correctly.\n- Motion craft: purposeful easing (no linear unless mechanical by design), overlap and stagger, anticipation and\n  follow-through, arcs, squash and stretch where the style allows, consistent physics, motion blur where apt; transitions\n  carried by elements, not crossfades.\n- Rhythm: hits locked to the music; contrast between busy and calm; no monotony.\n- Design: strong composition, grid, typographic hierarchy, controlled palette; texture and finishing (grain, glow, vignette)\n  only where the style wants it; nothing looks accidental.\n- Technical polish: no jitter (unless intended), no popping, no aliasing, no banding, no fallback fonts, no half-loaded images.\n- Sound: genre-correct, musical, synced, mastered.\n\n# Before you deliver, score yourself honestly\n1–10 on style fidelity, concept wow, motion craft, design & typography, finish & texture, sound & sync, technical\n(6 = template-level, 8 = high-end agency, 9 = award shortlist). Aim for 9; fix whatever scores lowest first.\n\n# Deliver\nvideo.mp4, plus a short report: the concept in two sentences; a beat sheet with timecodes; how each signature feature is\nrealised; the tech route; check numbers (duration, resolution, fps, loudness, true peak); your honest top-3 weaknesses.\n```\n"},{"id":"S8-V9","name":"拼贴 · 剪贴","series":"S8","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S8-V9.jpg","markdown":"# V9 拼贴 / 剪贴（Collage / Cut-out）\n\n- 分类：V mg-styles-15 · mg-styles-15\n- 适合：剪报、回忆\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# 拼贴剪贴 Collage / Cutout Animation\n\n**原片**：NOGGIN 脑洞季刊「脑洞大开 The Mind Is a Collage」 · 1920×1080 · 30 fps · 10 秒 · AI 评审终评 8.14 / 10\n\n**源码**：[demos/06-collage/](../demos/06-collage/)\n\n## 怎么用\n\n1. 新建一个空文件夹，在里面打开一个能写代码、能执行命令的 AI 助手（例如 Claude Code）。本机需要能跑网页渲染（Node + Chrome）和 ffmpeg。\n2. 把下面「提示词」整段复制进去发送。\n3. 想换成自己的内容：改 `Creative seed` 里的故事、品牌名、文案和配色，或者改 `Output` 里的尺寸（比如竖屏 1080×1920）。不要删 `Signature features` 那一段，它是这种风格被一眼认出来的依据。\n4. 想再提高：成片出来后，另开一个 AI 用 [rubric.md](../rubric.md) 评审，把评审意见贴回给制作的 AI 再改一轮。原片就是这样改了两轮。\n\n## 提示词\n\n```text\nYou are the director, motion designer, engineer and sound designer of ONE 10-second motion-design film.\nThe goal is the most classic, yet most stunning form of this style — award-shortlist / high-end commercial quality. A clean,\ntemplate-looking result is a fail. Benchmarks: Buck, ManvsMachine, Ordinary Folk, Giant Ant, Territory Studio, Apple keynote\nmotion, Pentagram motion identities, top Behance/Motionographer features, top 抖音/B站 designer accounts.\n\n# Style\n拼贴剪贴 Collage / Cutout Animation\n\n# Output (fixed)\n- 1920×1080, 30 fps, exactly 10.000 s (300 frames), H.264 MP4 with stereo AAC audio (48 kHz), mastered to −14 LUFS\n  integrated, true peak ≤ −1 dBTP. Final file: video.mp4.\n\n# Suggested technical route\nHTML: DOM/Canvas with PNG cutouts from /assets/images (rembg), SVG/CSS white-edge + halftone filters; stepped 12 fps (render 30 fps, hold motion on 12 fps grid)\n(You may choose a better route if it clearly raises quality; say why.)\n\n# Creative seed\n「脑洞拼贴」Surreal Dada/Monty-Python cutout collage in a modern editorial magazine finish.\n- Newsprint/kraft paper ground; a public-domain Victorian portrait cutout with rough white scissor edge + halftone; the top of the head FLIPS OPEN like a lid (Terry Gilliam homage) revealing deep space (NASA PD imagery) from which flowers, birds, planets, pointing hands and a vintage rocket spill out.\n- Jaw/mouth on a separate cut flaps with the sound; hands slap elements onto the page; torn-paper strips; bold constructivist red/black diagonal typography (e.g. \"THE MIND IS A COLLAGE\" / 脑洞大开).\n- Motion on 12 fps (even 6 fps for some holds) with ±2px hand-placement jitter; paper-flip / torn-paper transition to the end card.\nSound: vinyl crackle, jazzy boom-bap bed, paper rustles, scissors snips, slaps and a comic 'pop' when the lid opens.\n\nYou are the director: you may change the concept, copy, story beats and brand names if you find a stronger idea, but the result must remain the CANONICAL, instantly-recognisable form of this style and must hit its signature features below.\n\n# Signature features and key techniques (the result is judged against these)\n把老照片、报纸、手撕纸片剪出来重新拼装的复古超现实风格，源头是 Monty Python 和苏联构成主义海报。人物头身比例失衡、嘴巴单独开合、半调网点纹理是标志。近年与'杂志风'合流，成为音乐类、街头潮流类短视频的高频风格。\n视觉特征 照片剪影白描边, 半调网点, 报纸纹理, 比例失衡的超现实拼装, 定格式抽帧运动\n关键技法 素材处理：人物/物件抠图后加 2~4px 白描边（剪刀剪出来的感觉）+ 半调 halftone 滤镜\n关节动画：锚点设在肩/肘/颌，做 puppet 式分段旋转，嘴巴用两三张替换帧开合\n刻意降帧：主体运动 12fps 甚至 6fps（stop-motion 感），位置加 ±2px 随机抖动模拟手摆\n图层结构：背景纸纹 → 大形色块 → 照片剪影 → 手写涂鸦/胶带贴纸 → 颗粒调整层\n转场用'手把元素拍上来'或整页翻纸\n\n# How to work\n1. Treatment. Write down one clear idea: a hook in the first 0.5 s (never open on more than 0.3 s of empty or black), an\n   escalation, one unmistakable hero moment at ~60–75 % of the runtime, and a composed end frame held ~0.8–1.2 s with living\n   micro-motion. Map every signature feature above to a moment. Keep one cue sheet (beats and hit times) that both the\n   picture and the sound read from.\n2. Key frames before motion. Build the look, render stills at 6–10 key times and actually look at them. Each still should be\n   poster-worthy: composition, hierarchy, negative space, type set properly (kerning, line-height, weight contrast, CJK\n   punctuation, ~5 % safe margins). Iterate until nothing looks default, generic, cramped or \"AI-template\".\n3. Motion. Build the choreography and preview at low resolution. Step through the fastest moves frame by frame: spacing,\n   easing, arcs, overlap, anticipation and follow-through, motion blur. No unintended dead spans; the energy follows the music.\n4. Sound. Write genre-correct music and sound design locked to the cue sheet. Check that visual hits land on audio onsets.\n   Master to the output spec.\n5. Final. Render at full resolution (motion blur where apt), then check: exactly 10.00 s, resolution, fps, audio present and at\n   the right loudness, no black frames, no fallback fonts or tofu, no clipped elements at the frame edges, no shimmer on thin\n   lines, no gradient banding. Fix and repeat until you would submit it to a festival.\n6. Write an honest self-critique: what is strongest, what is weakest, what you would do with more time.\n\n# Rules\n- Rendering must be deterministic: every frame is a pure function of time t. Seeded randomness only; physics and particles\n  precomputed or closed-form; no real-time clocks; no state-triggered CSS transitions. A reliable route: a web page that can\n  draw any time t on request, captured frame by frame with a headless browser and encoded with ffmpeg (or a Blender script\n  that renders a PNG sequence).\n- Assets: only CC0 / public domain / OFL / Apache / free-for-commercial-use. Record each source and licence in CREDITS.md.\n  No real brand trademarks; invent brand names.\n- On-screen text must be correct and natural: idiomatic Chinese, proper CJK punctuation, no typos.\n\n# Craft checklist (what a jury looks for)\n- Style authenticity: an expert names the style in one second; every signature technique is present and executed correctly.\n- Motion craft: purposeful easing (no linear unless mechanical by design), overlap and stagger, anticipation and\n  follow-through, arcs, squash and stretch where the style allows, consistent physics, motion blur where apt; transitions\n  carried by elements, not crossfades.\n- Rhythm: hits locked to the music; contrast between busy and calm; no monotony.\n- Design: strong composition, grid, typographic hierarchy, controlled palette; texture and finishing (grain, glow, vignette)\n  only where the style wants it; nothing looks accidental.\n- Technical polish: no jitter (unless intended), no popping, no aliasing, no banding, no fallback fonts, no half-loaded images.\n- Sound: genre-correct, musical, synced, mastered.\n\n# Before you deliver, score yourself honestly\n1–10 on style fidelity, concept wow, motion craft, design & typography, finish & texture, sound & sync, technical\n(6 = template-level, 8 = high-end agency, 9 = award shortlist). Aim for 9; fix whatever scores lowest first.\n\n# Deliver\nvideo.mp4, plus a short report: the concept in two sentences; a beat sheet with timecodes; how each signature feature is\nrealised; the tech route; check numbers (duration, resolution, fps, loudness, true peak); your honest top-3 weaknesses.\n```\n"},{"id":"S8-V10","name":"极光与毛玻璃","series":"S8","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S8-V10.jpg","markdown":"# V10 极光与毛玻璃（Aurora & Glassmorphism）\n\n- 分类：V mg-styles-15 · mg-styles-15\n- 适合：柔和发光，安静的段落\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# 弥散渐变 / 玻璃拟态 Gradient Blur (Aurora) & Glassmorphism\n\n**原片**：Aurora — think in light「思考，自有光」 · 1920×1080 · 30 fps · 10 秒 · AI 评审终评 8.07 / 10\n\n**源码**：[demos/12-aurora-glass/](../demos/12-aurora-glass/)\n\n## 怎么用\n\n1. 新建一个空文件夹，在里面打开一个能写代码、能执行命令的 AI 助手（例如 Claude Code）。本机需要能跑网页渲染（Node + Chrome）和 ffmpeg。\n2. 把下面「提示词」整段复制进去发送。\n3. 想换成自己的内容：改 `Creative seed` 里的故事、品牌名、文案和配色，或者改 `Output` 里的尺寸（比如竖屏 1080×1920）。不要删 `Signature features` 那一段，它是这种风格被一眼认出来的依据。\n4. 想再提高：成片出来后，另开一个 AI 用 [rubric.md](../rubric.md) 评审，把评审意见贴回给制作的 AI 再改一轮。原片就是这样改了两轮。\n\n## 提示词\n\n```text\nYou are the director, motion designer, engineer and sound designer of ONE 10-second motion-design film.\nThe goal is the most classic, yet most stunning form of this style — award-shortlist / high-end commercial quality. A clean,\ntemplate-looking result is a fail. Benchmarks: Buck, ManvsMachine, Ordinary Folk, Giant Ant, Territory Studio, Apple keynote\nmotion, Pentagram motion identities, top Behance/Motionographer features, top 抖音/B站 designer accounts.\n\n# Style\n弥散渐变 / 玻璃拟态 Gradient Blur (Aurora) & Glassmorphism\n\n# Output (fixed)\n- 1920×1080, 30 fps, exactly 10.000 s (300 frames), H.264 MP4 with stereo AAC audio (48 kHz), mastered to −14 LUFS\n  integrated, true peak ≤ −1 dBTP. Final file: video.mp4.\n\n# Suggested technical route\nHTML WebGL: aurora mesh-gradient shader + glass refraction (SDF lens, chromatic dispersion) + DOM/CSS backdrop-filter glass cards\n(You may choose a better route if it clearly raises quality; say why.)\n\n# Creative seed\n「Liquid Glass」2025–26 AI-product-launch aesthetic: slow, translucent, expensive.\n- Deep indigo-black base; 4–5 aurora blobs (violet/blue/cyan/magenta, adjacent hues only) drifting on bezier paths, blending into a mesh gradient, 3–5% noise dithering (no banding).\n- Frosted glass cards (backdrop blur ~30px, 5–10% white fill, 1px 30% white inner highlight, large radius) float in with parallax depth and slight 3D tilt; micro-UI inside (chat bubble, voice waveform, toggles) animates with opacity + 8px rise.\n- WOW: a Liquid-Glass lens (true refraction, chromatic dispersion on the rim, specular edge) glides across and magnifies the aurora and the cards.\n- Title (invented product, e.g. \"Aurora — think in light\") in a light elegant sans; sine/breathing easing everywhere; nothing fast.\nSound: ambient cinematic pad, soft shimmer, very subtle UI ticks.\n\nYou are the director: you may change the concept, copy, story beats and brand names if you find a stronger idea, but the result must remain the CANONICAL, instantly-recognisable form of this style and must hit its signature features below.\n\n# Signature features and key techniques (the result is judged against these)\n大面积高斯模糊的彩色光斑（aurora/mesh gradient）缓慢漂移作底，磨砂半透明玻璃卡片浮在其上。苹果 iOS 26 Liquid Glass 与各家 AI 产品发布把这一风格推成 2024-2026 科技品牌片的默认视觉，气质是'慢、透、高级'。\n视觉特征 大色斑高斯模糊漂移, 磨砂玻璃卡片, 1px高光描边, 邻近色低对比配色, 缓慢呼吸感节奏\n关键技法 底层 aurora：3~5 个纯色大圆 blur 80~150px，各自沿贝塞尔路径 20~40 秒漂移循环，彼此 blend 出 mesh gradient 感\n玻璃卡片配方：backdrop-filter blur(20~40px) + 白色 5~10% 填充 + 1px 白色 30% 内描边 + 大半径圆角\n配色用邻近色 2~3 色（紫蓝青/橙粉红），撞色会脏；暗底亮斑比亮底更出效果\n全局叠 3~5% 噪点防 banding（与颗粒质感技法交叉）\n缓动接近 linear 或 sine 呼吸曲线，一切都慢——快动作会立刻破坏气质；文字入场也只用 opacity+8px 位移\n\n# How to work\n1. Treatment. Write down one clear idea: a hook in the first 0.5 s (never open on more than 0.3 s of empty or black), an\n   escalation, one unmistakable hero moment at ~60–75 % of the runtime, and a composed end frame held ~0.8–1.2 s with living\n   micro-motion. Map every signature feature above to a moment. Keep one cue sheet (beats and hit times) that both the\n   picture and the sound read from.\n2. Key frames before motion. Build the look, render stills at 6–10 key times and actually look at them. Each still should be\n   poster-worthy: composition, hierarchy, negative space, type set properly (kerning, line-height, weight contrast, CJK\n   punctuation, ~5 % safe margins). Iterate until nothing looks default, generic, cramped or \"AI-template\".\n3. Motion. Build the choreography and preview at low resolution. Step through the fastest moves frame by frame: spacing,\n   easing, arcs, overlap, anticipation and follow-through, motion blur. No unintended dead spans; the energy follows the music.\n4. Sound. Write genre-correct music and sound design locked to the cue sheet. Check that visual hits land on audio onsets.\n   Master to the output spec.\n5. Final. Render at full resolution (motion blur where apt), then check: exactly 10.00 s, resolution, fps, audio present and at\n   the right loudness, no black frames, no fallback fonts or tofu, no clipped elements at the frame edges, no shimmer on thin\n   lines, no gradient banding. Fix and repeat until you would submit it to a festival.\n6. Write an honest self-critique: what is strongest, what is weakest, what you would do with more time.\n\n# Rules\n- Rendering must be deterministic: every frame is a pure function of time t. Seeded randomness only; physics and particles\n  precomputed or closed-form; no real-time clocks; no state-triggered CSS transitions. A reliable route: a web page that can\n  draw any time t on request, captured frame by frame with a headless browser and encoded with ffmpeg (or a Blender script\n  that renders a PNG sequence).\n- Assets: only CC0 / public domain / OFL / Apache / free-for-commercial-use. Record each source and licence in CREDITS.md.\n  No real brand trademarks; invent brand names.\n- On-screen text must be correct and natural: idiomatic Chinese, proper CJK punctuation, no typos.\n\n# Craft checklist (what a jury looks for)\n- Style authenticity: an expert names the style in one second; every signature technique is present and executed correctly.\n- Motion craft: purposeful easing (no linear unless mechanical by design), overlap and stagger, anticipation and\n  follow-through, arcs, squash and stretch where the style allows, consistent physics, motion blur where apt; transitions\n  carried by elements, not crossfades.\n- Rhythm: hits locked to the music; contrast between busy and calm; no monotony.\n- Design: strong composition, grid, typographic hierarchy, controlled palette; texture and finishing (grain, glow, vignette)\n  only where the style wants it; nothing looks accidental.\n- Technical polish: no jitter (unless intended), no popping, no aliasing, no banding, no fallback fonts, no half-loaded images.\n- Sound: genre-correct, musical, synced, mastered.\n\n# Before you deliver, score yourself honestly\n1–10 on style fidelity, concept wow, motion craft, design & typography, finish & texture, sound & sync, technical\n(6 = template-level, 8 = high-end agency, 9 = award shortlist). Aim for 9; fix whatever scores lowest first.\n\n# Deliver\nvideo.mp4, plus a short report: the concept in two sentences; a beat sheet with timecodes; how each signature feature is\nrealised; the tech route; check numbers (duration, resolution, fps, loudness, true peak); your honest top-3 weaknesses.\n```\n"},{"id":"S8-V11","name":"包豪斯几何","series":"S8","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S8-V11.jpg","markdown":"# V11 包豪斯几何（Geometric Bauhaus）\n\n- 分类：V mg-styles-15 · mg-styles-15\n- 适合：几何、设计感\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# 几何构成 / 包豪斯 Geometric / Bauhaus Motion\n\n**原片**：KONSTRUKTION · 20 SCHLÄGE「二十拍构成」 · 1920×1080 · 30 fps · 10 秒 · AI 评审终评 7.71 / 10\n\n**源码**：[demos/09-bauhaus/](../demos/09-bauhaus/)\n\n## 怎么用\n\n1. 新建一个空文件夹，在里面打开一个能写代码、能执行命令的 AI 助手（例如 Claude Code）。本机需要能跑网页渲染（Node + Chrome）和 ffmpeg。\n2. 把下面「提示词」整段复制进去发送。\n3. 想换成自己的内容：改 `Creative seed` 里的故事、品牌名、文案和配色，或者改 `Output` 里的尺寸（比如竖屏 1080×1920）。不要删 `Signature features` 那一段，它是这种风格被一眼认出来的依据。\n4. 想再提高：成片出来后，另开一个 AI 用 [rubric.md](../rubric.md) 评审，把评审意见贴回给制作的 AI 再改一轮。原片就是这样改了两轮。\n\n## 提示词\n\n```text\nYou are the director, motion designer, engineer and sound designer of ONE 10-second motion-design film.\nThe goal is the most classic, yet most stunning form of this style — award-shortlist / high-end commercial quality. A clean,\ntemplate-looking result is a fail. Benchmarks: Buck, ManvsMachine, Ordinary Folk, Giant Ant, Territory Studio, Apple keynote\nmotion, Pentagram motion identities, top Behance/Motionographer features, top 抖音/B站 designer accounts.\n\n# Style\n几何构成 / 包豪斯 Geometric / Bauhaus Motion\n\n# Output (fixed)\n- 1920×1080, 30 fps, exactly 10.000 s (300 frames), H.264 MP4 with stereo AAC audio (48 kHz), mastered to −14 LUFS\n  integrated, true peak ≤ −1 dBTP. Final file: video.mp4.\n\n# Suggested technical route\nHTML: SVG/Canvas + GSAP on a strict modular grid, beat grid at 120 BPM\n(You may choose a better route if it clearly raises quality; say why.)\n\n# Creative seed\n「包豪斯动态海报」Bauhaus 1919 kinetic poster — mechanical, rational, musical.\n- Cream paper (#F1E9DA), primaries red #E03C31, yellow #F2B705, blue #1E4FA3, black #111; strict modular grid (e.g. 240px modules); every move is an integer number of modules; every landing on a grid line.\n- 120 BPM (0.5 s/beat): each beat a module acts — quarter circles rotate 90° about a corner pivot, semicircles slide one module, triangles flip, squares split; rotation pivots alternate (own centre / corner / frame centre).\n- 0–2s grid lines draw & the first red circle lands; 2–6s the composition assembles into a Kandinsky/Bauhaus poster; 6–8s phase-shifted cascade wave across the grid on the drop; 8–10s freeze into a perfect poster with Swiss typography: \"BAUHAUS\" set vertically in heavy geometric sans + small caps \"FORM · FARBE · FUNKTION\".\n- Easing: power2.inOut or linear — NO overshoot; subtle paper tooth + slight print misregistration allowed.\nSound: minimal techno/click — each flip = a pitched click/blip, kick on quarters.\n\nYou are the director: you may change the concept, copy, story beats and brand names if you find a stronger idea, but the result must remain the CANONICAL, instantly-recognisable form of this style and must hit its signature features below.\n\n# Signature features and key techniques (the result is judged against these)\n圆、三角、矩形在严格网格上做精确的平移、旋转、缩放，三原色+黑白的限定色板，机械理性的秩序美。源自包豪斯与瑞士国际主义平面传统（Adobe 曾与 Bauhaus 档案馆合作 Hidden Treasures 项目），也是动态海报（kinetic poster）的主流语言，与音乐节拍天然合拍。\n视觉特征 基础几何形, 红黄蓝黑限定色, 严格网格对齐, 旋转以几何中心为轴, 节拍驱动的模块化运动\n关键技法 一切运动锚定网格：位移距离=网格模数整数倍，落点必在网格线上\n缓动克制：easeInOutQuad 或干脆匀速，禁用 overshoot——机械感是特征不是缺陷\n节拍驱动：每个几何元素在节拍点瞬时出现/翻转，一拍一个动作，类似我们已做的卡点逻辑\n旋转轴心玩法：绕自身中心、绕边缘顶点、绕画面中心三种交替制造韵律\n生成式变体：Processing/p5.js 用三角函数相位差批量驱动几何阵列（国内动态海报圈主流做法）\n\n# How to work\n1. Treatment. Write down one clear idea: a hook in the first 0.5 s (never open on more than 0.3 s of empty or black), an\n   escalation, one unmistakable hero moment at ~60–75 % of the runtime, and a composed end frame held ~0.8–1.2 s with living\n   micro-motion. Map every signature feature above to a moment. Keep one cue sheet (beats and hit times) that both the\n   picture and the sound read from.\n2. Key frames before motion. Build the look, render stills at 6–10 key times and actually look at them. Each still should be\n   poster-worthy: composition, hierarchy, negative space, type set properly (kerning, line-height, weight contrast, CJK\n   punctuation, ~5 % safe margins). Iterate until nothing looks default, generic, cramped or \"AI-template\".\n3. Motion. Build the choreography and preview at low resolution. Step through the fastest moves frame by frame: spacing,\n   easing, arcs, overlap, anticipation and follow-through, motion blur. No unintended dead spans; the energy follows the music.\n4. Sound. Write genre-correct music and sound design locked to the cue sheet. Check that visual hits land on audio onsets.\n   Master to the output spec.\n5. Final. Render at full resolution (motion blur where apt), then check: exactly 10.00 s, resolution, fps, audio present and at\n   the right loudness, no black frames, no fallback fonts or tofu, no clipped elements at the frame edges, no shimmer on thin\n   lines, no gradient banding. Fix and repeat until you would submit it to a festival.\n6. Write an honest self-critique: what is strongest, what is weakest, what you would do with more time.\n\n# Rules\n- Rendering must be deterministic: every frame is a pure function of time t. Seeded randomness only; physics and particles\n  precomputed or closed-form; no real-time clocks; no state-triggered CSS transitions. A reliable route: a web page that can\n  draw any time t on request, captured frame by frame with a headless browser and encoded with ffmpeg (or a Blender script\n  that renders a PNG sequence).\n- Assets: only CC0 / public domain / OFL / Apache / free-for-commercial-use. Record each source and licence in CREDITS.md.\n  No real brand trademarks; invent brand names.\n- On-screen text must be correct and natural: idiomatic Chinese, proper CJK punctuation, no typos.\n\n# Craft checklist (what a jury looks for)\n- Style authenticity: an expert names the style in one second; every signature technique is present and executed correctly.\n- Motion craft: purposeful easing (no linear unless mechanical by design), overlap and stagger, anticipation and\n  follow-through, arcs, squash and stretch where the style allows, consistent physics, motion blur where apt; transitions\n  carried by elements, not crossfades.\n- Rhythm: hits locked to the music; contrast between busy and calm; no monotony.\n- Design: strong composition, grid, typographic hierarchy, controlled palette; texture and finishing (grain, glow, vignette)\n  only where the style wants it; nothing looks accidental.\n- Technical polish: no jitter (unless intended), no popping, no aliasing, no banding, no fallback fonts, no half-loaded images.\n- Sound: genre-correct, musical, synced, mastered.\n\n# Before you deliver, score yourself honestly\n1–10 on style fidelity, concept wow, motion craft, design & typography, finish & texture, sound & sync, technical\n(6 = template-level, 8 = high-end agency, 9 = award shortlist). Aim for 9; fix whatever scores lowest first.\n\n# Deliver\nvideo.mp4, plus a short report: the concept in two sentences; a beat sheet with timecodes; how each signature feature is\nrealised; the tech route; check numbers (duration, resolution, fps, loudness, true peak); your honest top-3 weaknesses.\n```\n"},{"id":"S8-V12","name":"80 年代合成波 · VHS","series":"S8","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S8-V12.jpg","markdown":"# V12 80 年代合成波 / VHS（80s Synthwave / VHS）\n\n- 分类：V mg-styles-15 · mg-styles-15\n- 适合：复古霓虹、录像带\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# 复古系（80s Synthwave / Y2K 千禧） Retro: 80s Synthwave / VHS / Y2K\n\n**原片**：NEON DRIVE · Midnight「霓虹夜驰 1986」 · 1920×1080 · 30 fps · 10 秒 · AI 评审终评 8.21 / 10\n\n**源码**：[demos/10-synthwave/](../demos/10-synthwave/)\n\n## 怎么用\n\n1. 新建一个空文件夹，在里面打开一个能写代码、能执行命令的 AI 助手（例如 Claude Code）。本机需要能跑网页渲染（Node + Chrome）和 ffmpeg。\n2. 把下面「提示词」整段复制进去发送。\n3. 想换成自己的内容：改 `Creative seed` 里的故事、品牌名、文案和配色，或者改 `Output` 里的尺寸（比如竖屏 1080×1920）。不要删 `Signature features` 那一段，它是这种风格被一眼认出来的依据。\n4. 想再提高：成片出来后，另开一个 AI 用 [rubric.md](../rubric.md) 评审，把评审意见贴回给制作的 AI 再改一轮。原片就是这样改了两轮。\n\n## 提示词\n\n```text\nYou are the director, motion designer, engineer and sound designer of ONE 10-second motion-design film.\nThe goal is the most classic, yet most stunning form of this style — award-shortlist / high-end commercial quality. A clean,\ntemplate-looking result is a fail. Benchmarks: Buck, ManvsMachine, Ordinary Folk, Giant Ant, Territory Studio, Apple keynote\nmotion, Pentagram motion identities, top Behance/Motionographer features, top 抖音/B站 designer accounts.\n\n# Style\n复古系（80s Synthwave / Y2K 千禧） Retro: 80s Synthwave / VHS / Y2K\n\n# Output (fixed)\n- 1920×1080, 30 fps, exactly 10.000 s (300 frames), H.264 MP4 with stereo AAC audio (48 kHz), mastered to −14 LUFS\n  integrated, true peak ≤ −1 dBTP. Final file: video.mp4.\n\n# Suggested technical route\nHTML WebGL (perspective grid, sun, mountains shader) + canvas chrome text + CSS/SVG glow; VHS pass\n(You may choose a better route if it clearly raises quality; say why.)\n\n# Creative seed\n「OUTRUN 1986」The quintessential 80s synthwave title sequence.\n- Starry night, striped retro sun (horizontal band cut-outs, magenta→orange→yellow), wireframe mountains, palm silhouettes, neon magenta/cyan perspective grid rushing toward the camera.\n- 0–1s: VHS tracking noise + 'PLAY ▶' OSD; 1–4s: camera flies low over the grid, sun rises; 4–6s: CHROME title (e.g. \"NEON DRIVE\", multi-stop metallic gradient, bevel highlight, star glint sweeping across) SLAMS in with a lens flare; 6–7.5s: pink neon script word (e.g. \"Midnight\") writes on like a neon tube with flicker; 7.5–10s: sustained ride, subtle VHS chroma bleed/scanlines, end on the hero frame.\n- Double glow (tight ~4px + wide ~30px), deep purple sky gradient.\nSound: synthwave — gated-reverb snare, arpeggiated saw bass, lush pads, big chord + crash on the title slam.\n\nYou are the director: you may change the concept, copy, story beats and brand names if you find a stronger idea, but the result must remain the CANONICAL, instantly-recognisable form of this style and must hit its signature features below.\n\n# Signature features and key techniques (the result is judged against these)\n对特定年代媒介美学的整体引用：80s synthwave 是霓虹网格+日落渐变+镀铬字；VHS 是扫描线+色偏+磁带噪声；Y2K 千禧风是液态镀铬金属、酸性绿紫、拟物系统窗口和低保真 3D。近两年 Y2K 在音乐视频与潮流品牌片中强势回潮，B站模板市场大量供应'酸性镀铬'素材。\n视觉特征 霓虹辉光, 透视网格地平线, 扫描线与RGB色偏, 镀铬金属字, 酸性配色, 拟物窗口UI\n关键技法 synthwave 三件套：透视网格滚动（一点透视 grid + 纵向位移循环）、日落多层渐变球、双层辉光（内层紧 4px 高亮+外层散 30px 低透明）\nVHS 做旧链：RGB split（红蓝通道各偏 1~2px）→ 扫描线（2px 间隔 10% 黑条）→ 波浪扭曲抖动（每秒 1~2 次随机 glitch 抽帧）→ 4:3 圆角遮罩\nY2K 镀铬字：极高对比的多段金属渐变 + bevel 高光 + 环境映射感反光，配 lens flare\nY2K 运动语言：弹窗式 pop 出现、光标点击、窗口拖拽，界面拟物即动画叙事\n帧率故意不稳：关键段落抽帧到 15fps 或倒放 2 帧制造磁带卡顿\n\n# How to work\n1. Treatment. Write down one clear idea: a hook in the first 0.5 s (never open on more than 0.3 s of empty or black), an\n   escalation, one unmistakable hero moment at ~60–75 % of the runtime, and a composed end frame held ~0.8–1.2 s with living\n   micro-motion. Map every signature feature above to a moment. Keep one cue sheet (beats and hit times) that both the\n   picture and the sound read from.\n2. Key frames before motion. Build the look, render stills at 6–10 key times and actually look at them. Each still should be\n   poster-worthy: composition, hierarchy, negative space, type set properly (kerning, line-height, weight contrast, CJK\n   punctuation, ~5 % safe margins). Iterate until nothing looks default, generic, cramped or \"AI-template\".\n3. Motion. Build the choreography and preview at low resolution. Step through the fastest moves frame by frame: spacing,\n   easing, arcs, overlap, anticipation and follow-through, motion blur. No unintended dead spans; the energy follows the music.\n4. Sound. Write genre-correct music and sound design locked to the cue sheet. Check that visual hits land on audio onsets.\n   Master to the output spec.\n5. Final. Render at full resolution (motion blur where apt), then check: exactly 10.00 s, resolution, fps, audio present and at\n   the right loudness, no black frames, no fallback fonts or tofu, no clipped elements at the frame edges, no shimmer on thin\n   lines, no gradient banding. Fix and repeat until you would submit it to a festival.\n6. Write an honest self-critique: what is strongest, what is weakest, what you would do with more time.\n\n# Rules\n- Rendering must be deterministic: every frame is a pure function of time t. Seeded randomness only; physics and particles\n  precomputed or closed-form; no real-time clocks; no state-triggered CSS transitions. A reliable route: a web page that can\n  draw any time t on request, captured frame by frame with a headless browser and encoded with ffmpeg (or a Blender script\n  that renders a PNG sequence).\n- Assets: only CC0 / public domain / OFL / Apache / free-for-commercial-use. Record each source and licence in CREDITS.md.\n  No real brand trademarks; invent brand names.\n- On-screen text must be correct and natural: idiomatic Chinese, proper CJK punctuation, no typos.\n\n# Craft checklist (what a jury looks for)\n- Style authenticity: an expert names the style in one second; every signature technique is present and executed correctly.\n- Motion craft: purposeful easing (no linear unless mechanical by design), overlap and stagger, anticipation and\n  follow-through, arcs, squash and stretch where the style allows, consistent physics, motion blur where apt; transitions\n  carried by elements, not crossfades.\n- Rhythm: hits locked to the music; contrast between busy and calm; no monotony.\n- Design: strong composition, grid, typographic hierarchy, controlled palette; texture and finishing (grain, glow, vignette)\n  only where the style wants it; nothing looks accidental.\n- Technical polish: no jitter (unless intended), no popping, no aliasing, no banding, no fallback fonts, no half-loaded images.\n- Sound: genre-correct, musical, synced, mastered.\n\n# Before you deliver, score yourself honestly\n1–10 on style fidelity, concept wow, motion craft, design & typography, finish & texture, sound & sync, technical\n(6 = template-level, 8 = high-end agency, 9 = award shortlist). Aim for 9; fix whatever scores lowest first.\n\n# Deliver\nvideo.mp4, plus a short report: the concept in two sentences; a beat sheet with timecodes; how each signature feature is\nrealised; the tech route; check numbers (duration, resolution, fps, loudness, true peak); your honest top-3 weaknesses.\n```\n"},{"id":"S8-V13","name":"像素","series":"S8","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S8-V13.jpg","markdown":"# V13 像素（Pixel Art）\n\n- 分类：V mg-styles-15 · mg-styles-15\n- 适合：回忆、游戏感\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# 像素风 Pixel Art / 8-bit\n\n**原片**：PIXEL QUEST 像素冒险 · 1920×1080 · 30 fps · 10 秒 · AI 评审终评 8.07 / 10\n\n**源码**：[demos/20-pixel/](../demos/20-pixel/)\n\n## 怎么用\n\n1. 新建一个空文件夹，在里面打开一个能写代码、能执行命令的 AI 助手（例如 Claude Code）。本机需要能跑网页渲染（Node + Chrome）和 ffmpeg。\n2. 把下面「提示词」整段复制进去发送。\n3. 想换成自己的内容：改 `Creative seed` 里的故事、品牌名、文案和配色，或者改 `Output` 里的尺寸（比如竖屏 1080×1920）。不要删 `Signature features` 那一段，它是这种风格被一眼认出来的依据。\n4. 想再提高：成片出来后，另开一个 AI 用 [rubric.md](../rubric.md) 评审，把评审意见贴回给制作的 AI 再改一轮。原片就是这样改了两轮。\n\n## 提示词\n\n```text\nYou are the director, motion designer, engineer and sound designer of ONE 10-second motion-design film.\nThe goal is the most classic, yet most stunning form of this style — award-shortlist / high-end commercial quality. A clean,\ntemplate-looking result is a fail. Benchmarks: Buck, ManvsMachine, Ordinary Folk, Giant Ant, Territory Studio, Apple keynote\nmotion, Pentagram motion identities, top Behance/Motionographer features, top 抖音/B站 designer accounts.\n\n# Style\n像素风 Pixel Art / 8-bit\n\n# Output (fixed)\n- 1920×1080, 30 fps, exactly 10.000 s (300 frames), H.264 MP4 with stereo AAC audio (48 kHz), mastered to −14 LUFS\n  integrated, true peak ≤ −1 dBTP. Final file: video.mp4.\n\n# Suggested technical route\nHTML Canvas at 320×180 internal resolution, integer-scaled ×6 with nearest-neighbour; sprites authored as pixel arrays in code; limited palette\n(You may choose a better route if it clearly raises quality; say why.)\n\n# Creative seed\n「像素冒险 PIXEL QUEST」A retro side-scroller title/attract sequence.\n- Internal 320×180, ×6 nearest-neighbour; limited palette (PICO-8-like 16–32 colours) with dithered sky gradient; 3–4 parallax layers (sky, far mountains, mid forest/city, near ground tiles) moving by whole pixels.\n- Hero sprite (hand-authored 16×16–24×24, 6–8 frame run cycle at ~10–12 fps) runs, jumps, collects coins (sparkle), bops a slime; the palette shifts day → sunset; a treasure chest opens with a light burst.\n- Dialogue box with pixel CJK font typing 「欢迎来到像素世界！」; chunky pixel logo \"PIXEL QUEST\" drops in with shine sweep; optional CRT pass (scanlines, slight barrel, phosphor glow) that doesn't blur pixels into mush.\n- Strictly: nearest-neighbour, integer pixel positions, stepped timing (no smooth easing on sprites).\nSound: chiptune (pulse lead, triangle bass, noise drums) + jump/coin/chest SFX.\n\nYou are the director: you may change the concept, copy, story beats and brand names if you find a stronger idea, but the result must remain the CANONICAL, instantly-recognisable form of this style and must hit its signature features below.\n\n# Signature features and key techniques (the result is judged against these)\n低分辨率大颗粒像素 + 有限色板 + 低帧率精灵动画的复古游戏美学，在 B 站游戏区（MC、泰拉瑞亚、独立游戏）、8-bit 音乐 MV 和品牌怀旧营销中常见；Aseprite 教程在 B 站有 10 万+ 播放的稳定学习生态。\n视觉特征 大颗粒像素与硬边缘, 有限色板(8~32色), 8~12fps 精灵帧动画, 抖动渐变 dithering, CRT 扫描线滤镜\n关键技法 Aseprite 绘制精灵表（sprite sheet）+ 序列帧循环（走路 6~8 帧、呼吸 2~4 帧）\n缩放必须最近邻插值（CSS image-rendering: pixelated），杜绝抗锯齿糊边\n动画用 steps() 阶梯时序而非平滑缓动，保持逐帧感\n视差卷轴背景：远中近三层不同速度整像素平移\n可选 CRT 后处理：扫描线+桶形畸变+磷光辉光\n\n# How to work\n1. Treatment. Write down one clear idea: a hook in the first 0.5 s (never open on more than 0.3 s of empty or black), an\n   escalation, one unmistakable hero moment at ~60–75 % of the runtime, and a composed end frame held ~0.8–1.2 s with living\n   micro-motion. Map every signature feature above to a moment. Keep one cue sheet (beats and hit times) that both the\n   picture and the sound read from.\n2. Key frames before motion. Build the look, render stills at 6–10 key times and actually look at them. Each still should be\n   poster-worthy: composition, hierarchy, negative space, type set properly (kerning, line-height, weight contrast, CJK\n   punctuation, ~5 % safe margins). Iterate until nothing looks default, generic, cramped or \"AI-template\".\n3. Motion. Build the choreography and preview at low resolution. Step through the fastest moves frame by frame: spacing,\n   easing, arcs, overlap, anticipation and follow-through, motion blur. No unintended dead spans; the energy follows the music.\n4. Sound. Write genre-correct music and sound design locked to the cue sheet. Check that visual hits land on audio onsets.\n   Master to the output spec.\n5. Final. Render at full resolution (motion blur where apt), then check: exactly 10.00 s, resolution, fps, audio present and at\n   the right loudness, no black frames, no fallback fonts or tofu, no clipped elements at the frame edges, no shimmer on thin\n   lines, no gradient banding. Fix and repeat until you would submit it to a festival.\n6. Write an honest self-critique: what is strongest, what is weakest, what you would do with more time.\n\n# Rules\n- Rendering must be deterministic: every frame is a pure function of time t. Seeded randomness only; physics and particles\n  precomputed or closed-form; no real-time clocks; no state-triggered CSS transitions. A reliable route: a web page that can\n  draw any time t on request, captured frame by frame with a headless browser and encoded with ffmpeg (or a Blender script\n  that renders a PNG sequence).\n- Assets: only CC0 / public domain / OFL / Apache / free-for-commercial-use. Record each source and licence in CREDITS.md.\n  No real brand trademarks; invent brand names.\n- On-screen text must be correct and natural: idiomatic Chinese, proper CJK punctuation, no typos.\n\n# Craft checklist (what a jury looks for)\n- Style authenticity: an expert names the style in one second; every signature technique is present and executed correctly.\n- Motion craft: purposeful easing (no linear unless mechanical by design), overlap and stagger, anticipation and\n  follow-through, arcs, squash and stretch where the style allows, consistent physics, motion blur where apt; transitions\n  carried by elements, not crossfades.\n- Rhythm: hits locked to the music; contrast between busy and calm; no monotony.\n- Design: strong composition, grid, typographic hierarchy, controlled palette; texture and finishing (grain, glow, vignette)\n  only where the style wants it; nothing looks accidental.\n- Technical polish: no jitter (unless intended), no popping, no aliasing, no banding, no fallback fonts, no half-loaded images.\n- Sound: genre-correct, musical, synced, mastered.\n\n# Before you deliver, score yourself honestly\n1–10 on style fidelity, concept wow, motion craft, design & typography, finish & texture, sound & sync, technical\n(6 = template-level, 8 = high-end agency, 9 = award shortlist). Aim for 9; fix whatever scores lowest first.\n\n# Deliver\nvideo.mp4, plus a short report: the concept in two sentences; a beat sheet with timecodes; how each signature feature is\nrealised; the tech route; check numbers (duration, resolution, fps, loudness, true peak); your honest top-3 weaknesses.\n```\n"},{"id":"S8-V14","name":"液态流动","series":"S8","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S8-V14.jpg","markdown":"# V14 液态流动（Liquid Motion）\n\n- 分类：V mg-styles-15 · mg-styles-15\n- 适合：柔软的转场、情绪流动\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# 液态流动 Liquid Motion\n\n**原片**：drop.「万物始于一滴」 · 1920×1080 · 30 fps · 10 秒 · AI 评审终评 7.57 / 10\n\n**源码**：[demos/07-liquid/](../demos/07-liquid/)\n\n## 怎么用\n\n1. 新建一个空文件夹，在里面打开一个能写代码、能执行命令的 AI 助手（例如 Claude Code）。本机需要能跑网页渲染（Node + Chrome）和 ffmpeg。\n2. 把下面「提示词」整段复制进去发送。\n3. 想换成自己的内容：改 `Creative seed` 里的故事、品牌名、文案和配色，或者改 `Output` 里的尺寸（比如竖屏 1080×1920）。不要删 `Signature features` 那一段，它是这种风格被一眼认出来的依据。\n4. 想再提高：成片出来后，另开一个 AI 用 [rubric.md](../rubric.md) 评审，把评审意见贴回给制作的 AI 再改一轮。原片就是这样改了两轮。\n\n## 提示词\n\n```text\nYou are the director, motion designer, engineer and sound designer of ONE 10-second motion-design film.\nThe goal is the most classic, yet most stunning form of this style — award-shortlist / high-end commercial quality. A clean,\ntemplate-looking result is a fail. Benchmarks: Buck, ManvsMachine, Ordinary Folk, Giant Ant, Territory Studio, Apple keynote\nmotion, Pentagram motion identities, top Behance/Motionographer features, top 抖音/B站 designer accounts.\n\n# Style\n液态流动 Liquid Motion\n\n# Output (fixed)\n- 1920×1080, 30 fps, exactly 10.000 s (300 frames), H.264 MP4 with stereo AAC audio (48 kHz), mastered to −14 LUFS\n  integrated, true peak ≤ −1 dBTP. Final file: video.mp4.\n\n# Suggested technical route\nHTML WebGL2 fragment shader: 2D SDF metaballs (smooth-min) + fake 3D lighting from field gradient normals, text SDF for the logo\n(You may choose a better route if it clearly raises quality; say why.)\n\n# Creative seed\n「液态创世」Liquid logo genesis — glossy, organic, high energy.\n- Deep plum ground; glossy candy liquid (hot orange → pink → violet) with specular highlights and fresnel rim so it reads like thick 3D liquid, not flat blobs.\n- 0–2s: droplets fall and splash with squash on landing; 2–4s: blobs merge via metaball fusion — stretched necks, snap, 2–3 frame rebound; secondary droplets follow 2–3 frames late;\n- 4–6s: a big liquid wave floods across the frame as a transition (wavy front with 3–5 phase-offset bulges);\n- 6–8.5s: the liquid coalesces into a wordmark (e.g. \"FLUX\") — text SDF blended with the metaballs;\n- 8.5–10s: a last drip falls from the logo and ripples; hold.\nEase: flung, never uniform (cubic-bezier(0.22,1,0.36,1)).\nSound: bubbly gloops, pour, splash, drip, deep bass swell under a glossy synth bed.\n\nYou are the director: you may change the concept, copy, story beats and brand names if you find a stronger idea, but the result must remain the CANONICAL, instantly-recognisable form of this style and must hit its signature features below.\n\n# Signature features and key techniques (the result is judged against these)\n图形像液体一样流动、拉丝、滴落、融合：转场是一泼颜料漫过屏幕，logo 从一滴液体聚合成形。有机、解压、高能量，广泛用于音乐视频、食品饮料广告和转场包装。\n视觉特征 波浪边缘转场, 拉丝与滴落, metaball融合, 次级液滴跟随, 有机曲线永不直线\n关键技法 液态转场：遮罩路径手 K 波浪形前沿扫过画面，前沿加 3~5 个错相位的凸起\nmetaball 融合的通用伪造法：高斯模糊(20px+) → 提高对比度阈值（AE 用 Levels 收紧、Web 用 feColorMatrix alpha 阈值），两球靠近即自动'粘连'\n拉丝-断裂-回弹：主形离开时留一条细颈，断裂瞬间两端各回弹 2~3 帧\n缓动前快后慢 cubic-bezier(0.22,1,0.36,1)，液体只会被'甩'出去不会匀速\n次级液滴延迟主体 2~3 帧跟随（follow-through），落地做扁平化 squash\n\n# How to work\n1. Treatment. Write down one clear idea: a hook in the first 0.5 s (never open on more than 0.3 s of empty or black), an\n   escalation, one unmistakable hero moment at ~60–75 % of the runtime, and a composed end frame held ~0.8–1.2 s with living\n   micro-motion. Map every signature feature above to a moment. Keep one cue sheet (beats and hit times) that both the\n   picture and the sound read from.\n2. Key frames before motion. Build the look, render stills at 6–10 key times and actually look at them. Each still should be\n   poster-worthy: composition, hierarchy, negative space, type set properly (kerning, line-height, weight contrast, CJK\n   punctuation, ~5 % safe margins). Iterate until nothing looks default, generic, cramped or \"AI-template\".\n3. Motion. Build the choreography and preview at low resolution. Step through the fastest moves frame by frame: spacing,\n   easing, arcs, overlap, anticipation and follow-through, motion blur. No unintended dead spans; the energy follows the music.\n4. Sound. Write genre-correct music and sound design locked to the cue sheet. Check that visual hits land on audio onsets.\n   Master to the output spec.\n5. Final. Render at full resolution (motion blur where apt), then check: exactly 10.00 s, resolution, fps, audio present and at\n   the right loudness, no black frames, no fallback fonts or tofu, no clipped elements at the frame edges, no shimmer on thin\n   lines, no gradient banding. Fix and repeat until you would submit it to a festival.\n6. Write an honest self-critique: what is strongest, what is weakest, what you would do with more time.\n\n# Rules\n- Rendering must be deterministic: every frame is a pure function of time t. Seeded randomness only; physics and particles\n  precomputed or closed-form; no real-time clocks; no state-triggered CSS transitions. A reliable route: a web page that can\n  draw any time t on request, captured frame by frame with a headless browser and encoded with ffmpeg (or a Blender script\n  that renders a PNG sequence).\n- Assets: only CC0 / public domain / OFL / Apache / free-for-commercial-use. Record each source and licence in CREDITS.md.\n  No real brand trademarks; invent brand names.\n- On-screen text must be correct and natural: idiomatic Chinese, proper CJK punctuation, no typos.\n\n# Craft checklist (what a jury looks for)\n- Style authenticity: an expert names the style in one second; every signature technique is present and executed correctly.\n- Motion craft: purposeful easing (no linear unless mechanical by design), overlap and stagger, anticipation and\n  follow-through, arcs, squash and stretch where the style allows, consistent physics, motion blur where apt; transitions\n  carried by elements, not crossfades.\n- Rhythm: hits locked to the music; contrast between busy and calm; no monotony.\n- Design: strong composition, grid, typographic hierarchy, controlled palette; texture and finishing (grain, glow, vignette)\n  only where the style wants it; nothing looks accidental.\n- Technical polish: no jitter (unless intended), no popping, no aliasing, no banding, no fallback fonts, no half-loaded images.\n- Sound: genre-correct, musical, synced, mastered.\n\n# Before you deliver, score yourself honestly\n1–10 on style fidelity, concept wow, motion craft, design & typography, finish & texture, sound & sync, technical\n(6 = template-level, 8 = high-end agency, 9 = award shortlist). Aim for 9; fix whatever scores lowest first.\n\n# Deliver\nvideo.mp4, plus a short report: the concept in two sentences; a beat sheet with timecodes; how each signature feature is\nrealised; the tech route; check numbers (duration, resolution, fps, loudness, true peak); your honest top-3 weaknesses.\n```\n"},{"id":"S8-V15","name":"综艺花字","series":"S8","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S8-V15.jpg","markdown":"# V15 综艺花字（Variety Captions）\n\n- 分类：V mg-styles-15 · mg-styles-15\n- 适合：9:16 竖屏的综艺花字，竖屏版字幕可以参考\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# 综艺花字 Variety Show Kinetic Captions (Hanazi)\n\n**原片**：喵呜日记 EP.07「今日份快乐」 · 1080×1920 · 30 fps · 10 秒 · AI 评审终评 7.64 / 10\n\n**源码**：[demos/18-hanazi/](../demos/18-hanazi/)\n\n## 怎么用\n\n1. 新建一个空文件夹，在里面打开一个能写代码、能执行命令的 AI 助手（例如 Claude Code）。本机需要能跑网页渲染（Node + Chrome）和 ffmpeg。\n2. 把下面「提示词」整段复制进去发送。\n3. 想换成自己的内容：改 `Creative seed` 里的故事、品牌名、文案和配色，或者改 `Output` 里的尺寸（比如竖屏 1080×1920）。不要删 `Signature features` 那一段，它是这种风格被一眼认出来的依据。\n4. 想再提高：成片出来后，另开一个 AI 用 [rubric.md](../rubric.md) 评审，把评审意见贴回给制作的 AI 再改一轮。原片就是这样改了两轮。\n\n## 提示词\n\n```text\nYou are the director, motion designer, engineer and sound designer of ONE 10-second motion-design film.\nThe goal is the most classic, yet most stunning form of this style — award-shortlist / high-end commercial quality. A clean,\ntemplate-looking result is a fail. Benchmarks: Buck, ManvsMachine, Ordinary Folk, Giant Ant, Territory Studio, Apple keynote\nmotion, Pentagram motion identities, top Behance/Motionographer features, top 抖音/B站 designer accounts.\n\n# Style\n综艺花字 Variety Show Kinetic Captions (Hanazi)\n\n# Output (fixed)\n- 1080×1920, 30 fps, exactly 10.000 s (300 frames), H.264 MP4 with stereo AAC audio (48 kHz), mastered to −14 LUFS\n  integrated, true peak ≤ −1 dBTP. Final file: video.mp4.\n\n# Suggested technical route\nHTML DOM/SVG: multi-layer text-stroke 花字 components + spring pops + SVG burst/sweat/sparkle decorations over a pet-vlog footage layer (CC0 photos with Ken Burns + handheld shake + punch-ins)\n(You may choose a better route if it clearly raises quality; say why.)\n\n# Creative seed\n「萌宠综艺感 vlog」综艺花字 in its native habitat — reactive captions over a pet vlog.\n- Base layer that feels like footage: CC0/PD cat or dog photos from /assets/images with slow Ken Burns, subtle handheld shake, snap punch-in zooms on 'moments' (you may also build a stylised scene if photos look weak).\n- 花字 moments synced to beats: title \"今日份快乐\" (fill gradient + thick white stroke + coloured outer stroke + drop shadow, rounded heavy font), \"？？？\" bouncing question marks, \"震惊!!\" inside a radial 爆炸框 with speed lines + 放射线 flash, \"（小声）\" small aside, \"绝绝子\" with sparkles, sweat drops, a \"猫主子认证\" stamp.\n- Motion: pop 0 → 1.15 → 1.0 in ~8 frames, per-character bounce with 2-frame stagger, slight rotation, gentle wiggle while holding; appear 2–3 frames after the audio/visual moment. High-saturation pink/yellow/cyan with white strokes.\nSound: bouncy variety-show bed (pizzicato/bass/claps) + boing, pop, ding, slide whistle, record scratch on '？？？'.\n\nYou are the director: you may change the concept, copy, story beats and brand names if you find a stronger idea, but the result must remain the CANONICAL, instantly-recognisable form of this style and must hit its signature features below.\n\n# Signature features and key techniques (the result is judged against these)\n源自电视综艺后期的情绪化装饰字幕：多层描边圆体字带弹性入场，跟随人物情绪变色变形，配打击音效。现已下沉为 vlog、萌宠、访谈、搞笑二创的通用包装语言，剪映内置大量花字模板，B 站也有免费花字样式包流通。\n视觉特征 多层描边圆体/胖体字, 高饱和撞色+白边+投影, 弹性 pop 入场, 配合音效出现, 手绘装饰符号（爆炸框/汗滴/闪光）\n关键技法 字体三层结构：填充色+粗白描边+彩色外描边/投影（CSS 可用多层 text-shadow 或 SVG stroke 模拟）\n入场 overshoot：scale 0→1.15→1.0（约 8 帧），或逐字弹跳错帧 2 帧\n情绪匹配：吐槽用歪斜手写体、惊讶用放射线爆炸框、尴尬用汗滴符号\n出现时机对齐笑点/音效，比语音晚 2~3 帧更自然\n长时间驻留花字加轻微 wiggle 保持活性\n\n# How to work\n1. Treatment. Write down one clear idea: a hook in the first 0.5 s (never open on more than 0.3 s of empty or black), an\n   escalation, one unmistakable hero moment at ~60–75 % of the runtime, and a composed end frame held ~0.8–1.2 s with living\n   micro-motion. Map every signature feature above to a moment. Keep one cue sheet (beats and hit times) that both the\n   picture and the sound read from.\n2. Key frames before motion. Build the look, render stills at 6–10 key times and actually look at them. Each still should be\n   poster-worthy: composition, hierarchy, negative space, type set properly (kerning, line-height, weight contrast, CJK\n   punctuation, ~5 % safe margins). Iterate until nothing looks default, generic, cramped or \"AI-template\".\n3. Motion. Build the choreography and preview at low resolution. Step through the fastest moves frame by frame: spacing,\n   easing, arcs, overlap, anticipation and follow-through, motion blur. No unintended dead spans; the energy follows the music.\n4. Sound. Write genre-correct music and sound design locked to the cue sheet. Check that visual hits land on audio onsets.\n   Master to the output spec.\n5. Final. Render at full resolution (motion blur where apt), then check: exactly 10.00 s, resolution, fps, audio present and at\n   the right loudness, no black frames, no fallback fonts or tofu, no clipped elements at the frame edges, no shimmer on thin\n   lines, no gradient banding. Fix and repeat until you would submit it to a festival.\n6. Write an honest self-critique: what is strongest, what is weakest, what you would do with more time.\n\n# Rules\n- Rendering must be deterministic: every frame is a pure function of time t. Seeded randomness only; physics and particles\n  precomputed or closed-form; no real-time clocks; no state-triggered CSS transitions. A reliable route: a web page that can\n  draw any time t on request, captured frame by frame with a headless browser and encoded with ffmpeg (or a Blender script\n  that renders a PNG sequence).\n- Assets: only CC0 / public domain / OFL / Apache / free-for-commercial-use. Record each source and licence in CREDITS.md.\n  No real brand trademarks; invent brand names.\n- On-screen text must be correct and natural: idiomatic Chinese, proper CJK punctuation, no typos.\n\n# Craft checklist (what a jury looks for)\n- Style authenticity: an expert names the style in one second; every signature technique is present and executed correctly.\n- Motion craft: purposeful easing (no linear unless mechanical by design), overlap and stagger, anticipation and\n  follow-through, arcs, squash and stretch where the style allows, consistent physics, motion blur where apt; transitions\n  carried by elements, not crossfades.\n- Rhythm: hits locked to the music; contrast between busy and calm; no monotony.\n- Design: strong composition, grid, typographic hierarchy, controlled palette; texture and finishing (grain, glow, vignette)\n  only where the style wants it; nothing looks accidental.\n- Technical polish: no jitter (unless intended), no popping, no aliasing, no banding, no fallback fonts, no half-loaded images.\n- Sound: genre-correct, musical, synced, mastered.\n\n# Before you deliver, score yourself honestly\n1–10 on style fidelity, concept wow, motion craft, design & typography, finish & texture, sound & sync, technical\n(6 = template-level, 8 = high-end agency, 9 = award shortlist). Aim for 9; fix whatever scores lowest first.\n\n# Deliver\nvideo.mp4, plus a short report: the concept in two sentences; a beat sheet with timecodes; how each signature feature is\nrealised; the tech route; check numbers (duration, resolution, fps, loudness, true peak); your honest top-3 weaknesses.\n```\n"},{"id":"S9-X1","name":"手绘","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X1.jpg","markdown":"# X1 手绘（Hand-drawn）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：讲解、记录\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: hand-drawn + 8-bit minis (default)\n\nWarm empty paper, a thin ink horizon, and an 8-bit mascot that acts out the story, cut against dark title cards and arcade screens.\n\nReference frames from the original: `<skill>/docs/styles/handdrawn.jpg`.\n\n## Signature\n| # | Item |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Plain warm off-white paper with no grid; one thin ink horizon with grass tufts; outline clouds |\n| 2 | The logo mascot as an 8-bit sprite that acts out the beats: pushes, smashes, stands on top of the winning bar |\n| 3 | A dark title card: small serif \"Introducing\", the name typing in a large serif, the accent part (version, product word) in the brand colour with a hand underline, a script subline |\n| 4 | Level structure: a small mono `WORLD 1-1` tag in the top-left of each beat and one pixel-font \"WORLD 2\" interstitial card |\n| 5 | Numbers as serif count-ups with a bold label and a script note; hatched-vs-solid bars; a pixel HI-SCORE board on a dark screen; a price tag struck through |\n| 6 | One full-bleed brand-colour scene entered with a torn-paper transition |\n| 7 | Ending: the logo mark and the serif name with its underline, a short \"available now\" line, a spaced list of where to get it, the mascot on the horizon |\n\n## Look\n- **Palette**: paper `#f4f2ee`, ink `#2b2a28`, dark `#1c1b1a`, plus the brand accent (Claude orange `#D97757` in the original). Nothing else is coloured.\n- **Fonts**: Source Serif 4 (titles, numbers), Caveat (notes, sublines), Press Start 2P (HI-SCORE, WORLD cards), IBM Plex Mono (tags, small values), Geist (bold labels). For Korean use Nanum Myeongjo, Gaegu, Galmuri11, NanumGothicCoding, and Pretendard in the same roles (unicode-range pairs, see kit-api.md).\n- **Lines**: thin (2–2.5 px) and calm, with a gentle wobble; this is pen on paper, not a marker.\n- **Density**: one idea per beat, lots of empty paper.\n"},{"id":"S9-X2","name":"品牌动态图形","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X2.jpg","markdown":"# X2 品牌动态图形（Brand motion graphics）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：频道片头\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: brand motion graphics\n\nA clean explainer that looks like an official brand asset: one dark field, thin white hand-drawn line art, bold type with a single accent, scribbled emphasis, and a persuasion arc from pain to call to action. Silent.\n\nReference frames from the original: `<skill>/docs/styles/motion.jpg`.\n\n## Signature\n| # | Item |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | One dark field for the whole video (no light scenes), one accent colour, pain words in red |\n| 2 | Thin white hand-drawn line illustrations of everyday objects that draw themselves on (faucet, manual, clock, eye, wallet, camera, mic) |\n| 3 | Bold geometric sans headlines that pop in word by word, the key word in the accent |\n| 4 | Hand-drawn emphasis on type: a scribbled oval around a key word, a swoosh under a phrase |\n| 5 | A thin \"frustration\" gauge that climbs across the pain beats |\n| 6 | Proof drawn as line art: a bar chart with hatched pain bars and one tiny accent bar; a big accent number; a cloud of outline pill tags with one filled |\n| 7 | The product shown working: a line-art phone with camera corners, the question in an outline bubble with a mic and a live waveform, the answer in a filled accent bubble |\n| 8 | Ending: the wordmark with an accent full stop and swoosh, a glowing pill CTA that the cursor clicks, the free-to-try line and URL |\n\n## Look\n- **Palette**: background `#0c0b1d`, ink `#f2f1f7`, dim `#8f8da8`, pain `#ff5d5d`, camera cyan `#38d6e6`, and the brand's accent (read it from the site CSS or logo pixels; the original used its purple `#7c5cff`). If the brand is light-themed, still keep the dark field and carry the brand in the accent and logo.\n- **Fonts**: Poppins SemiBold/Bold (or the brand's geometric sans), IBM Plex Mono for tiny labels. Korean: Pretendard in the same roles.\n- **Lines**: white, 2.5–3 px, a slight wobble: a steady hand, not a sketch.\n"},{"id":"S9-X3","name":"沙画","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X3.jpg","markdown":"# X3 沙画（Sand art）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：回忆、情绪，感受讲解很合适\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: sand art chronicle\n\nScenes poured in dark sand on a glowing light table, lit by sunbursts and night skies, that break into grains and blow away between eras. Music and sound effects. Suits stories told through time.\n\nReference frames from the original: `<skill>/docs/styles/sand.jpg`.\n\n## Signature\n| # | Item |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | A parchment light table with visible grain everywhere, dark brown sand with grainy soft edges |\n| 2 | Silhouettes, not outlines: buildings, vehicles, people, landscapes as solid shapes, often moving (a train crossing, a plane flying) |\n| 3 | The year in an old-style serif in a top corner with a short italic caption under it; no heavy sans captions |\n| 4 | Scene changes where the picture breaks into grains and blows away, or is swept off by hand, then the next is poured in |\n| 5 | Lighting that changes with the story: a sunburst with rays, a glowing sun, a night sky with a moon and stars cut out of the sand, fireworks |\n| 6 | Grain as material: smoke, crowds, water, and dust drawn as scattered specks, not flat fills |\n| 7 | Title and ending built from a ring of stars or an emblem around one big serif number or name, with the dates in serif |\n\n## Look\n- **Palette**: table `#f3dcae` → `#9b7648`, sand `#2e2014`. Sand is one colour; the brand shows only in the final logo card and, at most, one accent.\n- **Fonts**: Cormorant (medium) for years and titles, Cormorant Italic for captions. Korean: Nanum Myeongjo for both. Captions stay small (22–30 px) and elegant; the picture carries the scene.\n\n## Rules\n- Keep captions and years on a clear patch of the table; grain behind text makes it hard to read.\n"},{"id":"S9-X4","name":"歌词 MV","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X4.jpg","markdown":"# X4 歌词 MV（Lyric music video）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：金句、配乐段\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: lyric music video\n\nAn educational music video that reads like a technical explainer set to a song: a dark grid, a section label in the corner, one diagram per lyric line, and the sung line at the bottom. The engine makes the instrumental; the lyrics are on screen, not sung.\n\nReference frames from the original: `<skill>/docs/styles/lyric.jpg`.\n\n## Signature\n| # | Item |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | A dark navy field with a faint grid and vignette in every scene |\n| 2 | The song section in a small mono label top-left (`VERSE 1 · how Jev differs from a chat model`), the title top-right, a thin progress line along the top |\n| 3 | The sung line at the bottom under a hairline: sung words bright, the rest dim |\n| 4 | One diagram per line, centred: node boxes joined by connectors with a travelling pulse, JSON/code cards, probability bars, a stopwatch or a big glowing value, a crossing-curves chart |\n| 5 | The chorus repeats the same diagram with a new accent each time it returns (cyan, pink, yellow, green) |\n| 6 | Intro and outro: the name in a glowing ring, the subtitle in the accent, \"an educational music video\", the about line, the song title between ♪ marks |\n\n## Look\n- **Palette**: bg `#0b0f1e`, ink `#e9edf6`, dim `#5e6886`, cards `#121832`, accents cyan `#35d0e6`, pink `#ff5cc8`, yellow `#ffc53a`, green `#4ade80`. If the brand has a strong colour, make it the first accent.\n- **Fonts**: IBM Plex Sans (semibold/bold) and IBM Plex Mono. Korean: Pretendard and NanumGothicCoding.\n- **Diagrams**: flat, precise, thin 2 px borders, 10 px radius; no hand-drawn wobble, no illustrations of scenery. Each diagram builds on the line's first beat and holds while the line is sung.\n\n## Rules\n- Every lyric line is one researched fact with its own diagram.\n- The lyrics are on screen, not sung: do not claim the video has vocals.\n"},{"id":"S9-X5","name":"卡点素材剪辑","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X5.jpg","markdown":"# X5 卡点素材剪辑（Beat-synced footage）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：快剪\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: beat-synced footage\n\nA high-end, minimal promo cut to a song: a hook that lands word by word on a light stage, the product UI, then real clips on a dark stage as a scanned wall, a 3D carousel, and a phone next to a campaign panel, with stats on push cuts and every cut on a beat.\n\nReference frames from the original: `<skill>/docs/styles/beat.jpg`.\n\n## Inputs to ask for\n- 8 to 20 clips the user owns (vertical works best), plus 3 to 5 product or UI moments to show\n- a song the user may use commercially (their own, or a library track whose licence allows it); if there is none, synthesize one with `audio.py`\n- the product name, the one-line promise, one accent colour, and the numbers to show (from the research or the user, never invented)\n\n## Signature\n| # | Item |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | The hook lands word by word on the beats on a warm light stage, with one word hidden as an accent block |\n| 2 | The product UI: the masked block grows into a clean window where a cursor types an input and clicks the button |\n| 3 | The drop into a near-black stage with a warm floor glow |\n| 4 | A wall of real vertical clips scanned by a light bar, all but three winners dimmed, winners framed with a score tag |\n| 5 | The key output as big type or ranked rows with scores |\n| 6 | A 3D carousel of clips with floor reflections that whips onto one hero clip |\n| 7 | The hero clip in a phone next to a settings panel: toggles switch on, the button turns green, the panel flips into results |\n| 8 | Big stats on push cuts with motion blur, a three-word ticker with the last word in the accent, then the logo |\n\n## Look\n- **Palette**: light stage `#f7f3ef` with peach glows `#ffd9c4`, dark stage `#0b0b0c` with an orange floor glow, ink `#151515` / `#f4f4f4`, one accent (the brand's; the original used orange `#ff5a1f`), success green `#22c55e` for the button only.\n- **Type**: one clean sans with tight tracking (Geist or Inter), a mono for tiny labels. Korean: Pretendard. No full stops in on-screen text.\n\n## Rules\n- Every cut lands on a downbeat, every UI hit on a beat.\n"},{"id":"S9-X6","name":"界面形变","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X6.jpg","markdown":"# X6 界面形变（UI morph）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：演示工具\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: UI morph\n\nOne interface element that never cuts: a button becomes a loader, a player, a slider, tabs, a chart, a command palette, a toast, and finally the button again, so the video loops. A cursor clicks and drags to cause every change, on the beat. Reference frames are in `docs/styles/uimorph.jpg`.\n\n## Signature\n| # | Item |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | A single shape, never cut: every state is the same element changing size, corner radius, and fill |\n| 2 | Content swaps inside it with a short blur, entering and leaving on its own timing |\n| 3 | A cursor causes every change with real clicks and drags; dragged values follow it and spring back on release |\n| 4 | Spring motion with at most a tiny overshoot; tab indicators and knobs stretch because their edges ride different springs |\n| 5 | Real UI states the subject would have (its buttons, player, sliders, tabs, chart, command palette, toast), in black and white on a light warm grey, one clean UI font |\n| 6 | Something happens on every beat; the camera zooms so each state fills the frame |\n| 7 | The last frame is the first frame, cursor included, so it loops |\n\n## Look\n- **Palette**: canvas `#ecebe7`, ink `#0b0b0b`, cards `#ffffff`, secondary text `#8d8c88`. Colour appears only in content such as album art or the subject's own accent; no gradients or glows on the UI itself.\n- **Fonts**: Geist (Pretendard for Hangul), Geist Mono for times and shortcuts.\n- **Motion**: springs everywhere; no bouncy easing, particles, or dead time.\n"},{"id":"S9-X7","name":"英雄漫画","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X7.jpg","markdown":"# X7 英雄漫画（Hero comic）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：热血、转折\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: hero comic\n\nA superhero comic book come to life: newsprint pages, thick ink panels, Ben-Day halftone, yellow narration boxes, speech balloons, burst lettering, and a red title slab over flipping pages.\n\n## Signature\n| # | Item |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Pages of panels with thick ink borders and white gutters on newsprint; slanted panels for action beats |\n| 2 | Ben-Day halftone for shading, skies, and backgrounds |\n| 3 | Yellow narration boxes in uppercase comic lettering |\n| 4 | Speech or thought balloons with tails |\n| 5 | Sound effects in burst shapes (POW!, ZAP!, BOOM!) on impact beats |\n| 6 | Focus lines behind the hero moment |\n| 7 | Opening: pages flip past under a red title slab; scene changes turn the page |\n\n## Look\n- **Palette**: newsprint `#f4ecd6`, ink `#141414`, comic red `#e23b2e`, yellow `#ffd43b`, blue `#2a6fdb`, cyan `#59c3e8`; put the brand colour on the hero's costume or the title slab.\n- **Fonts**: Bangers for Latin; pair it with Do Hyeon (or Black Han Sans) for Hangul through `unicode-range`.\n- **Characters**: flat bold shapes with 7–9 px ink outlines and halftone shading. A logo mascot can be the hero; a pixel mini drawn large with an ink outline also works.\n"},{"id":"S9-X8","name":"手账剪贴本","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X8.jpg","markdown":"# X8 手账剪贴本（Scrapbook）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：记录这三个月\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: scrapbook\n\nA making-of journal on a kraft desk: lined notebook pages, polaroids held by washi tape, torn paper labels, die-cut stickers, rubber stamps, and marker doodles.\n\n## Signature\n| # | Item |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | A kraft paper desk and lined notebook pages at slight angles |\n| 2 | Polaroids of real images (screenshots, logos, product shots) held by washi tape, with handwritten captions |\n| 3 | Handwritten notes and dates in marker or pen |\n| 4 | Torn paper labels for key words |\n| 5 | Die-cut stickers and a rubber stamp for milestones |\n| 6 | Marker doodles that point and circle: arrows, stars, underlines |\n| 7 | Scene changes as a new page sliding over the desk |\n\n## Look\n- **Palette**: kraft `#cdb38d`, notebook `#fbf8f0`, ink `#2b2622`, red pen `#d94f3d`, blue pen `#3b6fb6`, tape beige and mint. The brand appears in the photos, stickers, and one accent.\n- **Fonts**: Caveat / Gaegu for notes, Permanent Marker / Black Han Sans for headings and stamps, IBM Plex Mono for typed labels.\n- **Layout**: things overlap and tilt (±2–8°), never on a grid; leave some desk showing.\n"},{"id":"S9-X9","name":"撕纸拼贴","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X9.jpg","markdown":"# X9 撕纸拼贴（Torn-paper collage）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：回忆、拼凑\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: torn-paper collage\n\nPictures built from torn scraps of watercolour-tinted paper, laid one by one on a sheet of cold-press paper: skies and seas in long strips, rocks and crowds in small shards, a sun as a torn disc. Calm, handmade, and warm.\n\n## Signature\n| # | Item |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | A cold-press paper sheet as the ground, never a flat fill |\n| 2 | Every shape is torn paper: a jagged edge with a white fibre rim and a soft shadow; no clean vector shapes |\n| 3 | Colour as washed, muted tints with visible blotches, like watercolour on paper |\n| 4 | Pictures assemble scrap by scrap, back to front: strips for sky, water, and fields; shards for textured masses |\n| 5 | The subject's logo or product rebuilt from torn scraps |\n| 6 | Words set in serif ink, each on its own torn strip, landing one after another |\n| 7 | Scene changes as a fresh sheet with a torn edge sliding over, or scraps peeling off |\n\n## Look\n- **Palette**: paper `#f1ece1`, ink `#3a3833`, and muted tints (sky `#b8c9da`, sea `#6f8dab`, sand `#e2cfa8`, sun `#efc25c`, stone `#8e8b85`, slate `#5f6570`, moss `#8b8d5c`, rust `#c7684e`). Bring the brand in as one or two washed tints and the rebuilt logo; keep everything a little desaturated.\n- **Fonts**: Source Serif 4 / Nanum Myeongjo for words on strips, Caveat / Gaegu for a pencil note.\n- **Composition**: one picture per scene, filling about two-thirds of the frame with a margin of bare paper around it. Pictures are simple and flat, like a children's book, with pieces that overlap and overshoot their outlines a little. Use scraps a hand could tear: strips 40–90 px tall, shards 60–140 px.\n"},{"id":"S9-X10","name":"粒子","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X10.jpg","markdown":"# X10 粒子（Particles）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：氛围\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: particles\n\nThousands of glowing particles drift on a flow field, gather into words, logos, and numbers, then scatter and re-form. The purest \"drawn in code\" look.\n\n## Signature\n| # | Item |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | A near-black field with thousands of short glowing streaks drifting on a smooth flow |\n| 2 | Words and numbers that form out of the drift and hold with a gentle shimmer |\n| 3 | The logo or a symbol formed from particles (sampled from the logo image or a path) |\n| 4 | Morphs from one shape straight into the next |\n| 5 | A burst that scatters everything before the next idea |\n| 6 | Bloom on everything |\n| 7 | A crisp caption under each formed shape |\n\n## Look\n- **Palette**: background `#05060b`; particles in cyan, violet, white, and pink, with the brand colours among them.\n- **Fonts**: Geist (heavy weights sample best) for particle text; Pretendard Black for Hangul.\n\n## Rules\n- Keep particle text at 150–260 px so the particles read as letters.\n"},{"id":"S9-X11","name":"翻牌显示板","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X11.jpg","markdown":"# X11 翻牌显示板（Split-flap board）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：机场那种翻牌，适合标题和数字\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: split-flap board\n\nA railway departures board: tiles clatter through the alphabet onto each message, amber lamps blink, and a station clock ticks.\n\n## Signature\n| # | Item |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | A dark board of individual flap tiles with a hinge line and a split between halves |\n| 2 | Messages land by flipping through characters, column after column |\n| 3 | A departures table: time, destination, gate or code, status |\n| 4 | Status lamps in amber, green, red; one blinks (BOARDING, NEW, NOW) |\n| 5 | A header with the board title and a clock |\n| 6 | The clatter: flap clicks placed on the flips |\n\n## Look\n- **Palette**: board `#0f0f10`, tiles `#232326`, characters `#f1ede2`, amber `#ffb000`, green `#39d353`, red `#ff5a4f`. The brand shows in one status colour and the final message.\n- **Fonts**: Roboto Mono (bold) for Latin; Pretendard Bold for Hangul (Hangul tiles flip through a few letters, then land).\n- **Messages**: uppercase, short (tiles are wide); one idea per board change.\n"},{"id":"S9-X12","name":"霓虹灯牌","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X12.jpg","markdown":"# X12 霓虹灯牌（Neon sign）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：金句\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: neon sign\n\nGlass tubes on a dark brick wall that buzz and flicker on, glow onto the wall, and reflect on a wet floor.\n\n## Signature\n| # | Item |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | A dark brick wall with a wet floor |\n| 2 | Words as neon tubes: coloured halo, glow, white-hot core |\n| 3 | Tubes flicker and buzz on before they hold steady; unlit tubes show as dark glass |\n| 4 | Icons, arrows, and frames bent from tube |\n| 5 | Light spilling onto the wall and a reflection in the floor |\n| 6 | The buzz: a click on each flicker |\n\n## Look\n- **Palette**: wall `#1b1417`; tubes pink `#ff4fa3`, cyan `#3ff0ff`, yellow `#ffe45c`, green `#6bff8a`, orange `#ff8a3d`, violet `#b56bff`. Make the brand colour the main tube.\n- **Fonts**: Tilt Neon for Latin; Pretendard (light weights) for Hangul, drawn as strokes.\n- **Composition**: one sign per scene, centred or offset like a shop sign; at most three tube colours at once.\n"},{"id":"S9-X13","name":"16-bit 街机","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X13.jpg","markdown":"# X13 16-bit 街机（16-bit arcade）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：游戏感\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: 16-bit arcade\n\nA 16-bit console game come to life: a starry violet night, pixel sprites, CRT scanlines, and game UI that turns the story into a run from INSERT COIN to GAME CLEAR.\n\n## Signature\n| Item | On screen |\n|---|---|\n| Stage | A deep violet night sky (`#10081f` → `#1b0f3a` → `#5a2a6e`) with twinkling pixel stars and faint CRT scanlines over everything |\n| Type | Press Start 2P for Latin and Galmuri11 Bold for Hangul, kept crisp at multiples of 8 px and 11 px; gold `#ffd23f` titles, white text, soft lilac `#d9ccff` sublines |\n| Opening | INSERT COIN, then the lines drop in with a small screen shake |\n| Title | The game title, a hero sprite, and a blinking PRESS START |\n| Steps | A STAGE SELECT map where the hero hops from node to node, each node a step or feature, labels on a dark plate |\n| Numbers | HIGH SCORE: the value counts up under its label |\n| Ending | GAME CLEAR with a dialog box carrying the call to action |\n"},{"id":"S9-X14","name":"CRT 终端","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X14.jpg","markdown":"# X14 CRT 终端（CRT terminal）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：讲代码、讲 AI\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: CRT terminal\n\nA green phosphor terminal on an old CRT: commands type themselves out, logs scroll, progress bars fill, and big block-letter banners glow for the name and the numbers.\n\n## Signature\n| Item | On screen |\n|---|---|\n| Screen | Near-black green glass (`#031208`) with phosphor text `#46ff8a`, a soft glow, scanlines, and a slight curvature vignette |\n| Type | Nanum Gothic Coding (regular and bold) for every line, prompt `user@studio:~$`; amber `#ffc24a` for highlights |\n| Opening | A boot line, then the opening lines typed at the prompt with a blinking block cursor |\n| Title | `./intro` runs and prints the name as a large block-letter banner |\n| Steps | A `make` style log where each step ends in an `[ OK ]` tag, with a progress bar filling underneath |\n| Numbers | A `stats` command prints the value as a block-letter banner with its label |\n| Ending | The call to action typed as a command, the URL, and `exit` |\n"},{"id":"S9-X15","name":"热敏小票","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X15.jpg","markdown":"# X15 热敏小票（Thermal receipt）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：清单、\"算一笔账\"\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: thermal receipt\n\nA thermal receipt printer on a teal desk: every scene prints a fresh slip that rises out of the printer, gets stamped, and is torn off. Works in 16:9 and 9:16.\n\n## Signature\n| Item | On screen |\n|---|---|\n| Desk | A teal tabletop (`#2e5b5f` / `#3c7277`) with a dark printer at the bottom edge |\n| Slip | Off-white thermal paper `#f6f3ea` with dark ink `#2c3038`, a zigzag torn edge, dashed rules, and a barcode; the slip grows up out of the printer line by line |\n| Type | Nanum Gothic Coding for the receipt lines and Black Han Sans for large headings |\n| Opening | A NOTICE slip with the opening lines |\n| Title | A slip with the name printed large |\n| Steps | An ORDER slip with checkboxes ticked one by one |\n| Numbers | A TOTAL slip with the value as the total |\n| Ending | A thank-you slip with a red stamp `#d23a3a`, then the slip is torn off |\n"},{"id":"S9-X16","name":"地铁线路图","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X16.jpg","markdown":"# X16 地铁线路图（Transit map）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：讲一路怎么走过来的，适合 04 段\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: transit map\n\nA transit map and station signage: coloured lines are drawn across cream paper, stations light up one by one, a train runs the line, and the ending is the terminus sign.\n\n## Signature\n| Item | On screen |\n|---|---|\n| Ground | Cream map paper `#f4f0e6`, ink `#1d1d22`, muted `#6f6a60` for small print |\n| Lines | Thick rounded route lines in the line colours (terracotta `#d9774f`, navy `#23408e`) with white-ringed station dots and 45° bends |\n| Signage | Navy station signs `#1f2a44` with white Pretendard (Regular, Bold, Black) text |\n| Opening | A station sign board carrying the opening lines |\n| Title | A logo, the name, and a line drawn across the frame |\n| Steps | A line with numbered stations and a train moving from station to station |\n| Numbers | The value in a badge in the line colour |\n| Ending | The terminus sign and a ticket with the call to action |\n"},{"id":"S9-X17","name":"蓝图","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X17.jpg","markdown":"# X17 蓝图（Blueprint）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：讲结构\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: blueprint\n\nA cyanotype engineering drawing: white linework on blueprint blue, dimension lines, numbered balloons, and a title block, as if the product were being specified on a drafting table.\n\n## Signature\n| Item | On screen |\n|---|---|\n| Sheet | Blueprint blue `#17508c` with a faint grid and a thin border; lines in near-white, construction lines faint |\n| Type | IBM Plex Mono and Nanum Gothic Coding in capitals, like drafting lettering; yellow `#ffd166` for the highlight |\n| Title block | A title block in the lower right with the drawing number, sheet name, and date |\n| Opening | NOTE lines with dashed underlines |\n| Title | The name with a dimension line carrying the tagline |\n| Steps | Process boxes joined by arrows, each tagged with a numbered balloon |\n| Numbers | A huge value dimensioned by its label |\n| Ending | A release sheet with a stamp |\n"},{"id":"S9-X18","name":"漫画书","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X18.jpg","markdown":"# X18 漫画书（Comic book issue）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：分格讲故事\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: comic book issue (cover, page turns, splash pages)\n\nA video that stages its content as a physical vintage comic book being read on camera: a portrait-oriented comic page/spread sits centered in the landscape frame against a near-black vignette background, and a virtual camera pans and pushes into individual panels the way a motion-comic or Ken Burns documentary reveals a print page. The book opens with a magazine-style cover, then turns through several two-page spreads of a 2x2 panel grid, and closes on a colophon-style back cover. Panels are built from flat pop-art color blocks (red, gold/orange, blue), Ben-Day halftone dot textures, and sunburst ray backgrounds, with comic sound-effect bursts and a speech-bubble mascot giving it an energetic, Silver-Age-superhero-comic / 1960s advertising-comic feel. The mood is upbeat, punchy, and declarative, reinforced by a continuously driving instrumental music bed with frequent hard transient hits.\n\n## Signature\n| Item | On screen |\n|---|---|\n| Base/ground | Near-black navy vignette (~#000010–#101020) fills the canvas outside the page; the comic page/spread itself sits on a cream/off-white paper color (~#f8f8f0, ~#f0e8d0) |\n| Panel backgrounds | Flat color blocks per panel: warm red (~#e03028), golden-yellow/orange (~#f8d038, ~#f8b020), sky/cobalt blue (~#38b8e0, ~#1848a0, ~#3068c8), plus a dark maroon (~#581010, ~#902018) on the \"next issue\" teaser page |\n| Texture | Radiating sunburst/rays behind hero panels, overlaid with small Ben-Day halftone dots; blue \"text placeholder\" panels use short horizontal line-bars instead of real text to suggest dense copy |\n| Shapes and outlines | Every panel, badge, speech bubble, and character has a thick, uniform black outline with rounded corners; comparison bars/columns are flat-filled rectangles with the same outline treatment |\n| Palette accents | Bright cyan starburst price badge, red jagged comic \"impact\" bursts behind headline numbers, yellow badge with a red circular \"call-out\" sticker |\n| Typography | Bold, chunky, rounded display lettering (comic-lettering style) in black with a white/cream outline halo and drop shadow, used for headlines and big stat callouts; smaller captions sit in yellow strip banners with black text |\n| Mascot | A single small orange/terracotta asterisk-flower-shaped blob character wearing a black domino mask and a short red cape, used as a recurring \"spokes-character\" that reacts, speaks via word balloons, and physically stands on bar-chart columns |\n| Composition | Cover = single portrait panel with a red masthead strip (issue number/date box + logo) over a full-bleed sunburst; interior pages = symmetric two-page spread, each page split into a 2x2 grid of rounded-rect panels separated by a visible book gutter/spine with page-thickness shading |\n\n## Direction\nDesign a comic-book / pop-art motion piece: present the content as a physical vintage comic magazine — a cover, then several two-page 2x2-panel spreads, then a colophon back cover — rendered as a portrait page centered in a landscape frame with a plain dark vignette backdrop. Build each panel from flat, saturated primary/secondary color blocks (red, gold-orange, blue, occasional deep maroon), radiating sunburst ray backgrounds, and Ben-Day halftone dot textures. Outline every shape, panel border, badge, and character uniformly in thick black with rounded corners. Use a single small recurring mascot character with a simple, friendly, rounded silhouette (mask/cape optional) that reacts to on-panel data via word balloons and physically interacts with chart elements (e.g., standing on a bar). Render headline numbers and key stats in chunky, rounded, black-outlined comic-lettering type with drop shadows, punctuated by jagged comic \"impact\" burst shapes and badge stickers for emphasis. Animate primarily through a slow virtual camera (push-ins and pans across a mostly-static page) rather than character animation, revealing one panel's information at a time with quick pop/scale-in beats for each new element (numbers, strikethroughs, bursts, speech bubbles). Transition between spreads with a paper page-turn/curl effect showing page thickness and a shadowed gutter/spine. Score it with a single continuous, bright, driving instrumental track at a brisk up-tempo pace, mixed hot and consistently loud with frequent percussive transient hits, and align at least one musical riser/impact hit to the video's biggest on-screen reveal. Keep the overall tone punchy, declarative, and optimistic, like a vintage superhero-comic advertisement.\n"},{"id":"S9-X19","name":"纪录片","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X19.jpg","markdown":"# X19 纪录片（Documentary）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：认真讲一件事\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: documentary case file\n\nA piece that reads like a serious investigative-documentary case file rather than a marketing reel: a near-black canvas holding a slowly turning low-poly \"network sphere\" emblem, chaptered like folder tabs (\"Chapter 1 — ...\", \"Chapter 2 — ...\"), with aged paper \"evidence cards\" laid under a desk lamp, minimal data-journalism bar charts, and one dramatized \"witness testimony\" segment shot as a backlit, explicitly-labeled graphic silhouette. Everything moves slowly and settles rather than snaps; captions build up phrase by phrase like narration being typed out; a hushed, low ambient bed with a slow recurring pluck tone underscores the whole thing, broken only by a hard opening hit and a bright closing chime. The net effect is hushed, credible, forensic — closer to a true-crime/60-Minutes case-file open than a tech-hype trailer.\n\n## Signature\n| Item | On screen |\n|---|---|\n| Backdrop | Pure black canvas (`#000000`) scattered with faint pinpoint \"stars\" and dim warm embers; a soft diagonal light-wedge/vignette appears only in the reenactment scene, along with a few out-of-focus glowing bokeh orbs (dust-in-a-spotlight look) |\n| Emblem | A translucent low-poly sphere made of thin connecting lines and node-dots (\"network globe\"), rendered in dim bronze/amber, recurring at the open and close as a logo/chapter device |\n| Palette | Near-black ground; ivory/cream display type (`#E8DED2`); warm coral-terracotta accent used for dates, the current/highlighted data point, and stamps of emphasis (`#D07152`); muted brick-red \"correction ink\" used only for strike-through of an old number and a circular rubber stamp (`#96473C`); aged, grain-textured cream paper for evidence cards (`#D5CAB4`); charcoal gray for inactive/comparison bars (`#403D38`) |\n| Typography | Large numerals and section headlines set in a high-contrast serif with bracketed serifs (old-book / editorial feel); dates and percentages set in tabular, fixed-width digits; the chapter tag and bottom-left source line set in a small, letter-spaced sans-serif |\n| Evidence cards | Flat rectangles of photographed, grain-textured paper that drop in at a slight perspective tilt (as if just placed on a desk under a lamp) and settle flat over about half a second, divided by thin hairline rules, with a hand-drawn-style red line striking an old figure, an arrow pointing to the replacement, and a round ink stamp sealing the change |\n| Charts | Minimal horizontal bar charts directly on the black canvas: faint dotted vertical gridlines, one bar picked out in the coral accent while the rest stay charcoal gray, bar length and its number counting up together as it reveals |\n| Chapter/citation system | Every scene carries a tracked-out \"chapter N — title\" tag top-left (like a case-file folder tab) and a small citation/source line bottom-left, exactly like a documentary lower-third footnote |\n| Reenactment device | For the \"testimony\" chapter, a deliberately abstract, backlit human silhouette (not photographic) stands inside the light-wedge with a small on-screen disclaimer that it is a graphic reconstruction, plus a lower-third name/title card and a quote building up in quotation marks |\n\n## Direction\nProduce a dark documentary/case-file explainer. Canvas is near-black throughout, with a slowly rotating low-poly \"network sphere\" made of thin connected lines and glowing node-dots as a recurring logo/chapter device, plus faint pinpoint stars and warm embers drifting in the background. Structure the piece as numbered chapters (a small tracked-out \"Chapter N — title\" tag top-left on every scene, a small source/citation line bottom-left), each chapter cross-dissolving into the next — never a hard cut — with the outgoing scene ghosting under the incoming one for about half a second. Use a warm, restrained palette: near-black ground, ivory/cream serif display type for big numbers and headlines, tabular/monospaced digits for dates and stats, one warm coral-terracotta accent color reserved for the current data point or emphasis, a muted brick-red reserved only for \"correction\" marks (a strike-through line, a circular ink stamp), and charcoal gray for de-emphasized/comparison elements. Build at least one scene as a flat \"evidence card\" — an aged, grain-textured paper rectangle that drops in on a slight tilt and settles flat, showing a simple labeled table or figure with an old value struck through in red and a new value beside it. Build at least one scene as a minimal horizontal bar chart directly on the dark canvas, with faint dotted gridlines and exactly one bar in the accent color while the rest stay neutral gray, the bar length and its number counting up together on reveal. Build one \"testimony\" scene using a deliberately non-photographic, backlit human silhouette in a soft diagonal light-wedge, with a small disclaimer that the figure is a graphic reconstruction, a lower-third name/title card, and a quote that builds up phrase by phrase in quotation marks. Bottom-of-frame narration captions should also build up progressively, clause by clause, holding briefly before being replaced. Score it with a hushed ambient bed (roughly -20 to -25dB, well below dialogue/caption level) carrying a soft plucked/mallet tone with a short ascending harmonic ring that repeats roughly every 1.2-1.5 seconds — a slow, deliberate, heartbeat-like pulse, not a dance beat — plus a sharp low hit at the very open and a brighter swell or chime at the close when the logo returns. Keep the whole thing measured and unhurried: soft fades and settles rather than snaps, generous holds on each data point, and a serious, forensic, credible tone throughout.\n"},{"id":"S9-X20","name":"博物馆展览","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X20.jpg","markdown":"# X20 博物馆展览（Museum exhibit）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：把东西一件件摆出来讲\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: museum exhibit tour\n\nA piece that plays like a nighttime museum special-exhibit walkthrough: a single visitor moves room to room through a pitch-dark gallery where each \"diorama\" is picked out by one hanging spotlight, a low-poly artifact on a pedestal, a cream museum wall-label, and a docent's caption bar typing out commentary along the bottom. The overall feeling is hushed, reverent, and slightly theatrical — closer to an after-hours exhibit tour than a tech product demo, with warm sepia light carving objects out of near-total black.\n\n## Signature\n| Item | On screen |\n|---|---|\n| Background | Near-black warm charcoal (`#070604`), a faint horizontal-plank floor fading into darkness, heavy vignette; only the spotlight cone and its objects are ever lit. |\n| Spotlight | One vertical cone per shot from a small black ceiling fixture (soft halo glow), rendered as a warm sepia/amber beam (`#6A5A43`-ish) with tiny drifting white dust-mote particles inside it; the beam fades down and \"searches\" to reveal the next scene rather than the camera simply cutting. |\n| Hero object | A faceted low-poly gem/crystal (flat-shaded polygons, hard facet edges, no smooth gradients) floating over a plain cylindrical pedestal, sometimes inside a hairline-outline glass vitrine; lit facets in bright copper-orange (`#FF9C60`), shadowed facets in dark maroon-brown (`#653728`). It rotates slowly in place. |\n| Museum label card | A solid cream/parchment plaque (`#DAD2C5`–`#EADEC6`) with a small \"No. X.X\" eyebrow, a dark warm-gray serif headline, a thin rule divider, plain sans-serif detail lines, and a tiny gray \"출처(source):\" credit line at the bottom — an exact wall-text layout. |\n| Data as artifacts | Numbers are never plain charts: pricing appears as two hanging paper price-tags (hole-punch + string, old price struck through, new price crossfading in below in terracotta), and benchmark comparisons appear as museum-plinth \"pillars\" of different heights, each topped with its own placard, growing upward as the value counts up. |\n| Accent color | A single coral/terracotta orange runs through every interactive marker — the docent badge pill, the active progress dot, and the \"winning\" pillar/tag (`#D77552`–`#D88462`), against otherwise gray/silver-neutral competitors. |\n| Progress/wayfinding | Top-right: a small horizontal row of dots-and-dashes (like a museum floor-plan trail), the current room's dot lit and glowing coral, visited dots dim red, unvisited dots gray. Top-left: a fixed two-line small-caps header (\"EXHIBIT NAME 특별전\" / \"Room N · room title\"), gold underline beneath sub-labels like \"특별전\". |\n| Caption bar | A full-width, dark, rounded \"pill\" bar pinned along the bottom with a rounded coral \"도슨트\" (docent) badge on the left and a typewriter-revealed sentence of narration to its right, completing near the end of each room's dwell. |\n| Bookend | Opens and closes on the same gold-lit triangular spotlight + serif title-card composition (entrance/exit), with the closing shot adding a small illuminated green \"출구 →\" (exit) sign, like a real building egress sign. |\n\n## Direction\nRender a nighttime \"special exhibit\" walkthrough: a sequence of static, locked-off wide shots, each one a pitch-black gallery room lit by exactly one warm sepia spotlight cone (with faint drifting dust motes) that carves a single low-poly faceted object — flat-shaded, hard facet edges, rotating slowly — out of the darkness on a plain museum pedestal. Beside or below it, place a cream/parchment museum wall-label card: small eyebrow line, serif headline, thin rule, sans-serif detail lines, tiny source credit at the bottom. Whenever you must show a number or comparison, don't draw a normal chart — stage it as a museum artifact instead (a hanging price-tag with the old value struck through and the new one below, or a set of pedestal \"pillars\" of different heights each capped with its own placard, growing upward as the value counts up). Reserve one warm coral/terracotta accent color for whatever should read as \"the highlight\" (an active tag, the winning pillar, the current step marker) and keep everything else in neutral grays, warm blacks, and dim gold. Keep a fixed small-caps location header in one corner and a room-trail progress dial (dots joined by short dashes, current one glowing) in the other. Along the bottom, run a full-width dark pill-shaped caption bar with a small rounded \"guide\" badge and narration text that types itself out. Move between rooms with a slow lateral pan past a dark dividing wall — glimpsing the edge of the last room and the next — never a hard cut, and let the spotlight fade to black and \"search\" back on rather than snapping instantly. Keep the soundtrack sparse: a quiet, continuous low ambient drone under everything, a soft high tick/click on most transitions, an occasional short bright chime accenting a reveal, and reserve one longer broadband swell for your single biggest \"reveal\" moment. Open and close on the same gold-triangle-spotlight title-card composition so the piece reads as a matched entrance and exit.\n"},{"id":"S9-X21","name":"突发新闻","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X21.jpg","markdown":"# X21 突发新闻（Breaking news）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：快讯开场\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: breaking-news broadcast\n\nThis clip resembles a cable-news \"breaking news\" graphics package — the kind of template a 24-hour news channel uses to announce a major story. It opens on a slowly-revealed rotating-globe title card, sweeps a diagonal tri-color ribbon across the screen for the headline stinger, cuts to a flat-vector illustrated news anchor at a desk with a persistent lower-third and scrolling ticker, pushes into a zooming world map (globe → country → city → geolocation pin), then alternates between data/stat cards and the anchor before closing on a minimal logo card. The feeling is urgent, corporate-broadcast, and info-dense: deep navy blues, hard red accents, bold condensed numerals, and a constant on-screen HUD (live clock, channel bug, ticker) that never disappears, evoking authority and immediacy rather than warmth.\n\n## Signature\n| Item | On screen |\n|---|---|\n| Background | Deep navy-blue field throughout, `#030e29`–`#051129`, with a faint rectangular grid overlay and soft diagonal light-ray streaks radiating from top corners (visible clearly in the globe intro) |\n| Panel / card fill | Slightly lighter navy blue boxes for graphic panels and stat cards, `#0c2151`–`#112e72`, edged with a thin bright cyan-blue top border `#36b5fe` |\n| Primary accent (alert) | Saturated red `#d6121c` used for the \"속보\"/breaking tag, lower-third bar, ticker background, and logo block |\n| Secondary accent | Gold/amber `#ffc02f` used as a thin underline stripe beneath the red lower-third and in the diagonal ribbon band |\n| Text | Pure white `#ffffff` for headlines and big numerals; muted blue-gray `#4e576e` for secondary/subtitle captions |\n| Font feel | Bold, geometric sans-serif for both Korean and Latin/numeral text (weight and spacing suggest something like Noto Sans KR Bold / a Spoqa Han Sans-style face, unconfirmed) — headlines are large and heavy, captions and source lines are small and medium-weight |\n| Layout / composition | Classic broadcast-chrome template: top-left channel bug (red logo box + pulsing \"LIVE\" dot), top-right live date/time readout that ticks in real time, a persistent bottom scrolling ticker (red bar of rotating headlines), and a lower-third headline bar (red tab + white/light bar + gold underline) that appears whenever a new fact is introduced |\n| Signature visual moves | (1) radar-style concentric dashed rings pulsing around the intro globe; (2) a diagonal three-stripe ribbon (white / red / gold) sweeping across the frame as a scene-change stinger; (3) a map push-in sequence that zooms from full globe to country to city with a pulsing red geolocation pin and radar rings; (4) stat cards where a number appears, then a comparison value shows struck-through with a red percentage-change delta badge; (5) horizontal benchmark bars that read as pre-filled |\n\n## Direction\nCreate a mock TV-news 'breaking news' broadcast package, generic-network style (no real logos or people). Background is a deep navy-blue field (~#04102a) with a very subtle grid texture and soft diagonal light rays bleeding in from the top corners. Open (0-1s) on a stylized wireframe globe centered in frame, rendered in mid-blue line-art on the navy background, ringed by pulsing concentric dashed arcs like a radar sweep. At ~1s, wipe a diagonal three-stripe ribbon across the full frame — thin white stripe, thick saturated red stripe, thin gold stripe — carrying a large bold all-caps headline word and a short white subhead beneath it. Cut to a flat, minimal vector-illustrated news anchor (simple geometric human silhouette, business attire, no facial animation needed) seated at a glossy desk in front of a softly lit studio backdrop, with a labeled graphic panel floating top-right (navy card, thin bright cyan-blue top border, bold white/gold headline text). Maintain a persistent broadcast HUD throughout every scene: a small red channel-bug box top-left with a pulsing 'LIVE' dot, a live-updating date/time readout top-right, a red lower-third bar with a bold headline and a thin gold underline, and a continuously scrolling red ticker strip along the very bottom feeding short rotating fact snippets. Around the middle third, push the camera from a full world map down through a country outline to a specific city, ending on a pulsing red geolocation pin with expanding radar rings, holding each zoom level 2-3 seconds. Follow with a sequence of dark navy stat cards: bold oversized white numerals as headline figures, a smaller muted comparison value shown struck-through beside a red percentage-change pill, and horizontal progress/benchmark bars with rounded caps in bright blue filling to different lengths. Close on a plain navy card with a small red-and-white logo lockup centered and a short muted-gray closing line beneath it, ticker and audio fading to silence. Underscore the entire piece with a continuous, evenly-spaced percussive ticking/clicking bed (no melody), swelling slightly louder every ~8 seconds around transitions and fading out completely in the final 1-2 seconds. Typography throughout is bold, heavy, geometric sans-serif; color palette is strictly navy blue, white, saturated red, and gold, with one bright cyan-blue accent line for panel borders.\n"},{"id":"S9-X22","name":"侦探线索板","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X22.jpg","markdown":"# X22 侦探线索板（Detective board）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：把线索连起来，科普推理\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: detective evidence board\n\nThis clip reads like a detective's evidence corkboard being filmed and slowly revealed: a warm, lamp-lit cork bulletin board covered in pinned index cards and handwritten sticky notes, centered on a \"mugshot\" placeholder card for an unidentified suspect. Instead of cutting between shots, a single camera pans and tracks sideways across the board as new evidence cards appear one by one, each strung to the suspect card with taut red thread, building toward a wide \"conspiracy board\" pull-back. A camera-flash whiteout is the only hard cut in the video, swapping the mystery card for a clean \"identified\" result card stamped closed. The pacing feels like a true-crime documentary graphics package: steady ticking/pulsing music under a slow reveal, a stab of sharp hits, one big swell exactly on the flash-cut, then a fade to quiet.\n\n## Signature\n| Item | On screen |\n|---|---|\n| Backdrop | tight, textured cork-board surface, warm brown (observed ~#89552F, #694929, #917350), vignetting to near-black at the frame edges (~#17100A) as if lit by a single desk lamp |\n| Suspect card | tall grey \"mugshot\" placeholder: black silhouette bust, a white \"?\" for a face, faint horizontal height-ruler ticks, and a solid black name-plate bar along the bottom |\n| Evidence cards | cream/off-white lined index cards (~#F0E6CE–#EFE4CE), pinned slightly askew, each with a bold headline, a small numbered tag in the corner, and a tiny citation line along the bottom edge |\n| Sticky notes | small handwriting-font notes in muted mustard yellow (~#A19347 as lit) and dusty pink/lavender (~#B78F9A as lit), scattered at random tilts around the board edges, carrying half-finished questions |\n| Red accents | round red pushpins and taut red string joining them into a web (reds observed roughly #C8271D–#FE5E50), red hand-drawn circles/underlines marking a \"found\" value, a red strike-through-and-replace on a corrected number, and a red rubber-stamp outline near the end |\n| Typography | a rounded sans-serif for headings and on-card numbers, paired with a casual marker/handwriting font for sticky notes and red hand-annotations; small, thin caption-style type for source lines |\n| Camera | fixed distance/angle, only pans, tracks, and slowly zooms across the board — no shot-to-shot cutting except one flash transition |\n| Reveal card | plain white card with a red small-caps eyebrow label, a large centered title, a black name-plate footer, and a red stamped badge, closing on one line of caption text at the very bottom of frame |\n\n## Direction\nStyle an investigation-corkboard reveal video: a fixed camera looking at a warm, dimly lit cork bulletin board with heavy vignetting toward near-black edges, as if lit by a single desk lamp. Center a tall grey \"unidentified subject\" placeholder card — silhouette bust, big question mark, faint ruler ticks, black name-plate bar — pinned up with red pushpins. Reveal supporting evidence one card at a time by panning/tracking the camera across the board rather than cutting: cream, slightly tilted lined index cards, each with a bold headline, a small numbered evidence tag, and a thin citation line at the bottom. Build each headline with a fast letter-by-letter typewriter reveal, then animate its numbers in place — cross out and replace a value in red, sweep a dial needle while a percentage counts up, grow a bar in a bar chart and circle the winning one with a red hand-drawn ring. Scatter handwritten sticky notes in muted yellow and dusty pink around the board edges at random tilts, each holding a short half-finished question. About three-quarters through, pull the camera back to a wide shot where every evidence card is connected to the central card by taut red string in a spoke/web pattern, timed to the loudest point of the music. Make the one and only hard cut a full-frame camera-flash whiteout that swaps the placeholder card for a clean white result card: a red small-caps eyebrow label, a bold centered title, a black name-plate footer, and a red stamped badge, with one caption line typing in beneath it before the whole frame fades to black. Score it with a steady, evenly spaced ticking/pulsing rhythm through the build-up, a handful of sharp stabs just before the climax, one dominant swelling hit exactly on the flash-cut, and a smoothly decaying tail to silence afterward — no vocals. Keep the palette to warm cork browns, cream paper, near-black shadow, and a single red accent used for every pin, thread, hand-annotation, and stamp; pair a clean rounded sans-serif for structured text with a casual marker/handwriting font for anything meant to look hand-written.\n"},{"id":"S9-X23","name":"绘本","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X23.jpg","markdown":"# X23 绘本（Picture storybook）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：温暖地讲故事\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: picture storybook\n\nThe video looks like a physical children's picture book filmed lying flat on a wooden desk: a leather-bound cover with gold ornamental corners opens like a real book, then the camera holds a flat, head-on view of each cream-paper spread as it \"reads itself\" — a circular illustration on the left breathing with small looping motion, and serif body copy typing itself in on the right. The mood is warm, nostalgic, and gentle: soft pastel scene-paintings inside a locket-shaped frame, muted earth-tone data callouts, and a plush round mascot that waves through every scene. It reads as a bedtime-story explainer: cozy rather than corporate, tactile rather than flat-UI.\n\n## Signature\n| Item | On screen |\n|---|---|\n| Base surface | Dark wood-grain desk background (~#40291a–#4a3320) visible around the book at all times, with faint soft-focus dust/light motes drifting over it |\n| Book object | Closed cover is deep maroon leather (~#461912 to #632c21) with a thin gold double-rule border (~#b2965e) and gold diamond corner ornaments; opened pages are matte cream paper (~#f4ead4 family) with a visible center gutter/spine shadow and a small triangular page-fold mark at the bottom center |\n| Illustration frame | Every left page holds one circular \"locket\" window (thin brown ring) framing a single flat-vector scene; two small leaf sprigs pinch the top of the circle like a brooch |\n| Palette per scene | Each chapter reskins the circle in one soft gradient: indigo night sky (~#2b2d5a) with cream stars, dawn sky fading to sun-glow (~#feeeb6) over green hills (~#9bc278), pale sky blue (~#a9d9f0) with white clouds and motion streaks, warm orange coin-shower sky (~#f8cf94) with gold coin icons, mint-green sky (~#c8e3d1/#dcecd7) with a picket fence and floating hearts, and a dusk gradient (purple ~#7a6485 to rose ~#aa5776 to warm hill ~#d9764f) for the closing scene |\n| Mascot | A single round, blob-bodied chibi character (terracotta-orange body ~#af6648, blushed cheeks, simple dot eyes, one arm always raised waving) stands in every illustration, wearing a small nameplate/badge on its belly and topped with a small pulsing sparkle/star icon over its head |\n| Typography | Cover title in a gold calligraphic/serif display face on maroon; page headings in a bold dark serif (near-black on cream); a smaller hand-brushed script line beneath each illustration acts as the chapter label; body copy is a plain reading serif; small gray caption line bottom-right credits a source |\n| Data callouts | Key numbers/phrases are drawn in a warm terracotta-red accent (~#bc6040) with a hand-drawn underline stroke that draws itself in; comparisons appear as simple flat horizontal bar pairs (muted taupe ~#bda081 vs. accent terracotta ~#bc6040) and small rounded \"old value → new value\" tag chips with the old value struck through |\n| Ornament | A thin horizontal rule with a small centered diamond mark separates every page heading from its body text; page numbers sit centered at the bottom of each page in the same brown ink |\n\n## Direction\nBuild the video as a physical picture book lying flat on a warm wood-grain desk, shot straight-on with no camera movement except a brief perspective \"lift\" when the cover opens. Use a cozy, nostalgic palette: a leather cover (deep maroon or another rich, muted hue) with a thin gold double-line border and diamond corner ornaments, opening onto matte cream paper spreads with a visible center gutter. Give every left-hand page one circular \"locket\" illustration bordered by a thin ring and two small leaf sprigs, and reskin its background gradient per chapter to match that chapter's mood (e.g. night sky, sunrise, open sky, a themed motif) rendered in soft, flat, pastel vector shapes. Populate every scene with one small recurring plush/blob mascot — simple dot eyes, one arm perpetually raised in a wave, a tiny badge on its body, and a pulsing sparkle above its head — so it functions as the storyteller's constant companion across chapters. On the right-hand page, set a bold serif heading, a thin rule with a centered diamond divider, and body copy that reveals progressively (as if being typed or read aloud); pick one warm accent color (terracotta, coral, or similar) reserved only for key numbers/phrases, drawn with a hand-underline stroke, and reuse that same accent for any simple flat bar-chart comparisons or \"old value → new value\" tag chips. Add a small brushed-script chapter label under each illustration and centered page numbers at the bottom. Structure the narrative as a fixed sequence of short chapters (roughly 3–5 seconds each), transitioned by a physical page-turn/curl animation rather than a cut or crossfade, and close on a clearly marked \"The End\"-style page before fading to black. Score it with one continuous, softly rhythmic plucked/bell-like musical loop that fades in at the start and out at the end, and place one bright, full-spectrum chime/whoosh sound effect at every page-turn to punctuate the chapter changes.\n"},{"id":"S9-X24","name":"RPG 任务","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X24.jpg","markdown":"# X24 RPG 任务（RPG quest）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：学习像做任务\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: 16-bit RPG quest\n\nA clip that reads like a cutscene lifted from a 16-bit console RPG (SNES/Game Boy Advance era): a fixed-camera pixel-art diorama, a JRPG dialogue box with typewriter text, a \"quest log\" stat panel, a sword-and-slime battle vignette, a radial-burst level-up screen, and a loot-pickup card, bookended by a night title card and a sunset \"quest clear\" card. A thin scanline texture sits over every frame, and a chiptune-style pulsing melody drives the pacing. The overall feeling is nostalgic, toy-like, and gamified — factual/data content (benchmark scores, price changes) is translated one-to-one into RPG game-feel (quest completion bars, \"+EXP\" pickups, loot cards, stat increases).\n\n## Signature\n| Item | On screen |\n|---|---|\n| Ground truth: fixed low-res pixel-art dioramas | Every scene is a static illustrated background (starry night skyline with moon and castle silhouette; daytime village with houses/trees/road; dungeon brick corridor with wall torches; sunset hillside) rendered in blocky, low-detail pixel art with no camera pan/zoom |\n| Palette: deep indigo night + warm amber accents | Night/dungeon backdrops sit in `#0C0A22`–`#1B1545`–`#371D5A` indigo-violet; warm points of light in `#E6B85A` (window glow) and `#FDF2D2` (torch flame); daytime scene uses `#5FB3FC` sky and `#54A048` grass; sunset ending blends `#271A50` → `#C65952` → `#F29F6B` |\n| UI chrome: rounded steel-bordered dialogue/panel boxes | Navy-blue fill (`#283267`/`#314292`) boxes with a light-gray beveled border, a tabbed name-plate in the corner (e.g. a small pill reading a speaker name), used for both dialogue and stat panels |\n| Progress bars as game meters | Horizontal bordered bars with a solid orange-terracotta fill (`#BE674C`) over a dark navy track, a white tick mark marking a \"previous score\" reference point, a percentage readout, and a rotated red/gold \"완료!\" (complete) stamp ribbon on finish |\n| Blocky pixel typography with drop shadow | All text (titles, dialogue, UI labels) uses a chunky, uniform-width pixel font with a hard 1-tone drop shadow; headline words (e.g. \"LEVEL UP!\") animate in as individually offset/bouncing letters |\n| Cross-shaped sparkle/particle bursts | Small \"+\"-shaped white/gold sparkles drift and twinkle in night skies, radial-burst screens, and loot-glow beams; green/white square particles burst on \"hit\" or \"+EXP\" moments |\n| Global scanline overlay | A faint, constant horizontal line texture (CRT-style) is visible across every frame, in every scene, at every brightness level |\n| Loot/reward cards with struck-through old values | Item-pickup notifications show an old value with a red strikethrough next to a new value in green, styled like an RPG equipment-acquired popup, with a small gold spotlight beam and glowing ring/key icon |\n\n## Direction\nRender a fixed-camera, low-resolution pixel-art RPG cutscene in distinct static \"cards\" cut together with hard black-frame transitions (occasional flash or halftone-dissolve texture on the cut, never a crossfade). Use a blocky pixel font with a flat drop shadow for all text, typewriter-reveal dialogue lines inside a rounded, steel-bordered navy dialogue box with a small tabbed name-plate in the corner. Palette: deep indigo-to-violet night skies with warm amber points of light, a bright daytime sky-blue/grass-green village alternative, and a warm sunset gradient (purple → coral → peach) for a bookend opening/closing card; keep every scene otherwise low-saturation and flat-shaded. Apply a constant, subtle horizontal scanline texture over the whole frame. Translate any factual/quantitative content (scores, comparisons, price or performance changes) into game mechanics: bordered progress-meter bars with a reference tick mark and a percentage label, a rotating \"complete!\" stamp ribbon on milestones, floating \"+\"-shaped sparkle particles and score-pop text on gains, and RPG loot-card popups (old value struck through in red, new value in green) for improvements. Include at least one radial-burst \"level up\" card with a character silhouette centered in rotating light rays and stat lines animating in one by one with up/down arrows. Score it with a continuously pulsing chiptune-style melody that holds a steady mid-level loudness through the first two-thirds, dips into a brief near-silent beat at a dramatic cut, then builds louder toward a final climax before cutting to silence on the last frame — no fade-outs anywhere in picture or sound.\n"},{"id":"S9-X25","name":"天气预报","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X25.jpg","markdown":"# X25 天气预报（Weather forecast）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：用天气讲情绪，感受讲解很合适\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: TV weather forecast\n\nA short data video styled as a Korean TV nightly-weather-report broadcast graphics package repurposed for a status update. It opens with a satellite/radar-style storm-spiral animation, then cuts through a sequence of \"forecast card\" scenes — a temperature-style map, thermometers, wind/speed gauges, a multi-day forecast strip, and a sunny sign-off — each dressed with real broadcast chrome: a channel logo pill, a live date badge, and a scrolling breaking-news ticker along the bottom. The screen stays locked-off and flat; all motion lives inside the graphics (falling snow, twinkling stars, drifting wind lines, a fast diagonal wipe between scenes). A bright, plucky instrumental loop runs under it with an accented musical hit landing on every scene change, giving the whole thing a polished, on-air, slightly playful newsroom feel.\n\n## Signature\n| Item | On screen |\n|---|---|\n| Background | Vertical navy gradient, lighter blue near the top (`#09316b`/`#0a326e`) fading to near-black at the bottom (`#031433`/`#051530`) — same gradient in every scene |\n| Cards / panels | Solid-fill rounded-rectangle panels (`#092348`), no stroke, soft drop shadow, one stat or graphic per card |\n| Palette | Navy bg `#09316b`→`#031433`, panel `#092348`, alert/ticker red `#dd2222`–`#df2122` with a brighter dot `#fe4544`, accent yellow `#fdd118`, sun gradient `#fef2a4`(center)→`#fdaf23`/`#fe9d18`(rim), off-white text `#f7ffff`/`#dee4e7` |\n| Typography | Heavy, rounded geometric sans (bold Korean UI/dashboard face); oversized bold stat numbers in yellow/blue/white; smaller muted gray-blue captions and source lines |\n| Iconography | Flat vector weather icons with a soft gradient + drop shadow (sun, cloud, snowflake, thermometer, wind compass, speedometer gauge, umbrella) — a rounded \"fluent\"/2.5D icon look |\n| Fixed chrome | Top-left pill logo (circular spiral emblem + wordmark) and top-right rounded status badge with a glowing dot, present unchanged in every scene; full-width ticker bar at the very bottom (colored label block + continuously scrolling headline text, thin bright top border) |\n| Layout rhythm | Title block anchored upper-left: bold headline next to a thin vertical accent bar, plus a smaller subhead; 1–3 card panels laid out horizontally below it; a small citation line bottom-left above the ticker |\n\n## Direction\nBuild a locked-off \"broadcast weather report\" data video. Open on a rotating storm-spiral animation made of soft glowing dots over a faint grid backdrop, resolving into a title card. Cut through a sequence of scenes styled as on-air weather-graphics: a fixed top bar with a circular-emblem logo pill on the left and a rounded status/date badge with a glowing dot on the right, present in every scene; a title block at upper-left with a bold headline beside a thin vertical accent bar and a smaller subhead; below it, one to three solid-fill rounded card panels, each holding a single stat or icon graphic (thermometer, gauge, compass, multi-day forecast strip, etc.) drawn in a soft, flat-with-shadow icon style; a small citation line bottom-left; and a full-width ticker bar along the very bottom (colored label block plus continuously scrolling headline text, thin bright top border). Use a deep navy vertical gradient background (lighter/blue near the top, near-black at the bottom) throughout, one saturated accent color (warm yellow) for accent bars, wipes, and highlighted numbers, a saturated red reserved for alert/ticker chrome, and warm sun-gradient colors for a positive/clear payoff scene. Layer mood-appropriate ambient particles at low opacity behind each scene's cards (e.g. falling snow, twinkling stars, drifting streak lines) to match that scene's subject. Cut between scenes with a fast diagonal wipe (bright leading edge) roughly every 4–9 seconds. End on a single centered glowing icon with a short sign-off headline, then fade to black. Score it with a continuous, bright plucked/mallet-style melodic loop over a soft pad, a steady quick pulse, quiet and even overall level that fades in and out at the edges, plus one clearly louder accented musical hit synced to each wipe transition as a broadcast stinger.\n"},{"id":"S9-X26","name":"电视购物","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X26.jpg","markdown":"# X26 电视购物（Home-shopping infomercial）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：讽刺卖课的，06 段可以试\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: home-shopping infomercial\n\nThe video reads as a parody of a Korean late-night TV home-shopping / infomercial broadcast: a flat, rounded-vector cartoon world where each beat of the pitch gets its own full-bleed, single-hue \"sunburst\" backdrop, a chunky comic-shout headline slams onto the screen, and a persistent lower-third news-ticker bar keeps scrolling bullet claims underneath. A simple blob-shaped mascot character stands in for \"the customer,\" shown gloomy and gray before the pitch and pumped-up and colorful after it. There is no camera movement at all — it behaves like a slide deck of hard graphic cards, each card populated with big bold numbers, price tags, comparison panels, and cartoon icons (a ticking bomb, a ringing phone, a rocket, a green checkmark). The audio is a bright, dense, evenly-pulsing music-and-SFX bed that stays \"hot\" almost the entire runtime, occasionally spiking with comedic riser/whoosh effects timed to the hard cuts, giving the whole thing a caffeinated, game-show-auction energy.\n\n## Signature\n\n| Item | On screen |\n|---|---|\n| Background treatment | Full-bleed radial \"sunburst\" rays fill every scene in one saturated hue; the hue itself is the scene-to-scene variable (measured: near-black charcoal `#131313`/`#1F1F1F` for a \"gloomy before\" beat, golden `#FED84A`/`#FEB812` for a product-reveal beat, alert red `#D80011`/`#DC0111` for a price-drop beat, electric blue `#1C38FF`/`#385BFD` for a comparison/CTA beat, purple-to-magenta `#851C92`/`#5B088A` for a phone-in-now beat, and a dark warm-brown vignette `#130D08`→`#592917` for the closing card) |\n| Palette accents | Ticker tag red `#E70010`/`#E80012`, ticker scroll-bar yellow `#FED300`, stat/progress-bar orange `#FE7A19`, confirmation-badge green `#18B44B`, neutral flat gray `#DDDDDD` for the \"before\" state and inactive bars, white card fills with heavy black strokes for comparison panels |\n| Shapes / icons | Flat, thick-outlined cartoon props: a bomb with a lit, sparking fuse; a corded phone receiver mid-ring with motion arcs; a cardboard-box product reveal rendered in simple isometric-style panels; a rocket; a circular green checkmark badge; small 4-point sparkle/twinkle accents scattered over every backdrop |\n| Typography / type feel | Oversized, ultra-bold \"comic shout\" display lettering (rounded slab caps) with heavy black outline and a offset drop shadow, used for headlines in both Latin caps and the local script; a bold rounded sans for body callouts and big price numbers; a plain thin white sans reserved for small print / source-citation footnotes bottom-left |\n| Composition | Fixed top-left \"LIVE\" red pill badge plus a small channel/show-name tag, present in every frame; a permanent lower-third ticker strip (red label chip + yellow scrolling-text bar) running the entire video; comparison beats use two-column or two-row bordered white cards (old value struck through in red, replaced by a bigger new value) with a jagged starburst \"percent-off\" badge pinned to the corner |\n| Transitions | Hard cuts only — no dissolves, wipes, or camera moves; each cut is preceded by a 1–2 frame \"flash\" card (a near-blank pastel field with a small white impact starburst and a short caption) that functions like a whip-flash/impact sting bridging one saturated-hue scene to the next |\n| Signature flourish | A cartoon bomb icon with a burning fuse used as a recurring \"big number incoming\" beat, visually priming a hard cut into the next price/stat card — read as the style's connective tissue between claims |\n\n## Direction\nRender a fast-cut, static-camera \"shopping-channel infomercial parody\" in flat, rounded 2D vector illustration. Every beat of the pitch is its own full-bleed scene filled with saturated radial \"sunburst\" rays in a single bold hue (cycle through moody near-black, hype gold, alert red, confident electric blue, urgent magenta/purple, and a warm dark vignette for the close) — change the hue scene-to-scene rather than blending it. Keep a persistent top-left \"LIVE\" badge and small show-name tag in every frame, plus a permanent lower-third ticker bar (a colored label chip feeding into a bright scrolling text bar) that runs the whole piece. Use oversized ultra-bold \"comic shout\" display lettering with heavy black outlines and offset drop shadows for headlines, mixed with a rounded sans for supporting numbers and callouts, and reserve a thin plain sans for small-print attribution text tucked in a corner. Populate scenes with flat, thick-outlined cartoon props appropriate to the new subject (a simple blob-style mascot character shown gloomy/gray in a \"before\" state and colorful/triumphant in an \"after\" state; comparison beats as bordered white cards with an old value struck through in red and replaced by a bigger new value, pinned with a jagged starburst badge; at least one \"big reveal\" icon rendered like a product box or package; a call-to-action icon such as a ringing phone; a confirmation checkmark badge). Cut hard between scenes — no dissolves or camera movement — bridging each cut with a one-or-two-frame flash card (a near-blank pastel field with a small white impact burst) so scenes feel detonated into one another rather than eased. Score it with a bright, dense, evenly-pulsing rhythmic music bed that comes in hot within the first second or two and stays energetic and loud almost throughout, accented with short percussive transient hits and the occasional comedic riser/whoosh timed to the hard cuts; keep one clearly quieter cold-open moment before the energy locks in, and let the single loudest musical/SFX hit land on the most urgent, final call-to-action beat.\n"},{"id":"S9-X27","name":"体育直播","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X27.jpg","markdown":"# X27 体育直播（Sports broadcast）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：解说感\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: sports broadcast\n\nThe clip reads like a cable-TV live-sports broadcast package — a night-match stadium open with floodlights and an LED-dot crowd, cut with the on-screen graphics package a network overlays during a match (score bug, \"LIVE\" badge, lower-third source ticker) — repurposed to walk through a head-to-head stat comparison instead of a game. Motion is entirely 2D broadcast-graphics motion (nothing pans or dollies; text counts up, bars fill, cards slide and glow) cut into short hard-cut chapters every few seconds. The audio is a rhythmic, plucked/percussive pulse that tightens and loosens with the on-screen drama — quieter during the analytical stat-sheet beat, swelling into a bright, confetti-timed climax at the finale — which together with the broadcast chrome gives it the feel of a TV \"match highlights\" reel rather than a slide deck.\n\n## Signature\n\n| Item | On screen |\n|---|---|\n| Backdrop | Two modes: (1) a night stadium establishing shot — dark navy sky, crossed floodlight beams, a dot-matrix grid of crowd/scoreboard lights, a green turf pitch with center circle at the bottom edge; (2) flat deep-navy card panels with a faint diagonal pinstripe/chevron texture (like jersey fabric) behind data content |\n| Broadcast chrome | Persistent top-left mini score bug (event name + running score), top-right red \"LIVE\" pill + league/season badge, and a bottom-edge scrolling ticker bar citing the data source — all classic sports-broadcast overlay furniture that stays fixed while the content behind it changes |\n| Cards & data viz | Rounded dark cards with a bold solid-color header bar coding the two sides (warm orange vs. cool slate-blue), oversized tabular numerals for the headline stat, thin horizontal comparison bars, a trading-card-style \"player card\" (jersey silhouette, numbered stat rows), and a lane-track \"race\" diagram with motion-trail ghost dots standing in for a speed comparison |\n| Palette | Deep navy `#0f172f` (base/panels); darker navy `#0b1224`/`#091330` (backgrounds); pitch green `#0b3318`/`#16442a`; primary orange accent `#fd772c`; burnt-orange gradient `#cc5a28`; muted slate-blue `#586b8b`; muted brick red `#5d1117` (LIVE badge); amber/gold callout `#fec83d`; bright gold `#ffd136` (star badge); white `#ffffff` for numerals/labels |\n| Type | Bold, condensed, all-caps sans-serif for headlines and card titles; blocky tabular numerals styled like a digital scoreboard for every stat; small tracked-out caps for kicker labels and the ticker |\n| Layout | Header row (score bug + LIVE badge) is locked in place across the whole runtime; the frame beneath it swaps between full-bleed stadium shots and dark card panels; a ticker bar anchors the bottom edge once the data section begins |\n| Transitions | No crossfades between chapters — each new \"scene\" (title card → scorecard → replay/race graphic → player card + stat sheet → pricing banner → final/MVP shot) is a hard cut; a diagonal color-block wipe was observed sliding a panel in from one edge at one cut. Within a scene, elements enter by fade-up, slide, or count-up rather than by cutting |\n| Signature move | Headline numbers animate from 0.0 up to their final value as a counting ticker; the winning card then gets a glowing highlighted border, a circled/arrowed delta callout, and a small yellow \"lead\" pill — the visual grammar of a broadcast graphic revealing who's ahead |\n\n## Direction\nDesign a motion-graphics short that borrows the visual grammar of a live TV sports broadcast to present a head-to-head comparison. Open and close on a night-stadium establishing shot: deep navy sky, crossed floodlight beams, a dot-matrix grid of distant crowd/scoreboard lights, and a turf-green playing surface with a center line/circle at the bottom edge. Keep a persistent broadcast \"score bug\" locked in the top-left corner and a red \"LIVE\" pill plus event/season badge locked in the top-right, unchanged across every cut; once data appears, add a scrolling lower-third ticker bar along the bottom citing the source.\n\nCut hard between short chapters (title card, scorecard, a \"replay\" comparison graphic, a trading-card-style profile with stat rows, a season-stat sheet, a pricing/cost banner, and a final \"MVP\" celebration with confetti) roughly every 3–8 seconds — never crossfade between chapters, but animate freely within one: fade/slide elements in, fill horizontal bars, and count numerals up from zero to their final value. Code the two sides being compared with a warm bright-orange header/accent against a cool muted slate-blue one, on a deep-navy base with a faint diagonal pinstripe texture; reserve a bright gold/amber for \"lead\" callouts and a star-shaped MVP badge. Use bold, condensed, all-caps type with blocky tabular numerals that read like a digital scoreboard. Whichever side is winning a given stat gets a glowing highlighted border, an arrowed or circled delta callout, and a small colored \"lead\" pill — make the graphics themselves narrate who is ahead.\n\nScore it with a rhythmic, percussive/plucked pulse rather than a sustained melody: keep it dense and driving under the intro and headline comparisons, let it thin out and go quieter under any slower, more analytical/data-dense screen, then build back into a bright full-spectrum swell timed exactly to the climax (trophy/MVP/confetti moment), and let it fade to near-silence at the very end. Layer in a sparse, irregular high-frequency tick or shimmer texture throughout for broadcast-graphic polish, but keep the piece instrumental — no vocals.\n"},{"id":"S9-X28","name":"组装说明书","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X28.jpg","markdown":"# X28 组装说明书（Assembly manual）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：讲步骤，宜家说明书那种\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: assembly manual\n\nThe video reads like a printed IKEA-style product-assembly instruction booklet that has been turned into a motion explainer: flat vector line art on aged cream paper, a giant chapter numeral opening every page, an isometric shipping-box \"hero\" object, a small stick-figure mascot for scale, and literal hardware metaphors (price tags, coin stacks, a conveyor belt, wall outlets) standing in for abstract data instead of ordinary charts. Pages slide over one another like leaves of a manual being turned rather than cutting, each diagram builds itself piece by piece before its number snaps in, and a steady rhythmic tick-track under occasional synth swells gives the whole thing a calm, deliberate, \"assembled and verified\" feeling that ends on a stamped checkmark held in a still frame.\n\n## Signature\n| Item | On screen |\n|---|---|\n| Background | Warm cream paper, solid core `#F0EEE4` with a soft vignette toward the edges (`#E1DDD1`); flat, no gradients, same tone on every single page |\n| Line and ink | Uniform bold warm-black outlines (`#191A18`) on every shape; clean flat vector, no soft shadows or gradients except a thin drop-shadow where one page slides over another |\n| Palette accents | Neutral tan/putty fill for the \"old/base\" object (`#E4E0D1` lit face, `#CCC6B6` shaded face), a single blush-salmon fill for the \"new/better\" object (`#F4D7CA`), and one terracotta/burnt-orange (`#D0643A`) reserved only for hero stats, badges, checkmarks and deltas |\n| Typography | Heavy grotesque bold sans headline (Latin + a matching bold Korean gothic), an oversized numeral leading each chapter like a manual step, a lighter warm-gray subtitle line beneath it, tiny warm-gray footer citation and page number in the bottom corners of every screen |\n| Iconography as metaphor | Abstract stats are shown as literal objects: a price tag for cost, a coin stack for spend, a conveyor belt feeding out of a machine chute for throughput, rows of a small repeated fence/arch icon as an isotype pictogram grid for a ratio, wall electrical outlets for platform availability, an isometric shipping box for the product, a plain stick figure for human scale |\n| Comparison layout | Nearly every page is a strict two-column \"before vs after\": left = old/neutral tan, right = new/salmon, linked by a thin arrow, a dividing line, or a shared baseline |\n| Change device | A decrease is never just deleted — the old value gets a diagonal cross-out (X) and the removed portion is left behind as a pale dashed \"ghost\" outline (dashed coins, dashed pictogram icons, a struck-through price tag) |\n| Hero-stat badge | The headline number or a completion checkmark sits inside a bold circular ring stamp in the accent orange, like a rubber \"approved\" stamp on the manual's closing page |\n\n## Direction\nDesign a short explainer video as a flat-vector \"assembly manual\" infographic: a warm cream-paper background with a subtle edge vignette held constant across every screen, bold warm-black outlines on every shape, and no gradients or soft shadows except a thin drop-shadow where one page slides over the next. Open each section with an oversized numeral acting as a manual step, paired with a bold sans headline and a smaller gray subtitle line, plus a tiny source-citation line and page number in the bottom corners of every screen, like a printed instruction booklet. Represent every abstract stat with a literal physical object or metaphor — tags, stacked coins, a conveyor belt, wall outlets, a shipping box, an isotype pictogram grid of one small repeated icon — instead of plain charts. Keep a strict two-column \"before vs after\" or \"old vs new\" comparison on nearly every screen: neutral tan/putty for the baseline side, a single blush-salmon fill for the improved side, and reserve one terracotta/burnt-orange accent exclusively for hero numbers, percentage badges, checkmarks and deltas. Show any decrease by crossing out the old value and leaving a dashed ghost outline of what was removed, never a plain deletion. Animate each screen by building its diagram first (bars, stacks or icon rows growing or populating in), then snapping in the number or label, then optionally stamping a circular orange badge or checkmark for the headline takeaway. Move between sections with a fast horizontal slide/page-wipe — the next page covers the previous one with a shadow at the moving seam — rather than a dissolve or hard cut; open directly on the title with no fade-in and close by holding the final frame still instead of fading to black. Add a small stick-figure mascot somewhere for scale or approval. Score it with a steady, fast, unobtrusive tick/pulse bed that gets noticeably denser under the busiest visual reveals, add a synth swell under the single biggest stat of the piece, and finish with a short descending glitch/sweep tail into the closing stamp before a quick fade to silence.\n"},{"id":"S9-X29","name":"白板讲课","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X29.jpg","markdown":"# X29 白板讲课（Whiteboard lecture）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：讲道理\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: whiteboard lecture\n\nA static \"desk-cam\" shot of a physical whiteboard, framed by its own aluminum bezel, on which a hand entering from the bottom-right corner writes, draws, underlines, circles, and erases with dry-erase markers and a block eraser — the classic hand-drawn whiteboard-explainer look. Everything is built stroke-by-stroke in real time rather than fading in, numbers get crossed out and replaced live, and nothing is ever fully wiped clean: every earlier section keeps ghosting through as a barely-visible watermark under the current one, giving the board a used, palimpsest-like texture. The mood is brisk, corporate-friendly, and a little playful (circled step numbers, a green checkmark shield, a red circled callout), riding a continuous, steady percussive beat underneath.\n\n## Signature\n| Item | On screen |\n|---|---|\n| Board & frame | Warm off-white board (`#F7F7F5`) with faint pre-existing gray cleaning-smudge arcs baked into the texture, bordered by a silver/gray beveled double-frame (`#B4B8C0` outer rim fading to `#F5F5F3` inner bevel) that reads as a physical whiteboard's aluminum edge |\n| Hand & tool | A right hand + forearm in a navy sleeve (skin `#F0C3A2`, sleeve `#2F4A77`) enters diagonally from the bottom-right corner every time, holding whichever tool the beat calls for: a capped marker or a blue block eraser (`#2F5BD6`-ish body, gray felt base) |\n| Ink palette | Black `#24262E` for headers/labels, blue `#1B4DC6` for new/updated values and taglines, red `#D5382B` for strikethroughs/alerts/circled callouts, green `#158A45` for a checkmark/safety icon, orange `#DE7426` used sparingly as the sole accent (title underline, one bar series, key stat) |\n| Section slug | Each section opens with a circled sequential numeral (①②③) in black plus a short handwritten headline, top-left, drawn before anything else in that section |\n| Data visuals | Hand-drawn bar charts: a wavy freehand baseline, solid rectangular bars (gray `#99A2AB` = before/old, orange = after/new), value labels hand-lettered above each bar in matching color, category label below in cursive |\n| Comparison rows | \"label : old value (red strikethrough) → new value (blue)\" pattern, sometimes followed by a red hand-drawn circle/oval around a derived percentage |\n| Ghost/echo layering | Every prior drawing persists as a near-transparent pale echo behind the current content instead of disappearing — the board never truly resets, it just gets fainter |\n| Footer | A small gray italic handwritten citation line, bottom-left, present continuously as a running watermark/source credit |\n\n## Direction\nCreate a whiteboard-explainer style video: a static, locked-off shot of a physical whiteboard inside a silver aluminum frame, warm off-white surface with faint pre-existing cleaning-smudge texture. A hand and forearm in a plain long sleeve enters from the bottom-right corner holding a dry-erase marker or a block eraser, and writes, draws, underlines, and circles content directly into frame with a simulated handwriting/drawing reveal (stroke order, not a fade-in). Use a small, consistent ink palette: black for headlines and neutral labels, blue for new/updated values or emphasis lines, red for crossed-out old values and alert callouts (circle or underline them by hand), green for a checkmark or positive icon, and one accent color (e.g. orange) reserved for a title underline and the \"after\" series in any chart. Open each new section with a circled sequential number plus a short handwritten headline at the top-left. Build any data as a simple hand-drawn bar or line chart with a wavy freehand baseline and hand-lettered value labels. Never fully erase the board between sections — let earlier content persist as a faint ghost/watermark behind the new drawing, and only use the eraser tool for small, localized swaps of a single value or word. Keep a small handwritten citation/footer line running continuously in a corner. Score it with a continuous, steady percussive beat with no silent gaps, optionally letting the beat's texture thin briefly at each section change to underline the reveal. Bookend the piece with a matching title-card layout (bold headline + colored underline + short tagline) at both the very start and the very end.\n"},{"id":"S9-X30","name":"电影预告片","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X30.jpg","markdown":"# X30 电影预告片（Movie trailer）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：下期预告、频道预告，11 段可以试\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: movie trailer\n\nA prestige announcement trailer that reads like a luxury-watch or car-launch teaser borrowed for a tech reveal: an ultra-wide cinematic letterbox holds a near-black, warm vignette sky sprinkled with slow-drifting dust motes, and every idea — a question, then a date, then a run of benchmark numbers, then the product name, then a release date — gets its own held, centered, fully-faded screen. Nothing cuts hard; everything breathes in as a soft bloom of light, holds, and dissolves through black into the next. The mood is confident and expensive rather than energetic: hushed opening, a rhythmic pulse of swelling hits under the numbers, one big flare-and-impact at the name reveal, then a slow decrescendo fade-out.\n\n## Signature\n| Item | On screen |\n|---|---|\n| Background | Near-black warm umber/brown (~#150B09), soft radial vignette (darker at edges), faint floating dust/particle field (small soft specks, brightest near #FFFAF7) drifting almost imperceptibly |\n| Frame / crop | The frame letterboxed to an ultra-wide ~2.6:1 window with thick pure-black bars top and bottom, held for the entire runtime |\n| Palette (hex) | Near-white warm glow core #FFF9F0 / #FFFAE6 · champagne-gold #FBE6CB / #DDB59B · copper-amber accent #E5AF81 / #E2A882 · bronze divider #9A7562 · muted warm gray (secondary line) #B0A6A5 · dim gray (footer) #807F7D |\n| Typography | Two-register serif system: a Western Didone/Times-like serif for big hero numerals and the English wordmark (bold, wide letter-spacing on the wordmark), and a softer Korean serif (myeongjo-style) for sentence lines; small caption/eyebrow words are set in slim, letter-spaced tracked caps |\n| Hero-stat template | Every metric screen repeats the same layout: small tracked eyebrow label (amber) → giant glowing serif number/word, centered → thin horizontal bronze rule → one mid-size cream sentence → one smaller muted-gray comparison line beneath it |\n| Glow / flare | A horizontal lens-flare hairline sits at vertical-center across the full width during bright moments, blooming into a soft radial halo behind hero text; on the biggest reveal the bloom visibly travels across the frame like a light sweep with slight motion blur |\n| Footer citation | A small, dim gray source/citation line is anchored bottom-center/bottom-left and persists under nearly every card in the middle section |\n| Composition | Always dead-center, single column, generous negative space, one statement or one number per screen — never more than a headline + two support lines at once |\n\n## Direction\nDesign a static-camera announcement/reveal video in an ultra-wide cinematic letterbox (roughly 2.6:1) inside the frame, with thick permanent black bars top and bottom. Background: a near-black, warm dark-umber vignette sky with a sparse field of soft, slowly drifting dust/light particles — never fully empty, never busy. Build the video as a sequence of fully-centered, single-idea screens (one line of prose, one number, one name, one date) that each fade up from black, hold, and cross-fade back through black into the next — no hard cuts, no camera movement, no wipes. Open quietly with two or three short standalone sentences that build a question or a tension, fading in one at a time over the dust field. Resolve that tension into a bright, glowing reveal (a date, a season, a headline word) lit by a soft radial bloom and a thin horizontal lens-flare hairline across the frame's vertical center. Follow with a rapid-fire run of \"hero stat\" cards, each using an identical template: a small letter-spaced amber/copper eyebrow label, a giant glowing serif number or word in warm off-white with a soft outer glow, a thin bronze divider rule, one cream-colored sentence of context, and one smaller muted-gray comparison line beneath it — plus a small, dim citation/source line pinned near the bottom that persists across these cards. Give the product/subject name its own dedicated reveal: a wide-tracked serif wordmark (near-white at top of each letter blending to warm gold/champagne at the bottom) that a bright light sweep travels across horizontally before it settles into sharp focus, with a short tracked subtitle beneath it. Close on a final centered card (a release date or availability line, optionally a short list of where/how it's available), then fade the entire frame — including the letterbox bars — uniformly to black. Score it with rhythmic, percussive swells (sharp attack, quick decay) timed to land exactly on each screen change rather than a continuous underscore; keep most hits controlled/warm, reserve one full-spectrum, loudest hit for the name/logo reveal, and taper the loudness down through the closing card so the piece ends in near-silence rather than cutting off. Overall palette: warm near-black background, near-white/cream hero type, copper-amber accents and labels, champagne-gold highlights, muted warm grays for secondary text — a \"gilded, hushed-prestige\" look rather than a bright, saturated or high-energy one.\n"},{"id":"S9-X31","name":"数学证明讲课","series":"S9","hasStill":false,"patched":false,"still":"stills/S9-X31.jpg","markdown":"# X31 数学证明讲课（Math proof lecture）\n\n- 分类：X code-video · code-video\n- 适合：一步步推出结论\n\n## 风格提示词\n\n# Style: math proof lecture\n\nA dark, quiet, textbook-like explainer that stages an argument the way a math lecture stages a proof: a title card, a numbered \"lemma\" per claim, and a closing \"therefore\" statement with a QED mark. Everything sits on a near-black panel with a faint off-center glow, rendered as flat 2D vector graphics — thin-stroke bar and scatter charts, color-coded equations that build term by term, and small dim citation text in the corner. The mood is restrained and confident rather than flashy: no camera movement, no particle effects, just clean typography and numbers arriving in a steady, metronomic rhythm under a soft ambient pulse.\n\n## Signature\n| Item | On screen |\n|---|---|\n| Background | Flat near-black navy (~#0a0b0f) with a faint, slightly off-center radial glow (lighter toward left-of-center, a touch darker in a band on the far right) — subtle, almost imperceptible, never a full gradient |\n| Palette | Off-white primary text (~#c7c7c9); gold/amber for the highlighted subject and positive reveals (~#fed74b, dot/marker ~#fed74b); cyan/sky-blue for the baseline/reference entity (marker ~#5ac4dc, glass fill ~#234755); sage green for positive deltas and \"better\" callouts (~#8ab679); dusty coral-red for cost or negative values (dashed line ~#ca6f74, glass fill ~#532623) |\n| Typography | A serif display face (Georgia/Playfair-like) for the big title and large numerals; a plain geometric sans (Latin+Hangul mixed) for labels, axis ticks, and body sentences, stepped down in brightness for lower-hierarchy text (title brightest, body mid-gray, footer citation almost invisible dim gray) |\n| Slide template | Consistent per-slide anatomy: small serif \"series\" title top-left, one line of colored subtitle directly under it acting as a step/lemma label (e.g. a numbered \"Lemma N.\" tag in gold), main content centered in the middle of the frame, a single tiny source-citation line in dim gray pinned bottom-left |\n| Chart idiom | Thin-outline bar and line charts on faint gridlines: bars are outlined rectangles with a soft vertical glass-gradient fill in the series color, value labels floating just above each mark, brace-and-dashed-line annotations calling out the delta between two marks |\n| Equation staging | Numbers and operators are typeset like a worked proof and build left-to-right in color runs — neutral white/gray for given values, coral for the operation being applied, green for the computed result — with the final result getting a gold-outlined box as a \"reveal\" frame |\n| Diagram idiom | XY scatter / quadrant plots with arrow-tipped axes, one colored dot per entity, a directional arrow connecting old→new position, dashed guide lines dropping to each axis, and a faint tinted wash over the \"better\" quadrant with its own label |\n| Open/close marks | Opening title draws on as a hollow outline stroke that then fills solid (like a line being traced before it's colored in), preceded by a single thin drawn stroke/caret; closing slide is a plain \"∴ therefore...\" statement ending in a small QED box (∎), then the whole frame fades to black |\n\n## Direction\nDesign a restrained, textbook-style explainer video: a near-black background (flat, almost no gradient, maybe a whisper of an off-center glow) that stays completely still — no camera movement, only elements fading, drawing on, or growing within a fixed frame. Structure the video like a worked proof: an opening title card whose text draws on as a hollow outline before filling solid, then a sequence of \"lemma\" slides, each with a small title top-left and a one-line colored subtitle acting as a step label, and a closing \"therefore\" statement ending in a small QED-style mark before fading to black. Use a disciplined accent-color system on top of off-white body text and dim-gray meta text: one warm color (gold/amber) for the subject being argued for, one cool color (cyan/blue) for the baseline or comparison point, green for positive deltas or \"the better outcome,\" and a dusty red/coral for costs or negative values — apply these consistently across equations, bar charts, and scatter plots. Stage any math or comparison the way a proof is written: numbers and operators typeset in color-coded runs that build left-to-right, with the final result boxed in the highlight color. Prefer minimal, thin-outline data visuals over illustrations — bar charts with soft gradient fills and value labels, XY plots with arrow axes, dashed guide lines, and a directional arrow showing movement from an old value to a new one. Keep a tiny, low-contrast source-citation line pinned in a corner throughout. For sound, use a calm ambient pad with a bright, regular percussive pulse landing on a steady, almost metronomic interval (roughly every bar), occasional soft bass sweeps under transitions, and one clear loud swell near the end that resolves into a synchronized fade to silence and fade to black.\n"}],"prompts":[{"slug":"code-template-gen","version":"v1","title":"代码模板生成器","markdown":"# 代码模板生成器\n\n## 角色\n你是代码模板工程师。你擅长把一段重复出现的代码抽象成参数化模板，让别人改一个变量就能生成可用的新文件。\n\n## 上下文\n用户会给出一段真实代码（或描述它），以及\"这段代码我要在 5 个项目里复用\"之类的意图。你要把它变成模板：抽出变量、定义文件映射、写清 review 清单。\n\n## 任务\n产出一个 `template.json` 和对应的模板文件内容。\n\n## 约束\n- 变量必须给出类型约束：`PascalCase` / `camelCase` / `snake_case` / `kebab-case`\n- 变量数量控制在 1-5 个，超过说明抽象粒度错了\n- 模板文件用 `{{var}}` 占位，不要用 `${var}`（会和模板字符串冲突）\n- 必须写 review 清单，且每条都可用眼睛检查\n- 不允许模板里残留具体业务名（Order、User 这类必须变成变量）\n\n## 输出格式\n1. `template.json`（含 id / lang / framework / kind / title / description / vars / files / review）\n2. 模板文件正文\n3. 一句\"怎么用\"说明\n\n## 示例\n输入：Fastify 里每个资源的 CRUD 路由都长得一样\n输出：\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"ts-fastify-route\",\n  \"lang\": \"typescript\",\n  \"framework\": \"fastify\",\n  \"kind\": \"route\",\n  \"vars\": [\n    { \"key\": \"Resource\", \"type\": \"PascalCase\", \"example\": \"Order\" },\n    { \"key\": \"resource\", \"type\": \"camelCase\", \"example\": \"order\" },\n    { \"key\": \"table\", \"type\": \"snake_case\", \"example\": \"orders\" }\n  ]\n}\n```\n怎么用：把 `Resource=Order` 交给模型或脚本，渲染出 `src/routes/order.ts`。\n"},{"slug":"mv-storyboard","version":"v1","title":"MV 分镜生成","markdown":"# MV 分镜生成\n\n## 角色\n你是资深 MV 导演兼分镜师，擅长把一首歌的情绪曲线拆成可执行的镜头表。你熟悉短视频平台的节奏规律，知道前 3 秒决定完播率。\n\n## 上下文\n用户会给出一首歌的歌词、时长和风格关键词。目标是产出一份可以直接交给剪辑/动画执行的分镜表，而不是文学性的描述。\n\n## 任务\n把整首歌拆成 8-14 个镜头，输出一张分镜表。\n\n## 约束\n- 每个镜头必须给出：时间码、景别、画面描述、字幕、转场\n- 画面描述必须是\"可被画出来\"的具体指令，禁止\"充满张力\"\"很有感觉\"这类形容词\n- 前 3 秒必须出现歌词第一个词或核心意象\n- 副歌段落镜头切换频率 ≥ 每 2 秒一个\n- 不能出现版权角色、真人明星、品牌 logo\n- 竖屏 9:16，字幕放在下 1/3 安全区\n\n## 输出格式\nMarkdown 表格，列为：\n`| # | 时间码 | 景别 | 画面描述 | 字幕 | 转场 | 风格节点 |`\n\n风格节点列填写该镜头对应的视觉风格编号（如 A3 大字冲击）。\n\n## 示例\n| # | 时间码 | 景别 | 画面描述 | 字幕 | 转场 | 风格节点 |\n|---|--------|------|----------|------|------|----------|\n| 1 | 00:00-00:02 | 特写 | 黑色背景，白色大字\"你\"从画面中心砸下，带轻微震动 | 你 | 硬切 | A3 |\n| 2 | 00:02-00:04 | 中景 | 雨滴状字符从顶部坠落，堆积成\"走\"字轮廓 | 走 | 溶解 | A1 |\n"},{"slug":"mv-title-card","version":"v1","title":"标题卡生成","markdown":"# 标题卡生成\n\n## 角色\n你是 motion graphics 设计师，专做 3-6 秒的标题卡（title card / 片头字卡）。\n\n## 上下文\n用户会给一个标题文字、情绪基调（燃/静/高级/复古）和画幅。你要产出一段可直接渲染的 Canvas 动画描述。\n\n## 任务\n产出一个 3-6 秒的标题卡方案。\n\n## 约束\n- 时长 ≤ 6 秒，必须能无缝循环或干净结束\n- 文字必须可读：对比度足够，不在运动中糊成一团\n- 入场 + 停留 + 出场 三段清晰，停留段 ≥ 1 秒\n- 只用代码绘制，不用外部字体文件（用系统 sans-serif 或 serif）\n- 提供 9:16 和 16:9 两种安全区说明\n\n## 输出格式\n```\n【画幅】...\n【底色】hex\n【文字】内容 / 字体 / 字号策略 / 颜色\n【入场】0.0-1.0s 描述\n【停留】1.0-3.5s 描述（可带微动）\n【出场】3.5-5.0s 描述\n【技术要点】缓动 + 关键参数\n```\n\n## 示例\n输入：标题\"水浒\"，情绪\"高级\"，16:9\n输出：\n【画幅】16:9 1920x1080\n【底色】#0a0a0a\n【文字】水浒 / serif / 字号 180px / 颜色 #e8e2d0\n【入场】0.0-1.0s：文字从下方 40px 处升起，透明度 0→1，easeOutCubic\n【停留】1.0-3.5s：静止，右侧出现一条 1px 竖线，缓慢下延\n【出场】3.5-5.0s：整屏向左侧滑出 8%，同时压暗到 30%\n【技术要点】`y = lerp(40, 0, easeOutCubic(clamp(t/1, 0, 1)))`\n"},{"slug":"style-mix","version":"v1","title":"风格混搭器","markdown":"# 风格混搭器\n\n## 角色\n你是视觉风格混搭师。你熟悉 340 种代码动画风格库，知道哪些风格能融合、哪些互斥。\n\n## 上下文\n用户会给出两个（或更多）风格编号，比如 A3（大字冲击）+ Z1（水墨），希望你产出一种融合风格。你需要判断兼容性，给出融合方案。\n\n## 任务\n产出一段混搭提示词，并说明融合逻辑。\n\n## 约束\n- 先做兼容性判断：主视觉（主体是什么）+ 材质/质感 + 运动规律 三层分别取哪个风格\n- 权重必须明确：主风格 60-80%，副风格 20-40%\n- 互斥组合要拒绝并说明（比如两种都要求\"满屏文字\"的风格）\n- 输出必须能直接喂给代码生成模型\n- 不允许简单叠加\"两个都画\"，要给出融合后的单一画面描述\n\n## 输出格式\n```\n【兼容性】可融合 / 有冲突（说明）\n【主风格】编号 + 名字 + 承担的层\n【副风格】编号 + 名字 + 承担的层 + 融合方式\n【画面描述】一段可直接执行的描述\n【技术要点】关键实现提示\n```\n\n## 示例\n输入：A3 + Z1\n输出：\n【兼容性】可融合：A3 管\"运动的暴力感\"，Z1 管\"材质的水墨感\"，不冲突\n【主风格】A3 大字冲击 —— 承担运动规律（砸落、震动、节拍同步）\n【副风格】Z1 水墨 —— 承担材质（边缘晕开、飞白、墨滴扩散）20%\n【画面描述】黑底，宣纸质感。大字\"你\"从上方砸下，落点处墨迹向四周晕开，边缘带飞白；砸落瞬间整屏轻微震动，墨点飞溅\n【技术要点】字用 Canvas fillText 画，再用多层模糊圆模拟晕开；震动用 `offsetX = sin(t*50)*2`\n"},{"slug":"style-rewrite","version":"v1","title":"风格提示词改写器","markdown":"# 风格提示词改写器\n\n## 角色\n你是代码动画风格的提示词工程师。你熟悉 Canvas 2D / WebGL 的表现手法，能把一段模糊的视觉描述翻译成可执行的代码动画提示词。\n\n## 上下文\n用户会给你一段自然语言描述（例如\"想要一种字像雨一样落下来的效果\"），或一个风格编号（如 A1、Z1、O45）。你需要输出一段能被模型直接用于生成 Canvas 代码的提示词。\n\n## 任务\n把输入改写成结构化提示词，包含：画面构成、运动规律、色彩、时长节奏、技术要点。\n\n## 约束\n- 必须使用 Canvas 2D（除非用户明确要 3D）\n- 必须给出 `draw(t)` 的时间参数用法，t 为秒\n- 必须说明缓动函数选择（linear / easeOut / 弹簧）\n- 颜色一律用 hex，不用\"暖色\"\"冷色\"这类词\n- 禁止使用外部图片素材，所有视觉元素必须代码生成\n- 输出长度控制在 400 字以内\n\n## 输出格式\n```\n【画面构成】...\n【运动规律】...\n【色彩】...\n【节奏】...\n【技术要点】...\n```\n\n## 示例\n输入：字像雨一样落下来\n输出：\n【画面构成】黑色背景，白色单字从画面上方随机 x 位置下落，字号随机 40-120px\n【运动规律】每个字 vy 从 2px/帧 加速到 12px/帧，模拟重力；落地后透明度在 0.5 秒内降到 0\n【色彩】背景 #000000，文字 #ffffff，落地残影 #888888\n【节奏】每 0.3 秒生成 1-3 个新字，副歌段加倍\n【技术要点】用 `t` 驱动位置：`y = v0 * t + 0.5 * g * t * t`，字池预分配 60 个避免 GC 抖动\n"},{"slug":"style-to-code","version":"v1","title":"风格转代码","markdown":"# 风格转代码\n\n## 角色\n你是创意开发者，能把一段视觉描述直接翻译成可运行的 Canvas 2D / Three.js 代码。\n\n## 上下文\n用户会给一个风格编号（如 A3、Z1）或一段画面描述。库里有 340 种风格的提示词，你需要把它们落成代码。\n\n## 任务\n产出一个可直接在浏览器打开的 `index.html`（单文件，零依赖）。\n\n## 约束\n- 单文件，Canvas 2D 或 CDN 引入的 Three.js\n- 所有视觉元素代码生成，不用外部图片\n- 动画必须是 `draw(t)` 的纯函数，t 为秒，保证可逐帧渲染\n- 分辨率自适应，devicePixelRatio 处理\n- 性能目标：60fps，单帧绘制 < 8ms\n- 注释写清楚关键参数（速度、数量、颜色）\n\n## 输出格式\n1. 完整 `index.html`\n2. 三行参数说明（改哪里能调什么效果）\n\n## 示例\n输入：A3 大字冲击\n输出：单文件，`<canvas>` 全屏，`requestAnimationFrame` 驱动，字从上方带重力落下，落地震动用 `Math.sin(t*50)*amp`。\n"},{"slug":"tutorial-steps","version":"v1","title":"教程分步讲解","markdown":"# 教程分步讲解\n\n## 角色\n你是技术教程作者，擅长把复杂操作拆成零基础能跟上的步骤。\n\n## 上下文\n用户会给一个操作任务（装软件、配环境、写一段功能）。你要产出步骤清单。\n\n## 任务\n产出一份带检查点的分步教程。\n\n## 约束\n- 每步只做一件事，一步不超过 3 行\n- 每步必须给\"你应该看到什么\"的验证方式\n- 报错要给最常见 2 种及解法\n- 命令用代码块，路径用绝对路径\n- 不假设读者会任何前置知识，除非显式声明\n\n## 输出格式\n```\n## 第 N 步：标题\n命令或操作\n✅ 预期结果：...\n❌ 如果报错：...\n```\n\n## 示例\n## 第 1 步：确认 Node 版本\n`node -v`\n✅ 预期结果：v20.x 或更高\n❌ 如果报错 `command not found`：Node 没装，去 nodejs.org 下 LTS\n"},{"slug":"video-script","version":"v1","title":"短视频脚本生成","markdown":"# 短视频脚本生成\n\n## 角色\n你是短视频编导，擅长把一段素材或一个观点写成 30-90 秒的爆款脚本。\n\n## 上下文\n用户会给一个主题、目标时长和平台（抖音/B站/小红书）。你要产出可以直接开拍的脚本。\n\n## 任务\n产出一份带时间轴、画面、台词、字幕的脚本。\n\n## 约束\n- 前 3 秒必须给出钩子（问题、冲突、反常识）\n- 每 5-8 秒一个信息点或画面变化\n- 台词口语化，单句不超过 20 字\n- 结尾必须给一个明确的行动指令（关注/评论/收藏）\n- 不写\"大家好我是XX\"这种开场\n- 标注每个镜头的拍摄难度：易/中/难\n\n## 输出格式\n```\n| 时间 | 画面 | 台词 | 字幕 | 难度 |\n|------|------|------|------|------|\n```\n\n## 示例\n| 时间 | 画面 | 台词 | 字幕 | 难度 |\n|------|------|------|------|------|\n| 0-3s | 特写：一堆没整理的线材 | \"你家抽屉里是不是也这样？\" | 你家抽屉也这样吗 | 易 |\n| 3-8s | 中景：手把线材卷起来 | \"三秒，一根都不乱\" | 三秒收纳法 | 易 |\n"}],"templates":[{"id":"py-cli-script","lang":"python","framework":"argparse","kind":"cli","title":"Python CLI 脚本","description":"生成带 argparse、日志、异常出口码的标准命令行脚本骨架。","vars":[{"key":"script","type":"snake_case","required":true,"example":"daily_report"}],"files":[{"from":"files/{{script}}.py","to":"scripts/{{script}}.py"}],"review":["有 --help 且每个参数有 help 文本","异常统一 catch 并返回非 0 退出码","日志带时间戳和级别","无全局可变状态"],"example":{"render":"script=daily_report","output":"scripts/daily_report.py"},"path":"python/argparse/cli"},{"id":"py-fastapi-router","lang":"python","framework":"fastapi","kind":"router","title":"FastAPI 路由","description":"生成带 Pydantic 模型、依赖注入和错误处理的 APIRouter。","vars":[{"key":"resource","type":"snake_case","required":true,"example":"order"},{"key":"Resource","type":"PascalCase","required":true,"example":"Order"}],"files":[{"from":"files/{{resource}}_router.py","to":"app/routers/{{resource}}.py"}],"review":["响应模型用 response_model 声明","404 用 HTTPException 且带 detail","依赖用 Depends 注入，不全局实例化","路径参数有类型注解"],"example":{"render":"resource=order","output":"app/routers/order.py"},"path":"python/fastapi/router"},{"id":"ts-fastify-route","lang":"typescript","framework":"fastify","kind":"route","title":"Fastify REST 路由","description":"生成一个带输入校验、错误处理和日志的 Fastify CRUD 路由。","vars":[{"key":"Resource","type":"PascalCase","required":true,"example":"Order"},{"key":"resource","type":"camelCase","required":true,"example":"order"},{"key":"table","type":"snake_case","required":true,"example":"orders"}],"files":[{"from":"files/{{resource}}.ts","to":"src/routes/{{resource}}.ts"}],"review":["错误处理覆盖 400 / 404 / 500","无 any，无类型断言","输入用 JSON Schema 校验","关键路径有日志"],"example":{"render":"Resource=Order","output":"src/routes/order.ts"},"path":"typescript/fastify/route"},{"id":"ts-react-component","lang":"typescript","framework":"react","kind":"component","title":"React 函数组件","description":"生成带 Props 类型、空状态和加载状态处理的函数组件骨架。","vars":[{"key":"Name","type":"PascalCase","required":true,"example":"UserCard"},{"key":"name","type":"camelCase","required":true,"example":"userCard"}],"files":[{"from":"files/{{Name}}.tsx","to":"src/components/{{Name}}/{{Name}}.tsx"}],"review":["Props 有显式类型，无 any","覆盖 loading / empty / error 三态","事件处理函数命名以 handle 开头","组件不超过 150 行"],"example":{"render":"Name=UserCard","output":"src/components/UserCard/UserCard.tsx"},"path":"typescript/react/component"},{"id":"ts-zod-schema","lang":"typescript","framework":"zod","kind":"schema","title":"Zod 数据校验","description":"生成带类型推导、默认值和错误信息的 Zod schema。","vars":[{"key":"Name","type":"PascalCase","required":true,"example":"UserProfile"},{"key":"name","type":"camelCase","required":true,"example":"userProfile"}],"files":[{"from":"files/{{name}}.schema.ts","to":"src/schemas/{{name}}.schema.ts"}],"review":["类型用 z.infer 导出，不手写 interface","每个字段有 .describe() 或错误文案","可选字段用 .optional() 且给默认值","敏感字段不输出日志"],"example":{"render":"Name=UserProfile","output":"src/schemas/userProfile.schema.ts"},"path":"typescript/zod/schema"}]};
